window.PA_SITUATIONS = [{"explain": "A corporate customer sent EUR 50,000 to a supplier.\nThe money left the account.\nThree days later, nobody could say where it was.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 raised by the customer\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ pacs.002 status report never checked\n→ pacs.028 status request never sent\n→ pacs.004 return never arrived\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show the payment as sent once pacs.008 goes out.\"\n\nSo the screen said sent, and the team stopped looking.\n\nWhy?\n\npacs.008 is only the instruction. It does not prove the payment arrived, settled, or was credited. The status lives in pacs.002, the investigation lives in pacs.028 and pacs.029, and a return only comes back as pacs.004. Without those messages, sent is just a guess.\n\nA message sent is not a payment completed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEach pacs message answers a different question, so keep them separate:\n\n→ pacs.008, was the instruction sent\n→ pacs.002, what is the status now\n→ pacs.028 and pacs.029, what is being investigated\n→ pacs.004, has the money come back\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we show it as sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment reached its destination?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a payment that showed as sent while the money was nowhere to be found?", "id": "ABH-01", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 1, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A message sent is not a payment completed.", "title": "EUR 50,000 sent, and nobody could say where it was", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "pacs.008 sent, EUR 50,000 nowhere to be found", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008", "note": "The instruction went out, but that proves nothing about arrival"}, {"label": "pacs.002", "note": "The status report that was never checked"}, {"label": "pacs.028", "note": "The status request nobody sent"}, {"label": "pacs.029", "note": "The investigation that would have chased the payment"}, {"label": "pacs.004", "note": "The return that never arrived, so no money came back"}, {"label": "pacs.009", "note": "The bank to bank leg that moves the funds between institutions"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009", "pacs.002", "pacs.004", "pacs.028", "pacs.029"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "ABH-02", "seq": 2, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 50,000 sent, the screening team still retyped it by hand", "explain": "A corporate sends EUR 50,000 to a supplier.\nThe payment carries full structured ISO 20022 data.\nYet the screening team retypes the beneficiary name by hand.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 arrives with rich structured fields\n→ The bank's screening tool reads only the free-text remittance line\n→ An analyst copies the name into a separate screening screen\n→ The payment waits two hours for a manual all-clear\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Screen every payment against the sanctions list.\"\n\nIt never said which field the screening must read.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe message was rich, but the control read the envelope, not the data inside it. Structured fields only help when the checks are built on them.\n\nRich data in the message ≠ Rich data in the control.\n\nA message is a control point only when validation, screening and reconciliation actually read its fields.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat the message as the control point, not the envelope.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Data received in the message\n→ Data the screening tool actually reads\n→ Data reconciliation matches on\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When do we screen?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the screening read the structured data, not a retyped copy?\"\n\nHave you seen a rich message wasted because a check still ran on a manual copy?", "takeaway": "Treat the message as the control point, not the envelope, and the automation follows.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Rich data, manual control", "parts": [{"label": "pain.001 arrives", "note": "Full structured fields travel with the EUR 50,000 payment"}, {"label": "Screening reads free text", "note": "The tool reads only the remittance line, not the structured fields"}, {"label": "Analyst retypes by hand", "note": "The beneficiary name is copied into a separate screen"}, {"label": "Two-hour wait", "note": "The payment waits for a manual all-clear"}, {"label": "Message as control point", "note": "Checks must read the fields, not the envelope"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"explain": "A customer bought goods worth GBP 850 on a card.\nThe terminal said approved.\nThe merchant said the money never arrived.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Authorisation approved by the issuer\n→ Clearing record exchanged between the banks\n→ Settlement to the merchant never completed\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Treat an approved authorisation as a completed payment.\"\n\nSo the order was released, and the merchant waited for money that had not moved.\n\nWhy?\n\nAuthorisation only checks the card, the balance, fraud signals and the PIN, CVV or 3DS check. Clearing only exchanges the record. Only settlement moves the funds to the merchant, and a clearing mismatch or a settlement failure can leave an approved payment unpaid.\n\nAuthorisation is permission. It is not money.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAuthorisation, clearing and settlement are three different events, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Authorisation status\n→ Clearing record status\n→ Settlement and funds received\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When was it approved?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the merchant was actually paid?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an approved card payment that the merchant never received?", "id": "ABH-04", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 4, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Authorisation is permission. It is not money.", "title": "Card approved for GBP 850, merchant says not paid", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Issuer checks card, balance, fraud signals and PIN, CVV or 3DS", "title": "Authorisation approved"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "No, approval is permission only", "title": "Is approval the same as payment?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Network exchanges the record between the banks", "title": "Clearing record exchanged"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Funds did not move to the merchant", "title": "Settlement never completed"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Approved on screen, not paid in fact", "title": "Merchant unpaid"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-05", "seq": 5, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 50,000 payment that passed every check and still failed", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 supplier payment passes validation, screening and the rail check.\nIt still fails at the beneficiary bank.\nNobody can say which team owns the failure.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 validated and sent as pacs.008\n→ The rail accepts it and settlement completes\n→ The beneficiary bank rejects it for a missing account detail\n→ Each team points at the next one, and the customer waits\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Knowing ISO 20022 and SWIFT is enough to run payments.\"\n\nIt is not. Each piece only makes sense through its joins to the others.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe lifecycle, cards, rails, clearing and settlement, fraud and AML, and APIs are one system. A failure at the join between two pieces belongs to neither piece alone.\n\nA payment that passes every check ≠ A payment that arrives.\n\nExpertise is the map of the joins, not the list of the parts.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nLearn the connections, not the components in isolation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Validation at initiation\n→ Settlement on the rail\n→ Application at the beneficiary bank\n→ Ownership of a failure at a join\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which team checks the payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every join in the journey was tested, not just each part?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail in the gap between two teams that each said their part was fine?", "takeaway": "Learn the connections, not the components in isolation, and the domain starts to behave as one system.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One connected payment lifecycle", "parts": [{"label": "pain.001 to pacs.008", "note": "Validation passes and the payment leaves"}, {"label": "Rail accepts it", "note": "Settlement completes on the network"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank rejects it", "note": "A missing account detail stops the credit"}, {"label": "No owner at the join", "note": "Each team points at the next one"}, {"label": "Map the joins", "note": "Expertise is the connections between the pieces"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch43-payment-security.html", "label": "Chapter 43: Payment Security", "chapter": "ch43", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-06", "seq": 6, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 50,000 stuck because the card team never met the rail team", "explain": "A customer pays EUR 50,000 by card, the merchant is never credited, and the refund takes nine days.\nThe card team says the authorisation was fine.\nThe rail team says settlement was fine.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Card authorised, ISO 8583 message approved\n→ Clearing runs, settlement moves on the rail\n→ The merchant account shows nothing\n→ Two teams each prove their own part worked\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Payments is a set of separate topics, and each team owns one.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRails, cards, ISO 8583 and ISO 20022, APIs, tokenization, the lifecycle and compliance all touch each other. A failure lives in the join, and nobody who memorises only their own part can find it.\n\nA system that works in silos ≠ A payment that arrives.\n\nThe professional who maps the joins will always outperform one who memorises the parts.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStudy every piece by asking how it connects to the rest.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorisation on the card side\n→ Clearing and settlement on the rail\n→ Credit to the merchant account\n→ Ownership of the join between them\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did each part work?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the whole journey worked end to end?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment lost between two teams that both swore their part was fine?", "takeaway": "A professional who maps the joins will always outperform one who memorises the parts.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Payments, one connected system", "parts": [{"label": "Card authorised", "note": "ISO 8583 approval looks fine to the card team"}, {"label": "Settlement moves", "note": "The rail team sees settlement complete"}, {"label": "Merchant never credited", "note": "The money never reaches the merchant account"}, {"label": "Two teams, no owner", "note": "Each part works, the join fails"}, {"label": "Map the joins", "note": "The failure lives where the pieces meet"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-07", "seq": 7, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 25,000 in two days, and nobody could say what it cost", "explain": "A business sends USD 25,000 to a supplier overseas.\nIt arrives in two days, which looks fast.\nThen the supplier reports USD 24,100 received, and nobody can explain the missing USD 900.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent down the correspondent chain\n→ Each bank takes its charge along the way\n→ The beneficiary bank credits what is left\n→ No single statement shows the full cost\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Make the cross-border payment fast.\"\n\nIt said nothing about cost, compliance or transparency.\n\nWhy?\n\nCross-border joins different countries and different systems into one flow. A payment that is fast but opaque, or cheap but non-compliant, fails the test whatever the corridor.\n\nFast ≠ Good.\n\nSpeed, cost, compliance and transparency have to be judged together, never one alone.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge every cross-border design on all four together.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Speed of arrival\n→ Total cost to the beneficiary\n→ Compliance screening at each hop\n→ Transparency of fees and status\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How fast did it arrive?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary received the full value, compliantly, with every charge visible?\"\n\nHave you seen a fast payment arrive short, with nobody able to explain where the money went?", "takeaway": "Judge every cross-border design on all four together, never on one alone.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Cross-border payments", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 sent", "note": "USD 25,000 leaves down the correspondent chain"}, {"label": "Charges taken en route", "note": "Each bank deducts its fee along the way"}, {"label": "USD 24,100 credited", "note": "The supplier receives less than was sent"}, {"label": "No full cost visible", "note": "No single statement shows every charge"}, {"label": "Four tests together", "note": "Speed, cost, compliance and transparency"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "ABH-08", "seq": 8, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The USD 100,000 Letter of Credit with no paper, and no control", "explain": "A USD 100,000 Letter of Credit goes fully digital.\nNo paper changes hands, and the shipment is released.\nThen the bank discovers the documents were never properly checked.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Digital LC issued, documents uploaded, not couriered\n→ The system marks the documents as received\n→ Payment is released against the digital set\n→ A mismatch in the shipping documents surfaces a week later\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Digitise the Letter of Credit.\"\n\nIt never said which paper checks the digital flow must replace.\n\nWhy?\n\nA fully digital LC shows even the most document-heavy product can run without paper, but digitisation is a redesign of the control model, not a scan of the documents. The paper checks did the work; removing the paper without replacing the checks removes the control.\n\nNo paper ≠ No control needed.\n\nThe real work is tracing what changes in process, data and controls when the paper goes away.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDigitisation is a redesign of the control model, not a scan of the documents.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Document receipt\n→ Document checking against the LC terms\n→ Payment release\n→ Evidence that each check ran\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the LC digital?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every paper check has a working digital replacement before payment is released?\"\n\nHave you seen a digital process drop a control that the paper version used to enforce?", "takeaway": "Digitisation is a redesign of the control model, not a scan of the documents.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A fully digital Letter of Credit", "parts": [{"label": "Digital LC issued", "note": "USD 100,000 LC runs with no paper at all"}, {"label": "Documents uploaded", "note": "The system marks the set as received"}, {"label": "Payment released", "note": "Funds move against the digital documents"}, {"label": "Mismatch found late", "note": "A shipping document error surfaces a week later"}, {"label": "Controls must be redesigned", "note": "Every paper check needs a digital replacement"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-09", "seq": 9, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 50,000 real-time payment the old system could not see", "explain": "A bank launches real-time payments, API-first design and ISO 20022.\nA customer sends EUR 50,000 in seconds.\nThe operations team cannot see it anywhere until the next morning.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 settles in seconds on the instant rail\n→ The API confirms the payment to the customer at once\n→ The bank's own ledger still runs on an overnight batch\n→ Reconciliation finds the payment a day late\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Real-time rails alone make the bank real-time.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBanking in Asia is moving to real-time payments, API-first design, cloud and ISO 20022 together. The rail is only one piece; the ledger, the APIs and the lifecycle have to move with it, or the payment is instant for the customer and invisible to the bank.\n\nInstant for the customer ≠ Instant for the bank.\n\nThe winners are the ones who can bridge technology and the banking transaction lifecycle.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the whole lifecycle at real-time speed, not just the rail.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instant settlement on the rail\n→ Ledger posting inside the bank\n→ Customer confirmation\n→ Reconciliation the same day\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the rail settle fast?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the ledger, the API and the reconciliation all saw the payment in real time too?\"\n\nHave you seen an instant payment that the bank itself only found the next day?", "takeaway": "The winners are the ones who can bridge the two.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Asia's banking upgrade", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 settles in seconds", "note": "The instant rail moves EUR 50,000 at once"}, {"label": "API confirms instantly", "note": "The customer sees success immediately"}, {"label": "Ledger runs overnight", "note": "The bank's own books still batch by night"}, {"label": "Reconciliation a day late", "note": "Operations finds the payment next morning"}, {"label": "Bridge both sides", "note": "Technology and the transaction lifecycle together"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "INS-121", "seq": 10, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 850 collected on the wrong day, and the debtor never agreed", "explain": "A utility collects EUR 850 by direct debit.\nThe money leaves the debtor's account three days early.\nThe debtor demands it back, and the bank has to refund it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor sends the collection file to its PSP\n→ The PSP passes it to the clearing system\n→ The debtor PSP debits the account on the wrong date\n→ The debtor disputes a collection they never expected that day\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Collect on the due date.\"\n\nIt never checked the mandate, the Creditor Identifier or the scheme rules first.\n\nWhy?\n\nDirect debit works because the creditor initiates the pull on the due date instead of waiting for the debtor to pay. That creditor-driven timing only works when the mandate, the Creditor Identifier and the scheme rules are all set up correctly.\n\nCreditor control ≠ Creditor freedom.\n\nA collection flow has to be designed around those three prerequisites from the start.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign every collection around the mandate, the identifier and the scheme rules.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate in place and valid\n→ Creditor Identifier correct on the file\n→ Due date as agreed with the debtor\n→ Collection actually submitted\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we collect?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the mandate, the identifier and the due date were all correct before the pull?\"\n\nHave you seen a direct debit taken on a date the debtor never agreed to?", "takeaway": "A collection only works when the mandate, the Creditor Identifier and the scheme rules are all set up correctly.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Creditor-initiated recurring collections", "parts": [{"label": "Collection file sent", "note": "Creditor sends the EUR 850 collection to its PSP"}, {"label": "Clearing system processes it", "note": "The file passes through the clearing system"}, {"label": "Debtor debited early", "note": "The account is debited three days before the due date"}, {"label": "Debtor disputes it", "note": "A collection on the wrong date must be refunded"}, {"label": "Three prerequisites", "note": "Mandate, Creditor Identifier and scheme rules"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-122", "seq": 11, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 1,200 collected under the wrong Creditor Identifier", "explain": "A business collects EUR 1,200 by SEPA Direct Debit.\nThe debtor bank cannot match the collection to any creditor.\nThe collection is rejected, and the business is paid late.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor sends the collection with its Creditor Identifier\n→ The identifier on the file has one wrong character\n→ The debtor bank cannot recognise who is pulling the funds\n→ The collection is rejected and the money never moves\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The Creditor Identifier is just a label on the file.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn SEPA Direct Debit the Creditor Identifier is the unique code that identifies the creditor across the scheme, and it travels with every collection. No correct identifier means no collection can be initiated or matched to the right creditor.\n\nA wrong identifier ≠ A small typo.\n\nThe identifier is a gating data item, not a label.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat the Creditor Identifier as a gate, not a label.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor identity on the mandate\n→ Creditor Identifier on the collection file\n→ Debtor bank recognition of the creditor\n→ Collection initiation\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the collection?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the identifier on the file matches the creditor on the mandate?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection rejected because one identifier character was wrong?", "takeaway": "No correct identifier means no collection can be initiated or matched to the right creditor, so the identifier is a gating data item, not a label.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Creditor and Creditor Identifier", "parts": [{"label": "Collection sent", "note": "EUR 1,200 collection carries the Creditor Identifier"}, {"label": "One wrong character", "note": "The identifier on the file does not match the mandate"}, {"label": "Debtor bank cannot recognise", "note": "The bank cannot tell who is pulling the funds"}, {"label": "Collection rejected", "note": "The money never moves and the business is paid late"}, {"label": "Identifier is a gate", "note": "Without it, no collection can be initiated"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-125", "seq": 12, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2,000 collected, and nobody knew which corner failed", "explain": "A EUR 2,000 direct debit collection fails.\nThe creditor blames its PSP.\nThe PSP blames the clearing system.\nThe debtor bank says it never received the file.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor sends the collection file to its PSP\n→ The PSP passes it to the clearing and settlement mechanism\n→ The mechanism routes it to the debtor PSP\n→ The debtor PSP never debits the debtor account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One failed collection has one owner, and someone will find them.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA SEPA Direct Debit moves through four corners: the creditor, the creditor PSP, the clearing and settlement mechanism, and the debtor PSP with the debtor. Each corner owns a distinct responsibility, and a failure with no corner assigned to it belongs to nobody.\n\nFour corners ≠ One owner.\n\nThe model pinpoints exactly which party owns the failure, but only if the flow is traced corner by corner.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace a failed collection corner by corner until one party owns it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor file creation\n→ Creditor PSP submission\n→ Clearing and settlement routing\n→ Debtor PSP debit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the collection fail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which corner held the file when it stopped?\"\n\nHave you seen a failed collection passed between four parties while the money sat still?", "takeaway": "Each corner owns a distinct responsibility, so when a collection fails the model pinpoints exactly which party owns the failure.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor sends collection file", "note": "EUR 2,000 collection file goes to the creditor PSP"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor PSP passes it on", "note": "The PSP sends the file to the clearing system"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing system routes it", "note": "The mechanism routes the collection to the debtor PSP"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Debtor PSP never debits", "note": "The debtor account is never debited"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which corner owns the failure?", "note": "Trace the file corner by corner until one party owns it"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-124", "seq": 13, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 5,000 debited on the due date, settled two days later", "explain": "A creditor expects EUR 5,000 on the due date.\nThe debtor is debited on that date, exactly as planned.\nThe creditor's account shows nothing for two more days.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Due date arrives and the debtor account is debited\n→ The funds sit in transit between the banks\n→ Settlement between the banks completes two days later\n→ The creditor's reconciliation shows a gap it cannot explain\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Due date, debit date and settlement date are the same day.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe due date is the date the creditor wants the money, the debit date is the date the debtor account is actually debited, and the settlement date is the date the funds move between the banks. The three often fall on the same day, but they can split apart when a PSP is closed or the clearing system is unavailable.\n\nDebited ≠ Settled.\n\nConfusing them produces wrong value dating and reconciliation breaks.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrack each date as a separate fact, never as one date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Due date the creditor expects\n→ Debit date on the debtor account\n→ Settlement date between the banks\n→ Value dating on the creditor account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When was the debtor debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the due, debit and settlement dates were each recorded and reconciled separately?\"\n\nHave you seen a reconciliation break because three dates were treated as one?", "takeaway": "Confusing them produces wrong value dating and reconciliation breaks, so each date must be tracked as a separate fact.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Due, debit and settlement dates", "parts": [{"label": "Due date", "note": "The date the creditor wants the EUR 5,000"}, {"label": "Debit date", "note": "The date the debtor account is actually debited"}, {"label": "Settlement date", "note": "The date the funds move between the banks"}, {"label": "Dates split apart", "note": "A closed PSP or unavailable clearing splits them"}, {"label": "Wrong dating risk", "note": "Confusing them breaks value dating and reconciliation"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-126", "seq": 14, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 600 collected, but the debtor was never told the amount changed", "explain": "A creditor raises a subscription from EUR 500 to EUR 600.\nThe debtor is debited EUR 600 without warning.\nThe debtor disputes the collection and gets it refunded.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor sends pre-notification for the old EUR 500 amount\n→ The collection file is generated for EUR 600\n→ The creditor PSP submits the EUR 600 file into the scheme\n→ The debtor sees a different amount than the notice promised\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Generating the collection file is the same as notifying the customer.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPre-notification is the notice the creditor sends the debtor before the collection, stating the amount and the date. Collection submission is the creditor PSP sending the file into the scheme. The two steps run on different timelines, and if the amount changes and the pre-notification is not updated, the debtor can dispute the collection.\n\nFile generated ≠ Customer notified.\n\nThe notice and the file must stay in step.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep the pre-notification and the collection file in step, every time the amount changes.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Pre-notification sent to the debtor\n→ Amount and date in the notice\n→ Amount and date in the collection file\n→ Collection submission into the scheme\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the file?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the debtor was told the exact amount and date before the collection ran?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection disputed because the notice and the file disagreed?", "takeaway": "If the amount changes and the pre-notification is not updated, the debtor can dispute the collection, so the notice and the file must stay in step.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Pre-notification sent", "note": "Creditor notifies the debtor of EUR 500 and the date"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Amount rises to EUR 600", "note": "The subscription price changes after the notice"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Collection file generated", "note": "The file is generated for EUR 600, the notice is not updated"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Collection submitted", "note": "The creditor PSP sends the EUR 600 file into the scheme"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Notice and file in step?", "note": "A mismatch between notice and file opens a dispute"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-157", "seq": 15, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 750 collected on 3 days' notice, with no agreement to prove it", "explain": "A creditor collects EUR 750 with only 3 days' notice.\nThe debtor disputes the collection.\nThe creditor cannot prove the debtor ever agreed to shorter notice.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor sends pre-notification 3 days before the collection\n→ The scheme expects 14 calendar days unless agreed otherwise\n→ The debtor disputes, saying the notice was too short\n→ The creditor searches for an agreement and finds none\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Notify the debtor before each collection.\"\n\nIt never said the evidence for a shorter timeline must be kept.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe scheme expects the creditor to notify the debtor before each collection, usually 14 calendar days in advance, unless creditor and debtor have agreed a different timeline. Any agreed alternative must be evidenced, so the creditor can prove the debtor accepted a shorter notice period.\n\nShorter notice ≠ Agreed notice.\n\nA shorter notice with no agreement behind it leaves the collection open to dispute.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat the evidence for a shorter notice as part of the collection itself.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Notice period applied\n→ Agreement for a shorter period\n→ Evidence the debtor accepted it\n→ Collection submitted\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we notify the debtor?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the debtor agreed to this shorter notice period?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection overturned because the agreement was never kept?", "takeaway": "A shorter notice with no agreement behind it leaves the collection open to dispute, so the evidence is as much part of the collection as the notice itself.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Creditor pre-notification agreement", "parts": [{"label": "Notice sent in 3 days", "note": "EUR 750 collection notified only 3 days ahead"}, {"label": "Scheme expects 14 days", "note": "Notice is usually 14 calendar days in advance"}, {"label": "Debtor disputes", "note": "The debtor says the notice was too short"}, {"label": "No agreement found", "note": "No evidence the debtor accepted a shorter period"}, {"label": "Evidence is part of the collection", "note": "The agreement must be provable, not assumed"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-128", "seq": 16, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 900 refund refused because the team counted the wrong days", "explain": "A debtor claims a EUR 900 refund inside the scheme window.\nThe bank refuses, saying the window has closed.\nThe debtor appeals, and the scheme says the bank counted wrong.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Refund window opens, counted in calendar days\n→ The bank counts it in banking business days\n→ The bank tells the debtor the window has closed\n→ The scheme confirms the window was still open\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"All scheme timelines are counted the same way.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSome scheme timelines run in calendar days and others in banking business days, and applying the wrong calendar produces the wrong date. Pre-notification, refund windows and scheme timelines each specify which calendar applies, so no two timelines can be assumed to work the same way.\n\nCalendar days ≠ Banking business days.\n\nThe discipline is simple: check the calendar for each timeline before computing any date.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the calendar for each timeline before computing any date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Timeline being computed\n→ Calendar the scheme specifies for it\n→ Date actually calculated\n→ Refund or notice decision made on it\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we count the days?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the right calendar was used for this timeline?\"\n\nHave you seen a deadline missed because the wrong calendar was counted?", "takeaway": "The discipline is simple: check the calendar for each timeline before computing any date.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Scheme timeline calendars", "parts": [{"label": "Refund window opens", "note": "EUR 900 refund claimed inside the scheme window"}, {"label": "Bank counts business days", "note": "The bank applies the wrong calendar"}, {"label": "Refund refused", "note": "The debtor is told the window has closed"}, {"label": "Scheme says still open", "note": "The window runs in calendar days, not business days"}, {"label": "Check each timeline", "note": "Every timeline specifies its own calendar"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-139", "seq": 19, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 40,000 traced to the last bank, and still nobody knew where the money was", "explain": "A EUR 40,000 payment is sent across three banks.\nThe sender traces it with the UETR to the last bank in the chain.\nThe last bank confirms it received the instruction.\nThe beneficiary still has no money.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent with its UETR\n→ Each bank in the chain logs the same reference\n→ The last bank confirms receipt of the instruction\n→ No bank can confirm the funds actually settled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If we can trace it, we know where the money is.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe UETR is a unique end-to-end transaction reference that stays with a payment across every bank and system it passes through. It lets any party trace the payment and ask each hop for its status using the same reference. A UETR proves the payment exists and can be tracked, but it does not by itself prove where the money is or that it settled.\n\nTraceable ≠ Settled.\n\nTracking and settlement evidence must never be confused.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk for settlement evidence at each hop, not just a trace.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR trace across the chain\n→ Instruction received at each hop\n→ Funds actually settled\n→ Credit to the beneficiary account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we trace the payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds settled at each hop, not just that the instruction arrived?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fully traceable and still missing?", "takeaway": "A UETR proves the payment exists and can be tracked, but it does not by itself prove where the money is or that it settled, so tracking and settlement evidence must never be confused.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "UETR tracking reference", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 sent with UETR", "note": "EUR 40,000 leaves with one end-to-end reference"}, {"label": "Same reference at every hop", "note": "Each bank logs the same UETR"}, {"label": "Last bank confirms receipt", "note": "The instruction is confirmed, the funds are not"}, {"label": "Beneficiary still unpaid", "note": "No bank can confirm settlement"}, {"label": "Trace is not settlement", "note": "Tracking proves existence, not where the money is"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "INS-152", "seq": 20, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 10,000 credited in seconds, settled the next day", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 10,000 by instant payment.\nThe beneficiary sees the credit within seconds and ships the goods.\nThe next morning, the sending bank discovers the interbank settlement never completed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent on the instant rail\n→ Beneficiary account credited within seconds\n→ Goods shipped on the strength of the credit\n→ Interbank settlement between the banks still pending\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary saw the money, so the payment is final.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a customer sends an instant payment, the beneficiary may see the credit within seconds, but the banks behind the payment may still settle between themselves later. The customer experience, the scheme processing and the settlement finality are three different things that do not always happen at the same moment.\n\nInstant credit ≠ Instant settlement.\n\nA payment can look instant to the customer while the interbank settlement is still pending, so the screen is never proof of finality.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever treat the customer screen as proof of settlement.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer credit on screen\n→ Scheme processing completed\n→ Interbank settlement finality\n→ Goods or funds released on the credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the beneficiary see the credit?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves interbank settlement is final before anything is released on it?\"\n\nHave you seen goods shipped on a credit that had not actually settled?", "takeaway": "A payment can look instant to the customer while the interbank settlement is still pending, so the screen is never proof of finality.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instant payment versus settlement", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 sent instantly", "note": "EUR 10,000 moves on the instant rail"}, {"label": "Beneficiary credited in seconds", "note": "The credit shows on screen at once"}, {"label": "Goods shipped", "note": "The seller releases goods on the strength of the credit"}, {"label": "Settlement still pending", "note": "The banks have not yet settled between themselves"}, {"label": "Screen is not finality", "note": "Credit on screen never proves settlement"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-168", "seq": 21, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 5,000 still took four days, despite the G20 targets", "explain": "A small business sends USD 5,000 to a supplier overseas.\nIt takes four days and costs USD 180 in fees.\nThe corridor team points at the G20 targets and says payments are getting faster.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent down the correspondent chain\n→ Each hop adds a day and a charge\n→ The supplier is credited late and short\n→ The team reports the corridor meets the industry goals\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The G20 targets guarantee this payment will be fast and cheap.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe G20 set targets for cross-border payments covering speed, cost, transparency and access, so that sending money across borders becomes cheaper, faster and easier to track. These are goals for the industry, not guarantees for a single payment, and each target needs concrete product and operational measures to be met.\n\nA target ≠ A guarantee.\n\nA business analyst uses the objectives to judge whether a corridor design is actually improving the customer outcome, which is the only test that matters.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge the corridor by the customer's outcome, not by the industry goal.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Speed this payment actually achieved\n→ Cost this payment actually bore\n→ Transparency the customer actually got\n→ Access the customer actually had\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the corridor meet the targets?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this payment was faster, cheaper and clearer for the customer?\"\n\nHave you seen a corridor claim success while a real payment still took four days?", "takeaway": "A business analyst uses the objectives to judge whether a corridor design is actually improving the customer outcome, which is the only test that matters.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "G20 cross-border payment objectives", "parts": [{"label": "USD 5,000 sent", "note": "A small business pays a supplier overseas"}, {"label": "Four days, USD 180 fees", "note": "The payment arrives late and short"}, {"label": "Targets quoted", "note": "The team points at industry goals"}, {"label": "Customer outcome unchanged", "note": "The payment is still slow and expensive"}, {"label": "Judge the outcome", "note": "The only test is the customer's experience"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A11", "seq": 23, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 6 million to pay, EUR 7 million showing, and the money arrives too late", "explain": "A treasurer must pay EUR 6 million at noon.\nThe account shows EUR 9 million opening balance and EUR 4 million coming in.\nThe EUR 4 million arrives at 4pm. The payment fails at noon.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ camt.053 end-of-day statement shows the full day position\n→ The team plans the noon payment on the statement balance\n→ camt.052 intraday report shows only EUR 9 million so far\n→ The EUR 4 million inflow lands after the payment deadline\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Check the balance before paying.\"\n\nIt never said which balance to check.\n\nWhy?\n\nLiquidity management turns on when funds are available, not only how much. The camt.052 account report shows the position accumulated so far today, the camt.053 statement shows the end-of-day position, and the camt.054 notification reports individual debits and credits as they occur. With EUR 9 million opening, EUR 4 million coming in and EUR 6 million to pay, the intraday view shows EUR 7 million only if the incoming payment arrives in time.\n\nEnd-of-day balance ≠ Money available now.\n\nRelying on camt.053 alone misses the intraday picture entirely.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlan every payment against the intraday position, not the statement.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ camt.052 intraday position so far today\n→ camt.054 notifications as credits land\n→ camt.053 end-of-day statement\n→ Payment deadline against funds actually available\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What is the balance?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds were available at the moment the payment had to go?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail because the team planned on money that had not arrived yet?", "takeaway": "With a EUR 9 million opening balance, EUR 4 million coming in and a EUR 6 million payment to make, the intraday view shows EUR 7 million only if the incoming payment arrives in time, so relying on camt.053 alone misses the intraday picture entirely.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three views of one account", "parts": [{"label": "camt.053 statement", "note": "End-of-day position shows the full day balance"}, {"label": "camt.052 intraday report", "note": "Position so far today: EUR 9 million only"}, {"label": "camt.054 notifications", "note": "Individual credits reported as they land"}, {"label": "EUR 4 million arrives at 4pm", "note": "The inflow lands after the noon deadline"}, {"label": "Noon payment fails", "note": "EUR 6 million cannot be paid from EUR 9 million minus nothing"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["camt.052", "camt.053", "camt.054"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A13", "seq": 24, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "Ten payments promised, seven pacs.008 sent, three 'missing'", "explain": "A corporate submits a pain.001 file with 10 payments.\nOnly 7 pacs.008 messages reach the next bank.\nOperations raises an incident: 3 payments are missing.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 file submitted with 10 payments\n→ 7 pacs.008 messages sent to the next bank\n→ Operations counts 7 and reports 3 missing\n→ The 3 other payments were on-us, handled inside the same bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every payment in a pain.001 becomes a pacs.008.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nNot every payment in the file becomes a pacs.008. When sender and beneficiary hold accounts with the same bank, the payment is on-us and is handled internally, with no message to the next bank. Counting only pacs.008 messages shows 7 payments and suggests 3 are missing, while comparing the file total with the message count reveals the 3 on-us payments and explains the gap.\n\nA pain.001 is a promise, not a guarantee of a pacs.008 for each one.\n\nA missing message ≠ A missing payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the file total against the message count before raising an incident.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payments promised in the pain.001 file\n→ pacs.008 messages sent onward\n→ On-us payments handled internally\n→ Payments actually missing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many pacs.008 did we send?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every payment in the file is accounted for, on-us or onward?\"\n\nHave you seen an incident raised for payments that were never missing at all?", "takeaway": "A pain.001 is therefore a promise that payments will be made, not a guarantee of a pacs.008 for each one.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pain.001 promises ten payments", "parts": [{"label": "pain.001 submitted", "note": "Corporate submits 10 payments in one file"}, {"label": "7 pacs.008 sent", "note": "Only 7 messages reach the next bank"}, {"label": "3 on-us payments", "note": "Sender and beneficiary share the same bank"}, {"label": "Incident raised", "note": "Operations reports 3 payments missing"}, {"label": "Promise, not guarantee", "note": "A pain.001 promises payments, not a pacs.008 each"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pain.001", "pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B01", "seq": 25, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2,500 debited, success shown, beneficiary never credited", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 2,500 on a payment app.\nThe account is debited at once.\nThe app shows Payment Successful.\nThe beneficiary never receives the money.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment sent, account debited\n→ The app shows success on the strength of the debit\n→ The beneficiary bank never confirms the credit\n→ The customer believes the payment is done, and waits\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The debit is the confirmation.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nConfirmation in payments comes from the receiving side, not from the debit. Payment Successful should appear only after the beneficiary bank confirms the credit and passes that reply back. In UPI the reply is RespPay, carrying credited or failed, and ISO 20022 schemes carry the same step under a different name, pacs.002, the Payment Status Report.\n\nA debit ≠ A confirmation.\n\nA payment stands confirmed only when the receiving side returns a status, so trust the confirmation, not the debit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nShow success only on the receiving side's status, never on the debit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Debit from the payer account\n→ Credit at the beneficiary bank\n→ Status reply returned (RespPay, pacs.002)\n→ Success shown to the customer\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the account debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary bank confirmed the credit before we showed success?\"\n\nHave you seen a success message shown for a payment that never arrived?", "takeaway": "A payment therefore stands confirmed only when the receiving side returns a status, so trust the confirmation, not the debit.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment sent, account debited", "note": "EUR 2,500 leaves the payer account at once"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "App shows success on the debit", "note": "Success appears before any confirmation arrives"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank never confirms", "note": "No credit is confirmed at the receiving side"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "RespPay or pacs.002 missing", "note": "The status reply carrying credited or failed never comes"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Show success, or wait for the reply?", "note": "Success must wait for the receiving side's status"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": ["pacs.002", "RespPay"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B11", "seq": 26, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The loan consent that quietly opened EUR 50,000 of data", "explain": "A customer applies for a loan and taps consent to share financial data.\nThe lender pulls EUR 50,000 of account history, investments and more.\nThe customer thought they had agreed to share one bank statement.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Consent screen asks for access to financial information\n→ The customer taps yes without reading the scope\n→ The lender accesses data far beyond the loan check\n→ The customer cannot say what they agreed to, or for how long\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Consent is just a yes.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nConsent to access financial information is not simply a yes to everything. Before agreeing, check who is asking for the data, what data they want, why they need it, how often they will access it, and how long the consent is valid, because two services can need very different data for different purposes. Under the India Account Aggregator ecosystem, consent can be revoked, and revoking it can stop further access to the data.\n\nA tap ≠ Informed agreement.\n\nGood consent states its purpose and duration plainly, with a clear way to withdraw, in plain language anyone can follow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake consent show its scope, purpose and duration before the tap.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Who is asking for the data\n→ What data and why\n→ How often and how long access lasts\n→ How consent is revoked\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the customer tap yes?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer understood the scope, the purpose and the duration before agreeing?\"\n\nHave you seen a consent tap open far more data than the customer expected?", "takeaway": "Good consent states its purpose and duration plainly, with a clear way to withdraw, in plain language anyone can follow.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Know what you are agreeing to", "parts": [{"label": "Consent screen shown", "note": "A loan application asks for access to financial data"}, {"label": "Customer taps yes", "note": "The scope, purpose and duration go unread"}, {"label": "Lender pulls wide data", "note": "Access reaches far beyond the loan check"}, {"label": "No way to undo it", "note": "Revocation exists, but the customer was never told"}, {"label": "Consent must be plain", "note": "Purpose, duration and withdrawal in plain language"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-B14", "seq": 27, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 10,000 loan offered by a bank the customer never joined", "explain": "A customer holds a salary account with one bank.\nA different bank offers them a EUR 10,000 loan at a better rate.\nThe customer asks: how can a bank I never joined offer me a loan?\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Salary account stays with the first bank\n→ The customer consents to share financial information\n→ A second bank reads the information and prices a loan\n→ The loan is offered without the customer ever opening an account there\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Your financial life has to sit with one bank.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe financial relationship no longer has to sit with a single institution. One customer can hold an account with one bank, a loan with another, insights with one app and payments with another, while the salary account stays where it is. What moves between providers is the financial information, and it moves only with the customer consent.\n\nThe information moves, the account stays.\n\nThat reframes the competitive question: not how to become your bank, but how to become the provider chosen for the next financial need.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for consented information sharing, not for owning the whole account.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Account held with the bank\n→ Information shared with consent\n→ Loan or product offered on that information\n→ Consent still valid or revoked\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the customer bank with us?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer consented to share the information this offer is built on?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer surprised that a bank they never joined knew enough to lend to them?", "takeaway": "That reframes the competitive question: not how to become your bank, but how to become the provider chosen for the next financial need.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The information moves, the account stays", "parts": [{"label": "Salary account stays put", "note": "The customer's account remains with the first bank"}, {"label": "Information shared with consent", "note": "Financial data moves only with the customer's consent"}, {"label": "Second bank prices a loan", "note": "A EUR 10,000 loan is offered without a new account"}, {"label": "No new account needed", "note": "The customer never joins the second bank"}, {"label": "Become the chosen provider", "note": "Win the next need, not the whole relationship"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C11", "seq": 30, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 25,000 vanished from the dashboard, but the money never moved", "explain": "A customer opens an aggregator app and sees EUR 25,000 across their accounts.\nOne day the dashboard shows EUR 0.\nThe customer calls the bank in a panic. The money is still there.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Aggregator pulls data from banks, mutual funds, insurers and NBFCs\n→ The customer revokes consent for one bank\n→ The dashboard loses that bank's data at once\n→ The customer reads a missing dashboard as missing money\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The app that shows my money holds my money.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn account aggregator is not a bank and does not hold your money; it connects you to the financial institutions that do, giving you one dashboard across banks, mutual funds, insurers and NBFCs. Its three roles are pulling the data together, managing the customer consent before any access, and sharing the information with other financial institutions only with that consent.\n\nA missing dashboard ≠ Missing money.\n\nThe single most important role of the aggregator is consent management, because it never holds the funds it connects you to.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake it unmistakable that the aggregator shows money, it never holds it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Funds held by the bank\n→ Data pulled into the dashboard\n→ Consent that allows the pull\n→ Sharing with other institutions\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the dashboard show the balance?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer understands the dashboard is a view, not a vault?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer panic over money that was safe, because a dashboard went blank?", "takeaway": "The single most important role of the aggregator is consent management, because it never holds the funds it connects you to.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Your money stays with your bank", "parts": [{"label": "Dashboard shows EUR 25,000", "note": "One view across banks, funds, insurers and NBFCs"}, {"label": "Consent revoked", "note": "The customer withdraws access for one bank"}, {"label": "Dashboard shows EUR 0", "note": "The view loses that bank's data at once"}, {"label": "Money still at the bank", "note": "The funds never moved, only the view changed"}, {"label": "Consent is the key role", "note": "The aggregator manages consent, it holds no funds"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C13", "seq": 31, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "Three accounts, one app, and a EUR 5,000 fee to see them", "explain": "A customer holds accounts at three banks.\nOne app promises to show all three in one place.\nTo see them, the app demands the customer open a new account with it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ App offers a single dashboard for three accounts\n→ The customer connects the first account through open APIs\n→ The app locks the data inside its own system\n→ Viewing the other two accounts now needs a new account with the app\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One dashboard means Open Banking.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOpen Banking uses APIs and the Account Information Service to let one app show accounts held at several banks, so a customer can see three accounts in one place without switching apps. An account aggregator combines those accounts into a single dashboard, and the aggregated data can also support payments and loan eligibility decisions. The test is whether the app reaches the banking information through open APIs; if it locks the data in instead, that is monetisation of a latch-on mechanism, not Open Banking.\n\nA dashboard ≠ Open Banking.\n\nA customer should not need a new bank account merely to view other accounts.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest the app on its APIs, not on its dashboard.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Accounts reached through open APIs\n→ Data combined into one view\n→ Data locked inside the app\n→ New account demanded to view\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the app show three accounts?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the data flows through open APIs, with no new account required to see it?\"\n\nHave you seen an app call itself open while locking your data inside?", "takeaway": "A customer should not need a new bank account merely to view other accounts.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three accounts, one place", "parts": [{"label": "Three accounts held", "note": "The customer banks with three different banks"}, {"label": "APIs promise one view", "note": "Open APIs should show all three in one place"}, {"label": "Data locked inside", "note": "The app traps the data in its own system"}, {"label": "New account demanded", "note": "Viewing the rest needs an account with the app"}, {"label": "Open APIs, not locked-in data", "note": "A latch-on is monetisation, not Open Banking"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C15", "seq": 32, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 8,000 insurance claim the bank app could not see", "explain": "A customer opens their bank app to check an EUR 8,000 insurance claim.\nThe app shows their bank accounts perfectly.\nIt shows nothing about the insurance, the pension or the mortgage.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank app shows accounts, expenses, bills and a loan offer\n→ The customer asks about an insurance claim\n→ The app has no view of insurance, investments or pensions\n→ The customer must chase three more providers separately\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Open Banking covers my whole financial life.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOpen Banking lets one app show a customer bank accounts, track expenses, pay bills or apply for a loan, but it covers only bank accounts. Open Finance goes further, letting insurers, mutual fund companies and banks share information about investments, insurance, pensions and mortgages through APIs, always with the customer consent.\n\nBank accounts ≠ Whole financial life.\n\nThe difference is scope: Open Banking is banking data, Open Finance is the customer whole financial life.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the scope honestly: banking data, or the whole financial life.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Banking data the app can show\n→ Insurance, investment, pension and mortgage data\n→ APIs that connect each provider\n→ Consent that allows each share\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the app show the accounts?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer knows which parts of their financial life this app can and cannot see?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer assume one app sees everything, and find out it sees only the bank accounts?", "takeaway": "The difference is scope: Open Banking is banking data, Open Finance is the customer whole financial life.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Open Finance is the whole financial life", "parts": [{"label": "Bank app shows accounts", "note": "Accounts, expenses, bills and a loan offer"}, {"label": "EUR 8,000 claim invisible", "note": "The insurance claim does not appear"}, {"label": "Pension and mortgage missing", "note": "No view beyond the bank accounts"}, {"label": "Open Finance goes further", "note": "Insurers, funds and banks share through APIs"}, {"label": "Scope is the difference", "note": "Banking data, or the whole financial life"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-070", "seq": 42, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 15,000 failed, and the merchant blamed the wrong provider", "explain": "A merchant's EUR 15,000 of card payments fail in one afternoon.\nThe merchant blames the gateway.\nThe gateway shows every instruction routed correctly.\nThe processor had the outage.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer pays, gateway routes the instruction\n→ Processor moves it through authorisation, clearing and settlement\n→ The processor fails, and the payments stop\n→ The merchant spends a day arguing with the gateway\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The gateway is the payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe gateway orchestrates the payment: it connects the merchant to the payment methods, routes the instruction and returns the outcome. The processor performs the transaction processing: it moves the instruction through authorisation, clearing and settlement with the scheme and the banks. Some providers do both, so check which role a provider actually performs before assuming what it is responsible for.\n\nRouting ≠ Processing.\n\nConfusing the two roles means blaming the wrong party when a payment fails.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck which role each provider actually performs before blaming anyone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gateway: routing the instruction and returning the outcome\n→ Processor: authorisation, clearing and settlement\n→ Provider doing both roles\n→ Party that actually failed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who is our payments provider?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which role failed, gateway or processor, before we escalate?\"\n\nHave you seen a day wasted blaming the provider that was working fine?", "takeaway": "Confusing the two roles means blaming the wrong party when a payment fails.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Gateway orchestrates, processor executes", "parts": [{"label": "Gateway routes it", "note": "The instruction is routed and the outcome returned"}, {"label": "Processor fails", "note": "Authorisation, clearing and settlement stop"}, {"label": "EUR 15,000 blocked", "note": "An afternoon of card payments fails"}, {"label": "Wrong party blamed", "note": "The merchant argues with the gateway"}, {"label": "Check the role first", "note": "Some providers do both, so verify the role"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C001", "seq": 49, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 75,000 sent serial, returned cover, and the trail went cold", "explain": "A EUR 75,000 payment goes out by the serial method.\nThe beneficiary bank returns it, but by the cover method.\nThe operations team cannot match the return to the original payment.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent down one chain, funds and instruction together\n→ The return comes back split: instruction one way, funds another\n→ The reference that linked the two legs is missing\n→ The team cannot prove which payment the return belongs to\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The return will follow the same method as the payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe serial method moves funds and payment information together down one chain, each bank passing the same instruction onward to the next. The cover method splits them: the instruction travels in an MT103 while the funds settle separately through an MT202 COV, so each leg is settled on its own. The method chosen governs tracking, charging and returns, so it must be identified before any of those are handled.\n\nSame payment ≠ Same method back.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nIdentify the method first, before tracking, charging or returning anything.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Method used on the way out\n→ Method used on the way back\n→ Reference linking instruction and funds\n→ Return matched to the original payment\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment go out?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we know the method on both legs before we handle a return?\"\n\nHave you seen a return go cold because nobody recorded which method the payment used?", "takeaway": "The method chosen governs tracking, charging and returns, so it must be identified before any of those are handled.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Serial versus cover", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 sent serial", "note": "Funds and instruction travel together in one chain"}, {"label": "Return comes back cover", "note": "Instruction and funds split on the way back"}, {"label": "Linking reference missing", "note": "Nothing binds the return to the original payment"}, {"label": "Trail goes cold", "note": "The team cannot match the return"}, {"label": "Identify the method first", "note": "It governs tracking, charges and returns"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C002", "seq": 50, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 60,000 MT202 with no customer behind it, and the team paid it anyway", "explain": "An MT202 for EUR 60,000 arrives at a bank.\nThe team treats it as cover for a customer payment and releases the funds.\nThere was no customer payment behind it at all.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT202 arrives, moving funds between banks\n→ The team assumes it settles a named MT103\n→ No linking reference is checked\n→ EUR 60,000 of the bank's own funds is paid out to a customer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every MT202 is a cover payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT202 COV exists to settle a specific MT103: it moves the funds for that customer payment and carries the reference that binds the two messages together. A plain MT202 is a bank's own transfer, moving proprietary funds between banks with no customer payment behind it. Both move money between banks, yet only the COV answers to a customer instruction.\n\nBoth move money ≠ Both answer to a customer.\n\nThat distinction is what trips people up.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the reference before treating any MT202 as cover.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT202 COV, bound to a named MT103\n→ Plain MT202, the bank's own funds\n→ Linking reference on the cover leg\n→ Customer payment the cover answers to\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did an MT202 arrive?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this MT202 is cover for a named customer payment, not the bank's own transfer?\"\n\nHave you seen a bank-to-bank transfer mistaken for a customer payment?", "takeaway": "Both move money between banks, yet only the COV answers to a customer instruction, and that distinction is what trips people up.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT202 COV versus plain MT202", "parts": [{"label": "MT202 arrives", "note": "EUR 60,000 moves between banks"}, {"label": "Assumed to be cover", "note": "The team treats it as settling a customer payment"}, {"label": "No reference checked", "note": "Nothing binds it to a named MT103"}, {"label": "Bank's own funds paid out", "note": "EUR 60,000 released to a customer by mistake"}, {"label": "Check the reference", "note": "Only the COV answers to a customer instruction"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT202", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C004", "seq": 52, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 120,000 sent on an MT202, and the beneficiary never existed", "explain": "A bank sends EUR 120,000 to another bank on an MT202.\nThe receiving bank asks: which customer is this for?\nThere is no customer. The MT202 was never a customer payment.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment instruction raised for a named beneficiary\n→ The team sends an MT202 instead of an MT103\n→ The receiving bank looks for a beneficiary and finds none\n→ The payment sits unapplied while the customer waits\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An MT202 can carry a customer payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 is a customer payment instruction: it orders the receiving bank to pay a named beneficiary a named amount. An MT202 is a bank-to-bank instruction that moves funds between banks with no customer beneficiary attached. Every customer payment starts with an MT103, and an MT202 appears only when a separate funds leg is required.\n\nA funds movement ≠ A customer payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart every customer payment with an MT103, and add an MT202 only for the funds leg.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer instruction (MT103) with named beneficiary\n→ Bank-to-bank funds leg (MT202) where one is needed\n→ Beneficiary the receiving bank can pay\n→ Payment applied, not sitting unapplied\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the money?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the receiving bank got a customer instruction it could actually apply?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer payment sent on a message that has nowhere to name the customer?", "takeaway": "Every customer payment starts with an MT103, and an MT202 appears only when a separate funds leg is required.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT103 and MT202: which does what", "parts": [{"label": "MT202 sent", "note": "EUR 120,000 moves bank to bank"}, {"label": "No beneficiary named", "note": "The message has no customer to pay"}, {"label": "Receiving bank stuck", "note": "It cannot apply a payment with no beneficiary"}, {"label": "Payment sits unapplied", "note": "The customer waits while the funds sit"}, {"label": "Start with MT103", "note": "Every customer payment starts with an MT103"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C005", "seq": 53, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 35,000 credited to the wrong account because field 59 was never read", "explain": "A EUR 35,000 payment arrives at a bank.\nThe operations team credits it using the account in the remittance text.\nThe real beneficiary, named in field 59, never receives it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives with field 59 naming the beneficiary and account\n→ Field 57 names the beneficiary bank by BIC\n→ The team reads the free-text remittance instead of field 59\n→ The money is credited to the wrong account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The remittance text tells us who to pay.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nField 59 names the beneficiary customer and the account to be credited, so it is the first field to check when a payment must reach the right person. Field 57 names the beneficiary bank by BIC, telling the chain which bank must receive the instruction. In a serial payment each bank reads both fields from the MT103 it receives and forwards the message to the next bank in the chain.\n\nFree text ≠ Field 59.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead field 59 first, every time, before any credit is posted.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 59, beneficiary customer and account\n→ Field 57, beneficiary bank by BIC\n→ Free-text remittance, never the source of the payee\n→ Credit posted only to the field 59 account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we credit someone?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the credit went to the account named in field 59?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment credited to the wrong person because a free-text note was trusted over the field?", "takeaway": "In a serial payment each bank reads both fields from the MT103 it receives and forwards the message to the next bank in the chain.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reading an MT103", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 arrives", "note": "EUR 35,000 payment with field 59 and field 57"}, {"label": "Field 59 names the beneficiary", "note": "The beneficiary customer and account to credit"}, {"label": "Field 57 names the bank", "note": "The beneficiary bank, by BIC"}, {"label": "Team reads free text", "note": "The remittance note is trusted instead"}, {"label": "Wrong account credited", "note": "The real beneficiary never receives it"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C006", "seq": 54, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 80,000 screened against the wrong name, because field 50 was skipped", "explain": "An EUR 80,000 payment is screened and cleared.\nDays later, compliance finds the ordering customer was on a watchlist.\nThe screening had checked the name in field 72, not the ordering customer in field 50.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives, field 50 naming the ordering customer\n→ Field 72 carries extra sender-to-receiver information\n→ Screening runs against a name pulled from field 72\n→ The real ordering customer is never checked\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any name in the message is good enough for screening.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nField 50 identifies the ordering customer, the party who instructed the payment, and it is the first field screening and reconciliation check. Field 72 carries sender-to-receiver information, the extra instructions that fit no structured field. The two fields answer different questions: field 50 says who is paying, field 72 says what else the receiver needs to know.\n\nField 72 ≠ Field 50.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nScreen the ordering customer in field 50 first, and treat field 72 as extra information only.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 50, who is paying\n→ Field 72, what else the receiver needs to know\n→ Screening run on the field 50 party\n→ Reconciliation matched on the field 50 party\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we screen a name?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves screening ran on the ordering customer in field 50?\"\n\nHave you seen a check pass because it ran on the wrong field?", "takeaway": "The two fields answer different questions: field 50 says who is paying, field 72 says what else the receiver needs to know.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fields 50 and 72 in an MT103", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 arrives", "note": "EUR 80,000 payment, field 50 and field 72 present"}, {"label": "Field 50: ordering customer", "note": "The party who instructed the payment"}, {"label": "Field 72: extra information", "note": "Sender-to-receiver notes with no structured field"}, {"label": "Screening checks field 72", "note": "The wrong name is screened and cleared"}, {"label": "Watchlist party missed", "note": "The real ordering customer was never checked"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C007", "seq": 55, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 4,000 refunded under SDD B2B, and the scheme said it could never happen", "explain": "A business debtor claims a EUR 4,000 refund on a direct debit.\nThe bank pays it, no reason asked.\nThe creditor protests: this was an SDD B2B collection, and B2B grants no refund right.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Collection set up as SDD B2B, business to business only\n→ The debtor claims a refund within eight weeks\n→ The bank applies the SDD Core rule and refunds it\n→ The creditor points out the scheme was B2B, not Core\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"All SEPA direct debits give an eight-week refund.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSDD Core covers collections from consumers and businesses, and it gives the debtor an eight-week refund right with no reason required. SDD B2B is restricted to business-to-business collections and grants no such refund right, which is why the debtor bank must verify the mandate before the first collection. The schemes differ on refunds and mandate checks.\n\nCore ≠ B2B.\n\nUsing the wrong scheme is a real error, not a paperwork slip.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nVerify the scheme and the mandate before the first collection, and apply that scheme's refund rule.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme used: Core or B2B\n→ Mandate verified before the first collection\n→ Refund right that scheme actually grants\n→ Refund decision made on the right scheme\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the debtor ask within eight weeks?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we applied the refund rule of the scheme this collection actually ran under?\"\n\nHave you seen a refund paid that the scheme never allowed?", "takeaway": "The schemes differ on refunds and mandate checks, so using the wrong one is a real error, not a paperwork slip.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "SDD Core versus SDD B2B", "parts": [{"label": "Collection set up as B2B", "note": "Business-to-business collection, no refund right"}, {"label": "Debtor claims EUR 4,000", "note": "Refund claimed within eight weeks"}, {"label": "Bank applies Core rule", "note": "The refund is paid, no reason asked"}, {"label": "Creditor protests", "note": "B2B grants no such refund right"}, {"label": "Scheme decides the refund", "note": "Core and B2B differ on refunds and mandates"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C008", "seq": 56, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 25,000 SEPA payment rejected for the wrong reason code", "explain": "A EUR 25,000 SEPA payment is rejected.\nThe team fixes the message format and resends it.\nIt is rejected again, with the same reason code.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment initiated with a pain.001\n→ The scheme rejects it for a timing breach\n→ The team rebuilds the message in a different format\n→ The scheme rejects it again: the rule, not the format, was the problem\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The format is the payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA SEPA payment is defined by scheme rules on timing, reason codes and returns, not by the message format that happens to carry it. The format is only the envelope: the same payment can be initiated with an MT101 or an MT103 as well as with a pain.001. That is why an MT103 and a pacs.008 can carry one underlying SEPA payment, the pacs.008 being its ISO 20022 form.\n\nA new envelope ≠ A fixed payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFix the scheme rule that failed, not the format that carried it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme rules: timing, reason codes, returns\n→ Format carrying the payment\n→ Reason code on the rejection\n→ Fix applied to the rule that failed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we resend it in a new format?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we fixed the scheme rule named in the reason code?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment resubmitted in a new format and rejected for the same rule?", "takeaway": "That is why an MT103 and a pacs.008 can carry one underlying SEPA payment, the pacs.008 being its ISO 20022 form.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A SEPA payment", "parts": [{"label": "pain.001 sent", "note": "EUR 25,000 SEPA payment initiated"}, {"label": "Rejected for timing", "note": "The scheme rejects it on a timing rule"}, {"label": "Format rebuilt", "note": "The team resends in a different format"}, {"label": "Rejected again", "note": "The same reason code comes back"}, {"label": "Fix the rule", "note": "The scheme defines the payment, not the format"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pain.001", "pacs.008", "MT101", "MT103"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C059", "seq": 59, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 45,000 MT103 arrived, and the team could not read it in MX", "explain": "A EUR 45,000 payment arrives as an MT103.\nThe bank's new ISO 20022 platform cannot map it.\nThe team treats it as a new kind of payment and builds a new process for it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 received, customer credit transfer\n→ Platform expects pacs.008 and finds no mapping\n→ The team builds a separate MT handling process\n→ Two processes now do the same job, and returns fall between them\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT and MX are different payments.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.008 is the ISO 20022 customer credit transfer, the equivalent of an MT103, and a pacs.009 is the financial institution credit transfer, the equivalent of an MT202. A pacs.004 is the payment return, used when a pacs.008 cannot be applied and the funds must go back. The two families describe the same payments.\n\nSame legs, new names.\n\nA team moving from MT to MX only has to learn the new names for the same legs.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap MT to MX by the leg, not by building a second process.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer leg: MT103, pacs.008\n→ Bank leg: MT202, pacs.009\n→ Return leg: pacs.004\n→ One process handling both families\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we build an MX process?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every MT leg has an MX twin in the same process?\"\n\nHave you seen two processes doing one job because nobody mapped the names?", "takeaway": "The two families describe the same payments, so a team moving from MT to MX only has to learn the new names for the same legs.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.008, pacs.009 and pacs.004", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 received", "note": "EUR 45,000 customer credit transfer arrives"}, {"label": "No MX mapping", "note": "The new platform cannot read the MT leg"}, {"label": "Second process built", "note": "Two processes now do the same job"}, {"label": "Returns fall between", "note": "A return has no home in either process"}, {"label": "Same legs, new names", "note": "pacs.008 is the MT103, pacs.009 the MT202"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009", "pacs.004", "MT103", "MT202"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C068", "seq": 60, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 200,000 MT103 sent by a corporate, and the chain rejected it", "explain": "A corporate with its own BIC sends an MT103 for EUR 200,000 direct to a clearer.\nThe clearer rejects it.\nThe corporate's payment never enters the chain.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Corporate instructs with an MT103, not an MT101\n→ The clearer expects an instruction from a bank, not a corporate\n→ The message is rejected at the first hop\n→ The supplier waits while the corporate argues it has a BIC\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A BIC of our own means we can send an MT103.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA corporation connected to SWIFT instructs its bank with an MT101, and it is the bank that sends the MT103, or the MT103 plus an MT202 COV, into the interbank chain. Even a corporate with its own BIC and a direct relationship with a clearer does not send an MT103 itself.\n\nCorporates instruct, banks pay.\n\nA BIC ≠ A banking licence to pay.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute every corporate instruction through the bank, as an MT101, and let the bank issue the MT103.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Corporate instruction (MT101)\n→ Bank payment into the chain (MT103, plus MT202 COV where needed)\n→ Clearer relationship held by the bank\n→ Payment actually entering the chain\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the corporate have a BIC?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the instruction reached the chain as a bank-issued MT103?\"\n\nHave you seen a corporate payment rejected because it skipped its own bank?", "takeaway": "The rule is simple: corporates instruct, banks pay.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Corporate sends MT103", "note": "EUR 200,000 sent direct to a clearer"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearer rejects it", "note": "The clearer expects a bank, not a corporate"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment never enters the chain", "note": "The supplier waits while the corporate argues"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Instruct or pay?", "note": "A corporate instructs with MT101, the bank pays with MT103"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resent through the bank", "note": "The bank issues the MT103 and the chain accepts it"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C077", "seq": 61, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 500,000 nostro debited twice, because two banks read one account", "explain": "A bank holds USD 500,000 in a nostro account with another bank.\nBoth banks debit the same account for the same payment.\nThe account goes short, and neither bank can say why.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank A holds a USD nostro with Bank B\n→ Bank B calls the same account a vostro\n→ A payment is debited from the nostro side\n→ The same payment is debited again from the vostro side\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Nostro and vostro are two different accounts.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a bank holds an account with another bank in a foreign currency, it calls that account a nostro account, meaning our account with you. The bank that holds the account calls the very same account a vostro account, meaning your account with us. There is only one account, and the two words simply say which side of it each bank is standing on.\n\nOne account, two names.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the single account from both sides, and never debit it twice for one payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Nostro view: our account with you\n→ Vostro view: your account with us\n→ Single balance both sides must agree on\n→ One debit per payment, on the one account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we debit the nostro?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both sides agree this is one account with one debit?\"\n\nHave you seen one account debited twice because two names made it look like two?", "takeaway": "There is only one account, and the two words simply say which side of it each bank is standing on.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Nostro and vostro: one account", "parts": [{"label": "Bank A holds a nostro", "note": "USD 500,000 with Bank B, our account with you"}, {"label": "Bank B calls it vostro", "note": "The same account, your account with us"}, {"label": "Payment debited twice", "note": "Both sides debit the one account"}, {"label": "Account goes short", "note": "Neither bank can explain the shortfall"}, {"label": "One account, two names", "note": "The words say which side each bank stands on"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C080", "seq": 62, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 70,000 payment read backwards, because nobody knew whose copy it was", "explain": "An operations team investigates a EUR 70,000 payment.\nThe stored message says it is an output message.\nThe team concludes the bank received it.\nThe bank had actually sent it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 stored in the archive with block 2\n→ Team reads block 2 as an output message\n→ They conclude the payment came into the bank\n→ The real direction was the opposite: the bank sent it out\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Block 2 means the same thing on every copy.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBlock 2 of a SWIFT message, the application header, says whether the message is entering or leaving the SWIFT network. The same payment looks different from the two sides: from the sending bank it is an input message, and from the receiving bank it is an output message.\n\nInput or output depends on your side.\n\nAn operator reading a stored message must therefore know which bank's copy it is looking at before deciding what block 2 means.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nIdentify whose copy you hold before reading block 2.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bank whose copy is in hand\n→ Direction from that bank's side\n→ Block 2 read against that side\n→ Conclusion about where the payment went\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What does block 2 say?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we know whose copy this is before we decide the direction?\"\n\nHave you seen an investigation go backwards because the wrong copy was read?", "takeaway": "An operator reading a stored message must therefore know which bank's copy it is looking at before deciding what block 2 means.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Block 2: input or output", "parts": [{"label": "Stored message read", "note": "EUR 70,000 MT103 pulled from the archive"}, {"label": "Block 2 says output", "note": "The team reads it as a message received"}, {"label": "Conclusion: bank received it", "note": "The investigation assumes an incoming payment"}, {"label": "Bank actually sent it", "note": "The copy belonged to the sending side"}, {"label": "Know whose copy", "note": "Direction depends on which bank's copy you hold"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C083", "seq": 63, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 150,000 for two suppliers, squeezed into one MT103", "explain": "A corporate must pay two suppliers EUR 150,000 in total.\nIt sends one MT103 naming both.\nThe second supplier is never paid, and the first is paid the full amount.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ One MT103 raised for a seller and a manufacturer\n→ Field 59 carries a single beneficiary\n→ The second beneficiary has nowhere to go in the message\n→ The full amount is credited to the first supplier only\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One message can pay two people.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 carries a single beneficiary in field 59, so one message pays one beneficiary at one bank. A payment that must reach a seller and a manufacturer at different banks needs two separate MT103 messages, one for each beneficiary.\n\nOne MT103, one beneficiary.\n\nTrying to name two beneficiaries in a single MT103 is not possible, because the message has nowhere to put the second one.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSplit every multi-beneficiary payment into one MT103 per beneficiary before sending.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Beneficiary one, with its own MT103\n→ Beneficiary two, with its own MT103\n→ Amount split per beneficiary\n→ Credit confirmed per beneficiary\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the money?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every beneficiary got their own MT103 for their own amount?\"\n\nHave you seen a supplier unpaid because two payees were squeezed into one message?", "takeaway": "Trying to name two beneficiaries in a single MT103 is not possible, because the message has nowhere to put the second one.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One MT103, one beneficiary", "parts": [{"label": "One MT103 for two suppliers", "note": "EUR 150,000 raised for a seller and a manufacturer"}, {"label": "Field 59 holds one name", "note": "A single beneficiary fits in the message"}, {"label": "Second supplier nowhere", "note": "The message has no place for a second beneficiary"}, {"label": "First supplier paid in full", "note": "The whole amount goes to one payee"}, {"label": "Split the payment", "note": "Two beneficiaries need two MT103 messages"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C085", "seq": 64, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 55,000 valued a day late, because field 32A was read as the send date", "explain": "A EUR 55,000 payment is sent on a Monday.\nThe receiving bank makes the funds available on Wednesday.\nThe beneficiary complains the value date was wrong, and the bank cannot explain it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent on Monday, field 32A carrying a Wednesday value date\n→ The team reads the send date as the value date\n→ The beneficiary is told the funds are available Monday\n→ The funds are actually available Wednesday, per field 32A\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The value date is the date the message was sent.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nField 32A carries three things: the value date in year-month-day order, the currency, and the interbank settlement amount. The value date is the date the funds are to be available to the receiver, not the date the message was sent. The currency in field 32A is the currency the receiver's bank actually receives, which is why it can differ from the currency the ordering customer instructed in field 33B.\n\nSend date ≠ Value date.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the value date, currency and settlement amount from field 32A, and tell the beneficiary the field 32A date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Value date in field 32A\n→ Currency the receiver actually gets\n→ Interbank settlement amount\n→ Currency instructed in field 33B, which can differ\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When did we send it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary was told the field 32A value date, not the send date?\"\n\nHave you seen a beneficiary promised funds on a date the field never said?", "takeaway": "The currency in field 32A is the currency the receiver's bank actually receives, which is why it can differ from the currency the ordering customer instructed in field 33B.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 32A in an MT103", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 sent Monday", "note": "EUR 55,000 payment leaves on a Monday"}, {"label": "Field 32A says Wednesday", "note": "The value date is Wednesday, not Monday"}, {"label": "Beneficiary told Monday", "note": "The team promises funds on the send date"}, {"label": "Funds available Wednesday", "note": "The receiver's bank releases them per field 32A"}, {"label": "Read field 32A", "note": "Value date, currency and settlement amount live there"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C091", "seq": 65, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 30,000 payment rejected because the name had one wrong character", "explain": "A EUR 30,000 payment names a beneficiary with characters from their own script.\nThe SWIFT network rejects the message outright.\nThe beneficiary waits while the name is rewritten.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 raised with the beneficiary name in local script\n→ The message enters the SWIFT network\n→ The network rejects it: the characters are outside the allowed set\n→ The payment never leaves the sending bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A name is a name, in any script.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSWIFT MT messages use a restricted character set, and characters outside it cannot be used in a message at all. That is why country-specific characters, such as Japanese or Thai script, cannot appear in an MT101 or an MT103, even when the underlying instruction is domestic.\n\nNames and addresses must be written with the characters the SWIFT set allows, or the message will not be accepted.\n\nA name in the wrong script ≠ A valid message.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidate every name and address against the SWIFT character set before the message is sent.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Name as the customer writes it\n→ Name as the SWIFT set allows it\n→ Validation before sending\n→ Rejection handled before the beneficiary waits\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we spell the name right?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every character in the message is inside the SWIFT set?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment rejected for a single character the network would not accept?", "takeaway": "Names and addresses must be written with the characters the SWIFT set allows, or the message will not be accepted.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The restricted SWIFT character set", "parts": [{"label": "Name in local script", "note": "EUR 30,000 payment names the beneficiary in local characters"}, {"label": "Message enters SWIFT", "note": "The MT103 is sent into the network"}, {"label": "Network rejects it", "note": "Characters outside the allowed set are refused"}, {"label": "Payment never leaves", "note": "The beneficiary waits while the name is rewritten"}, {"label": "Validate the set first", "note": "Only allowed characters may appear in the message"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C097", "seq": 66, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "One MT101, three transfers, and the team built three processes", "explain": "A corporate uses an MT101 to move EUR 40,000 between its own accounts.\nIt uses the same message to pay a domestic supplier EUR 25,000.\nIt uses it again to pay an overseas supplier EUR 60,000.\nThe bank built three separate processes for the same message.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT101 received for an own-account transfer\n→ MT101 received for a domestic third-party payment\n→ MT101 received for an international third-party payment\n→ Three teams each build their own handling, and one fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Different beneficiaries need different messages.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT101 can move funds between an ordering customer's own accounts, in favour of a third party domestically, and in favour of a third party internationally. All three uses are valid, because the message is a general request for transfer, not a payment for one kind of beneficiary.\n\nOne message, three uses.\n\nThe same message therefore serves a corporate moving money between its own accounts and a corporate paying a supplier abroad.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHandle the MT101 once, as a general request for transfer, whatever the beneficiary.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Own-account transfer on MT101\n→ Domestic third-party payment on MT101\n→ International third-party payment on MT101\n→ One handling process for all three\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which kind of transfer is this?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves all three uses of the MT101 run through the same tested process?\"\n\nHave you seen three processes built for one message that does three jobs?", "takeaway": "The same message therefore serves a corporate moving money between its own accounts and a corporate paying a supplier abroad.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What an MT101 can move", "parts": [{"label": "Own accounts", "note": "EUR 40,000 moved between the customer's own accounts"}, {"label": "Domestic third party", "note": "EUR 25,000 paid to a supplier at home"}, {"label": "International third party", "note": "EUR 60,000 paid to a supplier abroad"}, {"label": "Three processes built", "note": "Three teams each handle the same message"}, {"label": "One general request", "note": "An MT101 is a general request for transfer"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-009", "seq": 68, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 300,000 of advices, and the day-end statement disagreed", "explain": "A corporate receives MT900 and MT910 advices all day for EUR 300,000 of movements.\nAt day end the MT950 statement arrives.\nThe statement total does not match the advices.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT900 and MT910 advices arrive as each debit and credit lands\n→ The owner tracks every movement as it happens\n→ The MT950 gathers all the movements into one statement\n→ One movement is on the advices but missing from the statement\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The statement is always right, so the advices can be ignored.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThrough the day a bank sends MT900 and MT910 advices as each debit and credit lands on the account, so the owner hears about every movement as it happens. At day end the MT950 arrives and gathers all those movements into a single statement. The two must reconcile, since the advices and the statement describe the same day from different angles.\n\nAdvices ≠ Statement, until they reconcile.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the advices against the MT950 every day, and chase every difference.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT900 debit advices through the day\n→ MT910 credit advices through the day\n→ MT950 statement at day end\n→ Reconciliation of the two views\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the statement arrive?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every advice is on the statement, and every difference was chased?\"\n\nHave you seen a day-end statement disagree with the advices that described the same day?", "takeaway": "The two must reconcile, since the advices and the statement describe the same day from different angles.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Advices go out through the day", "note": "MT900 and MT910 report each debit and credit as it lands"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Owner sees each movement", "note": "EUR 300,000 of movements tracked as they happen"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT950 arrives at day end", "note": "The statement gathers all the day movements into one document"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Advices and statement agree?", "note": "Both describe the same day from two angles, so they must reconcile"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Difference chased", "note": "One movement on the advices is missing from the statement"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT900", "MT910", "MT950"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-010", "seq": 69, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 95,000 'confirmed' by instruction, but the credit never landed", "explain": "A corporate is told EUR 95,000 is on its way.\nIt treats the funds as received and releases goods.\nThe credit never lands, because the instruction was never a posting.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment instruction sent toward the account\n→ The corporate treats the instruction as proof of receipt\n→ No MT910 arrives, because no credit was ever posted\n→ The goods are gone and the money never arrived\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An instruction to pay is proof the money arrived.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe MT910 goes from the account servicing bank to the account owner as confirmation that a credit has been posted. It is triggered by the credit entry itself, so it follows real posting rather than a mere instruction to pay. On receipt, the owner can treat the funds as received, with certainty that the account was credited.\n\nAn instruction ≠ A posting.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat funds as received only on the MT910, never on the instruction.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction to pay, sent\n→ Credit entry posted on the account\n→ MT910 confirmation of the posting\n→ Goods or funds released on receipt\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the payment instructed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the credit was actually posted before anything was released on it?\"\n\nHave you seen goods released on an instruction that never became a credit?", "takeaway": "On receipt, the owner can treat the funds as received, with certainty that the account was credited.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT910 confirmation of credit", "parts": [{"label": "Instruction sent", "note": "EUR 95,000 payment instruction heads toward the account"}, {"label": "Treated as received", "note": "The corporate releases goods on the instruction"}, {"label": "No MT910 arrives", "note": "No credit was ever posted, so no confirmation comes"}, {"label": "Money never lands", "note": "The goods are gone and the account shows nothing"}, {"label": "MT910 is the proof", "note": "It follows a real posting, not an instruction"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": ["MT910"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch70-accounts-and-balances.html", "label": "Chapter 70: Accounts and balances", "chapter": "ch70", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-001", "seq": 70, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 250,000 in the mirror, and zero in the real nostro", "explain": "A bank needs USD 250,000 from its nostro account.\nIts own books, the mirror nostro, show USD 250,000 available.\nThe real account at the other bank shows zero.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank A opens a USD account with Bank B, its nostro\n→ Bank A keeps a mirror nostro in its own books\n→ The mirror shows USD 250,000 expected\n→ The real account at Bank B was never funded\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The mirror holds the money.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen Bank A opens an account with Bank B, that account is Bank A's nostro, held in Bank B's books, and the real funds sit there. Bank A also keeps a mirror nostro in its own books, but the mirror holds no funds; it only reflects activity on the real account. The mirror is kept in the real account's currency, so a USD nostro carries a USD mirror, and the mirror lets a bank work out what the real account should show.\n\nMirror ≠ Money.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFund and check the real nostro, and use the mirror only to work out what it should show.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Real nostro at the other bank, where funds sit\n→ Mirror nostro in our own books, holding no funds\n→ Currency matched on both sides\n→ Real balance checked before any payment is promised\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What does the mirror show?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the real account at the other bank holds the funds?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment promised on a mirror balance that the real account never had?", "takeaway": "The mirror is kept in the real account's currency, so a USD nostro carries a USD mirror, and the mirror lets a bank work out what the real account should show.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Nostro and its mirror", "parts": [{"label": "Real nostro at Bank B", "note": "Bank A's USD account sits in Bank B's books, where funds are held"}, {"label": "Mirror nostro at Bank A", "note": "Bank A keeps a mirror in its own books; it holds no funds"}, {"label": "Mirror shows USD 250,000", "note": "The mirror says the money should be there"}, {"label": "Real account shows zero", "note": "The real account was never funded"}, {"label": "Mirror is not money", "note": "It only reflects what the real account should show"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-004", "seq": 73, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 8,000 sent 'on SWIFT' inside one country, and it never arrived", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 8,000 to another account in the same country.\nThe sending bank routes it on SWIFT.\nThe payment never arrives, because the domestic system never sees it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Domestic payment raised inside one country\n→ The team sends it through the SWIFT network\n→ The domestic clearing system never receives it\n→ The beneficiary waits while the payment sits in the wrong network\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SWIFT carries every payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment inside one country runs through that country's own clearing and settlement systems, in local formats built on flat or XML files, and never touches the SWIFT network. SWIFT was created for cross-border payments, and the large majority of those still travel on it.\n\nLocal networks carry local payments and SWIFT carries payments that cross a border; the two worlds stay separate.\n\nA domestic payment on SWIFT ≠ A payment on its way.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute domestic payments through the domestic system, and SWIFT only across a border.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payment inside one country, local clearing\n→ Payment across a border, SWIFT\n→ Local format used for the domestic leg\n→ Beneficiary reached on the right network\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment travelled on the network built for its journey?\"\n\nHave you seen a domestic payment lost in a network it never belonged on?", "takeaway": "Local networks carry local payments and SWIFT carries payments that cross a border; the two worlds stay separate.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Domestic and cross-border rails", "parts": [{"label": "Domestic payment raised", "note": "EUR 8,000 sent inside one country"}, {"label": "Routed on SWIFT", "note": "The team sends it through the cross-border network"}, {"label": "Domestic system never sees it", "note": "Local clearing receives nothing"}, {"label": "Beneficiary waits", "note": "The payment sits in the wrong network"}, {"label": "Two worlds stay separate", "note": "Local networks for local, SWIFT for cross-border"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-005", "seq": 74, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 100,000 of currency bought at the wrong price, because bid and ask were read backwards", "explain": "A customer sells EUR 100,000 of foreign currency to the bank.\nThey expect the higher price they saw quoted.\nThey receive the lower one, and the bank keeps the difference.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank quotes a bid price and an ask price\n→ The customer reads the ask as the price they will get\n→ The bank buys from the customer at the bid, the lower price\n→ The customer loses the spread they never knew existed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Bid and ask are the same from both sides.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBid means buy and ask means sell, and the bank sets both prices, not the customer. The bank buys currency from the customer at the bid rate and sells it to the customer at the ask rate, so the customer buys at the higher ask and sells at the lower bid, and the bank keeps the difference. A base currency exporter therefore watches the ask price, while a base currency importer watches the bid price.\n\nThe customer's buy ≠ The bank's buy.\n\nAlways read bid and ask from the bank's side.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nQuote and check every FX price from the bank's side, and show the customer which side they are on.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bid: the bank buys, the customer sells low\n→ Ask: the bank sells, the customer buys high\n→ Exporter watches the ask\n→ Importer watches the bid\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we quote a price?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer was shown the price from their side of the trade?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer lose the spread because the prices were read from the wrong side?", "takeaway": "A base currency exporter therefore watches the ask price, while a base currency importer watches the bid price.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Bid and ask, bank's side first", "parts": [{"label": "Bank quotes bid and ask", "note": "Bid means buy, ask means sell, set by the bank"}, {"label": "Bank buys at bid", "note": "The bank buys currency from the customer at the bid rate"}, {"label": "Bank sells at ask", "note": "The bank sells currency to the customer at the ask rate"}, {"label": "Customer buys high, sells low", "note": "The customer buys at the ask and sells at the bid"}, {"label": "Exporter watches ask", "note": "A base currency exporter watches the ask price"}, {"label": "Importer watches bid", "note": "A base currency importer watches the bid price"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN4-006", "seq": 75, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 50,000 quoted the wrong way round, and the deal was priced in the wrong currency", "explain": "A euro area bank quotes EUR/USD for a EUR 50,000 deal.\nThe dealer prices the deal in dollars.\nThe customer is charged in the wrong currency, and the deal has to be rebooked.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank in the euro area quotes EUR/USD\n→ The dealer treats the dollar as the price currency\n→ The deal is priced in USD instead of EUR\n→ The customer is charged in the wrong currency\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Quoted and price currency are different things.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a direct quote, the quoted currency is the domestic one, and quoted and price currency mean the same thing. For a bank in the euro area quoting EUR/USD, the euro is therefore the domestic currency and the price is expressed in euros. Keeping this the right way round matters, because the direct and indirect quotes describe the same pair from opposite sides.\n\nDirect ≠ Indirect.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPrice every direct quote in the domestic currency, and check the side before the deal is booked.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic currency of the quoting bank\n→ Quoted currency, the same as the price currency\n→ Direct quote, price in domestic currency\n→ Indirect quote, the same pair from the opposite side\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we quote the pair?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the deal was priced in the domestic currency of the quoting bank?\"\n\nHave you seen a deal rebooked because the quote was read from the wrong side?", "takeaway": "Keeping this the right way round matters, because the direct and indirect quotes describe the same pair from opposite sides.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Direct quote, price in domestic currency", "parts": [{"label": "Euro area bank quotes EUR/USD", "note": "The euro is the domestic currency for this bank"}, {"label": "Quoted is price currency", "note": "In a direct quote they mean the same thing"}, {"label": "Dealer prices in dollars", "note": "The deal is priced in the wrong currency"}, {"label": "Customer charged wrongly", "note": "The deal has to be rebooked in euros"}, {"label": "Opposite of indirect", "note": "Direct and indirect view the same pair from opposite sides"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN4-007", "seq": 76, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Dealer buys EUR/USD, expects dollars, gets euros", "explain": "A dealer buys EUR/USD for EUR 500,000.\nThe quote is 1.1635.\nHe expects USD 500,000 to land in the account.\nInstead EUR 500,000 arrives, and USD 581,750 leaves it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Order placed: buy EUR/USD 500,000\n→ EUR is the base currency, USD is the quoted currency\n→ Buying means receiving EUR and paying USD\n→ EUR 500,000 received, USD 581,750 paid at 1.1635\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Buy EUR/USD means buy dollars.\"\n\nIt does not. Every buy or sell in a currency pair refers to the base currency, the first one named.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe quote number 1.1635 only states how much quoted currency one unit of base is worth. It says nothing about which currency changes hands. The trader read the pair backwards and booked the wrong side of the deal.\n\nBuy EUR/USD means buy EUR, not USD.\n\nWhy does the pair name decide what you actually receive?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nIn EUR/USD, EUR is the base and USD is the quote, and every instruction refers to the base.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Base currency, what you buy or sell\n→ Quoted currency, what you pay or receive\n→ Quote number, the price of one unit of base\n\nShow the base currency, the amount in base, and the amount in quoted currency on every deal ticket before it is confirmed.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the rate?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the dealer knows which currency is being bought?\"\n\nHave you seen a deal booked the wrong way round because the pair was read backwards?", "takeaway": "Every buy or sell in EUR/USD refers to EUR, the base currency, never to USD.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Buy EUR/USD means buy EUR", "parts": [{"label": "EUR is the base", "note": "In EUR/USD, EUR is the base currency and USD is the quoted one."}, {"label": "Buy means receive EUR", "note": "Buying EUR/USD means receiving EUR and giving USD."}, {"label": "Sell means give EUR", "note": "Selling EUR/USD means giving EUR and receiving USD."}, {"label": "Quote 1.1635", "note": "The number states how much USD one EUR is worth."}, {"label": "EUR 500,000 dealt", "note": "EUR 500,000 received, USD 581,750 paid at 1.1635."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-004", "seq": 77, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Merchant pays per card sale, holder pays nothing per sale", "explain": "A shop takes EUR 10,000 in card sales in a week.\nThe owner expects the cardholders to carry the cost.\nInstead his own statement shows a charge on every single sale.\nThe cardholders paid nothing per transaction.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer pays EUR 100 by card\n→ Merchant is charged by the issuer and the processing chain\n→ Cardholder pays nothing for that transaction\n→ Cardholder pays only the annual fee, plus interest if the balance is unpaid\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The person who pays by card pays for the card payment.\"\n\nThe split works the other way round. The merchant pays per deal. The holder pays for holding the card.\n\nWhy?\n\nA card transaction has two different bills. The merchant funds the acceptance chain on every sale, while the holder funds the card itself through the annual fee and through interest when a statement balance is not paid in full.\n\nThe merchant pays per deal. The holder pays per year.\n\nWhy does the same EUR 100 sale cost the merchant money and the holder nothing?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWho pays what on a card deal is fixed by role, not by who taps the card.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Merchant charges, per transaction accepted\n→ Holder annual fee, for holding the card\n→ Holder interest, only when a balance is unpaid\n\nShow the merchant fee per transaction and the holder fee per year on separate lines, never as one blended card cost.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does a card payment cost?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each side sees only the cost it actually bears?\"\n\nHave you seen a merchant surprised by the charge on a card sale they thought was free?", "takeaway": "The merchant pays per card sale. The holder pays nothing per transaction, only the annual fee and interest on an unpaid balance.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who pays what on a card deal", "parts": [{"label": "Merchant pays per sale", "note": "The merchant is charged by the issuer and the processing chain."}, {"label": "Holder pays nothing per sale", "note": "The cardholder pays nothing per transaction."}, {"label": "Holder pays the annual fee", "note": "The cardholder pays the annual fee for the card."}, {"label": "Holder pays interest if unpaid", "note": "Interest applies when a statement balance is not paid in full."}, {"label": "EUR 10,000 a week", "note": "Card sales of EUR 10,000 cost the merchant on every sale."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-006", "seq": 78, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Monthly fee stays flat, processing fee follows every sale", "explain": "A merchant pays EUR 200 a month to a processor.\nIn a quiet month he still pays EUR 200.\nIn a busy month the bill jumps to EUR 1,400.\nHe complains the monthly fee has gone up. It has not.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Monthly fee charged: EUR 200, fixed every month\n→ Processing fee charged: per transaction\n→ Quiet month, few transactions, processing fee small\n→ Busy month, many transactions, processing fee large\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One fee to the processor, so one bill that moves together.\"\n\nThere are two fees, and they move on different rules. The monthly fee is fixed for using the service. The processing fee rises and falls with volume.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe processor charges for access and for use. Access is the monthly fee, set by the processing firm and the same every month. Use is the per-transaction fee, so a busy month costs more even though the monthly fee never changed.\n\nA fixed fee and a volume fee are not the same bill.\n\nWhy does the bill jump in a busy month when the monthly fee never moves?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSeparate the fee for access from the fee for use, or every busy month looks like a price rise.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Monthly fee, fixed for the processing service\n→ Processing fee, per transaction, driven by volume\n→ Total bill, the sum of the two, never one blended line\n\nShow both fees on the statement, with the transaction count beside the processing fee.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the processor fee?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the merchant can see which part of the bill is fixed and which part follows volume?\"\n\nHave you seen a fixed fee blamed for a bill that really grew with volume?", "takeaway": "The monthly fee is fixed. The processing fee is per transaction, so it rises and falls with volume.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two merchant fees, easily confused", "parts": [{"label": "Monthly fee is fixed", "note": "EUR 200 charged every month for the processing service."}, {"label": "Processor sets the level", "note": "Its level is set by the processing firm."}, {"label": "Processing fee is per transaction", "note": "The payment processing fee is charged per transaction."}, {"label": "It rises with volume", "note": "It rises and falls with the number of transactions processed."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-008", "seq": 79, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Cheque for EUR 25,000 clears, the paper never arrives", "explain": "A customer deposits a cheque for EUR 25,000.\nThe paper cheque is lost in the post on its way to the paying bank.\nThe money still clears.\nThe branch had expected the paper to be essential. It was not.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Beneficiary bank receives the cheque for EUR 25,000\n→ Beneficiary bank presents an image of the cheque to the drawee bank\n→ Drawee bank collects the funds from the image\n→ No physical paper ever travels between the banks\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A cheque must travel as paper to be collected.\"\n\nIn many countries it no longer has to. The beneficiary bank presents only an image, and clearing runs on the image.\n\nWhy?\n\nCheque clearing moved from moving paper to moving images. Once the image is accepted as the presentment, the physical journey adds nothing, and losing the paper no longer stops the collection.\n\nThe image is the presentment, not the paper.\n\nWhy does a lost paper cheque still clear when the image arrives safely?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClearing follows the presentment the scheme accepts, and in many countries that presentment is an image.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Image captured, the presentment itself\n→ Paper handling, storage and transport of the original\n→ Funds collected, proved by the clearing response\n\nRecord the image presentment and the clearing result, not the paper journey, as the proof the cheque cleared.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Where is the paper cheque?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cheque was presented and the funds were collected?\"\n\nHave you seen a cheque clear from an image while the paper was still missing?", "takeaway": "Cheque clearing now runs on images, not on the movement of paper.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cheque deposited for EUR 25,000", "note": "The beneficiary bank receives the cheque."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Image presented, not paper", "note": "Only an image is presented to the drawee bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Image accepted as presentment?", "note": "If yes, clearing proceeds without the paper."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds collected from the image", "note": "The drawee bank pays on the image presentment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing runs on images", "note": "No physical presentation is needed in those countries."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-102", "seq": 80, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "File accepted, EUR 120,000 supplier still unpaid", "explain": "A company sends a supplier file for EUR 120,000.\nThe bank replies: file accepted.\nFinance tells the supplier the money is on its way.\nThree days later the supplier says nothing arrived.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 file sent, EUR 120,000 to suppliers\n→ File acknowledgement received, receipt only\n→ Validation passes, but clearing rejects two suppliers\n→ Settlement never happens for those two, and no one notices\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the file is accepted, every supplier is paid.\"\n\nAcceptance is only receipt. A payment is seven stages, and the file acknowledgement covers the first one.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach stage has its own owner, status and failure mode: initiation, validation, processing, routing, clearing, settlement and confirmation. A rejection at clearing stays hidden when the team reads acceptance as settlement, so the failure only surfaces when the supplier complains.\n\nFile accepted is not money paid.\n\nWhy does a clean acknowledgement hide two unpaid suppliers for three days?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDiagnose by stage and evidence, never by the first status that looks good.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ File receipt, the acknowledgement only\n→ Clearing result, accepted or rejected per supplier\n→ Settlement and confirmation, proved per payment\n\nShow the status of every supplier at every stage, and alert on any clearing rejection the same day.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the file accepted?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each supplier was settled and confirmed?\"\n\nHave you seen a file acknowledgement treated as proof of payment?", "takeaway": "File accepted means receipt only. A payment is seven stages, and mistaking acceptance for settlement hides failures.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the instruction", "note": "Capture the instruction under a unique reference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the instruction", "note": "Check schema, fields, limits, account state and screening."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Process and enrich", "note": "Enrich the instruction and reserve funds where required."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Select the route", "note": "Choose the clearing path and intermediary chain."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Clearing accepted per supplier?", "note": "Send the interbank message and await acceptance or rejection."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settle the funds", "note": "Move funds between accounts, irrevocably."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm to the customer", "note": "Update customer status on authoritative evidence only."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "INS-104", "seq": 81, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "Migration team changes the network, the messages stay broken", "explain": "A bank starts an ISO 20022 migration for a EUR 50,000 payment flow.\nThe team plans a network change.\nMonths later the messages still fail validation.\nThe network was never the problem.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment travels over SWIFT as MT messages\n→ Team scopes a network change, SWIFT to something new\n→ Message content stays in the old MT format\n→ Validation still fails, because the content never changed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SWIFT and ISO 20022 are the same thing, so changing one fixes the other.\"\n\nThey are different things. SWIFT is the network and service carrying messages between banks. ISO 20022 is the standard defining what those messages contain.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe same payment can travel over SWIFT as older MT or newer MX messages, and changing the standard changes nothing about the network. The migration is about message content, mapping and validation, not about a network change, so a network plan leaves the real work untouched.\n\nCarrier is not content.\n\nWhy does a network change leave the same messages failing validation?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nScope the migration to what actually changes: content, mapping and validation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Network, who carries the message\n→ Standard, what the message contains\n→ Mapping and validation, how old content becomes new\n\nWrite the scope as message content first, and name the network only where it truly changes.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which network do we move to?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each message maps and validates under the new standard?\"\n\nHave you seen a migration scoped as a network change when the messages were the real work?", "takeaway": "SWIFT is the network. ISO 20022 is the standard. Changing one changes nothing about the other.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Carrier and content, kept apart", "parts": [{"label": "SWIFT: the network", "note": "The service carrying messages between banks."}, {"label": "ISO 20022: the standard", "note": "The standard defining message content."}, {"label": "MT family", "note": "Older message family, still in use over SWIFT."}, {"label": "MX family", "note": "ISO 20022 message family, richer content."}, {"label": "EUR 50,000 flow", "note": "The same payment can travel as MT or MX over SWIFT."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-154", "seq": 82, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "First collection for EUR 850 rejected, mandate was never set up", "explain": "A creditor sends its first collection for EUR 850 under a new mandate.\nThe file is built, checked and sent.\nThe scheme rejects it the same day.\nThe mandate was never set up.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Mandate signed by the debtor\n→ Collection file built for EUR 850\n→ Mandate setup check skipped, file sent first\n→ Scheme rejects the first collection, no mandate on record\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Build the file, then check the mandate.\"\n\nThat order is backwards. A collection cannot run before its mandate is set up and its authorisation evidence is in place.\n\nWhy?\n\nCreditor and debtor roles must be unambiguous, mandate data complete, and the same mandate reference carried through every downstream system the scheme will see. A first collection on an incomplete mandate is rejected, so the setup check belongs before the file is built, not after the rejection lands.\n\nNo mandate, no collection.\n\nWhy does a signed mandate still fail when the setup check runs after the file is built?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the setup check before the file, and carry one mandate reference through every system.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate setup, complete and evidenced\n→ Mandate reference, the same in every system\n→ First collection, only after setup is proved\n\nBlock file build until mandate setup passes, and show the mandate reference on the collection.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the file sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the mandate is set up before the first collection?\"\n\nHave you seen a first collection rejected because the mandate setup was checked too late?", "takeaway": "A first collection on an incomplete mandate is rejected, so put the setup check before the file is built.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the mandate record", "note": "Take the full mandate record."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the authorisation evidence", "note": "Evidence that the debtor agreed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Mandate setup complete?", "note": "Prove setup before building the file."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Carry the reference downstream", "note": "One reference in every system."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send the first collection", "note": "First collection for EUR 850, only after setup passes."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-156", "seq": 83, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "Same debtor, same EUR 850, wrong sequence, collection fails", "explain": "A creditor collects EUR 850 from the same debtor every month.\nThis month the collection is marked recurring.\nThe debtor bank treats it under the wrong rules and it fails.\nIt was the first collection under a new mandate.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ New mandate signed, first collection due for EUR 850\n→ Collection marked recurring, copied from last month\n→ Debtor bank applies recurring rules to a first collection\n→ Collection fails under the wrong sequence\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Same amount, same debtor, so the same sequence.\"\n\nFirst and recurring collections are different instructions, even with the same amount and the same debtor.\n\nWhy?\n\nSequence and mandate state decide which scheme data the collection carries and how the debtor bank treats it. Marking from a guess, or copying last month, processes the collection under the wrong rules.\n\nFirst or recurring is decided by the mandate, never by a guess.\n\nWhy does copying last month's sequence break a first collection?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMark each collection first or recurring from the mandate state, and never copy the sequence from the previous run.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate state, what decides the sequence\n→ Sequence flag, first or recurring per collection\n→ Scheme data, carried per the sequence\n\nDerive the sequence from the mandate state in the system, and show it on the collection before it is sent.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What was the sequence last month?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this collection carries the right sequence for this mandate?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection fail because first and recurring were mixed up?", "takeaway": "Mark each collection first or recurring from the mandate state, never from a guess.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Sequence comes from the mandate", "parts": [{"label": "First debit", "note": "First debit under the mandate, EUR 850."}, {"label": "Later debits", "note": "Subsequent debits under the same mandate."}, {"label": "Mandate state decides", "note": "The state that fixes the sequence."}, {"label": "Data per sequence", "note": "Different scheme data per sequence."}, {"label": "Wrong sequence fails", "note": "The wrong sequence processes the collection under the wrong rules."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "SCN-15", "seq": 84, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 40,000 payment stopped, but which stop is it?", "explain": "A customer asks to stop a EUR 40,000 payment.\nOperations logs it as a return.\nThe payment had not settled yet, so there was nothing to return.\nThe stop fails, and the money still moves.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer asks to stop the EUR 40,000 payment\n→ Team treats it as a return, pacs.004\n→ Payment is still pre-settlement, nothing to return\n→ Correct path was a cancellation, camt.056, answered by camt.029\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Reject, return and cancel are three words for the same stop.\"\n\nThey are three mechanisms separated by one boundary, settlement.\n\nWhy?\n\nA reject is a pre-settlement refusal reported by pacs.002 with an RJCT status, and no money moves. A return is a post-settlement reversal moving funds back through pacs.004. A cancellation is a request to stop a queued or pending payment, sent by camt.055 or camt.056 and answered by camt.029. Picking the wrong one sends the wrong message at the wrong time.\n\nSettlement decides which stop applies.\n\nWhy does logging a pre-settlement stop as a return let the money move anyway?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the mechanism by the settlement boundary, and send the message that matches it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reject, pre-settlement, pacs.002 RJCT, no money moved\n→ Return, post-settlement, pacs.004 moves funds back\n→ Cancellation, queued payment, camt.056 answered by camt.029\n\nShow the settlement state on every stop request before the team picks a mechanism.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we stop the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we used the right mechanism for this settlement state?\"\n\nHave you seen a stop logged as the wrong mechanism?", "takeaway": "Reject, return and cancellation are separated by one boundary: settlement.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One boundary, three paths", "parts": [{"label": "Rejection", "note": "Pre-settlement refusal on pacs.002 RJCT, no money moved."}, {"label": "Return of funds", "note": "Post-settlement reversal, pacs.004 returns funds."}, {"label": "Cancellation request", "note": "Stop a queued payment, camt.055 or camt.056."}, {"label": "Settlement decides", "note": "The boundary that picks the right mechanism."}, {"label": "EUR 40,000 stopped wrong", "note": "Logged as a return before settlement, so the stop failed."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004", "camt.055", "camt.056", "camt.029"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "SCN-17", "seq": 85, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "pacs.002 says rejected, EUR 40,000 still sits in the account", "explain": "A EUR 40,000 payment is rejected.\nThe team waits for the money to come back.\nNothing comes back, because nothing ever left.\nThey were waiting on a status report, not a payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent for EUR 40,000\n→ pacs.002 received with RJCT status\n→ Team waits for a pacs.004 return\n→ No return arrives, because settlement never happened\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A rejection means money must be returned.\"\n\nA pacs.002 is a status report saying accepted, rejected or pending, and it moves nothing. A pacs.004 is a payment return, an actual settlement instruction in reverse that can carry charges.\n\nWhy?\n\nSettlement is the boundary. Before it, pacs.002. After it, pacs.004. Both draw on the same reference family, so systems can link the messages together, but only one of them moves money.\n\nA report is not a refund.\n\nWhy does the team wait for a return after a pre-settlement rejection?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead pacs.002 as a status and pacs.004 as money, and never wait for one when the other arrived.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.002 status, accepted, rejected or pending, moves nothing\n→ pacs.004 return, money moving back after settlement\n→ References, UETR, MsgId and EndToEndId linking them\n\nOn an RJCT, show that no funds moved and close the case, instead of opening a return wait.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the return arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether money moved at all?\"\n\nHave you seen a team wait for a refund that a status report could never bring?", "takeaway": "A pacs.002 reports a status and moves nothing. A pacs.004 moves money back.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Report or money movement", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.002: status only", "note": "Accepted, rejected or pending, moves nothing."}, {"label": "pacs.004: money back", "note": "Settlement instruction in reverse, can carry charges."}, {"label": "The settlement boundary", "note": "Before settlement pacs.002, after it pacs.004."}, {"label": "References they share", "note": "UETR, MsgId and EndToEndId link them."}, {"label": "EUR 40,000 never left", "note": "Rejected before settlement, so nothing to return."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "SCN-20", "seq": 86, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 60,000 cross-border payment, six messages, one missing link", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 60,000 abroad.\nThe bank can see the payment left.\nIt cannot say where it is now.\nThe team is reading one message, not the chain.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 opens it from the customer\n→ pain.002 answers acceptance or rejection\n→ pacs.008 carries the interbank leg\n→ pacs.002 reports status along the way\n→ camt.052, camt.053 and camt.054 close the loop for reconciliation\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One payment, one message, one status.\"\n\nA cross-border credit transfer is a chain of messages, not a single payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nEvery step can branch into an exception with its own message. pacs.004 returns funds after settlement, and camt.056 with camt.029 handles cancellation requests. pacs.009 covers the funds where the cover method is used. Reading only one message leaves the rest of the chain invisible.\n\nThe message set is the lifecycle.\n\nWhy does the payment vanish from view after it leaves the bank?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the payment through the whole message chain, and name the exception message at every branch.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiation, pain.001 and pain.002\n→ Interbank leg, pacs.008 and pacs.009 cover\n→ Status and exceptions, pacs.002, pacs.004, camt.056 and camt.029\n→ Reconciliation, camt.052, camt.053 and camt.054\n\nShow the full chain on one screen, with the latest message at each step.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment leave?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where the payment is at each step of its chain?\"\n\nHave you traced a payment that vanished between two messages?", "takeaway": "Every step can branch into an exception with its own message, so the message set is the lifecycle.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pain.001: initiation", "note": "Customer initiation arrives for EUR 60,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pain.002: the bank answers", "note": "Bank answers acceptance or rejection per transaction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008: interbank transfer", "note": "Interbank customer credit transfer between banks."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Exception at this step?", "note": "pacs.004 returns funds, camt.056 and camt.029 handle cancellation."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.002: status reports", "note": "ACTC, ACSP, ACSC and RJCT mark the journey."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt: statements close the loop", "note": "Intraday, end-of-day and debit-credit notices close the loop."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": ["pain.001", "pain.002", "pacs.008", "pacs.002", "pacs.009", "camt.052", "camt.053", "camt.054", "pacs.004", "camt.056", "camt.029"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F12", "seq": 89, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "Mandate signed, EUR 1,200 collections stop a year later", "explain": "A creditor collects EUR 1,200 a month by direct debit.\nA year later the collections start failing.\nThe mandate was signed, filed, and then forgotten.\nNobody owned it after creation.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Mandate created, debtor authorises the collections\n→ Collections run, month after month\n→ Mandate details change, nobody updates them\n→ A broken lifecycle blocks the next collection\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Obtaining a mandate is the job. The rest is administration.\"\n\nObtaining a mandate is only the beginning. The creditor owns managing it throughout the customer relationship, through creation, management, amendment, usage, cancellation and expiry.\n\nWhy?\n\nCollections must be based on a valid mandate, and unlike a credit transfer, a direct debit is initiated by the creditor. A mandate that is never maintained goes stale, and a stale mandate blocks future collections without warning.\n\nA mandate is a lifecycle, not a signature.\n\nWhy do collections fail a year after a perfectly good mandate was signed?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nManage the mandate for its whole life, and treat a change as a payments event, not paperwork.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate creation, authorisation captured\n→ Mandate management, details kept current\n→ Amendment, usage, cancellation and expiry, each tracked\n\nShow the mandate state on every collection, and alert before expiry or after an unrecorded change.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was a mandate signed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the mandate is still valid for this collection?\"\n\nHave you seen collections stop because a mandate was filed and forgotten?", "takeaway": "Mandate management is part of the payments journey, not only administration.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Mandate lifecycle", "parts": [{"label": "Creation", "note": "The debtor gives authorisation."}, {"label": "Management", "note": "Mandate information is properly maintained."}, {"label": "Amendment", "note": "Relevant changes are managed."}, {"label": "Usage", "note": "Collections are based on a valid mandate."}, {"label": "Cancellation and expiry", "note": "A mandate can be cancelled or become invalid."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-F10", "seq": 90, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 300 a month, no mandate, no direct debit", "explain": "A business wants to collect EUR 300 a month from a customer by direct debit.\nIt sends the first collection straight away.\nThe bank rejects it.\nThere was no mandate, and a direct debit without one does not exist.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Business plans recurring collections of EUR 300\n→ No mandate signed by the debtor\n→ First collection sent anyway\n→ Collection rejected, no authorisation behind it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The collection is the product. The mandate is paperwork.\"\n\nEvery direct debit journey starts with a signature on a mandate. From there the creditor can initiate recurring collections under the agreed arrangement.\n\nWhy?\n\nA direct debit is initiated by the creditor, not the debtor, so the mandate is the only proof the creditor may collect at all. Without it there is no journey, only a rejected instruction, however convenient the collection would have been.\n\nNo mandate, no direct debit.\n\nWhy does a simple monthly collection fail before it even starts?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart every direct debit with the mandate, and let the mandate carry the journey.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate, the debtor signature and agreement\n→ Collection, initiated by the creditor under the mandate\n→ Rejection, return and refund, each handled on its own rules\n\nBlock any collection with no valid mandate behind it, and show the mandate on the collection record.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we collect this month?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the debtor authorised this creditor to collect?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection sent before its mandate existed?", "takeaway": "The journey runs from mandate through collection, rejection, return and refund.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Why the mandate matters", "parts": [{"label": "Mandate first", "note": "Every journey starts with a signature."}, {"label": "Creditor initiates", "note": "The creditor initiates recurring collections."}, {"label": "Customer convenience", "note": "Recurring payments without repeated action."}, {"label": "Business predictability", "note": "Collections follow the agreed arrangement."}, {"label": "Lower merchant cost", "note": "Cheaper collection than other payment methods."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch62-sepa-direct-debit-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 62: SEPA Direct Debit Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch62", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-D03", "seq": 91, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2,500 collected Monday, debited Tuesday, customer complains", "explain": "A creditor collects EUR 2,500 by direct debit.\nThe creditor is funded on Monday, the due date.\nThe debtor sees the debit on Tuesday.\nThe debtor complains the money was taken late. It was not.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Due Date, collection falls due, debtor PSP checks funds\n→ Settlement Date, interbank settlement funds the creditor PSP through the CSM\n→ Debit Date, the debtor account is actually debited\n→ Across countries, the debit can slip to the next Business Day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One collection, one date, one event.\"\n\nEach date marks a different event.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen both PSPs sit in the same country the three normally coincide, but across countries the debit can slip to the next Business Day, so the creditor PSP can be funded on the Due Date while the debtor account is debited a day later. The split is a calendar and processing-window effect, not an error.\n\nThree dates, three events.\n\nWhy is the creditor paid on Monday while the debtor is debited on Tuesday?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead each date against its own event, and never judge the debit by the settlement date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Due Date, when the collection falls due\n→ Settlement Date, when the creditor PSP is funded\n→ Debit Date, when the debtor account is actually debited\n\nShow all three dates on the collection, each labelled with its own event.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When was the collection paid?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which date belongs to which event?\"\n\nHave you explained a one-day gap between funding and debit to a customer?", "takeaway": "Three dates, three events. The split is a calendar effect, not an error.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three dates, three separate events", "parts": [{"label": "Due Date", "note": "Collection falls due, debtor PSP checks the account for funds."}, {"label": "Settlement Date", "note": "Interbank settlement through the CSM, funding the creditor PSP."}, {"label": "Debit Date", "note": "Debtor account is actually debited, sometimes the next Business Day."}, {"label": "Same country", "note": "All three dates normally coincide when both PSPs share one country."}, {"label": "Different countries", "note": "Separate calendars and processing windows can push the debit a day later."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D08", "seq": 93, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "INR 50,000 from India to Singapore, instant at last", "explain": "A worker in India sends INR 50,000 to family in Singapore.\nIn the old world it would take days and cost a visible fee.\nThis time it arrives instantly.\nTwo instant systems talked to each other.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer sends INR 50,000 on UPI in India\n→ UPI links with PayNow in Singapore\n→ Banks verify, review and authorise the transaction\n→ Beneficiary is credited instantly in Singapore\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Instant systems only work inside their own country.\"\n\nIndia UPI moves money in real time, and its link with Singapore PayNow shows two such systems working together so an India to Singapore payment arrives instantly.\n\nWhy?\n\nUPI joins wallets, banks and small businesses in a point-to-point architecture, with banks verifying, reviewing and authorising its transactions. Linked systems give users faster and more transparent payments, and prove that instant systems can interoperate instead of operating in isolation.\n\nInstant at home can be instant abroad, once systems link.\n\nWhy did this cross-border payment arrive in seconds when others take days?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for linked systems, not for isolated ones, and keep bank verification at every step.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UPI leg, India real time\n→ PayNow leg, Singapore real time\n→ Bank verification, verify, review and authorise\n→ Customer promise, instant and transparent end to end\n\nShow both legs and the link between them on the payment status, not just the sending leg.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment leave India?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves both systems completed their leg?\"\n\nHave you sent money abroad and watched it arrive instantly?", "takeaway": "Linked systems give faster and more transparent payments. Instant systems can interoperate.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two instant systems, one payment", "parts": [{"label": "UPI, India real time", "note": "Real-time payment system for instant money transfer."}, {"label": "PayNow, Singapore", "note": "Singapore real-time payment system."}, {"label": "Integration", "note": "The two systems work together so payments arrive instantly."}, {"label": "Verified by banks", "note": "Transactions are verified, reviewed and authorised by banks."}, {"label": "INR 50,000 instant", "note": "An India to Singapore payment arrives instantly."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D14", "seq": 95, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2,000 at midnight on Sunday, no bank open, payment still instant", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 2,000 at midnight on a Sunday.\nNo branch is open. No banker is at a desk.\nThe payment still arrives instantly.\nThe phone is only the front door.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer taps pay at midnight, Sunday\n→ Fast Payment System processes around the clock, 24x7x365\n→ No 9 AM to 3 PM window, no banking-hours wait\n→ Payment completes while every branch is closed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Payments happen when banks are open.\"\n\nA Fast Payment System lets customers pay instantly, at any time and from anywhere, replacing banking hours with round-the-clock processing.\n\nWhy?\n\nBehind the mobile phone sits a payment ecosystem of many interacting parts, and Fast Payment Systems now run in many countries. In India, customers pay through IMPS, UPI, NEFT, mobile banking and wallets, while UPI launched in 2016 and SEPA Instant followed in 2017. The phone starts the payment, the ecosystem finishes it.\n\nThe phone is only the front door.\n\nWhy does a midnight payment complete when no bank is open?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild for 24x7x365, and never mistake the channel for the system behind it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channel, the phone the customer sees\n→ Ecosystem, the many parts behind it\n→ Availability, instant at any time, anywhere\n\nShow the system status behind the channel, so a midnight failure is traced to the right part.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the app working?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every part behind the phone is running around the clock?\"\n\nHave you paid at midnight and wondered what was actually open?", "takeaway": "Behind the mobile phone sits a payment ecosystem of many interacting parts.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fast Payment System", "parts": [{"label": "Anytime, anywhere", "note": "Instant payments at any time, not only during banking hours."}, {"label": "24x7x365", "note": "Customers expect payments completed around the clock."}, {"label": "UPI, 2016", "note": "Launched in India in 2016, now accepted in several countries."}, {"label": "SEPA Instant, 2017", "note": "Launched in the EU in 2017, now across European countries."}, {"label": "An ecosystem behind it", "note": "Many interacting parts run behind the mobile phone."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch74-global-instant-systems.html", "label": "Chapter 74: Global instant systems", "chapter": "ch74", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E10", "seq": 99, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 100,000 sent to T2, Bank B waits for a payment that is already settled", "explain": "Bank A sends EUR 100,000 to Bank B.\nBank B waits for settlement before crediting the beneficiary.\nSettlement already happened.\nBank B is waiting on a model that no longer applies.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank A sends the full pacs.008 to T2\n→ T2 validates and settles it\n→ T2 forwards the settled payment to Bank B\n→ Bank B treats it as settled, because settlement already happened inside T2\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every payment is held until settlement, then released.\"\n\nThat is the Y-Copy model. In Y-Copy, Bank A sends the payment instruction and a copy goes to the settlement mechanism, which holds the payment until settlement and authorisation complete and only then releases it to Bank B.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn the T2 V-Shape model, Bank A sends the full payment to T2, which validates and settles it and then forwards the settled payment to Bank B. The distinction is not the message format but the architecture: who receives the payment for settlement, whether a copy or the full payment is sent, when settlement happens, and when the payment is released.\n\nCopy held, or full payment settled first.\n\nWhy does Bank B wait for settlement that already happened inside T2?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the architecture before you name the message, and let the architecture decide who waits for what.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Y-Copy, copy held until settlement, then released\n→ V-Shape, full pacs.008 settled inside T2, then forwarded\n→ Release to Bank B, after settlement in both models, at different moments\n\nShow the model, Y-Copy or V-Shape, on every high-value flow, beside the message name.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which message was sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves settlement happened before Bank B was asked to credit?\"\n\nHave you seen a bank wait for settlement under the wrong model?", "takeaway": "The distinction is not the message format but the architecture.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Copy or full payment to settlement?", "parts": [{"label": "Y-Copy: copy held", "note": "A copy goes to the settlement mechanism and the payment is held until authorised."}, {"label": "Y-Copy: released after", "note": "The payment is released to Bank B only after settlement completes."}, {"label": "V-Shape: full payment", "note": "The full pacs.008 goes to T2 for validation and settlement."}, {"label": "V-Shape: forwarded settled", "note": "T2 forwards the payment to Bank B after settlement is complete."}, {"label": "EUR 100,000 settled first", "note": "Settlement happened inside T2, before Bank B received it."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E11", "seq": 100, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "INR 10,000 arrives instantly, the banks settle it later", "explain": "Customer A at Bank A sends INR 10,000 to Customer B at Bank B.\nCustomer B sees it instantly.\nThe two banks have not settled a rupee between them yet.\nBoth statements are true at the same time.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer A sends INR 10,000 on UPI\n→ Customer B is credited instantly\n→ Banks record positions against each other\n→ Interbank settlement happens later, including through Net Deferred Settlement\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Instant for the customer means instant between the banks.\"\n\nCustomer credit, payment processing and clearing, and interbank settlement are separate layers.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe INR 10,000 need not be settled between the two banks at that exact moment. The banks' resulting positions can be settled later through the applicable mechanism. Speed at one layer says nothing about speed at the next, and who pays for the infrastructure is a separate question again.\n\nInstant at which layer?\n\nWhy is the beneficiary paid instantly while the banks settle hours later?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk instant at which layer, and never promise settlement speed on the strength of customer speed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer credit, money available to the beneficiary immediately\n→ Processing and clearing, obligations set between banks\n→ Interbank settlement, bank positions settled later\n→ Who pays, pricing and infrastructure funding apart from speed\n\nShow the layer on every instant claim: customer, clearing, or settlement.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the payment instant?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which layer was instant, and when the next layer settles?\"\n\nHave you explained to a customer that instant credit is not instant settlement?", "takeaway": "Ask instant at which layer, and who pays for the infrastructure.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instant at which layer?", "parts": [{"label": "Customer credit", "note": "Money is available to the beneficiary immediately."}, {"label": "Processing and clearing", "note": "The system processes the transaction and sets obligations between banks."}, {"label": "Interbank settlement", "note": "Bank positions settle later, including through Net Deferred Settlement."}, {"label": "Who pays", "note": "Pricing and infrastructure funding are separate questions from speed."}, {"label": "INR 10,000 instant", "note": "Credited instantly, settled between banks later."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-E12", "seq": 101, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 80,000 payment, the team cannot say who clears it", "explain": "A EUR 80,000 payment is stuck.\nThe team knows the message is pacs.008.\nIt cannot say who transports it, who clears it, or who settles it.\nThree different jobs are being treated as one.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment initiated, written as an ISO 20022 message\n→ Message sent through a network to the next bank\n→ Clearing and settlement run under scheme rules\n→ Confirmation comes back, but no one can name each hand-off\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"ISO 20022, the network and the clearing house are one thing.\"\n\nISO 20022 is the language and standard, a common data model with business concepts and message structures such as pacs.008, pacs.009, pacs.004, and camt.053, and it neither transports nor clears nor settles a payment on its own.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe network provides the messaging services that let institutions exchange messages and can carry ISO 20022, while a CSM is the clearing and settlement infrastructure that processes payments under scheme rules and can adopt ISO 20022 without being the network. The payment moves from initiation to an ISO 20022 message, through the network, into the CSM for clearing and settlement, and on to confirmation.\n\nLanguage, network, infrastructure: three roles.\n\nWhy can nobody say who clears a payment everyone can see?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk separately what the standard is, who transports the message, who clears it, who settles it, and which scheme rules apply.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ ISO 20022, the language and message structures\n→ Network, who transports the message\n→ CSM, who clears and settles under scheme rules\n→ Scheme rules, which rules govern the payment\n\nName all four on every payment flow, never just the message.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which message was it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who does each job on this payment?\"\n\nHave you traced a payment where language, network and clearing were mixed into one?", "takeaway": "Ask separately what the standard is, who transports the message, who clears it, and who settles it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Language, network, infrastructure", "parts": [{"label": "ISO 20022: the language", "note": "The standard, data model, and message structures."}, {"label": "Network: the transport", "note": "Messaging services that exchange the messages between institutions."}, {"label": "CSM: the infrastructure", "note": "Clearing and settlement under the rules of the scheme."}, {"label": "Same language, many rails", "note": "ISO 20022 can run across different networks and infrastructures."}, {"label": "EUR 80,000 stuck", "note": "Message known, clearer and settler unnamed."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009", "pacs.004", "camt.053"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E13", "seq": 102, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 150,000 engine change, a downstream failure nobody traced", "explain": "A team changes the payment engine for a EUR 150,000 daily flow.\nThe change passes testing.\nA week later a downstream system fails on a status it no longer recognises.\nNobody traced the change past the engine.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment initiated through a channel into the engine\n→ Validation and screening pass, format, limits, sanctions, fraud, AML\n→ Clearing and settlement run through central bank systems or correspondent banking\n→ Accounting posts debit and credit entries, nostro movements, reconciliation\n→ Operations finds the failure downstream, after settlement\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The payment ends at settlement.\"\n\nA basic answer stops at instruction, processing, sending, crediting, and settlement, but that only describes one application and says nothing about what happens after settlement.\n\nWhy?\n\nOperations and investigations close the loop by tracking status and fixing downstream failures, and any engine change must be traced through every downstream consumer. In payments a point left to clarify later can become an expensive production problem.\n\nSettlement is not the end of the lifecycle.\n\nWhy does a tested engine change break a system nobody looked at?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace every change through every downstream consumer, and close the loop in operations.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiation and validation, channel, format, limits, screening\n→ Clearing and settlement, central bank or correspondent banking\n→ Accounting, entries, nostro movements, reconciliation\n→ Operations and investigations, status tracked, failures fixed\n\nList every downstream consumer of an engine change before it ships, and confirm each one still reads the new output.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the engine change pass testing?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every downstream consumer still works after the change?\"\n\nHave you seen a change pass testing and fail downstream?", "takeaway": "In payments a point left to clarify later can become an expensive production problem.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Initiation", "note": "The customer instructs the payment through a channel into the payment engine."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation and screening", "note": "Format, limits, and mandatory fields checked; sanctions, fraud, and AML screened."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing and settlement", "note": "The payment moves through central bank systems or correspondent banking."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Accounting", "note": "Debit and credit entries, nostro movement, ledger update, and reconciliation."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Every downstream consumer traced?", "note": "Status tracked, failures investigated, reconciled, and fixed."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E14", "seq": 103, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 200,000 from Bank A to Bank C, who actually settles it?", "explain": "Bank A asks Bank B to move EUR 200,000 onward to Bank C.\nBank B does it.\nFinance asks who settled the money.\nThe team names Bank B. Bank B only carried the instruction.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank A instructs Bank B to move funds onward to Bank C\n→ Bank A is the Instructing Agent, Bank B the Instructed Agent\n→ Bank B carries out the next step\n→ A separate Settlement Agent settles the funds on behalf of one of them\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Whoever moves the payment settles the money.\"\n\nKnowing who instructs whom explains the message flow, but it does not say who settles the money.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe Settlement Agent is a separate party that settles on behalf of the Instructing or Instructed Agent and belongs to the settlement flow rather than the message flow. Naming the Settlement Agent makes the debit and credit entries and the nostro movements straightforward to work out.\n\nMessage flow is not settlement responsibility.\n\nWhy does naming the right bank still leave the settlement unexplained?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the Settlement Agent on every chain, and read the settlement flow apart from the message flow.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instructing Agent, who issues the instruction for the next step\n→ Instructed Agent, who receives it and carries that step out\n→ Settlement Agent, who actually settles the funds\n→ Settlement flow, who pays, apart from the message flow\n\nShow all three agents on the payment chain, with the settlement agent labelled as such.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Who instructed whom?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who settled the money, and through which account?\"\n\nHave you mixed up the bank that instructed with the bank that settled?", "takeaway": "Naming the Settlement Agent makes the debit and credit entries and the nostro movements straightforward.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who instructs, who is instructed, who settles", "parts": [{"label": "Instructing Agent", "note": "The party that instructs the next party to carry out the payment."}, {"label": "Instructed Agent", "note": "The party that receives the instruction and executes the next step."}, {"label": "Settlement Agent", "note": "The party that actually settles the funds on behalf of another agent."}, {"label": "Two different flows", "note": "Message flow shows who instructs whom; settlement flow shows who pays."}, {"label": "EUR 200,000 onward", "note": "Bank A to Bank B to Bank C, settlement by a separate agent."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-001", "seq": 104, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 500,000 bill refused at first presentment, never returns", "explain": "A seller presents a bill for EUR 500,000 for acceptance.\nThe drawee refuses to sign.\nThe seller plans to present it again next week.\nIt will never return for a second acceptance.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Paper drawn on a bill form, drawee has not signed\n→ Presented for acceptance, drawee refuses\n→ Dishonour by non-acceptance at first presentment\n→ Instrument never returns for a second acceptance\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A refusal is a delay. Present it again.\"\n\nAcceptance is the act that converts a drawn paper into a bill of exchange in the strict sense.\n\nWhy?\n\nUntil the drawee signs, the instrument remains an unaccepted paper on a bill form, however loosely market usage labels it. Dishonour by non-acceptance therefore describes refusal at first presentment, and the instrument never returns for a second acceptance.\n\nNo signature, no bill of exchange.\n\nWhy does a refused bill never get a second chance at acceptance?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat first presentment as the only acceptance event, and act on a refusal at once.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Unaccepted paper, a paper on a bill form only\n→ Acceptance, the drawee signature that creates the bill\n→ Dishonour by non-acceptance, refusal at first presentment\n\nRecord the presentment date and the refusal on the day it happens, and start the dishonour process there.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we present it again?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves acceptance happened, or dishonour was recorded at first presentment?\"\n\nHave you seen a refused bill presented again as if refusal were a delay?", "takeaway": "Dishonour by non-acceptance describes refusal at first presentment, and the instrument never returns for a second acceptance.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Acceptance creates the bill of exchange", "parts": [{"label": "Before acceptance", "note": "An unsigned paper on a bill form only."}, {"label": "Drawee signs", "note": "Signature converts the paper into a bill of exchange."}, {"label": "Refusal at first presentment", "note": "Non-acceptance means refusal when first presented."}, {"label": "One acceptance only", "note": "An accepted bill never goes back for acceptance again."}, {"label": "EUR 500,000 at stake", "note": "Refusal at first presentment ends the acceptance path."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-002", "seq": 105, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 500,000 bill looks complete, the payee is not named", "explain": "A bill for EUR 500,000 arrives for discounting.\nIt names a bank, it looks official, it looks complete.\nThe payee is not identified without ambiguity.\nThe bill is not valid.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bill presented for EUR 500,000\n→ Bank name present, paperwork looks complete\n→ Payor named, payee not identified without ambiguity\n→ Drawee acceptance missing, validity fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A bank name on the bill makes it valid.\"\n\nValidity turns on the parties and the acceptance, not on any bank name.\n\nWhy?\n\nPayor and payee must both be identified without ambiguity, and the drawee must have accepted the instrument. Naming a bank is not a mandatory element, and a bill that fails on parties or acceptance fails outright, however official it looks.\n\nParties and acceptance decide validity, not the bank name.\n\nWhy does a bill with a bank name still fail as invalid?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck parties and acceptance first, and never let a familiar name stand in for a mandatory element.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payor identified, the party who must pay\n→ Payee identified, the party to be paid\n→ Drawee acceptance, the signature that creates the bill\n→ Bank name, not a mandatory element\n\nCheck each mandatory element on its own line before discounting or accepting the bill.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does the bill look complete?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every mandatory element is present and unambiguous?\"\n\nHave you seen a bill rejected for a party nobody had named clearly?", "takeaway": "Payor and payee must both be identified without ambiguity, and the drawee must have accepted the instrument.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What a valid bill must carry", "parts": [{"label": "Payor identified", "note": "The party who must pay is named without ambiguity."}, {"label": "Payee identified", "note": "The party to be paid is named without ambiguity."}, {"label": "Drawee acceptance", "note": "The drawee has signed to accept the bill."}, {"label": "No bank name required", "note": "Naming a bank is not a mandatory element."}, {"label": "EUR 500,000 invalid", "note": "Payee unclear and acceptance missing, so the bill fails."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-004", "seq": 106, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 1,000,000 moves on a network where every bank is a node", "explain": "EUR 1,000,000 moves between banks on a blockchain payment network.\nA customer asks which single system processed it.\nThere is no single system.\nEvery participant processed it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment enters the blockchain network\n→ Each institution operates as a node\n→ Banks, clearing and settlement mechanisms and insurers take part directly\n→ Transaction is processed across the nodes, not by one centre\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Some central system must process every payment.\"\n\nParticipation is confined to financial institutions, and each institution operates as a node and takes a direct part in processing transactions.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe network has no central processor to point at. Banks, clearing and settlement mechanisms and insurers among them each act as a node, so the processing is shared across the participants rather than handed to one of them.\n\nEvery participant is a node.\n\nWhy is there no single system to blame when the payment is delayed?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for a network of nodes, and give every participant its own processing and reconciliation duty.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Banks as nodes, processing transactions directly\n→ Clearing and settlement mechanisms as nodes\n→ Insurers and other financial institutions as nodes\n→ Shared processing, no single central system\n\nShow the node that handled each step, so a delay can be traced to a participant.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which system processed it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which node processed each step?\"\n\nHave you traced a payment across a network with no centre?", "takeaway": "Each institution operates as a node and takes a direct part in processing transactions.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Participants in a blockchain payment network", "parts": [{"label": "Banks", "note": "Banks act as nodes and process transactions."}, {"label": "Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "note": "A CSM can act as a node and handle settlement."}, {"label": "Insurance firms", "note": "Insurers and other financial institutions can be nodes."}, {"label": "Every participant is a node", "note": "All participants take part in transaction processing."}, {"label": "EUR 1,000,000 shared", "note": "No single centre, processing is shared across nodes."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D15 Advanced and emerging payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-006", "seq": 107, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 3,000 SEPA payment, customer blames the clearing system", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 3,000 by SEPA credit transfer.\nA fee appears on the statement.\nThe customer complains the clearing system charged him.\nThe clearing system charged nothing.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer sends EUR 3,000 by SEPA credit transfer\n→ Fee appears on the customer statement\n→ Customer blames the clearing system\n→ The fee came from his own bank, not the clearing layer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The system that clears the payment charges for clearing it.\"\n\nThe charge a customer sees is a bank charge, not a clearing charge.\n\nWhy?\n\nA bank may set its own price for sending a SEPA credit transfer, and likewise for a SEPA direct debit. The clearing system itself levies no interchange fee for processing the transaction, so the fee the customer sees is his bank's price, not the system's.\n\nThe clearing system charges nothing. The bank does.\n\nWhy does the customer blame the clearing system for his own bank's fee?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the fee owner on the statement, and never let a bank price look like a system charge.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bank charge, set by the customer's own bank\n→ Clearing charge, none levied by the clearing system\n→ Customer statement, showing whose fee it is\n\nLabel the fee with the bank that set it, and the service it pays for.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What did the payment cost?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who set the fee the customer paid?\"\n\nHave you seen a bank fee blamed on the clearing system?", "takeaway": "The clearing system itself levies no interchange fee for processing the transaction.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Charges on a SEPA payment", "parts": [{"label": "Bank may charge the customer", "note": "A bank may price a SEPA credit transfer or direct debit it sends."}, {"label": "Clearing system charges nothing", "note": "The clearing system levies no interchange fee for processing."}, {"label": "Fee comes from your bank", "note": "What the customer pays comes from their own bank, not the clearing layer."}, {"label": "EUR 3,000 sent", "note": "The fee on the statement is the bank's price."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-008", "seq": 108, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 10,000,000 cleared but not settled, the team used the wrong word", "explain": "EUR 10,000,000 in payments is cleared for the day.\nOperations reports the day as settled.\nIt is not settled. The money has not moved.\nThe team used the wrong word, and the wrong word hid the gap.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payments exchanged and cleared through the system\n→ Team calls the clearing house the settlement too\n→ Settlement leg not yet run, funds not yet moved\n→ Day reported settled while money still waits\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Clearing and settlement are the same job, so one word covers both.\"\n\nA clearing and settlement mechanism is the system through which banks exchange and clear payment transactions and then settle them. An automated clearing house concentrates on the clearing leg.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe term CSM is preferred because it makes the link between clearing and settlement explicit. Calling an ACH a settlement covers the clearing and hides the settlement that still has to happen, so the day looks finished before the money moves.\n\nClearing is not settlement.\n\nWhy does a cleared day still have EUR 10,000,000 unsettled?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName clearing and settlement as two legs, and report each on its own evidence.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Clearing, transactions exchanged and cleared\n→ Settlement, funds actually moved\n→ CSM, the mechanism that links the two explicitly\n\nReport clearing and settlement as two lines, each with its own completion proof.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Were the payments cleared?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds settled after clearing?\"\n\nHave you seen a day called settled when only clearing had finished?", "takeaway": "The term CSM is preferred because it makes the link between clearing and settlement explicit.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "ACH or CSM", "parts": [{"label": "CSM", "note": "The clearing and settlement mechanism covers clearing and settlement together."}, {"label": "ACH", "note": "The automated clearing house concentrates on clearing."}, {"label": "Why CSM is used", "note": "It makes the link between clearing and settlement explicit."}, {"label": "EUR 10,000,000 cleared", "note": "Cleared but not yet settled, the gap the wrong word hid."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-016", "seq": 109, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Small bank sends EUR 5,000,000, has no direct link, still holds a central bank account", "explain": "A small bank sends EUR 5,000,000 in payments every day.\nIt has no direct connection to the settlement system.\nA customer assumes it holds no central bank account.\nEvery bank holds one.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Small bank routes its settlement through another bank\n→ Team calls it an indirect participant\n→ Customer assumes indirect means no central bank account\n→ The bank holds a central bank account in its own country, like every bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Indirect participant means no account at the central bank.\"\n\nEvery bank holds a central bank account in its own country, which the central bank needs to oversee the banking system.\n\nWhy?\n\nDirect participation only means a bank routes its real time gross settlement exchanges and settlement through another bank. A bank stays indirect because connecting directly demands setting up and running complex infrastructure, worthwhile only with enough volume. Indirect is about the route, not about the account.\n\nIndirect is a route, not an absence.\n\nWhy does a bank with no direct link still hold a central bank account?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSeparate the account a bank holds from the route it uses to settle.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Central bank account, held by every bank\n→ Direct participation, own connection to the settlement system\n→ Indirect participation, settlement routed through another bank\n→ Volume, what makes a direct connection worthwhile\n\nShow the account and the route as two facts on every participant record.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the bank direct or indirect?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the account exists and the route it settles through?\"\n\nHave you assumed an indirect bank held no central bank account?", "takeaway": "A bank stays indirect because connecting directly demands complex infrastructure, worthwhile only with enough volume.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Indirect participants and central bank accounts", "parts": [{"label": "Every bank has one", "note": "All banks hold an account with their country's central bank."}, {"label": "The central bank oversees", "note": "The account lets the central bank oversee the banking system."}, {"label": "Direct means using another bank", "note": "An indirect participant routes RTGS exchanges through another bank."}, {"label": "Why banks stay indirect", "note": "Connecting directly needs complex infrastructure, only worth it with volume."}, {"label": "EUR 5,000,000 a day", "note": "Settled daily through another bank, account held throughout."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-019", "seq": 110, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "MT103 for EUR 250,000 cannot carry the data the MX can", "explain": "A bank sends an MT103 for EUR 250,000.\nThe beneficiary bank asks for remittance data the MT103 cannot carry.\nThe payment arrives, the data does not.\nThe format ran out of room.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent, ISO 15022, flat files and tags\n→ Beneficiary needs richer remittance and party data\n→ MT format has no place to carry it\n→ MX equivalent, ISO 20022 XML, carries far more information\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT and MX are two names for the same message.\"\n\nMT rests on ISO 15022 and its flat files and tags, while MX rests on ISO 20022 and its XML files and message elements.\n\nWhy?\n\nISO 20022 is far richer, so an MX message carries much more information, and almost every MT message has a corresponding MX message. MT is giving way to MX over time, yet both standards serve the same situations today, so a team that treats them as equal will keep hitting the MT ceiling.\n\nRicher standard, richer message.\n\nWhy does the same EUR 250,000 payment carry less data as MT than as MX?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nChoose the format by the data the payment must carry, and map MT to MX where the data matters.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT, ISO 15022, flat files and tags\n→ MX, ISO 20022, XML and message elements\n→ Mapping, almost every MT has an MX equivalent\n→ Replacement, MT giving way to MX over time\n\nShow the MT and MX equivalents side by side, with the data only MX can carry marked.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the MT103 arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment carried all the data the beneficiary needs?\"\n\nHave you lost remittance data to an MT format limit?", "takeaway": "MT is giving way to MX over time, yet both standards serve the same situations today.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "SWIFT MT versus SWIFT MX", "parts": [{"label": "MT: ISO 15022, flat files", "note": "MT messages use flat files and tags."}, {"label": "MX: ISO 20022, XML", "note": "MX messages use XML files and message elements."}, {"label": "MX carries more", "note": "ISO 20022 is richer and carries much more information."}, {"label": "One maps to the other", "note": "Almost every MT message has a corresponding MX message."}, {"label": "MT is being replaced", "note": "MX replaces MT over time, but both work in the same situations today."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch8-mt-to-mx-migration-and-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 8: MT to MX Migration and CBPR+", "chapter": "ch8", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-023", "seq": 111, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Corporate sends MT101 for EUR 120,000, bank cannot read the extra data", "explain": "A corporate sends an MT101 for EUR 120,000.\nIt wants to attach rich remittance data for its supplier.\nThe MT101 has no room for it.\nThe supplier is paid, but cannot match the payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT101 sent, customer to bank, ISO 15022 flat files and tags\n→ Corporate needs richer data for the supplier\n→ MT101 cannot carry it\n→ pain.001, ISO 20022 XML, holds far more information\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT101 and pain.001 are the same instruction in different wrapping.\"\n\nBoth messages carry credit transfer instructions to the bank, but they are built on different standards.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe MT101 leg rests on ISO 15022, a SWIFT MT message built on flat files and tags, while pain.001 is built on ISO 20022, with XML files and message elements. ISO 20022 is far richer, so pain.001 holds far more information than MT101 can, and the supplier matching fails on the thinner message.\n\nSame instruction, different capacity.\n\nWhy can the supplier not match a payment that arrived in full?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPick the instruction format by the data the supplier needs to reconcile, not by habit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT101, ISO 15022, flat files and tags\n→ pain.001, ISO 20022, XML and message elements\n→ Remittance data, what the supplier needs to match the payment\n\nShow the data each format can carry before the corporate chooses one.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the instruction sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the instruction carried the data the supplier needs?\"\n\nHave you seen a supplier unable to match a payment that arrived in full?", "takeaway": "ISO 20022 is far richer, so pain.001 holds far more information than MT101 can.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT101 versus pain.001", "parts": [{"label": "Both instruct the bank", "note": "Both send credit transfer instructions to the bank."}, {"label": "MT101: ISO 15022, flat files", "note": "MT101 uses flat files and tags."}, {"label": "pain.001: ISO 20022, XML", "note": "pain.001 uses XML files and message elements."}, {"label": "pain.001 carries more", "note": "ISO 20022 is richer, so pain.001 carries much more information."}, {"label": "EUR 120,000 unmatched", "note": "Paid in full, but the supplier cannot match it."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "pain.001"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-028", "seq": 112, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "MT101 sent for EUR 90,000, team waits for money that has not moved", "explain": "A corporate sends an MT101 for EUR 90,000.\nThe beneficiary bank waits for the money.\nNothing arrives.\nThe team is waiting on an instruction, not a payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT101 goes out before execution, customer to bank\n→ Ordering bank receives the instruction\n→ Ordering account debited\n→ MT103 follows, bank to bank, moving the money\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT101 moves the money.\"\n\nMT101 belongs to the customer-to-bank space and MT103 to the interbank space.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT101 goes out before execution, carrying the payment instruction to the ordering bank. The MT103 follows as the next step, leaving the ordering bank for the beneficiary or correspondent banks after the ordering account has been debited. Waiting for funds on an MT101 is waiting on the wrong message.\n\nMT101 instructs. MT103 moves the money.\n\nWhy does the beneficiary wait for money an MT101 can never bring?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead MT101 as an instruction and MT103 as the money, and track each on its own timeline.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT101, customer to bank, before execution\n→ Debit, ordering account debited\n→ MT103, bank to bank, after the debit\n\nShow the MT101 and the MT103 as two steps, and never mark the payment sent until the MT103 leaves.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the MT101 sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the MT103 left after the debit?\"\n\nHave you waited for funds on an instruction message?", "takeaway": "MT101 instructs the payment, MT103 moves the money.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT101 versus MT103", "parts": [{"label": "MT101: customer to bank", "note": "MT101 carries the instruction from customer to bank."}, {"label": "MT103: bank to bank", "note": "MT103 moves funds between banks."}, {"label": "MT101 is before execution", "note": "MT101 sends the instruction to the ordering bank."}, {"label": "MT103 is after the debit", "note": "MT103 goes out once the ordering account has been debited."}, {"label": "One instructs, one moves", "note": "MT101 instructs the payment, MT103 moves the money."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-029", "seq": 113, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 600 mandate unused for three years, no one told the creditor", "explain": "A creditor holds a mandate for EUR 600 a month.\nNo collection runs under it for three years.\nOn the fourth year the creditor sends a collection.\nIt is rejected. The mandate expired silently.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Mandate signed for EUR 600 monthly collections\n→ 36 months pass with no orders related to it\n→ Mandate expires after 36 months with no orders\n→ No message tells any party it expired\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A mandate lasts until someone cancels it.\"\n\nA SEPA mandate expires after 36 months with no orders related to it.\n\nWhy?\n\nExpiry triggers no SWIFT message to any party. The mandate simply lapses quietly after three years without use, so a creditor that does not track inactivity discovers the expiry only when the next collection is rejected.\n\nSilent expiry, loud rejection.\n\nWhy does a mandate nobody cancelled stop working?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrack mandate inactivity like a deadline, and warn long before the 36 months run out.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Last use, the date of the last order under the mandate\n→ 36-month clock, expiry after three years without use\n→ No message, no party is told when it lapses\n→ Next collection, rejected if the mandate has lapsed\n\nShow the months since last use on every mandate, with an alert before expiry.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the mandate cancelled?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the mandate is still alive for this collection?\"\n\nHave you seen a mandate expire quietly after years without use?", "takeaway": "The mandate simply lapses quietly after three years without use.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "When a mandate expires", "parts": [{"label": "36 months without orders", "note": "A mandate expires after 36 months with no related orders."}, {"label": "No message is sent", "note": "No message tells any party the mandate expired."}, {"label": "It lapses quietly", "note": "The mandate simply ends after three years without use."}, {"label": "EUR 600 collection rejected", "note": "The next collection fails on the lapsed mandate."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-032", "seq": 114, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 15,000 on-us transfer built on a different standard, breaks reconciliation", "explain": "A bank moves EUR 15,000 between two accounts it holds itself.\nA team builds it on a different message standard from interbank payments.\nReconciliation breaks, because the two standards do not line up.\nThe payment never left the bank.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ On-us transfer, both accounts in the same bank\n→ Team uses a separate standard for intrabank traffic\n→ Interbank traffic uses the standard ISO 20022 set\n→ Reconciliation fails across the two standards\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Inside the bank, any standard will do.\"\n\nIntrabank book transfers run on the same standard messages as interbank ones.\n\nWhy?\n\nFor a SEPA credit transfer that means the standard ISO 20022 set. Running different standards for interbank and intrabank traffic would create many problems over the long term, so an on-us transfer travels on the same message standards as any other payment.\n\nSame bank, same standard.\n\nWhy does a payment that never leaves the bank break reconciliation?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun on-us transfers on the same standard as interbank, and reconcile both through one path.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ On-us transfers, same standard messages as interbank\n→ Interbank transfers, the standard ISO 20022 set\n→ Reconciliation, one path for both\n\nShow the message standard on every transfer, on-us or interbank, and block any separate intrabank standard.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the money move inside the bank?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the on-us transfer reconciles on the same standard as any other payment?\"\n\nHave you seen an internal transfer break reconciliation?", "takeaway": "An on-us transfer travels on the same message standards as any other payment.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Messages for on-us intrabank transfers", "parts": [{"label": "Same standards as interbank", "note": "Intrabank transfers use the same standard messages."}, {"label": "SCT uses ISO 20022", "note": "For a SEPA credit transfer the ISO 20022 set applies."}, {"label": "Different standards cause problems", "note": "Separate standards for interbank and intrabank would create many problems."}, {"label": "On-us travels on the same rails", "note": "An on-us transfer uses the same message standards as any payment."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-008", "seq": 115, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 8,000 foreign cheque, the bank cannot tell what type it is", "explain": "A customer deposits a cheque for EUR 8,000 from another country.\nThe deposit slip says \"foreign cheque\".\nThe bank cannot process it.\nForeign is not a complete type.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Cheque arrives from another country, EUR 8,000\n→ Recorded as a foreign cheque, no type named\n→ Clearing team asks: bank cheque or order cheque?\n→ No answer on the slip, processing stalls\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Foreign is a type of cheque.\"\n\nForeign on its own is not a complete cheque type.\n\nWhy?\n\nA cheque from another country still carries a type in that country, so it may be a foreign bank cheque, or equally a foreign order cheque. The word foreign only says where the cheque comes from, and the type must be named as well, or clearing cannot decide how to handle it.\n\nOrigin is not type.\n\nWhy does a cheque with a clear origin stall in clearing?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecord origin and type as two separate fields, and never let one stand in for the other.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Origin, where the cheque comes from\n→ Type, bank cheque, order cheque, or the type in its own country\n→ Clearing path, decided by the type, not the origin\n\nRequire both fields on every foreign cheque deposit before it enters clearing.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Where did the cheque come from?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cheque type, so clearing knows how to handle it?\"\n\nHave you seen a cheque stall because its type was never named?", "takeaway": "The word foreign only says where the cheque comes from, and the type must be named as well.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What a foreign cheque really is", "parts": [{"label": "Foreign is not a complete type", "note": "A foreign cheque on its own is not a complete type of cheque."}, {"label": "Foreign bank cheque", "note": "You can have a foreign bank cheque."}, {"label": "Foreign order cheque", "note": "You can have a foreign order cheque."}, {"label": "Foreign describes where it comes from", "note": "The word foreign describes where the cheque comes from."}, {"label": "The type must be named as well", "note": "A cheque from another country still has a type, and it must be named."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-11", "seq": 118, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 300,000 corporate payment, stuck between channel and core banking", "explain": "A corporate sends EUR 300,000 through its channel.\nThe payment never reaches core banking.\nNobody can say where it stopped.\nThere are seven hand-offs, and no one owns the gaps between them.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Corporate channel starts the payment\n→ API gateway, entry point into the bank systems\n→ Validation, payment checked before it moves on\n→ Payment hub, central processing\n→ Compliance screening, a failed screen stops the payment here\n→ Routing and clearing, then core banking debit and credit posting\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A payment flows in one smooth line from channel to ledger.\"\n\nA corporate payment starts in a channel, passes an API gateway, validation and the payment hub, then compliance screening, routing and clearing, and finally the core banking debit and credit posting.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach hand-off is a place where the payment can be checked, enriched or stopped. That chain of hand-offs is what makes the flow controllable end to end, but only when each hand-off is owned and visible. A payment that vanishes between two stages was never tracked across the gap.\n\nHand-offs are controls, not plumbing.\n\nWhy does a EUR 300,000 payment vanish between two systems nobody owns?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOwn every hand-off, and show the payment at each stage, including the gaps.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channel and gateway, where the payment enters\n→ Validation and hub, where it is checked and processed\n→ Compliance screening, where it can be stopped\n→ Routing, clearing and core posting, where it completes\n\nShow the stage, the owner, and the time at each hand-off on one screen.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment reach core banking?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment passed each hand-off, and who owned it there?\"\n\nHave you lost a payment between two systems?", "takeaway": "That chain of hand-offs is what makes the flow controllable end to end.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Corporate channel", "note": "Where the payment starts, EUR 300,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "API gateway", "note": "Entry point into the bank systems."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation", "note": "Payment checked before it moves on."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment hub", "note": "Central processing for the payment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance screening clear?", "note": "A failed screen stops the payment here."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing and clearing", "note": "Payment routed to the right rail and cleared."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Core banking posting", "note": "Debit and credit posted to the ledger."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-13", "seq": 120, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "VND 200,000,000 sent in Vietnam, supplier says it never arrived", "explain": "A corporate sends VND 200,000,000 to a supplier in Vietnam.\nThe channel says sent.\nThe supplier says nothing arrived.\nThe payment is somewhere in the middle, and reconciliation is the only proof of where.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Channel, where the payment is sent from\n→ Core banking, payment enters the bank core\n→ Payment hub, central processing and control\n→ Validation and routing, checked, then routed\n→ NAPAS, the domestic rail that carries it\n→ Receiving bank, then reconciliation\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Sent means arrived.\"\n\nA domestic payment in Vietnam moves from the channel to core banking, then the payment hub, validation and routing, NAPAS, the receiving bank and reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach stage has a job, and reconciliation at the end proves the money arrived where it should. A payment that is only tracked to the rail has no proof at the far end, so the supplier's word and the channel's word disagree with nothing to settle it.\n\nSent is not arrived. Reconciliation is the proof.\n\nWhy do the channel and the supplier disagree about the same payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrack every stage, and let reconciliation close the payment, not the channel.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channel, where the payment is sent from\n→ Core banking and hub, where it is processed\n→ NAPAS and receiving bank, where it travels and lands\n→ Reconciliation, proving the money arrived where it should\n\nShow all seven stages on the payment record, and flag any payment with no reconciliation proof.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the payment sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the money arrived where it should?\"\n\nHave you argued sent against arrived with no reconciliation to settle it?", "takeaway": "Each stage has a job, and reconciliation at the end proves the money arrived where it should.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Channel", "note": "Where the payment is sent from, VND 200,000,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Core banking", "note": "Payment enters the bank core."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment hub", "note": "Central processing and control."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation and routing", "note": "Checked, then routed to the right path."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Validation and routing clear?", "note": "A failed check stops the payment here."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "NAPAS and receiving bank", "note": "The domestic rail carries it to the receiving bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconciliation", "note": "Proves the money arrived where it should."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-19", "seq": 126, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 180,000 corporate payment, the issue has no stage to belong to", "explain": "A EUR 180,000 corporate payment fails.\nThe support team cannot say where.\nThey have no list of stages to place it against.\nThe issue floats, unowned, for two days.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Initiation, payment origination starts the journey\n→ Validation, payment checked before it moves on\n→ Compliance, checked before routing\n→ Routing, payment routed to the right rail\n→ Clearing, payment cleared through the network\n→ Settlement, funds move to complete the payment\n→ Posting and reporting, final posting closes the journey\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Knowing the flow in general is enough to find a failure.\"\n\nIn corporate banking, GPP runs a nine-step journey from payment origination to settlement and reporting.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe steps cover initiation, validation, compliance, routing, clearing, and the final posting and reporting. Knowing the nine steps lets a BA place any issue at the right stage, and without the list, an issue has nowhere to belong and no owner to take it.\n\nNo stage list, no owner.\n\nWhy does a failed payment float unowned for two days?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlace every issue at its stage, and let the stage name its owner.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiation and validation, where the payment starts and is checked\n→ Compliance and routing, where it is cleared to travel\n→ Clearing and settlement, where the money moves\n→ Posting and reporting, where the journey closes\n\nShow the nine steps on every incident, with the failing step marked.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What went wrong?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which step failed, and who owns it?\"\n\nHave you chased an issue that had no stage to belong to?", "takeaway": "Knowing the nine steps lets a BA place any issue at the right stage.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Initiation", "note": "Payment origination starts the journey, EUR 180,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation", "note": "Payment checked before it moves on."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance clear?", "note": "Compliance checked before routing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing", "note": "Payment routed to the right rail."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "Payment cleared through the network."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Funds move to complete the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Posting and reporting", "note": "Final posting and reporting close the journey."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-20", "seq": 127, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 500,000 on four rails, four views, one customer asking where it is", "explain": "A bank runs EUR 500,000 a day across four rails.\nDomestic, cross-border, real-time and high-value each have their own view.\nA customer asks where a payment is.\nFour teams give four answers.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Domestic payment, one platform view\n→ Cross-border payment, another view\n→ Real-time payment, a third view\n→ High-value payment, a fourth view\n→ Same customer, same question, four different answers\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each rail needs its own platform and its own controls.\"\n\nGPP orchestrates multiple payment types and rails from one platform: domestic, cross-border, real-time and high-value payments, with routing, compliance and reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nOne platform means one set of controls and one view of the payment, whatever the rail. Separate platforms give each rail its own truth, and the customer hears all four at once.\n\nOne platform, one view, whatever the rail.\n\nWhy do four teams give four answers about one payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun every rail on one platform, with one set of controls and one view.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic payments, on the one platform\n→ Cross-border payments, on the one platform\n→ Real-time payments, on the one platform\n→ High-value payments, on the one platform\n→ Routing, compliance and reconciliation, one set of controls\n\nShow the payment once, with its rail labelled, never once per rail system.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which rail was it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves one platform saw the whole payment?\"\n\nHave you heard four answers about one payment?", "takeaway": "One platform means one set of controls and one view of the payment, whatever the rail.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One platform, many rails", "parts": [{"label": "Domestic payments", "note": "Domestic payments on the one platform."}, {"label": "Cross-border payments", "note": "Cross-border payments on the one platform."}, {"label": "Real-time payments", "note": "Real-time payments on the one platform."}, {"label": "High-value payments", "note": "High-value payments on the one platform."}, {"label": "Routing", "note": "One set of controls, whatever the rail."}, {"label": "Compliance", "note": "One set of controls, whatever the rail."}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "One view of the payment, whatever the rail."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-21", "seq": 128, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 750,000 cross-border payment, the weakest hand-off breaks it", "explain": "A EUR 750,000 cross-border payment crosses seven hand-offs.\nSix are strong. One is weak.\nThe payment fails at the weak one.\nThe integration was judged by its best hand-off.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Initiation, payment starts in the channel\n→ Validation and enrichment, checked and enriched\n→ Compliance, checked before routing\n→ Routing and orchestration, routed across rails from one platform\n→ ISO 20022 and SWIFT, mapped and sent\n→ Exceptions, handled when a stage fails\n→ Settlement and reconciliation, settled, then reconciled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Most hand-offs strong means the integration is strong.\"\n\nGPP integration in cross-border payments covers initiation, validation and enrichment, compliance, routing and orchestration, ISO 20022 and SWIFT, exceptions, and settlement with reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach stage is a hand-off, and the integration is only as strong as the weakest hand-off. A chain judged by its strongest link hides the one that breaks, and the payment finds it.\n\nStrongest link thinking breaks chains.\n\nWhy does a payment with six strong hand-offs fail?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest the weakest hand-off first, and strengthen it before praising the rest.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiation and validation, where the payment is prepared\n→ Compliance and routing, where it is cleared and sent\n→ ISO 20022 and SWIFT, where it is mapped and carried\n→ Exceptions, where a stage failure is handled\n→ Settlement and reconciliation, where it closes\n\nScore every hand-off, and report the weakest score as the integration score.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How strong is the integration?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the weakest hand-off holds?\"\n\nHave you seen a chain break at its weakest hand-off?", "takeaway": "Each stage is a hand-off, and the integration is only as strong as the weakest hand-off.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Initiation", "note": "Payment starts in the channel, EUR 750,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation and enrichment", "note": "Checked and enriched before it moves on."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance clear?", "note": "Compliance checked before routing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing and orchestration", "note": "Routed across rails from one platform."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ISO 20022 and SWIFT", "note": "Mapped to the standard and sent on SWIFT."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Exceptions", "note": "Handled when a stage fails."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement and reconciliation", "note": "Settled, then reconciled to close the flow."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "ABH-22", "seq": 129, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 900,000 payment works, the lending product beside it breaks", "explain": "A BA fixes a EUR 900,000 payment flow.\nThe payment works.\nA lending product that shares the same platform breaks the same week.\nThe BA never looked beyond payments.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment flow fixed, tested, working\n→ Wider platform untouched, never reviewed\n→ Lending product shares platform services\n→ Change in payments breaks lending, no one saw the link\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Payments is the whole platform.\"\n\nThe banking technology platform is a banking ecosystem, broader than a payment processor alone.\n\nWhy?\n\nIts products cover more of the bank than payments, so a BA working with the platform must understand the wider platform, not only the payment flow. A change that is safe inside payments can break a product that shares its services, and the BA who never looked wider never sees the link.\n\nPayments is one part of the platform.\n\nWhy does a safe payments change break lending?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap the wider platform before changing any part of it, and check every shared service.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payments, one part of the wider platform\n→ Wider banking products, covering more of the bank than payments\n→ Shared services, where a change in one product touches another\n→ The BA view, the whole platform, not only payments\n\nList the products that share each service before any change ships.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does the payment work?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves no wider product broke when the payment changed?\"\n\nHave you fixed payments and broken something beside them?", "takeaway": "Its products cover more of the bank than payments, so a BA must understand the wider platform, not only the payment flow.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Banking ecosystem, wider than payments", "parts": [{"label": "Payments", "note": "One part of the wider platform."}, {"label": "Wider banking products", "note": "Cover more of the bank than payments."}, {"label": "Shared services", "note": "A change in payments can touch lending and more."}, {"label": "The BA view", "note": "Understand the wider platform, not only payments."}, {"label": "EUR 900,000 payment", "note": "Payment fixed, lending broken beside it."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-23", "seq": 130, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 1,200,000 a day, channels connected, hub never reached", "explain": "A bank connects its channels and systems to the payment hub.\nEUR 1,200,000 a day should flow end to end.\nIt does not.\nThe plumbing was connected, but the nine-step journey was never tested through it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Channels and systems connected to the hub\n→ Payment enters the channel\n→ Hub receives it, journey should run end to end\n→ One hand-off in the middle was never wired, journey stalls\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Connecting the systems is the same as connecting the journey.\"\n\nIntegration connects the bank's channels and systems to the payment hub so that a payment can flow end to end.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe integration is the plumbing that makes the nine-step journey possible, but plumbing alone is not a journey. A connection that was never tested end to end leaves a hand-off unwired, and the payment stalls in the middle of a flow everyone believed was connected.\n\nConnected systems, disconnected journey.\n\nWhy does EUR 1,200,000 a day stall in a fully connected bank?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest the journey, not the connections, and prove every hand-off carries a payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channels and systems, connected to the hub\n→ Payment hub, where payments flow end to end\n→ Nine-step journey, made possible by the integration\n→ End-to-end proof, a payment through every hand-off\n\nRun a payment through all nine steps after every integration change, and show the proof.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are the systems connected?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves a payment flows end to end through the integration?\"\n\nHave you seen connected systems with a disconnected journey?", "takeaway": "The integration is the plumbing that makes the nine-step journey possible.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Integration, the big picture", "parts": [{"label": "Channels and systems", "note": "Connected to the payment hub."}, {"label": "Payment hub", "note": "Where payments flow end to end."}, {"label": "Nine-step journey", "note": "Made possible by the integration."}, {"label": "EUR 1,200,000 a day", "note": "Should flow end to end, stalls on one unwired hand-off."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A08", "seq": 161, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Treasury saw EUR 18 million and thought EUR 2 million was lost", "explain": "Treasury expects EUR 20 million.\n\nThe advice shows EUR 18 million.\n\nThe missing EUR 2 million is not lost. It is still in transit.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ pacs.009 ADV arrives\n→ Treasury reads EUR 18 million\n→ The missing EUR 2 million is treated as lost\n→ pacs.008, the payment itself, has not arrived yet\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The advice is the payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.009 ADV is an advice that funds are on the way, giving treasury visibility before arrival for liquidity planning and forecasting. It is not a pacs.008 payment instruction, and it may carry only part of the amount.\n\nAn advice is not a payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the advice and the payment apart. Treasury must know what has arrived, what is advised, and what is still in transit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Advice amount in pacs.009 ADV\n→ Payment amount in pacs.008\n→ Funds still in transit\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How much does the advice show?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves how much has actually arrived?\"\n\nHave you seen an advice mistaken for the payment itself?", "takeaway": "An advice is not a payment. pacs.009 ADV gives treasury sight, it does not move money.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.009 ADV: advice, not payment", "parts": [{"label": "Treasury expects EUR 20 million", "note": "The full amount is expected"}, {"label": "ADV shows EUR 18 million", "note": "The advice may carry only part of the amount"}, {"label": "Missing EUR 2 million in transit", "note": "The missing EUR 2 million is in transit rather than lost"}, {"label": "pacs.008 is the payment", "note": "The payment itself is still the pacs.008, not the advice"}, {"label": "Treasury visibility", "note": "The advice gives visibility before the money arrives"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pacs.009", "pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B04", "seq": 165, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The team asked for pacs.009 and got the wrong one", "explain": "A EUR 100,000 cover payment needs to move.\n\nThe requirement says one word: pacs.009.\n\nThe team builds Core. The payment needs Cover. The funding leg never moves.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Requirement says pacs.009 is needed\n→ No use case is named\n→ Team builds Core, a bank-to-bank transfer\n→ The business needed COV, the cover leg\n→ The cover cannot settle, and the customer payment stalls\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"pacs.009 is the requirement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCOV and ADV are not separate messages in the ISO 20022 catalogue. They share one underlying pacs.009 message, applied to a different business use case. CBPR+ sets out Core, Cover and Advise for cross-border payments, while the EPC SEPA schemes do not use pacs.009 at all.\n\nA message name is not a use case.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA requirement that says only that pacs.009 is needed stays incomplete until it also names the use case, the scheme, the usage guideline and the business context.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message name\n→ Business use case\n→ Scheme and usage guideline\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which message do we need?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the use case, not just the message name?\"\n\nHave you seen a build go wrong because the requirement named a message but never named the use case?", "takeaway": "A message name is not a use case. Name the use case, the scheme and the business context.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One pacs.009, three use cases", "parts": [{"label": "Requirement says pacs.009", "note": "Only the message name is given, no use case"}, {"label": "Core: bank-to-bank transfer", "note": "A bank-to-bank transfer of funds"}, {"label": "COV: cover leg", "note": "The cover or funding leg for an underlying customer payment"}, {"label": "ADV: advice of funds movement", "note": "Advice of an expected funds movement"}, {"label": "CBPR+ defines all three", "note": "CBPR+ sets out Core, Cover and Advise for cross-border payments"}, {"label": "SEPA does not use pacs.009", "note": "The EPC SEPA schemes do not use pacs.009 at all"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["pacs.009"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C04", "seq": 173, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The team heard MT was dead, and stopped planning for it", "explain": "The CBPR+ November 2026 deadline is announced as the end of MT for cross-border payments.\n\nThe team stops planning for MT altogether.\n\nThen a EUR 500,000 payment type that still runs on MT after the deadline breaks, because nobody planned for it.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ November 2026 deadline is announced\n→ Team reads it as the end of all MT\n→ Many MT messages do migrate to MX by then\n→ Some were already retired, and others keep running after the deadline for specialised or domestic use\n→ A payment type still on MT is left without a plan\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT ends in November 2026.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSWIFT is driving the migration because MT cannot carry the rich data, structured addresses and granularity that compliance and monitoring need. But the end date is not the same for every message, and that nuance matters in migration planning.\n\nMT is retiring, not retired.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKnow the nuance rather than repeating the slogan. Plan per message, not per deadline.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message inventory\n→ Migration date per message\n→ Specialised and domestic use that continues\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When does MT end?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which messages migrate, and when?\"\n\nHave you seen a migration plan break because the deadline was treated as one date for every message?", "takeaway": "MT is retiring, not retired. The end date is not the same for every message.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT is retiring, not retired", "parts": [{"label": "Many migrate by November 2026", "note": "Many MT messages migrate to MX by the deadline"}, {"label": "Some already retired", "note": "Some messages were already retired before the deadline"}, {"label": "Others keep running after the deadline", "note": "Others keep running for specialised or domestic use"}, {"label": "MT cannot carry rich data", "note": "MT cannot carry the rich data, structured addresses and granularity needed"}, {"label": "Compliance and monitoring need more", "note": "Compliance and monitoring need the richer data that MX carries"}, {"label": "End date differs per message", "note": "The end date is not the same for every message"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C08", "seq": 176, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The MT103 that lost its address in translation", "explain": "An MT103 arrives for a EUR 60,000 payment.\n\nThe address is free text, four lines of 35 characters.\n\nIn MX it must be structured: name, town, country. The mapping drops the town, and Compliance holds the payment.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives with a free-text address\n→ Translator converts field by field to MX\n→ Town and country are not carried across\n→ MX arrives with an incomplete structured address\n→ Compliance holds the EUR 60,000 payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Conversion is a format change.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMT messages are FIN messages written as structured text with character limits, while MX messages are ISO 20022 XML with properly structured data. The translator must also look at the MT structure, correspondent and account information and the business rules to derive the right MX settlement method.\n\nMT to MX is a business mapping exercise, not a format change.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGet the mapping right early, because that is what keeps straight-through processing high. Test the mapping with real addresses, not clean samples.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT structure and limits\n→ MX structured data\n→ Settlement method derivation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the format change?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every MT field lands in the right MX element?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment held because a field was lost in MT to MX translation?", "takeaway": "MT to MX is a business mapping exercise, not a format change. Get the mapping right early.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT to MX: same SWIFT, different data", "parts": [{"label": "MT: structured text, character limits", "note": "MT messages are FIN messages written as structured text with character limits"}, {"label": "MX: structured XML data", "note": "MX messages are ISO 20022 XML with properly structured data"}, {"label": "Translate field by field", "note": "Converting a payment means translating it field by field and message by message"}, {"label": "Derive the settlement method", "note": "Use MT structure, correspondent and account information and business rules"}, {"label": "Migrate payment types to MX", "note": "Banks must migrate certain payment types to MX under CBPR+"}, {"label": "Business mapping, not format change", "note": "Getting the mapping right early keeps straight-through processing high"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-001", "seq": 179, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The Rs. 4,500 card payment that cleared but never settled", "explain": "A customer pays Rs. 4,500 by card.\n\nAuthorisation is approved. Clearing completes.\n\nSettlement breaks, and the ledger does not agree. The customer thinks the payment worked. The money is unaccounted for.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Authorisation: issuer approves the payment at the point of sale\n→ Clearing: network exchanges records between acquirer and issuer\n→ Settlement: funds should move between the banks\n→ Reconciliation: clearing records, settlement positions and ledger postings do not agree\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Authorisation means the money moved.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA card transaction moves through four stages across four parties, and a failure at any stage has a different owner and a different fix. The issuer, the acquirer, the network and the merchant each play a distinct role at each stage.\n\nKnow the stage, and you know who must act.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck each stage in order. Authorisation, clearing, settlement and reconciliation are separate controls, and each needs its own evidence.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorisation\n→ Clearing\n→ Settlement\n→ Reconciliation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the payment approved?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each stage completed, not just the first?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment that looked successful at the till, and failed three stages later?", "takeaway": "Know the stage, and you know who must act. Authorisation is not settlement.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Authorisation", "note": "The issuer approves the payment at the point of sale."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "The network exchanges records between acquirer and issuer."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Funds move between the banks."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Do the records agree?", "note": "Clearing records, settlement positions and ledger postings must agree."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconciliation", "note": "A break is investigated, not written off."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-002", "seq": 180, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Same terminal, same Rs. 10,000, two different kinds of money", "explain": "Two customers each pay Rs. 10,000 at the same terminal.\n\nOne pays by debit card, one by credit card.\n\nThe terminal experience looks identical. The money movement is entirely different.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Debit card: payment draws directly on the customer balance\n→ Funds leave the account at authorisation\n→ Credit card: payment draws on a credit line\n→ Issuer funds the payment, and the customer repays later\n→ Settlement timing, interest treatment and issuer risk all differ\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A card payment is a card payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA debit card moves money the customer already holds, while a credit card moves money the issuer lends. The terminal hides the difference, and the ledger does not.\n\nThe terminal looks identical. The money movement is not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAlways ask which funding source a payment uses, because it decides settlement timing, interest and risk.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Funding source\n→ Settlement timing\n→ Issuer risk\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the card accepted?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which funding source this payment used?\"\n\nHave you seen a card flow designed as if debit and credit were the same thing?", "takeaway": "The terminal looks identical, but the account and settlement implications are entirely different.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Same terminal, different money movement", "parts": [{"label": "Debit card", "note": "Draws on the customer balance; funds leave at authorisation."}, {"label": "Credit card", "note": "Draws on a credit line; the issuer funds it, the customer repays later."}, {"label": "Settlement timing differs", "note": "Timing, interest treatment and issuer risk differ."}, {"label": "Same Rs. 10,000, different money", "note": "Two identical-looking payments move money in two different ways."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-028", "seq": 184, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The Rs. 40 lakh payment sent on the wrong rail", "explain": "A customer needs Rs. 40 lakh to reach a supplier today.\n\nThe payment goes by NEFT, in a batch, because that is the usual rail.\n\nIt arrives in the next cycle. The supplier is not paid today, and the customer is furious.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment is urgent and high value\n→ NEFT is chosen by habit\n→ NEFT settles in batches, and the payment waits\n→ RTGS would have settled it individually in central bank money\n→ IMPS would have settled it instantly, around the clock\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Use the usual rail.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nNEFT, RTGS and IMPS are three domestic rails with different operating models. Compare them on speed, availability, settlement model and customer use case, and use the current scheme rules rather than an old summary.\n\nThe rail is chosen by the payment's need, not by habit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute by need: batch and can wait, individual and final, or instant and around the clock. Check the current scheme rules before choosing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Speed the payment needs\n→ Availability the payment needs\n→ Settlement model that fits\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which rail do we usually use?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the rail matches the payment's need?\"\n\nHave you seen an urgent payment miss its day because it went on the wrong rail?", "takeaway": "The rail is chosen by the payment's need, not by habit.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three rails, three settlement models", "parts": [{"label": "NEFT", "note": "Settles in batches, for payments that can wait."}, {"label": "RTGS", "note": "Settles each payment individually in central bank money."}, {"label": "IMPS", "note": "Settles instantly around the clock."}, {"label": "Rs. 40 lakh, needed today", "note": "An urgent high value payment sent on the batch rail arrives late."}, {"label": "How to compare", "note": "Speed, availability, settlement model and use case."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-027", "seq": 185, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The Rs. 30 lakh loan that stalled between approval and money", "explain": "A customer is approved for a Rs. 30 lakh loan.\n\nThe money does not arrive.\n\nNobody can say where it stopped, because the failure sits at a later stage than the approval.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Application collects the request and supporting information\n→ Decision assesses credit and affordability\n→ Agreement records the terms\n→ Disbursement should release the funds\n→ Servicing collects repayments and manages changes\n→ Closure confirms the balance is settled\n→ A weak link at one stage shows up downstream\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Approval means the money is on its way.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nEach stage has its own data, controls and hand-offs, so a failure at one stage does not always show up until a later stage.\n\nA loan is a chain, and a weak link anywhere shows up downstream.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the chain stage by stage. Do not assume the approval was the problem.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Decision and agreement\n→ Disbursement\n→ Servicing and closure\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the loan approved?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every stage handed off cleanly?\"\n\nHave you seen a loan stall between approval and disbursement, with nobody able to say where it stopped?", "takeaway": "A loan is a chain, and a weak link anywhere shows up downstream.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Application", "note": "The bank collects the request and supporting information."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Decision", "note": "Credit and affordability are assessed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Agreement", "note": "The terms are recorded."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Disbursement", "note": "The funds are released."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Did the money reach the customer?", "note": "A failure at one stage does not always show up until a later stage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Servicing and closure", "note": "Repayments are collected, and the balance is confirmed settled."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "INS-090", "seq": 187, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Tomorrow, the customer was told, and the money came in two days", "explain": "A customer sends Rs. 2 lakh and is told the money will arrive tomorrow.\n\nIt arrives in two days.\n\nThe complaint was avoidable. The timing quoted was never the scheme's timing.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment is made\n→ Customer is told it arrives tomorrow\n→ Scheme settles in two days for this product\n→ Customer waits, then complains\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"T+1 is the timing.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA settlement cycle is the period after which funds move between banks, and T+1 or T+2 are only examples of how long that can take. The actual timing depends on the scheme and the product.\n\nTell the customer the real timing for that product.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCustomer communication must state the real timing for that product, because telling a customer the money will arrive tomorrow when the scheme settles in two days creates a complaint that was avoidable.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme rule\n→ Product rule\n→ Customer message\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the usual timing?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the timing we quoted matches the scheme rule?\"\n\nHave you seen a complaint created by a timing promise the scheme could never keep?", "takeaway": "Customer communication must state the real timing for that product, not a generic T+1.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Settlement timing: use the product rule", "parts": [{"label": "Settlement cycle", "note": "The period after which funds move between banks."}, {"label": "T+1 or T+2", "note": "Only examples of how long a cycle can take."}, {"label": "Scheme and product rules", "note": "Actual timing depends on these, not a generic label."}, {"label": "Customer told tomorrow", "note": "The customer was told tomorrow, and the money came in two days."}, {"label": "Customer communication", "note": "Must state the real timing for that product."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-069", "seq": 188, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 20,000 payment that could be taken but never refunded", "explain": "A customer pays EUR 20,000 through a new checkout.\n\nThe payment goes through. Then the customer asks for a refund.\n\nThere is no refund API. There is no reversal API. The money is stuck.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Checkout API initiates the payment\n→ Authentication API proves who is paying\n→ Status API reports where the payment stands\n→ Refund API does not exist\n→ Reversal API does not exist\n→ A payment that can be taken cannot be refunded or checked\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Build the payment API first.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment product exposes several APIs across the lifecycle, not just one for taking payment. Map each touchpoint to the lifecycle stage it serves, or the product ends up with a payment that can be taken but cannot be refunded or checked.\n\nCoverage of the whole lifecycle is what makes a payment product complete.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCover initiate, authenticate, check status, refund and reverse before launch. A missing lifecycle stage is a customer problem, not a backlog item.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiate and authenticate\n→ Status\n→ Refund and reversal\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we take the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every lifecycle stage has an API?\"\n\nHave you seen a product that could take money but had no way to give it back?", "takeaway": "Coverage of the whole lifecycle is what makes a payment product complete.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Payment product APIs across the lifecycle", "parts": [{"label": "Checkout API", "note": "Initiates the payment."}, {"label": "Authentication API", "note": "Proves who is paying."}, {"label": "Status API", "note": "Reports where a payment stands."}, {"label": "Refund API", "note": "Returns money for a completed payment. Missing here."}, {"label": "Reversal API", "note": "Cancels a payment that should not have completed. Missing here."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "INS-088", "seq": 189, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Refund or reversal: the Rs. 8,000 that was posted twice", "explain": "A Rs. 8,000 payment should never have completed.\n\nThe team issues a refund.\n\nA new credit posts to the customer, while the original posting still stands. The customer is credited twice.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment should not have completed\n→ Team calls it a refund\n→ Refund posts as a new credit to the customer\n→ Original posting is never unwound\n→ Customer is credited twice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Refund and reversal are the same thing.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA refund returns money for a completed payment and posts as a new credit, while a reversal cancels a payment that should not have completed and unwinds the original posting. Validate the rail and provider semantics before building either one, because the same word can mean different things on different rails.\n\nBuild the ledger effect first, and the name will follow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDecide the ledger effect before the name. A refund creates a credit, a reversal unwinds the posting.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Trigger for the action\n→ Timing of the action\n→ Ledger effect\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What do we call it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the ledger effect matches the word we used?\"\n\nHave you seen a refund and a reversal confused, and the ledger pay for it?", "takeaway": "Build the ledger effect first, and the name will follow. A refund creates a credit, a reversal unwinds the posting.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Refund creates a credit, reversal unwinds the posting", "parts": [{"label": "Refund", "note": "Returns money for a completed payment, a new credit to the customer."}, {"label": "Reversal", "note": "Cancels a payment that should not have completed, unwinds the posting."}, {"label": "Shared link", "note": "Both are linked to the original transaction."}, {"label": "Rs. 8,000 posted twice", "note": "A refund was used where a reversal was needed, and the original posting still stood."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-012", "seq": 190, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The Rs. 50,000 payment failed, and three parties pointed at each other", "explain": "A Rs. 50,000 card payment fails.\n\nThe gateway says it is the bank. The bank says it is the merchant. The merchant says it is the gateway.\n\nThe customer waits while three parties argue about ownership.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Gateway orchestrates the instruction\n→ Bank authorises and processes the payment\n→ Merchant is notified of the outcome\n→ Payment fails\n→ Nobody owns the fix, and nobody tells the customer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Someone will handle the failure.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe gateway, the bank and the merchant each own a distinct part. When a payment fails, knowing which party owns which step decides who must fix it and who must tell the customer.\n\nUnclear ownership turns every failure into a dispute between parties.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake the boundary between gateway, bank and merchant explicit in the design, for the happy path and for every failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gateway ownership\n→ Bank ownership\n→ Merchant ownership\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Who built this step?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who owns each step when it fails?\"\n\nHave you seen a failed payment turn into a dispute because nobody owned the step that broke?", "takeaway": "Unclear ownership turns every failure into a dispute between parties.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Gateway, bank and merchant ownership", "parts": [{"label": "Gateway", "note": "Orchestrates the instruction and returns the outcome."}, {"label": "Bank", "note": "Authorises and processes the payment and moves the funds."}, {"label": "Merchant", "note": "Is notified of the outcome and must act on it."}, {"label": "Failure with no owner", "note": "A Rs. 50,000 payment fails, and three parties point at each other."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C022", "seq": 244, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 90,000 cover, and two different ordering customers", "explain": "A EUR 90,000 cover payment arrived.\nThe MT103 named one ordering customer.\nThe MT202 COV named a different one.\nThe operations team spent a day arguing over who had sent the payment.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 names the instructing customer\n→ MT202 COV names the funding bank\n→ Team treats them as one party\n→ Screening and reporting land on the wrong party\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One payment, one ordering customer.\"\nEach message has its own ordering party for its own leg.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe MT103 names the customer who instructed the payment, while the MT202 COV names the bank moving the funds. Treating them as one party confuses the screening, the reporting and the audit trail.\n\nEach message has its own ordering party for its own leg.\n\nWhat proves which party counts for which leg?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the ordering party per message, per leg. Screen the MT103 party as the instructing customer and the MT202 COV party as the funding bank, and never merge the two.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Ordering party on the MT103\n→ Ordering party on the MT202 COV\n→ Screening per leg\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who sent this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which party counts for which leg?\"\n\nHave you ever seen two messages name two different ordering customers?", "takeaway": "Each message has its own ordering party for its own leg.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The ordering customer in a EUR 90,000 cover payment", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 names the instructing customer", "note": "The MT103 names the customer who instructed the payment"}, {"label": "MT202 COV names the funding bank", "note": "The MT202 COV names the bank moving the funds"}, {"label": "Each message has its own ordering party", "note": "Each leg has its own ordering party"}, {"label": "Treating them as one party confuses", "note": "Merging the two parties is a common source of confusion"}, {"label": "Screen each leg separately", "note": "Screening must land on the right party per leg."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C023", "seq": 245, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 75,000 cover, and field 72 sent the funds to the wrong place", "explain": "A EUR 75,000 cover payment was sent with a free-text instruction in field 72.\nThe next bank read it the wrong way.\nThe funds went to the wrong place, and settlement stopped.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT202 COV sent with field 72 filled\n→ Instruction written loosely, in free text\n→ Next bank acts on the wrong reading\n→ Funds sent astray, settlement delayed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Field 72 is free text, so any wording will do.\"\nFree text is exactly why discipline matters.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 72 carries sender-to-receiver information, and in a cover payment it tells the next bank what the cover is for and what to do with it. A wrong instruction can send the funds to the wrong place or delay settlement.\n\nPut only the instruction the next bank needs, in the form the next bank expects.\n\nWhat proves the next bank will read this instruction the way we meant it?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut only the instruction the next bank needs, in the form the next bank expects. Nothing extra, nothing vague. Check the wording against what the receiving bank expects before sending.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction the next bank needs\n→ Form the next bank expects\n→ Nothing extra, nothing vague\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was field 72 filled in?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the next bank will read this instruction the way we meant it?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a free-text field send funds astray?", "takeaway": "Put only the instruction the next bank needs, in the form the next bank expects.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 72 in a EUR 75,000 cover payment", "parts": [{"label": "Sender-to-receiver information", "note": "Field 72 carries sender-to-receiver information"}, {"label": "Tells the next bank what the cover is for", "note": "It tells the next bank what the cover is for and what to do"}, {"label": "Free text, so discipline matters", "note": "Free text means a wrong instruction can send funds astray"}, {"label": "Only the instruction the next bank needs", "note": "Put only what the next bank needs, in the form it expects"}, {"label": "Check the wording before sending", "note": "A vague instruction is a settlement risk."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C024", "seq": 246, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 100,000 sent, USD 99,850 received, and no one could explain the gap", "explain": "A customer sent USD 100,000 by serial payment.\nThe beneficiary was credited USD 99,850.\nUSD 150 had vanished, and no one at the bank could explain where it went.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Ordering customer sends USD 100,000\n→ First intermediary deducts its charge\n→ Second intermediary deducts its charge\n→ Beneficiary credited USD 99,850\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary receives what the sender sent.\"\nIn a serial payment, every intermediary deducts its own charge on the way.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe ordering customer bears the charges, and each intermediary bank deducts its own charge from the amount as the payment passes through. The beneficiary therefore receives the amount minus the charges deducted at each step.\n\nIn a serial payment, every deduction in the chain lands on the beneficiary.\n\nWhat proves each deduction in the chain was agreed and expected?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPrice a serial payment by accounting for every deduction in the chain, not just the first one. Show the customer the expected credit before sending, and reconcile the actual credit against every charge taken.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount sent\n→ Each intermediary charge\n→ Final credit\n→ Customer told the expected credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the beneficiary get the full amount?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each deduction in the chain was agreed and expected?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain a short credit to a customer who expected the full amount?", "takeaway": "In a serial payment, every deduction in the chain lands on the beneficiary.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Ordering customer sends USD 100,000", "note": "The ordering customer bears the charges"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each intermediary deducts its charge", "note": "Each intermediary takes its own charge as the payment passes"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check each deduction is expected", "note": "Every deduction must be agreed and traceable"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary receives USD 99,850", "note": "The credit is the amount sent minus every deduction"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconcile the credit against the charges", "note": "Pricing must cover every deduction in the chain"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C026", "seq": 248, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 100,000, and the charges were counted twice", "explain": "A USD 100,000 payment mixed serial and cover legs.\nCharges were deducted on the serial leg, then charged again on the cover leg.\nThe beneficiary was short, and the customer was charged twice for the same journey.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Serial leg deducts charges step by step\n→ Cover leg handles its charges separately\n→ Payment mixes the two methods\n→ Beneficiary short, customer double-charged\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Charges work the same way in every payment.\"\nCharge arithmetic follows the payment method.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a serial payment charges are deducted step by step as the payment passes through each intermediary, while in a cover payment the charges on the information leg and the funds leg are handled separately. A payment that mixes the two methods without care can double-charge or under-charge.\n\nKnow which method applies before working out what the beneficiary will receive.\n\nWhat proves the charges were counted once, under the right method?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nIdentify the method first, serial or cover, then apply its charge arithmetic. Never mix the two without working out where each charge lands.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payment method\n→ Charges on the serial leg\n→ Charges on the cover leg\n→ Final credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Were the charges calculated correctly?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the charges were counted once, under the right method?\"\n\nHave you ever found a payment where the charges were counted twice?", "takeaway": "Know which method applies before working out what the beneficiary will receive.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Charges in a USD 100,000 serial and cover payment", "parts": [{"label": "Serial payment", "note": "Charges deducted step by step at each intermediary"}, {"label": "Cover payment", "note": "Information leg and funds leg charges handled separately"}, {"label": "Mixed methods", "note": "Mixing the two can double-charge or under-charge"}, {"label": "Identify the method first", "note": "Know which method applies before calculating."}, {"label": "Reconcile the final credit", "note": "The credit must match the method's arithmetic."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C033", "seq": 255, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 25,000 stuck, and the team sent a camt.056 when they needed a camt.026", "explain": "EUR 25,000 could not be applied by the receiving bank.\nThe team sent a camt.056 to recall it.\nNothing happened, because a recall asks for money back, and this payment needed a report that it could not be applied.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment cannot be applied\n→ Team sends camt.056 recall\n→ No funds to recall, nothing happens\n→ Correct message, camt.026, never sent\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Both messages are about a payment that did not complete, so either will do.\"\nThey do different jobs.\n\nWhy?\n\nA camt.056 requests a recall of a payment, asking the beneficiary bank to return funds that have already been credited. A camt.026 reports that a payment cannot be applied, telling the sender that the instruction could not be processed.\n\nOne asks for money back. The other reports it could not be applied.\n\nWhat proves we sent the message that matches what actually happened?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk first what happened: were funds credited and need returning, or could the instruction not be applied at all? Send camt.056 for the first, camt.026 for the second, and never swap them.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What actually happened\n→ Funds credited or not\n→ Message that matches\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which message do we send?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we sent the message that matches what actually happened?\"\n\nHave you ever sent the wrong message for a payment that could not be applied?", "takeaway": "One asks for money back. The other reports it could not be applied.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "camt.026 versus camt.056 on EUR 25,000", "parts": [{"label": "camt.026", "note": "Reports a payment that cannot be applied"}, {"label": "camt.056", "note": "Requests recall of funds already credited"}, {"label": "Different jobs", "note": "One asks for money back, the other reports it could not be applied"}, {"label": "Ask what happened first", "note": "Credited funds or an instruction that could not be applied."}, {"label": "Never swap them", "note": "The wrong message gets no answer."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.026", "camt.056"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C039", "seq": 261, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 65,000 MT103 arrived with field 57 empty", "explain": "An MT103 for EUR 65,000 arrived with field 57 empty.\nIt named no beneficiary bank.\nThe receiving bank held it, unsure whether the payment was broken or meant to be handled in house.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives with field 57 empty\n→ No beneficiary bank named\n→ Receiving bank works out the route from the other fields\n→ Payment held while the team decides\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An empty field is an error.\"\nThe empty field is not necessarily an error.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn empty field 57 can mean the payment is to be handled by the receiving bank itself, or that the beneficiary bank is identified elsewhere in the chain. The analyst must read the whole message before deciding whether the empty field is a gap or a deliberate omission.\n\nRead the whole message before deciding whether the empty field is a gap or a deliberate omission.\n\nWhat proves this empty field is deliberate, not a gap?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the whole MT103 before judging field 57. Check the other fields for the beneficiary bank, and only raise an exception when the message as a whole names no route.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Whole message read\n→ Beneficiary bank found elsewhere or not\n→ Route decided on the full message\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is field 57 empty?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this empty field is deliberate, not a gap?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment over an empty field that turned out to be deliberate?", "takeaway": "Read the whole message before deciding whether the empty field is a gap or a deliberate omission.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "An MT103 with field 57 empty", "parts": [{"label": "No beneficiary bank named", "note": "The message names no beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "Receiving bank works out the route", "note": "The receiving bank must work out the route from the other fields"}, {"label": "Not necessarily an error", "note": "It can mean the receiving bank handles it, or the bank is named elsewhere"}, {"label": "Read the whole message first", "note": "Read the whole message before judging the empty field"}, {"label": "Raise an exception only on the full picture", "note": "Judge the message, not one field."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C040", "seq": 262, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Parent paid EUR 300,000 for three subsidiaries, and the screening landed on the wrong party", "explain": "A parent company paid EUR 300,000 for three subsidiaries with one MT101.\nThe bank reported all three payments under the parent.\nScreening and accounting landed on the wrong party for every one of them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Parent instructs with an MT101\n→ General instruction sequence carried\n→ Subsidiary named per transaction\n→ Bank reports under the parent, not the subsidiary\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The sender is the party that matters.\"\nThe subsidiary is the ordering customer for its own transaction.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe MT101 carries a general instruction sequence and a per-transaction sequence, and the subsidiary appears as the ordering customer in the per-transaction sequence. The parent's bank must then report each transaction under the correct subsidiary, so the accounting and the screening both land on the right party.\n\nReport each transaction under the correct subsidiary, so the accounting and the screening both land on the right party.\n\nWhat proves each transaction was reported under the right subsidiary?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the subsidiary as the ordering customer in the per-transaction sequence. Report, screen and account for each transaction under that subsidiary, not under the parent.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Ordering customer per transaction\n→ Reporting per subsidiary\n→ Screening per subsidiary\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who sent this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each transaction was reported under the right subsidiary?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a group payment reported under the wrong company?", "takeaway": "Report each transaction under the correct subsidiary, so the accounting and the screening both land on the right party.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Parent instructs with an MT101", "note": "A parent company pays on behalf of its subsidiaries using an MT101"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "General instruction sequence carried", "note": "The MT101 carries a general instruction sequence"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Subsidiary named per transaction", "note": "The subsidiary appears as ordering customer in the per-transaction sequence"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank reports under the right subsidiary", "note": "Each transaction is reported under the correct subsidiary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Screening and accounting land correctly", "note": "Accounting and screening both land on the right party"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C055", "seq": 264, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 120,000 credited twice because a notification was sent as an MT103", "explain": "EUR 120,000 was credited to a customer.\nThe bank then sent an MT103 to report the credit.\nThe receiving bank read it as an instruction to pay, and debited the account a second time.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Credit already made to the customer\n→ Bank sends an MT103 to report it\n→ Receiving bank reads it as an instruction to pay\n→ Account debited a second time\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An MT103 can report a credit.\"\nAn MT103 instructs a payment. It is not a notification.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 instructs the receiving bank to debit the sender's account and pay the beneficiary, so it is an instruction to move money. When a bank only wants to say that an account has already been credited, it uses an MT910 confirmation of credit or an MT950 or MT940 statement instead.\n\nAn instruction is not a notification. Sending one as the other moves money twice.\n\nWhat proves this message instructs a payment, rather than reporting one?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse an MT103 only to instruct a payment. Report a credit already made with an MT910, an MT950 or an MT940, and never send an instruction where a notification belongs.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message purpose\n→ Instruction or notification\n→ Correct message chosen\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the credit reported?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this message instructs a payment, rather than reporting one?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a notification move money because it used the wrong message?", "takeaway": "An instruction is not a notification. Sending one as the other moves money twice.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A EUR 120,000 credit notification is not an MT103", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 instructs a payment", "note": "An MT103 instructs the receiving bank to debit the sender and pay the beneficiary"}, {"label": "MT910 confirms a credit", "note": "A bank reporting a credit already made uses an MT910"}, {"label": "MT950 or MT940 statement instead", "note": "An MT950 or MT940 statement can also report a credit already made"}, {"label": "Instruction confused with notification", "note": "Sending an MT103 for a credit already made may debit the wrong account"}, {"label": "Reverse the second debit", "note": "A double debit must be fixed, not explained away."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT910", "MT950", "MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C056", "seq": 265, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 75,000 MT103, and the intermediary was named in the wrong field", "explain": "A USD 75,000 MT103 travelled through an intermediary bank.\nThe intermediary was named in field 55 instead of field 56.\nThe funds went for reimbursement through a bank that was never meant to handle them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 names the intermediary in field 55\n→ Field 56 left empty\n→ Funds routed as a reimbursement, not a forward\n→ Payment goes astray\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Fields 55 and 56 both name banks in the middle, so either will do.\"\nField 56 names the intermediary. Field 55 names a third reimbursement institution.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 56 names the intermediary institution, the bank that receives the payment from the sender and forwards it to the account with institution in field 57. Field 55 names a third reimbursement institution, used when the funds are reimbursed through a bank that is neither the sender nor the receiver.\n\nField 56 names the intermediary. Field 55 names the reimbursement bank. Swap them and the funds go astray.\n\nWhat proves the intermediary is named in field 56, not field 55?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the intermediary in field 56A when a serial chain gains an intermediary. Use field 55 only for a third reimbursement institution, and leave field 56 out entirely when the receiver is its own correspondent.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 56 intermediary\n→ Field 55 reimbursement\n→ Field 57 account with institution\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is a bank named in the middle?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the intermediary is named in field 56, not field 55?\"\n\nHave you ever seen fields 55 and 56 swapped on an MT103?", "takeaway": "Field 56 names the intermediary. Field 55 names the reimbursement bank. Swap them and the funds go astray.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fields 55 and 56 in a USD 75,000 MT103", "parts": [{"label": "Field 56: intermediary institution", "note": "Field 56 names the intermediary that forwards the payment to field 57"}, {"label": "Field 55: third reimbursement institution", "note": "Field 55 names a third reimbursement institution for reimbursement elsewhere"}, {"label": "Field 56A added with an intermediary", "note": "When a serial chain gains an intermediary, field 56A is added"}, {"label": "No field 56 if receiver is own correspondent", "note": "When the receiver is its own correspondent, field 56 is not needed"}, {"label": "Swap them and the funds go astray", "note": "The two fields do different jobs."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C057", "seq": 266, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 90,000 debited, and the customer demanded proof that never came", "explain": "EUR 90,000 was debited on an MT103 instruction.\nThe customer asked for proof of the debit.\nNo MT900 ever arrived, and the customer assumed the payment had failed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank debits the account on the MT103\n→ No MT900 confirmation sent\n→ Customer sees the debit but no proof\n→ Customer assumes the payment failed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every debit is confirmed with an MT900.\"\nWhether one is sent depends on the agreement between the two banks.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a bank debits an account on the instruction of an MT103, it can confirm the debit with an MT900, a confirmation of debit. The MT900 is a separate message, not part of the MT103 itself, and a payment can therefore settle correctly without any MT900 being exchanged.\n\nA payment can settle correctly without any MT900 being exchanged.\n\nWhat proves this payment settled, with or without an MT900?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nProve settlement from the account entries and the MT103, not from the presence of an MT900. Tell the customer upfront whether an MT900 is part of the agreement, so silence is not read as failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Debit posted\n→ MT103 instruction\n→ MT900 agreement\n→ Settlement proven from the entries\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was an MT900 sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this payment settled, with or without an MT900?\"\n\nHave you ever had a customer read silence as a failed payment?", "takeaway": "A payment can settle correctly without any MT900 being exchanged.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT900: confirmation of a EUR 90,000 debit", "parts": [{"label": "Confirms a debit from an MT103", "note": "A bank debiting on an MT103 instruction can confirm it with an MT900"}, {"label": "Separate message, not part of MT103", "note": "The MT900 is a separate message, not part of the MT103"}, {"label": "Sent only by agreement", "note": "Whether one is sent depends on the agreement between the banks"}, {"label": "Payment can settle without an MT900", "note": "A payment can settle correctly with no MT900 exchanged"}, {"label": "Prove settlement from the entries", "note": "The account entries settle the question."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT900", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C063", "seq": 271, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 175,000 with no SSI on file, and no one knew where to send it", "explain": "USD 175,000 had to be paid to a beneficiary bank.\nThe sending bank had no standing settlement instruction on file for that bank.\nNo one knew which correspondent to send the funds to, and the payment waited.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment due to the beneficiary bank\n→ No SSI on file for that bank in USD\n→ Correspondent unknown\n→ Payment held while the route is found\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The payment engine will know where to send it.\"\nWithout an SSI, the engine has nothing to build the payment from.\n\nWhy?\n\nBanks tell each other how to send them funds in each currency through standing settlement instructions, exchanged as MT670 and MT671 messages and stored in payment engine databases. An SSI names the correspondent of the receiver and any intermediaries on the route, so the sending bank can build the MT103 and the cover from the database instead of asking every time.\n\nWithout an SSI, the sender must find the correspondent before the payment can move.\n\nWhat proves we know the receiver's correspondent for this currency?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep SSIs current in the payment engine database. When one is missing, find the correspondent through SWIFT services or by asking the receiver directly, and never guess the route.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ SSI on file\n→ Correspondent named\n→ Intermediaries on the route\n→ Payment built from the SSI\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we send this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we know the receiver's correspondent for this currency?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment because no SSI was on file?", "takeaway": "Without an SSI, the sender must find the correspondent before the payment can move.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Standing settlement instructions for USD 175,000", "parts": [{"label": "Exchanged as MT670 and MT671", "note": "SSIs are exchanged as MT670 and MT671 and stored in payment engine databases"}, {"label": "Names the receiver's correspondent", "note": "An SSI names the correspondent and any intermediaries on the route"}, {"label": "Sending bank builds from the database", "note": "The sending bank builds the MT103 and the cover from the database"}, {"label": "Unknown correspondent must be found out", "note": "An unknown correspondent must be found via SWIFT services or by asking"}, {"label": "Never guess the route", "note": "A guessed correspondent sends funds astray."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT670", "MT671", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C065", "seq": 273, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 80,000 sent to the wrong bank because field 57a was read the same way twice", "explain": "EUR 80,000 was sent in a cover payment.\nThe team read field 57a the way it works in a serial MT103.\nThe funds went to the beneficiary bank's correspondent instead of the beneficiary bank.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Serial MT103 read first, field 57a noted\n→ MT202 COV read the same way\n→ Field 57a points at a different bank in each leg\n→ Funds sent to the wrong bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Field 57a always means the same bank.\"\nThe same field number points at a different bank in each leg.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a serial MT103, field 57a names the bank that holds the beneficiary's account, and field 56 names the intermediary that forwards the payment to it. In an MT202 COV the roles shift: field 58a names the beneficiary bank, while field 57a names the correspondent bank of the beneficiary bank.\n\nThe same field number points at a different bank in each leg.\n\nWhat proves we read field 57a for the leg we are actually in?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead field 57a per message: in a serial MT103 it is the bank holding the beneficiary's account, in an MT202 COV it is the beneficiary bank's correspondent. Check field 58a for the beneficiary bank on the cover leg.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 57a in the MT103\n→ Field 57a in the MT202 COV\n→ Field 58a in the MT202 COV\n→ Field 56 in the MT103\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which bank does field 57a name?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we read field 57a for the leg we are actually in?\"\n\nHave you ever sent funds to the wrong bank by reading a field the same way twice?", "takeaway": "The same field number points at a different bank in each leg.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 57a in a EUR 80,000 MT103 and MT202 COV", "parts": [{"label": "Serial MT103: bank holding the account", "note": "In a serial MT103, field 57a names the bank holding the beneficiary's account"}, {"label": "Serial MT103: field 56 is the intermediary", "note": "In a serial MT103, field 56 names the intermediary that forwards the payment"}, {"label": "MT202 COV: field 58a is the beneficiary bank", "note": "In an MT202 COV, field 58a names the beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "MT202 COV: field 57a is the correspondent", "note": "In an MT202 COV, field 57a names the correspondent of the beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "Reading 57a the same way is an error", "note": "The same field number points at a different bank in each leg"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C066", "seq": 274, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 140,000 cover treated as a different kind of payment", "explain": "A EUR 140,000 cover payment arrived.\nThe team treated it as a different kind of movement from a serial payment.\nThey built separate handling for it, and the cover leg stalled while they worked out rules that already existed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT202 COV arrives\n→ Team treats it as a new kind of payment\n→ Separate handling built from scratch\n→ Cover leg stalls\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A cover is a different kind of movement.\"\nThe cover is the same serial mechanism carrying the funds leg.\n\nWhy?\n\nLooked at on its own, an MT202 COV is a serial payment: the sender instructs the receiver to debit the sender's account and credit the next bank in the chain. Each bank in the cover chain debits the previous bank's account and instructs the next bank, until the funds reach the beneficiary bank.\n\nThe cover is the same serial mechanism carrying the funds leg of a customer payment.\n\nWhat proves we handled the cover leg with the rules we already have?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHandle an MT202 COV as a serial payment: debit the sender's account, credit the next bank, instruct the next bank in the chain. Apply the serial rules you already have, instead of inventing new ones.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sender debited\n→ Next bank credited\n→ Next bank instructed\n→ Funds reach the beneficiary bank\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What kind of payment is a cover?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we handled the cover leg with the rules we already have?\"\n\nHave you ever built new handling for a cover that the serial rules already covered?", "takeaway": "The cover is the same serial mechanism carrying the funds leg of a customer payment.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Sender instructs receiver to debit its account", "note": "The sender instructs the receiver to debit the sender's account and credit the next bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each bank debits the previous bank", "note": "Each bank in the cover chain debits the previous bank's account"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Same serial rules or new rules", "note": "The cover is the same serial mechanism, not a new kind of movement"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each bank instructs the next bank", "note": "Each bank instructs the next bank in the chain"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds reach the beneficiary bank", "note": "The funds reach the beneficiary bank through the same serial mechanism"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C069", "seq": 275, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 1,200 in charges taken from a EUR 200,000 cover", "explain": "A EUR 200,000 cover payment arrived EUR 1,200 short.\nAn intermediary had taken its charges straight from the cover.\nAn MT191 then arrived, claiming the same charges again.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Cover arrives EUR 1,200 short\n→ Intermediary took charges from the cover\n→ MT191 arrives claiming the same charges\n→ Customer charged twice for one leg\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Charges come out of the funds in transit.\"\nCharges must not be deducted from a cover payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen one bank owes another bank charges or interest on a payment, the claim travels in an MT191, a request for payment of charges, and the advice travels in an MT190. These messages exist because charges must not be deducted from a cover payment, so an intermediary that is owed money asks for it separately instead of taking it from the funds.\n\nAn MT191 in a chain is the charges leg, not the payment itself.\n\nWhat proves the charges were claimed separately, not taken from the cover?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClaim charges with an MT191 and advise them with an MT190. Never take them from the cover, and check that a charge claimed by MT191 was not also deducted from the funds.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover amount in full\n→ MT191 claim\n→ MT190 advice\n→ No double charge\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Were the charges taken from the cover?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the charges were claimed separately, not taken from the cover?\"\n\nHave you ever found the same charges claimed twice, once from the cover and once by MT191?", "takeaway": "An MT191 in a chain is the charges leg, not the payment itself.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT190 and MT191 move EUR 1,200 in charges", "parts": [{"label": "MT191: request for payment of charges", "note": "When one bank owes another charges or interest, the claim travels in an MT191"}, {"label": "MT190: advice of charges", "note": "The advice of charges or interest travels in an MT190"}, {"label": "Charges must not come from the cover", "note": "Charges must not come from the cover, so an intermediary asks separately"}, {"label": "MT191 is the charges leg, not the payment", "note": "An MT191 in a chain is the charges leg, not the payment itself"}, {"label": "Check for a double charge", "note": "A charge taken from the cover and claimed by MT191 is charged twice."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT190", "MT191"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C070", "seq": 276, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 60,000 sent in dollars, credited short after a double conversion", "explain": "USD 60,000 was sent to a United States dollar account.\nThe beneficiary was credited short.\nAn intermediary had converted the payment twice on the way, and no one had asked for a conversion.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sender sends USD 60,000\n→ Intermediary converts the payment\n→ Second intermediary converts it again\n→ Beneficiary credited short in dollars\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Intermediaries can convert as they pass the payment on.\"\nNo intermediary may change the currency.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe sender decides the currency of a transaction, and no intermediary may change it on the way. If the beneficiary's account is in a different currency, the conversion happens as a separate foreign exchange step, and the beneficiary is credited in the currency of the account.\n\nA payment sent in dollars to a dollar account that arrives short after a double conversion has gone wrong.\n\nWhat proves no intermediary changed the currency on the way?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPass the currency unchanged through every intermediary. Convert only as a separate foreign exchange step when the beneficiary's account is in another currency, and challenge any bank in the chain when a same-currency payment arrives short.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Currency sent\n→ Currency at each intermediary\n→ Separate FX step if needed\n→ Currency credited\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the currency changed on the way?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves no intermediary changed the currency on the way?\"\n\nHave you ever had to challenge a bank over a same-currency payment that arrived short?", "takeaway": "A payment sent in dollars to a dollar account that arrives short after a double conversion has gone wrong.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Sender decides the currency", "note": "The sender decides the currency of a transaction"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediaries pass the currency unchanged", "note": "No intermediary may change the currency on the way"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Conversion is a separate FX step", "note": "If the account is in another currency, conversion is a separate foreign exchange step"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary credited in account currency", "note": "The beneficiary is credited in the currency of the account"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Payment arriving short is wrong", "note": "A payment arriving short after a double conversion has gone wrong"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C074", "seq": 278, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 100,000, and the beneficiary received less than the sender instructed", "explain": "A sender instructed USD 100,000 with field 71A as BEN.\nThe beneficiary was credited as if the instruction had been SHA.\nThe beneficiary received less than the sender had instructed, and the bank copy showed a different code from the sender's instruction.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sender instructs 71A BEN\n→ Each bank deducts its charge\n→ Beneficiary bears every charge\n→ Bank copy shows SHA, beneficiary credited differently\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The bank copy is just a copy.\"\nA bank copy that shows SHA where the sender instructed BEN is a discrepancy worth querying.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen field 71A carries BEN, every charge in the chain is borne by the beneficiary, and each bank deducts its charge from the amount as the payment passes through. The sender does not prepay the downstream charges, because the beneficiary is the one who pays them.\n\nA bank copy that shows SHA where the sender instructed BEN is a discrepancy worth querying.\n\nWhat proves the charge code on the bank copy matches the sender's instruction?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck field 71A on the bank copy against the sender's instruction before the payment goes out. Query any copy that shows SHA where BEN was instructed, because the two codes give the beneficiary a different amount.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ 71A on the instruction\n→ 71A on the bank copy\n→ Charges deducted\n→ Beneficiary credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the code on the copy matter?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the charge code on the bank copy matches the sender's instruction?\"\n\nHave you ever caught a charge code changed between the instruction and the bank copy?", "takeaway": "A bank copy that shows SHA where the sender instructed BEN is a discrepancy worth querying.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "71A BEN: beneficiary pays all charges on USD 100,000", "parts": [{"label": "Every charge borne by the beneficiary", "note": "When field 71A carries BEN, every charge is borne by the beneficiary"}, {"label": "Each bank deducts its charge", "note": "Each bank deducts its charge as the payment passes through"}, {"label": "Sender does not prepay downstream charges", "note": "The sender does not prepay downstream charges, the beneficiary pays them"}, {"label": "SHA shown where BEN was instructed", "note": "A bank copy showing SHA where BEN was instructed is a discrepancy worth querying"}, {"label": "Query the discrepancy", "note": "Two codes give the beneficiary a different amount."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C078", "seq": 280, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 55,000 MT103 with a BIC stuffed into field 57B", "explain": "A EUR 55,000 MT103 arrived with field 57B carrying a BIC.\nThe receiving system could not route it straight through.\nThe payment stopped for manual handling, because a BIC must never be passed in option B.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Field 57B carries a BIC\n→ Receiving system cannot straight-through process it\n→ Payment stops for manual handling\n→ Correct option, A, never used\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The BIC is in the field, so the bank is identified.\"\nOption B carries a party identifier, generally an account number, with the location of the account.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 57 offers four options, and option A, with a BIC, is the one that allows straight-through processing. Option D needs no routing code at all and can simply carry the bank's name and address, while the /C/ or /D/ code before an account number is part of the standard but almost never used in practice.\n\nOption A is the default whenever a BIC is available.\n\nWhat proves the right option was used for the identifier we had?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse option A whenever a BIC is available. Use option B only for a party identifier such as an account number with its location, and option D for a name and address with no routing code.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Identifier available\n→ Option chosen\n→ Straight-through processing achieved\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the BIC in the message?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the right option was used for the identifier we had?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an STP payment stop because a BIC was put in the wrong option?", "takeaway": "Option A is the default whenever a BIC is available.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Options B, C and D of field 57 on EUR 55,000", "parts": [{"label": "Option A allows straight-through processing", "note": "Option A, with a BIC, allows straight-through processing"}, {"label": "Option B: party identifier and location", "note": "Option B carries a party identifier, generally an account number, with the location"}, {"label": "No BIC in option B", "note": "A BIC must not be passed in option B; use option A instead"}, {"label": "Option D: name and address only", "note": "Option D needs no routing code and can carry the bank's name and address"}, {"label": "Codes /C/ and /D/ are rarely used", "note": "The /C/ or /D/ code before an account number is standard but almost never used"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C079", "seq": 281, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 80,000 to a US bank, and the clearing account was a guess", "explain": "USD 80,000 was sent to a United States bank.\nField 57A carried only the BIC.\nThe receiving bank had several clearing accounts, and the operations team had to guess which one the payment was for.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Field 57A carries the BIC only\n→ Receiving bank holds several clearing accounts\n→ No CHIPS UID to pin down the account\n→ Payment held while the account is confirmed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The BIC alone is always enough.\"\nUsing the BIC alone is valid, but the UID removes any doubt.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn option A of field 57, the line before the BIC is the party identifier, and for a United States payment that identifier can be the beneficiary bank's CHIPS UID. The CHIPS UID identifies the bank in the United States clearing system, while the BIC identifies it on SWIFT, so the two sit together in the same field.\n\nThe BIC alone is valid, but the UID removes any doubt about which clearing account should be used.\n\nWhat proves the payment will land in the right clearing account?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the CHIPS UID on the party identifier line before the BIC in field 57A for United States payments. The BIC names the bank on SWIFT, the UID names its clearing account, and together they leave no doubt.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Party identifier line\n→ CHIPS UID\n→ BIC\n→ Clearing account pinned down\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the BIC enough?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment will land in the right clearing account?\"\n\nHave you ever had to guess which clearing account a payment was for?", "takeaway": "The BIC alone is valid, but the UID removes any doubt about which clearing account should be used.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A CHIPS UID in field 57A on USD 80,000", "parts": [{"label": "Party identifier line before the BIC", "note": "In option A of field 57, the line before the BIC is the party identifier"}, {"label": "CHIPS UID identifies the US clearing bank", "note": "For a United States payment the identifier can be the beneficiary bank's CHIPS UID"}, {"label": "BIC identifies the bank on SWIFT", "note": "The CHIPS UID identifies the bank in US clearing, the BIC on SWIFT"}, {"label": "BIC alone is also valid", "note": "The BIC alone is valid, but the UID removes doubt about the clearing account"}, {"label": "Both together leave no doubt", "note": "UID plus BIC pins down the clearing account."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C081", "seq": 282, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 110,000 debited from the wrong nostro account", "explain": "A bank held three euro accounts with the same correspondent.\nEUR 110,000 was debited from the wrong one.\nLiquidity planning for the week broke, because field 53B had never named the account to debit.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank holds several accounts with one correspondent\n→ Field 53 left vague\n→ Wrong nostro account debited\n→ Liquidity plan breaks\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Field 53 always means the nostro account.\"\nField 53 does not always contain the sender's nostro account.\n\nWhy?\n\nA bank can hold more than one account with the same correspondent in the same currency, and field 53B exists to say exactly which account must be debited. Using option B tells the receiver that the sender has many accounts with it and wants a specific one used to settle the transaction.\n\nField 53B exists to say exactly which account must be debited.\n\nWhat proves the right account was debited?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse field 53B to name the exact account to debit whenever the sender holds more than one account with the receiver. Remember that field 53 itself is optional in an MT103, and its three options can carry different information.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Accounts held with the correspondent\n→ Field 53B account named\n→ Account debited\n→ Liquidity plan intact\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which account was debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the right account was debited?\"\n\nHave you ever found a payment debited from the wrong nostro account?", "takeaway": "Field 53B exists to say exactly which account must be debited.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 53B: which account to debit for EUR 110,000", "parts": [{"label": "Bank can hold several accounts", "note": "A bank can hold more than one account with the same correspondent in the same currency"}, {"label": "Field 53B names the exact account", "note": "Field 53B says exactly which account must be debited"}, {"label": "Option B signals many accounts", "note": "Option B tells the receiver the sender has many accounts and wants one used"}, {"label": "Field 53 is optional in an MT103", "note": "Field 53 itself is optional in an MT103"}, {"label": "Not always the sender's nostro account", "note": "Field 53 does not always contain the sender's nostro account, as its options differ"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C082", "seq": 283, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 35,000 remittance reference that broke the 16-character limit", "explain": "A EUR 35,000 payment carried a remittance reference in field 70.\nThe reference after /RFB/ ran past 16 characters.\nThe beneficiary's system could not match it, and the payment sat unreconciled.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Field 70 carries several references\n→ /RFB/ reference runs past 16 characters\n→ Beneficiary system cannot match it\n→ Payment sits unreconciled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A longer reference is a clearer reference.\"\nThe reference that follows /RFB/ is limited to 16 characters.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 70 carries remittance information, and several references can be given if they are separated by a double slash, but one code such as /RFB/ or /INV/ cannot serve two references of the same kind. A longer description must continue on the following lines of the field instead of stretching the reference.\n\nA field 70 that repeats a code or overruns the limit is not a valid instruction.\n\nWhat proves field 70 follows the limits?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep the reference after /RFB/ within 16 characters. Use one code per reference kind, separate references with a double slash, and put longer text on the following lines, not inside the reference.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ References separated by double slash\n→ One code per reference kind\n→ /RFB/ within 16 characters\n→ Longer text on following lines\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the reference clear enough?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves field 70 follows the limits?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment sit unreconciled over a reference that was too long?", "takeaway": "A field 70 that repeats a code or overruns the limit is not a valid instruction.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 70: remittance information on EUR 35,000", "parts": [{"label": "Several references, separated by double slash", "note": "Several references can be given if separated by a double slash"}, {"label": "A code must not be repeated", "note": "A code such as /RFB/ or /INV/ cannot serve two references of the same kind"}, {"label": "/RFB/ reference limited to 16 characters", "note": "The reference following /RFB/ is limited to 16 characters"}, {"label": "Longer text continues on following lines", "note": "A longer description continues on the following lines, not in the reference"}, {"label": "Invalid field, even if not rejected", "note": "A field 70 repeating a code or overrunning the limit is not valid"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C084", "seq": 284, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 48,000 credited to the named beneficiary when it belonged further on", "explain": "EUR 48,000 was sent to a named beneficiary.\nThe funds were meant for an account beyond that beneficiary.\nThe beneficiary bank credited the named beneficiary directly, because no instruction told it to look further.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 names the beneficiary in field 59\n→ Funds meant for a further account\n→ No /FFC/ instruction in field 72\n→ Beneficiary credited directly, onward account never paid\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary bank will know where the funds really belong.\"\nWithout the code, the bank has no instruction to look beyond the beneficiary named in field 59.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen the funds must be credited to an account other than the beneficiary's own account, the instruction travels as /FFC/, for further credit, in field 72, sender to receiver information. The beneficiary bank reads the code and passes the funds on to the account named after it, instead of crediting the beneficiary directly.\n\nWithout the code, the bank has no instruction to look beyond the beneficiary named in field 59.\n\nWhat proves the funds were meant to go further than the named beneficiary?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut /FFC/ in field 72 with the onward account named after it, whenever the funds belong beyond the beneficiary. Check field 72 before sending, because the beneficiary bank will only do what the instruction says.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Beneficiary in field 59\n→ /FFC/ in field 72\n→ Onward account named\n→ Funds passed onward\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the beneficiary the final account?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds were meant to go further than the named beneficiary?\"\n\nHave you ever seen funds stop at the named beneficiary when they belonged further on?", "takeaway": "Without the code, the bank has no instruction to look beyond the beneficiary named in field 59.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "FFC: credit a further account for EUR 48,000", "parts": [{"label": "Instruction travels as /FFC/ in field 72", "note": "The instruction travels as /FFC/, for further credit, in field 72"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank passes funds onward", "note": "The beneficiary bank reads the code and passes the funds to the account named"}, {"label": "Without the code, no onward instruction", "note": "Without the code, the bank has no instruction beyond the beneficiary in field 59"}, {"label": "Name the onward account", "note": "The account after /FFC/ is where the funds belong."}, {"label": "Check field 72 before sending", "note": "The bank only does what the instruction says."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C087", "seq": 285, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 160,000 late, and the MT103 copy proved nothing", "explain": "EUR 160,000 was late by four days.\nThe customer produced a copy of the MT103 as proof the money had moved.\nThe copy proved nothing, and the bank could not trace the payment from it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment late by four days\n→ Customer shows a copy of the MT103\n→ Copy proves nothing about whether the money moved\n→ Bank cannot trace the payment from the copy\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An MT103 copy proves the money moved.\"\nA copy of an MT103 on its own proves nothing about whether the money moved.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe unique end-to-end transaction reference, the UETR, is carried in field 121 of block 3 of the message header, not in the body of the MT103. When a payment is late or a bank cannot trace it, the UETR is the reference to give the bank, because every bank in the chain can use it to follow the payment across the network.\n\nA copy of an MT103 on its own proves nothing about whether the money moved.\n\nWhat proves this payment moved, and where it is now?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace with the UETR from field 121 of block 3, together with the sending bank's records. Give the bank the UETR, not a copy of the MT103, and let every bank in the chain follow the same reference.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR in field 121 of block 3\n→ Sending bank records\n→ Every bank in the chain traces the same reference\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we have the MT103 copy?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this payment moved, and where it is now?\"\n\nHave you ever tried to trace a payment with only an MT103 copy?", "takeaway": "A copy of an MT103 on its own proves nothing about whether the money moved.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "UETR carried in field 121 of block 3", "note": "The UETR sits in field 121 of block 3 of the header, not in the body"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Every bank in the chain can use it", "note": "Every bank in the chain can use it to follow the payment across the network"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Payment late or untraceable", "note": "When a payment is late or untraceable, the UETR is the reference to give the bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 copy alone proves nothing", "note": "A copy of an MT103 alone proves nothing about whether the money moved"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "UETR and bank records settle the question", "note": "The UETR and the sending bank's records settle the question"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C088", "seq": 286, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 22,000 ordering name rejected for spilling onto a second line", "explain": "An ordering customer had a long company name.\nIt ran over two lines in field 50K.\nThe receiving bank rejected the MT103, saying a name must fit on one line.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Ordering customer name spans two lines in field 50K\n→ Receiving bank rejects the message\n→ Payment for EUR 22,000 stops\n→ Sender told the name must fit on one line\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A name must fit on the first line.\"\nThe standard does not restrict the name to the first line.\n\nWhy?\n\nOption K of field 50a allows the ordering customer's name and address in up to four lines of 35 characters each. The name does not have to fit on the first line: it can sit on one line with the address on the rest, or run over two or three lines with the address on what remains.\n\nThe standard does not restrict the name to the first line, so a name split across lines is valid.\n\nWhat proves a name split across lines is valid in field 50K?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse the four lines of 35 characters as the standard allows. Put the name on one line with the address on the rest, or let the name run over two or three lines, and challenge any rejection that claims a name must fit on one line.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Four lines of 35 characters\n→ Name on one line, address on the rest\n→ Name over two or three lines valid\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the name fit on one line?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a name split across lines is valid in field 50K?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment rejected because a name spilled onto a second line?", "takeaway": "The standard does not restrict the name to the first line, so a name split across lines is valid.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Field 50K: name over several lines on EUR 22,000", "parts": [{"label": "Up to four lines of 35 characters", "note": "Field 50K allows the name and address in up to four lines of 35 characters"}, {"label": "Name need not fit on the first line", "note": "The name does not have to fit on the first line"}, {"label": "Name on one line, address on the rest", "note": "The name can sit on one line with the address on the rest"}, {"label": "Name over two or three lines is valid", "note": "The name can run over two or three lines with the address on what remains"}, {"label": "Challenge a one-line rejection", "note": "A rejection on that ground is wrong."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C092", "seq": 289, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 85,000 MT101, and the two references pointed at two different owners", "explain": "An MT101 for EUR 85,000 carried two references.\nField 20 and field 21R showed different values.\nThe operations team treated them as the same reference, and the reconciliation broke.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Field 20 assigned by the sending bank\n→ Field 21R assigned by the ordering customer\n→ Team treats them as one reference\n→ Reconciliation breaks\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Two references on one message are the same reference.\"\nThey belong to different parties.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 20 is the sender's reference, assigned by the bank that sends the MT101 to identify the payment. Field 21R is the ordering customer's reference, assigned by the customer who ordered the payment, and it stays the same across every message sent for one order. The two references can coincide when the sender is also the ordering customer, but they belong to different parties, and field 21R is optional while field 20 is mandatory.\n\nField 20 belongs to the sender. Field 21R belongs to the ordering customer.\n\nWhat proves we matched this payment on the right reference?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMatch on field 20 for the sender's reference and on field 21R for the ordering customer's reference, which stays the same across every message for one order. Never merge the two.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 20 mandatory\n→ Field 21R optional\n→ Field 21R stable across messages\n→ Match on the right reference\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are the two references the same?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we matched this payment on the right reference?\"\n\nHave you ever seen two references on one message treated as one?", "takeaway": "Field 20 belongs to the sender. Field 21R belongs to the ordering customer.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fields 20 and 21R in an EUR 85,000 MT101", "parts": [{"label": "Field 20: sender's reference", "note": "Field 20 is the sender's reference, assigned by the bank sending the MT101"}, {"label": "Field 21R: ordering customer's reference", "note": "Field 21R is the ordering customer's reference, assigned by the customer"}, {"label": "Field 21R stays the same across messages", "note": "Field 21R stays the same across every message sent for one order"}, {"label": "References can coincide", "note": "The two references can coincide when the sender is also the ordering customer"}, {"label": "Field 20 mandatory, field 21R optional", "note": "Field 21R is optional while field 20 is mandatory"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C093", "seq": 290, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 120,000 paid by a parent, and the beneficiary thanked the wrong company", "explain": "A parent company paid EUR 120,000 on behalf of a subsidiary.\nThe beneficiary thanked the parent for a payment the subsidiary had really made.\nThe subsidiary's own records showed nothing, because the beneficiary had been told the payment was from the subsidiary, and no one had read the field that said so.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Parent sends the MT101\n→ Subsidiary named in the Instructing Party field\n→ Beneficiary sees the parent as the sender\n→ Beneficiary thanks the wrong company\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The sender is the party the payment is from.\"\nThe beneficiary is told the payment is from the subsidiary through the Instructing Party field.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a parent company sends an MT101 to pay on behalf of a subsidiary, the parent company stands as the sender, not the subsidiary. The beneficiary is told the payment is from the subsidiary through the Instructing Party field, which names the subsidiary even though the parent sent the instruction.\n\nThe beneficiary sees the parent as the sender and the subsidiary as the party the payment is really from.\n\nWhat proves the beneficiary knows which company this payment is really from?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the subsidiary in the Instructing Party field on every MT101 sent for it. Read that field before telling a beneficiary who paid them, and record the payment under the subsidiary, not the sender.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sender is the parent\n→ Instructing Party names the subsidiary\n→ Beneficiary told the real source\n→ Recorded under the subsidiary\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who paid this?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary knows which company this payment is really from?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a beneficiary thank the wrong company for a payment?", "takeaway": "The beneficiary sees the parent as the sender and the subsidiary as the party the payment is really from.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who the beneficiary sees on EUR 120,000", "parts": [{"label": "Sender is the parent company", "note": "When a parent sends an MT101 for a subsidiary, the sender is the parent company"}, {"label": "Instructing Party names the subsidiary", "note": "The beneficiary is told the payment is from the subsidiary via the Instructing Party field"}, {"label": "Beneficiary sees parent as sender", "note": "The beneficiary sees the parent as sender and the subsidiary as the real source"}, {"label": "Record it under the subsidiary", "note": "The payment belongs to the subsidiary's records, not the parent's."}, {"label": "Read the field before answering", "note": "The Instructing Party field tells the real story."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C095", "seq": 292, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "One MT101 for EUR 500,000 across six banks, or six MT101s", "explain": "A large corporate held accounts at six banks.\nIt had to pay EUR 500,000 across them.\nThe team prepared six separate MT101s, one per bank, and spent a day on work one message could have done.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Corporate holds accounts at many banks\n→ Six MT101s prepared, one per bank\n→ Day spent on six messages\n→ One MT101 to a concentrating bank would have done it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each bank needs its own MT101 from us.\"\nOne MT101 to a concentrating bank is enough.\n\nWhy?\n\nA large corporate with accounts at many banks can send a single MT101 to one concentrating bank, and that bank forwards the instruction to each bank that holds an account to be debited. The alternative is for the corporate to prepare a separate MT101 for every bank, which is slower and harder to manage.\n\nThe forwarding service is a genuine value-added service, and the concentrating bank charges for it.\n\nWhat proves one MT101 reached every bank that had to be debited?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend one MT101 to the concentrating bank and let it forward the instruction to each bank holding an account to be debited. Agree the forwarding charge upfront, and confirm every leg was forwarded.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ One MT101 sent\n→ Concentrating bank forwards\n→ Every bank debited\n→ Forwarding charge agreed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we need one MT101 per bank?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves one MT101 reached every bank that had to be debited?\"\n\nHave you ever prepared six messages when one would have done?", "takeaway": "The forwarding service is a genuine value-added service, and the concentrating bank charges for it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Corporate holds accounts at many banks", "note": "A large corporate has accounts at many banks"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "One MT101 or one per bank", "note": "The alternative is a separate MT101 for every bank, which is slower and harder to manage"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "One MT101 to the concentrating bank", "note": "The corporate sends a single MT101 to one concentrating bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Concentrating bank forwards to each bank", "note": "That bank forwards the instruction to each bank holding an account to be debited"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Concentrating bank charges for the service", "note": "The forwarding service is a genuine value-added service, and the bank charges for it"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C096", "seq": 293, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 2 million payroll split across five MT101s, and one arrived late", "explain": "A EUR 2 million payroll order was too big for one MT101.\nIt was split across five messages, numbered 1 of 5 to 5 of 5.\nMessage 4 arrived a day late, and the bank processed the rest without it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Order exceeds the 10,000 character limit\n→ Order split across five MT101s\n→ Messages numbered in field 28D\n→ Message 4 arrives late, others processed alone\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each message in the chain can be processed as it arrives.\"\nBanks hold a chained message until every message in the chain has arrived.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT101 message is limited to 10,000 characters, so an order with many transactions must be split across several MT101 messages, numbered in field 28D as 1 of 5, 2 of 5, and so on. Processing one element alone could pay the wrong amounts.\n\nBanks hold a chained message until every message in the chain has arrived.\n\nWhat proves every message in the chain arrived before any was processed?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNumber every message in field 28D and hold the chain until all of them have arrived. Agree very large orders with the bank before sending, because banks set their own limits on how many transactions they will accept in one order.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 28D numbering\n→ All messages arrived\n→ Chain held until complete\n→ Large order agreed with the bank\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we process the messages that arrived?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every message in the chain arrived before any was processed?\"\n\nHave you ever seen part of a chained order processed without the rest?", "takeaway": "Banks hold a chained message until every message in the chain has arrived.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Order exceeds the 10,000 character limit", "note": "An MT101 message is limited to 10,000 characters"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Order split across several MT101s", "note": "An order with many transactions must be split across several MT101 messages"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Messages numbered in field 28D", "note": "Messages are numbered in field 28D as 1 of 5, 2 of 5, and so on"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "All messages arrived or not", "note": "Banks hold a chained message until every message in the chain has arrived"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Very large order agreed with the bank", "note": "A very large order should be agreed with the bank first, as banks set their own limits"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C098", "seq": 294, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 15,000 moved inside one bank, and the team still sent an MT103", "explain": "EUR 15,000 moved between two accounts at the same bank.\nThe team sent an MT101, then an MT103 as well.\nThe MT103 went nowhere, because the payment never left the bank.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Same bank holds both accounts\n→ Payment never leaves the bank\n→ Single MT101 sent\n→ MT103 sent too, for a payment that needs no interbank message\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every payment needs an MT103.\"\nAn MT103 is only required when the beneficiary's bank is different from the debtor's bank.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf the same bank holds both the debtor's account and the beneficiary's account, the payment never leaves the bank, so no interbank message is needed. A single MT101 is enough: the bank debits the debtor's account and credits the beneficiary's account on its own books.\n\nAn MT103 is only required when the beneficiary's bank is different from the debtor's bank.\n\nWhat proves this payment needed no interbank message?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck whether both accounts sit at the same bank before choosing the message. Same bank means one MT101 and a book transfer. Different banks means an MT103.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Debtor bank\n→ Beneficiary bank\n→ Same bank or different\n→ Message chosen\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we need an MT103 for this?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this payment needed no interbank message?\"\n\nHave you ever sent an interbank message for a payment that never left the bank?", "takeaway": "An MT103 is only required when the beneficiary's bank is different from the debtor's bank.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Same bank holds both accounts", "note": "The same bank holds both the debtor's and the beneficiary's account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment never leaves the bank", "note": "The payment never leaves the bank, so no interbank message is needed"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Same bank or different banks", "note": "An MT103 is only required when the beneficiary's bank differs from the debtor's bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Single MT101 is enough", "note": "A single MT101 is enough for the payment"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank debits debtor and credits beneficiary", "note": "The bank debits the debtor and credits the beneficiary on its own books"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C100", "seq": 295, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 4 million statement, and the closing balance appeared twice", "explain": "A EUR 4 million statement was split across several MT940 messages.\nAn intermediate message carried tag 62F, the final closing balance.\nThe client system treated the statement as finished and never generated the messages that followed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Statement split across several MT940s\n→ Intermediate message carries tag 62F\n→ Client system treats the statement as finished\n→ Later messages never generated\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every message can carry a closing balance.\"\nThere is only one closing balance for the whole statement.\n\nWhy?\n\nA statement split across several MT940 messages must use the intermediate closing balance, tag 62M, in every message except the last. Only the final message carries the final closing balance, tag 62F.\n\nThere is only one closing balance for the whole statement.\n\nWhat proves the closing balance appears only on the final message?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse tag 62M on every intermediate message and tag 62F only on the last. Check the tag on every message in the chain before sending, because one early 62F ends the statement for the receiver.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Tag 62M on intermediate messages\n→ Tag 62F only on the final message\n→ One closing balance\n→ Receiver generates every message\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can this message carry the closing balance?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the closing balance appears only on the final message?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a statement end early because of one wrong tag?", "takeaway": "There is only one closing balance for the whole statement.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One MT940, one closing balance on EUR 4 million", "parts": [{"label": "Intermediate messages use tag 62M", "note": "A statement split across several MT940s uses tag 62M in every message except the last"}, {"label": "Only the final message carries 62F", "note": "Only the final message carries the final closing balance, tag 62F"}, {"label": "Only one closing balance exists", "note": "There is only one closing balance for the whole statement"}, {"label": "Early 62F breaks the receiver's system", "note": "A client system receiving a 62F mid-statement may treat the statement as finished"}, {"label": "Check every tag before sending", "note": "One early 62F ends the statement."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT940"], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C101", "seq": 296, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 4 million statement, and the intermediate balance dates did not match", "explain": "A EUR 4 million statement arrived as several MT940s.\nThe intermediate balance dates did not match the last entry in each message.\nThe receiver questioned the statement, even though the balances themselves were right.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Statement split across several MT940s\n→ Tags 60M and 62M show the wrong dates\n→ Balances correct, dates do not match the last entry\n→ Receiver questions the statement\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Only the balances matter, the dates do not.\"\nThe dates that truly matter are the opening, closing, closing available and forward available balances, and the intermediate dates should match the last entry.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a statement is split across several MT940 messages, the intermediate opening balance tag 60M and the intermediate closing balance tag 62M should ideally show the date of the last entry in that message. In practice receivers care most that the balances themselves are correct.\n\nReceivers care most that the balances themselves are correct.\n\nWhat proves the intermediate dates match the last entry in each message?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSet tags 60M and 62M to the date of the last entry in each message. Fix the intermediate dates whenever they can be fixed easily, and always get the opening, closing, closing available and forward available balance dates right.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Tags 60M and 62M\n→ Date of the last entry\n→ Balances correct\n→ Key balance dates right\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do the intermediate dates matter?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the intermediate dates match the last entry in each message?\"\n\nHave you ever had a statement questioned over a balance date?", "takeaway": "Receivers care most that the balances themselves are correct.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Dates on intermediate MT940 balances for EUR 4 million", "parts": [{"label": "Tags 60M and 62M should match the last entry", "note": "Tags 60M and 62M should ideally show the date of the last entry in that message"}, {"label": "Receivers care most that balances are correct", "note": "In practice receivers care most that the balances themselves are correct"}, {"label": "Dates that truly matter", "note": "The dates that matter are opening, closing, closing available and forward available balances"}, {"label": "Fix intermediate dates if easy", "note": "If the intermediate dates can be fixed easily, they should be"}, {"label": "Balances first, dates second", "note": "A wrong balance is worse than a wrong intermediate date."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C102", "seq": 297, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A statement with 900 entries arrived as one MT940, and the rest never came", "explain": "A statement with 900 entries was sent as a single MT940.\nOnly the first entries arrived.\nThe rest never came, because a single MT940 can carry only a limited number of entries.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Statement has 900 entries\n→ Single MT940 sent\n→ Only the first entries arrive\n→ Rest of the statement never comes\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One statement, one message.\"\nA statement with many entries is split across several MT940 messages, one after another.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach message carries a statement number and a sequence number, and the sequence number rises with each new message, so the receiver can put the statement back together in order. The chain can run to a very large number of sequences, which is more than enough in practice.\n\nThe sequence number rises with each message, so the receiver can rebuild the order.\n\nWhat proves the receiver got every part of the statement, in order?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSplit long statements across several MT940s, one after another. Put the statement number and the rising sequence number on each message, and confirm the receiver rebuilt the full statement.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Entries per message\n→ Statement number\n→ Sequence number rising\n→ Full statement rebuilt\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the whole statement sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the receiver got every part of the statement, in order?\"\n\nHave you ever received half a statement because it was sent as one message?", "takeaway": "The sequence number rises with each message, so the receiver can rebuild the order.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Statement has many entries", "note": "A single MT940 can carry only a limited number of entries"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Statement split across several MT940s", "note": "The statement is split across several MT940 messages, one after another"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each message carries statement and sequence", "note": "Each message carries a statement number and a sequence number"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Sequence complete or a message missing", "note": "The sequence number rises with each message, so the receiver can rebuild the order"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Receiver rebuilds the full statement", "note": "The chain can run to a very large number of sequences, more than enough in practice"}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": ["MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-003", "seq": 299, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 30,000 credit advice arrived in a format the receiver could not read", "explain": "EUR 30,000 landed in a customer's account.\nThe credit advice arrived as a camt.054.\nThe receiver's system could only read MT900, so the customer was never told the money had arrived.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Credit of EUR 30,000 applied\n→ Advice sent as camt.054\n→ Receiver can only read MT900\n→ Customer never told the money arrived\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Everyone reads the format we send.\"\nNo rule compels one format over the other, so the sender picks the form it will use.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a bank tells its customer that a credit has landed, it may use a camt.054 in ISO 20022 or an MT900 in MT format, and both do the same job. The receiving side must therefore be ready to read either format when the advice arrives.\n\nThe receiving side must be ready to read either format when the advice arrives.\n\nWhat proves the receiver can read whichever advice format arrives?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the receiving side to read both camt.054 and MT900. Agree the format with the sender where you can, but never assume the advice will arrive in the format you prefer.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ camt.054 readable\n→ MT900 readable\n→ Format agreed where possible\n→ Customer told on arrival\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which format will the advice use?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the receiver can read whichever advice format arrives?\"\n\nHave you ever missed a credit because the advice arrived in a format you could not read?", "takeaway": "The receiving side must be ready to read either format when the advice arrives.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Credit advice: two formats, one job, on EUR 30,000", "parts": [{"label": "camt.054", "note": "ISO 20022 advice telling the customer a credit has been applied."}, {"label": "MT900", "note": "MT format advice doing the same job of reporting a credit."}, {"label": "Sender picks the format", "note": "No governing rule forces one form; the sender chooses."}, {"label": "Receiver reads either", "note": "The receiving side must handle whichever format arrives."}, {"label": "Customer told on arrival", "note": "An unread advice is a customer left waiting."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": ["camt.054", "MT900"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-007", "seq": 303, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The MT950 arrived at noon. The team ignored it, and the day went wrong.", "explain": "An MT950 for the account arrived at noon.\nThe team treated it as tomorrow's statement and ignored it.\nBy close of business the account was short and nobody knew why.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT950 received intraday\n→ Team files it as the end of day statement\n→ Intraday movements go unchecked\n→ Shortfall found only at close of business\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"An MT950 is always the end of day statement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT950 is normally the end of day statement, yet it can also be produced intraday alongside the MT940, when the account servicing bank and the account owner agree. That agreement sets what the receiver should expect during the day, and ignoring an intraday statement means ignoring live balances.\n\nEnd of day is the default. Intraday is the agreement.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the agreement on every account before deciding what an MT950 means, and treat an intraday statement as live information.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Statement timing agreed\n→ Intraday balances\n→ End of day close\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether this MT950 is intraday or end of day?\"\n\nHave you ever missed a shortfall because a statement arrived earlier than expected?", "takeaway": "End of day is the default. Intraday is the agreement.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT950, end of day or intraday", "parts": [{"label": "Usually end of day", "note": "The MT950 normally closes the day as the account statement."}, {"label": "Intraday possible", "note": "It can also be generated during the day, alongside the MT940."}, {"label": "Agreement required", "note": "Both the servicing bank and the account owner must agree to it."}, {"label": "Live balances", "note": "An intraday MT950 shows live balances, not tomorrow's news."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": ["MT950", "MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-008", "seq": 304, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Twenty transactions, one closing balance, and EUR 4,000 that did not add up.", "explain": "An MT950 shows twenty transactions and a closing balance.\nThe closing balance is EUR 4,000 out from the opening balance plus the entries.\nThe team trusts the balance and moves on.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT950 received with twenty field 61 entries\n→ Field 62F closing balance read once\n→ Opening balance plus field 61 entries checked\n→ EUR 4,000 gap found in the arithmetic\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The closing balance is always right.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nEvery transaction on an MT950 sits in its own repeating field 61, so twenty transactions mean twenty field 61 blocks. The closing balance in field 62F appears a single time, at the very end. That balance must agree with the opening balance plus all field 61 entries, and checking the arithmetic is the fastest completeness test.\n\nField 61 repeats. Field 62F must add up.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecompute the closing balance from field 61 on every MT950 before trusting it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 61 entries\n→ Field 62F closing balance\n→ Opening balance\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this statement is complete?\"\n\nHave you caught a missing transaction just by adding up a statement?", "takeaway": "Field 61 repeats. Field 62F must add up.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT950 statement structure", "parts": [{"label": "Field 61 repeats", "note": "One field 61 block carries each transaction on the statement."}, {"label": "Twenty transactions, twenty blocks", "note": "The count of field 61 blocks matches the count of transactions."}, {"label": "Field 62F once", "note": "The closing balance appears a single time, at the end."}, {"label": "Arithmetic must agree", "note": "62F must equal the opening balance plus all field 61 entries."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT950"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-011", "seq": 305, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 75,000 moved. The statement shows it. Nobody knows why.", "explain": "EUR 75,000 leaves the account.\nThe MT940 shows the movement in field 61.\nThe customer asks why it moved, and the team has no answer.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 75,000 debit on the MT940\n→ Field 61 shows the date, amount and entry type\n→ Field 86 checked for the narrative\n→ No narrative found, customer query stalls\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Field 61 is enough to reconcile a payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn an MT940, field 61 is the statement line, carrying the date, the amount and the entry type for each transaction. Field 86 sits beside that line and carries the information to the account owner, the narrative behind the movement. Field 61 alone shows that money moved. Field 86 shows why, and reconciliation needs both together.\n\nField 61 shows money moved. Field 86 shows why.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever close a reconciliation on field 61 alone. Pair every line with its field 86 narrative.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 61 movement\n→ Field 86 narrative\n→ Reconciliation evidence\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves why this money moved, not just that it moved?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a payment the statement could show but never explain?", "takeaway": "Field 61 shows money moved. Field 86 shows why.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT940: line and narrative", "parts": [{"label": "Field 61, the line", "note": "Each transaction line holds the date, amount and entry type."}, {"label": "Field 86, the narrative", "note": "It carries the information to the account owner about the movement."}, {"label": "61 shows movement", "note": "Field 61 alone shows that money moved."}, {"label": "86 shows the reason", "note": "Field 86 explains why the money moved, and both feed reconciliation."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-012", "seq": 306, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The treasurer thought EUR 2 million was available. It was not.", "explain": "The treasurer reads the MT940 and sees EUR 2 million.\nA payment of EUR 2 million is released against it.\nThe payment fails. The funds were never available that day.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT940 balances read at day end\n→ EUR 2 million taken as available funds\n→ Payment released against the wrong balance\n→ Payment fails for lack of funds\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The biggest balance on the statement is the money available.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nField 62M is the interim closing balance, struck when the statement was cut during the day. Field 64 is the closing available balance, the funds actually available at day end, while field 65 is the forward available balance, the funds expected on a future date. Confusing them overstates or understates liquidity, so each balance must be read for the question it answers.\n\n62M, 64 and 65 answer different questions.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the question before reading a balance, and use only the field that answers it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ 62M interim balance\n→ 64 closing available balance\n→ 65 forward available balance\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves these funds are available today, not tomorrow?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment fail because the wrong balance was read?", "takeaway": "62M, 64 and 65 answer different questions.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT940: three balances, three questions", "parts": [{"label": "62M", "note": "Interim closing balance, struck when the statement was cut."}, {"label": "64", "note": "Closing available balance, funds available at day end."}, {"label": "65", "note": "Forward available balance, funds expected on a future date."}, {"label": "Read the right field", "note": "Each balance answers a different liquidity question."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-013", "seq": 307, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "One MT940 never arrived. Nobody noticed for a week.", "explain": "A week of statements arrives, and one MT940 is missing.\nEvery transaction in the week still reconciles.\nThe gap is found only when a customer disputes a payment from the missing day.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT940 statements arrive through the week\n→ Field 28C sequence checked late\n→ Gap found in the statement numbers\n→ Missing day traced to a failed delivery\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the transactions reconcile, the statements are complete.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nField 28C carries the statement number and the sequence number, pinning down exactly which statement this is and where it sits in the run. A missing statement, or one arriving out of order, shows as a gap in the 28C sequence before any transaction is examined. That makes 28C the first completeness check on every MT940 received.\n\n28C is the first completeness check.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the 28C sequence on arrival, before any transaction is examined.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ 28C sequence\n→ Statement delivery\n→ Transaction reconciliation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves no statement is missing from this run?\"\n\nHave you found a missing statement only after a customer complained?", "takeaway": "28C is the first completeness check.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT940 completeness via 28C", "parts": [{"label": "28C names the statement", "note": "Statement number and sequence number identify this statement and its place."}, {"label": "Gaps show in the sequence", "note": "A missing or out of order statement leaves a gap in 28C."}, {"label": "Checked before transactions", "note": "The gap appears before any transaction is examined."}, {"label": "First completeness check", "note": "28C is the first completeness check on each MT940."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT940"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-009", "seq": 309, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 250,000 went to the wrong correspondent. The instructions were published years ago.", "explain": "A EUR 250,000 payment is sent to a correspondent named in the payment engine.\nThe correspondent no longer serves that currency.\nThe payment is rejected, and the team discovers the instructions were never updated.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment routed on stored settlement instructions\n→ Correspondent named in the engine\n→ Correspondent no longer serves the currency\n→ Payment rejected downstream\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Settlement instructions, once stored, stay correct.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBanks tell each other who their correspondents are through Standing Settlement Instructions, exchanged as SWIFT MT670 and MT671 messages, and receiving banks store them in their payment engines. The MT671 goes out to all SWIFT institutions, whether or not they hold a direct relationship with the sender. Where a bank has several correspondents in one currency it lists them all, leaving the sender to choose among them.\n\nPublished instructions go stale. Stored instructions go stale faster.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat settlement instructions as reference data with an owner and a refresh date, not as a one-time setup.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT670 and MT671 publication\n→ Stored instructions\n→ Correspondent choice\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this correspondent still serves this currency today?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail on instructions everyone assumed were current?", "takeaway": "Published instructions go stale. Stored instructions go stale faster.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Banks publish their correspondents", "note": "Each bank tells others who its correspondents are."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT670 and MT671 carry them", "note": "The instructions travel as SWIFT MT670 and MT671 messages."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT671 goes to everyone", "note": "MT671 is broadcast to all SWIFT institutions, related or not."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the stored instruction still current?", "note": "Check the refresh date before routing on it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sender picks a correspondent", "note": "With several correspondents listed, the sender chooses one."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT670", "MT671"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-018", "seq": 317, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 600,000 of goods shipped. The documents were perfect. The bank paid nothing.", "explain": "An exporter ships EUR 600,000 of goods under a documentary collection.\nThe documents are in order and the buyer pays.\nThe exporter then asks the collecting bank for the money, and the bank says it owes nothing.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Documentary collection for EUR 600,000\n→ Documents presented in order\n→ Drawee pays what is due\n→ Exporter claims payment from the collecting bank\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the documents are in order, the bank must pay.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a documentary collection the collecting bank and the remitting bank act only as intermediaries between the drawer and the drawee. Unlike a documentary credit, the collecting bank owes the drawer no payment even when the documents are in order. It releases the documents to the drawee when the drawee pays what is due, and the payment undertaking found under a letter of credit simply does not exist here.\n\nA collection intermediates. It never promises payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSay plainly in every collection requirement that the bank is an intermediary only, and name who actually pays.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Collecting bank role\n→ Remitting bank role\n→ Drawee payment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who owes the exporter this money?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection mistaken for a letter of credit?", "takeaway": "A collection intermediates. It never promises payment.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Documentary collection, no payment promise", "parts": [{"label": "Collecting bank intermediates", "note": "It acts only as an intermediary between drawer and drawee."}, {"label": "Remitting bank intermediates", "note": "It too acts only as an intermediary in the collection."}, {"label": "No duty to pay", "note": "The collecting bank owes no payment even if documents are in order."}, {"label": "Documents released on payment", "note": "The drawee gets the documents when it pays what is due."}, {"label": "No payment undertaking", "note": "The undertaking behind a letter of credit does not exist here."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-001", "seq": 318, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A customer paid GBP 250 by card. The merchant was paid before the customer was.", "explain": "A customer pays GBP 250 by card on Monday.\nThe merchant is paid at settlement on Tuesday.\nThe customer pays the issuer weeks later, after the statement arrives.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Card used to pay GBP 250\n→ Issuer sends the statement\n→ Acquirer pays the merchant at settlement\n→ Cardholder pays the issuer after the statement\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The customer pays first, then the merchant gets paid.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPaying by card moves money in an order that surprises many people. First the card is used to pay, then the issuer sends the statement, then the merchant is paid by the acquirer at settlement, and finally the cardholder pays the issuer. The acquirer settles with the merchant every day across all transactions, while the cardholder's payment to the issuer comes only after the statement arrives.\n\nThe merchant is paid before the cardholder pays.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nExplain the card order in every card requirement, so no one builds a flow that assumes the customer funds the merchant.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Card use\n→ Acquirer settlement\n→ Cardholder payment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who funded this transaction, and when?\"\n\nDid you know the merchant is paid before the cardholder pays?", "takeaway": "The merchant is paid before the cardholder pays.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Card used to pay", "note": "First the cardholder pays with the card."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Issuer sends the statement", "note": "Then the issuer sends the statement."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Acquirer pays the merchant", "note": "The merchant is paid by the acquirer at settlement, every day."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Cardholder pays in full?", "note": "Full payment, or a balance carried forward."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cardholder pays the issuer", "note": "Finally the cardholder pays the issuer, after the statement arrives."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-002", "seq": 319, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "GBP 1,000 spent on day one of the cycle. No interest for 45 days. Spent on day 29, far less.", "explain": "A cardholder spends GBP 1,000 on the first day of the billing cycle.\nThe statement arrives, and the cardholder pays in full 21 days later.\nNo interest is charged, and the cardholder assumes every purchase gets 45 days free.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ GBP 1,000 spent at the start of the cycle\n→ Statement sent at cycle end\n→ Full payment inside the 21 day window\n→ No interest charged this time\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Every card purchase gets 45 days interest free.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe interest-free credit period depends on where in the billing cycle the payment fell, and it can reach about 45 days for a payment made at the start of a cycle. When the cycle ends, the issuer sends the statement, and the cardholder then has a set period, usually about 21 days, to pay. Paying the full statement amount inside that window means no interest, while paying less leaves interest charged on the balance.\n\nThe free window depends on the day you spend, not the card you hold.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nState the window as a range tied to the purchase date, never as a single number.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Purchase date in the cycle\n→ Statement date\n→ Payment window\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves how many interest-free days this purchase actually gets?\"\n\nHave you explained the free credit window as a fixed number when it is not?", "takeaway": "The free window depends on the day you spend, not the card you hold.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Card statement cycle and interest", "parts": [{"label": "Free credit up to 45 days", "note": "A payment at the start of a cycle can stretch to about 45 days."}, {"label": "Statement ends the cycle", "note": "The issuer sends the statement when the cycle ends."}, {"label": "About 21 days to pay", "note": "The cardholder then has usually about 21 days to pay."}, {"label": "Pay in full, no interest", "note": "Full payment inside the window means no interest is charged."}, {"label": "Pay less, interest charged", "note": "Paying less leaves interest charged on the balance."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-003", "seq": 320, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A merchant pays GBP 100 in card fees. GBP 80 of it goes to a bank the customer never sees.", "explain": "A merchant accepts a card payment and pays GBP 100 in merchant service charges.\nAbout GBP 80 of that goes to the issuer.\nThe merchant asks the customer to cover it, and the customer refuses.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Card transaction settled\n→ Merchant service charges of GBP 100 applied\n→ Interchange of about GBP 80 paid to the issuer\n→ Merchant tries to pass the cost to the cardholder\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The cardholder pays the interchange fee.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nInterchange is what the acquirer pays the issuer per card transaction, known too as the wholesale fee or interexchange fee. It is the issuer's transaction fee in clearing and settlement, typically running to 75 to 85 percent of the merchant service charges. The merchant bears this cost through the fees charged on the transaction, not the cardholder.\n\nInterchange is the acquirer paying the issuer. The merchant bears it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the payer and the bearer of every card fee in the requirement, so no flow charges the wrong party.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Acquirer payment\n→ Issuer receipt\n→ Merchant cost\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who bears this fee?\"\n\nHave you seen a fee charged to the wrong side of a card transaction?", "takeaway": "Interchange is the acquirer paying the issuer. The merchant bears it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Interchange: acquirer pays issuer", "parts": [{"label": "What it is", "note": "Compensation the acquirer pays the issuer per transaction."}, {"label": "Also called", "note": "Wholesale fee or interexchange fee."}, {"label": "Size", "note": "Typically 75 to 85 percent of merchant service charges."}, {"label": "Who bears it", "note": "The merchant bears the cost, not the cardholder."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN5-007", "seq": 321, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Two merchants, same volume, wildly different scheme bills. The cards were different.", "explain": "Two merchants each process the same volume.\nOne takes mostly one scheme's cards, the other the other scheme's.\nTheir scheme bills look nothing alike, and neither merchant can explain why.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Merchants process the same volume\n→ Different card mix on each merchant\n→ Scheme fees charged in different ways\n→ Bills differ with no explanation\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Scheme fees are charged the same way by every scheme.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe card schemes collect fees from merchants alongside the processor, and each scheme charges in its own way. The processing firm usually takes what it is owed straight from the merchant's account. Mastercard charges per transaction, while Visa charges yearly or quarterly memberships, so a merchant's scheme cost depends on which cards its customers use and how much volume goes through each scheme.\n\nSame volume, different scheme bill. The card mix decides it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBreak every scheme cost down by scheme and by charging method, so a merchant can see what drives the bill.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Processor fees\n→ Per transaction charges\n→ Membership charges\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which scheme drove this merchant's bill?\"\n\nHave you compared two merchants' card costs without checking the card mix?", "takeaway": "Same volume, different scheme bill. The card mix decides it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "How schemes collect their fees", "parts": [{"label": "Processor", "note": "Usually takes its fees straight from the merchant account."}, {"label": "Mastercard", "note": "Charges per transaction."}, {"label": "Visa", "note": "Charges yearly or quarterly memberships."}, {"label": "Merchant cost varies", "note": "Cost depends on card mix and volume through each scheme."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-101", "seq": 322, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 100,000 was due to settle. Both banks waited for the other to move it.", "explain": "A EUR 100,000 credit transfer sits ready to settle.\nEach bank assumes the other will move the funds.\nThe payment waits, and the beneficiary calls to ask where the money is.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 ready for EUR 100,000\n→ Settlement method field never read\n→ Each bank waits for the other\n→ Payment stalls with no owner\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Settlement happens by itself once the instruction is sent.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn an ISO 20022 credit transfer the settlement method is the field that assigns settlement to one bank in the chain. INDA puts settlement on the instructed agent, so the next bank moves the funds, while INGA puts it on the instructing agent, so the sending bank moves the funds itself. Read the field first in any funding dispute, because it fixes which bank must hold the liquidity, which account is debited, and how both sides reconcile.\n\nINDA or INGA decides who moves the funds. Read it first.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake the settlement method the first field read in every funding dispute, and name the settling bank before anything else is investigated.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Settlement method field\n→ Settling bank liquidity\n→ Account debited\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which bank must move these funds?\"\n\nHave you watched two banks wait for each other to settle a payment?", "takeaway": "INDA or INGA decides who moves the funds. Read it first.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who settles the payment", "parts": [{"label": "INDA", "note": "Instructed agent settles; the next bank moves funds."}, {"label": "INGA", "note": "Instructing agent settles; the sending bank moves funds."}, {"label": "Liquidity burden", "note": "Liquidity must sit with the settling bank."}, {"label": "Settlement timing", "note": "Settlement timing follows the settling bank."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-105", "seq": 323, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A customer paid INR 50,000 by UPI in seconds. The banks settled hours later.", "explain": "A customer pays INR 50,000 by UPI.\nThe debit shows at once and the merchant confirms receipt in seconds.\nThe payment then fails in reconciliation, and the team treats it as a customer failure.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ UPI debit of INR 50,000 at once\n→ Merchant receipt confirmed in seconds\n→ Interbank settlement still pending\n→ Reconciliation failure blamed on the customer leg\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Instant for the customer means instant for the banks.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUPI compresses the customer experience into seconds, debit seen at once, merchant receipt confirmed almost immediately, while the banks still settle with each other later under the scheme settlement cycle. Customer leg and interbank settlement are two separate events, and the bank funds the liquidity and reconciliation exposure between them. In any failure, separate the two events first and ask which one broke: the customer leg or the settlement leg.\n\nInstant to the customer, settled later between banks.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSeparate the customer leg from the settlement leg before diagnosing any UPI failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer leg\n→ Settlement leg\n→ Liquidity gap\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which leg broke, the customer leg or the settlement leg?\"\n\nHave you blamed a customer leg for a settlement leg failure?", "takeaway": "Instant to the customer, settled later between banks.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two events behind one payment", "parts": [{"label": "Debit at once", "note": "Account debited at once."}, {"label": "Credit in seconds", "note": "Receipt confirmed within seconds."}, {"label": "Settlement later", "note": "Banks settle later, on the scheme cycle."}, {"label": "The gap the bank funds", "note": "The bank funds the gap in between."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-123", "seq": 330, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 600 collection was rejected. The mandate evidence was in three systems, and none of it matched.", "explain": "A EUR 600 collection is rejected for a missing or invalid mandate.\nThe mandate evidence is scattered: a paper scan in a shared drive, an online consent in another system, an amendment as a free-text note.\nThe rejection cannot be explained, and the customer is not convinced either.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 600 collection rejected\n→ Mandate evidence scattered across systems\n→ Amendment recorded as a free-text note\n→ Rejection cannot be explained to the customer\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A mandate is a record you file once.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThink of a mandate as a lifecycle, created, amended when a customer changes bank accounts, and cancelled, with a reference and evidence at every step. Scatter that evidence, a paper scan in a shared drive, an online consent in another system, an amendment as a free-text note, and a collection rejected for a missing or invalid mandate becomes unexplainable. Give billing one view of each mandate, its current state and the evidence that proves it.\n\nA mandate is a lifecycle, not a record.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHold one view of each mandate, its current state and the evidence at every step, from creation to cancellation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate creation\n→ Mandate amendment\n→ Mandate cancellation\n→ Mandate evidence\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this mandate is valid, and where does it live?\"\n\nHave you tried to defend a collection when the mandate evidence was scattered?", "takeaway": "A mandate is a lifecycle, not a record.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Create the mandate", "note": "Record the mandate with its reference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Amend the mandate", "note": "Log changes such as a new bank account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cancel the mandate", "note": "Close the mandate with its evidence."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the evidence in one view?", "note": "Scattered evidence cannot defend a collection."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Hold the evidence together", "note": "Hold one view of state and proof."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-155", "seq": 331, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 450 mandate was amended twice. The collections could not be traced to it.", "explain": "A EUR 450 mandate is amended when the customer changes bank accounts.\nThe amendment is recorded under a new reference.\nWhen the collections are challenged, none of them trace back to the mandate that authorised them.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 450 mandate created with one reference\n→ Amendment recorded under a new reference\n→ Collections challenged by the debtor\n→ No collection traces to the authorising mandate\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A new amendment needs a new mandate reference.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA mandate reference is generated once and stays with the mandate from creation through amendments to cancellation. Every amendment and cancellation is recorded against it with an audit history, so any collection traces back to the mandate that authorised it. Reuse it, change it or lose it and the mandate cannot be proven, and its collections cannot be defended.\n\nOne mandate, one reference, for its whole life.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGenerate the reference once, record every change against it, and never reuse or change it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate reference\n→ Amendment history\n→ Cancellation record\n→ Audit trail\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this collection traces to the mandate that authorised it?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection fail because its mandate reference had changed?", "takeaway": "One mandate, one reference, for its whole life.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One reference, whole life", "parts": [{"label": "Generated once", "note": "Created once and unique to the mandate."}, {"label": "Amendments logged", "note": "Logged against the same reference."}, {"label": "Cancellation logged", "note": "Logged against the same reference."}, {"label": "Full audit history", "note": "Each change is traceable."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-163", "seq": 336, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A business was collected under Core. It claimed a refund the creditor never expected.", "explain": "A business is collected under SDD Core for EUR 5,000.\nThe debtor claims a refund the creditor assumed did not exist.\nThe creditor had run one process for both schemes, and the differences were hidden.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 5,000 collected under Core\n→ Debtor claims a refund\n→ Creditor assumed no refund right existed\n→ One process used for both schemes\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Core and B2B can run on one process.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCore and B2B differ exactly where risk is decided: who may use them, what refund rights the debtor holds, and what the debtor bank must verify before the first collection. A business collected under Core holds a refund right the creditor may not expect, while under B2B there is no such right but the mandate must be verified first. Running one process for both hides those differences, so record the scheme on each mandate and follow that scheme's current rulebook.\n\nTwo schemes, two risk profiles. One process hides them.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecord the scheme on each mandate and follow that scheme's rulebook, never a blended process.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme on the mandate\n→ Refund rights\n→ Mandate verification\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which scheme governs this mandate?\"\n\nHave you run one process for Core and B2B and been caught by the difference?", "takeaway": "Two schemes, two risk profiles. One process hides them.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two schemes, two risk profiles", "parts": [{"label": "Who may use it", "note": "Core: consumers and businesses. B2B: businesses only."}, {"label": "Debtor refund rights", "note": "Core debtors hold a refund right; B2B debtors do not."}, {"label": "Mandate verification", "note": "B2B mandates are verified before the first collection."}, {"label": "Rulebook per scheme", "note": "Each scheme runs on its own rulebook."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-165", "seq": 337, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 140 transfer arrived short by 11.39 percent. The customer was told the fee was small.", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 140 abroad.\nAbout 11.39 percent disappears in fees before it arrives.\nThe customer was quoted one small fee, and nobody split the total into its parts.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 140 transfer sent\n→ Total fee of about 11.39 percent taken\n→ Fee never split into components\n→ Customer cannot compare corridors\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The fee on a transfer is one charge.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTake the cited example, a 140 euro transfer carrying an 11.39 percent fee, and the corridor cost structure becomes visible: the fee is not one charge but transfer fee plus exchange margin plus intermediary charges along the route. Splitting the total into components is what lets a customer compare corridors and an analyst say which part is avoidable. These figures describe that cited example only, not every transfer on the corridor, so present them as one case, never as the corridor rate.\n\nOne transfer, three stacked charges. Split the total to see them.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSplit every transfer fee into transfer fee, exchange margin and intermediary charges, and label the example as one case only.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Transfer fee\n→ Exchange margin\n→ Intermediary charges\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves what each part of this fee actually cost?\"\n\nHave you quoted a transfer fee that hid two more charges inside it?", "takeaway": "One transfer, three stacked charges. Split the total to see them.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Stacked cost of one transfer", "parts": [{"label": "Sending fee", "note": "Charged for sending the payment."}, {"label": "FX margin", "note": "Cost hidden inside the FX rate."}, {"label": "Charges en route", "note": "Taken by banks along the route."}, {"label": "Total paid", "note": "What the customer actually pays in this one example."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D01", "seq": 365, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 10,000 SDD collection fell on a national holiday abroad. The team moved the due date.", "explain": "A EUR 10,000 SDD collection is due on a day that is a national holiday in another country.\nThe team moves the due date, assuming the holiday stops the collection.\nThe collection was valid all along, because the interbank leg runs on TARGET.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 10,000 SDD collection due\n→ National holiday in another country\n→ Team moves the due date\n→ Collection delayed for no reason\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A national holiday anywhere stops an SDD collection.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSDD counting runs on Inter-PSP Business Days, defined as TARGET Business Days, so a national holiday elsewhere leaves the TARGET schedule and the collection validity untouched. TARGET drives Due Date, D-1 and settlement for the interbank journey, while each PSP applies its own Business Day calendar only to customer-facing service. Treating a national holiday calendar as the SDD Business Day calendar is the classic error, and the TARGET calendar is the one that controls the interbank leg.\n\nTARGET governs the interbank leg. National holidays do not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse the TARGET calendar for the interbank leg, and each PSP's own calendar only for customer-facing service.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ TARGET calendar\n→ PSP calendar\n→ Due Date\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which calendar governs this collection?\"\n\nHave you moved a due date for a holiday that never applied?", "takeaway": "TARGET governs the interbank leg. National holidays do not.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two calendars inside one SDD collection", "parts": [{"label": "TARGET calendar, interbank leg", "note": "Sets Due Date, D-1, D-14 and settlement, and ignores national holidays."}, {"label": "PSP calendar, customer service", "note": "Each bank applies its own calendar to decide when customer service is open."}, {"label": "National holiday abroad", "note": "A holiday in another country does not halt interbank processing on a TARGET Business Day."}, {"label": "Finding the calendar", "note": "The ECB publishes the TARGET Calendar, which covers all TARGET services."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-D10", "seq": 371, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 50,000 payment went missing. The beneficiary could not prove it had been sent.", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 payment is sent across borders.\nThe beneficiary says it never arrived.\nThe team searches by amount and date, and finds three matching payments.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 50,000 payment sent\n→ Beneficiary says it never arrived\n→ Team searches by amount and date\n→ Three payments match, none can be proven\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Amount and date are enough to trace a payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe UETR, the Unique End-to-End Transaction Reference, is a 36-character RFC 4122 identifier issued by the initiating bank and unchanged from originator to beneficiary. It lets a bank follow the journey from end to end, pinpoint where the payment sits at any moment, and carry the relevant details forward along the way, while also helping banks manage liquidity, exceptions and reconciliation. When the beneficiary thinks the money has not arrived, the UETR lets them claim, trace and prove the payment, which is why a payment lacking a UETR travels with no track at all.\n\nOne reference for the whole journey. Without it, there is no track.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace every payment by its UETR, unchanged from originator to beneficiary, and never by amount and date alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR\n→ Payment journey\n→ Claim and proof\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which payment this is, end to end?\"\n\nHave you tried to trace a payment with only an amount and a date?", "takeaway": "One reference for the whole journey. Without it, there is no track.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "UETR: one reference, end-to-end visibility", "parts": [{"label": "36 characters, RFC 4122", "note": "Assigned by the initiating bank, unchanged for the whole journey."}, {"label": "Trace the journey", "note": "Shows where the payment is at any moment."}, {"label": "Claim and prove", "note": "Beneficiary can use it to claim, trace and prove the payment."}, {"label": "Supports the bank", "note": "Helps manage liquidity, exceptions and reconciliation."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D11", "seq": 372, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 40,000 payment crossed borders in 60 seconds. The beneficiary got exactly what was expected.", "explain": "A EUR 40,000 cross-border payment is sent under OCT Inst.\nPayment and settlement complete in 60 seconds.\nThe non-euro leg is converted before the beneficiary is paid, so the amount lands exactly as expected.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 40,000 OCT Inst payment sent\n→ Payment and settlement in 60 seconds\n→ Non-euro leg converted before payment\n→ Beneficiary receives the expected amount\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Speed is the test for a cross-border instant payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOCT Inst is already live and processes cross-border payments in 60 seconds, handling both the payment and the settlement, with both legs settled through the Euro system. The non-euro leg runs wholly outside the Euro area, governed by non-euro rules, standards and bank policies, with conversion completed before the beneficiary is paid, so the beneficiary receives exactly the amount expected. Speed is not the test here, reliability and rule-following are.\n\nSpeed is not the test. Reliability and rule-following are.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge every OCT Inst payment on whether the non-euro leg followed its rules and the beneficiary got the expected amount.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ OCT Inst settlement\n→ Non-euro leg rules\n→ Beneficiary amount\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the non-euro leg followed its rules?\"\n\nHave you judged a fast payment as a good payment?", "takeaway": "Speed is not the test. Reliability and rule-following are.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The non-euro leg", "parts": [{"label": "OCT Inst in 60 seconds", "note": "Cross-border payments processed in 60 seconds, payment and settlement together."}, {"label": "Both legs through the Euro system", "note": "Both legs are settled through the Euro system."}, {"label": "Non-euro leg outside the Euro area", "note": "Operates under non-euro rules, standards and bank policies."}, {"label": "Beneficiary gets the expected amount", "note": "Conversion is handled before the beneficiary is paid."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D12", "seq": 373, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 25,000 payment left Europe for outside it. It cleared in 10 seconds, around the clock.", "explain": "A EUR 25,000 payment starts in Europe and goes outside it.\nThe euro leg and the leg outside the euro area both have to work.\nOCT Inst clears it in 10 seconds, and the team assumes the speed is the point.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 25,000 One-Leg Out payment sent\n→ Euro leg plus non-euro leg\n→ OCT Inst clears in 10 seconds\n→ Team judges it on speed alone\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"One-Leg Out is a speed story.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOne-Leg Out means a payment starts in Europe and goes outside it, with a euro leg plus a leg outside the euro area, and the One-Leg Out Instant Credit Transfer, OCT Inst, clears such cross-border payments in 10 seconds. As part of SEPA, OCT Inst follows the SEPA rule set, and clears and settles through the Euro leg, while payments can be made around the clock, every day of the year. Speed is not the point, an efficient, consistent and compliant process is.\n\nSpeed is not the point. An efficient, consistent and compliant process is.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge One-Leg Out on efficiency, consistency and compliance, with the 10 second clear as the result, not the goal.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Euro leg\n→ Non-euro leg\n→ SEPA rule set\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payment was efficient, consistent and compliant?\"\n\nHave you called a 10 second payment a success without checking the process behind it?", "takeaway": "Speed is not the point. An efficient, consistent and compliant process is.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment starts in Europe", "note": "One-Leg Out: the payment starts in Europe and goes outside it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Euro leg", "note": "Euro amount is paid by the Euro Leg Payer PSP and credited to the Euro Leg Payee PSP."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Non-euro leg", "note": "Leg outside the Euro area, under non-euro rules and bank policies."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Did both legs follow their rules?", "note": "Speed alone cannot prove it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cleared and settled in 10 seconds", "note": "OCT Inst clears and settles through the Euro leg in 10 seconds, 24/7/365."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-003", "seq": 380, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 500,000 payment on a blockchain network, and no one could say who settles it", "explain": "A EUR 500,000 payment was sent across a blockchain payment network.\n\nIt arrived, but Operations could not say who had settled it or where the settlement proof sat.\n\nThe network had no single settlement account to point at.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Payment sent between validated, keyed nodes\n→ Every node holds a copy of the distributed ledger\n→ Ledger refreshed with the transaction\n→ Accounting posted to participant accounts inside the ledger\n→ Settlement left to the network, with no central settlement account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Settlement works the same way as in a clearing system.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a blockchain network there is no central bank account and no clearing house holding the settlement. Each participant maintains the ledger, and settlement is the ledger update itself. Compliance checks still have to run alongside near real time processing, and that is still a live discussion.\n\nA shared ledger is not a settlement account.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore routing a payment onto a new network, name the settlement proof, the account that holds it, and the control that runs beside the speed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Node validation and keys\n→ Ledger update as settlement\n→ Participant accounts inside the ledger\n→ Sanctions and compliance checks\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the network move the money fast?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment settled, and who holds that proof?\"\n\nHave you seen a new payment network where settlement proof was never named?", "takeaway": "A shared ledger is not a settlement account.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "How a blockchain payment network settles", "parts": [{"label": "Validated keyed nodes", "note": "Each participant holds cryptographic keys for encryption."}, {"label": "Mutual awareness", "note": "Every node learns of a new participant joining."}, {"label": "Ledger settlement", "note": "Each participant maintains a ledger updated per transaction."}, {"label": "Accounts inside the ledger", "note": "Accounting posts to participant accounts in the ledger."}, {"label": "Controls alongside speed", "note": "Sanctions checks run beside near real time processing."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D15 Advanced and emerging payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-007", "seq": 381, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 2 million day settled in three moves, not two thousand", "explain": "Two banks exchanged 2,000 payments in a day, worth EUR 2 million in total.\n\nUnder bilateral clearing they would have settled 2,000 separate positions.\n\nUnder multilateral clearing they settled just three net positions, and the day closed on time.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ 2,000 payments flow between banks during the day\n→ Netting collapses them into a few positions\n→ Each bank ends in one net debit or net credit\n→ One connection to the clearing mechanism reaches every other bank\n→ The mechanism manages credit risk for all participants\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every payment needs its own settlement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nNetting removes the need to settle each payment separately. Multilateral clearing dominates because it saves a huge number of transactions, and bilateral clearing stays rare, gaining appeal only where the banks involved are very large.\n\nNetting turns thousands of payments into a handful of positions.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a design proposes bilateral settlement, ask what netting would save and who carries the credit risk without a central mechanism.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gross payment flows\n→ Net positions after netting\n→ Credit risk management\n→ The single connection to the mechanism\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How do we settle each payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the net position is right and the risk is covered?\"\n\nHave you seen a design settle gross when netting would have done the job?", "takeaway": "Netting turns thousands of payments into a handful of positions.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Bilateral versus multilateral clearing", "parts": [{"label": "2,000 payments, EUR 2 million", "note": "The gross flows between banks in a single day."}, {"label": "Multilateral is most frequent", "note": "Netting many payments into a few positions saves a huge number of transactions."}, {"label": "Bilateral is seldom used", "note": "It gains appeal mainly where the banks involved are very large."}, {"label": "One connection to the CSM", "note": "Banks prefer one connection to reach all other banks."}, {"label": "CSM manages credit risk", "note": "The mechanism manages credit risk for all participating banks."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-009", "seq": 382, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 4 million in net debits, and the settlement account was not a bank account", "explain": "At the end of the clearing cycle, three banks owed a combined EUR 4 million in net debits.\n\nOperations looked for the clearing mechanism's bank account to pay into.\n\nThere was none. The mechanism is a company, not a bank.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Netting ends, each bank has a net debit or a net credit position\n→ Net debit banks transfer funds to the mechanism\n→ The mechanism settles through an account it holds with the central bank\n→ The mechanism credits the accounts of banks in net credit positions\n→ Settlement completes in central bank money\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The clearing mechanism holds the money like a bank.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe mechanism is a company. It does not take deposits. It settles through its central bank account, so the funds move in central bank money with the mechanism in the middle.\n\nThe mechanism moves the money, it does not hold it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a design names a settlement account, check who owns it and what kind of money moves through it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Net debit positions\n→ Net credit positions\n→ The central bank settlement account\n→ The mechanism as intermediary, not holder\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Where do we send the settlement?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds moved in central bank money?\"\n\nHave you seen a settlement design assume the clearing mechanism was a bank?", "takeaway": "The mechanism moves the money, it does not hold it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Banks end in net debit or credit", "note": "After netting, each bank has a net debit or a net credit position."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Net debit banks pay the CSM", "note": "Banks in net debit transfer EUR 4 million to the CSM account at the central bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Funds in the central bank account?", "note": "Settlement only proceeds once the net debit funds have arrived."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "CSM credits net credit banks", "note": "The CSM credits the accounts of the banks in net credit positions."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement completes", "note": "Funds move through central bank accounts, with the CSM in the middle."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-010", "seq": 383, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 50,000 payment arrived after cut-off, and the cycle had already closed", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 payment was sent five minutes after the clearing cut-off.\n\nThe customer expected it in the same cycle.\n\nIt waited for the next one, because the cut-off had already fixed the cycle.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Transactions flow to the mechanism from all banks\n→ The mechanism tracks how many each bank sent and received\n→ Cut-off arrives, no further transactions enter that cycle\n→ Netting starts on what is available\n→ Amounts in the messages let the mechanism compute each participant position\n→ The late EUR 50,000 payment waits for the next cycle\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Send the payment and it will settle today.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA cut-off is a hard time limit, not a guideline. Once it passes, netting runs on the transactions already in the cycle, and anything later is simply not in it.\n\nCut-off is a wall, not a suggestion.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEvery payment design needs the cut-off named, the cycle it belongs to, and what happens to a payment that misses it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Transaction flows into the cycle\n→ The cut-off time\n→ Netting on what is available\n→ Positions computed from message amounts\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When was the payment sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which cycle the payment belongs to?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer promised same-cycle settlement after the cut-off passed?", "takeaway": "Cut-off is a wall, not a suggestion.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "CSM receives all transactions", "note": "It gets transactions from all banks to each bank and from each bank to all others."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "CSM tracks sent and received", "note": "It keeps count of how many transactions each bank sent and received."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Cut-off passed?", "note": "After the cut-off, no more transactions are accepted for that cycle."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Netting starts", "note": "The CSM nets with what is available, using the amounts in the messages."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Positions computed", "note": "Each participant net position is computed from the amounts."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-018", "seq": 386, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A pacs.008 worth EUR 120,000 failed validation, and no one could say which rule it broke", "explain": "A pacs.008 worth EUR 120,000 failed validation.\n\nThe team argued for a day about which rule it had broken.\n\nThe answer was sitting in a free document nobody had opened.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 built and sent for validation\n→ Validation fails, no rule cited\n→ Team debates the message structure from memory\n→ The Message Definition Report holds the content and the validation rules\n→ The rule is found in the MDR and the message is fixed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Validation rules live in the team's heads.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nValidation of a SWIFT MX message follows the ISO 20022 standard, and the authoritative place to read how a given message is validated is its Message Definition Report. The reports are free to download from the ISO 20022 website.\n\nThe MDR is the rulebook, not the team's memory.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a message fails validation, go to the MDR first. Name the message, the rule, and the evidence before debating the fix.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The message as sent\n→ The ISO 20022 standard\n→ The MDR validation rules\n→ The fix, applied to the right rule\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Why did validation fail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence in the MDR proves which rule was broken?\"\n\nHave you seen a validation failure debated for a day when the MDR had the answer?", "takeaway": "The MDR is the rulebook, not the team's memory.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Validating a SWIFT MX message", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 EUR 120,000", "note": "The message that failed validation."}, {"label": "Based on ISO 20022", "note": "MX validation follows the ISO 20022 standard."}, {"label": "Read the MDR", "note": "The Message Definition Report holds content and validation details."}, {"label": "Free to download", "note": "MDRs can be downloaded free from the ISO 20022 website."}, {"label": "Rule found, message fixed", "note": "The broken rule was in the MDR all along."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-020", "seq": 387, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 25,000 pacs.008 arrived, and the beneficiary waited until the next day", "explain": "A EUR 25,000 pacs.008 arrived at the beneficiary bank on the settlement day.\n\nThe beneficiary was credited the next day.\n\nThe bank had kept a day of float it was never allowed to keep.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 arrives at the creditor agent\n→ Settlement day arrives, funds available\n→ Creditor agent holds the funds overnight\n→ Beneficiary credited the next day\n→ Customer complains, and the delay is found in the posting\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Credit the beneficiary when the posting batch runs.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOnce pacs.008 arrives, the creditor agent must put the funds at the beneficiary disposal on the settlement day. The Payment Services Directive leaves the beneficiary bank no float, so the credit cannot slip past that day. The settlement day itself is the maximum window.\n\nThe settlement day is the deadline, not the start of the window.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA credit deadline is a same-day obligation. If the posting batch runs the next day, the batch is wrong, not the rule.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.008 arrival\n→ Settlement day\n→ Beneficiary credit\n→ Float, which the bank is not allowed to keep\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When does the batch run?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary was credited on the settlement day?\"\n\nHave you seen a beneficiary credited late because the batch ran the next day?", "takeaway": "The settlement day is the deadline, not the start of the window.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Crediting the beneficiary after pacs.008", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 EUR 25,000", "note": "The payment arrives at the creditor agent."}, {"label": "Funds available on settlement day", "note": "The creditor agent must credit the beneficiary on the settlement day."}, {"label": "No float for the bank", "note": "The Payment Services Directive allows no float for the beneficiary bank."}, {"label": "Credit slipped a day", "note": "The beneficiary was credited the next day, a day late."}, {"label": "Maximum window is the day", "note": "The beneficiary is credited on the settlement day, not later."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-021", "seq": 388, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "One MT101 for EUR 60,000 in payables, and the ERP only spoke one language", "explain": "A corporate had EUR 60,000 in payables to send: domestic transfers, a cheque payment, and a cross-border payment.\n\nIts ERP generated one MT101.\n\nEvery payment went out on the same message, because the ERP only had to speak one language.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Corporate ERP generates one MT101 for all payables\n→ Domestic RTGS, EFT and ACH payments start on MT101\n→ Cheque payments start on MT101\n→ Cross-border payments start on MT101\n→ One standard format covers every kind of payment the corporate makes\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT101 is only for cross-border payments.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCorporates initiate domestic payments with MT101 just as they initiate cross-border ones. The commercial logic is simple: one standard format from the ERP initiates all payables, so the corporate does not maintain a different format per payment type.\n\nOne format from the ERP, every payable covered.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a corporate asks for a new payment type, check first whether the MT101 it already sends can carry it, before building a second channel.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic payments on MT101\n→ Cross-border payments on MT101\n→ The single ERP format\n→ Each payment type it initiates\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which message starts this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the existing MT101 flow already covers it?\"\n\nHave you seen a second payment channel built when the MT101 already did the job?", "takeaway": "One format from the ERP, every payable covered.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT101 for domestic and cross-border payments", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 60,000 in payables", "note": "Domestic, cheque and cross-border payments in one batch."}, {"label": "Domestic payments too", "note": "MT101 starts RTGS, EFT, ACH and cheque payments in country."}, {"label": "Cross-border payments", "note": "MT101 also starts international payments."}, {"label": "One format for the ERP", "note": "The corporate ERP generates a single standard format for all payables."}, {"label": "Every payable, one message", "note": "One MT101 format can initiate every kind of corporate payment."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-022", "seq": 389, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 90,000 MT101 arrived at the concentrating bank, and the bank passed it on untouched", "explain": "A corporate sent one MT101 worth EUR 90,000 to its concentrating bank, covering three destination banks.\n\nThe concentrating bank did not write three new instructions.\n\nIt forwarded the same MT101, split per destination, and processed only its own share.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Corporate sends one MT101 to the concentrating bank\n→ Concentrating bank receives the MT101\n→ Same MT101 forwarded to each destination bank\n→ The bank extracts the MT101 due to each destination\n→ It processes its own instruction, the one where it stands as debtor bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each bank in the chain writes a new instruction.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn the concentration cases the bank forwards the MT101 it received instead of originating a new one. The same instruction travels onward, split per destination where needed, so nothing is re-keyed and nothing drifts from the original.\n\nForward the instruction, do not rewrite it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment passes through a concentrating bank, check that the original instruction survives intact and that each destination gets its own extract.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The MT101 as received\n→ The MT101 as forwarded per destination\n→ The bank's own instruction as debtor bank\n→ The original instruction, unchanged\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we create the onward instructions?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the forwarded MT101 matches the original?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment re-keyed in the middle of a chain and changed by it?", "takeaway": "Forward the instruction, do not rewrite it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Concentrating bank receives MT101", "note": "The corporate sends one MT101 worth EUR 90,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Same MT101 is forwarded", "note": "The bank forwards the same MT101, not a new one."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "One destination or several?", "note": "The instruction is split per destination where needed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Extract per destination", "note": "It extracts the MT101 to be forwarded to each destination bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Process its own share", "note": "It processes the MT101 for which it is the debtor bank."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-025", "seq": 390, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 15,000 SEPA payment on an MT101, and the bank said it could not carry it", "explain": "A corporate sent a EUR 15,000 SEPA payment on an MT101.\n\nOne bank processed it. Another refused it.\n\nBoth were right, because whether a bank processes MT101 as a SEPA payment turns entirely on that bank's own capabilities.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Corporate sends a SEPA payment on MT101\n→ Bank A processes it as SEPA\n→ Bank B refuses it, the message cannot hold all the information\n→ The missing information is what pain.001 can carry and MT101 cannot\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If MT101 can carry SEPA, every bank will process it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA SEPA payment can travel on an MT101, but the message cannot hold all the information a pain.001 can carry. Where the information fits, MT101 works for SEPA. Where it does not, pain.001 is the right message.\n\nMT101 can carry SEPA, but only as far as the information fits.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore choosing the message, check what information the payment needs and whether the receiving bank can process it. The message choice follows the data, not the habit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The information the payment needs\n→ What MT101 can carry\n→ What pain.001 can carry\n→ The receiving bank's capability\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can MT101 carry SEPA?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the receiving bank can process this payment on MT101?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment refused because the message could not carry the data?", "takeaway": "MT101 can carry SEPA, but only as far as the information fits.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT101 as a SEPA payment", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 15,000 SEPA on MT101", "note": "A SEPA payment sent with an MT101."}, {"label": "Bank A processes it", "note": "Where the information fits, MT101 works for SEPA."}, {"label": "Bank B refuses it", "note": "The message cannot hold all the information the payment needs."}, {"label": "Depends on the bank", "note": "Whether a bank processes MT101 as SEPA depends on its capabilities."}, {"label": "pain.001 when it does not fit", "note": "Where the information does not fit, pain.001 is the right message."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "pain.001"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-026", "seq": 391, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "An MT101 for EUR 40,000 arrived, and the debit and the money took different messages", "explain": "A corporate sent an MT101 for EUR 40,000 to its bank.\n\nThe account was debited, but no money moved.\n\nThe MT101 only instructs the debit. The money moves on a different message.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Corporate sends MT101 to the bank\n→ Account servicing institution holds the account to be debited\n→ ASI processes the MT101 against that account\n→ ASI issues an MT103 to move the funds onward\n→ The MT101 instructs the debit and the MT103 carries the money\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The MT101 moves the money.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe account servicing institution is the bank holding the account to be debited. On receiving the MT101 it processes the instruction against that account, then it issues an MT103 to move the funds onward. Two messages, two jobs.\n\nMT101 instructs, MT103 carries.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment stalls after the debit, check whether the onward MT103 was ever issued. The debit is not the payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The MT101 instruction\n→ The account debit\n→ The MT103 onward movement\n→ The funds arriving at the beneficiary\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the account debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the MT103 was issued and the funds moved?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment treated as done because the debit posted?", "takeaway": "MT101 instructs, MT103 carries.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT101 arrives", "note": "The ordering customer sends an MT101 for EUR 40,000 to the bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ASI holds the account", "note": "The account servicing institution holds the account to be debited."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ASI processes the MT101", "note": "It processes the instruction against the account."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Debit posted?", "note": "The debit is only the first half; the funds still need to move."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "ASI issues an MT103", "note": "It issues an MT103 to move the funds onward."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-031", "seq": 392, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 3,000 direct debit arrived with no mandate, and the creditor bank paid it anyway", "explain": "A pacs.003 direct debit for EUR 3,000 arrived at the creditor bank.\n\nThere was no active mandate behind it.\n\nThe bank processed it anyway, because the scheme does not require it to check.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Creditor sends pacs.003 for EUR 3,000\n→ Creditor bank receives the collection\n→ No mandate check is run\n→ Collection processed, debtor account debited\n→ Debtor disputes the collection afterwards\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The creditor bank must check the mandate.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSEPA applies the creditor driven mandate flow, so the creditor bank carries no obligation to check a pacs.003 against an active mandate. The creditor bank may still validate if it chooses, but the scheme does not require it. That contrasts with a debtor driven flow, where the debtor side holds the mandate.\n\nNo obligation to check does not mean no risk.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a design relies on a mandate check, name who owns it. If the creditor bank does not own it, the control must live somewhere else, or the dispute will.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The pacs.003 collection\n→ The mandate, held on the creditor side\n→ The optional validation\n→ The dispute when the mandate is missing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we check the mandate?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves someone owns the mandate check?\"\n\nHave you seen a direct debit collected with no mandate behind it?", "takeaway": "No obligation to check does not mean no risk.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Checking mandates for pacs.003", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.003 EUR 3,000", "note": "A direct debit collection arrives with no active mandate."}, {"label": "Creditor driven flow applies", "note": "In SEPA the creditor driven mandate flow is used."}, {"label": "No obligation to check", "note": "The creditor bank need not validate pacs.003 against mandates."}, {"label": "Validation is optional", "note": "The creditor bank may still choose to validate."}, {"label": "Debtor driven differs", "note": "In a debtor driven flow the debtor side holds the mandate."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": ["pacs.003"], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch62-sepa-direct-debit-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 62: SEPA Direct Debit Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch62", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-001", "seq": 393, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A store voucher for R1,000 shoes worked, and the customer asked why it could not pay anywhere", "explain": "A customer bought shoes worth R1,000 with a clothing store voucher.\n\nIt worked perfectly in that store.\n\nThen the customer tried to use the same voucher at a shop across the road, and it was refused.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer pays R1,000 with a store voucher\n→ Voucher accepted at the issuing store\n→ Customer tries the voucher at another shop\n→ Voucher refused, it only works inside its own loop\n→ Customer asks why a payment that worked once cannot work everywhere\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A payment that works is a payment that works everywhere.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe voucher is a closed loop system, usually run by a non-financial service provider, and the payment is used for goods or services inside that loop only. An open loop system is run by financial service providers regulated within the national payment system, and its payments work across the system.\n\nA closed loop payment works inside its loop, and only there.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment product is designed, name the loop first. The loop decides where the payment works, who regulates it, and what the customer can expect.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The closed loop and its issuer\n→ The open loop and its regulated providers\n→ Where the payment is accepted\n→ What the customer was told\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the customer pay?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer knows where this payment works?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer caught out by a payment that only worked in one place?", "takeaway": "A closed loop payment works inside its loop, and only there.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Open loop and closed loop payment systems", "parts": [{"label": "Store voucher R1,000", "note": "A clothing store voucher for a pair of shoes worth R1,000."}, {"label": "Closed loop: run by non-financial service providers", "note": "The payment is used for goods or services inside the loop."}, {"label": "Works in one store only", "note": "Refused at the shop across the road."}, {"label": "Open loop: run by financial service providers", "note": "Providers regulated in the national payment system environment."}, {"label": "Works across the system", "note": "An open loop payment works wherever the system reaches."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN7-002", "seq": 394, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A customer tried to pay EUR 10,000 with a promissory note, and the bank said no", "explain": "A customer walked into a bank with a promissory note for EUR 10,000 and asked to pay with it.\n\nThe law in that country allowed private individuals to issue one.\n\nThe bank refused it anyway.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer offers a personal promissory note for EUR 10,000\n→ In some countries, such as France, only businesses can issue promissory notes\n→ In this country the law permits private individuals to issue them\n→ The bank stays silent about them and steers people toward bank loans\n→ The bank refuses the note, even where the law allows it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the law allows it, the bank must accept it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nLegal permission is not the same as bank acceptance. Banks stay silent about promissory notes and steer people toward bank loans, which are legally and financially more interesting for the banks. A bank or finance company may therefore refuse a personal promissory note even where the law allows it.\n\nLegal does not mean accepted.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment instrument is designed, check three gates separately: the law, the bank's policy, and the customer's understanding. All three must pass.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What the law permits\n→ What the bank accepts\n→ What the customer is told\n→ The alternative the bank offers instead\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is this instrument legal?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the bank will accept it?\"\n\nHave you seen a legal payment method refused at the counter?", "takeaway": "Legal does not mean accepted.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Can a private individual pay with a promissory note?", "parts": [{"label": "Promissory note EUR 10,000", "note": "A customer offers a personal promissory note to pay."}, {"label": "France: only businesses can issue", "note": "In some countries, such as France, promissory notes can be issued only by businesses."}, {"label": "Other places: the law may allow it", "note": "The law may allow private individuals to issue them."}, {"label": "Banks do not communicate about them", "note": "Banks would rather people take bank loans, which are legally and financially more interesting for the banks."}, {"label": "Refusal is possible", "note": "A bank or finance company may refuse a personal promissory note even where the law allows it."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN7-003", "seq": 395, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 2,000 card payment through a wallet arrived in seconds, but the bank account was debited two days later", "explain": "A customer paid EUR 2,000 by card through a wallet.\n\nThe seller was credited right away.\n\nThe customer's bank account was debited two days later, and the customer thought the money had been taken twice.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer pays EUR 2,000 by card through the wallet\n→ Wallet checks the balance and credits the beneficiary at once\n→ Bank account behind the card is debited one or two days later\n→ Customer sees the credit and the late debit and reports a double charge\n→ Investigation shows one payment, two timings\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Push and pull describe the technology.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPush and pull describe the movement of the instruction and the funds, never the technology behind the payment. A credit transfer started directly from a bank account counts as push, with the account debited right away. A PayPal reserve debit sent straight to the beneficiary counts as push. Paying by card through the wallet counts as pull, and the timing gap is built into it.\n\nThe same payment can look instant and delayed at the same time.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment journey mixes push and pull, show the customer both timings. The debit timing and the credit timing are separate facts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The instruction and the funds movement\n→ The beneficiary credit timing\n→ The bank account debit timing\n→ The customer's view of both\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment go through?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer can see when each leg moves?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer report a double charge that was really one pull payment?", "takeaway": "The same payment can look instant and delayed at the same time.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Push or pull describes the movement", "note": "It describes how the instruction and the funds move, not the technology used."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Card payment is pull", "note": "Wallet checks the balance, credits the beneficiary at once, but the bank account is debited one or two days later."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Customer sees two timings?", "note": "The credit is instant, the debit is late, and it looks like a double charge."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank account credit transfer is push", "note": "A credit transfer initiated directly from a bank account is a push transaction, with the account debited right away."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Show both timings", "note": "The debit timing and the credit timing are separate facts to show the customer."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D03 Payment lifecycle", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch4-payment-lifecycle-end-to-end.html", "label": "Chapter 4: Payment Lifecycle End to End", "chapter": "ch4", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-004", "seq": 396, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 5,000 wallet transfer failed, and the money was in the other account all along", "explain": "A customer tried to move EUR 5,000 from a mobile wallet to a bank account.\n\nThe transfer failed for insufficient funds.\n\nThe customer had EUR 5,000 in the bank account, and assumed that was enough.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer moves EUR 5,000 from wallet to bank\n→ Wallet holds only EUR 1,200\n→ Transfer refused, wallet has insufficient funds\n→ Customer complains, pointing at the bank balance\n→ The bank balance is irrelevant, the wallet is the source account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"My money is my money, whichever account it sits in.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMoving funds between a mobile wallet and a bank account means moving money between two accounts belonging to the same customer, and push transactions should be enough for these moves. But a wallet to bank transfer should start only when the wallet holds sufficient funds, and a bank to wallet transfer only when the bank account holds sufficient funds. The source account is the one that must be funded.\n\nSame owner, different accounts, different balances.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a transfer moves money between a customer's own accounts, the funds check must run on the source account, and the error must say which account is short.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The wallet balance\n→ The bank account balance\n→ The source account funds check\n→ The error message naming the short account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the customer have the money?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the source account holds the funds?\"\n\nHave you seen a transfer refused when the customer clearly had the money, in the other account?", "takeaway": "Same owner, different accounts, different balances.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Moving money between a mobile wallet and a bank account", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 5,000 wallet to bank", "note": "The customer tries to move EUR 5,000 from the wallet."}, {"label": "Wallet holds EUR 1,200", "note": "The wallet is the source account and it is short."}, {"label": "Bank holds EUR 5,000", "note": "The bank balance is irrelevant to a wallet to bank transfer."}, {"label": "Push transactions should be enough", "note": "Push transactions should be enough for these moves."}, {"label": "Check the source account", "note": "A transfer should start only when the source account holds sufficient funds."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch70-accounts-and-balances.html", "label": "Chapter 70: Accounts and balances", "chapter": "ch70", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-005", "seq": 397, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A card worked in every SEPA country, but the cash still did not travel", "explain": "A traveller used a bank card in five SEPA countries on one trip.\n\nIt worked everywhere.\n\nThen the traveller tried to manage cash the same way across those countries, and found the rules changed at every border.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Card used in any SEPA country, regardless of where it was issued\n→ SEPA Cards Framework complete across the SEPA area\n→ Visa and Mastercard as the two main card networks\n→ Interoperability that was already working, accelerated by SEPA\n→ Cash handling still different everywhere, the SEPA Cash initiative remains a work in progress\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SEPA finished the job for cards and cash together.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe SEPA Cards Framework stands complete, and major projects that began in 2007 to 2010 are complete. But cash is a sensitive area and standardising practices everywhere in SEPA is a real challenge, so the SEPA Cash initiative is still ongoing.\n\nCards finished, cash still catching up.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a programme claims SEPA coverage, ask which instrument it covers. A complete card framework says nothing about cash.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The SEPA Cards Framework, complete\n→ The SEPA Cash initiative, ongoing\n→ Card interoperability across countries\n→ Cash practices, still different per country\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are we SEPA compliant?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which SEPA instrument this covers?\"\n\nHave you seen a SEPA claim that covered cards but not cash?", "takeaway": "Cards finished, cash still catching up.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "SEPA Cards Framework and SEPA Cash: where each stands", "parts": [{"label": "Card works in five countries", "note": "One trip, one card, accepted everywhere in SEPA."}, {"label": "SEPA Cards Framework is complete", "note": "It is complete in the SEPA area, with Visa and Mastercard as the two main card networks."}, {"label": "Interoperability accelerated", "note": "Interoperability that was already working was accelerated by SEPA."}, {"label": "Major projects 2007 to 2010 complete", "note": "Major projects began in 2007 to 2010 and are complete."}, {"label": "SEPA Cash is still ongoing", "note": "Cash is a sensitive area and standardizing practices everywhere in SEPA is a real challenge."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-007", "seq": 398, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "An MT103 for EUR 75,000 was sent to the wrong application, and the payment vanished from view", "explain": "A bank sent an MT103 for EUR 75,000 to another bank.\n\nIt never arrived in the payment queue.\n\nIt had been sent through the General Purpose Application, which only carries system messages.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Bank sends MT103 for EUR 75,000\n→ Message routed through the General Purpose Application\n→ GPA carries system messages only: a user writing to SWIFT, or SWIFT writing to a user\n→ No user to user traffic is allowed on GPA\n→ Payment never reaches the other bank's payment queue\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SWIFT is SWIFT, any application will carry any message.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe FIN Application carries messages from one user to another, and alongside that it covers the user-to-user range MT1xx through MT9xx, the system messages MT0xx, plus other service messages such as acknowledgements. The distinction is about who a message can travel between, not the network itself.\n\nThe right network, the wrong application.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a message goes missing, check the application it travelled on before checking the message itself. A user to user message on a system-only application never arrives.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The message and its type\n→ The application it travels on\n→ Who the message can travel between\n→ The receiving queue it should land in\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the message?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves it travelled on the application that carries user to user traffic?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment lost because it went through the wrong application?", "takeaway": "The right network, the wrong application.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "SWIFT General Purpose Application versus FIN Application", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 EUR 75,000", "note": "A user to user payment message sent to another bank."}, {"label": "Sent on GPA", "note": "The General Purpose Application carries system messages only."}, {"label": "GPA: user to SWIFT only", "note": "It allows only system messages, from a user to SWIFT or from SWIFT to a user, but not from one user to another."}, {"label": "FIN carries user to user", "note": "It allows messages from one user to another, MT1xx through MT9xx."}, {"label": "FIN includes MT0xx and acks", "note": "It includes the system messages MT0xx and other service messages such as acknowledgements."}, {"label": "The difference is who messages travel between", "note": "The difference is who the message can travel between, not the network itself."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT0xx", "MT1xx", "MT9xx"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-009", "seq": 399, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 1,500 card payment was guaranteed, and the merchant asked who actually paid for that promise", "explain": "A merchant accepted a EUR 1,500 card payment.\n\nThe customer later defaulted.\n\nThe merchant was still paid, because the issuer had guaranteed the payment.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer pays EUR 1,500 by card\n→ Issuer authorises the transaction after accepting all risk\n→ Issuer guarantees payment to the merchant\n→ Customer defaults\n→ Merchant still paid, the issuer absorbs the loss\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Interchange is a fee the card network charges.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCard networks do not charge interchange. The fee compensates the issuer, who takes on all the risk, authorises the transaction and guarantees payment to the merchant. That guarantee is what interchange pays for.\n\nInterchange pays for the guarantee, not the network.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a pricing discussion treats interchange as a network charge, correct it. The fee follows the risk, and the risk sits with the issuer.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The network, which does not charge interchange\n→ The issuer, who takes the risk\n→ The authorisation decision\n→ The guarantee to the merchant\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What does the network charge?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves who carries the risk on this transaction?\"\n\nHave you seen interchange blamed on the network when the issuer carries the risk?", "takeaway": "Interchange pays for the guarantee, not the network.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Why interchange exists: the issuer takes the risk", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 1,500 card payment", "note": "The merchant accepted the payment and was paid."}, {"label": "Not a network charge", "note": "Card networks do not charge interchange."}, {"label": "It compensates the issuer", "note": "Interchange compensates the issuer."}, {"label": "The issuer authorises after accepting all risk", "note": "The issuer authorises the transaction after accepting all risk."}, {"label": "The issuer guarantees payment to the merchant", "note": "The issuer guarantees the payment to the merchant."}, {"label": "The guarantee is what interchange pays for", "note": "The issuer's guarantee is what the interchange pays for."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch63-card-payments-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 63: Card Payments Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch63", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-27", "seq": 400, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An AI agent paid a EUR 12,000 vendor invoice, and no human had authorised it", "explain": "A vendor invoice for EUR 12,000 was paid on a Tuesday morning.\n\nNo human had approved the payment.\n\nAn AI agent had run its six steps and decided the invoice was fine.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Agent understands the payment request\n→ Agent verifies the payment details\n→ Agent evaluates the payment\n→ Agent authorises the payment itself\n→ Agent executes the payment of EUR 12,000\n→ Agent confirms the payment to the customer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the agent follows the steps, the payment is safe.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe authorisation step is the control that makes the whole flow safe, and in this flow the agent authorised itself. Understand, verify and evaluate are checks. Authorise is the decision, and it cannot be the agent approving its own work.\n\nAn agent that authorises itself is not controlled, it is unsupervised.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen an AI agent pays, the authorisation must sit with a human or a separate control, with a limit, a named approver, and evidence of the approval. Execution without independent authorisation is not automation, it is exposure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Understanding and verification\n→ Evaluation\n→ Independent authorisation\n→ Execution and confirmation\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the agent pay the invoice?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a human approved this payment before it moved?\"\n\nHave you seen an automated payment go out with no independent approval?", "takeaway": "An agent that authorises itself is not controlled, it is unsupervised.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Understand", "note": "Agent understands the EUR 12,000 payment request."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Verify", "note": "Agent verifies the payment details."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Evaluate", "note": "Agent evaluates the payment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Authorize", "note": "The control that makes the flow safe, and here the agent authorised itself."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Execute", "note": "Pays the EUR 12,000 vendor invoice with no human approval."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm", "note": "Payment confirmed to the customer after the money moved."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-28", "seq": 401, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An AI agent spent EUR 8,000 on API calls in one weekend, a few cents at a time", "explain": "An AI agent paid for API calls with stablecoins all weekend.\n\nEach call cost a few cents.\n\nBy Monday morning the total was EUR 8,000, and no one had set a limit.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Agent calls an API to do its work\n→ Each call is paid instantly in stablecoins, pay per use\n→ Micropayments flow with no human in the loop\n→ Programmable payments trigger themselves\n→ EUR 8,000 accumulates over the weekend, a few cents at a time\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Small payments cannot add up to a big loss.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nInternet-native payments let a machine pay a machine for exactly what it used, and that is the point. But pay per use with no cap, no daily limit and no alert turns a clever mechanism into an open tap. Micropayments are still payments.\n\nA thousand tiny payments is still EUR 8,000.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a machine pays a machine, the controls must be machine sized too: a spend cap, a velocity limit, an alert threshold, and a named owner who sees the total.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The payment mechanism\n→ The spend cap\n→ The velocity and alert limits\n→ The owner who sees the total\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the agent pay per use?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the total spend stayed inside its limit?\"\n\nHave you seen micropayments add up to a loss no one noticed?", "takeaway": "A thousand tiny payments is still EUR 8,000.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Internet-native payments, machine to machine", "parts": [{"label": "Stablecoins", "note": "The payment currency for agents."}, {"label": "Pay-per-use", "note": "Pay for exactly what was used, each API call a few cents."}, {"label": "Micropayments", "note": "Small payments machines can make, EUR 8,000 by Monday."}, {"label": "Programmable payments", "note": "Payments a machine can trigger with no human in the loop."}, {"label": "APIs and digital services", "note": "What agents pay for."}, {"label": "No cap, no alert", "note": "The missing control that let the total run away."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D15 Advanced and emerging payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "ABH-29", "seq": 402, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An AI assistant drafted a BRD in minutes, and the EUR 100,000 settlement leg was missing", "explain": "A bank asked an AI assistant built for banking to draft a business requirements document for a new payment flow.\n\nThe BRD arrived in minutes, and it looked complete.\n\nThe settlement leg for EUR 100,000 was missing from it entirely.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ AI assistant drafts the BRD from banking knowledge\n→ Customer conversation flows and requirements captured\n→ Document reads well and is approved quickly\n→ Build starts on the BRD\n→ Settlement leg discovered missing during testing, EUR 100,000 with nowhere to settle\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An assistant built for banking understands banking.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe assistant is aimed at the daily work of a banking professional, and it can draft fast. But a BRD is not a summary of banking knowledge. It is a complete statement of one specific flow, and only a person who owns that flow can say whether a leg is missing.\n\nFast drafting is not complete requirements.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAn AI-drafted BRD still needs a human owner to walk the full payment journey, message by message, and sign that every leg is in it. Speed is a benefit only after completeness is proved.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The AI draft\n→ The human walkthrough of the full journey\n→ The settlement leg, named explicitly\n→ The sign-off that the BRD is complete\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the assistant draft the BRD?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every leg of the payment is in the document?\"\n\nHave you seen an AI-drafted document approved before anyone checked it was complete?", "takeaway": "Fast drafting is not complete requirements.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "An AI assistant for banking work", "parts": [{"label": "Banking knowledge", "note": "Answers grounded in banking."}, {"label": "BRD creation", "note": "Drafts business requirements in minutes."}, {"label": "Customer conversations", "note": "Handles customer-facing chat."}, {"label": "Settlement leg missing", "note": "The EUR 100,000 settlement leg was not in the draft."}, {"label": "Human walkthrough needed", "note": "Only a person who owns the flow can say whether a leg is missing."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-30", "seq": 403, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A BRD promised in ten minutes took three days to fix, and the payment was late", "explain": "A banking chatbot and BRD tool promised the same document in ten minutes instead of three days.\n\nThe team took the ten-minute BRD and started building.\n\nThree days later they were still fixing it, and the payment flow went live late.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Tool promises a BRD in ten minutes\n→ Document delivered fast, same shape as the old three-day version\n→ Build starts immediately\n→ Gaps found in the payment logic during build\n→ Three days spent fixing the document the tool had produced in ten minutes\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Faster means better.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe claim is about speed: the same banking document, produced far faster. But a document produced fast and then fixed for three days is not faster. The time moved from drafting to rework, and the payment still went live late.\n\nTen minutes to draft, three days to fix, is not ten minutes.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMeasure a tool by the time from request to a correct document, not from request to a first draft. Rework time counts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Drafting time\n→ Review and rework time\n→ The payment logic, verified end to end\n→ The go-live date, protected\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How fast can the document be produced?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the document was right the first time?\"\n\nHave you seen a fast draft cost more time in rework than the slow version would have?", "takeaway": "Ten minutes to draft, three days to fix, is not ten minutes.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Smarter banking chatbots and BRDs", "parts": [{"label": "BRD in ten minutes", "note": "Same document, produced far faster."}, {"label": "BRD in three days", "note": "Old time to produce the document."}, {"label": "Three days of fixes", "note": "The fast draft needed three days of rework."}, {"label": "Payment went live late", "note": "The rework pushed the go-live date."}, {"label": "Speed", "note": "Claim is about speed, not new content."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-029", "seq": 448, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 60,000 payment vanished between states, and no one could say which state it was in", "explain": "A customer sent a EUR 60,000 payment.\n\nIt never arrived.\n\nOperations searched for it, but no one could say which state it was in, because no state had a named owner or a piece of evidence.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Initiation creates the instruction with an instruction ID and an end-to-end ID\n→ Validation checks the instruction\n→ Routing chooses the rail\n→ Clearing moves it through the scheme\n→ Settlement moves the funds between banks\n→ Posting records it in the ledger\n→ Confirmation tells the customer it is done\n→ The payment stopped somewhere in the chain, and the evidence stopped with it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The payment will be found by asking around.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment moves through a chain of states from initiation to confirmation, and each state needs a named owner and a named piece of evidence. When a payment goes missing, the investigation follows this chain state by state, because the state where the evidence stops is the state where the payment is stuck.\n\nThe state where the evidence stops is where the payment is stuck.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every state an owner and an evidence item before the first payment flows. When one goes missing, walk the chain state by state and stop where the evidence stops.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiation and its IDs\n→ Validation, routing, clearing\n→ Settlement and posting\n→ Confirmation to the customer\n→ The owner and evidence for each state\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Where is the payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which state it reached?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment vanish because no state had an owner?", "takeaway": "The state where the evidence stops is where the payment is stuck.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Initiation", "note": "Creates the instruction with instruction and end-to-end IDs."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Evidence stops here?", "note": "The state where the evidence stops is the state where the payment is stuck."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "Moves the payment through the scheme."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Moves the funds between banks."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Posting", "note": "Records the payment in the ledger."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirmation", "note": "Tells the customer the payment is done."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch68-payment-operations-architecture-state-resilience-and-control.html", "label": "Chapter 68: Payment Operations Architecture: State, Resilience and Control", "chapter": "ch68", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-031", "seq": 449, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 2,500 card payment was declined at authorisation, and the team investigated settlement", "explain": "A customer tried to pay EUR 2,500 by card.\n\nThe payment was declined at the terminal.\n\nThe team spent a day investigating settlement, because no one had named which stage the failure belonged to.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Terminal sends card and transaction details to the acquirer\n→ Scheme routes them to the issuer\n→ Issuer checks card status, available credit and fraud rules\n→ Issuer declines and places no hold on the funds\n→ Team investigates clearing and settlement, the wrong stages\n→ The failure was at authorisation, in seconds\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A card failure is a card failure, any stage will do.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA credit card transaction has four distinct stages with different owners and different evidence. Authorisation happens in seconds, clearing exchanges the transaction records, settlement moves the funds between the banks, and billing and dispute touchpoints handle the customer statement and any later dispute. Each stage must be understood separately, because a problem at authorisation looks nothing like a problem at settlement.\n\nThe wrong stage means the wrong investigation.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a card payment fails, name the stage first: authorisation, clearing, settlement, or billing and disputes. Then investigate that stage, with its owner and its evidence.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorisation, in seconds\n→ Clearing, exchanging records\n→ Settlement, moving funds\n→ Billing and disputes, handling the statement\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Why did the card payment fail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which stage it failed at?\"\n\nHave you seen a team investigate the wrong stage of a card payment?", "takeaway": "The wrong stage means the wrong investigation.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Authorisation", "note": "Issuer checks card, credit and fraud rules, then holds funds. The EUR 2,500 payment was declined here."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Declined at authorisation?", "note": "Name the stage first, before investigating any other stage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "Exchanges the transaction records."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Moves the funds between the banks."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Billing and disputes", "note": "Handles the statement and any later dispute."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch21-card-authorization-clearing-and-settlement.html", "label": "Chapter 21: Card Authorization, Clearing and Settlement", "chapter": "ch21", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C041", "seq": 478, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "pacs.008 passed the clearing system. Where is the pacs.002?", "explain": "A EUR 75,000 payment is sent through the clearing system to the beneficiary bank.\nThe pacs.008 has gone.\nThe beneficiary bank is waiting, the customer is asking, and nobody can say where the status reply is.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent to the clearing system\n→ Clearing system processes the payment\n→ pacs.008 reaches the beneficiary bank\n→ pacs.002 status reply travels back through the clearing system\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The clearing system is just a pipe.\"\n\nThe clearing system sits between the originator bank and the beneficiary bank, so the pacs.008 passes through it and the pacs.002 returns through it. An analyst drawing the flow must include the clearing system as a party, not as a pipe, because it sends and receives messages of its own.\n\nThe clearing system is a party, not a pipe.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDraw the clearing system as a party with messages of its own. The pacs.008 passes through it on the way out, and the pacs.002 passes through it on the way back, and each hop is a message with a sender, a receiver and a status.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.008 outward\n→ Clearing system processing\n→ pacs.008 to beneficiary bank\n→ pacs.002 status back\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment leave?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the pacs.008 is, and where the pacs.002 reply stands?\"\n\nHave you ever drawn a payment flow and left the clearing system out as if it were just a wire?", "takeaway": "An analyst drawing the flow must include the clearing system as a party, not as a pipe, because it sends and receives messages of its own.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 passes through clearing system", "note": "The pacs.008 reaches the beneficiary bank through the clearing system."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing system sends and receives", "note": "The clearing system is a party with messages of its own."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.002 reports status back", "note": "The pacs.002 reports the status back through the clearing system."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the status reply missing or delayed?", "note": "Draw the clearing system as a party, not as a pipe."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.002"], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C043", "seq": 480, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The MT103 in a language nobody at the bank speaks", "explain": "A EUR 15,000 payment is sent to a beneficiary bank in a country whose language is not English.\nThe names and addresses in the message are in the local language.\nThe operations team stares at the MT103 and wonders whether it can even be processed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives with names in the local language\n→ Structured fields carry the account, currency and amount\n→ Processing follows the coded fields\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The message must be in English to be processed.\"\n\nWhen the beneficiary bank is in a country whose language is not English, the MT103 is still processed from its structured fields, which are coded and do not depend on the language of the country. The names and addresses in the message may be in the local language, but the fields that drive processing, such as the account number, the currency, and the amount, are numeric or coded. Processing therefore does not stop at a language barrier; it follows the fields.\n\nProcessing follows the fields, not the language.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the coded fields, not the alphabet. Account number, currency and amount are numeric or coded, and they drive processing whatever language the names are written in.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Structured fields are coded\n→ Names may be in the local language\n→ Account, currency and amount drive processing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we read the names?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the coded fields are complete and valid?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment because the names were in a language nobody on the team could read?", "takeaway": "Processing therefore does not stop at a language barrier; it follows the fields.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A payment to a non-English-speaking country", "parts": [{"label": "Structured fields are coded", "note": "The MT103 is processed from coded structured fields."}, {"label": "Names may be in the local language", "note": "Names and addresses may be in the local language."}, {"label": "Account, currency and amount drive processing", "note": "Account number, currency and amount are numeric or coded."}, {"label": "Processing follows the fields", "note": "Processing follows the fields, not the language."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C048", "seq": 485, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Faster Payments is not SEPA with a different name", "explain": "A team that knows SEPA is asked to build the flow for UK Faster Payments and BACS.\nThey reach for the SEPA messages, because the concepts look the same: initiation, clearing, status reporting.\nThen the first GBP 25,000 payment fails, because the UK messages are not the SEPA messages.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ SEPA concepts: initiation, clearing, status\n→ UK Faster Payments messages differ from SEPA\n→ BACS messages differ from SEPA\n→ Payment fails on the wrong message set\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SEPA messages work everywhere.\"\n\nThe messages exchanged between banks in UK Faster Payments and in BACS are different from the messages used in SEPA. The SEPA message set is a good starting point, because the concepts of initiation, clearing, and status reporting are the same, but the actual messages and the clearing systems differ. An analyst who understands the SEPA flow can learn the UK flows by mapping each SEPA message to its UK equivalent, rather than starting from nothing.\n\nSame concepts, different messages. Map, do not assume.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse SEPA as the starting point, then map each SEPA message to its UK equivalent. Initiation, clearing and status reporting work the same way, but the actual messages and the clearing systems differ, and the mapping is the work.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ SEPA initiation message\n→ UK Faster Payments equivalent\n→ BACS equivalent\n→ Clearing system per rail\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we know SEPA?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each SEPA message has a named UK equivalent?\"\n\nHave you ever assumed one message set worked on another rail and watched the payment fail?", "takeaway": "An analyst who understands the SEPA flow can learn the UK flows by mapping each SEPA message to its UK equivalent, rather than starting from nothing.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "UK Faster Payments and BACS flows", "parts": [{"label": "Different messages from SEPA", "note": "Faster Payments and BACS messages differ from SEPA messages."}, {"label": "Same concepts: initiation, clearing, status", "note": "Initiation, clearing and status reporting work the same way."}, {"label": "Actual messages and clearing differ", "note": "The actual messages and clearing systems differ from SEPA."}, {"label": "Map each SEPA message to its UK equivalent", "note": "Learn UK flows by mapping each SEPA message to its UK equivalent."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C050", "seq": 487, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Faster Payments settles in seconds. Salaries still run on BACS.", "explain": "Faster Payments settles a payment in seconds.\nYet every month, a business with 2,000 employees pays salaries through BACS, a system older than most of its staff.\nThe faster rail is there, and the business still chooses the slower one.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Faster Payments settles single payments in seconds\n→ Business pays 2,000 salaries through BACS\n→ Bulk run arrives on schedule, at low cost\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Faster makes BACS obsolete.\"\n\nFaster Payments is built for single, urgent payments, and it costs more per payment and carries limits that bulk runs do not want. BACS is built for bulk, scheduled, low-cost payments such as salaries and supplier runs. The two systems therefore serve different needs, and the older one survives because it fits the bulk use case better.\n\nThe older rail survives because it fits the bulk use case.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMatch the rail to the use case, not to the speed. Single and urgent goes Faster Payments. Bulk, scheduled and low-cost goes BACS, and the salary run is the textbook bulk case.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Single urgent payments\n→ Bulk scheduled payments\n→ Cost per payment\n→ Limits per payment\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which rail is faster?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this rail fits this use case on cost, limits and timing?\"\n\nHave you ever recommended the fastest rail for a job the oldest rail does better?", "takeaway": "The two systems therefore serve different needs, and the older one survives because it fits the bulk use case better.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Why BACS is still preferred after Faster Payments", "parts": [{"label": "BACS", "note": "Bulk, scheduled, low-cost payments such as salaries and supplier runs."}, {"label": "Faster Payments", "note": "Single, urgent payments, more costly per payment."}, {"label": "Different needs", "note": "The older system survives because it fits the bulk use case."}, {"label": "Match the rail to the job", "note": "Speed is one factor. Cost, limits and scheduling decide the rest."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C052", "seq": 489, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The MT103 says euros. The MT202 COV says dollars.", "explain": "A cover payment is sent, and the two legs carry different currencies.\nThe MT103 says euros. The MT202 COV says dollars.\nThe operations team assumes one of them is wrong, and holds the EUR 150,000 payment.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 instruction in euros\n→ MT202 COV in dollars\n→ Fields 52, 53 and 54 describe the chain\n→ Payment held while the mismatch is investigated\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Both legs must carry the same currency.\"\n\nIn a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 can carry different currencies, and the question is how the currency of each leg is decided and what fields 52, 53 and 54 carry. Field 52 identifies the ordering institution, field 53 identifies the sender's correspondent, and field 54 identifies the receiver's correspondent, so together they describe the chain of banks that will fund the payment. The currency of the cover leg follows the correspondent account it settles in, which is why the two legs can differ.\n\nThe cover currency follows the correspondent account it settles in.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead fields 52, 53 and 54 before calling a mismatch. They name the ordering institution, the sender's correspondent and the receiver's correspondent, and the cover currency follows the correspondent account it settles in.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 52, ordering institution\n→ Field 53, sender's correspondent\n→ Field 54, receiver's correspondent\n→ Cover currency follows its account\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do the two legs match?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each leg's currency follows the account it settles in?\"\n\nHave you ever held a cover payment because the two legs showed different currencies?", "takeaway": "The currency of the cover leg follows the correspondent account it settles in, which is why the two legs can differ.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Currency and fields 52, 53 and 54", "parts": [{"label": "Legs can carry different currencies", "note": "The MT103 and the MT202 can carry different currencies."}, {"label": "Field 52: ordering institution", "note": "Field 52 identifies the ordering institution."}, {"label": "Field 53: sender's correspondent", "note": "Field 53 identifies the sender's correspondent."}, {"label": "Field 54: receiver's correspondent", "note": "Field 54 identifies the receiver's correspondent."}, {"label": "Cover currency follows its account", "note": "The cover currency follows the correspondent account it settles in."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C086", "seq": 495, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A future-dated MT103 for EUR 200,000, offered as security", "explain": "An MT103 value-dated far in the future is presented to a bank, and the holder asks the bank to discount it.\nEUR 200,000, dated months ahead, offered as if it were money already promised.\nThe bank declines, and the holder cannot understand why a payment instruction is not a promise.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 value-dated far in the future\n→ Presented to the bank for discounting\n→ MT103 is an instruction to pay\n→ Payer account holds sufficient funds, or it does not\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A future MT103 is proof the money will arrive.\"\n\nAn MT103 is an instruction to pay, not a proof of financial commitment: when the due date arrives, execution depends on the payer account holding sufficient funds. If the payer does not pay, the beneficiary has no recourse, and neither does a bank that discounted it, unless it signed a separate contract with the payer.\n\nAn MT103 is an instruction to pay, not proof of financial commitment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever treat an MT103 as security. At the due date the payment executes only if the payer's account holds sufficient funds, and without a separate contract with the payer, a discounting bank has no recourse.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT103 as instruction\n→ Payer funds at the due date\n→ Beneficiary recourse\n→ Separate contract with the payer\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the MT103 genuine?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payer is committed to pay, beyond the instruction itself?\"\n\nHave you ever been offered a future-dated MT103 as if it were a guarantee?", "takeaway": "If the payer does not pay, the beneficiary has no recourse, and neither does a bank that discounted it, unless it signed a separate contract with the payer.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 value-dated far in the future", "note": "An MT103 can be value-dated far in the future."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 is an instruction to pay", "note": "An MT103 is an instruction to pay, not proof of financial commitment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Payer account holds sufficient funds", "note": "At the due date the payment executes only if the payer's account holds sufficient funds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary has no recourse if unpaid", "note": "If the payer does not pay, the beneficiary has no recourse."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Discounting bank needs a separate contract", "note": "A bank that discounted it has no recourse without a separate contract with the payer."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-020", "seq": 499, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "France before SEPA: PNS, TBF, then CORE", "explain": "Before SEPA, France already ran EBA systems for interbank payments alongside high value systems called PNS and TBF.\nThen SEPA arrived, and CORE, the retail clearing system, came after it to process XML format files.\nOld systems, new systems, and a market that changed underneath them, with a EUR 2 million retail file caught in the middle of the changeover.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ PNS and TBF run high value payments\n→ EBA systems handle interbank payments\n→ SEPA arrives, market infrastructures change\n→ CORE arrives after SEPA for retail\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"SEPA replaced everything overnight.\"\n\nThe market infrastructures changed greatly with the SEPA project, with old and new systems sitting side by side for a time. CORE, the retail clearing system, arrived after SEPA to process XML format files and improve retail payment services.\n\nOld and new systems sat side by side while the market changed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap the changeover as a timeline, not a switch. PNS and TBF ran high value, EBA systems ran interbank, and CORE arrived after SEPA for retail, with old and new sitting side by side.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ PNS and TBF, high value\n→ EBA systems for interbank\n→ CORE for retail\n→ Old and new side by side\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which system do we use now?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which system carried each payment during the changeover?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain why two systems ran side by side for the same kind of payment?", "takeaway": "The market infrastructures changed greatly with the SEPA project, with old and new systems sitting side by side for a time.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "France: systems before and after SEPA", "parts": [{"label": "PNS and TBF, high value", "note": "France ran high value systems called PNS and TBF before SEPA."}, {"label": "EBA systems for interbank", "note": "EBA systems already handled interbank payments before SEPA."}, {"label": "CORE for retail", "note": "CORE, the retail clearing system, arrived after SEPA."}, {"label": "Better retail services", "note": "CORE improved services for retail payments."}, {"label": "Old and new side by side", "note": "The infrastructures changed greatly, with old and new side by side."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN5-005", "seq": 500, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "RuPay: the scheme that is also the clearing house", "explain": "A RuPay card payment of Rs 12,000 is made between two RuPay member banks.\nThe issuer expects the transaction to clear through a separate clearing house.\nIt does not. The scheme clears it itself.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Issuer and acquirer are both RuPay members\n→ Transaction clears through RuPay itself\n→ NPCI operates RuPay in India\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A scheme always needs a separate clearing house.\"\n\nOn RuPay, clearing and settlement run between domestic and other network partners, with RuPay acting as both the card scheme and the clearing house. In India the clearing house role sits with NPCI, which operates RuPay. When issuer and acquirer are both RuPay members, they clear their transactions through RuPay itself rather than through a separate clearing house.\n\nTwo RuPay members clear through RuPay itself.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck membership before assuming a separate clearing house. When issuer and acquirer are both RuPay members, clearing and settlement run through RuPay itself, operated by NPCI.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme role\n→ Clearing house role\n→ NPCI as operator\n→ Member to member clearing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which clearing house cleared it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both banks are RuPay members, and that RuPay cleared it?\"\n\nHave you ever looked for a separate clearing house behind a payment the scheme cleared itself?", "takeaway": "When issuer and acquirer are both RuPay members, they clear their transactions through RuPay itself rather than through a separate clearing house.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "RuPay clearing and settlement", "parts": [{"label": "Scheme and clearing house", "note": "RuPay acts as both the card scheme and the clearing house."}, {"label": "NPCI operates RuPay", "note": "In India the clearing house role sits with NPCI."}, {"label": "Clearing between partners", "note": "Clearing and settlement run between domestic and other network partners."}, {"label": "Members clear through RuPay", "note": "Two RuPay members clear through RuPay itself, not a separate house."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch21-card-authorization-clearing-and-settlement.html", "label": "Chapter 21: Card Authorization, Clearing and Settlement", "chapter": "ch21", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-011", "seq": 522, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Who sends the pacs.002? It depends on the rail.", "explain": "A EUR 30,000 payment is sent, and the status reply never arrives.\nThe team waits for the beneficiary bank to answer.\nOn this rail, the beneficiary bank was never the one who answers.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment sent\n→ Status reply expected\n→ Wrong party waited on\n→ Reply already sent by the clearing mechanism\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary bank always sends the status.\"\n\nThe sender of a status response depends on the rail the payment travelled. On a real time gross settlement system the clearing and settlement mechanism sends pacs.002, pacs.004 and camt.029 to the originator. On a cross-border payment the beneficiary bank, or instructed bank, sends them.\n\nOn RTGS the clearing mechanism answers. Abroad, the beneficiary bank does.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the rail before waiting on a reply. On RTGS the clearing and settlement mechanism sends pacs.002, pacs.004 and camt.029 to the originator. On a cross-border payment the beneficiary bank, or instructed bank, sends them.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ RTGS, the CSM answers\n→ Cross-border, the beneficiary bank answers\n→ pacs.002, pacs.004, camt.029\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who do we usually wait for?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which party sends the status on this rail?\"\n\nHave you ever waited for a status reply from a bank that was never going to send it?", "takeaway": "On a cross-border payment the beneficiary bank, or instructed bank, sends them.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who sends the status responses", "parts": [{"label": "RTGS: the CSM", "note": "In real time gross settlement, the CSM sends pacs.002, pacs.004 and camt.029."}, {"label": "Cross-border: the beneficiary bank", "note": "In cross-border payments, the beneficiary or instructed bank sends them."}, {"label": "It depends on the rail", "note": "The sender depends on which rail the payment travelled on."}, {"label": "Wait on the right party", "note": "Waiting on the wrong party means waiting forever."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004", "camt.029"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-015", "seq": 524, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Cycle 00 arrived. The indirect participant got nothing.", "explain": "A direct participant signs up for scheme cycle 00.\nThe cycle 00 transactions arrive, and the direct participant receives them all.\nThe indirect participant, who never signed up, receives nothing, and its customers wait for payments that were never forwarded.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Direct participant signs up for cycle 00\n→ Cycle 00 transactions arrive\n→ Indirect participant did not enrol\n→ Nothing forwarded to the indirect participant\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Signing up covers everyone downstream.\"\n\nCycle sign-up belongs to the direct participant alone, so a direct participant enrolled for cycle 00 receives those transactions yet sends nothing to an indirect participant that did not enrol. Forwarding runs on a timetable the two agree separately, for instance once at the end of the day, and stays independent of the cycles. In classic SEPA only the direct participant exchanges files with the clearing and settlement mechanism. In SCT Inst some indirect participants connect directly to the mechanism and files move immediately, so no cycles exist there.\n\nA direct participant enrolled for cycle 00 receives those transactions yet sends nothing to an indirect participant that did not enrol.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSign up each participant, and agree forwarding separately. Cycle sign-up belongs to the direct participant alone, forwarding runs on a timetable the two agree, and in SCT Inst some indirect participants connect directly, so no cycles exist there.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cycle sign-up, direct participant only\n→ Forwarding, separately agreed\n→ Classic SEPA, direct participant only\n→ SCT Inst, direct indirect links\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we sign up for the cycle?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the indirect participant is enrolled, or will be forwarded to?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a cycle arrive in full while a downstream participant received nothing?", "takeaway": "In SCT Inst some indirect participants connect directly to the mechanism and files move immediately, so no cycles exist there.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Scheme cycles for direct and indirect participants", "parts": [{"label": "Only the DP signs up", "note": "Only the direct participant can sign up for scheme cycles."}, {"label": "DP receives, IP may not", "note": "The DP gets cycle 00 transactions but sends nothing to an IP that did not sign up."}, {"label": "Forwarding is separately agreed", "note": "The IP receives files when the DP forwards them, on an agreed schedule."}, {"label": "Classic SEPA: DP only", "note": "Only the DP exchanges files with the CSM in classic SEPA."}, {"label": "SCT Inst: direct IP links", "note": "In SCT Inst some IPs connect directly and files move immediately, with no cycles."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-017", "seq": 525, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Two fields in pacs.008 tell you who really paid", "explain": "A EUR 28,000 pacs.008 arrives, and the operations team cannot tell whether the payer was a direct participant or an indirect one.\nThe answer is in the message, in two fields they never thought to read together.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 arrives\n→ Instructing agent named\n→ Debtor agent named\n→ Participation model revealed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The debtor agent is always the payer.\"\n\nTwo agents in pacs.008 reveal the participation model behind a payment. Where the payer is a direct participant, both instructing agent and debtor agent name that direct participant. Where the payer is indirect, the instructing agent still names the direct participant while the debtor agent names the indirect participant.\n\nTwo fields tell the story. Read them together.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead instructing agent and debtor agent together. Direct payer: both name the direct participant. Indirect payer: the instructing agent still names the direct participant, while the debtor agent names the indirect participant.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instructing agent\n→ Debtor agent\n→ Direct payer pattern\n→ Indirect payer pattern\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who sent the message?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves who really paid, the instructing agent or the debtor agent?\"\n\nHave you ever read one agent field and assumed you knew who paid?", "takeaway": "Where the payer is indirect, the instructing agent still names the direct participant while the debtor agent names the indirect participant.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.008 for direct and indirect payments", "parts": [{"label": "Direct: instructing agent is the DP", "note": "The direct participant instructs the payment."}, {"label": "Direct: debtor agent is the DP", "note": "The direct participant is also the debtor."}, {"label": "Indirect: instructing agent is the DP", "note": "The direct participant still instructs the payment."}, {"label": "Indirect: debtor agent is the IP", "note": "The indirect participant is the debtor."}, {"label": "Two fields tell the story", "note": "Reading both fields shows which kind of participant paid."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-024", "seq": 526, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "TAG 21 never leaves the MT101", "explain": "A corporate customer sends an MT101 for EUR 18,000, with TAG 21 filled in for its own reporting.\nThe MT103 that follows carries no TAG 21.\nThe customer calls to ask where their reference went, and the operations team cannot explain why it stopped.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT101 received with TAG 21\n→ MT103 generated without TAG 21\n→ Customer asks where the reference went\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every field travels forward.\"\n\nTAG 21 of an MT101 never maps into the MT103 that follows. It serves reporting to the ordering customer about the MT101, letting that customer reconcile the reporting automatically with the original transaction. TAG 21 stops there and never travels to the next party in the chain.\n\nTAG 21 stops there and never travels to the next party in the chain.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTell the customer up front: TAG 21 is for reporting to the ordering customer, so they can reconcile the reporting with the original transaction. It never maps into the MT103, and it never travels to the next party in the chain.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ TAG 21 in the MT101\n→ Customer reporting\n→ Reconciliation with the original transaction\n→ MT103 without TAG 21\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the MT103 carry everything?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer knows which fields travel forward and which stop?\"\n\nHave you ever had a customer chase a reference that was never meant to travel beyond their own report?", "takeaway": "TAG 21 stops there and never travels to the next party in the chain.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "TAG 21 of MT101", "parts": [{"label": "Not mapped to MT103", "note": "TAG 21 does not appear in the MT103 that follows."}, {"label": "Used in customer reporting", "note": "It appears in reporting sent to the ordering customer."}, {"label": "Enables reconciliation", "note": "The customer reconciles the reporting with the original transaction."}, {"label": "Not forwarded onward", "note": "TAG 21 is not passed to the next party in the chain."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-030", "seq": 528, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "E-mandates need a router. It does not have to be a CSM.", "explain": "A creditor bank needs to route e-mandate messages to debtor banks across the SEPA area.\nThe team assumes only a clearing and settlement mechanism can do it.\nThe mandate for EUR 2,000 a month waits while they look for a CSM to hire.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor bank needs e-mandate routing\n→ No CSM in the middle\n→ Mandates wait for a router\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Only a clearing system can route e-mandates.\"\n\nA creditor bank needs a player in between to route e-mandate messages to debtor banks, but that player does not have to be a clearing and settlement mechanism. The operating model defines two services for this: routing and validation, each delivered by a service provider, and either service can come from a debtor bank, a creditor bank or a CSM. Market players provide this kind of routing service, and a search for SEPA e-mandate providers finds more names.\n\nThe operating model defines two services, routing and validation, and either can come from a debtor bank, a creditor bank or a CSM.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuy the two services, not a clearing system. Routing and validation are each delivered by a service provider, and either one can come from a debtor bank, a creditor bank or a CSM.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Routing service\n→ Validation service\n→ Service provider options\n→ Debtor bank, creditor bank or CSM\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which CSM do we use?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the routing and validation services are covered, whoever provides them?\"\n\nHave you ever assumed only a clearing system could do a job any service provider could do?", "takeaway": "The operating model defines two services for this: routing and validation, each delivered by a service provider, and either service can come from a debtor bank, a creditor bank or a CSM.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Routing e-mandate messages without a CSM", "parts": [{"label": "A player in between is needed", "note": "The creditor bank needs someone to route the message to debtor banks."}, {"label": "Routing service provider", "note": "Routes the messages between the parties."}, {"label": "Validation service provider", "note": "Validates the messages in the flow."}, {"label": "Any party can provide them", "note": "A debtor bank, creditor bank or CSM can provide either service."}, {"label": "Market players exist", "note": "Specialist providers offer this kind of routing service."}]}}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "The monthly salary file went to BACS a day late.\nPayday moved a day.\nNo one could hurry it back.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Salary file submitted one day late\n→ Day one for submission becomes day two\n→ Day two for processing becomes day three\n→ Day three for settlement becomes day four\n→ Salaries land a day after payday\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"BACS is electronic. It can be hurried.\"\n\nSo the file missed its submission day and the team discovered that the three-day cycle is a rule, not a suggestion.\n\nWhy?\n\nBACS takes three working days: day one for submission, day two for processing, and day three for settlement. BACS is built for bulk, scheduled, low-cost payments such as salaries and supplier runs, and that is why it outlasts Faster Payments for bulk pay. Faster Payments is built for single, urgent payments, and it costs more per payment and carries limits that bulk runs do not want.\n\nBACS does not hurry. The calendar is the control.\n\nA day late at submission is a day late at payday, every time.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA BACS file is a three-day promise. Submit it on day one or move payday.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Submission day, fixed in the payroll calendar\n→ Processing day, owned by the file team\n→ Settlement day, the payday everyone sees\n→ Fallback plan when a file misses its day\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we send the salaries today?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the file was submitted in time for payday?\"\n\nHave you seen a payday move because a BACS file missed its day?", "id": "ADD-010", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 539, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "BACS does not hurry. A BACS file is a three-day promise, submit it on day one or move payday.", "title": "Salary file a day late, payday moved a day", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "File must be in on this day", "title": "Day one: submission"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If late, every later day slips with it", "title": "File in on day one?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "File is processed, no shortcut exists", "title": "Day two: processing"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Money lands, a day after payday", "title": "Day three: settlement"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Thousands of salaries, one day late", "title": "Payday moved"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A supplier invoice for EUR 50,000 was due on Friday. The payment team sent it serial, straight down the chain, and marked the job done.\n\nOn Monday the supplier had still not been paid. The money was sitting with an intermediary bank, going nowhere.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent serial to the first intermediary\n→ Intermediary deducts its charge and passes it on\n→ Next bank has no account relationship with the beneficiary bank\n→ MT103 returned, funds parked, supplier unpaid\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Send every MT103 serial unless told otherwise.\"\n\nSo nobody checked whether the route could actually settle.\n\nWhy?\n\nA serial payment only works where each bank in the chain holds an account with the next bank. The sending bank held no account with the beneficiary bank, so the correct route was a cover payment: an MT103 sent direct to the beneficiary bank, and an MT202 COV settling the funds through the correspondent that held the dollars.\n\nSerial is a chain of accounts. No account, no chain.\n\nThe method has to be chosen from the account relationships, not from habit. Check the relationships first, then pick serial or cover.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe payment method is a design decision, not a default.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Account relationships on the route\n→ Method selection, serial or cover\n→ Intermediary count and charges\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which method do we usually use?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this route can settle before we send?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a payment bounce between intermediaries because nobody checked the route first?", "id": "XBR-001", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 550, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Serial is a chain of accounts. No account, no chain.", "title": "EUR 50,000 sent serial, but the chain had no account to settle through", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "Serial needs an account at every step", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 sent serial", "note": "Instruction and funds travel together down one chain"}, {"label": "No account link", "note": "Sending bank holds no account with the beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "Payment stalls", "note": "An intermediary cannot pass the funds onward"}, {"label": "Cover was the route", "note": "MT103 direct, MT202 COV through the correspondent"}, {"label": "Method follows accounts", "note": "Check relationships first, then choose serial or cover"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "A customer sent USD 25,000 to pay an invoice and was told the payment had gone. Three days later the beneficiary called to say nothing had arrived.\n\nThe payment had gone. The money had not.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ MT202 COV still travelling through the cover chain\n→ Beneficiary bank holds the instruction and waits\n→ Customer told \"payment sent\" on the MT103 date\n→ Beneficiary unpaid until the cover settles\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Confirm the payment as sent when the MT103 leaves.\"\n\nSo the customer was told the job was done while the funds were still in transit.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 COV travel separately, so the MT103 can reach the beneficiary bank before the funds leg arrives. The beneficiary bank must hold the instruction and wait for the cover, because it cannot credit the beneficiary until the funds have settled. This timing gap is a normal feature of the cover method, not an error.\n\nSent is not settled. The MT103 is the message, the cover is the money.\n\nThe gap is also the moment when an amount or reference mismatch is most likely to surface, so the wait is doing real work.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nInstruction sent and funds settled are two different events.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction status\n→ Cover funds status\n→ Beneficiary credit status\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When did the MT103 leave?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds have settled before we tell the customer it is done?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain to a customer that \"sent\" did not mean \"received\"?", "id": "XBR-003", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 552, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Sent is not settled. The MT103 is the message, the cover is the money.", "title": "USD 25,000 \"sent\" on Monday, beneficiary paid on Thursday", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "MT103 first, funds later", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 arrives", "note": "Instruction reaches the beneficiary bank first"}, {"label": "Cover in transit", "note": "MT202 COV still travelling the correspondent chain"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank waits", "note": "It cannot credit until the funds settle"}, {"label": "Customer misled", "note": "Told sent when only the message had left"}, {"label": "Credit on settlement", "note": "Beneficiary paid only when the cover arrives"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A company paid an overseas employee EUR 5,000 in salary by MT103. The employee received EUR 4,940 and asked where the missing EUR 60 had gone.\n\nNobody had made an error. The payment had simply passed through two intermediary banks.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent for EUR 5,000\n→ First intermediary deducts EUR 35\n→ Second intermediary deducts EUR 25\n→ Beneficiary bank credits EUR 4,940\n→ Employee asks why the salary is short\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The amount sent is the amount the beneficiary receives.\"\n\nSo nobody warned the employee, and nobody priced the route.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a serial payment the ordering customer bears the charges, and each intermediary bank deducts its own charge from the amount as the payment passes through. The beneficiary therefore receives the amount minus the charges deducted at each step. The shortfall is the charge, not an error, and it is why the charge-bearer matters before the payment is sent.\n\nIn a serial payment, every bank in the chain takes its cut from the amount.\n\nField 71A states who bears the charges, and field 71F states the amount of each charge deducted, so the final credit can be reconciled against the amount the ordering customer sent.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPrice the whole chain, not just the first step.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Charge bearer in field 71A\n→ Each deduction in field 71F\n→ Beneficiary communication before the payment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we send EUR 5,000?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the beneficiary will receive the amount we promised?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain a short salary to an employee who did nothing wrong?", "id": "XBR-006", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 555, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "In a serial payment, every bank in the chain takes its cut from the amount.", "title": "EUR 5,000 salary sent, EUR 4,940 received, and nobody made an error", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Ordering customer sends EUR 5,000", "title": "MT103 sent"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "First intermediary deducts EUR 35", "title": "First deduction"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Second intermediary deducts EUR 25", "title": "Second deduction"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Beneficiary credited EUR 4,940", "title": "Short credit"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Was the charge bearer agreed and the employee warned?", "title": "Check the agreement"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch51-documentary-collections-and-swift-category-4.html", "label": "Chapter 51: Documentary Collections and SWIFT Category 4", "chapter": "ch51", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A company made its first ever payment to a new beneficiary bank: EUR 18,000 for an urgent order. The MT103 was rejected before it was even read.\n\nNobody had asked the beneficiary bank to accept messages from the sending bank.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ New beneficiary bank identified, payment prepared\n→ MT103 sent over SWIFT\n→ Receiving bank system refuses the message\n→ Payment never arrives, order delayed\n→ Team resends the same MT103, rejected again\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If we have the BIC, we can send the payment.\"\n\nSo the payment was built, approved and sent with no check that the receiving bank would accept it.\n\nWhy?\n\nBefore one bank can send an MT103 to another over SWIFT, the receiving bank must set up an RMA, a relationship management authorisation, for that sender. Without an RMA the receiving bank's system will not accept the message. The RMA is therefore a precondition for the payment, not a formality that can be sorted out afterwards.\n\nAuthorisation comes before the payment, not after it.\n\nStanding settlement instructions matter the same way. Banks tell each other how to send them funds in each currency through SSIs, exchanged as MT670 and MT671 messages. A sender who does not know the receiver's correspondent must find it out through SWIFT services or by asking the receiver directly, before sending.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the door is open before you send the payment through it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ RMA in place for the receiving bank\n→ Standing settlement instructions on file\n→ Pre-send validation for a new beneficiary bank\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do we have the BIC?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the receiving bank will accept our message?\"\n\nHave you ever sent a payment to a bank that was never set up to receive it?", "id": "XBR-012", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 561, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Authorisation comes before the payment, not after it.", "title": "EUR 18,000 to a new bank, rejected before it was even read", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "New beneficiary bank, first payment prepared", "title": "Payment prepared"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Has the receiving bank set up an RMA for us?", "title": "Check the RMA"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "No RMA, so the system refuses the MT103", "title": "Message rejected"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Ask the beneficiary bank to establish the RMA", "title": "Set up authorisation"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Resend only after the RMA is confirmed", "title": "Send when accepted"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 1, "stage_name": "Payment mechanics", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A payer sent GBP 12,000 to a supplier.\nVerification of Payee returned No Match.\nThe payer carried on, and the money went to the wrong account.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Payer enters the payee name and account details\n→ VoP check returns No Match\n→ Payer reviews the warning and continues anyway\n→ Payment reaches the wrong payee\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A No Match stops the payment.\"\n\nBut the build did not stop it. Depending on the bank process, the payer can review and correct the details, or continue knowing the payment may reach the wrong payee.\n\nWhy?\n\nA No Match says only that the name does not match the name the payee bank holds. It does not say whether it is fraud or a simple typing error, a trading name, or a joint account held under a different name. The check warns, it does not decide.\n\nNo Match is a warning, not a verdict.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA name check, a payer decision and a payment outcome are three different things, so keep them separate:\n\n→ VoP result\n→ Payer decision to correct or continue\n→ Payment destination confirmed\n\nAdd a clear warning, a correction path, and a record of the payer choosing to continue.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we stop the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payer understood the risk before continuing?\"\n\nHave you seen a No Match warning ignored and the money land in the wrong account?", "id": "JEB-C01", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 28, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "No Match is a warning, not a verdict.", "title": "VoP said No Match, GBP 12,000 went anyway", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "No Match warned, GBP 12,000 went to the wrong account", "parts": [{"label": "Name match check", "note": "VoP compares the payer name with the name the payee bank holds"}, {"label": "No Match returned", "note": "The names do not match, nothing more is proven"}, {"label": "Review or continue", "note": "The payer can correct the details or carry on"}, {"label": "Wrong payee paid", "note": "The payment reached the wrong account"}, {"label": "Not proof of fraud", "note": "No Match does not prove fraud or a simple error"}, {"label": "Capture the decision", "note": "Record that the payer chose to continue after the warning"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C003", "seq": 51, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 90,000 MT103 arrived in minutes, the funds took three days", "explain": "A EUR 90,000 payment instruction reaches the beneficiary bank in minutes.\nThe beneficiary calls to ask where the money is.\nThe funds are still working through the correspondent chain, three days later.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 travels fast to the beneficiary bank\n→ The funds settle separately through an MT202 COV\n→ The MT202 COV waits on correspondent accounts at its own pace\n→ The beneficiary bank knows about the payment but holds no funds\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The instruction arriving means the money arrived.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe cover method separates the payment instruction from the funds so the MT103 can reach the beneficiary bank fast while settlement works through correspondent accounts at its own pace. Keeping customer details out of the funds leg also protects privacy and keeps the correspondent chain clean. The price is reconciliation: the two legs must match on amount and reference, and any mismatch becomes an exception.\n\nFast instruction ≠ Fast funds.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the two legs on amount and reference before anyone is told the money is there.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction received (MT103)\n→ Funds settled (MT202 COV)\n→ Amount matched across both legs\n→ Reference matched across both legs\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the MT103 arrive?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds leg settled and matched the instruction before the beneficiary is told?\"\n\nHave you seen a beneficiary promised money that was still three days away?", "takeaway": "The price is reconciliation: the two legs must match on amount and reference, and any mismatch becomes an exception.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Why split the two legs", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 arrives in minutes", "note": "The instruction reaches the beneficiary bank fast"}, {"label": "Funds settle separately", "note": "The MT202 COV moves through correspondent accounts"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank holds nothing", "note": "It knows of the payment but has no funds"}, {"label": "Three days later", "note": "Settlement completes at its own pace"}, {"label": "Match both legs", "note": "Amount and reference must agree, or it is an exception"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E08", "seq": 97, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 75,000 payment, no route found, reject or repair?", "explain": "A EUR 75,000 payment arrives with no route the system can determine.\nThe system offers the easy answer: reject it.\nA competitor would repair it, find another route, and send it through.\nThe customer would never know the difference, except in the result.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment arrives for EUR 75,000\n→ Routing cannot be determined automatically\n→ Option one: reject, easy, fast, customer loses\n→ Option two: repair and reroute, more work, payment succeeds\n→ Option three: manual intervention, a person decides\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"When the system cannot route, rejection is the only honest answer.\"\n\nRejecting is the easiest answer but not necessarily the best one.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach option carries different consequences for customer experience, revenue, operational effort, SLA, risk, and payment success rate. The real choice sits between rejection, repair with alternative routing, and manual intervention, and it is a business choice, not a system default.\n\nReject is a decision, not a default.\n\nWhy does the easy answer cost a customer the bank could have kept?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nIdentify the decision point, frame the options with their impact, and take the decision to the business owner.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reject, what the customer loses\n→ Repair and reroute, what it costs to save the payment\n→ Manual intervention, when a person must decide\n\nShow all three options with their impact on experience, revenue, SLA, risk and success rate before anyone picks one.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does the system do when routing is unknown?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the business chose this outcome, knowing what each option costs?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment rejected that a repair could have saved?", "takeaway": "The most valuable question is what the business wants the system to do when this situation occurs.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Routing unknown: reject or repair?", "parts": [{"label": "Reject", "note": "The easiest answer, but the weakest customer experience."}, {"label": "Repair and reroute", "note": "Find an alternative route, enrich the payment, and send it through."}, {"label": "Manual intervention", "note": "A person decides when automation cannot determine the route."}, {"label": "Impact of each option", "note": "Customer experience, revenue, effort, SLA, risk, and success rate."}, {"label": "EUR 75,000 at stake", "note": "One decision decides whether the payment succeeds."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E09", "seq": 98, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 45,000 payment, every mandatory field filled, still fails", "explain": "A Eurozone customer payment for EUR 45,000 arrives.\nReference, account number and customer name are all filled.\nThe debtor postal address has no street name.\nThe payment fails downstream. The form said it was complete.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment arrives, mandatory fields all present\n→ Debtor postal address missing its street name\n→ Checks pass, because the street is not mandatory\n→ Screening and the debit instruction fail downstream on the missing data\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If every mandatory field is filled, the payment is meaningful.\"\n\nChecking only mandatory fields misses the point.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe debit instruction and the screening of the payment can depend on data that is not mandatory but is relevant in cross-border payments, and a missing street name can look harmless while the payment itself is not meaningful. Some missing fields are business rules that will fail the payment in processing.\n\nMandatory is not the same as meaningful.\n\nWhy does a payment with every mandatory field filled still fail?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk what happens if the data is missing, and treat a business-critical gap as a failure before it reaches processing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandatory fields, what the form requires\n→ Business-critical data, what screening and processing need\n→ Downstream impact, rejection, block, or breach of a rule\n\nList the data each downstream step needs, and validate against that list, not only against the mandatory list.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are the mandatory fields filled?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment carries everything processing and screening need?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail on a field nobody marked mandatory?", "takeaway": "Some missing fields are business rules that will fail the payment in processing.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Mandatory is not the same as meaningful", "parts": [{"label": "Only mandatory checked", "note": "A check of mandatory fields only assumes the rest is irrelevant."}, {"label": "Screening needs more", "note": "Debit instruction and screening can depend on non-mandatory data."}, {"label": "Business impact missed", "note": "Some missing data is business-critical, not a data quality issue."}, {"label": "Ask what happens", "note": "Does the gap reject, block, or breach a rule downstream."}, {"label": "EUR 45,000 failed", "note": "All mandatory fields filled, street name missing, payment failed."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch43-payment-security.html", "label": "Chapter 43: Payment Security", "chapter": "ch43", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A maker raised a corporate payment of EUR 75,000 in one click.\nWorkflow approved it.\nThe supplier never received it.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Maker initiates the payment\n→ Preprocessor checks entitlement, account, limits, fees and payee\n→ Workflow approves the payment\n→ Bank screening holds it\n→ Account never debited, network never clears it, ledger never closes it\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Once workflow approves, the payment is sent.\"\n\nSo the screen showed approved, and everyone assumed the money was on its way.\n\nWhy?\n\nFifteen steps and nine decisions stand between maker and ledger. Every Yes moves the payment forward, and every No stops or re-routes it. A No at bank screening stops the payment after approval, and an approved payment that never clears is still an unpaid payment.\n\nApproval is not settlement.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nApproval, screening, debit, clearing and ledger posting are separate stages, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Workflow approval status\n→ Bank screening outcome\n→ Account debit confirmed\n→ Network clearing and ledger posting\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When was it approved?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each step completed, including the final ledger posting?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an approved payment stall silently at a later step?", "id": "ABH-10", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 117, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Approval is not settlement.", "title": "Approved in one click, EUR 75,000 never reached the supplier", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Fifteen steps and nine decisions to the ledger", "title": "Maker initiates EUR 75,000"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Entitlement, account, limits, fees and payee", "title": "Preprocessor checks clear?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Approval moves it to the bank", "title": "Workflow approves"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "A No stops or re-routes the payment", "title": "Bank screening clear?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Never reached, so the supplier was never paid", "title": "Debit, clearing and ledger"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-12", "seq": 119, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "VND 500,000,000 domestic payment, nothing reaches the rail until the preprocessor clears it", "explain": "A corporate in Vietnam sends VND 500,000,000 to a supplier.\nThe payment sits, unmoving, for hours.\nThe rail is fine. The beneficiary bank is fine.\nThe payment never cleared the preprocessor.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Corporate initiates the payment\n→ Preprocessor, the central control point\n→ Payment hub, central processing after the preprocessor\n→ NAPAS, the domestic rail in Vietnam\n→ Beneficiary bank credits the beneficiary\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The rail carries the payment, so check the rail first.\"\n\nIn a domestic payment in Vietnam, the payment preprocessor is the central control point.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe flow runs from the corporate, through the preprocessor, to the payment hub, then NAPAS, then the beneficiary bank. Nothing reaches the rail until the preprocessor has cleared it, so a payment stuck before the rail looks like a rail failure from the outside.\n\nThe gate is before the rail.\n\nWhy does a payment sit for hours on a rail that is working fine?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the control point first, and show its decision before the rail status.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Preprocessor decision, cleared or held\n→ Payment hub, processing after the preprocessor\n→ NAPAS rail, carrying what the preprocessor cleared\n→ Beneficiary bank, crediting the beneficiary\n\nShow the preprocessor result on the payment status, with the reason for any hold.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the rail working?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the preprocessor cleared this payment?\"\n\nHave you blamed a rail for a payment held at the gate before it?", "takeaway": "Nothing reaches the rail until the preprocessor has cleared it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Corporate initiates payment", "note": "The payment starts with the corporate, VND 500,000,000."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Preprocessor clears it?", "note": "Nothing reaches the rail until it clears here."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment hub", "note": "Central processing after the preprocessor."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "NAPAS", "note": "The domestic rail in Vietnam."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank", "note": "Final bank that credits the beneficiary."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-14", "seq": 121, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "VND 800,000,000 payment, a duplicate slips past six checks", "explain": "A corporate sends VND 800,000,000 in Vietnam.\nThe same payment is sent twice by mistake.\nSix checks pass. The duplicate check is the one that matters.\nIt catches it, minutes before the money moves twice.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Account validation, account exists and is valid\n→ Format check, message format is correct\n→ Authorisation check, payment is properly authorised\n→ Limits, AML and sanctions, all checked\n→ Duplicate check, payment is not a repeat\n→ Routing and enrichment, then it moves on\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any one check is enough. They all check the same thing.\"\n\nBefore a domestic payment moves on, the preprocessor runs the full gate: account, format, authorisation, limits, AML and sanctions, duplicates, routing and enrichment.\n\nWhy?\n\nThose checks are the reason a payment can be trusted to reach the right account. Each check catches a different failure, and the duplicate check is the only one that sees a payment sent twice, however valid it looks on every other test.\n\nSeven checks, seven different failures caught.\n\nWhy does a perfectly valid payment still need a duplicate check?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun the full gate, in order, and never let a payment skip a check because the others passed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Account, format and authorisation, is it real and allowed\n→ Limits, AML and sanctions, is it permitted\n→ Duplicates, has it been sent before\n→ Routing and enrichment, where it goes and what it carries\n\nShow all seven check results on the payment, and block on any single failure.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment pass validation?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every check in the gate ran and passed?\"\n\nHave you seen a duplicate caught only by the last check?", "takeaway": "Those checks are the reason a payment can be trusted to reach the right account.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Account validation", "note": "Account exists and is valid."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Format check", "note": "Message format is correct."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Authorisation check", "note": "Payment is properly authorised."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Limits, AML and sanctions clear?", "note": "Limits, AML and sanctions all checked."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Duplicate check clear?", "note": "Payment is not a repeat, VND 800,000,000 sent once only."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing", "note": "Payment routed to the right path."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Enrichment", "note": "Payment enriched before it moves on."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "ABH-15", "seq": 122, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 400,000 cross-border payment, rejected three times, preprocessor was missing", "explain": "A bank sends EUR 400,000 across borders.\nIt is rejected three times.\nEach rejection names a different fault.\nThere was no control tower watching the payment before it left.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment prepared, no preprocessor in the flow\n→ Sent with thin data, no enrichment\n→ Compliance check fails downstream\n→ Wrong route, rejected again, then rejected a third time\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each system checks its own part, so no one needs to watch the whole payment.\"\n\nIn cross-border payments the preprocessor is the control tower: it validates, enriches, checks compliance, routes and processes the payment, so fewer payments are rejected and more go straight through.\n\nWhy?\n\nA strong preprocessor is what makes straight-through processing possible. Without it, each fault is found by a different system downstream, and the payment is rejected again and again for faults one gate would have caught together.\n\nOne gate, fewer rejections.\n\nWhy does the same payment get rejected three times for three different faults?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the control tower before the rail, and let it catch every fault in one place.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Validate, checks before the payment moves on\n→ Enrich, data added for straight-through processing\n→ Compliance, checked before routing\n→ Route and process, sent down the right rail\n\nShow the preprocessor result, with every check, before the payment leaves the bank.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the payment sent?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment was validated, enriched and compliance-checked before it left?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment rejected again and again for faults one gate would have caught?", "takeaway": "A strong preprocessor is what makes straight-through processing possible.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the payment", "note": "Checks before the payment moves on, EUR 400,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Enrich the payment", "note": "Data added for straight-through processing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance checks clear?", "note": "Compliance checked before routing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Route the payment", "note": "Sent down the right rail."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Process the payment", "note": "Fewer rejections, more straight-through."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-16", "seq": 123, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 250,000 payment fails, every layer says the fault is in another layer", "explain": "A EUR 250,000 payment fails.\nThe technical team blames the data.\nThe data team blames the business rules.\nThe business team blames compliance.\nEvery layer is pointing at another layer.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Technical layer, format and transport\n→ Payment data layer, fields and values\n→ Business layer, rules and limits\n→ Compliance layer, screening and sanctions\n→ Routing layer, path and destination\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Validation is one check. Find it and fix it.\"\n\nPreprocessor validation works in layers: technical, payment data, business, compliance and routing.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach layer catches a different kind of error, and the full lifecycle runs from initiation through to settlement and reconciliation. A failure with no layer named is a failure nobody owns, so each team blames the next layer down.\n\nName the layer, own the fault.\n\nWhy does every team blame another layer for the same failed payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidate layer by layer, and record which layer caught each fault.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Technical, catches technical errors\n→ Payment data, catches payment data errors\n→ Business, catches business errors\n→ Compliance, catches compliance errors\n→ Routing, catches routing errors\n\nShow the layer on every validation failure, with the lifecycle stage beside it.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Why did the payment fail?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which layer caught the fault, and at which lifecycle stage?\"\n\nHave you seen five teams blame each other for one failed payment?", "takeaway": "Each layer catches a different kind of error, and the full lifecycle runs from initiation through to settlement and reconciliation.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Preprocessor validation, layer by layer", "parts": [{"label": "Technical", "note": "Catches technical errors."}, {"label": "Payment data", "note": "Catches payment data errors."}, {"label": "Business", "note": "Catches business errors."}, {"label": "Compliance", "note": "Catches compliance errors."}, {"label": "Routing", "note": "Catches routing errors."}, {"label": "Full lifecycle", "note": "Initiation through settlement and reconciliation, EUR 250,000."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-17", "seq": 124, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 600,000 payment enters GPP, vanishes before the rail", "explain": "A EUR 600,000 payment enters the payment hub.\nIt is parsed, validated, mapped and screened.\nThen it vanishes.\nThe rail never sees it, and core banking never posts it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Channel, where the payment starts\n→ API gateway and message queue, entry and queuing\n→ GPP parse and validate, parsed, validated and duplicate-checked\n→ Map to ISO 20022, payment mapped to the rich data standard\n→ Compliance screening, screened before routing\n→ Route to SWIFT or domestic rail, then acknowledge, post and reconcile\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Once it is inside GPP, the payment is safe.\"\n\nFrom a Tech BA view, a payment flows from the channel through the API gateway and message queue into GPP, where it is parsed, validated, checked for duplicates, mapped to ISO 20022, screened for compliance, routed to a SWIFT or domestic rail, acknowledged, and finally posted to core banking with reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nExceptions are handled at each stage, and a payment can fail at any of them. A flow that is only watched at the channel and at the ledger has a blind middle, and a payment that fails inside GPP looks like it vanished.\n\nInside the hub is where payments are lost.\n\nWhy does a payment disappear between parsing and posting?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHandle exceptions at each stage, and show the payment at every stage inside the hub.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Entry, gateway and queue\n→ GPP processing, parse, validate, duplicate check, map\n→ Compliance screening, before routing\n→ Rail, posting and reconciliation, the close of the flow\n\nShow the stage and the exception handler at every step, so no stage is blind.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment enter GPP?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment passed each stage inside GPP?\"\n\nHave you lost a payment inside a hub nobody was watching?", "takeaway": "Exceptions are handled at each stage.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Channel", "note": "Where the payment starts, EUR 600,000."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "API gateway and message queue", "note": "Entry and queuing into the bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "GPP parse and validate", "note": "Parsed, validated and duplicate-checked."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Map to ISO 20022", "note": "Payment mapped to the rich data standard."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance screening clear?", "note": "Screened before routing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Route to SWIFT or domestic rail", "note": "Routed to the right rail."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Acknowledge, post and reconcile", "note": "Acknowledged, posted to core banking, reconciled."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "ABH-24", "seq": 131, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 50,000 paid twice, the retry had no idempotency key", "explain": "A customer retries a EUR 50,000 payment after a timeout.\nThe retry succeeds.\nSo does the first attempt, a second later.\nEUR 100,000 leaves the account for a EUR 50,000 payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ First call times out, outcome unknown\n→ Customer retries the same payment\n→ API has no idempotency protection\n→ Both calls post, EUR 50,000 paid twice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A retry is safe. The system will know it is the same payment.\"\n\nA reliable payment API rests on stateless validation, synchronous fraud and risk checks, idempotency, metadata-driven configuration, orchestration, compliance and reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nIdempotency matters because a retried call must never create a duplicate payment. Without it, a timeout plus a retry is a duplicate by design, and the second posting is not a system error, it is the missing control.\n\nA retry without idempotency is a second payment.\n\nWhy does a careful retry double the payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake every payment call idempotent, and prove a retry returns the first result instead of a second payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Stateless validation, checked without stored session state\n→ Synchronous fraud and risk checks, in line before posting\n→ Idempotency, a retried call never creates a duplicate\n→ Orchestration, compliance and reconciliation, closing the flow\n\nShow the idempotency key on every payment call, and test the timeout-then-retry path.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the retry succeed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the retry returned the first payment instead of creating a second?\"\n\nHave you seen a retry double a payment?", "takeaway": "Idempotency matters because a retried call must never create a duplicate payment.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A reliable payment API", "parts": [{"label": "Stateless validation", "note": "Validation without stored session state."}, {"label": "Synchronous fraud and risk checks", "note": "Fraud and risk checked in line."}, {"label": "Idempotency", "note": "A retried call never creates a duplicate payment."}, {"label": "Metadata-driven configuration", "note": "Behaviour driven by metadata."}, {"label": "Orchestration", "note": "Steps coordinated across the flow."}, {"label": "Compliance", "note": "Rules enforced in the API flow."}, {"label": "Reconciliation", "note": "Proves payments completed correctly."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-138", "seq": 144, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 5,000 sent abroad, beneficiary receives EUR 4,850, rate never shown", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 5,000 abroad.\nThe beneficiary receives EUR 4,850.\nThe customer was never shown the rate, the fees, or how long the rate held.\nThe price was agreed after the fact, which is no agreement at all.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer pays in a different currency, EUR 5,000\n→ Rate and fees not shown clearly before confirmation\n→ Rate changes before the payment completes\n→ Beneficiary credited a different amount, customer cannot reconcile it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The rate at the time is the rate the customer agreed to.\"\n\nWhen a customer pays in a different currency, they need to see the exchange rate, the fees, how long the rate is valid, and the exact amount the beneficiary will receive.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf the rate changes or the fees are not shown clearly, the beneficiary is credited a different amount and the customer cannot reconcile the payment. Transparency means showing the total cost and the applied rate before the customer confirms, because a price agreed after the fact is no agreement at all.\n\nA price shown after is not a price agreed.\n\nWhy does the beneficiary receive EUR 4,850 for a EUR 5,000 payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nShow the full deal upfront, rate, fees, validity and beneficiary amount, before confirmation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Exchange rate, shown before confirmation\n→ Fees, shown in full\n→ Rate validity, how long the rate holds\n→ Beneficiary amount, the exact amount to be received\n\nLock the rate for its stated validity, and show all four figures on the confirmation screen.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What rate applied?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the customer saw the full deal before confirming?\"\n\nHave you sent money abroad and found the beneficiary short?", "takeaway": "Transparency means showing the total cost and the applied rate before the customer confirms.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "FX transparency for customer", "parts": [{"label": "Exchange rate", "note": "Customer needs to see the exchange rate."}, {"label": "Fees", "note": "Customer needs to see the fees."}, {"label": "Rate validity", "note": "Customer needs to see how long the rate is valid."}, {"label": "Beneficiary amount", "note": "Customer needs the exact amount the beneficiary will receive."}, {"label": "Rate change risk", "note": "If the rate changes, the beneficiary is credited a different amount."}, {"label": "Unclear fees risk", "note": "If fees are not shown clearly, the beneficiary is credited a different amount."}, {"label": "Show before confirming", "note": "Show the total cost and the applied rate before the customer confirms."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-140", "seq": 145, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 30,000 to a supplier, paid in full, cannot be matched to any invoice", "explain": "A company pays a supplier EUR 30,000.\nThe money arrives in full.\nThe supplier cannot match it to any invoice.\nThe payment carries no structured remittance data, so the cash sits unallocated.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment sent for EUR 30,000, payer, beneficiary and purpose attached\n→ Remittance data unstructured or incomplete\n→ Supplier cannot match the payment to the invoice\n→ Screening cannot run properly on the thin data\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Data quality is a formatting preference.\"\n\nEvery cross-border payment carries payer, beneficiary, purpose and remittance data that the supplier needs to allocate the cash and that compliance screening depends on.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf the data is unstructured or incomplete, the supplier cannot match the payment to the invoice and the screening cannot run properly. Structured data is what makes a payment reconcilable and compliant at the same time, so data quality is a payment control, not a formatting preference.\n\nUnstructured data is unallocated cash.\n\nWhy does a payment that arrived in full sit unallocated?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat structured data as a payment control, and validate it before the payment leaves.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payer and beneficiary data, who sent and who receives\n→ Purpose data, why the payment was made\n→ Remittance data, what the supplier needs to allocate cash\n→ Compliance screening, what runs on the structured data\n\nRequire structured remittance data on every cross-border payment, and reject what cannot be matched.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the money arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the supplier can allocate it and screening can run on it?\"\n\nHave you paid a supplier in full and still been chased for the invoice?", "takeaway": "Structured data is what makes a payment reconcilable and compliant at the same time.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Structured payment data", "parts": [{"label": "Payer data", "note": "Payment carries payer data the supplier needs."}, {"label": "Beneficiary data", "note": "Payment carries beneficiary data."}, {"label": "Purpose data", "note": "Payment carries purpose data."}, {"label": "Remittance data", "note": "Payment carries remittance data the supplier needs to allocate cash."}, {"label": "Compliance screening", "note": "Structured data lets screening run properly."}, {"label": "Unstructured data risk", "note": "Unstructured or incomplete data means supplier cannot match payment to invoice."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-113", "seq": 152, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The reject reason the customer must never see", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 payment is rejected.\n\nInside the bank, the reason is precise: the account is closed.\n\nThe customer is told the same words. In that country, sharing it breaks the privacy rules.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment is rejected\n→ Internal reason recorded: account closed\n→ Privacy rules differ by country\n→ The reason is shown to the customer without a country check\n→ Sensitive information is exposed\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show the customer the reject reason.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe internal diagnostic reason can be precise and sensitive, and privacy rules differ by country. A reason that is safe to show in one country can be a breach in another.\n\nPrecise inside, careful outside.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe bank must configure what reason is shown, at what scope and through which channel, so the customer gets a useful explanation without exposing sensitive information.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Internal diagnostic reason\n→ Country privacy rule\n→ Channel and scope of what is shown\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What reason do we show?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the customer was told enough, without exposing what must stay private?\"\n\nHave you seen a reject reason shared with a customer that should never have left the bank?", "takeaway": "Precise inside, careful outside. The reason shown must pass the privacy rule first.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reject reason: precise inside, careful outside", "parts": [{"label": "Payment rejected", "note": "A EUR 50,000 payment is rejected"}, {"label": "Internal reason: account closed", "note": "The internal diagnostic reason is precise and sensitive"}, {"label": "Privacy rules differ by country", "note": "What can be shared depends on the country"}, {"label": "Configure what is shown", "note": "The bank must configure the reason, the scope and the channel"}, {"label": "Useful but safe explanation", "note": "The customer gets an explanation without exposed sensitive information"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-117", "seq": 153, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 150,000 payment that could not go instant", "explain": "A customer sends a EUR 150,000 instant payment.\n\nThe customer limit allows it. The scheme maximum does not.\n\nThe payment cannot go as an instant payment at all.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Customer submits a EUR 150,000 instant payment\n→ Customer limit check passes\n→ Scheme maximum check fails\n→ The payment cannot go instant, whatever the customer limit says\n→ It must be rerouted to a standard transfer\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Apply the customer limit.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe scheme maximum and the customer limit are two different numbers, and an older internal guide still carries the old scheme figure after the regulation changed it. The product configuration and the routing rules were never updated together.\n\nThe scheme maximum is the hard gate, not the customer limit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA payment above the scheme maximum cannot go as an instant payment at all. Update the configuration and the routing rules together, and check the guide against the current regulation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme maximum\n→ Customer limit\n→ Routing rule\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the customer limit?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the scheme figure in the system matches the current regulation?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail because an old guide still carried the old scheme maximum?", "takeaway": "The scheme maximum is the hard gate, not the customer limit.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Scheme maximum is the hard gate", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 150,000 instant payment", "note": "The customer submits a payment above the scheme maximum"}, {"label": "Customer limit allows it", "note": "The bank limit for this customer is higher"}, {"label": "Scheme maximum blocks it", "note": "The scheme figure is the hard gate"}, {"label": "Old guide carries the old figure", "note": "The internal guide was never updated after the regulation changed"}, {"label": "Update configuration and routing", "note": "Configuration and routing rules must be updated together"}, {"label": "Reroute to standard transfer", "note": "Above the maximum, the payment cannot go instant at all"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch43-payment-security.html", "label": "Chapter 43: Payment Security", "chapter": "ch43", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A06", "seq": 160, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The customer asked for Citi, the payment went through Chase", "explain": "A customer sends a USD 250,000 payment and names Citi New York as intermediary.\n\nThe payment routes through Chase New York.\n\nNobody changed the instruction. A standing rule overruled it.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Customer instruction names Citi New York\n→ Standing Settlement Instruction names Chase New York\n→ Engine applies the SSI\n→ Payment routes through Chase New York\n→ A reference data update does not displace the rule\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The instruction decides the route.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRouting precedence puts the Standing Settlement Instruction ahead of a conflicting customer instruction, because an SSI is a settlement instruction agreed in advance between banks, naming the preferred correspondent for a currency and counterparty.\n\nRouting follows the governing rule, not the message alone.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA different intermediary requires the SSI to be updated first, or an explicit override in the payment message. Routing follows the governing rules as much as the message itself.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer instruction\n→ Standing Settlement Instruction\n→ Explicit override in the message\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What did the customer ask for?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which rule governed the route?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment take a route nobody in the instruction asked for?", "takeaway": "Routing follows the governing rule, not the message alone. Update the SSI first, or override explicitly.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer names Citi New York", "note": "The instruction names Citi New York as intermediary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Engine applies the SSI", "note": "An SSI names the preferred correspondent in advance"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Chase New York is routed", "note": "The engine chose Chase New York, and reference data did not change that"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Different intermediary wanted?", "note": "Change requires an SSI update first or an explicit override"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Update SSI or override in message", "note": "Routing follows the governing rules, not only the message"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A09", "seq": 162, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 5 million valid on Monday, failed on Friday", "explain": "A EUR 5 million payment is accepted on Monday for Friday execution, against a EUR 6 million balance.\n\nBy Friday it fails.\n\nNothing was wrong on Monday. Everything moved in between.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ EUR 5 million accepted Monday for Friday execution\n→ Payment is warehoused\n→ Balance, account details, sanctions status and cut-off times move before Friday\n→ Friday arrives, and the payment is not re-validated\n→ Execution fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Accepted means it will execute.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWarehousing turns a future-dated payment into a commitment to execute, not merely a stored instruction. Validity on Monday proves nothing about Friday.\n\nWarehousing is a commitment to re-check, not a guarantee.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRe-validate on balance, account status, sanctions and cut-offs at execution, then execute or reject. Warehousing is risk management across the interval.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Acceptance checks on Monday\n→ Execution checks on Friday\n→ Balance, sanctions and cut-off position\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the payment accepted?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment is still valid at execution?\"\n\nHave you seen a warehoused payment fail on the day it was meant to go out?", "takeaway": "Warehousing is a commitment to re-check, not a guarantee. Re-validate at execution.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "EUR 5 million accepted Monday", "note": "Future-dated payment accepted and stored for Friday execution"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Warehousing is a commitment", "note": "Warehousing commits to execute, it is not just storage"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Conditions move before Friday", "note": "Balance, account details, sanctions status and cut-off times can all change"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Re-validate at execution", "note": "Check balance, account status, sanctions and cut-offs again at execution"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Still valid on Friday?", "note": "A payment valid on Monday is not necessarily valid on Friday"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Execute or reject", "note": "Execute the payment, or reject it if re-validation fails"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B02", "seq": 164, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "3,000 payments arrived at once, and the SLA broke on sequencing", "explain": "Thousands of payments arrive together.\n\nEvery control runs in sequence. The SLA breaks.\n\nThe checks were all correct. The order was wrong.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Thousands of payments arrive together\n→ Every control runs one after another\n→ Compliance takes 3 seconds, fraud takes 4 seconds, run in sequence they take 7\n→ The SLA is missed\n→ Payments queue behind checks that could have run together\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Run every control in order.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nControl design is a business decision about ordering, not a technical default. If the account is closed and rejection is the defined outcome, the payment can go to rejection once the account check is done. Compliance and fraud can run in parallel, so checks of 3 and 4 seconds finish in 4 seconds together instead of 7 in sequence.\n\nControl order is a business decision, not a technical default.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSpell out the intended outcome for a control that fails, times out or comes back inconclusive, or the decision is left to technical defaults.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sequential validation checks\n→ Parallel screening checks\n→ Outcome for a failed, timed out or inconclusive control\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which controls do we run?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each control runs in the order the business chose?\"\n\nHave you seen an SLA missed because controls ran in sequence that could have run in parallel?", "takeaway": "Control order is a business decision, not a technical default. Sequential for validation, parallel for screening.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Thousands of payments arrive", "note": "Running every control one after another is not always the smartest design"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Account closed, outcome clear?", "note": "If the account is closed and the outcome is rejection, reject after the account check"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Run compliance and fraud in parallel", "note": "Checks of 3 and 4 seconds finish in 4 seconds together instead of 7"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Protect the SLA", "note": "Parallel checks can decide whether the SLA is met"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define failure outcomes", "note": "Define what happens when a control fails, times out or returns inconclusive"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B08", "seq": 168, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Treasury needed one answer before cut-off, and camt.053 could not give it", "explain": "Treasury waits on a EUR 30 million Nostro credit before releasing an outgoing payment.\n\nThe cut-off is an hour away.\n\nThe team reads camt.053. It answers tomorrow, not today.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Treasury waits for a Nostro credit\n→ Outgoing payment is held until the credit arrives\n→ camt.053 shows movements and balance at close of business\n→ The decision must be made during the day\n→ camt.052 intraday report or camt.054 notification can answer it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Read the statement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe camt.053 statement holds the account movements and the balance at close of business, so it cannot answer a question that must be decided during the day.\n\nChoose the message by the decision it must support, not by its definition alone.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart from the business decision, then pick the message that can answer it in time.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Decision that must be made\n→ Timing the decision needs\n→ Message that can answer in time\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which report do we use?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the credit arrived before the cut-off?\"\n\nHave you picked a message by its definition, and found it answered the wrong question?", "takeaway": "Choose the message by the decision it must support, not by its definition alone.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Treasury waits for Nostro credit", "note": "Treasury needs the credit before releasing an outgoing payment"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has the expected credit arrived?", "note": "The question must be answered before the cut-off"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.053 cannot answer it", "note": "The statement shows the balance at close of business, too late for the day"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.052 and camt.054 are candidates", "note": "The intraday report and the debit or credit notification can answer during the day"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Choose by the decision", "note": "Choose the message by the business decision it must support, not by its definition alone"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["camt.053", "camt.052", "camt.054"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B09", "seq": 169, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A flawless pain.001, a USD 200,000 payment in the wrong engine", "explain": "A customer sends a flawless pain.001 for USD 200,000.\n\nIt lands in an instant payment engine that handles EUR only.\n\nThe payment is held, delayed and then rejected, and the instruction was perfect.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Valid pain.001 arrives\n→ Routing rules are set by the payment business and applied by the acquisition layer\n→ pain.001 provides the clues: instrument, currency and amount\n→ The rules are incomplete, and the route is wrong\n→ USD payment lands in a EUR-only instant engine\n→ Payment is held, delayed or rejected\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A valid message finds its own route.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe engine never invents or overrides the route. If the rules are incomplete or incorrectly applied, a valid payment can land in the wrong engine despite a correct customer instruction.\n\nThe message provides the clues, the rules decide the route.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRouting rules are a business responsibility. Test the rules against every instrument, currency and amount combination before they go live.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message clues in pain.001\n→ Routing rules\n→ Engine assignment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the message valid?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the routing rules cover this payment?\"\n\nHave you seen a perfect instruction fail because the routing rules sent it to the wrong engine?", "takeaway": "The message provides the clues, and the business rules determine the route.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Valid pain.001 arrives", "note": "Even a flawless pain.001 can turn into a production incident"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing rules are defined", "note": "The payment business defines the routing rules and the acquisition layer applies them"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pain.001 provides the clues", "note": "Instrument, currency and amount are the clues, but the engine does not invent the route"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Rules complete and correct?", "note": "If the rules are incomplete or incorrectly applied, the route can be wrong"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "USD payment reaches EUR engine", "note": "A valid USD payment can land in an instant payment engine handling EUR only"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment held, delayed or rejected", "note": "The payment can be held, delayed or rejected despite a correct instruction"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pain.001"], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B12", "seq": 170, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The API said 200 OK, and the EUR 80,000 payment was still rejected", "explain": "An API call returns HTTP 200.\n\nThe EUR 80,000 payment is still rejected.\n\nThe call succeeded. The business decision failed.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ API call succeeds, HTTP 200\n→ Account is open, so the first check passes\n→ Account currency is EUR, and the product does not support FX\n→ Fraud score comes back 95\n→ Engine rejects the payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"HTTP 200 means the payment will succeed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTechnical success and business outcome are different things. Checking whether an account is open or closed is not enough if the engine also needs the account currency. A fraud score works the same way: the fraud system controls its scoring model, while the engine must convert the score into an action, above 90 reject, 50 to 90 process with a customer alert, and below 50 process normally.\n\nAPI success is not a business decision.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart with the business decision the process needs to make, not the API to build. Ask what information the engine needs to decide, then build the call around that.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Technical response\n→ Business rules\n→ Score to action mapping\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the API succeed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the engine has everything it needs to decide?\"\n\nHave you seen an API return success while the payment still failed?", "takeaway": "API success is not a business decision. Start with the decision the process needs to make.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "API success is not a business decision", "parts": [{"label": "HTTP 200 is only technical", "note": "An API can return HTTP 200 while the engine still rejects the payment"}, {"label": "Account currency matters", "note": "Checking open or closed is not enough if the engine also needs the currency"}, {"label": "Fraud score above 90: reject", "note": "Above 90 reject, the engine translates the score into an action"}, {"label": "Score 50 to 90: alert", "note": "From 50 to 90 process with a customer alert"}, {"label": "Score below 50: process normally", "note": "Below 50 process the payment normally"}, {"label": "Start with the business decision", "note": "Start with the business decision the process needs to make, not the API"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch80-api-design-standards-and-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 80: API design standards and integration patterns", "chapter": "ch80", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C03", "seq": 172, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 10,000 to India: one message, or two?", "explain": "A US bank sends USD 10,000 to HDFC Bank in India, for an INR account.\n\nOne pacs.008 with settlement will not work. There is no direct relationship, and the settlement currency does not match.\n\nThe payment needs two legs, and the team sent one.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ USD 10,000 instructed from a US bank to India\n→ No nostro account exists in the beneficiary bank country\n→ Settlement currency differs from the payer account currency\n→ pacs.008 goes without settlement\n→ A separate pacs.009 cover must move the funds through an intermediary\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Send one pacs.008 with settlement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSerial and cover are infrastructure choices, not customer choices. Serial suits cases where correspondent or nostro accounts exist and the settlement currency matches the payer account currency. Cover suits cases with no direct relationship or a different settlement currency.\n\nSerial or cover is a settlement decision, not a wording choice.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDecide by checking whether nostro accounts exist in the beneficiary bank country and which settlement systems the currency needs, such as Fedwire, TARGET2 or CHAPS.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Nostro accounts in the beneficiary country\n→ Settlement currency against payer currency\n→ Settlement system the currency needs\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Serial or cover?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the settlement path fits the accounts and the currency?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment sent serial when the accounts and currency demanded cover?", "takeaway": "Serial or cover is a settlement decision, not a wording choice. Decide by accounts and currency.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "decision", "title": "Do nostro accounts exist?", "note": "Check whether nostro accounts exist in the beneficiary bank country"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the currency match?", "note": "Check whether the settlement currency matches the payer account currency"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Serial: one pacs.008 with settlement", "note": "Suits cases where correspondent accounts exist and the currency matches"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cover: pacs.008 plus pacs.009", "note": "Suits cases with no direct relationship or a different settlement currency"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check the settlement systems", "note": "Check which systems the currency needs, such as Fedwire, TARGET2 or CHAPS"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Choice is about infrastructure", "note": "The cover method adds an extra leg, so the choice is about settlement"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C05", "seq": 174, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The optional address field that cost EUR 12,000 in deductions", "explain": "A pain.001 is sent without the sending business agent address and postcode.\n\nThe field is optional, so nobody captures it.\n\nThe beneficiary bank falls back to default values, and the EUR 120,000 payment arrives EUR 12,000 short after deductions and a compliance flag.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ pain.001 sent without the agent address\n→ Field is marked optional in the message\n→ Beneficiary or intermediary bank falls back to default values\n→ Compliance issues, errors or deductions follow\n→ Payment goes through, but short\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Optional means not needed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA field M, O or C status describes the message, while whether the information must be captured is decided by the business rules of the process. If the field is left out, the receiving bank may fall back to defaults.\n\nA field status describes the message, not the process.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAlways ask whether the field is needed for the process, rather than trusting the message status alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message field status\n→ Business capture rule\n→ Default value handling\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the field mandatory?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the field is captured when the process needs it?\"\n\nHave you seen an optional field left out, and the payment arrive short because of it?", "takeaway": "A field status describes the message, not the process. Ask whether the field is needed for the process.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Optional can still be mandatory", "parts": [{"label": "Optional field, needed by the bank", "note": "A field marked optional may still be needed by the sender bank"}, {"label": "Agent address and postcode", "note": "For example the sending business agent address and postcode"}, {"label": "Default values cause problems", "note": "Falling back to defaults can cause compliance issues, errors or deductions"}, {"label": "M, O or C describes the message", "note": "A field status describes the message, not the process"}, {"label": "Business rules decide capture", "note": "Whether the information must be captured is decided by business rules"}, {"label": "Ask whether the field is needed", "note": "Always ask whether the field is needed for the process"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": ["pain.001"], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C06", "seq": 175, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 90,000 payment that waited for a check it did not need", "explain": "A EUR 90,000 payment enters the engine.\n\nValidation checks run one after another, as they should.\n\nThen sanction screening, fraud screening, limit checks and risk checks also run one after another, for no reason at all. The payment is late.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment enters the engine\n→ Validation checks run in sequence and stop on first failure\n→ Screening checks also run in sequence\n→ Each screening check waits for the previous one\n→ The payment is delayed, and the SLA is at risk\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Run all controls in order.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nControl ordering follows dependency. Validation checks such as account number and currency, name matching, account status and closure depend on each other, so they run one after another. Sanction screening, fraud screening, limit checks, risk assessment and regulatory checks have no dependency, so they can run at the same time.\n\nSequential for validation, parallel for screening.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe design question is which controls depend on each other and which do not, because that decides both speed and the stopping point.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Dependent validation checks\n→ Independent screening checks\n→ Stopping point when a check fails\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which controls do we run?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which controls depend on each other?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment delayed because independent checks were forced to run in sequence?", "takeaway": "Sequential for validation, parallel for screening. Order follows dependency.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Controls with dependencies run in sequence", "note": "They run one after another and stop the payment as soon as one fails"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validation checks are sequential", "note": "Account number and currency, name matching, account status and closure"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Controls without dependencies run in parallel", "note": "They can run at the same time because they have no dependency"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Screening checks are parallel", "note": "Sanction, fraud, limit, risk and regulatory checks"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which controls depend on each other?", "note": "Which controls depend on each other decides both speed and the stopping point"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sequential validation, parallel screening", "note": "The rule of thumb is sequential for validation and parallel for screening"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch95-screening-fraud-and-sanctions-operations-in-detail.html", "label": "Chapter 95: Screening, fraud and sanctions operations in detail", "chapter": "ch95", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-003", "seq": 181, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Balance was enough, and the Rs. 25,000 payment was still declined", "explain": "A customer tries to pay Rs. 25,000 by card.\n\nThe balance is sufficient. The payment is declined.\n\nThe customer is sure the bank made a mistake. The bank followed its own rules.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Card is presented\n→ Card status check passes\n→ Funds check passes\n→ A fraud rule is breached\n→ Decline returned with a response code\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Sufficient funds means approval.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe issuer checks card status, available funds or credit, transaction limits and fraud rules together. A payment can be declined even when the balance is sufficient, if a fraud rule or a limit is breached.\n\nSufficient funds alone never guarantee approval.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign the decline to say which check failed, so the customer and the support team are not left guessing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Card status\n→ Funds or credit\n→ Limits and fraud rules\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does the customer have the money?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which check declined the payment?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer told their card was declined, with nobody able to say which check failed?", "takeaway": "Sufficient funds alone never guarantee approval. The checks decide together.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Card presented", "note": "The issuer must decide whether to approve or decline."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check card status", "note": "The issuer checks the card status."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check funds or credit", "note": "The issuer checks available funds or credit."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check limits and fraud rules", "note": "Limits and fraud rules are checked."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return approval or decline", "note": "An approval or decline is returned with a response code."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-024", "seq": 192, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 100,000 transaction with no audit trail", "explain": "A EUR 100,000 transaction is screened and cleared.\n\nMonths later, an auditor asks for the evidence.\n\nThere is none. The control ran, but nothing proves it ran.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Customer risk controls run\n→ Transaction risk controls run\n→ A case is escalated\n→ The audit trail is incomplete\n→ The control is treated as if it did not happen\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"We ran the control.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAnti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing controls cover both the customer and the transaction, and an analyst must understand them well enough to write requirements that keep the audit trail complete. A control that cannot be evidenced is treated as if it did not happen.\n\nEvidence is the control.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite requirements that keep the screening outcomes, the case escalation path and the audit trail complete from the start.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer risk controls\n→ Transaction risk controls\n→ Case escalation and audit trail\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we run the control?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the control happened?\"\n\nHave you seen a control treated as absent because the audit trail was missing?", "takeaway": "Evidence is the control. A control that cannot be evidenced is treated as if it did not happen.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "AML and CFT controls for a BA", "parts": [{"label": "Customer risk controls", "note": "Cover the customer side of the controls."}, {"label": "Transaction risk controls", "note": "Cover the transaction side of the controls."}, {"label": "Screening outcomes", "note": "Recorded as part of the control set."}, {"label": "Case escalation path", "note": "Shows how a case moves when it is raised."}, {"label": "Audit trail", "note": "A control that cannot be evidenced is treated as absent."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C020", "seq": 242, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 150,000 cover sent to the wrong bank", "explain": "A EUR 150,000 cover payment was sent.\nThe MT202 COV went to a bank that held no settlement account for it.\nThe funds landed where no one expected them, and settlement stopped.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT202 COV addressed to the wrong bank\n→ Funds land in an account that was not expecting them\n→ Beneficiary bank waits for a cover that never comes\n→ Settlement stops\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The address on the cover is an administrative detail.\"\nThe address is part of the settlement design.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a chain, the MT202 COV is addressed to the bank that holds the account from which the funds will settle, and each bank addresses its own cover leg to the next bank that will fund it. Addressing the cover to the wrong bank breaks settlement.\n\nThe address is part of the settlement design, not an administrative detail.\n\nWhat proves this cover was addressed to the bank that will fund it?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAddress each cover leg to the bank holding the settlement account. Check the address before sending, and treat a cover that lands in an unexpecting account as a settlement break, not a delay.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover address\n→ Settlement account holder\n→ Next bank in the chain\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the cover sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this cover was addressed to the bank that will fund it?\"\n\nHave you ever traced a cover that landed at the wrong bank?", "takeaway": "The address is part of the settlement design, not an administrative detail.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cover addressed to the funding bank", "note": "The MT202 COV goes to the bank holding the settlement account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each bank addresses its own leg onward", "note": "Each bank aims its cover leg at the next funding bank"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Wrong address breaks settlement", "note": "Funds landing in an unexpecting account break settlement"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement stops until the cover is rerouted", "note": "The funds must be moved to the bank that was expecting them"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C035", "seq": 257, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 130,000 cover, screened on one leg and missed on the other", "explain": "A EUR 130,000 cover payment was screened and cleared.\nThe MT103 was checked. The MT202 COV was not.\nThe funds leg carried a sanctioned party, and the payment went through anyway.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 screened before credit\n→ MT202 COV never screened\n→ Sanctioned party on the funds leg missed\n→ Payment credited, gap found later\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Screening the instruction screens the payment.\"\nThe information leg and the funds leg are screened separately.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 and its MT202 COV are both subject to sanction screening, and the screening must happen before the payment is credited to the beneficiary. A hit on either leg stops the payment. A bank that screens only the MT103 and not the cover, or the reverse, leaves a gap that a regulator will find.\n\nScreening one leg only leaves a gap a regulator will find.\n\nWhat proves both legs of this cover payment were screened?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nScreen the MT103 and the MT202 COV separately, before crediting. Stop the payment on a hit on either leg, and record both screening results on the case.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT103 screening\n→ MT202 COV screening\n→ Hit on either leg stops the payment\n→ Both results recorded\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the payment screened?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both legs of this cover payment were screened?\"\n\nHave you ever found a payment where only one leg had been screened?", "takeaway": "Screening one leg only leaves a gap a regulator will find.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 screened before credit", "note": "The information leg is screened before the payment is credited"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT202 COV screened separately", "note": "The funds leg is screened separately from the information leg"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Hit on either leg stops payment", "note": "A hit on either leg stops the payment"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Screening gap risks a regulator finding", "note": "Screening one leg only leaves a gap a regulator will find"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Record both screening results", "note": "Both legs must show a clear result before credit"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C054", "seq": 263, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 40,000 MT103 STP stopped because one field used the wrong option", "explain": "An MT103 STP for EUR 40,000 was sent to straight-through processing.\nIt stopped for manual handling.\nOne field had used an option other than A, and the system could not identify the bank.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 STP sent for straight-through processing\n→ Field 57 uses an option other than A\n→ System cannot identify the bank without human help\n→ Payment stops for manual handling\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any option that names the bank will do.\"\nAn MT103 STP only allows option A for fields such as 52, 54 and 57.\n\nWhy?\n\nOption A carries the party identifier and the BIC, so the receiving system can identify the bank without human help. Any other option leaves the bank to work out who the party is by hand, which is why those options are not allowed in an STP message.\n\nAny other option leaves the bank to work out who the party is by hand.\n\nWhat proves every party field in this STP message uses option A?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse option A, with the party identifier and the BIC, on every party field in an MT103 STP. Check fields 52, 54 and 57 before sending, and treat any other option as a manual-handling case.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 52 option A\n→ Field 54 option A\n→ Field 57 option A\n→ Straight-through processing achieved\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the field name the bank?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every party field in this STP message uses option A?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an STP payment stop over one wrong option?", "takeaway": "Any other option leaves the bank to work out who the party is by hand.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "MT103 STP for EUR 40,000", "parts": [{"label": "Straight-through-processing version", "note": "An MT103 STP is the straight-through-processing version of the MT103"}, {"label": "Only option A allowed", "note": "Only option A is allowed for fields such as 52, 54 and 57"}, {"label": "Option A carries party identifier and BIC", "note": "Option A carries the party identifier and the BIC"}, {"label": "Receiving system identifies the bank", "note": "The receiving system identifies the bank without human help"}, {"label": "Other options need manual work", "note": "Other options leave the bank to identify the party by hand"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C058", "seq": 267, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 250,000, no account relationship, and the wrong route chosen", "explain": "A Canadian bank had to pay USD 250,000 to a United States beneficiary.\nIt held its dollars with Wells Fargo.\nThe beneficiary held its account with Citibank New York.\nThe bank sent a single serial MT103, and the payment had nowhere to settle.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Canadian bank holds USD with Wells Fargo\n→ Beneficiary holds account with Citibank\n→ No account relationship between the sending bank and Citibank\n→ Serial MT103 sent, settlement fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One MT103 reaches any beneficiary.\"\nAccount relationships decide serial or cover.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause the sending bank had no account relationship with Citibank, the correct route was a cover payment: an MT103 sent directly to Citibank and an MT202 COV sent to Wells Fargo. If the sending bank had held an account with the beneficiary's bank, a single serial MT103 would have been enough.\n\nAccount relationships decide the route. No relationship means cover, not serial.\n\nWhat proves the chosen route matches the account relationships?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the account relationships before choosing the route. No direct relationship with the beneficiary's bank means cover: MT103 to the beneficiary bank, MT202 COV to the bank holding the funds.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sending bank accounts\n→ Beneficiary bank accounts\n→ Direct relationship or not\n→ Route chosen\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we send one MT103?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the chosen route matches the account relationships?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment fail because the route ignored the account relationships?", "takeaway": "Account relationships decide the route. No relationship means cover, not serial.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Canadian bank holds USD with Wells Fargo", "note": "The sending bank held its United States dollars with Wells Fargo"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary holds account with Citibank", "note": "The beneficiary held its account with Citibank New York"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Direct account relationship or not", "note": "The sending bank had no account relationship with Citibank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cover payment used: MT103 plus MT202 COV", "note": "An MT103 went directly to Citibank and an MT202 COV went to Wells Fargo"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Serial MT103 would have sufficed", "note": "With a direct account relationship, a single serial MT103 would have sufficed"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C061", "seq": 269, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 100,000 MT103 refused before it was even read", "explain": "EUR 100,000 was due to a beneficiary.\nThe MT103 was sent.\nThe receiving bank's system refused it outright, before anyone read it, because no RMA existed for the sender.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ No RMA set up for the sender\n→ Receiving system refuses the message\n→ Payment never starts\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The RMA can be sorted out afterwards.\"\nWithout an RMA the receiving bank's system will not accept the message.\n\nWhy?\n\nBefore one bank can send an MT103 to another over SWIFT, the receiving bank must set up an RMA, a relationship management authorisation, for that sender. The RMA is therefore a precondition for the payment, not a formality that can be sorted out afterwards.\n\nThe RMA is a precondition for the payment, not a formality that can be sorted out afterwards.\n\nWhat proves the RMA is in place before the MT103 is sent?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk the beneficiary bank to establish the RMA before the payment is sent. Check it before sending, and never promise a payment date until the authorisation exists.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ RMA in place\n→ MT103 sent\n→ Message accepted\n→ Payment on its way\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we send the MT103 now?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the RMA is in place before the MT103 is sent?\"\n\nHave you ever had a payment refused because the RMA was missing?", "takeaway": "The RMA is a precondition for the payment, not a formality that can be sorted out afterwards.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "RMA before a EUR 100,000 MT103 can arrive", "parts": [{"label": "Receiving bank sets up the RMA", "note": "The receiving bank sets up a relationship management authorisation for the sender"}, {"label": "Without an RMA, the message is refused", "note": "Without an RMA the receiving bank's system will not accept the message"}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank asked to establish it first", "note": "A beneficiary bank is asked to establish one before a payment is sent"}, {"label": "Precondition, not a formality", "note": "The RMA is a precondition, not a formality for afterwards"}, {"label": "Check before sending", "note": "Never promise a date until the authorisation exists."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C064", "seq": 272, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 175,000 took the long route when a shorter one was on file", "explain": "USD 175,000 was sent by the preferred route.\nIt took three days.\nA shorter route was on file the whole time, and no one used it because the preferred instruction felt like an order.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank publishes several SSIs for USD\n→ One route marked as preferred\n→ Sender uses the preferred route by default\n→ Beneficiary waits three days, shorter route unused\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Preferred means we must use it.\"\nThe preferred route is an option, not an order.\n\nWhy?\n\nA bank can publish several standing settlement instructions for one currency, marking one as preferred, but the preferred route is an option, not an order. The sender may use a shorter route, even one that passes through a bank named in a non-preferred instruction, because the aim is for the funds to reach the beneficiary.\n\nPreferred is an option, not an order. If the beneficiary is paid faster on the shorter route, no one will complain.\n\nWhat proves we chose the route that pays the beneficiary fastest?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCompare the routes on file before sending. Use the shorter route when it pays the beneficiary faster, even if it runs through a bank named in a non-preferred instruction.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Routes on file\n→ Preferred route\n→ Shorter route\n→ Beneficiary paid fastest\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which route should we use?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we chose the route that pays the beneficiary fastest?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a payment take the long route when a shorter one was on file?", "takeaway": "Preferred is an option, not an order. If the beneficiary is paid faster on the shorter route, no one will complain.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Bank publishes several SSIs", "note": "A bank can publish several standing settlement instructions for one currency"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "One route marked as preferred", "note": "One instruction is marked preferred, but it is an option, not an order"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Preferred route or shorter route", "note": "The sender may use a shorter route through a bank in a non-preferred instruction"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sender may use a shorter route", "note": "The sender may use a shorter route through a bank in a non-preferred instruction"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary paid faster", "note": "If the beneficiary is paid faster on the shorter route, no one will complain"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C094", "seq": 291, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 95,000 drawn on the parent's account with no authority on file", "explain": "A subsidiary instructed a EUR 95,000 payment from its parent company's account.\nThe payment went through.\nNo authorisation was on file, and the parent only found out when the statement arrived.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Subsidiary wants to use the parent's account\n→ No authorisation on file\n→ Payment drawn from the parent's account\n→ Parent learns of it from the statement\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A subsidiary can use the parent's account. It is the same group.\"\nAn authorisation is required before the subsidiary may draw on the parent's account.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn the MT101 the subsidiary appears as the Instructing Party in field 50L, while the parent company appears as the ordering customer in field 50H, because the parent owns the account that will be debited. The same need for authorisation applies whenever the sender of an instruction is not the owner of the account being debited.\n\nThe same need for authorisation applies whenever the sender is not the owner of the account being debited.\n\nWhat proves the sender was authorised to draw on this account?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the authorisation before debiting an account for a sender who does not own it. Record the subsidiary as Instructing Party in field 50L and the parent as ordering customer in field 50H, and never debit on group membership alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorisation on file\n→ Instructing Party in field 50L\n→ Ordering customer in field 50H\n→ Account owner confirmed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the subsidiary use this account?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the sender was authorised to draw on this account?\"\n\nHave you ever found a payment drawn on an account with no authority on file?", "takeaway": "The same need for authorisation applies whenever the sender is not the owner of the account being debited.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Subsidiary wants to use parent's account", "note": "A subsidiary cannot simply use its parent company's account to pay"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Authorisation on file or not", "note": "Authorisation is required before the subsidiary may draw on the parent's account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Subsidiary appears as Instructing Party", "note": "In the MT101 the subsidiary appears as the Instructing Party in field 50L"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Parent appears as ordering customer", "note": "The parent company appears as the ordering customer in field 50H, as it owns the account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Rule applies whenever sender is not owner", "note": "The same need applies whenever the sender is not the owner of the account debited"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT101"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-012", "seq": 312, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "An FX deal for EUR 800,000 settled a day late. The trade was placed at 4pm.", "explain": "A EUR 800,000 FX deal is traded late in the day.\nThe team expects settlement on the tenor date.\nThe funds arrive a day later, and a payment misses its value date.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ FX deal traded after the cut-off\n→ Tenor counted from the trade day\n→ Settlement slips by one day\n→ Payment misses its value date\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"An FX tenor always counts from the trade day.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn FX tenor such as Today, Tomorrow or Spot counts from the day the deal is traded, provided the trade happens before the FX cut-off time. Traded today before the cut-off, a Today tenor settles today, a Tomorrow tenor settles tomorrow, and Spot settles in two days or more. A trade after the cut-off settles a day later, because once the cut-off passes the bank is already working on the next day.\n\nAfter the cut-off, the trade day is tomorrow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the cut-off before promising any FX settlement date, and say so plainly when a trade lands after it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Trade time\n→ FX cut-off\n→ Tenor settlement date\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this trade was placed before the cut-off?\"\n\nHave you promised a settlement date that the cut-off had already moved?", "takeaway": "After the cut-off, the trade day is tomorrow.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Trade placed", "note": "Tenors count from the trade day when traded before the cut-off."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Traded before the cut-off?", "note": "A trade after the cut-off settles a day later."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Today tenor settles today", "note": "Traded today before cut-off, a Today tenor settles the same day."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Tomorrow tenor settles tomorrow", "note": "A Tomorrow tenor settles the next day on a pre cut-off trade."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Spot settles in two days or more", "note": "A Spot tenor settles in two days or more on a pre cut-off trade."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "After cut-off, a day later", "note": "Past the cut-off the bank works on the next day, so settlement slips."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-106", "seq": 324, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Collections moved to D-1 to be faster. EUR 90,000 of collections failed on day one.", "explain": "A creditor moves direct debit submission forward to D-1.\nEUR 90,000 of collections go out on the new date.\nThey fail at the first step, because the file cut-off and the scheme calendar were never checked.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Submission moved forward to D-1\n→ EUR 90,000 of collections submitted\n→ Scheme calendar and file cut-off never validated\n→ Collections fail at the first step\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Moving a date forward is a diary change.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBringing direct debit submission forward to D-1 is an operational commitment, not a diary change. Four things must support the earlier date, the scheme calendar, the file cut-off, the bank processing window and operational capacity, or collections fail at the first step. Verify all four before moving the date, because a date the infrastructure cannot meet turns into rejects, complaints and refunds.\n\nD-1 is an operational commitment, not a diary change.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nProve all four readiness checks before any submission date moves, and name the owner of each check.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme calendar\n→ File cut-off\n→ Bank processing window\n→ Operational capacity\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the infrastructure can meet this earlier date?\"\n\nHave you moved a submission date that the systems could not meet?", "takeaway": "D-1 is an operational commitment, not a diary change.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the scheme calendar", "note": "Prove the earlier date is a valid scheme day."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the file cut-off", "note": "Prove the file can be built in time."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the bank processing window", "note": "Prove the processing window fits."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Do all four checks pass?", "note": "One failed check stops the move."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate operational capacity", "note": "Prove staffing and systems can run the earlier date."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-110", "seq": 326, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A Request to Pay for EUR 3,000 had no date. Three systems settled it on three different days.", "explain": "A SEPA Request to Pay for EUR 3,000 arrives with no requested execution date.\nOne system derives a date, one asks the customer, one applies a rule.\nThe same request settles on three different dates.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Request to Pay for EUR 3,000 received\n→ No requested execution date on the request\n→ Each system derives its own date\n→ Same request settles on different dates\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The payment date can be left to each system to work out.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWithout a requested execution date a SEPA Request to Pay has no payment date, so one must be derived, chosen by the customer, or validated against a rule. Unless derivation, choice and validation are all defined, the same request settles on different dates in different systems. Execution then follows the agreed date, and a date that fails validation is rejected before it ever reaches the scheme.\n\nNo date on the request means no single payment date.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine derivation, customer choice and validation for the execution date before the first Request to Pay is accepted.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Date derivation rule\n→ Customer date choice\n→ Date validation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which date this request must settle on?\"\n\nHave you seen one request settle on different dates in different systems?", "takeaway": "No date on the request means no single payment date.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The missing execution date", "parts": [{"label": "Deriving the date", "note": "A defined rule fixes the date."}, {"label": "Customer picks the date", "note": "The customer may select the date."}, {"label": "Checking the date", "note": "The date is tested before use."}, {"label": "Executing downstream", "note": "Execution runs on the agreed date."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-111", "seq": 327, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 500,000 payment passed sanctions. The check that should have run first ran last.", "explain": "A EUR 500,000 payment is screened by four controls.\nThe controls were reordered for speed, and one ran after the payment it was meant to stop.\nThe payment passes, and the control that assumed a predecessor had run never fires.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 500,000 payment enters screening\n→ Controls reordered from sequential to parallel\n→ One control assumes a predecessor has run\n→ Bad payment passes the gap\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Control order is a performance tweak.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRunning payment controls sequentially or in parallel is a risk decision, not a performance tweak: it changes latency, risk coverage and behaviour under load. Sequential controls are slower but easy to reason about, since each waits for its predecessor, while parallel controls are faster but demand explicit rules for one control failing while another passes. Map control dependencies first, because a control that assumes a predecessor has run will pass bad payments the moment the order changes.\n\nControl order is a risk decision, not a performance tweak.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap control dependencies before any control is reordered, and write the rule for a control that fails while another passes.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Control dependencies\n→ Sequential order\n→ Parallel failure rules\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves no control runs ahead of the control it depends on?\"\n\nHave you reordered controls and opened a gap a bad payment walked through?", "takeaway": "Control order is a risk decision, not a performance tweak.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "How controls are orchestrated", "parts": [{"label": "Controls in sequence", "note": "In turn: slower, but easy to reason about."}, {"label": "Controls in parallel", "note": "Together: faster, but failure rules are essential."}, {"label": "Time taken", "note": "Order sets how long the flow takes."}, {"label": "Coverage and order", "note": "No control may run ahead of its dependency."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "SCN-09", "seq": 341, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 70,000 held on a party name that looked wrong. The team rejected it outright.", "explain": "A EUR 70,000 payment arrives with ultimate debtor details that look wrong, but are not proven wrong.\nThe team rejects it outright.\nA legitimate payment dies, and the customer loses a supplier deadline.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 70,000 payment with uncertain party details\n→ Details look wrong but are not proven wrong\n→ Payment rejected outright\n→ Legitimate payment lost\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Uncertain details mean reject the payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUltimate debtor or creditor details that look wrong but are not proven wrong call for a question, not a rejection, because rejecting outright can kill a legitimate payment. Park the payment under a clear status, request the additional information back up the chain, and give the sender a bounded window to answer. Clean facts release it, confirmed problems mean rejection or return depending on settlement state, and a missed deadline falls back to the policy default.\n\nUncertain means ask, not kill.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPark uncertain payments with a clear status, ask up the chain, and decide only on the facts that come back.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Party details under question\n→ Information request\n→ Bounded answer window\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves these details are wrong, not just uncertain?\"\n\nHave you killed a legitimate payment by rejecting what you should have questioned?", "takeaway": "Uncertain means ask, not kill.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Park it with a clear status", "note": "Held in a visible queue, never lost."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ask for the missing information", "note": "Ask the sender bank what is missing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Decide on the facts", "note": "Clean facts release; confirmed problems reject or return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fall back to the default on timeout", "note": "Pre-settlement rejects, post-settlement returns."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-12", "seq": 343, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 180,000 was covered but never credited. The instruction and the money took different roads.", "explain": "A EUR 180,000 cross-border payment is sent by cover.\nThe pacs.009 cover arrives, but the pacs.008 instruction does not.\nThe beneficiary bank cannot credit the customer, and the money sits unmatched.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 instruction sent\n→ pacs.009 COVE cover sent separately\n→ Cover arrives, instruction does not\n→ Beneficiary bank cannot credit the customer\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the cover arrives, the customer can be credited.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe cover method splits the payment in two: the pacs.008 carries customer details to the beneficiary bank, while the pacs.009 with settlement method COVE moves the funds through correspondents, the pair linked by one UETR. A sanctions hold on either leg stops the whole payment, because the beneficiary bank may credit the customer only when both legs reconcile. A cover that arrives alone parks as an unmatched item until its instruction completes the pair.\n\nInstruction one way, money another. Both must arrive.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the pacs.008 and the pacs.009 as a pair on one UETR, and never credit on the cover alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.008 instruction\n→ pacs.009 cover\n→ UETR pair\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves both legs of this cover payment have arrived and reconciled?\"\n\nHave you seen a cover arrive alone and sit unmatched?", "takeaway": "Instruction one way, money another. Both must arrive.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instruction and money travel apart", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008: the instruction", "note": "Customer and remittance details."}, {"label": "pacs.009 COVE: the funds", "note": "Funds through correspondents."}, {"label": "Chain of correspondents", "note": "Funding moves through Nostro accounts."}, {"label": "Hold on either leg", "note": "A hold on either leg stops the payment."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "SCN-18", "seq": 347, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 300,000 payment went quiet for a day. Nobody owned it, and nobody called the customer.", "explain": "A EUR 300,000 payment passes its expected window with no status update.\nIt sits on a dashboard, flagged but unowned.\nThe deadline passes, the customer calls first, and the team starts from zero.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 300,000 payment passes its expected window\n→ No status update from the tracker\n→ Flag raised with no owner\n→ Customer calls before the team acts\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A dashboard flag is enough to manage at-risk payments.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAt-risk has a precise meaning: no status update within the expected window for that corridor and amount, drawn from the tracker, pacs.002 statuses and internal hop timestamps. Every flagged payment needs a named owner, operations, the relationship manager, or investigations, and action before the deadline, reroute, chase the intermediary, or warn the customer early. A dashboard of unowned flags is decoration; a flag works only with an owner and a next action.\n\nA flag without an owner is decoration.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every at-risk flag a named owner and a next action before the deadline, and warn the customer early.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ At-risk definition\n→ Named owner\n→ Next action\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payment is owned and being worked?\"\n\nHave you watched a flagged payment sit unowned until the customer called?", "takeaway": "A flag without an owner is decoration.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define at-risk precisely", "note": "No update inside the corridor expected window."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Pull the signals", "note": "Tracker, pacs.002 statuses, hop timestamps."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who owns this flag?", "note": "Operations, the relationship manager, or investigations."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Route it to an owner", "note": "Operations, the relationship manager, or investigations."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Act ahead of the deadline", "note": "Reroute, chase, or warn the customer early."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002"], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-23", "seq": 350, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A surname parked a 28 million dirham letter of credit for 8 working days.", "explain": "A legitimate 28 million dirham letter of credit is stopped by a fuzzy sanctions match on a common surname.\nIt sits parked for 8 working days.\nThe screening team treats every alert the same, and the real business waits.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ 28 million dirham letter of credit presented\n→ Fuzzy match on a common surname\n→ Payment parked for 8 working days\n→ Every alert treated the same\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Every sanctions alert deserves the same attention.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSanctions screening is a false-positive business: about 95 percent of alerts are false positives, yet each one delays real business, and a fuzzy match on a common surname parked a legitimate 28 million dirham letter of credit for 8 working days. The answer is risk triage, exact matches and high-risk corridors first, with matching thresholds tuned per corridor and name frequency instead of one global setting. Judge the screening flow on its KPIs, the false-positive rate and the average hold time, and measure and report both.\n\nMost alerts are false positives. Triage by risk, not by queue order.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTriage screening alerts by risk, tune thresholds per corridor, and report the false-positive rate and the hold time.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Alert triage\n→ Matching thresholds\n→ Hold time KPI\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this alert was triaged by risk, not just queued?\"\n\nHave you seen a real deal parked for days on a common surname?", "takeaway": "Most alerts are false positives. Triage by risk, not by queue order.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "False positives that delay business", "parts": [{"label": "Fuzzy name match", "note": "Common surname parked a real letter of credit."}, {"label": "Triage by risk", "note": "Exact matches and high-risk corridors go first."}, {"label": "Thresholds per corridor", "note": "Set per corridor and name frequency."}, {"label": "Hold time as KPI", "note": "False-positive rate and hold time, measured."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch95-screening-fraud-and-sanctions-operations-in-detail.html", "label": "Chapter 95: Screening, fraud and sanctions operations in detail", "chapter": "ch95", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "SCN-24", "seq": 351, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A USD 500,000 payment matched the sanctions list. The funds froze, and the trail mattered.", "explain": "A USD 500,000 payment matches a name on the sanctions list.\nThe payment is held at once and no funds move.\nThe analyst wonders whether to release it quietly and move on.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ USD 500,000 payment screened\n→ Name matches the sanctions list\n→ Payment held immediately\n→ Analyst considers releasing it without a trail\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A sanctions hold is a pause, not a case.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA sanctions list match stops the payment at once: it is held immediately and no funds move while the review is open. The analyst escalates to the compliance officer with the match evidence attached, and true-match verification decides the path, release if false, block if true. Where the activity meets the threshold a Suspicious Activity Report is filed, and every step is documented, because the decision trail is what regulators will ask for.\n\nA list match stops the payment. The trail proves the decision.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHold on a match, escalate with the evidence, verify the true match, and document every step.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Match evidence\n→ True match verification\n→ Decision trail\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this match was verified, not just held or released?\"\n\nHave you held a payment on a list match with no documented trail?", "takeaway": "A list match stops the payment. The trail proves the decision.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Screening flags a match", "note": "Beneficiary name fuzzy-matches the SDN list."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Hold the payment", "note": "Funds frozen while the review is open."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send it to compliance", "note": "Send the match evidence up."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "True match or false match?", "note": "False releases, true blocks."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "File the SAR if needed", "note": "File where the activity meets the threshold."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch95-screening-fraud-and-sanctions-operations-in-detail.html", "label": "Chapter 95: Screening, fraud and sanctions operations in detail", "chapter": "ch95", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "SCN-25", "seq": 352, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 85,000 left a customer's account. The bank called it authorised, and refused to pay it back.", "explain": "A scammer posing as a bank representative wins remote access to a victim's computer.\nUSD 85,000 leaves from the victim's own login and device.\nThe bank classifies the payment as authorised, and the customer loses everything.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Scammer poses as a bank representative\n→ Remote access won to the victim computer\n→ USD 85,000 sent from the victim's own login\n→ Bank refuses reimbursement as authorised\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A payment sent by the victim must be refunded.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAuthorised push payment fraud exploits the gap between a valid payment and a deceived customer. A scammer posing as a bank representative won remote access to the victim computer, and the wire left from the victim's own login and device, so the bank classified it as authorised and refused reimbursement. The defences are confirmation of payee, friction on first-time payees, warnings at the point of payment, and behavioural detection, and the regulatory trend is shifting liability back toward banks.\n\nValid payment, deceived customer. Authorised is not the same as safe.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the defences before the payment, not the argument after it: confirmation of payee, friction on first-time payees, warnings and behavioural detection.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Confirmation of payee\n→ First-time payee friction\n→ Point of payment warnings\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payee was checked before the money left?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer lose money on a payment the system called valid?", "takeaway": "Valid payment, deceived customer. Authorised is not the same as safe.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Valid payment, deceived customer", "parts": [{"label": "The deception", "note": "Fake bank representative wins remote access."}, {"label": "Technically valid", "note": "Sent from the victim's own login and device."}, {"label": "The reimbursement gap", "note": "Deceived customer, rule-following payment."}, {"label": "Controls that prevent it", "note": "Confirmation of payee, friction, warnings and detection."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch95-screening-fraud-and-sanctions-operations-in-detail.html", "label": "Chapter 95: Screening, fraud and sanctions operations in detail", "chapter": "ch95", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "SCN-31", "seq": 355, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "One mule account looked like a case. Linked together, they were a network moving millions.", "explain": "Small transfers arrive from unrelated sources into one account.\nThe funds leave within minutes of credit, and almost nothing is retained.\nThe team treats the account as a single case, while the network keeps moving.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Small inward transfers from unrelated sources\n→ Funds moved out within minutes of credit\n→ Minimal balance retention\n→ Common device and IP linkage across accounts\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"One suspicious account is one case to close.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA mule network is a pattern, not an account: small inward transfers from unrelated sources, funds moved out within minutes of credit, minimal balance retention, and common device or IP linkage. One account is a case, linked accounts are a network, so freeze suspect accounts on debit first, then run EDD and KYC reviews and escalate to investigations. Recalibrate detection rules so the pattern is caught earlier next time, without punishing genuine customers.\n\nRead the network, not the case.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFreeze on debit first, then link the accounts, run EDD and KYC, and recalibrate the rules to catch the pattern earlier.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Network linkage\n→ Debit freeze\n→ EDD and KYC review\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves these accounts are linked, not just individually suspicious?\"\n\nHave you closed one mule account while the network kept moving?", "takeaway": "Read the network, not the case.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Spot the red flags", "note": "Small credits in, out in minutes, tiny balances, shared devices."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the network linkage", "note": "Linked accounts make a network, not a case."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Freeze on debit first?", "note": "Stop the flow at once."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Run EDD and KYC checks", "note": "Review every flagged account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recalibrate the rules", "note": "Catch the pattern sooner next time."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch95-screening-fraud-and-sanctions-operations-in-detail.html", "label": "Chapter 95: Screening, fraud and sanctions operations in detail", "chapter": "ch95", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-F11", "seq": 358, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Due date 15 October. Five calendars broke, and the SDD timeline broke with them.", "explain": "A collection is due on 15 October, with due date, settlement date and debit date all the same.\nA new national holiday is declared after planning, and one PSP is closed.\nThe team assumes the due date moves, and the collection fails.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection due 15 October\n→ Creditor PSP closed, TARGET open\n→ New national holiday declared after planning\n→ Team assumes the due date moves\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"On a normal due date, the timeline takes care of itself.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOn a normal due date the due date, settlement date and debit date are the same, but the timeline breaks when calendars do not align. Five scenarios test it for a single due date: the creditor PSP closed, TARGET or the CSM closed, the debtor PSP closed, both PSPs closed, and a new national holiday declared after planning. In each case the BA must ask which parties are open, what activity needs to happen and which calendar applies, rather than assuming which date moves, because a good SDD timeline is designed for the broken assumptions, not only for the happy flow.\n\nDesign the timeline for the broken assumptions, not only the happy flow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest every SDD timeline against all five break scenarios, and ask which parties are open and which calendar applies.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor PSP calendar\n→ TARGET calendar\n→ Debtor PSP calendar\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this timeline holds when a calendar breaks?\"\n\nHave you built a timeline that only worked on a perfect day?", "takeaway": "Design the timeline for the broken assumptions, not only the happy flow.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Due Date 15 October", "parts": [{"label": "Creditor PSP closed", "note": "Creditor closed, debtor and TARGET open"}, {"label": "TARGET or CSM closed", "note": "Both PSPs open, no inter-PSP settlement"}, {"label": "Debtor PSP closed", "note": "Can the debtor account be debited that day"}, {"label": "Both PSPs closed", "note": "Infrastructure open, neither PSP working"}, {"label": "New national holiday", "note": "A holiday declared after the timeline was planned"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/lifecycle.html", "label": "Payment Lifecycle Simulator", "slug": "lifecycle"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F07", "seq": 360, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 900 direct debit failed for insufficient funds. The scheduler retried it in the wrong order.", "explain": "A EUR 900 direct debit fails because the account cannot fund it.\nThe scheduler retries in first-in-first-out order and fails again.\nThe salary credit lands two days later, and the D+5 window is almost gone.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 900 debit fails for insufficient funds\n→ Scheduler retries in FIFO order\n→ Salary credit lands on a later date\n→ D+5 window nearly closed\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Retries should run in first-in-first-out order.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a direct debit fails for insufficient funds, retries are confined to the D+5 window of inter-PSP business days, and D+5 is a window, not a penalty or a fine. A scheduler that retries in first-in-first-out order can keep failing, because retry logic is also seasonal: salary credits land on specific dates, and salary accounts hold a balance on those dates. The better requirement checks the balance daily and collects when the account can fund the debit, rather than assuming the scheduler is the optimum solution. Who owns a retry failure, the BA, the developer or the product owner, is a stakeholder agreement, not a technical fact.\n\nD+5 is a window, not a penalty. Collect when the balance can fund it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the balance daily inside the D+5 window, collect when the account can fund the debit, and agree who owns a retry failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ D+5 retry window\n→ Daily balance check\n→ Retry ownership\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this retry was timed to the balance, not to the queue?\"\n\nHave you retried a debit on a schedule that ignored payday?", "takeaway": "D+5 is a window, not a penalty. Collect when the balance can fund it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Debit fails for insufficient funds", "note": "The account cannot fund the collection on the due date"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Within the D+5 retry window?", "note": "Retries are allowed only inside D+5 inter-PSP business days"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check the balance daily", "note": "Salary crediting dates change when an account can pay"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Balance sufficient now?", "note": "Collect when the balance funds the debit, not by FIFO order"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Collect or let the window close", "note": "A failed collection after D+5 stays failed"}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "insufficient funds", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch62-sepa-direct-debit-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 62: SEPA Direct Debit Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch62", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-D02", "seq": 366, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 4,000 collection was presented on D-1. The team thought the window had closed at D-14.", "explain": "A EUR 4,000 collection is presented to the inter-PSP space.\nThe team thinks D-14 was the deadline, so the collection must be late.\nD-14 opens the window, D-1 closes it, and the collection is inside it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 4,000 collection presented\n→ Team reads D-14 as the submission deadline\n→ Collection assumed late\n→ Window actually runs D-14 to D-1\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"D-14 is the deadline for presenting a collection.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nD-14 marks the earliest Inter-PSP Business Day for presenting a collection to the Inter-PSP Space, and it doubles as the pre-notification date, by which the debtor must be told about the collection at least 14 calendar days ahead of the Due Date. Receipt by the debtor PSP through the CSM must fall inside that span, with D-1 as the latest permitted day, and for a 15 October Due Date the window is 1 October to 14 October, while the debtor pre-notification in the example went out on 15 September. Confusing D-14 with a submission deadline misreads the rule, since D-14 opens the window and D-1 closes it.\n\nD-14 opens the window. D-1 closes it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck receipt against the D-14 to D-1 window, and never read D-14 as a deadline.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ D-14 opening\n→ D-1 closing\n→ Debtor pre-notification\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this collection was received inside the window?\"\n\nHave you called a collection late when the window had only just opened?", "takeaway": "D-14 opens the window. D-1 closes it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "D-14 opens the window", "note": "Earliest Inter-PSP Business Day to present the collection to the Inter-PSP Space."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Notify the debtor first", "note": "Debtor must be told at least 14 calendar days before the Due Date."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Present through the CSM", "note": "Debtor PSP must receive the collection via the CSM inside the window."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Inside D-14 to D-1?", "note": "Receipt earlier than D-14 or later than D-1 breaks the rule."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-D06", "seq": 369, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 15,000 payment reached the beneficiary in 10 seconds. Compliance had not finished checking it.", "explain": "A EUR 15,000 payment reaches the beneficiary in 10 seconds.\nThe AML and sanctions checks run longer than the payment window allows.\nThe team wonders whether instant means the checks can be skipped.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 15,000 instant payment sent\n→ Beneficiary credited in 10 seconds\n→ Compliance checks still running\n→ Team asks if checks can be skipped\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Instant payments move too fast for compliance.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment reaching the beneficiary in 10 seconds still needs AML, sanctions and fraud controls, because instant means compliance must work at payment speed, not that controls disappear. The difficulty is that screening for AML, sanctions and fraud often runs longer than the instant window allows, yet newer technology is making screens smarter and automated, with same-day handling and, in some cases, real-time checks. Once the check sits inside the journey instead of blocking it, instant payments become instant, intelligent and compliant.\n\nInstant means compliance at payment speed, not no compliance.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the checks inside the instant journey, with smarter automated screening, instead of bolting them on after it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ AML checks\n→ Sanctions screening\n→ Fraud controls\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the checks ran at payment speed on this payment?\"\n\nHave you treated instant as a reason to skip a check?", "takeaway": "Instant means compliance at payment speed, not no compliance.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instant, yet fully controlled", "parts": [{"label": "Instant is not uncontrolled", "note": "AML, sanctions and fraud controls still apply to a 10-second payment."}, {"label": "Timing gap", "note": "Compliance checks often run longer than the instant payment window allows."}, {"label": "Smarter checks", "note": "Screens are now intelligent, automated, same-day, and sometimes real-time."}, {"label": "Check inside the journey", "note": "Compliance becomes part of the payment journey, not a roadblock."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch43-payment-security.html", "label": "Chapter 43: Payment Security", "chapter": "ch43", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-E15", "seq": 379, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Intermediary Agent 2 was optional, so a EUR 80,000 payment was left waiting", "explain": "A EUR 80,000 cover payment was sent with Intermediary Agent 2 left out.\n\nThe message definition says the field is optional, 0..1.\n\nSo the sender left it out, and the beneficiary bank held the payment.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 cover payment sent without Intermediary Agent 2\n→ Beneficiary bank receives the payment but cannot see the expected cover path\n→ Payment held for cover information\n→ Investigation opened, credit delayed\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Intermediary Agent 2 is optional, so leave it out.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMessage cardinality answers a design question, not a business question. The beneficiary bank needs the intermediary chain to understand where the cover is coming from, and without it the payment cannot be matched with confidence.\n\nOptional in the message does not mean unnecessary in the flow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe working test is not whether the field is mandatory. It is whether the business scenario needs it, who consumes it, and what happens if it is missing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message definition and cardinality\n→ Business scenario need\n→ Who consumes the field\n→ What breaks if it is missing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the field mandatory?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the receiving bank can process this payment without it?\"\n\nHave you seen an optional field left out that stopped a payment?", "takeaway": "Optional in the message does not mean unnecessary in the flow.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Optional at message level, necessary in the flow", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 cover payment", "note": "EUR 80,000 sent without Intermediary Agent 2."}, {"label": "Intermediary Agent 2, 0..1", "note": "Optional in the message definition, so it reads as safe to omit."}, {"label": "Beneficiary bank without it", "note": "Cannot see the expected cover path, so it holds the payment."}, {"label": "Held and investigated", "note": "Payment waits for cover information instead of being credited."}, {"label": "The working test", "note": "Ask who consumes the field, in which scenario, and what breaks without it."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-012", "seq": 384, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A bank was EUR 3 million short at settlement, and the last check caught it", "explain": "A bank ended the clearing cycle EUR 3 million short on its settlement account.\n\nSettlement was minutes away.\n\nThe final check caught the shortfall before the money moved.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Participants must hold enough money on the settlement account\n→ The mechanism reviews short participants at intervals\n→ A bank drifts short during the cycle\n→ Final check runs immediately before settlement\n→ Shortfall caught, settlement held until the bank funds its account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Periodic checks are enough.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPositions move between checks. A bank that was funded at the last review can be short by settlement time, so the check that matters most is the one just before settlement.\n\nThe last check before settlement is the one that saves the cycle.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRisk control needs procedures that keep participants funded, interval reviews, and a final check immediately before settlement. No settlement should complete without that last check.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Funding procedures\n→ Interval reviews of short participants\n→ Final check before settlement\n→ Settlement itself\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we check the positions today?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every participant is funded right now, before settlement?\"\n\nHave you seen a settlement saved by a last-minute funding check?", "takeaway": "The last check before settlement is the one that saves the cycle.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "How the CSM checks short positions", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 3 million short", "note": "A bank ended the cycle short on its settlement account."}, {"label": "Procedures for enough funds", "note": "Participants must hold enough money on the settlement account."}, {"label": "Checks from time to time", "note": "The CSM reviews short participants positions at intervals."}, {"label": "Final check before settlement", "note": "The last check happens just before settlement."}, {"label": "Shortfall caught early", "note": "A bank that cannot cover its position is caught before settlement completes."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-013", "seq": 385, "module": "Core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "An indirect participant sent EUR 200,000 it did not have, and the direct participant paid for it", "explain": "An indirect participant sent a EUR 200,000 debit through its direct participant.\n\nIts account with the direct participant held only EUR 40,000.\n\nThe direct participant carried the risk, because accountability for the indirect participant sits with it.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Indirect participant funds an account held with the direct participant\n→ Debit instruction arrives for EUR 200,000\n→ Direct participant draws the money from that account first\n→ Funds are insufficient, so the transaction is rejected\n→ For a credit, the direct participant credits the indirect participant account and forwards the transaction\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The indirect participant manages its own risk.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt does not. The direct participant is accountable for risk arising from indirect participants, so it must check funds before sending a debit on their behalf and reject when the account is short.\n\nSponsoring a participant means carrying its risk.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a design adds an indirect participant, name who funds the account, who checks before sending, and who absorbs the loss when the funds are not there.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Indirect participant funding\n→ Funds check before the debit\n→ Rejection when funds are short\n→ Credit forwarding\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the indirect participant send payments?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds were there before the debit was sent?\"\n\nHave you seen a direct participant absorb a loss for an indirect participant?", "takeaway": "Sponsoring a participant means carrying its risk.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "IP opens an account with the DP", "note": "The indirect participant puts EUR 40,000 on an account with the direct participant."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "DP checks funds before debit", "note": "Before sending a EUR 200,000 debit, the DP takes the money from that account."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Funds sufficient?", "note": "If the account is short, the direct participant rejects the transaction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Insufficient funds means rejection", "note": "The EUR 200,000 debit is rejected, the risk stays with the DP."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Credits are forwarded", "note": "For credit transactions, the DP credits the IP account and forwards the transaction."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "insufficient funds", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-169", "seq": 428, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A mobile number pointed at the wrong account, and INR 25,000 went to a stranger", "explain": "A customer sent INR 25,000 to a family member using a mobile number.\n\nThe money left the account instantly.\n\nIt arrived in a stranger's account, because the proxy resolved to the wrong person.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer enters a mobile number as the beneficiary\n→ System resolves the mobile number to an account\n→ Resolution rules differ by scheme, and the wrong rule was applied\n→ INR 25,000 credited to the wrong account\n→ Recovery depends on the stranger agreeing to return it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Proxy resolution is a lookup convenience.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a linked payment ecosystem, the beneficiary can be identified by a mobile number, a UPI ID or virtual payment address, or a traditional account number. The system must resolve that identifier to the correct account before the payment is sent, and the resolution rules differ by scheme. Getting the proxy resolution wrong sends the money to the wrong person.\n\nResolution is a payment control, not a lookup.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat proxy resolution as a control: resolve before sending, confirm the resolved name to the sender, and log the rule that was applied. A wrong resolution is a wrong payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The identifier entered\n→ The resolution rule applied\n→ The account resolved\n→ The name confirmed to the sender before sending\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the proxy resolve?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves it resolved to the right person?\"\n\nHave you seen money go to the wrong person because a proxy resolved wrong?", "takeaway": "Resolution is a payment control, not a lookup.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Beneficiary identifier resolution", "parts": [{"label": "Mobile number", "note": "Beneficiary identified by a mobile number."}, {"label": "UPI ID or virtual address", "note": "Beneficiary can be identified by a UPI ID or virtual payment address."}, {"label": "Traditional account number", "note": "Beneficiary can be identified by a traditional account number."}, {"label": "Resolve before sending", "note": "System must resolve the identifier to the correct account before sending."}, {"label": "Resolution rules differ", "note": "Resolution rules differ by scheme."}, {"label": "INR 25,000 to the wrong person", "note": "Getting the proxy resolution wrong sends the money to the wrong person."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-149", "seq": 431, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A customer confirmed a EUR 8,000 transfer, then found the real cost was EUR 8,400", "explain": "A customer sent EUR 8,000 across borders.\n\nThe screen showed the amount and a confirm button.\n\nAfter confirming, the customer found the total cost was EUR 8,400, and the delivery date was three days later than expected.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer enters EUR 8,000 to send\n→ Screen shows the amount, hides the fee breakdown\n→ Exchange rate shown, but not the rate actually applied\n→ No expected delivery status shown\n→ Customer confirms, then learns the real cost and the real date\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Transparency can be added as a footnote.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA customer sending money across borders needs to see the total cost, the applied exchange rate, the fee breakdown, and the expected delivery status before confirming. If any of those are hidden or unclear, the customer cannot judge whether the payment is good value or when it will arrive.\n\nA hidden cost is a broken promise.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTransparency is a product requirement, not a nice-to-have, and it has to be designed into the journey, not added as a footnote. Show total cost, applied rate, fee breakdown and delivery status before the confirm button.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Total cost\n→ Applied exchange rate\n→ Fee breakdown\n→ Expected delivery status\n→ The confirm button, after all four\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the customer confirm?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the customer saw the full cost before confirming?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer confirm a payment without seeing its real cost?", "takeaway": "A hidden cost is a broken promise.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Customer transparency on fees and FX", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 8,000 sent, EUR 8,400 cost", "note": "The real total only appeared after confirmation."}, {"label": "Total cost", "note": "Customer needs to see the total cost."}, {"label": "Applied exchange rate", "note": "Customer needs to see the applied exchange rate."}, {"label": "Fee breakdown", "note": "Customer needs to see the fee breakdown."}, {"label": "Expected delivery status", "note": "Customer needs to see the expected delivery status."}, {"label": "Product requirement", "note": "Transparency is a product requirement, not a nice-to-have."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-145", "seq": 433, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 5,000 payment had to clear in 10 seconds, and the sanctions check needed 40", "explain": "A customer sent a EUR 5,000 instant payment.\n\nIt had to complete in 10 seconds.\n\nThe sanctions check needed 40 seconds, and the team was asked to skip it to meet the speed promise.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ EUR 5,000 instant payment sent\n→ Rail allows 10 seconds to complete\n→ Sanctions, anti-money laundering and fraud checks still required\n→ Checks take longer than the rail allows\n→ Pressure to bypass the checks to meet the speed promise\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Speed and controls can trade off against each other.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn instant payment that completes in 10 seconds still needs sanctions, anti-money laundering and fraud checks. The controls must be fast and automated, with a clear escalation path and a full audit trail when a payment is held. The speed target and the control standard have to hold at the same time.\n\nA 10-second payment still needs its controls.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign the controls to fit the rail: fast, automated, with escalation and a full audit trail when a payment is held. Never let a design imply that controls are bypassed to meet the speed promise.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The 10-second speed target\n→ Sanctions, AML and fraud checks\n→ The escalation path when a payment is held\n→ The audit trail, complete\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment complete in 10 seconds?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the controls ran and the audit trail is complete?\"\n\nHave you seen speed pressure used to justify skipping a control?", "takeaway": "A 10-second payment still needs its controls.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment completes in 10 seconds", "note": "Instant payment completes in 10 seconds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sanctions, AML and fraud checks", "note": "Sanctions, anti-money laundering, and fraud checks are still needed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Checks slower than the rail?", "note": "Checks can take longer than the rail allows."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fast automated controls", "note": "Controls must be fast and automated."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Escalation and audit trail", "note": "Clear escalation path and a full audit trail when a payment is held."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Never bypass controls", "note": "Design must never imply controls are bypassed to meet the speed promise."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch23-sanctions-screening.html", "label": "Chapter 23: Sanctions Screening", "chapter": "ch23", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-146", "seq": 434, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A fraudster moved EUR 9,000 out of an account in 20 seconds, before the customer noticed", "explain": "A fraudster gained access to a customer's account.\n\nIn 20 seconds, EUR 9,000 had left it through instant payments.\n\nThe customer noticed the next morning, long after the money was gone.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Fraudster gains access to the account\n→ Instant payments settle in seconds\n→ EUR 9,000 moves out before the customer notices\n→ No intervention point stops the flow\n→ Post-transaction monitoring finds it the next day, too late\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One fraud control is enough.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nInstant payments settle in seconds, which is what customers want and what fraudsters want too. An authorised push payment scam can also persuade the customer to send the money themselves. No single control can cover a journey that completes in seconds, so the controls must work together.\n\nSeconds to settle means seconds to steal.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nLayer the controls: risk signals, velocity checks, confirmation of payee, intervention points, and post-transaction monitoring together. Each one covers a gap the others cannot.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Risk signals\n→ Velocity checks\n→ Confirmation of payee\n→ Intervention points\n→ Post-transaction monitoring\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we have a fraud control?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the controls together cover a journey that completes in seconds?\"\n\nHave you seen a fraud move faster than the controls built to stop it?", "takeaway": "Seconds to settle means seconds to steal.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Instant payments settle in seconds", "note": "Instant payments settle in seconds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fraudster gains access", "note": "Fraudster who gains access can move EUR 9,000 out before customer notices."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Authorised push payment scam", "note": "Scam can persuade the customer to send the money themselves."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Any control stopping it?", "note": "No single control can cover a journey that completes in seconds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Risk signals, velocity, payee check", "note": "Controls must use risk signals, velocity checks and confirmation of payee."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intervention and monitoring", "note": "Controls must use intervention points and post-transaction monitoring."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-A07", "seq": 438, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One pacs.008 for EUR 5 million went to the wrong system, because the route code was never read", "explain": "A pacs.008 for EUR 5 million was sent.\n\nIt arrived in the wrong settlement system.\n\nThe message was perfect. The route code on it was never read.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 for EUR 5 million prepared\n→ BAH BizSvc code declares the route, not the message\n→ Payment meant for T2, central bank money settlement via ESMIG to the ECB\n→ BizSvc read as a CBPR+ route, sent cross-border through SWIFT FINplus\n→ Payment lands in the wrong system, settlement, timing, cost and risk all wrong\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The message decides the route.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe same pacs.008 resolves to a different route according to its destination, and the BAH BizSvc code, not the message, declares that route. A T2 payment travels through ESMIG to the ECB, identified by a BizSvc such as trgt.002.001.01, while a CBPR+ payment travels cross-border through SWIFT FINplus, identified by a BizSvc such as swift.cbprplus.02. Because the route sets settlement, timing, cost and risk, a payment routed wrongly can face delay, rejection or incorrect settlement.\n\nThe pacs.008 is the instruction. The BAH plus BizSvc is the route.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a pacs.008 is built, check the BAH BizSvc before sending. The message says what to pay. The BizSvc says where it settles.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The pacs.008 instruction\n→ The BAH BizSvc route code\n→ T2 settlement, central bank money\n→ CBPR+ settlement, cross-border\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the pacs.008 correct?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the BizSvc matches the intended settlement system?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment routed wrong because the BizSvc was never checked?", "takeaway": "The pacs.008 is the instruction. The BAH plus BizSvc is the route.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "BAH BizSvc declares the route", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 EUR 5 million", "note": "One message, different route according to destination."}, {"label": "T2 via ESMIG to ECB", "note": "Central bank money settlement, BizSvc trgt.002.001.01."}, {"label": "CBPR+ via SWIFT FINplus", "note": "Cross-border route, BizSvc swift.cbprplus.02."}, {"label": "Settlement, timing, cost, risk", "note": "The route sets settlement, timing, cost and risk."}, {"label": "Wrong route consequences", "note": "Wrong routing can delay, reject or mis-settle a payment."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C045", "seq": 482, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "We hold each other's accounts. Do we still need an intermediary?", "explain": "The ordering bank and the beneficiary bank already hold accounts with each other.\nA EUR 90,000 international payment needs to move between them.\nThe operations team still routes it through an intermediary, paying the fee and adding a day, out of habit.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Ordering bank and beneficiary bank hold accounts with each other\n→ Payment routed through an intermediary anyway\n→ Extra fee charged, extra day added\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An intermediary is always needed for a cross-border payment.\"\n\nA direct relationship can carry the payment without an intermediary, which is faster and cheaper, but an intermediary may still be used for a currency, a corridor, or a compliance reason. The choice is a design decision, not a rule, and it depends on the corridor and the banks involved.\n\nDirect is faster and cheaper, but the corridor decides.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest the direct route first. If the accounts exist, the payment can travel directly, and an intermediary is justified only by a currency, corridor or compliance reason that is named, not assumed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Direct route, faster and cheaper\n→ Intermediary for currency needs\n→ Intermediary for corridor needs\n→ Intermediary for compliance reasons\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we use an intermediary?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the intermediary is needed for this corridor?\"\n\nHave you ever paid an intermediary fee on a payment that could have gone direct?", "takeaway": "The choice is a design decision, not a rule, and it depends on the corridor and the banks involved.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Direct account relationship", "parts": [{"label": "Direct route is faster and cheaper", "note": "A direct relationship carries the payment without an intermediary."}, {"label": "Intermediary may still be used", "note": "An intermediary may still serve a currency, corridor or compliance need."}, {"label": "Choice is a design decision", "note": "The choice depends on the corridor and the banks involved."}, {"label": "Name the reason", "note": "A currency, corridor or compliance reason must be named, not assumed."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-107", "seq": 502, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The correspondent cut the limit without warning", "explain": "A correspondent cuts an intraday credit limit without warning.\nThe routing engine keeps feeding the same corridor, EUR 300,000 of payments queue up, and rejects arrive that nobody can explain.\nThe corridor was fine yesterday. The limit changed, and the engine never noticed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Correspondent cuts the intraday limit\n→ Engine keeps feeding the same corridor\n→ Payments queue and rejects arrive unexplained\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Routing rules are written for the quiet day.\"\n\nGood routing rules are written for the bad day, not the quiet one: a correspondent can cut an intraday credit limit without warning, and an engine that keeps feeding the same corridor will queue payments and generate rejects it cannot explain. Build the rules with eligibility checks, fallback routes, a defined decision order, and an audit trail naming the affected payments and the reason.\n\nA routing decision nobody can reconstruct is a control failure, not a routing outcome.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild eligibility checks, fallback routes, a defined decision order and an audit trail into the routing rules. When a limit changes, the engine re-routes and records which payments were affected and why.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Corridor eligibility check\n→ Fallback route\n→ Defined decision order\n→ Audit trail naming payments and reason\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment route?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves why it routed that way, and what happens when the limit changes?\"\n\nHave you ever watched an engine keep feeding a corridor whose limit had just been cut?", "takeaway": "A routing decision nobody can reconstruct is a control failure, not a routing outcome.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Test corridor eligibility", "note": "Test whether the corridor can take the payment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has the limit or capacity changed?", "note": "Re-route or queue, and record the reason."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Apply the routing rules", "note": "Follow the defined decision order."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Take the fallback route", "note": "Take the next eligible corridor when the first is blocked."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Record the decision and audit trail", "note": "Show which payments were affected and why."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F02", "seq": 516, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The customer cut-off is 3:25 PM. The deadline is 4:00 PM.", "explain": "A customer submits a EUR 70,000 payment at 3:30 PM and expects it out today.\nThe downstream deadline is 4:00 PM.\nThe payment misses the day, and the customer is told the cut-off was 3:25 PM, a time that appears nowhere in the product.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Downstream deadline 4:00 PM\n→ Screening takes 20 minutes\n→ Buffer takes 15 minutes\n→ Customer cut-off lands at 3:25 PM\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A cut-off is a time on the clock.\"\n\nA cut-off time is a calculated risk decision, not a time on the clock, and it starts from the downstream deadline, subtracting screening time, buffer, processing capacity, file size, treasury funding and expected rejections. In the worked example a 4:00 PM downstream deadline minus a 15-minute buffer minus 20 minutes of screening gives a 3:25 PM customer cut-off, with guaranteed service until 3:00 PM, best effort until 3:25 PM and next-day service after that.\n\nA cut-off is a calculated risk decision, not a time on the clock.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCalculate the cut-off backwards from the downstream deadline, subtracting screening, buffer, processing capacity, file size, funding and expected rejections. Then publish the tiers: guaranteed until 3:00 PM, best effort until 3:25 PM, next-day service after that.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Downstream deadline\n→ Screening time\n→ Buffer\n→ Processing capacity and file size\n→ Treasury funding\n→ Expected rejections\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What time is the cut-off?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the cut-off leaves enough time for screening, buffer and funding?\"\n\nHave you ever missed a cut-off that was calculated, not published?", "takeaway": "A payment BA should ask what time the payment must be out of the file, whether the deadline is hard, and whether tiers are needed in the requirements.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Start from the downstream deadline", "note": "A 4:00 PM correspondent or scheme deadline is the anchor."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Subtract screening, buffer and processing time", "note": "20 minutes screening and a 15-minute buffer give 3:25 PM."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is funding available for the full volume?", "note": "Funding and format variation decide the service tiers."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Publish tiered service levels", "note": "Guaranteed until 3:00 PM, best effort until 3:25 PM, next day after."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "RUN6-027", "seq": 527, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The cheapest rail that still arrives on time", "explain": "A debtor bank must send a EUR 75,000 payment, and the treasurer wants the cheapest route.\nThe cheapest route takes three days.\nThe beneficiary needs the money tomorrow, and the cheapest rail that arrives on time is not the cheapest rail.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment to send, EUR 75,000\n→ Cheapest route takes three days\n→ Beneficiary needs it tomorrow\n→ Routing rules pick the cheapest rail that arrives on time\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Cheapest is always best.\"\n\nA debtor bank looks for the cheapest way to reach the beneficiary on time. When the receiver sits in the same country the payment goes through the local clearing and settlement mechanism or the local real time gross settlement system, and when the receiver sits abroad it goes through SWIFT. Routing rules set up in the payment engine apply that choice automatically, including for bulk MT101 and pain.001 files.\n\nCheapest on time, not cheapest at any cost.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite the routing rules as cheapest on time. Same country goes through the local clearing mechanism or the local RTGS, abroad goes through SWIFT, and the payment engine applies the choice automatically, including for bulk MT101 and pain.001 files.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Same country, local rail\n→ Another country, SWIFT\n→ Value date required\n→ Routing rules in the engine\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What is the cheapest rail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the chosen rail is the cheapest one that still arrives on time?\"\n\nHave you ever picked the cheapest rail and missed the value date?", "takeaway": "Routing rules set up in the payment engine apply that choice automatically, including for bulk MT101 and pain.001 files.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Find the cheapest way on time", "note": "The debtor bank looks for the cheapest route that still arrives on time."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Same country or abroad?", "note": "The receiver's location decides the rail."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Same country: local rail", "note": "The payment goes through the local CSM or the local RTGS."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Another country: SWIFT", "note": "The payment goes through SWIFT."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing rules decide", "note": "Rules in the payment engine apply the choice automatically, even for bulk files."}]}}, "stage": 2, "stage_name": "Processing decisions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": ["MT101", "pain.001"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"explain": "A SEPA payment of EUR 20,000 failed.\nThe team raised it as a reject and asked the customer to resend it.\nThe customer had already been debited, and the money came back twice.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 instruction raised\n→ pacs.008 sent for EUR 20,000\n→ Payment settles\n→ pacs.004 return arrives after settlement\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Treat every failed SEPA payment the same way.\"\n\nSo the team followed the reject path for a payment that had already settled.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn SEPA, timing is the whole distinction. A reject happens before settlement and is reported with pacs.002. A return happens after settlement and uses pacs.004. A reject means the money never moved, so resend is safe. A return means the money moved and came back, so resending without checking credits the customer twice.\n\nThe stage decides the path.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore and after settlement are different worlds, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Reject before settlement, pacs.002\n→ Return after settlement, pacs.004\n→ Funds position confirmed before any resend\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When did it fail?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether the funds moved, before we tell the customer to resend?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a return handled as a reject and the customer paid twice?", "id": "ABH-03", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 3, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "The stage decides the path.", "title": "EUR 20,000 SEPA payment came back, the team treated it as a reject", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "EUR 20,000 SEPA payment leaves the bank", "title": "pacs.008 sent"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "The stage decides the handling path", "title": "Did it fail before or after settlement?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Reported with pacs.002, money never moved", "title": "Reject before settlement"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Reported with pacs.004, money moved and came back", "title": "Return after settlement"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Resending blind risks paying twice", "title": "Check funds before resend"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"explain": "A customer tried to pay GBP 4,500.\nIt failed three times.\nEach time the screen said the same thing: declined.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ First attempt fails on a timeout, no response arrives in time\n→ Second attempt fails on a limit, the amount exceeds the daily limit\n→ Third attempt fails on a downstream error after acceptance\n→ Customer is told declined on all three attempts\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show one generic failure message for every failed payment.\"\n\nSo the customer kept retrying the same payment, and support kept blaming the issuer.\n\nWhy?\n\nA timeout, a limit failure and a downstream failure need completely different actions. A timeout may succeed on retry, a limit needs a higher limit or a split payment, and a downstream failure needs operations to trace the payment after acceptance. One label hides the cause and sends everyone down the wrong path.\n\nA failure label is not a diagnosis.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe cause decides who acts and what the customer is told, so keep the causes separate:\n\n→ Issuer decline and validation failure\n→ Limit failure and timeout failure\n→ Connectivity, risk and downstream failure\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we show declined?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which cause failed, and who must act on it?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a declined payment that turned out to be a timeout?", "id": "INS-086", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 43, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A failure label is not a diagnosis.", "title": "GBP 4,500 failed three times, and the reason was wrong every time", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "One label, declined, hid three different causes", "parts": [{"label": "Timeout failure", "note": "No response arrived in time, retry may succeed"}, {"label": "Limit failure", "note": "An amount or frequency limit was exceeded"}, {"label": "Issuer decline", "note": "The issuer refused authorisation"}, {"label": "Validation failure", "note": "The instruction was incomplete or wrong"}, {"label": "Risk failure", "note": "A fraud or sanctions screen blocked the payment"}, {"label": "Downstream failure", "note": "The failure happened after the payment was accepted"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C009", "seq": 57, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 18,000 recalled by camt.056, and the bank waited for a pacs.004", "explain": "A customer asks for EUR 18,000 back after a payment was credited in error.\nThe bank sends a camt.056 recall request.\nThen it waits for a pacs.004, and nothing arrives for a week.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment already settled and credited to the beneficiary\n→ camt.056 sent, asking the beneficiary bank to return it\n→ The team treats the camt.056 as if it moves the money\n→ No pacs.004 ever comes, because the beneficiary bank has not agreed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A recall request is a return.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.004 returns a payment that has already settled, sending the funds back along the chain to the originator. A camt.056 requests a recall, asking the beneficiary bank to return a payment already credited. The two are often confused, yet they do opposite jobs: the pacs.004 moves the money back, while the camt.056 asks for the money back.\n\nAsking ≠ Returning.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrack the recall and the return as two separate things, and never promise the customer on the request alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ camt.056 recall requested\n→ Beneficiary bank decision on the recall\n→ pacs.004 return actually received\n→ Funds credited back to the customer\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the recall?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds actually came back, not just that we asked for them?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer promised a refund on a recall request that was never agreed?", "takeaway": "The two are often confused, yet they do opposite jobs: the pacs.004 moves the money back, while the camt.056 asks for the money back.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.004 returns, camt.056 recalls", "parts": [{"label": "Payment credited in error", "note": "EUR 18,000 already settled to the beneficiary"}, {"label": "camt.056 sent", "note": "The bank asks the beneficiary bank to return it"}, {"label": "Team waits for money", "note": "The request is treated as if it moves the funds"}, {"label": "No pacs.004 arrives", "note": "The beneficiary bank has not agreed to return it"}, {"label": "Ask is not return", "note": "camt.056 asks, pacs.004 moves the money back"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004", "camt.056"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C010", "seq": 58, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 12,000 paid twice from one pacs.008 file", "explain": "A bulk pacs.008 file pays a supplier EUR 12,000.\nThe same supplier is paid EUR 12,000 again the next day.\nThe file contained the same transaction twice.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 carries one instruction more than once\n→ pacs.008 file built with the same payment inside it twice\n→ No duplicate check runs before processing\n→ Both payments settle, and the supplier is paid double\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The file is clean because the customer sent it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nEvery payment inside a pacs.008 file carries its own unique identification, and that identifier is the control that catches a file containing the same transaction twice before it is processed. The check matters because a bulk file such as a pain.001 can carry one instruction more than once, and paying it twice is a real loss.\n\nDuplicate detection is a pre-processing discipline, not a post-payment investigation.\n\nA file received ≠ A file checked.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun the duplicate check on unique identification before any payment in the file is processed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Unique identification on every payment\n→ Duplicate check before processing\n→ Payments released for settlement\n→ Recovery if a duplicate slips through\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we process the file?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the duplicate check ran on every payment before settlement?\"\n\nHave you seen a supplier paid twice because a file was trusted without a check?", "takeaway": "Duplicate detection is therefore a pre-processing discipline, not a post-payment investigation.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 file built", "note": "A bulk file carries the EUR 12,000 payment"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Same payment inside twice", "note": "One instruction appears more than once in the file"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Duplicate check run?", "note": "Unique identification must be checked before processing"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No check, both settle", "note": "The supplier is paid EUR 12,000 twice"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recovery starts late", "note": "A post-payment investigation chases money already gone"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pain.001"], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-001", "seq": 67, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 22,000 SEPA payment rejected, and the beneficiary bank never knew it existed", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 22,000 by SEPA credit transfer.\nThe beneficiary calls to say nothing arrived.\nThe beneficiary bank says it never saw the payment at all.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Originator bank sends pacs.008 into the clearing and settlement mechanism\n→ The mechanism validates it against its own rules and it fails\n→ A pacs.002 rejection goes back to the originator bank\n→ The beneficiary bank is never told the payment existed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A rejection means the beneficiary bank turned it down.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a SEPA credit transfer, the pacs.008 passes through the clearing and settlement mechanism on its way to the beneficiary bank, and the mechanism validates it against its own rules. A failure at that stage comes back as a pacs.002, so the payment never reaches the beneficiary bank and that bank stays unaware of it. A fault spotted later, at the beneficiary bank itself, travels back as a pacs.004 return instead, because receipt has already happened by then.\n\nA pacs.002 rejection ≠ A beneficiary bank decision.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the rejection to find where it failed: mechanism, or beneficiary bank.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.002 rejection at the mechanism, beneficiary bank unaware\n→ pacs.004 return at the beneficiary bank, receipt already happened\n→ Reason for the failure at the right stage\n→ Customer told which stage stopped it\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the payment rejected?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we know which stage rejected it, and told the customer the truth about it?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer chase a beneficiary bank that never saw their payment?", "takeaway": "A fault spotted later, at the beneficiary bank itself, travels back as a pacs.004 return instead, because receipt has already happened by then.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank sends pacs.008", "note": "EUR 22,000 enters the clearing and settlement mechanism"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Mechanism validation passed?", "note": "A failed validation is rejected here by the mechanism itself"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.002 sent back to the originator", "note": "Rejection at this stage means the beneficiary bank never sees it"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank unaware", "note": "The bank stays unaware the payment ever existed"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Later fault is a pacs.004", "note": "A fault at the beneficiary bank returns as pacs.004, after receipt"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.008", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-129", "seq": 134, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 4,000 collection due Friday, creditor bank closed, collection never sent", "explain": "A creditor plans a EUR 4,000 collection for Friday.\nThe debtor bank is open. The scheme is open.\nThe creditor bank is closed for a holiday.\nThe collection is never sent.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Due date arrives, Friday\n→ Debtor PSP open, ready to debit\n→ Creditor PSP closed on the due date\n→ Collection cannot be submitted into the scheme that day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the debtor bank is open, the collection can run.\"\n\nIf the creditor PSP is closed on the due date, the collection cannot be submitted into the scheme on that date, even if the debtor PSP is open.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe creditor-side processing constraint moves the collection to the next business day. The creditor must plan for its own closure days, not just the debtor's, because its own calendar is a hard gate on submission.\n\nYour own holiday stops your own collection.\n\nWhy does a collection fail when the debtor bank is open and ready?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlan collections against your own calendar first, and move the due date before the holiday, not after the failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor PSP calendar, the hard gate on submission\n→ Debtor PSP calendar, the gate on the debit\n→ Scheme calendar, the gate on clearing and settlement\n→ Next business day, where the collection lands\n\nCheck all three calendars before setting a due date, and show the creditor closure on the collection plan.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the debtor bank open?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every calendar in the chain is open on the due date?\"\n\nHave you missed a collection because your own bank was closed?", "takeaway": "The creditor must plan for its own closure days, not just the debtor's, because its own calendar is a hard gate on submission.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Creditor PSP closed on due date", "parts": [{"label": "Creditor PSP closed", "note": "Creditor PSP is closed on the due date."}, {"label": "Cannot submit", "note": "Collection cannot be submitted into the scheme on that date."}, {"label": "Debtor PSP open", "note": "Debtor PSP may be open, but that does not help."}, {"label": "Moves to next business day", "note": "Collection moves to the next business day."}, {"label": "Plan for own closure", "note": "Creditor must plan for its own closure days, not just the debtor's."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-130", "seq": 135, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 4,000 collection due Friday, debtor bank closed, debit lands Monday", "explain": "A creditor sends a EUR 4,000 collection for Friday.\nThe creditor bank is open. The scheme is open.\nThe debtor bank is closed for a holiday.\nThe debit lands on Monday, and the creditor had promised the money for Friday.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Due date arrives, Friday\n→ Creditor PSP open, collection submitted\n→ Debtor PSP closed on the due date\n→ Debtor account cannot be debited that day, debit moves to Monday\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Submitted on Friday means debited on Friday.\"\n\nIf the debtor PSP is closed on the due date, the debtor account cannot be debited on that date, so the debit moves to the next business day the debtor PSP is open.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe collection is not lost, but the timing shifts, and the creditor must know which date the debit will actually happen. This is why the debtor PSP calendar matters as much as the creditor's: it fixes the real debit date.\n\nSubmitted is not debited.\n\nWhy does a Friday collection debit on Monday?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPromise the real debit date, taken from the debtor PSP calendar, not the due date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Submission date, when the collection enters the scheme\n→ Debtor PSP calendar, what fixes the real debit date\n→ Actual debit date, the next business day the debtor PSP is open\n→ Customer promise, dated from the actual debit date\n\nShow the actual debit date beside the due date on every collection.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the collection submitted on time?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the debit date the creditor promised?\"\n\nHave you promised Friday money that debited on Monday?", "takeaway": "The debtor PSP calendar matters as much as the creditor's: it fixes the real debit date.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Debtor PSP closed on due date", "parts": [{"label": "Debtor PSP closed", "note": "Debtor PSP is closed on the due date."}, {"label": "Cannot debit", "note": "Debtor account cannot be debited on that date."}, {"label": "Moves to next business day", "note": "Debit moves to the next business day the debtor PSP is open."}, {"label": "Collection is not lost", "note": "Collection is not lost, but the timing shifts."}, {"label": "Creditor must know actual date", "note": "Creditor must know which date the debit will actually happen."}, {"label": "Debtor calendar matters", "note": "Debtor PSP calendar matters as much as the creditor's."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-131", "seq": 136, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 12,000 in collections due, both banks open, clearing closed", "explain": "EUR 12,000 in collections is due today.\nBoth banks are open and working.\nNothing settles.\nThe clearing mechanism between them is closed.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Due date arrives, collections ready\n→ Creditor PSP open, debtor PSP open\n→ Clearing and settlement mechanism closed on the due date\n→ Collections cannot be submitted and settled that day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If both banks are open, the payment can settle.\"\n\nThe clearing and settlement mechanism is the infrastructure that moves collections between the creditor PSP and the debtor PSP.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf it is closed on the due date, the collections cannot be submitted and settled that day, even when both banks are open and working. Both banks being open is not enough: the infrastructure between them must also be open, so the mechanism's calendar belongs in every collection plan.\n\nTwo open banks, one closed road between them.\n\nWhy does nothing settle when both banks are open?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the infrastructure calendar as well as the bank calendars, and plan the due date around all three.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor PSP calendar, open or closed\n→ Debtor PSP calendar, open or closed\n→ Clearing mechanism calendar, the infrastructure between them\n→ Settlement date, only when all three are open\n\nShow all three calendars on the collection plan, with the mechanism calendar beside the bank calendars.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are the banks open?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the infrastructure between them is open too?\"\n\nHave you watched two open banks fail to settle?", "takeaway": "Both banks being open is not enough: the infrastructure between them must also be open.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Clearing mechanism closed", "parts": [{"label": "Clearing mechanism", "note": "Infrastructure that moves collections between creditor PSP and debtor PSP."}, {"label": "Closed on due date", "note": "Clearing and settlement mechanism is closed on the due date."}, {"label": "Cannot submit and settle", "note": "Collections cannot be submitted and settled that day."}, {"label": "Both banks open", "note": "Both banks can be open and working."}, {"label": "Infrastructure must be open", "note": "Infrastructure between them must also be open."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-132", "seq": 137, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 950 debited with permission, customer wants it back in week seven", "explain": "A debtor authorised a direct debit of EUR 950.\nSeven weeks after the debit, the debtor asks for the money back.\nThe creditor calls it an exception.\nIt is not an exception. It is a scheme right.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Debtor authorises the collection of EUR 950\n→ Debit happens, collection completes\n→ Week seven, debtor asks for a refund\n→ Creditor treats the request as a dispute\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An authorised debit cannot be refunded.\"\n\nWhen a debtor authorised a SEPA Direct Debit collection, they can ask for a refund within eight weeks of the debit date.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe eight-week rule is the stated scheme position, but the exact application can vary by jurisdiction, so it must be checked against the current rulebook. A refund inside this window is a normal scheme right, not an exception, and treating it as a dispute delays a right the debtor already holds.\n\nAuthorised does not mean unrefundable.\n\nWhy does a lawful refund request get treated as a dispute?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHandle an authorised refund as a normal scheme right, inside its eight-week window.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorised collection, the debit the debtor agreed to\n→ Eight-week window, the refund right from the debit date\n→ Rulebook check, exact application per jurisdiction\n→ Refund, a normal scheme right, not an exception\n\nShow the eight-week deadline on every authorised debit, and route refund requests to the refund process, not the dispute process.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the debit authorised?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the refund was handled inside the scheme window?\"\n\nHave you seen a scheme right treated as a dispute?", "takeaway": "A refund inside this window is a normal scheme right, not an exception.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Authorised refund window", "parts": [{"label": "Authorised collection", "note": "Debtor authorised the SEPA Direct Debit collection."}, {"label": "Eight weeks", "note": "Refund can be asked within eight weeks of the debit date."}, {"label": "Scheme position", "note": "Eight-week rule is the stated scheme position."}, {"label": "Varies by jurisdiction", "note": "Exact application can vary by jurisdiction."}, {"label": "Check the rulebook", "note": "Must be checked against the current rulebook."}, {"label": "Normal scheme right", "note": "Refund inside this window is a normal scheme right, not an exception."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-133", "seq": 138, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 1,100 taken without authorisation, claim arrives in month twelve", "explain": "A debtor finds a direct debit of EUR 1,100 on the statement.\nThe debtor never authorised it.\nTwelve months have passed.\nThe creditor says it is too late. It is not.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Direct debit of EUR 1,100 taken, no authorisation behind it\n→ Debtor discovers it months later\n→ Claim filed in month twelve\n→ Creditor handles it as an authorised refund, and refuses it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"All refunds work the same way, with the same window.\"\n\nWhen a debtor says a direct debit was never authorised, they can claim a refund for up to 13 months after the debit.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe 13-month period is the stated scheme position, and the precise legal and scheme conditions must be checked against the current rulebook. An unauthorised claim is a different process from an authorised refund, with a longer window and different evidence, so the two must never be handled as one case type.\n\nUnauthorised is a different case, not a late authorised one.\n\nWhy does a twelve-month claim get refused as too late?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute unauthorised claims to the unauthorised process, with its 13-month window and its own evidence.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Authorised refund, eight weeks, debtor agreed\n→ Unauthorised claim, up to 13 months, debtor never agreed\n→ Evidence, different for each case type\n→ Rulebook check, precise conditions per case\n\nShow the case type on every refund request, and never judge an unauthorised claim by the authorised window.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How long ago was the debit?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this claim was handled under the right case type?\"\n\nHave you seen an unauthorised claim refused under the wrong window?", "takeaway": "An unauthorised claim is a different process from an authorised refund, with a longer window and different evidence.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Unauthorised claim window", "parts": [{"label": "Never authorised", "note": "Debtor says a direct debit was never authorised."}, {"label": "13 months", "note": "Refund can be claimed for up to 13 months after the debit."}, {"label": "Scheme position", "note": "13-month period is the stated scheme position."}, {"label": "Check the rulebook", "note": "Precise legal and scheme conditions must be checked against the rulebook."}, {"label": "Different process", "note": "Unauthorised claim is a different process from an authorised refund."}, {"label": "Longer window, more evidence", "note": "Longer window and different evidence apply."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-134", "seq": 139, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2,000 comes back, the ledger books it as the wrong event", "explain": "EUR 2,000 comes back after a direct debit.\nThe ledger books it as a refund.\nIt was a return, started by the debtor bank because the account could not be debited.\nThe timeline, the message and the accounting are all wrong now.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection of EUR 2,000 presented\n→ Debtor PSP cannot debit the account, sends it back\n→ Team books it as a refund, debtor asked for money back\n→ Real event was a return, different initiator, different rules\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Money back is money back. One event covers them all.\"\n\nA return, a refund and a reversal are three different money-back events with different initiators, reasons, messages and accounting treatment.\n\nWhy?\n\nA return is started by the debtor PSP, usually because the account cannot be debited. A refund is started by the debtor asking for money back. A reversal is started by the creditor PSP to undo a collection. Treating them as the same thing causes wrong messages, wrong timelines and wrong ledger entries, so the three must be modelled as separate events.\n\nThree events, three initiators, three treatments.\n\nWhy does one money-back booking corrupt the message, the timeline and the ledger?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nModel return, refund and reversal as separate events, and let the initiator decide which one it is.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return, started by the debtor PSP, account cannot be debited\n→ Refund, started by the debtor asking for money back\n→ Reversal, started by the creditor PSP to undo a collection\n→ Messages, timelines and ledger entries, different for each\n\nRequire the initiator on every money-back event before it is booked.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the money come back?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which of the three events brought it back?\"\n\nHave you booked a return as a refund?", "takeaway": "Treating them as the same thing causes wrong messages, wrong timelines and wrong ledger entries.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Return, refund and reversal", "parts": [{"label": "Return", "note": "Started by the debtor PSP, usually because the account cannot be debited."}, {"label": "Refund", "note": "Started by the debtor asking for money back."}, {"label": "Reversal", "note": "Started by the creditor PSP to undo a collection."}, {"label": "Different initiators", "note": "Three events have different initiators."}, {"label": "Different reasons", "note": "Three events have different reasons."}, {"label": "Different messages and accounting", "note": "Different messages and accounting treatment apply."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-109", "seq": 140, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 1,500 return and EUR 1,500 refund land the same day, team nets them", "explain": "Two EUR 1,500 amounts land on the same day.\nOne is a return, one is a refund.\nThe team nets them and closes both cases.\nBoth cases are still open, and both are now wrong.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection presented for EUR 1,500\n→ Debtor bank sends it back, insufficient funds, pacs.004\n→ Debtor asks for an authorised collection to be repaid, refund\n→ Both arrive the same day and look similar\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Same day, same amount, same event.\"\n\nA SEPA Direct Debit return and a refund can arrive on the same day and look similar, but they are different business events.\n\nWhy?\n\nA return happens when the debtor bank sends the collection back, for example because the account has insufficient funds, and it travels as a pacs.004, while a refund happens when the debtor asks for an authorised collection to be repaid. Each has its own trigger, timeline and accounting treatment, so the system must handle them as separate events even when they land together.\n\nSame day is not the same event.\n\nWhy does netting two same-day amounts close two cases that are both still open?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHandle return and refund as separate events, even when they land together.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return, debtor bank sends it back, pacs.004, insufficient funds\n→ Refund, debtor asks for an authorised collection to be repaid\n→ Trigger, timeline and accounting, different for each\n\nNever net a return against a refund, and show each on its own case line.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the amounts match?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each event was handled on its own trigger and timeline?\"\n\nHave you seen a return and a refund netted into one case?", "takeaway": "Each has its own trigger, timeline and accounting treatment, so handle them as separate events even when they land together.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Collection presented", "note": "Collection is presented for debit, EUR 1,500."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Return or refund?", "note": "Return and refund can arrive the same day and look similar but differ."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return travels as pacs.004", "note": "Debtor bank sends the collection back, for example insufficient funds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Refund on debtor request", "note": "Debtor asks for an authorised collection to be repaid."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Handle them separately", "note": "Each has its own trigger, timeline, and accounting treatment."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "insufficient funds", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-162", "seq": 141, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 3,200 collection returned, reason code unread for four days", "explain": "A collection of EUR 3,200 is returned.\nThe pacs.004 carries a reason code.\nNobody on the returns team can read it consistently.\nThe creditor finds out four days late, with no clear explanation.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection returned, pacs.004 arrives\n→ Reason code inside pacs.004 explains why\n→ Returns team interprets the code differently each time\n→ Creditor learns days late, with no clear explanation\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The return message is enough. The code will explain itself.\"\n\nWhen a direct debit collection is returned, the pacs.004 carries a reason code that explains why, and the scheme sets a timeline for when the return must happen.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe creditor needs to learn about the return quickly and understand whether the collection can be presented again. If the returns team cannot interpret the reason codes consistently, creditors find out days late with no clear explanation, so code interpretation is an operational capability, not a back-office nicety.\n\nAn unread code is an unexplained return.\n\nWhy does the creditor learn about a return four days late?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake reason-code interpretation a trained, consistent capability, and tell the creditor fast.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code, in pacs.004, explaining why\n→ Scheme timeline, when the return must happen\n→ Creditor notice, fast, with the explanation\n→ Re-present decision, can the collection be presented again\n\nShow the code, its plain-language meaning, and the re-present answer on every return the same day it arrives.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the pacs.004 arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the creditor was told quickly, in words they can act on?\"\n\nHave you seen a return sit unread while the creditor waited?", "takeaway": "If the returns team cannot interpret the reason codes consistently, creditors find out days late with no clear explanation.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Collection is returned", "note": "Direct debit collection is returned, EUR 3,200."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reason code in pacs.004", "note": "pacs.004 carries a reason code that explains why."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Scheme timeline applies", "note": "Scheme sets a timeline for when the return must happen."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor learns quickly", "note": "Creditor needs to learn about the return quickly."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Can it be presented again?", "note": "Creditor must understand whether the collection can be presented again."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Interpret codes consistently", "note": "Without consistent interpretation, creditors find out days late."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-112", "seq": 151, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "90 percent of a remittance file lands in repair", "explain": "A corporate customer sends a daily remittance file with thousands of supplier payments, worth EUR 2.4 million.\n\nAround 90 percent of the payments land in the manual repair queue.\n\nThe file is on time. The money is not.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ File arrives with thousands of supplier payments\n→ Account numbers fail the modulus check\n→ Remittance information arrives incomplete or inconsistent\n→ Payments drop into manual repair\n→ The repair queue becomes the bottleneck\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The repair team will handle the exceptions.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe data is bad at the source. The queue is a symptom of bad input, not of slow repair. Adding repair staff only feeds a bigger queue.\n\nThe queue is a symptom, not the problem.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFix the data at the source. Repair capacity is the last control, not the first.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Source data quality\n→ Validation at capture\n→ Repair capacity\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast can we repair?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the file is clean before it is accepted?\"\n\nHave you seen a repair queue grow because the source data was never fixed?", "takeaway": "The queue is a symptom, not the problem. Fix the data at the source.", "visual": {"type": "stacked", "stacked": {"segments": [{"label": "Enters manual repair", "note": "Around 90 percent enter the repair queue, key data missing or inconsistent", "value": 90}, {"label": "Passes without repair", "note": "Remaining share passes without entering the manual repair queue", "value": 10}], "unit": "%"}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A03", "seq": 158, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "camt.055 sent after settlement: EUR 75,000 already gone", "explain": "A customer asks to cancel a EUR 75,000 payment.\n\nThe team sends camt.055 to stop it.\n\nThe payment already settled and the beneficiary is already credited.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment settles and beneficiary is credited\n→ camt.055 sent to stop the payment\n→ camt.055 is rejected\n→ camt.029 arrives carrying RJCR\n→ Recovery switches to the return route\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Send camt.055 to stop it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCancellation and return are governed by lifecycle position, not by urgency. camt.055 can stop a payment only while it remains before settlement. Once settlement and credit have completed, the only route back is a camt.056 requesting the funds, or a pacs.004 carrying them back.\n\ncamt.055 stops a payment. It cannot undo one.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEstablish the lifecycle stage before choosing the message. The disciplined move is to locate the payment first, then pick cancellation or return.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Lifecycle stage of the payment\n→ Cancellation message\n→ Return route\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we cancel it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where the payment sits before we choose the message?\"\n\nHave you sent a cancellation for a payment that had already settled?", "takeaway": "camt.055 stops a payment. It cannot undo one. Check the lifecycle first.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Establish the lifecycle stage", "note": "Locate the payment before choosing any cancellation or return message"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Initiation through settlement", "note": "camt.055 can act from initiation through validation, processing and settlement"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Settled and credited already?", "note": "After settlement and credit, camt.055 is rejected"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.029 arrives with RJCR", "note": "The rejection is reported in camt.029 with RJCR status"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recover by return route", "note": "Request return with camt.056, or receive the funds back in pacs.004"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.055", "camt.029", "camt.056", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A05", "seq": 159, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "pacs.009 cover rejected, but the EUR 40,000 payment had not failed", "explain": "A pacs.009 cover is rejected with FF01, invalid account number.\n\nThe team assumes the payment failed and re-credits the customer EUR 40,000.\n\nThe funds were still held by the bank. The customer is now paid twice.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ pacs.009 COV rejected, FF01\n→ Team reads the rejection as a failed payment\n→ Customer is re-credited EUR 40,000\n→ Funds are still held by the bank\n→ Customer is paid twice, or funds go to the wrong account\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A rejected cover means the payment failed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA rejected cover does not by itself prove the underlying payment failed. The credit advice may simply carry a wrong account mapping while the funds remain held. The reason code governs the next move.\n\nA rejection code is a clue, not a verdict.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nInvestigate the reason code before re-crediting. The rejection alone must never trigger the refund.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code\n→ Funds position\n→ Re-credit decision\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was something rejected?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the payment failed before we re-credit?\"\n\nHave you seen a re-credit go out before anyone checked what the reason code actually meant?", "takeaway": "A rejection code is a clue, not a verdict. The rejection alone must never trigger the refund.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.009 COV rejected, FF01", "note": "Cover rejected for invalid account number, yet funds do not return"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Did the payment fail itself?", "note": "A rejected cover does not necessarily mean the payment failed"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigate the reason code", "note": "Test whether it is an account mapping issue with funds still held"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Hold the re-credit", "note": "Fast re-crediting can pay twice or send funds to the wrong account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Let the code decide", "note": "The reason code, not the rejection alone, decides the next step"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.009"], "failure_category": "invalid beneficiary data", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A12", "seq": 163, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "camt.056 sent, EUR 60,000 stuck in silence", "explain": "A bank asks for EUR 60,000 back with a camt.056.\n\nNobody answers.\n\nThe payment sits in a waiting state, neither completed nor rejected, and the funds just hang.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ camt.056 sent to request the return\n→ No answer arrives\n→ Payment sits waiting, neither completed nor rejected\n→ No status check is planned\n→ No escalation route exists\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary will answer.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn unanswered camt.056 leaves the payment stuck while the return of funds hangs. The exit is a camt.029 status check, followed by waiting for a response or escalating when the beneficiary does not reply within the agreed time.\n\nEvery waiting state needs a way out.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign every waiting state in advance with an entry, an exit, a time limit, a status check and an escalation route.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Waiting state and its time limit\n→ Status check with camt.029\n→ Escalation route\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How long do we wait?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we know what happens if nobody ever replies?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment sit in a waiting state with no agreed way out?", "takeaway": "Every waiting state needs a way out. Design the exit before the wait begins.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "camt.056 sent, no answer", "note": "Bank asks for a return of funds and receives no answer at all"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment sits waiting", "note": "Not completed and not rejected, just stuck in a waiting state"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send camt.029 status check", "note": "Check the status and wait for a response"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Reply within agreed time?", "note": "Escalate if the beneficiary does not reply within the agreed time"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Agree the way out in advance", "note": "Each waiting state needs an entry, an exit, a time limit and an escalation route"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "camt.029"], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-005", "seq": 183, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The Rs. 12,000 chargeback that changed hands three times", "explain": "A cardholder disputes a Rs. 12,000 transaction.\n\nThe issuer raises it. The merchant fights it with representment evidence.\n\nThe loss moves between issuer, acquirer and merchant, and the reason code decides who holds it at each stage.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Cardholder disputes the transaction\n→ Issuer raises the chargeback with the scheme\n→ Acquirer and merchant respond with representment evidence\n→ Scheme applies its timelines and reason codes\n→ Financial impact moves between issuer, acquirer and merchant\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The reason code is just a label.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe reason code decides what evidence is needed and who bears the loss at each stage. The wrong code means the wrong evidence, and the wrong party pays.\n\nThe reason code is the case, not a label on it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart every chargeback from the reason code, and build the evidence around what that code demands.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code\n→ Representment evidence\n→ Scheme timelines\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the transaction disputed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we hold the evidence the reason code demands?\"\n\nHave you seen a chargeback lost because the team argued the facts and ignored the reason code?", "takeaway": "The reason code is the case, not a label on it. It decides the evidence and who bears the loss.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cardholder disputes", "note": "The cardholder disputes a transaction; the issuer raises it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Acquirer and merchant respond", "note": "Representment evidence is provided."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Scheme applies rules", "note": "The scheme applies its timelines and reason codes."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the evidence fit the reason code?", "note": "The reason code decides what evidence is needed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Financial impact moves", "note": "Impact moves between issuer, acquirer and merchant."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C011", "seq": 233, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 80,000 came back, but the return was sent to the wrong bank", "explain": "A beneficiary bank returned EUR 80,000.\nThe originator bank waited for it.\nThe return never arrived, because it was sent straight to the originator instead of back through the chain.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent EUR 80,000 to the beneficiary\n→ Beneficiary bank returns it with pacs.004\n→ Return sent directly to the originator, skipping the intermediary\n→ Originator cannot match it, and the funds sit unreconciled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A return goes straight back to where the payment came from.\"\nIn a three-bank chain, the return must travel the chain in reverse.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe intermediary that received the pacs.004 must send a new pacs.004 back to the originator bank, carrying the reference to the original payment. Skip the intermediary and the link between the return and the original payment breaks.\n\nA return travels the chain in reverse, or it travels nowhere.\n\nWhat proves the return reached the bank that sent the payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat every return as a chain, not a single hop. Each bank forwards the pacs.004 to the bank that sent it the payment, with the original reference intact, so the originator can match it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return status\n→ Original references\n→ Funds received and reconciled\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the money come back?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the return reached the bank that sent the payment?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a return that was sent to the wrong bank in the chain?", "takeaway": "A return travels the chain in reverse, or it travels nowhere.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 sent EUR 80,000", "note": "The payment went to the beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank returns with pacs.004", "note": "The return starts at the beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Return via the intermediary or direct", "note": "The return must go back through the intermediary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediary sends a new pacs.004 onward", "note": "It forwards the return with the original reference"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank matches the return", "note": "The originator matches it to the payment it sent"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C012", "seq": 234, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 80,000 arrived back, but no one could say which payment it was", "explain": "EUR 80,000 arrived back at the originator bank.\nThe credit sat in the account for two days.\nNo one could say which payment it belonged to.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → pacs.004 arrives as a credit\n→ → Original instruction identification missing from the trail\n→ → Original end-to-end identification missing too\n→ → Reconciliation cannot place the credit\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A credit is a credit. Book it and move on.\"\nA credit with no story cannot be booked against any payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.004 carries the original instruction identification and the original end-to-end identification, and those two references tie it to the payment it returns. Without them the return is only a credit, and reconciliation cannot prove which payment it belongs to.\n\nWithout those references a return is only a credit with no story.\n\nWhat proves this credit belongs to that payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever book a return on amount alone. Match the original instruction identification and the original end-to-end identification first, and hold the credit unreconciled until both references check out.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Original instruction identification\n→ Original end-to-end identification\n→ Reconciliation match\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the money come back?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this credit belongs to that payment?\"\n\nHave you ever had a credit arrive that no one could place?", "takeaway": "Without those references a return is only a credit with no story.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "How a EUR 80,000 pacs.004 links back", "parts": [{"label": "Original instruction identification", "note": "The pacs.004 carries the original instruction identifier"}, {"label": "Original end-to-end identification", "note": "The pacs.004 carries the original end-to-end identifier"}, {"label": "Originator bank matches the return", "note": "The originator bank reads both references and matches the return"}, {"label": "Without references, no story", "note": "Without them a return is a credit reconciliation cannot place"}, {"label": "Hold the credit unreconciled", "note": "Book nothing until both references check out."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C013", "seq": 235, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 120,000 recalled, and the recall had to cross three banks", "explain": "A customer asked for EUR 120,000 back.\nThe payment had already crossed an intermediary bank.\nOne message could not bring it back, because the money sat at the end of a chain.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → Originator bank sends camt.056 to the intermediary\n→ → Intermediary forwards camt.056 to the beneficiary bank\n→ → Beneficiary bank checks whether the recall is feasible\n→ → Beneficiary bank returns funds with pacs.004\n→ → Intermediary sends pacs.004 back to the originator\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Send one recall and the money comes back.\"\nA recall is a chain of messages, not a single message.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach bank in the chain must act in turn. The funds come back only when every bank in the chain acts, so a recall that stops at any link leaves the money where it is.\n\nA recall is a chain of messages, not a single message.\n\nWhat proves every bank in the chain acted on the recall?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the camt.056 through the same chain the payment took. Track the recall at each bank, wait for the camt.029 answer, and only then expect the pacs.004.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Recall request sent\n→ Response received\n→ Funds returned\n→ Books reconciled\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the recall been sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every bank in the chain acted on the recall?\"\n\nHave you ever waited on a recall that stopped at one bank in the chain?", "takeaway": "A recall is a chain of messages, not a single message.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank sends camt.056", "note": "The recall goes first to the intermediary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediary forwards camt.056", "note": "The intermediary passes the recall to the beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Beneficiary bank checks feasibility", "note": "The beneficiary bank checks whether the recall can be done"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank returns funds by pacs.004", "note": "If feasible, funds return by pacs.004 to the intermediary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediary sends pacs.004 to originator", "note": "The intermediary sends a pacs.004 back to the originator bank"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C014", "seq": 236, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 60,000 returned, and both ends claimed the return was theirs to start", "explain": "EUR 60,000 had to come back.\nThe originator bank said it would start the return.\nThe beneficiary bank said it would start the return.\nBoth were right, and the team argued about it for a day.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → Originator side recalls the payment\n→ → Beneficiary side rejects a payment it cannot apply\n→ → Both use the same message, pacs.004\n→ → Only the initiating bank and the reason differ\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A return can only start at one end.\"\nA return can be started from either end of the payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe originator side starts a pacs.004 when it recalls or reverses a payment, and the beneficiary side starts one when it rejects or returns a payment it cannot apply. The message is identical in both cases.\n\nThe message is identical in both cases. Only the initiating bank and the reason differ.\n\nWhat proves who started this return, and why?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the initiating bank and the reason code on the pacs.004, not the message name. The name tells you it is a return. The starter and the reason tell you what happened.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Initiating bank\n→ Reason code\n→ Original payment reference\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who sent the return?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves who started this return, and why?\"\n\nHave you ever argued over which end of a payment started a return?", "takeaway": "The message is identical in both cases. Only the initiating bank and the reason differ.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Who can start a EUR 60,000 pacs.004", "parts": [{"label": "Originator payment service provider", "note": "Starts a return when recalling or reversing a payment"}, {"label": "Beneficiary payment service provider", "note": "Starts a return when rejecting a payment it cannot apply"}, {"label": "Same message, different starter", "note": "One message. Only the initiating bank and reason differ"}, {"label": "Reason code tells the story", "note": "Read the reason to know what happened."}, {"label": "Original reference links it back", "note": "The reference ties the return to the payment."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C015", "seq": 237, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 45,000 recall sent, and the only answer was silence", "explain": "A EUR 45,000 payment went to the wrong beneficiary.\nThe originator bank sent a camt.056 recall.\nDays passed. No answer came, and no one could close the case.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → camt.056 recall sent\n→ → No camt.029 received\n→ → Funds still with the beneficiary bank\n→ → Originator cannot close the recall\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The recall was sent, so the case is moving.\"\nSending a recall proves nothing. Only the answer closes the question.\n\nWhy?\n\nA camt.029 answers a camt.056 by stating whether the recall has been accepted or refused. The originator bank cannot close the recall until that response arrives and confirms whether the funds are coming back.\n\nNo camt.029 means no closure. Silence is not an answer.\n\nWhat proves the recall was accepted or refused?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrack the recall as open until the camt.029 arrives. Treat an accepted recall as a decision, not as money moved, and wait for the pacs.004 before telling the customer the funds are back.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Recall request sent\n→ Response received\n→ Funds returned\n→ Customer told only on evidence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the recall been sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the recall was accepted or refused?\"\n\nHave you ever had a recall sit open because no answer ever came?", "takeaway": "No camt.029 means no closure. Silence is not an answer.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Recall request sent as camt.056", "note": "The recall starts with a camt.056 request"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Waiting for camt.029", "note": "No closure until the response arrives"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "camt.029 accepted or refused", "note": "The response states whether the recall was accepted or refused"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank closes the recall", "note": "Only the response lets the bank close the case"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds return by pacs.004 if accepted", "note": "An accepted recall still needs the return to arrive"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.029", "camt.056"], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C016", "seq": 238, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 30,000 returned with a reason code no bank recognised", "explain": "EUR 30,000 came back as a pacs.004.\nThe reason code on it did not exist in the scheme rulebook.\nThe next bank in the chain rejected the return, and the money sat in limbo.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → Payment returned with a pacs.004\n→ → Reason code invented by the returning bank\n→ → Next bank does not recognise the code\n→ → Return rejected, funds stuck\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any code that describes the problem will do.\"\nA reason code is a controlled vocabulary, not a free-text field.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe reason code comes from the bank that refused the payment, and the scheme rulebook lists the codes that bank may use. A bank that invents one risks the return being rejected by the next bank in the chain.\n\nA reason code is a controlled vocabulary, not a free-text field.\n\nWhat proves this reason code is one the next bank will accept?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse only codes from the scheme rulebook. Check the code against the rulebook before sending, and treat an unrecognised code as a second exception, not a description.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code\n→ Rulebook list\n→ Next bank acceptance\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the code describe the problem?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this reason code is one the next bank will accept?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a return rejected because of its reason code?", "takeaway": "A reason code is a controlled vocabulary, not a free-text field.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The reason for a EUR 30,000 return", "parts": [{"label": "Reason code comes from the refusing bank", "note": "The bank that refused the payment supplies the code"}, {"label": "Rulebook lists the permitted codes", "note": "Only the codes in the scheme rulebook may be used"}, {"label": "Invented codes risk rejection", "note": "An invented code risks rejection by the next bank"}, {"label": "Controlled vocabulary, not free text", "note": "Reason codes are a closed list, not free text"}, {"label": "Funds stuck in limbo", "note": "A rejected return leaves the money nowhere."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C017", "seq": 239, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 95,000 rejected, and the pacs.002 said nothing useful", "explain": "A EUR 95,000 pacs.008 was rejected.\nThe pacs.002 that reported it named the payment and nothing else.\nThe originator bank spent a day guessing what had gone wrong.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → pacs.008 sent for EUR 95,000\n→ → Payment rejected\n→ → pacs.002 arrives with no status and no reason code\n→ → Originator bank guesses at the cause\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Reporting the rejection is enough.\"\nA report without a reason is a message, not an answer.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a pacs.008 is rejected, the pacs.002 must identify the original payment and carry the status and the reason code, so the originator bank knows the payment did not settle and why.\n\nA pacs.002 without status and reason leaves the originator guessing.\n\nWhat proves why this payment was rejected?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDemand the full pacs.002: the original payment identified, the status stated, the reason code carried. Treat a pacs.002 without those fields as incomplete, and chase the missing fields before acting.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Original payment identified\n→ Status stated\n→ Reason code carried\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the payment rejected?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves why this payment was rejected?\"\n\nHave you ever received a rejection report that explained nothing?", "takeaway": "A pacs.002 without status and reason leaves the originator guessing.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A pacs.002 reporting a EUR 95,000 rejection", "parts": [{"label": "Identifies the original payment", "note": "The pacs.002 must name the rejected payment"}, {"label": "Carries the status", "note": "It must state the payment did not settle"}, {"label": "Carries the reason code", "note": "It must state why the payment was rejected"}, {"label": "Without it, the originator guesses", "note": "Without these fields the originator cannot tell what failed"}, {"label": "Chase the missing fields", "note": "An incomplete pacs.002 is a case, not an answer."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.008"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C021", "seq": 243, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 150,000 cover rejected, and the refund went to the wrong bank", "explain": "A EUR 150,000 cover payment was rejected.\nThe bank holding the funds sent them back.\nThey went to the wrong bank, and one exception became two.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Cover payment is rejected\n→ Bank holding the cover returns the funds\n→ Return sent to the wrong bank in the chain\n→ Second exception opened on top of the first\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any bank in the chain can return the funds.\"\nThe funds must travel back along the same correspondent path they came on.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a cover payment is rejected, the funds leg and the information leg have different senders, so the bank holding the rejected cover must return the funds to the bank that sent them. A return sent to the wrong bank creates a second exception on top of the first.\n\nA return sent to the wrong bank creates a second exception on top of the first.\n\nWhat proves the refund went back along the path the funds came on?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReturn the funds to the bank that sent them, along the same correspondent path. Check the sender of the cover leg before returning, and treat a wrong-bank return as a new exception to be fixed, not a closed case.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bank that sent the cover\n→ Correspondent path\n→ Return received and matched\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Were the funds returned?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the refund went back along the path the funds came on?\"\n\nHave you ever seen one exception become two because the refund went to the wrong bank?", "takeaway": "A return sent to the wrong bank creates a second exception on top of the first.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cover payment is rejected", "note": "The funds leg and the information leg have different senders"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank holding the cover returns the funds", "note": "The bank holding the rejected cover must return the funds"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Return to the bank that sent it", "note": "The money returns along the correspondent path it came on"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return reaches the bank that sent it", "note": "The funds go back to the bank that sent them"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Second exception if sent to the wrong bank", "note": "A wrong-bank return creates a second exception"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C027", "seq": 249, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 85,000 return that tried to jump the chain", "explain": "A USD 85,000 MT103 was returned.\nThe return was sent straight to the originator bank.\nIt never arrived, because the chain it had travelled on was skipped.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 is returned\n→ Return sent directly to the originator\n→ Intermediary banks skipped\n→ Return cannot be matched, funds sit unreconciled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A return is a new payment, so send it direct.\"\nA return is not a new payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen an MT103 is returned, the funds come back through the same chain of banks that carried the payment forward, each bank sending the return to the bank that sent it the payment. The charges on the return follow fields 71A and 71F, so the amount that comes back is the amount sent minus the charges already deducted.\n\nA return is not a new payment. It is the original payment travelling backwards.\n\nWhat proves the return travelled back through the same chain?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the return through the same banks that carried the payment forward, in reverse order. Apply fields 71A and 71F to the returned amount, and trace the return at every link.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return route\n→ Fields 71A and 71F\n→ Amount returned\n→ Chain trace\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the return sent?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the return travelled back through the same chain?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a return that tried to jump the chain?", "takeaway": "A return is not a new payment. It is the original payment travelling backwards.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 is returned", "note": "The return starts when the MT103 is returned"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each bank sends the return backwards", "note": "Each bank sends the return to the bank that sent it the payment"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Return via the same chain or direct", "note": "A direct return skips the banks that hold the funds"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Charges follow fields 71A and 71F", "note": "The amount returned is the amount sent minus charges deducted"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return is the payment travelling backwards", "note": "A return is the original payment in reverse, and the chain must trace it"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C028", "seq": 250, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 40,000 sent to an account that did not exist", "explain": "USD 40,000 was sent to a beneficiary account.\nThe account number was wrong.\nThe beneficiary bank could not credit anyone, and the customer asked where the money was.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Beneficiary bank finds the account invalid\n→ Wrong number, closed account or mismatched name\n→ Beneficiary bank returns the payment\n→ Return travels back through the intermediary to the originator\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The beneficiary bank can sort it out and keep the funds.\"\nThe beneficiary bank cannot keep funds it cannot apply.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen the beneficiary bank finds the customer account invalid, it returns the payment, and the return must travel back through the intermediary to the originator bank. It cannot return the funds directly to the originator while an intermediary sits in between.\n\nAn invalid account is the most common reason for a return, and the handling never changes.\n\nWhat proves the return is on its way back through the chain?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReturn the payment through the intermediary, with the original references, and tell the customer the reason plainly. The handling is the same whether the fault is a wrong number, a closed account or a name that does not match.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason for the return\n→ Return route through the intermediary\n→ Original references\n→ Customer told the reason\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the beneficiary bank keep the funds?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the return is on its way back through the chain?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer their payment went to an account that did not exist?", "takeaway": "An invalid account is the most common reason for a return, and the handling never changes.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank finds account invalid", "note": "The fault may be a wrong number, a closed account, or a mismatched name"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank returns the payment", "note": "The beneficiary bank cannot keep the funds"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Return via the intermediary or direct", "note": "The return must pass back through the intermediary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return travels back through intermediary", "note": "The return must pass back through the intermediary to the originator"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank receives the return", "note": "The return reaches the originator bank through the chain"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C029", "seq": 251, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 60,000 settled, and the recall had nowhere to go", "explain": "A customer asked for EUR 60,000 back after the payment had settled.\nThe originator bank sent a camt.056 recall.\nThe clearing house did not support the message, so the recall went nowhere.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment settles through the clearing house\n→ Originator bank sends camt.056\n→ Clearing house does not support the recall message\n→ Recall cannot reach the beneficiary bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every clearing system carries recalls.\"\nSome clearing houses do not support the camt.056 recall message.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe originator bank and the clearing system cannot send a recall to the beneficiary bank for a payment that has already settled when the clearing house does not support the message. The Estonian clearing house is a real example: it does not support the recall message, so a settled payment cannot be recalled through that route.\n\nKnow which clearing systems support a recall before promising a customer a payment can be taken back.\n\nWhat proves this clearing system can carry a recall?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck recall support before promising a customer a payment can be taken back. If the clearing house cannot carry a camt.056, say so early and find the route that can, instead of sending a recall into a dead end.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Clearing system\n→ Recall support\n→ Route that can carry the recall\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we recall this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this clearing system can carry a recall?\"\n\nHave you ever promised a recall the clearing system could not carry?", "takeaway": "Know which clearing systems support a recall before promising a customer a payment can be taken back.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A clearing house without camt.056", "parts": [{"label": "Recall cannot be sent through it", "note": "Originator bank and clearing system cannot recall a settled payment"}, {"label": "Estonian clearing house example", "note": "The Estonian clearing house does not support the recall message"}, {"label": "Analyst must check support first", "note": "Check recall support before promising a customer a take-back"}, {"label": "Find the route that can", "note": "If the clearing house cannot carry it, find the route that can."}, {"label": "Say so early", "note": "A dead-end recall wastes days the customer does not have."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056"], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C030", "seq": 252, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 110,000 recalled after the beneficiary had already spent it", "explain": "EUR 110,000 was paid to the wrong beneficiary.\nBy the time the customer noticed, the beneficiary had already spent it.\nThe recall went out anyway, and the customer expected the money back.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment executed and funds spent\n→ camt.056 sent to ask for the return\n→ Beneficiary bank asks its customer whether it agrees\n→ Funds no longer there, recall cannot complete by message alone\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A recall reverses a payment.\"\nOnce the money has been spent, a recall is a request, not a reversal.\n\nWhy?\n\nA camt.056 can still be sent to ask the beneficiary bank to return the money, but the beneficiary bank must ask its customer whether it agrees. If the funds are no longer there, the recall cannot be completed by message alone, and the customer must take action against the beneficiary.\n\nOnce the money has been spent, a recall is a request, not a reversal.\n\nWhat proves the funds are still there to be returned?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the camt.056, but tell the customer plainly that the recall depends on the beneficiary agreeing and the funds still being there. If the money is gone, the recovery is a claim against the beneficiary, not a message.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Recall request sent\n→ Beneficiary agreement\n→ Funds still held\n→ Recovery route if spent\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we reverse this payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds are still there to be returned?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer a recall could not bring the money back?", "takeaway": "Once the money has been spent, a recall is a request, not a reversal.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment executed and funds spent", "note": "The beneficiary has already spent the funds"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.056 still sent to ask for return", "note": "A camt.056 can still ask the beneficiary bank to return the money"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank asks its customer", "note": "The beneficiary bank must ask its customer whether it agrees"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Funds still there or not", "note": "If the funds are gone, the recall cannot complete by message alone"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer must act against beneficiary", "note": "The customer must take action against the beneficiary to recover the money"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C031", "seq": 253, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 70,000 recall for a third party, sent to the wrong bank", "explain": "A EUR 70,000 recall was made in favour of a third-party beneficiary.\nThe recall was sent straight to the third party.\nIt sat unread, because the third party was never meant to receive it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Recall made for a third-party beneficiary\n→ Recall sent directly to the third party\n→ Third party cannot act on it\n→ Recall stalls, funds stay put\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The recall goes to the party the money is for.\"\nThe third party never receives the recall directly.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe recall is sent to the first agent in the chain, the bank that must return the credit, and that bank then passes the recall onward. The third party receives the result through the bank that acts for it.\n\nThe third party never receives the recall directly. It receives the result through the bank that acts for it.\n\nWhat proves the recall reached the bank that must return the credit?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the recall to the first agent in the chain, not to the third party. Track the recall through the bank that acts for the third party, and report the result to the third party through that bank.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ First agent in the chain\n→ Bank that must return the credit\n→ Result reported to the third party\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Who should receive this recall?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the recall reached the bank that must return the credit?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a recall sent to the party that could not act on it?", "takeaway": "The third party never receives the recall directly. It receives the result through the bank that acts for it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Recall made for a third-party beneficiary", "note": "For example, a bank acting as debtor agent"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recall sent to the first agent", "note": "The recall goes to the first agent, the bank that must return the credit"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Send to the agent or to the third party", "note": "The third party cannot act on the recall directly"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "First agent passes the recall onward", "note": "That bank then passes the recall onward"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Third party receives the result", "note": "The third party receives the result through the bank acting for it"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C032", "seq": 254, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 55,000 recall rejected, with a reason code that did not belong there", "explain": "A EUR 55,000 recall was refused.\nThe refusal came back as a pacs.004 carrying the reason code GVC.\nGVC is not a code for a return, so the handling was wrong, and the case had to be reopened.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ camt.056 recall request sent\n→ Recall refused\n→ Refusal returned as pacs.004 with GVC\n→ Code does not belong on a return, second exception opened\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any reason code explains a refusal.\"\nA reason code must match the message and the situation.\n\nWhy?\n\nA camt.056 recall request can itself be rejected, and the reason code on the refusal must fit the message carrying it. A pacs.004 carrying GVC is a code used in the wrong place, and it creates a second exception on top of the first.\n\nA reason code must match the message and the situation.\n\nWhat proves this reason code belongs on this message?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck every reason code against the message it travels on and the situation it describes. Treat a mismatched code as a second exception, and correct it before the case can close.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code\n→ Message it travels on\n→ Situation it describes\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the code explain the refusal?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this reason code belongs on this message?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a refusal rejected again because of its own reason code?", "takeaway": "A reason code must match the message and the situation.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A rejected EUR 55,000 camt.056 recall request", "parts": [{"label": "Recall request can itself be rejected", "note": "A camt.056 recall request can itself be rejected"}, {"label": "GVC code on a pacs.004 return", "note": "One real case: a recall returned with a pacs.004 carrying GVC"}, {"label": "GVC should not be used for a return", "note": "GVC is not a code for a return, so the handling was wrong"}, {"label": "Code must match message and situation", "note": "A code in the wrong place creates a second exception"}, {"label": "Reopen and correct the code", "note": "Fix the code before the case can close."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["camt.056", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C034", "seq": 256, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 180,000 returned without a pacs.004, and the chain lost the link", "explain": "EUR 180,000 had to come back.\nThe returning bank sent the funds by another route, not a pacs.004.\nThe money arrived, but no bank in the chain could prove which payment it belonged to.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment must be returned\n→ Return sent by another route, not pacs.004\n→ Funds arrive with no link to the original payment\n→ Chain cannot match the return\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Any route that moves the money is good enough.\"\nUsing another route breaks the link.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe pacs.004 is the ISO 20022 message designed for returns in the SEPA interbank space, and it carries the link to the original payment that lets every bank in the chain match it. Using another route breaks that link.\n\nUsing another route breaks the link, which is why the pacs.004 is the expected message for a return.\n\nWhat proves this return belongs to that original payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReturn with a pacs.004, every time. Carry the original references on it, and treat a return that arrives by another route as unmatched until the link is proven some other way.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return message\n→ Original references\n→ Chain match\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the money come back?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this return belongs to that original payment?\"\n\nHave you ever received a return that arrived with no link to the original payment?", "takeaway": "Using another route breaks the link, which is why the pacs.004 is the expected message for a return.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Is a pacs.004 mandatory for a EUR 180,000 return", "parts": [{"label": "Designed for returns in SEPA", "note": "The ISO 20022 message designed for returns in the SEPA interbank space"}, {"label": "Carries the link to the original payment", "note": "Its link lets every bank in the chain match the return"}, {"label": "Another route breaks the link", "note": "Another route breaks the link to the original payment"}, {"label": "Expected message for a return", "note": "The pacs.004 is the expected message for a return"}, {"label": "Treat other routes as unmatched", "note": "Hold the funds until the link is proven."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C037", "seq": 259, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 95,000 serial recall that tried to skip a bank", "explain": "A USD 95,000 serial MT103 had to be recalled.\nThe originator bank sent the recall straight to the beneficiary bank.\nIt failed, because the bank that was skipped still held the funds.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Originator bank sends the recall\n→ Recall skips the intermediary banks\n→ Skipped bank still holds the funds\n→ Recall fails, money stays put\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The recall only needs the two ends.\"\nA serial MT103 can be recalled, but the recall must travel the same chain.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe originator bank sends the recall to the next bank in the chain, and each bank passes it on until it reaches the beneficiary bank, which decides whether the funds can be returned. A recall that skips a bank cannot work, because the bank that was skipped still holds the funds.\n\nA recall that skips a bank cannot work, because the bank that was skipped still holds the funds.\n\nWhat proves the recall travelled the same chain as the payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the recall bank by bank, along the chain the payment took. Track it at every link, and let the beneficiary bank decide on the funds only after the recall has reached it through the chain.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Recall route\n→ Each bank in the chain\n→ Beneficiary bank decision\n→ Funds returned\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the recall sent to the beneficiary bank?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the recall travelled the same chain as the payment?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a recall fail because it skipped a bank?", "takeaway": "A recall that skips a bank cannot work, because the bank that was skipped still holds the funds.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank sends the recall", "note": "The recall goes to the next bank in the chain"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each bank passes the recall onward", "note": "Each bank passes it on until it reaches the beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Recall via the chain or direct", "note": "A skipped bank still holds the funds, so a direct recall fails"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank decides on the funds", "note": "The beneficiary bank decides whether the funds can be returned"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds return through the chain", "note": "The return travels back the way the payment came"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C060", "seq": 268, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 200,000 cover arrived EUR 150 short", "explain": "An MT103 announced EUR 200,000.\nThe cover arrived for EUR 199,850.\nAn intermediary had taken its EUR 150 charge from the cover, and the beneficiary bank refused to credit the short amount.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 announces EUR 200,000\n→ Intermediary takes EUR 150 from the MT202 COV\n→ Cover arrives EUR 150 short\n→ Beneficiary bank holds the payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An intermediary can take its charge from the cover.\"\nIt is forbidden to take charges from an MT202 COV.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe cover amount must never be lower than the amount announced in the MT103. If an intermediary needs to be paid for its part in the chain, it asks the sending bank to pay through an MT191 or debits the account directly under the agreement between the banks.\n\nA cover that arrives short of the announced amount is an exception, not a normal deduction.\n\nWhat proves the cover matches the announced amount in full?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever deduct from a cover. Claim charges separately through an MT191 or by direct debit under the agreement, and treat any short cover as an exception to be fixed before crediting.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount announced\n→ Cover amount\n→ Charges claimed separately\n→ Exception on a short cover\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the cover a little short?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the cover matches the announced amount in full?\"\n\nHave you ever had a cover arrive short because a charge was taken from it?", "takeaway": "A cover that arrives short of the announced amount is an exception, not a normal deduction.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Charges must not be taken from MT202 COV", "note": "It is forbidden to take charges from an MT202 COV"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cover amount must match the MT103 amount", "note": "The cover amount must never be lower than the amount in the MT103"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediary claims charges separately", "note": "An intermediary asks the sending bank through an MT191 or debits the account directly"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Cover arriving short is an exception", "note": "A cover arriving short of the announced amount is an exception"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Hold the payment until the cover is made whole", "note": "No credit on a short cover"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202", "MT191"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C072", "seq": 277, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 45,000 missing for two weeks, and everyone said to wait", "explain": "USD 45,000 was sent to a supplier.\nThree days passed. Then a week. Then two.\nEveryone said to wait a little longer, while the supplier threatened to stop deliveries.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Transfer sent in a major currency\n→ 48 to 72 hours pass, nothing arrives\n→ One week passes, still nothing\n→ Two weeks pass, still nothing\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Cross-border payments just take a while.\"\nAfter two weeks, there is definitely a problem.\n\nWhy?\n\nA transfer in a major currency such as the United States dollar or the euro usually arrives in 48 to 72 hours, while a transfer in a less common local currency takes longer because the currency is harder to buy. A large amount can also slow a payment down, because it may need extra checks before it is released.\n\nIf the money has not arrived after two weeks, there is definitely a problem.\n\nWhat proves this payment is still moving, rather than lost?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk the sending bank to trace it. The sending bank knows every party in the chain, and a trace, not more waiting, is what finds a payment that has not arrived after two weeks.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Time since sending\n→ Currency and amount checks\n→ Trace requested\n→ Every party in the chain named\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Should we keep waiting?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this payment is still moving, rather than lost?\"\n\nHave you ever waited two weeks for a payment everyone said was just slow?", "takeaway": "If the money has not arrived after two weeks, there is definitely a problem.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Major currency transfer takes 48 to 72 hours", "note": "A transfer in United States dollars or euros usually arrives in 48 to 72 hours"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Less common currency takes longer", "note": "A less common local currency takes longer because it is harder to buy"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Large amount may need extra checks", "note": "A large amount can slow a payment down because it may need extra checks"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Money not arrived after two weeks", "note": "If the money has not arrived after two weeks, there is definitely a problem"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sending bank asked to trace it", "note": "The sending bank should be asked to trace it, since it knows every party"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C090", "seq": 288, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 70,000 MT103 with an IBAN but no name", "explain": "An MT103 for EUR 70,000 arrived showing only the beneficiary's IBAN and address in field 59.\nNo name at all.\nThe beneficiary bank held the payment, unable to confirm the account belonged to the person the payment was meant for.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 shows only IBAN and address in field 59\n→ No beneficiary name\n→ Bank cannot confirm the account owner\n→ Bank holds the payment and asks for confirmation\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An IBAN identifies the beneficiary.\"\nField 59 is meant to identify the beneficiary.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 59 is meant to identify the beneficiary, so a missing name leaves the beneficiary bank unable to confirm that the account belongs to the person the payment was meant for. The bank may hold the payment and ask for confirmation instead of crediting it, and the sender's bank is the one that must supply the missing name.\n\nA missing name leaves the bank unable to confirm the account belongs to the right person.\n\nWhat proves the account belongs to the person this payment was meant for?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSupply the beneficiary name in field 59 on every MT103. When a payment arrives without one, hold it, ask the sender's bank for the name, and credit only when the name and the account agree.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Beneficiary name in field 59\n→ IBAN and address\n→ Name and account agree\n→ Credit only on agreement\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the IBAN enough?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the account belongs to the person this payment was meant for?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment because the beneficiary had no name?", "takeaway": "A missing name leaves the bank unable to confirm the account belongs to the right person.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "An MT103 with no beneficiary name on EUR 70,000", "parts": [{"label": "Only IBAN and address in field 59", "note": "An MT103 can arrive showing only the beneficiary's IBAN and address in field 59"}, {"label": "Field 59 must identify the beneficiary", "note": "Field 59 is meant to identify the beneficiary"}, {"label": "Bank cannot confirm the account owner", "note": "A missing name leaves the bank unable to confirm the account belongs to the right person"}, {"label": "Bank may hold and ask for confirmation", "note": "The bank may hold the payment and ask for confirmation instead of crediting it"}, {"label": "Sender's bank must supply the name", "note": "The sender's bank must supply the missing name"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "invalid beneficiary data", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-004", "seq": 300, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 12,000 SDD collection, silence for a week, and the team assumed it had failed", "explain": "A EUR 12,000 direct debit collection went out as a pacs.007.\nA week passed in silence.\nThe team assumed the collection had failed and sent it again, and the debtor was debited twice.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor bank sends pacs.007\n→ No scheme response follows\n→ Team reads silence as failure\n→ Collection sent again, debtor debited twice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Silence means it failed.\"\nSilence after pacs.007 is normal, not failure.\n\nWhy?\n\nUnder the SDD scheme the debtor bank sends no scheme response once it has the pacs.007 collection instruction, so silence after that point says nothing about the outcome. When the collection comes back, it arrives as a pacs.004 return or refund, and the creditor bank must process it without any option to refuse.\n\nSilence after pacs.007 is normal, not failure.\n\nWhat proves this collection failed, rather than simply being silent?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWait for the outcome instead of resending. Build processing around handling pacs.004 returns and refunds, which the creditor bank must process without any option to refuse, and never treat silence as a failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.007 sent\n→ Silence expected\n→ pacs.004 return handled\n→ No resend on silence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the collection failed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this collection failed, rather than simply being silent?\"\n\nHave you ever resent a collection because silence looked like failure?", "takeaway": "Silence after pacs.007 is normal, not failure.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor bank sends pacs.007", "note": "The collection instruction goes to the debtor bank under SDD."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No scheme response follows", "note": "Silence after this point does not signal a failed collection."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Silence means failure or normal", "note": "Silence after pacs.007 is normal, not failure"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.004 comes back", "note": "A return or refund arrives in a pacs.004."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor bank must process it", "note": "The creditor bank has no option to refuse the pacs.004."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.007", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-005", "seq": 301, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A pacs.004 arrived for EUR 50,000. Return or refund? Nobody could tell.", "explain": "A pacs.004 for EUR 50,000 lands in the queue.\nThe customer is waiting for an answer.\nThe team cannot tell whether it is a return or a refund.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.004 received for EUR 50,000\n→ Initiator field checked\n→ Return rules and refund rules both look possible\n→ Payment held while the team argues\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A pacs.004 is a return.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.004 can carry either a return or a refund. The debtor bank starts a return, the debtor starts a refund, and the two events run under different rules and different time limits. Reading the message type alone tells you nothing. Only the initiator tells you which event it is.\n\nOne message, two events. Read the initiator.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat the initiator as the first check on every pacs.004, before any rule or time limit is applied.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return status\n→ Refund status\n→ Initiator identity\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether this pacs.004 is a return or a refund?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment because the message type alone could not tell you what it was?", "takeaway": "One message, two events. Read the initiator.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One message, two events", "parts": [{"label": "Return", "note": "Started by the debtor bank, using a pacs.004."}, {"label": "Refund", "note": "Started by the debtor, using the same pacs.004."}, {"label": "Read the initiator", "note": "The initiator identifies which event the message carries."}, {"label": "Different rules, different limits", "note": "Each event runs under its own rules and time limits."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-006", "seq": 302, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 1,200 collected with no mandate. The debtor claims it back, 12 months later.", "explain": "A EUR 1,200 direct debit was collected every month.\nTwelve months later the debtor says there was never a valid mandate.\nThe creditor is sure the 8 week refund window has closed.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ SDD Core collection of EUR 1,200\n→ Debtor claims a refund after 12 months\n→ Creditor checks the 8 week window\n→ Mandate checked and found missing\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Refunds are always claimed within 8 weeks.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn SDD Core the 8 week window applies only where a valid mandate stands behind the collection. Where no valid mandate exists at all, the window is 13 months. The two periods must never be confused, and the creditor holds the mandate, so the creditor must produce it when a claim is disputed.\n\n8 weeks needs a mandate. No mandate means 13 months.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecord which window applies on every refund claim, and ask for the mandate before arguing about the date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate validity\n→ Refund window\n→ Mandate evidence\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves a valid mandate existed for this collection?\"\n\nHave you seen a refund claim turn on a mandate nobody could produce?", "takeaway": "8 weeks needs a mandate. No mandate means 13 months.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "SDD Core refund windows", "parts": [{"label": "8 weeks, valid mandate", "note": "Refund claim window where a valid mandate backs the collection."}, {"label": "13 months, no valid mandate", "note": "The longer window applies only where no valid mandate exists."}, {"label": "Creditor holds the mandate", "note": "The creditor must produce the mandate when a claim is disputed."}, {"label": "Check the mandate first", "note": "The window follows the mandate, never the other way round."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-108", "seq": 325, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 200,000 to a new corridor. The intermediary could not serve it, and the payment died.", "explain": "A EUR 200,000 payment is sent down a new corridor.\nThe instruction names an intermediary that holds no settlement relationship there.\nThe payment dies downstream, and message mapping, routing data and routing logic all look equally guilty.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 200,000 sent on a new corridor\n→ Intermediary named in the instruction\n→ Intermediary cannot serve the destination currency\n→ Payment rejected downstream\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the payment fails downstream, the mapping must be wrong.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn instruction can name an intermediary that cannot serve the destination currency and holds no settlement relationship for that corridor, and the payment dies downstream. The failure is then unreadable: message mapping, routing reference data and routing logic all look equally guilty. Start from the agent chain data, validate every intermediary against the corridor, repair the reference data, and track repair rates so the bad intermediary is caught before its next payment.\n\nStart from the agent chain, not from the mapping.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidate every intermediary against its corridor before the payment goes out, and track repair rates so bad data is caught early.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Agent chain data\n→ Intermediary corridor coverage\n→ Reference data repair\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this intermediary serves this corridor and currency?\"\n\nHave you chased a mapping fault that turned out to be bad intermediary data?", "takeaway": "Start from the agent chain, not from the mapping.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Test the intermediary", "note": "Test currency and corridor coverage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Test the agent chain data", "note": "Confirm the chain is complete at every agent."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does every intermediary serve this corridor?", "note": "One gap stops the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Repair the reference data", "note": "Fix mapping or routing data at its source."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Track the repair rate", "note": "Spot a bad intermediary before the next payment uses it."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-116", "seq": 328, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 80,000 held at the border. The purpose code was optional at the front, mandatory at the back.", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 80,000 abroad and leaves the purpose code blank, because the screen says it is optional.\nDownstream, a system that mandates the code holds the payment.\nNobody owns the repair, so the payment waits while the customer calls.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 80,000 payment sent without a purpose code\n→ Code optional at the customer, mandatory downstream\n→ Payment held at the downstream system\n→ No named owner for the repair\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"An optional field can stay optional all the way through.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA purpose code that is optional at the customer and mandatory downstream is a defect waiting for a payment. Name the downstream system that needs it, give enrichment a named owning source, and validate early enough to catch a missing code before the payment reaches that system. And define the repair behaviour up front, who fixes it, how fast, and what happens to the payment while it waits.\n\nOptional at the front, mandatory at the back, is a defect.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the downstream system, the enrichment owner and the repair behaviour for every code that is mandatory anywhere in the chain.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Downstream requirement\n→ Enrichment owner\n→ Repair behaviour\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payment carries every code the downstream system needs?\"\n\nHave you watched a payment stall on a field the customer was told was optional?", "takeaway": "Optional at the front, mandatory at the back, is a defect.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Name the downstream requirement", "note": "Name the system that needs the code."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assign the enrichment owner", "note": "Give a named source the job of supplying it."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the code present before the payment is sent?", "note": "Catch the missing code early."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate before the payment is sent", "note": "Catch the missing code early."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define the repair behaviour", "note": "Settle who fixes it, how fast, and what waits."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-118", "seq": 329, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 120,000 routed to a bank that stopped serving the corridor years ago.", "explain": "A EUR 120,000 payment is routed through an intermediary named in the reference data.\nThe bank stopped serving that corridor years ago, and nobody updated the data.\nThe payment is rejected, and the same bad intermediary is used again next week.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 120,000 routed via Intermediary Agent 2\n→ Reference data points at a bank that left the corridor\n→ Payment rejected downstream\n→ Same intermediary used again the next week\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Reference data looks after itself.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nReference data without an owner decays, and Intermediary Agent 2 is the proof: nobody maintains it, so it quietly points payments at a bank that no longer serves the corridor. The control set is fourfold, a named owner, a validation rule, an update process, and a routing control that stops a payment whose intermediary data is out of date. Leave any of the four out and the same bad intermediary keeps reappearing until a rejection or a repair exposes it.\n\nReference data without an owner decays.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every intermediary field a named owner, a validation rule, an update process and a routing block on stale data.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Named owner\n→ Validation rule\n→ Update process\n→ Routing block\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this intermediary data is current today?\"\n\nHave you seen the same bad intermediary reappear after a rejection?", "takeaway": "Reference data without an owner decays.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Intermediary Agent 2, unmaintained", "parts": [{"label": "One named owner", "note": "One person accountable for the data."}, {"label": "Currency check", "note": "A rule testing whether the data is current."}, {"label": "Timely updates", "note": "Changes land in the data on time."}, {"label": "Routing block on stale data", "note": "Stale data blocks the payment at routing."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-158", "seq": 332, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 25,000 collected from a customer who cancelled. The file was built on last month's data.", "explain": "A collection file goes out for EUR 25,000.\nIt includes a customer who cancelled, and an amount changed after the file was built.\nThe file is submitted after the scheme deadline, and the refunds and complaints arrive together.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection file built on old data\n→ Cancelled customer still collected\n→ Amount changed after the file was built\n→ File submitted after the scheme deadline\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A collection file is a spreadsheet job.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTreat a collection file as a regulated scheme submission, not a spreadsheet job: the same errors return every month, a cancelled customer still collected, an amount changed after the file was built, a file submitted after the scheme deadline. The discipline is the sequence, build from current mandate and billing data, validate, win creditor PSP acceptance, submit inside the scheme window. Skip a step and the cost arrives as refunds, complaints and rework.\n\nA collection file is a scheme submission, not a spreadsheet job.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun the file through the full sequence every time, and never submit a file built on stale data.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Current mandate data\n→ File validation\n→ Creditor PSP acceptance\n→ Scheme window\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this file was built on current data and submitted inside the window?\"\n\nHave you seen the same collection errors return every month?", "takeaway": "A collection file is a scheme submission, not a spreadsheet job.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Build on current data", "note": "Build on live mandate and billing data."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the file", "note": "Test amounts, mandates and dates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Win creditor PSP acceptance", "note": "Win the PSP acceptance for submission."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Inside the scheme window?", "note": "A late file is a failed file."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Submit inside the scheme window", "note": "Submit before the scheme deadline."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-159", "seq": 333, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A customer cancelled a EUR 800 collection. The money was still debited.", "explain": "A customer asks billing to cancel a EUR 800 collection.\nBilling deletes it from the file.\nThe file is already with the creditor PSP, so the debit goes ahead anyway.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 800 collection submitted to the creditor PSP\n→ Customer asks for cancellation\n→ Billing deletes it from the local file\n→ Collection debited because deletion no longer works\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Deleting it from the file cancels the collection.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOnce a file is with the creditor PSP, cancelling a collection is a scheme process, not a delete key. Cancellation follows the process for the collection state at that moment, and a collection already in processing can only be stopped on the defined cancellation path. Billing must know the exact lifecycle point where deletion stops working and cancellation takes over, because that point decides whether the customer is debited.\n\nAfter submission, deletion stops working. Cancellation takes over.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTeach billing the exact lifecycle point where deletion ends and the scheme cancellation path begins.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Collection lifecycle state\n→ Deletion window\n→ Scheme cancellation path\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this collection was cancelled on the right path for its state?\"\n\nHave you deleted a collection that was already with the PSP?", "takeaway": "After submission, deletion stops working. Cancellation takes over.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Locate the collection state", "note": "Locate the collection in its lifecycle."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the file still yours?", "note": "Deletion works only before submission."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cancel ahead of submission", "note": "Delete it while the file is still yours."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cancel once submitted", "note": "Follow the scheme cancellation path."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Prove the cancellation held", "note": "Evidence the customer will not be debited."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-160", "seq": 334, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A member cancelled. A clerk revoked. The EUR 300 collection was debited anyway.", "explain": "A member cancels a membership and expects no further collection.\nA billing clerk revokes the next EUR 300 collection instead.\nThe wrong mechanism is used for the collection's state, and the member is debited anyway.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Member cancels the membership\n→ Clerk revokes the EUR 300 collection\n→ Wrong mechanism for the collection state\n→ Member debited despite the cancellation\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Cancellation and revocation are the same thing.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCancellation and revocation are different mechanisms that happen to sound alike: different parties start them, at different times, for collections in different states. A member cancelling a membership expects no further collection, a billing clerk revoking a collection expects it never to leave the building, and a creditor PSP receiving a cancellation request expects a scheme message with a response. Choose by collection state, because the wrong mechanism debits the member anyway or gets the request rejected by the bank.\n\nSame word, different mechanism. State picks it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nChoose the mechanism by collection state, and name the state on every cancellation and revocation request.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Collection state\n→ Cancelling path\n→ Revoking path\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this collection was in the state this mechanism serves?\"\n\nHave you used the wrong word and debited a member who had cancelled?", "takeaway": "Same word, different mechanism. State picks it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "State picks the mechanism", "parts": [{"label": "Cancelling", "note": "Ends future collections for the membership."}, {"label": "Revoking", "note": "Stops a collection before it leaves."}, {"label": "Who starts it", "note": "Member, clerk or PSP, each on its own path."}, {"label": "State decides", "note": "State, not wording, decides the mechanism."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-161", "seq": 335, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A duplicate EUR 1,100 collection settled. Someone deleted it, and reconciliation broke.", "explain": "A duplicate EUR 1,100 collection settles by mistake.\nSomeone deletes the settled entry to fix it.\nReconciliation now has a hole where the money went, and the audit trail is gone.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Duplicate EUR 1,100 collection settles\n→ Entry deleted to fix the duplicate\n→ Reconciliation finds a hole\n→ Audit trail lost\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A settled duplicate can be deleted to fix it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA reversal is its own transaction, not a deletion and not a debtor refund claim: a separate, linked entry returning money already settled, used when a duplicate collection can no longer be cancelled or revoked because the money has moved. It carries the original references, is initiated by the creditor, and posts as its own accounting entry so reconciliation pairs it with the original instead of finding a hole. Deleting settled entries breaks reconciliation, so the duplicate is reversed, never erased.\n\nSettled money is reversed, never erased.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReverse a settled duplicate as its own linked transaction, with the original references attached.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Original collection\n→ Reversal entry\n→ Reconciliation pair\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this duplicate was reversed, not deleted?\"\n\nHave you seen a deleted entry leave a hole in reconciliation?", "takeaway": "Settled money is reversed, never erased.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the money has settled", "note": "Cancellation and revocation are past their point."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Open the reversal", "note": "Creditor opens a linked return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Attach the original references", "note": "Attach the duplicate references."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the reversal linked to the original?", "note": "An unlinked reversal is another break."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconcile the pair", "note": "Match the reversal to the original collection."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-04", "seq": 338, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A pacs.008 for EUR 60,000 was rejected. The team sent a pacs.004 to fix it.", "explain": "A pacs.008 for EUR 60,000 is rejected before settlement.\nThe pacs.002 carries status RJCT and a reason code.\nThe team opens a return, even though no money has moved.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 for EUR 60,000 sent\n→ pacs.002 returns RJCT with a reason code\n→ Team opens a pacs.004 return\n→ No money has moved, so there is nothing to return\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A rejected payment is fixed with a return.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn RJCT on a pacs.002 is a pre-settlement verdict: the status report says the instruction failed, and no money has moved. The reason code names the failure precisely, technical, such as a schema or format error, or business, such as a sanctions flag, and each kind has its own owner. The remedy is repair at source and resubmission as a new instruction, new message ID, same UETR, so tracking stays continuous.\n\nRJCT before settlement means repair, not return.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClassify the RJCT by reason code, repair at source, and resubmit on the same UETR.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.002 RJCT status\n→ Reason code\n→ Resubmission on the same UETR\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves no money moved, so repair is the right remedy?\"\n\nHave you returned a payment that had never settled?", "takeaway": "RJCT before settlement means repair, not return.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Read the pacs.002 report", "note": "Status RJCT plus reason code names the failure."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Classify the failure", "note": "Schema or format is technical; sanctions or rules is business."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm nothing settled", "note": "Nothing settled, so repair applies, not return."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Repair at source, or escalate to the owner?", "note": "Each failure kind has its own owner."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resubmit on the same UETR", "note": "New message ID, same UETR, unbroken tracking."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-05", "seq": 339, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 90,000 payment settled, then failed. The team tried to fix it with a status report.", "explain": "A EUR 90,000 payment settles, then turns out to be wrong.\nThe team sends another pacs.002 to fix it.\nNothing moves, because a status report never moves money.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 90,000 payment settles\n→ Error found after settlement\n→ Team tries a pacs.002 to fix it\n→ Funds stay where they are\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A status report can fix a settled payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSettlement closes the pacs.002 path for good: a status report never moves money, so a settled payment cannot be fixed by reporting. The funds must come back through a pacs.004, a real settlement instruction in reverse, travelling the return chain through every bank the original passed. Return charges may come off along the way, so the amount returning can differ from the amount sent, and the UETR ties both legs together for reconciliation.\n\nAfter settlement, only a return moves money.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOnce a payment is settled, open a pacs.004 and trace the return chain, never another status report.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Settlement state\n→ pacs.004 return\n→ UETR linkage\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payment settled, so only a return can move it?\"\n\nHave you tried to fix a settled payment with a report?", "takeaway": "After settlement, only a return moves money.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the settlement", "note": "ACSC means funds moved; the pacs.002 path is closed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Open the pacs.004", "note": "Open a genuine settlement instruction in reverse."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the return chain", "note": "Outbound A to B to C to D, return D to C to B to A."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the returned amount match the original?", "note": "Return charges may have come off along the way."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconcile the variance", "note": "Charges on the way back may shrink the amount."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "SCN-08", "seq": 340, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 40,000 credited to a closed account. The beneficiary bank sent it back itself.", "explain": "EUR 40,000 is credited to an account, then found to be closed.\nThe beneficiary bank does not wait to be asked.\nIt sends the money back itself, and the sender waits for a recall that never comes.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 40,000 credited\n→ Account found closed after the credit\n→ Beneficiary bank opens a pacs.004\n→ Sender waits for a recall request\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Only the sender can start a return.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA beneficiary bank that credits an account and then finds it closed or frozen does not wait to be asked. It becomes the return originator and sends the pacs.004 back along the chain in reverse. Charges may be deducted at hops, so the sender recovers the original minus charges, and the return is matched to the original payment by UETR.\n\nThe beneficiary bank can send the money back itself.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen funds land on a closed or frozen account, expect the beneficiary bank to originate the return, and match it by UETR.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Closed account finding\n→ Beneficiary bank return\n→ UETR match\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who originated this return?\"\n\nHave you waited for a recall when the beneficiary bank had already returned the funds?", "takeaway": "The beneficiary bank can send the money back itself.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Find the problem after credit", "note": "Account closed or frozen, found after the funds landed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Open the pacs.004 return", "note": "Beneficiary bank originates the return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send it back along the chain", "note": "Each intermediary validates and passes the return on."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the returned amount match, minus charges?", "note": "Charges at hops shrink the return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Match the return to the original", "note": "Pair the return with the original by UETR."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-11", "seq": 342, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 110,000 payment timed out. The team retried it blind, and paid twice.", "explain": "A EUR 110,000 payment times out mid-chain.\nNobody knows whether it settled.\nThe team retries it, and the beneficiary is paid twice.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 110,000 payment times out\n→ Outcome unknown, no status received\n→ Blind retry sent\n→ Beneficiary paid twice\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A timeout means the payment failed, so retry it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA timeout reports nothing about the outcome, and a blind retry on an unknown can double-pay. Freeze the payment, then work the evidence, UETR, EndToEndId and TxId across the tracker and hop logs, late responses, and statements against camt.054 notifications. Act only on the true state found: resubmit if it truly failed, recall or claim if it settled incorrectly.\n\nA timeout is an unknown, not a failure.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFreeze every timed-out payment, prove its true state from the tracker and the statements, and act only on what you find.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Timeout state\n→ UETR evidence\n→ Statement proof\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether this payment settled or failed?\"\n\nHave you ever retried a payment before knowing what happened to the first one?", "takeaway": "A timeout is an unknown, not a failure.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Freeze the payment", "note": "Stop automatic retry until the state is known."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigate by UETR", "note": "Work the tracker and hop logs."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check for late responses", "note": "A pacs.002, pacs.004 or pacs.009 may arrive late."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Did the funds actually move?", "note": "Test the statements against camt.054 notifications."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Act on the true state", "note": "Resubmit only on a proven failure."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.054"], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-13", "seq": 344, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 75,000 from India to the USA vanished between hops. The tracker showed the last good hop.", "explain": "A USD 75,000 payment leaves India for the USA.\nIt never reaches the beneficiary.\nThe team searches the whole chain at once, and finds nothing.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ USD 75,000 pacs.008 sent from India\n→ Fedwire leg never completes\n→ Team searches the whole chain\n→ Failure point never found\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A failed cross-border payment is investigated as one rail.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCross-border tracing works leg by leg, never as one rail: a SWIFT pacs.008 moves across correspondents, then Fedwire completes final US dollar settlement. Start from the UETR in the tracker, locate the last successful hop, and read the status message there, because its reason code names the failure. Then test the corridor suspects, a missing purpose code, a sanctions hold, an invalid routing number, or an FX or cut-off gap, and close the case with the right message, a pacs.004 return or a camt.056 recall.\n\nTrace leg by leg, from the last good hop.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart every cross-border trace at the UETR, find the last good hop, and test the corridor suspects in order.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR tracker\n→ Last good hop\n→ Reason code\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where this payment stopped?\"\n\nHave you searched a whole chain when the last good hop would have told you?", "takeaway": "Trace leg by leg, from the last good hop.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Take the UETR", "note": "Use the tracker to see every hop."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find the last good hop", "note": "Failure sits at or just after that point."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Read the status and reason code", "note": "AC04 account closed, BE01 details mismatch, RC01 bad routing, AM05 duplicate."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which corridor suspect fits?", "note": "Purpose code, sanctions, routing, FX or cut-off gap."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resolve with the right message", "note": "Return or recall, closed with a camt.029."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.004", "camt.056"], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "SCN-14", "seq": 345, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 95,000 payment timed out mid-chain. The recall was the brake, and it was pulled too late.", "explain": "A EUR 95,000 payment times out mid-chain with its state unknown.\nThe team debates whether to recall it.\nBy the time the recall goes out, the credit has completed, and the recall cannot undo it.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 95,000 pacs.008 times out\n→ True state unknown\n→ Recall debated instead of sent\n→ Credit completes before the recall lands\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A recall can undo a completed payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment timed out mid-chain with its true state unknown is exactly when a recall earns its name as an emergency brake: send the request down the chain and stop it before it completes. The brake has one limit, it cannot undo a completed credit. Where the credit already completed, switch to the return flow via pacs.004, and update the customer at each step, because silence turns incidents into escalations.\n\nA recall is a brake, not a reverse gear.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPull the recall the moment a payment times out mid-chain, and switch to a pacs.004 the moment the credit completes.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Timeout point\n→ Recall request\n→ Return flow\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this payment is still stoppable, not already credited?\"\n\nHave you pulled the recall brake too late?", "takeaway": "A recall is a brake, not a reverse gear.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Pin down the timeout point", "note": "pacs.008 left core banking; the delay is downstream."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Track by UETR", "note": "Test whether funds actually moved."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has the credit completed?", "note": "A completed credit cannot be recalled."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send the recall request", "note": "Brake the payment, do not reverse it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Switch to pacs.004 once completed", "note": "Completed credit means return the funds."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-16", "seq": 346, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 55,000 returned, and the team treated it as a cancellation. It was a payment in reverse.", "explain": "EUR 55,000 settles to the wrong beneficiary.\nA pacs.004 comes back through the chain.\nThe team files it as a cancellation, with no charges, no references and no controls.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 55,000 settles to the wrong beneficiary\n→ pacs.004 return sent in reverse\n→ Return filed as a cancellation\n→ Charges and references never checked\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A return is just a cancellation with money attached.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTreat a pacs.004 as what it is, a real payment in reverse: settled funds moving backward through the same chain, carrying its own charges and its own references. It applies when the beneficiary bank finds a closed or frozen account, a duplicate payment, or a wrong beneficiary after settlement. Its return chain element preserves the original chain so every intermediary knows its place, and returns deserve the same operational controls as outward payments.\n\nA return is a payment in reverse.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun every pacs.004 with the same controls as an outward payment: charges, references and chain included.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return chain\n→ Return charges\n→ Original references\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this return followed the original chain, with its charges accounted for?\"\n\nHave you treated a return as paperwork when it was really money moving?", "takeaway": "A return is a payment in reverse.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Trigger only after settlement", "note": "Closed account, duplicate, or wrong beneficiary, after settlement."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send the pacs.004 in reverse", "note": "Settled funds move backward."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Deduct the return charges", "note": "Returned amount can differ from the amount sent."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the return carry the original chain?", "note": "Every intermediary must know its place."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Link the return to the original", "note": "Tie it by UETR and message references."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-19", "seq": 348, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 130,000 payment went missing. The investigation started with a free-text email.", "explain": "A EUR 130,000 payment goes missing and a customer complains.\nThe investigation starts with a free-text email to the correspondent, with no references.\nDays pass, the case has no deadline, and the backlog grows.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 130,000 payment missing\n→ Investigation opened with no SLA\n→ Correspondent contacted by free-text email\n→ Case drifts with no deadline\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Investigations run on experience and instinct.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment investigation is a fixed sequence: intake and triage, identify the payment, verify the true status, check compliance, contact the correspondent, and resolve. Give every case type an SLA and an escalation matrix, because investigations without deadlines become backlogs. And contact the correspondent with structured queries and full references, never free-text emails that go nowhere.\n\nInvestigations run on rails, not instinct.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun every investigation through the fixed sequence, with an SLA, an escalation matrix and structured correspondent queries.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Case SLA\n→ Escalation matrix\n→ Structured queries\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this case is on track, with a deadline and an owner?\"\n\nHave you chased a missing payment with a free-text email that went nowhere?", "takeaway": "Investigations run on rails, not instinct.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Take in and triage", "note": "Sort the trigger: beneficiary error, delay, hold, Nostro break, duplicate."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Identify the payment", "note": "Fix it with UETR and references."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Verify the true status", "note": "Find the hop reached, settled or not."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compliance clear to proceed?", "note": "Screening and regulatory constraints come first."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Contact the correspondent", "note": "Structured queries, full references."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Resolve and close the case", "note": "Return, recall, repair or re-release, closed with evidence."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-32", "seq": 356, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 250,000 held for three days on a name mismatch. The status said Pending, and the team read it as rejected.", "explain": "A EUR 250,000 payment sits uncredited for three days.\nThe pacs.002 shows Pending, and the team treats it as a rejection.\nThe beneficiary bank is holding the payment, waiting for the name to be corrected.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 250,000 payment sent\n→ pacs.002 shows Pending\n→ Team reads Pending as rejected\n→ Beneficiary bank holds the payment on a name mismatch\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Pending means rejected.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA 250,000 euro payment sat uncredited for three days because the beneficiary bank flagged a name mismatch. A pacs.002 showing Pending means the beneficiary bank is holding the payment, not rejecting it, so open with an investigation query for the exact mismatch detail. Correct and verify the name and the bank applies the funds; if it cannot be resolved the payment returns via pacs.004, so validate beneficiary details at initiation, including confirmation of payee where the corridor supports it.\n\nPending is a hold, not a rejection.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead Pending as a hold, ask for the exact mismatch detail, and validate beneficiary names at initiation.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.002 Pending status\n→ Name mismatch detail\n→ Beneficiary validation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves what the name mismatch actually is?\"\n\nHave you written off a payment that was only ever pending?", "takeaway": "Pending is a hold, not a rejection.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Read the pacs.002 Pending", "note": "Pending is a hold, not a rejection."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Raise the investigation query", "note": "Ask for the exact mismatch detail."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Can the name be corrected and verified?", "note": "Verify the correction, or return the funds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correct the name or return the funds", "note": "Verify the correction, or return the funds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate at the start", "note": "Confirmation of payee where the corridor supports it."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "name mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F14", "seq": 359, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 2,000 collection was made by mistake. The team used one cancel button for all three stages.", "explain": "A creditor realises a EUR 2,000 collection was made by mistake.\nThe team reaches for one generic cancel requirement.\nThe collection is already in the clearing flow, and the generic cancel does nothing.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 2,000 collection made by mistake\n→ Creditor wants it stopped\n→ Collection sits in the clearing flow\n→ Generic cancel requirement applied\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"One cancel requirement covers every mistaken collection.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a creditor realises a collection was made by mistake, the right mechanism depends on where the collection sits in its lifecycle. While it is warehoused with the creditor PSP and has not entered the inter-PSP flow, the creditor revokes it, and once it is in the clearing flow but before settlement, the creditor requests cancellation through the PSP or CSM process. After clearing and settlement, only a reversal can reimburse the debtor for the erroneous collection, and one generic cancel requirement cannot cover all three because the stage, initiator, mechanism and responsibilities differ, with refund the remaining step after settlement.\n\nStop a mistaken collection by its lifecycle stage.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the stage first, then choose revoke, cancel or reverse, and never write one generic cancel requirement.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Collection lifecycle stage\n→ Revoke path\n→ Cancel path\n→ Reversal path\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which stage this collection is in?\"\n\nHave you tried to cancel a collection that had already moved past cancelling?", "takeaway": "Stop a mistaken collection by its lifecycle stage.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Creditor spots a mistake in a collection", "note": "The collection must be stopped, but the mechanism depends on its stage"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Where is the collection?", "note": "Warehoused, in the clearing flow, or already settled"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Warehoused: revoke it", "note": "Revocation goes from creditor to creditor PSP"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "In clearing: request cancellation", "note": "Cancellation before settlement through the PSP or CSM process"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settled: reverse it", "note": "Reversal reimburses the debtor after clearing and settlement"}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-D04", "seq": 367, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 60,000 payment was processed, but by mid-morning there was still no credit. The customer called, and nobody could trace it.", "explain": "A EUR 60,000 cross-border payment is processed.\nBy mid-morning the beneficiary still has no credit.\nThe customer calls, and the team has no live trace, so one query becomes hours of back-and-forth.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 60,000 payment processed\n→ No credit by mid-morning\n→ Customer query arrives\n→ No live trace available\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A processed payment can be found by asking around.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA processed cross-border payment can still show no credit by mid-morning because it may have failed silently, queued, stalled on missing details, or stopped for compliance checks, and without a live trace one customer query becomes hours of back-and-forth, reconciliation and investigation. Visibility comes from three elements working together: the UETR, ISO 20022 structured data, and connected systems and processes. A payment without a trace leaves a customer question unanswered, while a tracked payment reconciles faster, clears exceptions sooner, and avoids duplication, failed credits and manual rework.\n\nNo trace turns one question into an investigation.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every payment a live trace built on the UETR, structured ISO 20022 data and connected systems.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR trace\n→ Structured data\n→ Connected systems\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where this payment sits right now?\"\n\nHave you answered a customer query with no trace to check?", "takeaway": "No trace turns one question into an investigation.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Making the payment journey visible", "parts": [{"label": "UETR", "note": "Unique End-to-End Transaction Reference, tracking the payment through its journey."}, {"label": "ISO 20022 structured data", "note": "Rich structured data travelling with the payment."}, {"label": "Connected systems", "note": "Systems and processes sharing status end to end."}, {"label": "No real-time trace", "note": "One customer query becomes hours of reconciliation and investigation."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D05", "seq": 368, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "An instant payment of EUR 5,000 left the account at once. The beneficiary received nothing.", "explain": "An instant payment of EUR 5,000 is debited at once.\nThe beneficiary receives nothing.\nThe team assumes it was rejected and the money is on its way back, but the payment is stuck, not rejected.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 5,000 instant payment sent\n→ Debit lands immediately\n→ Beneficiary receives nothing\n→ Team assumes a rejection\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A failed instant payment means the money comes straight back.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen an instant payment fails, the debit can land immediately while the beneficiary receives nothing, so the first task is to establish the cause: a timeout, an account issue, a technical problem, a compliance issue, or insufficient funds. Failed payments split into two kinds: genuinely rejected ones, where the money returns to the payer, and stuck ones, which need investigation, sometimes with the receiving bank helping to return the money. The same payment can even end differently in different countries, so the operative question is not only whether money left, but what the outcome is and where the money now sits.\n\nFind the outcome first. Rejected and stuck are not the same.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEstablish the outcome before telling the customer anything: rejected means the money returns, stuck means investigate.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Failure cause\n→ Rejected outcome\n→ Stuck outcome\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves whether this payment was rejected or is stuck?\"\n\nHave you told a customer the money was coming back when it was only stuck?", "takeaway": "Find the outcome first. Rejected and stuck are not the same.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Instant payment sent", "note": "Money is debited at once, yet the beneficiary receives nothing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "What caused the failure?", "note": "Insufficient funds, compliance, technical fault, account issue, or timeout."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Genuinely rejected", "note": "Money returns to the payer."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Stuck, investigate", "note": "Payment needs investigation, and the receiving bank can sometimes help return it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Outcome varies by country", "note": "The same payment can end differently in different countries."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E04", "seq": 376, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "90 percent of a remittance file failed, and the repair queue could not cope", "explain": "A remittance file worth EUR 480,000 arrived for processing.\n\n90 percent of its transactions failed a banking control.\n\nOperations sent them all to the repair queue, and the queue collapsed under the volume.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ File received with 200 transactions\n→ 180 transactions fail a control\n→ Every failed item goes to repair\n→ Valid items sit unprocessed while the queue grows\n→ The customer cannot tell what was processed, what was not, and what must be corrected\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Send failed transactions to the repair queue.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRepair works when only a few items fail. At 90 percent failure the problem is the file, not the items, and a repair queue cannot fix a bad file one transaction at a time.\n\nA repair queue is not a file correction process.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSize decides the route. Small files can send exceptions to repair while valid items continue. Large files need a threshold.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ File-level threshold check\n→ Item-level repair\n→ Release of valid transactions\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Where do failed items go?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this file is safe to process at all?\"\n\nHave you seen a repair queue used to fix a file that should have been rejected?", "takeaway": "A repair queue is not a file correction process.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "File arrives", "note": "200 transactions worth EUR 480,000, 180 fail a control."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "File has 20 or more transactions?", "note": "Small files carry no threshold; larger files apply the 5 percent rule."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "At or below 5 percent", "note": "Valid transactions continue and exceptions go to repair."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Above 5 percent", "note": "The whole file is rejected and the client corrects and resubmits it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check before releasing", "note": "No transaction is released before the threshold is checked."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A02", "seq": 436, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "No pacs.002 after 20 seconds, EUR 5,000 gone, and the customer asked for it to be sent again", "explain": "A customer sent a EUR 5,000 instant payment.\n\nTwenty seconds passed. No pacs.002 arrived.\n\nThe account stood debited, the funds had left, and the customer asked for the payment to be sent again.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ EUR 5,000 SCT Inst payment sent\n→ 20 second timeout passes, no pacs.002\n→ Customer account debited, funds gone, credit unconfirmed\n→ Customer asks to resend the payment\n→ Resending would risk a duplicate EUR 5,000 payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No confirmation means the payment failed, so send it again.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn SCT Inst, absence of a pacs.002 after the 20 second timeout leaves the payment in limbo. The debit is posted and the funds have left, but credit to the beneficiary stays unconfirmed. Resending would risk a duplicate, so the sender bank must instead issue a pacs.028 status request and let the recipient bank investigate.\n\nNo confirmation is not a failure. It is a question.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever resend on a missing pacs.002. Issue a pacs.028 status request. That investigation either confirms the credit, closing the case, or confirms rejection, triggering an automatic refund and restoration of the account.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The debit, posted\n→ The confirmation, missing\n→ The resend, forbidden\n→ The pacs.028 investigation\n→ The refund, if rejection is confirmed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment go through?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment's true state before we act?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment resent because no confirmation arrived?", "takeaway": "No confirmation is not a failure. It is a question.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "EUR 5,000 instant payment sent", "note": "An SCT Inst payment should complete within seconds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "20 second timeout, no pacs.002", "note": "No final confirmation arrives within the scheme timeout."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Beneficiary credited or not?", "note": "Debit is posted and funds have left, but credit stays unconfirmed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Never send it a second time", "note": "A second debit and payment would create a duplicate."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Issue pacs.028 status request", "note": "Ask the recipient bank to investigate the original payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Close the case or refund", "note": "Confirmed credit closes the case; rejection triggers automatic refund."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.028"], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A14", "seq": 440, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 100,000 payment was tracked to the wrong person, and tracking could not stop it", "explain": "A EUR 100,000 payment was sent to the wrong beneficiary.\n\nThe team watched it on tracking, and it showed ACSC, credited, within 30 minutes.\n\nThey sent a stop message. It had no effect, because the money was already with the beneficiary.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ EUR 100,000 sent to the wrong beneficiary\n→ Tracking shows the payment moving, then ACSC within 30 minutes\n→ Team sends a stop message\n→ Stop has no effect, the payment is already credited\n→ Recovery now needs a Stop and Recall request\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Tracking a payment means we can stop it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTracking only shows where a payment is, while stopping it prevents credit to the beneficiary, and the two must never be confused. Once credited, the bank needs a Stop and Recall request, routed by urgency: FAST, acknowledged within 2 hours when the payment is settled and credited; MEDIUM, answered within 12 hours when the beneficiary bank needs time to reach the beneficiary; and SLOW, answered within 72 hours when more time is needed. Delay steadily shrinks the chance of recovery.\n\nTracking shows. Recall recovers.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment goes to the wrong beneficiary, check the status first. If it is credited, skip the stop and send the Stop and Recall at the right urgency. Speed decides whether a recall remains possible.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Tracking, which only shows location\n→ The credit status, ACSC or not\n→ The Stop and Recall request\n→ The urgency queue: FAST, MEDIUM, SLOW\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we see the payment?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment is still stoppable, or that recall is the only route?\"\n\nHave you seen a stop message sent for a payment that was already credited?", "takeaway": "Tracking shows. Recall recovers.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Tracking shows the location", "note": "Tracking only shows where the EUR 100,000 payment is."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Already credited as ACSC?", "note": "Once credited to the beneficiary, a stop message has no effect."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send Stop and Recall request", "note": "Stopping needs a Stop and Recall request instead of tracking."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "FAST queue: within 2 hours", "note": "Acknowledged within 2 hours when the payment is settled and credited."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MEDIUM queue: within 12 hours", "note": "Answered within 12 hours when the bank needs time to contact the beneficiary."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "SLOW queue: within 72 hours", "note": "Answered within 72 hours when more time is needed."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B13", "seq": 445, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A camt.055 arrived for a EUR 75,000 payment, and the team sent a camt.056 before asking where the payment sat", "explain": "A camt.055 cancellation request arrived for a EUR 75,000 payment.\n\nThe team sent a camt.056 straight away.\n\nThe payment was future-dated and had not been released, so the whole interbank chase was unnecessary.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ camt.055 cancellation request arrives\n→ Team sends camt.056 without checking the lifecycle\n→ Payment was future-dated, warehoused, never released\n→ It could have been cancelled before execution, internally\n→ Interbank messages sent for a payment that never left the bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A cancellation request means send a camt.056.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a camt.055 arrives, do not assume a camt.056 should be sent straight away. The first question is where the original payment sits in its lifecycle. A future-dated or warehoused payment that has not been released may be cancelled before execution, a payment pending a compliance decision must be considered alongside that process, and a payment already processed by the debtor bank is no longer an internal decision. In a direct and cover model, each leg is controlled by a different party, so the pacs.008 leg and the pacs.009 COV leg may each need their own follow-up.\n\nA cancellation request only asks. It does not guarantee.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore any cancellation message, ask where the payment sits. Then act on that leg, and that leg only. If the cancellation fails, determine the appropriate return or investigation path.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The payment lifecycle position\n→ The warehoused leg, cancellable internally\n→ The compliance-pending leg\n→ The processed leg, no longer internal\n→ Each leg in a direct and cover model\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send the cancellation?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the payment sits before we act?\"\n\nHave you seen a camt.056 sent for a payment that never left the bank?", "takeaway": "A cancellation request only asks. It does not guarantee.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "camt.055 cancellation arrives", "note": "Do not assume a camt.056 should be sent straight away."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Where does the payment sit?", "note": "First ask where the original payment sits in its lifecycle."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Warehoused: cancel before execution", "note": "A future-dated payment not yet released may be cancelled before execution."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Already processed: not internal", "note": "A payment already processed by the debtor bank is no longer an internal decision."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each leg needs its own follow-up", "note": "In a direct and cover model, the pacs.008 and pacs.009 COV legs may each need follow-up."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "If it fails, find the return path", "note": "A cancellation is a request, not a guarantee; determine the return or investigation path."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.055", "camt.056", "pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C12", "seq": 447, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Four messages, one recall, and a EUR 35,000 question no one could answer", "explain": "A EUR 35,000 payment needed recalling.\n\nThe team had four messages in front of them: camt.055, camt.056, pacs.004 and camt.029.\n\nThey argued for an hour about which one was not used in the flow, and the payment waited.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer cancellation request, camt.055\n→ Interbank cancellation request, camt.056\n→ Payment return, pacs.004\n→ Resolution of investigation, camt.029\n→ Team debates which message is not used in the flow\n→ EUR 35,000 payment waits while the debate runs\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The debate matters more than the journey.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt does not. The end-to-end recall journey runs through camt.055, camt.056, pacs.004 and camt.029, and connecting the four messages into one journey is the point of the exercise, not naming each message on its own. The question of which message is not used stays open, and guessing it does not move the money.\n\nConnect the journey first. Debate second.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a recall lands, run the journey first: request, interbank request, return, resolution. The poll question can wait. The customer's money cannot.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The customer request, camt.055\n→ The interbank request, camt.056\n→ The return, pacs.004\n→ The resolution, camt.029\n→ The open question, answered with evidence, not a guess\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which message is not used?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each step of the journey ran?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment wait while a team debated the messages?", "takeaway": "Connect the journey first. Debate second.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "camt.055: customer cancellation request", "note": "The journey starts with the customer cancellation request."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.056: interbank cancellation request", "note": "The bank sends the interbank cancellation request."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.004: the payment return", "note": "The return of the EUR 35,000 payment follows."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "camt.029: resolution of investigation", "note": "The investigation is resolved with a camt.029."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "One message is not used: poll open", "note": "The question stays open rather than guessed; the journey is the point."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.055", "camt.056", "pacs.004", "camt.029"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-087", "seq": 453, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Supplier paid twice, EUR 12,000 doubled", "explain": "A supplier payment appears twice on the account statement for the same amount, to the same supplier, on the same day.\nThe supplier confirms receiving two credits.\nEUR 12,000 has left the account twice.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment submitted for EUR 12,000\n→ Statement shows two debits, same amount, same day\n→ Supplier confirms two credits received\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Same amount, same day, so it must be one payment shown twice.\"\n\nTwo identical credits are two payments until the evidence says otherwise. Reconstruct both payments end to end from the same evidence set: channel timestamp, idempotency key and payload hash, gateway reference, payment engine reference, ledger posting reference and clearing acknowledgement. Comparing the two sets shows whether the duplicate came from a client retry, a gateway redelivery or a missing idempotency check, and each cause has a different fix.\n\nTwo identical credits are two payments until the evidence says otherwise.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCollect the full evidence set for each payment before deciding the cause. A client retry, a gateway redelivery and a missing idempotency check all look the same on a statement, and each one needs a different fix.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Idempotency key and payload hash\n→ Gateway reference\n→ Payment engine reference\n→ Ledger posting reference\n→ Clearing acknowledgement\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was it paid twice?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which submission created the second payment?\"\n\nHave you ever found two identical credits and had to prove whether it was a retry or a redelivery?", "takeaway": "Comparing the two sets of evidence shows whether the duplicate came from a client retry, a gateway redelivery or a missing idempotency check.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Collect evidence, first payment", "note": "Timestamps, idempotency key, hashes and references."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Collect evidence, second payment", "note": "The same evidence set for the second payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare the two sets", "note": "Comparing shows where the duplicate came from."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which cause is it?", "note": "Client retry, gateway redelivery or missing check, each with its own fix."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-050", "seq": 454, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "New channel live, customers charged twice", "explain": "A new corporate payment channel goes live.\nDays later the operations team notices duplicate debits: several corporate customers charged twice for the same instruction, and a few suppliers paid twice.\nThe release passed testing. The duplicates are real.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ New channel released\n→ Duplicate debits noticed across customers\n→ Some suppliers paid twice for one instruction\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The release passed testing, so duplicates cannot happen.\"\n\nTesting passed, but the live path still created duplicates. Contain the failure first by confirming whether duplicates are still being created and, if so, stopping the affected path or routing it through a manual check. Then establish the affected population using stable identifiers rather than guesswork, matching on idempotency key, instruction reference, end-to-end identification, debtor, creditor, amount, currency and value date, and separate confirmed duplicates from suspected ones before remediating.\n\nA duplicate is a control failure, not a display error.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nContain before you diagnose. Stop the path that is still creating duplicates, then build the affected population from stable identifiers, separate confirmed duplicates from suspected ones, and only then remediate.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Confirmed duplicates\n→ Suspected duplicates\n→ Remediation per case\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many duplicates are there?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a payment is a confirmed duplicate, and is the path still creating more?\"\n\nHave you ever had to stop a live channel because it was still creating duplicates while you investigated?", "takeaway": "Establish the affected population using stable identifiers rather than guesswork, and separate confirmed duplicates from suspected ones before remediating.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Contain the failure", "note": "Confirm duplicates are still being created; stop the path."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish the population", "note": "Match on stable identifiers, not guesswork."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Separate confirmed and suspected", "note": "Separate confirmed duplicates from suspected ones."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Remediate confirmed cases first?", "note": "Fix the cause and put right the affected payments."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C042", "seq": 479, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Debited, rejected, and the EUR 20,000 must come back", "explain": "The originator bank deducts EUR 20,000 from the customer for a payment.\nThe payment never reaches the beneficiary, because the clearing system rejected it.\nThe customer sees a deduction with no payment, and the money is sitting with the clearing system.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Originator bank deducts EUR 20,000 from the customer\n→ Clearing system rejects the payment\n→ Clearing system holds the rejected funds\n→ Return travels back to the originator bank\n→ Originator bank credits the customer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A rejected payment returns itself.\"\n\nThe clearing system holds the funds it rejected, so the return starts there and travels back to the originator bank, which credits the customer. A rejection is not a return. Until the return message and the funds travel back, the customer has paid for nothing.\n\nA rejection without a return leaves the customer paying for nothing.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the return, not just the rejection. The funds sit with the clearing system that rejected them, the return starts there, and the customer is whole only when the originator bank credits the account.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Deduction from the customer\n→ Rejection by the clearing system\n→ Funds held by the clearing system\n→ Return to the originator bank\n→ Credit to the customer\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the payment rejected?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the rejected funds have travelled back to the customer?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain to a customer that the payment was rejected, but the money had not come back yet?", "takeaway": "The customer sees a deduction with no payment, and the return is what makes the customer whole.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Originator bank deducts from customer", "note": "The originator bank deducts the amount from the customer."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing system rejects the payment", "note": "The clearing system rejects it, so it never reaches the beneficiary."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing system holds the rejected funds", "note": "The clearing system holds the funds it rejected."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return travels back to originator bank", "note": "The return starts at the clearing system and travels back."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Customer credited yet?", "note": "The originator bank credits the customer, making the customer whole."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C046", "seq": 483, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "One misspelt name in field 50, EUR 30,000 sent back", "explain": "An MT103 for EUR 30,000 is sent to pay a supplier.\nThe ordering customer name in field 50 is misspelt by one letter.\nThe payment comes back. The supplier is unpaid, and the ordering customer cannot understand why one letter stopped EUR 30,000.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Name in field 50 is misspelt\n→ Screening and reconciliation flag the name\n→ MT103 returned to the originator bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A small spelling error cannot stop a payment.\"\n\nThe misspelling matters because screening and reconciliation match on the name, and a name that does not match the account can look like a different party. The return is therefore a data-quality failure, not a funds failure, and it is fixed by correcting the name and resending.\n\nA return over a name is a data failure, not a funds failure.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the name against the account before sending, not after the return. Screening matches on the name, and a name that does not match looks like a different party.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 50 name\n→ Account holder name\n→ Screening match\n→ Reconciliation match\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the money there?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the name in field 50 matches the account holder?\"\n\nHave you ever had a payment returned over a single misspelt letter?", "takeaway": "The return is therefore a data-quality failure, not a funds failure, and it is fixed by correcting the name and resending.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Name in field 50 is misspelt", "note": "The ordering customer name in field 50 does not match the account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Screening and reconciliation flag the name", "note": "A mismatched name looks like a different party to screening."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 returned to originator bank", "note": "The MT103 can be returned to the originator bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Correct and resend, or investigate further?", "note": "The fix is to correct the name and resend."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Name corrected and payment resent", "note": "The payment goes out with the name matching the account."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C053", "seq": 490, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Cancelled, recalled, disputed: three words, three different acts", "explain": "A EUR 40,000 SEPA payment has gone wrong.\nThe team argues about whether to cancel it, recall it, or dispute it, as if the three words meant the same thing.\nThey do not, and picking the wrong one sends the wrong message.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment has gone wrong\n→ Is it settled or not?\n→ Cancellation stops a payment before settlement\n→ Recall requested with camt.056, answered with camt.029\n→ Return made with pacs.004\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Undoing a payment is one action.\"\n\nIn SEPA, a dispute, a cancellation, and a recall are three different actions. A recall is requested with a camt.056 and answered with a camt.029, a return is made with a pacs.004, and a cancellation stops a payment before it settles. The three are often confused because they all undo a payment, but they apply at different stages and they use different messages.\n\nCancellation, recall and return apply at different stages and use different messages.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk first whether the payment has settled. Before settlement, cancel it. After settlement, recall it with camt.056 and wait for the camt.029, and the return itself travels as pacs.004.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cancellation before settlement\n→ Recall with camt.056\n→ Answer with camt.029\n→ Return with pacs.004\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How do we undo it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which stage the payment is at, and which message that stage needs?\"\n\nHave you ever sent a recall for a payment that should have been cancelled, or the other way round?", "takeaway": "The three are often confused because they all undo a payment, but they apply at different stages and they use different messages.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "decision", "title": "Payment already settled or not", "note": "The three actions apply at different stages of the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cancellation stops a payment before settlement", "note": "A cancellation stops a payment before it settles."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Recall requested with camt.056", "note": "A recall is requested with a camt.056 and answered with a camt.029."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return made with pacs.004", "note": "A return is made with a pacs.004."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Match the message to the stage", "note": "The wrong message at the wrong stage undoes nothing."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "camt.029", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C073", "seq": 493, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Five returns in six weeks over one wrong field", "explain": "A payment sent to Australia was returned five times over six weeks.\nEach time the note said the same thing: the beneficiary bank's SWIFT code had to appear in field 57A.\nThe code was right there in the message, in field 72 and in field 52A, where the receiving bank could see it.\nIt still came back, five times.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment sent to Australia\n→ SWIFT code placed in field 72 and 52A\n→ Payment returned, code required in field 57A\n→ Beneficiary bank taken over, code changed\n→ New code used in field 57A, payment applied\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the receiving bank can see the code, the field does not matter.\"\n\nThe code had been placed in field 72 and in field 52A, where the receiving bank could see it, but that did not satisfy the validation: field 57A is the field the receiving bank reads to find the account with institution. The beneficiary bank had also been taken over and its SWIFT code had changed, so the sender had to use the new code in field 57A before the payment could be applied.\n\nThe right value in the wrong field is still wrong.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the code where the receiving bank reads it, and check the code is still current. Field 57A is the account with institution field, and a takeover can change the code without anyone telling the sender.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 57A, account with institution\n→ Field 72, sender to receiver information\n→ Field 52A, ordering institution\n→ Current SWIFT code after takeover\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the code in the message?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the current code sits in the field the receiving bank reads?\"\n\nHave you ever had the same payment returned five times over one field?", "takeaway": "The beneficiary bank had also been taken over and its SWIFT code had changed, so the sender had to use the new code in field 57A before the payment could be applied.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment sent to Australia", "note": "A payment sent to Australia was returned five times over six weeks."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "SWIFT code placed in field 72 and 52A", "note": "The beneficiary bank's SWIFT code was placed in field 72 and in field 52A."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Receiving bank requires code in 57A", "note": "Field 57A is the field the receiving bank reads to find the account with institution."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank taken over, code changed", "note": "The beneficiary bank had been taken over and its SWIFT code had changed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "New code in field 57A?", "note": "The sender had to use the new code in field 57A before the payment could be applied."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "invalid beneficiary data", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-06", "seq": 506, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Credited already? The recall is only a request", "explain": "A EUR 18,000 payment has been credited to the wrong beneficiary.\nThe sender bank wants the money back.\nIt has no power to command it back. It can only ask.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sender bank issues camt.056\n→ Beneficiary bank tests whether the funds are still available\n→ camt.029 answers, accepted or rejected\n→ pacs.004 moves the money only on acceptance\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A recall commands the money back.\"\n\nOnce the beneficiary bank has credited the customer, the sender bank has no power to command the money back; it can only request it. The sender sends a camt.056 cancellation request, the beneficiary bank tests whether the funds are still available and answers with a camt.029, and a pacs.004 moves the money only on acceptance. A rejection leaves the sender with a commercial claim outside the payment rails, so the camt.056 must go out fast, while the funds are still there.\n\nA recall is a request, not a command.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSend the camt.056 fast, while the funds are still there. The beneficiary bank tests availability, answers with camt.029, and the pacs.004 moves the money only on acceptance. A rejection leaves a commercial claim outside the rails.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ camt.056 request\n→ camt.029 answer\n→ pacs.004 return on acceptance\n→ Commercial claim on rejection\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we recall it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds are still available, and how fast did we ask?\"\n\nHave you ever sent a recall a day too late, and found the funds already gone?", "takeaway": "A rejection leaves the sender with a commercial claim outside the payment rails, so the camt.056 must go out fast, while the funds are still there.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Issue the camt.056", "note": "Sender bank requests the money back."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank tests the funds", "note": "Tests availability and any fraud concern."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "The camt.029 answer", "note": "Acceptance moves the money; rejection leaves a commercial claim."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.004 follows acceptance", "note": "Money moves back only on acceptance."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "camt.029", "pacs.004"], "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "SCN-22", "seq": 509, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Duplicate payouts: 90 percent recovered, 10 percent still missing", "explain": "A payout batch fires twice, and suppliers are paid double.\nThe team starts diagnosing the cause while the batch is still running.\nMore duplicates are being created while they investigate, and 10 percent of the duplicated value is still missing.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payout batch fires twice\n→ Duplicates still being created\n→ Recovery at 90 percent, 10 percent still missing\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Diagnose first, contain later.\"\n\nDuplicates demand containment before diagnosis: freeze the retry and payout batch so no more duplicates are created while the team investigates. Then identify the full population by reference or idempotency key, reverse the duplicates systematically, and track recovery until every case is closed. The cause here was a retry firing twice after a timeout through an idempotency gap, and the permanent fix is idempotency keys on every payout plus real-time reconciliation flagging double credits.\n\nContain first. A duplicate still being created is worse than one already made.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFreeze the retry and the payout batch first. Then identify the full population by reference or idempotency key, reverse systematically, track recovery to closure, and fix the idempotency gap so it cannot fire twice again.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Freeze the batch\n→ Full population by reference or idempotency key\n→ Systematic reversal\n→ Recovery tracked to closure\n→ Idempotency fix\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What caused it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves no more duplicates are being created, and every case is closed?\"\n\nHave you ever investigated a duplicate while the batch was still creating more of them?", "takeaway": "The cause here was a retry firing twice after a timeout through an idempotency gap, and the permanent fix is idempotency keys on every payout plus real-time reconciliation flagging double credits.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Freeze the retry and payout batch", "note": "Contain the incident before investigating."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Identify the full population", "note": "Match on reference or idempotency key."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reverse the duplicates", "note": "Reverse systematically; track recovery to closure."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the idempotency gap", "note": "Retry fired twice after a timeout, through an idempotency gap."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Gate the release?", "note": "Failure-injection test every retry-logic change first."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-30", "seq": 511, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "GBP 125,000 left. No trace, no rejection, no return.", "explain": "A 125,000 pound international payment left the sender.\nIt never arrived.\nNo rejection, no return, no confirmation. The beneficiary is waiting, the sender is asking, and the payment has vanished from every screen that matters.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment leaves the sender\n→ No arrival, no rejection, no return\n→ Beneficiary waiting, sender asking\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No news means it failed. Send it again.\"\n\nBefore acting, prove what happened: pull the UETR, check the tracker for the last confirmed hop, and ask whether the money sits with an intermediary, in a compliance review, or stuck in a queue. Never re-send until the original is accounted for, because a duplicate is worse than a delay, and keep both sender and expectant beneficiary updated.\n\nNever re-send until the original is accounted for. A duplicate is worse than a delay.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nProve first. Pull the UETR, find the last confirmed hop, and ask the three questions: intermediary, compliance review, or queue. Contact the intermediary with structured references, never a vague question, and update both sides while the uncertainty lasts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR and last confirmed hop\n→ Intermediary hold\n→ Compliance review\n→ Queue\n→ Structured enquiry\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did it fail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the original payment is right now?\"\n\nHave you ever had GBP 125,000 vanish with no trace, and had to prove what happened before anyone would act?", "takeaway": "Never re-send until the original is accounted for, because a duplicate is worse than a delay, and keep both sender and expectant beneficiary updated.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Prove what happened first", "note": "Failed outright, or not yet proven."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Pull the UETR and last hop", "note": "Tracker shows the last confirmed hop."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ask the three questions", "note": "Intermediary, compliance review or queue."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Contact the intermediary", "note": "Structured references, never a vague question."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Re-send or wait?", "note": "Never re-send until the original is accounted for."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E02", "seq": 519, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "pacs.004 arrived. The debtor is still waiting.", "explain": "A pacs.004 arrives, and the operations team credits the debtor at once.\nThe EUR 85,000 payment was a cover payment, a pacs.009, and the pacs.008 instruction was never rejected.\nThe debtor has been credited for a payment that was never returned, and the original instruction is still live.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.004 arrives\n→ Team credits the debtor at once\n→ Return relates to a rejected pacs.009 cover\n→ pacs.008 instruction never rejected\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A return means credit the debtor.\"\n\nA pacs.004 only says a payment was returned, so crediting the debtor on receipt is an event-to-action shortcut that skips the questions that matter: what the return is for, who initiated it, whether the original was a pacs.008 or a pacs.009, why it was returned, and what happened to the original payment. When the pacs.004 relates to a rejected pacs.009 cover payment, the related pacs.008 cannot be assumed rejected, since the cover may have failed for lack of correspondent funds, wrong beneficiary correspondent or bank details, or a cover rejection for incorrect bank information.\n\nA pacs.004 only says a payment was returned. It does not decide the debtor credit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nInvestigate before crediting. Establish what the return is for, who initiated it, whether the original was a pacs.008 or a pacs.009, why it was returned, and the status of the original payment and any open exception. Only then choose between re-initiating the pacs.009, returning funds to the debtor, re-initiating the pacs.008, or another defined action.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What the return is for\n→ Who initiated it\n→ Original pacs.008 or pacs.009\n→ Why it was returned\n→ Status of the original payment\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did a pacs.004 arrive?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what the return relates to, and what should happen to the original?\"\n\nHave you ever credited a debtor on a pacs.004, then found the original payment was never returned?", "takeaway": "A pacs.004 only says a payment was returned, so crediting the debtor on receipt is an event-to-action shortcut that skips the questions that matter.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.004 arrives", "note": "A return is received; the reason and the original transaction are not yet known."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Original pacs.008 or pacs.009?", "note": "Identify which transaction the return relates to and who initiated it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigate the rejection", "note": "Establish why the cover was rejected and the current status of the pacs.008."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Choose the business outcome", "note": "Re-initiate the cover, return funds, re-initiate the instruction, or take another defined action."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.004", "pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-006", "seq": 529, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Cancelling a SEPA payment: the right message each time", "explain": "A customer asks to cancel a EUR 12,000 SEPA payment, and the team reaches for camt.056.\nThe payment is a direct debit, not a credit transfer.\nThe camt.056 goes nowhere, and the debit is collected anyway.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer asks to cancel a SEPA payment\n→ Is it a credit transfer, a direct debit, or a pain.008?\n→ Wrong message sent, payment proceeds\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One cancellation message fits every SEPA payment.\"\n\nIn the SEPA Credit Transfer scheme camt.056 is the message used to request cancellation of an SCT transaction. For SDD Core reversals the creditor bank sends pacs.007 against funds mistakenly debited. Corporations may also send camt.055 through their bank to cancel a pain.008: an unexecuted transaction is cancelled, while an executed one is reversed by the bank with pacs.007 instead.\n\nThe message depends on the scheme and on whether the payment has executed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk two questions before sending anything: which scheme, and has the payment executed? SCT takes camt.056. SDD Core takes pacs.007. A pain.008 takes camt.055, and an executed one is reversed by the bank with pacs.007 instead.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ SCT, camt.056\n→ SDD Core, pacs.007\n→ pain.008, camt.055\n→ Executed or not\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How do we cancel it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which scheme it is, and whether it has executed?\"\n\nHave you ever sent the right cancellation message to the wrong kind of payment?", "takeaway": "Corporations may also send camt.055 through their bank to cancel a pain.008: an unexecuted transaction is cancelled, while an executed one is reversed by the bank with pacs.007 instead.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "camt.056 cancels an SCT transaction", "note": "In SEPA, the camt.056 is supported in the SEPA Credit Transfer scheme as a request to cancel an SCT transaction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.007 reverses a mistaken SDD debit", "note": "In SDD Core, the creditor bank sends a pacs.007 to reverse funds that were mistakenly debited."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has the pain.008 transaction been executed?", "note": "Corporations send a camt.055 to cancel a pain.008; the outcome depends on whether it has been executed yet."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Not executed: it is cancelled", "note": "If the transaction has not been executed yet it is cancelled."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Executed: pacs.007 reverses the funds", "note": "If it has been executed, the bank sends a pacs.007 to reverse the funds instead."}]}}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "camt.055", "pacs.007", "pain.008"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"explain": "EUR 25,000 was sent to a new beneficiary bank for the first time.\nThe customer was told it was sent.\nThree days later, nothing had arrived.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ Receiving bank system refuses the message\n→ No RMA exists for this sender\n→ Sender receives no credit advice\n→ Customer waits for money that never left\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Send the MT103. The relationship paperwork can follow.\"\n\nSo the payment went out before anyone checked whether the receiving bank was authorised to accept it.\n\nWhy?\n\nBefore one bank can send an MT103 to another over SWIFT, the receiving bank must set up an RMA, a relationship management authorisation, for that sender. Without an RMA the receiving bank system will not accept the message at all.\n\nNo RMA, no message. No message, no payment.\n\nAn RMA is a precondition, not a formality to sort out afterwards.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAn RMA is a precondition for the payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ RMA in place for the receiving bank\n→ MT103 sent and accepted\n→ Credit advice received\n\nCheck the RMA before the first payment to any new bank, and confirm it again after a merger or a BIC change.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we send the MT103?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the receiving bank can accept our message?\"\n\nHave you seen a first payment fail because nobody checked the RMA?", "id": "ADD-002", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 531, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "An RMA is a precondition, not paperwork to finish later. No RMA, no message, no payment.", "title": "First payment to a new bank, MT103 never left", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "MT103 rejected: no RMA", "parts": [{"label": "Sender bank", "note": "Sends the MT103 in good faith"}, {"label": "RMA check", "note": "Receiving bank never authorised this sender"}, {"label": "Receiving bank", "note": "System refuses the message outright"}, {"label": "No credit advice", "note": "Sender has no proof the payment landed"}, {"label": "Customer waiting", "note": "Told the money was sent, three days ago"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "EUR 12,000 was sent to pay a supplier invoice.\nThe IBAN was correct.\nThe money sat in suspense for a week.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent with field 59 showing IBAN and address\n→ No beneficiary name anywhere in field 59\n→ Beneficiary bank cannot confirm who the account belongs to\n→ Payment held in suspense\n→ Sender bank asked to supply the missing name\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"The IBAN is enough. The name is a nicety.\"\n\nSo the payment carried an account number and no identity, and the receiving bank had to choose between guessing and holding.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 59 is meant to identify the beneficiary. An MT103 can arrive showing only the IBAN and address, with no name at all, and then the beneficiary bank cannot confirm that the account belongs to the person the payment was meant for. It holds the payment and asks for confirmation instead of crediting it.\n\nAn account number is not an identity.\n\nA correct IBAN with no name is a payment the bank cannot safely apply.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nField 59 must name the person the money is for.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ IBAN valid and complete\n→ Beneficiary name present in field 59\n→ Name matched to the account before credit\n\nValidate the name at instruction, not after the money is stuck.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the IBAN right?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this account belongs to the intended beneficiary?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment held because the name was missing?", "id": "ADD-003", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 532, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Field 59 must name the person the money is for. An account number is not an identity.", "title": "IBAN correct, no name, EUR 12,000 in suspense", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Field 59 carries IBAN and address only", "title": "MT103 sent"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no name, the bank cannot check at all", "title": "Name matches the account?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Funds sit in suspense, unapplied", "title": "Payment held"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Sender bank must supply the missing name", "title": "Name requested"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Only the name unlocks the payment", "title": "Credit after proof"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "EUR 48,000 was paid against a supplier invoice.\nThe supplier was credited in full.\nThe supplier still chased the payment as unpaid.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent with field 70 carrying a 24-character reference\n→ The /RFB/ reference is cut at 16 characters\n→ Beneficiary credited the full EUR 48,000\n→ Supplier reconciliation cannot match the truncated reference\n→ Funds sit unapplied while the invoice shows overdue\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Put the full reference in field 70. The system will carry it.\"\n\nSo a reference longer than the field allows was sent anyway, and the part that identified the invoice never arrived.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 70 carries remittance information, and the reference that follows /RFB/ is limited to 16 characters. A longer description must continue on the following lines of the field instead of stretching the reference, and a field 70 that repeats a code or overruns the limit is not a valid instruction.\n\nCredited is not reconciled.\n\nThe money arrived. The meaning of the money did not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA reference the receiver cannot read is a payment the supplier cannot find.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reference fits the 16-character limit\n→ Remittance detail continues on the next lines\n→ Beneficiary confirms the invoice is matched\n\nValidate reference length at instruction, before the MT103 leaves.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the money arrive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the receiver matched it to the right invoice?\"\n\nHave you seen a paid invoice chased as unpaid because the reference was cut?", "id": "ADD-004", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 533, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Credited is not reconciled. A reference the receiver cannot read is a payment the supplier cannot find.", "title": "Reference cut at 16 characters, invoice never matched", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "EUR 48,000 credited, invoice unmatched", "parts": [{"label": "Field 70 sent", "note": "A 24-character reference after /RFB/"}, {"label": "Cut at 16 characters", "note": "The identifying tail never arrives"}, {"label": "Beneficiary credited", "note": "Full amount lands on the account"}, {"label": "Reconciliation fails", "note": "Supplier cannot match it to any invoice"}, {"label": "Invoice shows overdue", "note": "Paid money chased as unpaid"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "EUR 60,000 was sent to a supplier through the usual corridor.\nThe payment was rejected downstream.\nThe intermediary named in the instruction had stopped serving that corridor months earlier.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment instructed with Intermediary Agent 2 named\n→ Routing trusts the stored intermediary data\n→ Intermediary cannot serve the currency or the corridor\n→ Payment rejected downstream\n→ Repair starts, with no owner to fix the data\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Reference data looks after itself.\"\n\nSo nobody owned the intermediary data, nobody validated it, and the same dead bank kept being named until a rejection exposed it.\n\nWhy?\n\nReference data without an owner decays. Intermediary Agent 2 quietly points payments at a bank that no longer serves the corridor, and the failure is unreadable at first, because message mapping, routing reference data and routing logic all look equally guilty.\n\nReference data without an owner decays.\n\nThe payment was right. The map it followed was out of date.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEvery routing field needs a named owner and a validation rule.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Intermediary owner named\n→ Validation rule against the corridor\n→ Update process when a bank exits a corridor\n→ Routing control that stops a stale intermediary\n\nLeave any of the four out and the same bad intermediary keeps reappearing.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Who do we route through?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this intermediary still serves this corridor?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment die on an intermediary that had already left?", "id": "ADD-005", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 534, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Reference data without an owner decays. Every routing field needs a named owner and a validation rule.", "title": "The intermediary quit the corridor, nobody told routing", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Intermediary Agent 2 taken from stored data", "title": "Payment instructed"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no owner checks, nobody knows it left", "title": "Intermediary valid here?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Bank cannot serve this currency or corridor", "title": "Rejected downstream"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Message, mapping and routing all look guilty", "title": "Repair begins"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Only the rejection forced an update", "title": "Data fixed, at last"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "EUR 90,000 was sent to settle a trade.\nThe purpose code field was left blank.\nThe beneficiary bank held the payment for compliance, and the trade could not settle.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer sends the payment without a purpose code\n→ Payment travels the chain without it\n→ Beneficiary bank compliance needs the code\n→ Payment held pending the missing detail\n→ Trade settlement waits on a blank field\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"The customer can leave it blank. Someone downstream will fill it in.\"\n\nSo a code that was optional at the front and mandatory downstream had no owner, no enrichment source and no early validation.\n\nWhy?\n\nA purpose code that is optional at the customer and mandatory downstream is a defect waiting for a payment. Enrichment needs a named owning source, and validation must happen early enough to catch a missing code before the payment reaches the system that needs it.\n\nOptional at the front is not optional downstream.\n\nThe payment was complete at the front. It was incomplete where it mattered.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the system that needs the code, and validate before the payment travels.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Downstream system that needs the code, named\n→ Enrichment owning source, named\n→ Early validation before the payment travels\n→ Repair behaviour, who fixes it and how fast\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the customer leave it blank?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every downstream system has what it needs?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment held for a field nobody owned?", "id": "ADD-007", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 536, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Optional at the front is not optional downstream. Name the system that needs the code, and validate early.", "title": "Purpose code missing, EUR 90,000 held at the border", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Purpose code left blank by the customer", "title": "Payment sent"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no owner enriches it, the gap travels on", "title": "Purpose code present?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Compliance needs the code to release it", "title": "Held at beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "No one was named to fix it, or how fast", "title": "Repair starts late"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Settlement stalls on a blank field", "title": "Trade waits"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch16-cross-border-exceptions.html", "label": "Chapter 16: Cross-Border Exceptions", "chapter": "ch16", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "A supplier payment went out at 2am by Faster Payments.\nIt was meant to arrive in seconds.\nIt arrived in the morning.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment sent at 2am by Faster Payments\n→ Faster Payments runs around the clock\n→ Sending bank hits its liquidity limit\n→ Payment queues until liquidity returns\n→ Supplier paid in the morning, not in seconds\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Faster Payments runs around the clock, so it always arrives in seconds.\"\n\nSo the team chose the rail for speed and never checked the liquidity behind it.\n\nWhy?\n\nFaster Payments runs around the clock, but with liquidity limits. The scheme is always open, and the bank still needs the funds to settle. An urgent payment at 2am is fast only if the sending bank holds the liquidity to back it.\n\nAround the clock is the scheme. Liquidity is the bank.\n\nThe rail was open all night. The money was not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAn urgent payment needs liquidity checked, not just the rail chosen.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Rail availability, around the clock\n→ Sender liquidity, checked before sending\n→ Queued payment, watched until release\n→ Supplier promise, matched to the real arrival time\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which rail is fastest?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds are there to settle this payment now?\"\n\nHave you seen an instant payment wait until morning?", "id": "ADD-011", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 540, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Around the clock is the scheme. Liquidity is the bank. Check liquidity, not just the rail.", "title": "Faster Payments at 2am, queued till morning", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "2am payment, morning arrival", "parts": [{"label": "Sent at 2am", "note": "Faster Payments is open around the clock"}, {"label": "Liquidity limit", "note": "Sending bank has hit its limit"}, {"label": "Payment queues", "note": "Waits for liquidity to return"}, {"label": "Released in the morning", "note": "Arrives hours after it was promised"}, {"label": "Supplier waits", "note": "An urgent payment that was not urgent"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A subscription collection was presented after three quiet years.\nIt was returned.\nNo message warned anyone it would happen.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Mandate signed, collections run for a while\n→ Customer pauses the service, no orders for 36 months\n→ Mandate lapses silently\n→ New collection presented on the lapsed mandate\n→ Collection returned, service cut off\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"The mandate is on file. It stays valid.\"\n\nSo nobody tracked the last use of the mandate, and the first anyone knew it had expired was the return.\n\nWhy?\n\nA SEPA mandate expires after 36 months with no orders related to it. Expiry triggers no message to any party. The mandate simply lapses quietly after three years without use.\n\nA mandate lapses quietly. Nobody is told.\n\nThe file still held the mandate. The scheme no longer recognised it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA mandate is a lifecycle. Track its last use or lose the collection.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Mandate last use date, tracked\n→ Expiry at 36 months, watched\n→ Renewal before the next collection\n→ Customer told before the collection fails\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do we hold a mandate?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this mandate is still alive on the collection date?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection fail on a mandate that expired in silence?", "id": "ADD-012", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 541, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "A mandate lapses quietly. Nobody is told. Track its last use or lose the collection.", "title": "Mandate silent for 36 months, the collection bounced", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Collections run, then stop for years", "title": "Mandate signed"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "No orders, no message, no warning", "title": "36 months pass"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no one tracks it, no one knows", "title": "Mandate still alive?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "On a mandate that has already lapsed", "title": "Collection presented"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Service cut off, customer surprised", "title": "Collection returned"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A creditor changed the collection amount for the month.\nThe pre-notification still showed the old amount.\nThe debtor disputed the collection, and it was returned.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Collection amount changes for the month\n→ Pre-notification sent with the old amount\n→ Collection presented at the new amount\n→ Debtor sees a different amount from the notice\n→ Debtor disputes, collection returned\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Pre-notification is a formality. The file carries the real amount.\"\n\nSo the notice and the file drifted apart, and the debtor was asked to pay an amount nobody had told them about.\n\nWhy?\n\nPre-notification is the notice the creditor sends the debtor before the collection, stating the amount and the date, while collection submission is a separate step. Pre-notification is not collection submission, and if the notice is not updated, the debtor can dispute the collection. The notice and the file must stay in step.\n\nThe notice is the agreement. The file is just the execution.\n\nThe amount in the file was right. The amount in the notice was wrong, and the notice is what the debtor trusts.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen the amount changes, the notice changes first.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount and date in the notice\n→ Amount and date in the collection file\n→ Check that notice and file agree before submission\n→ Dispute path when they do not agree\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the file correct?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the debtor was told the amount they are about to pay?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection disputed because the notice was out of date?", "id": "ADD-013", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 542, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "The notice is the agreement. When the amount changes, the notice changes first.", "title": "Amount changed, notice not updated, debtor disputed", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "New amount agreed for the month", "title": "Amount changes"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the debtor will see a surprise", "title": "Notice matches the file?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Debtor told the previous amount", "title": "Old notice sent"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "New amount, old notice", "title": "Collection presented"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Collection returned, service interrupted", "title": "Debtor disputes"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A supplier payment was promised with value today.\nIt arrived with value tomorrow.\nThe supplier charged a late fee.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment instruction complete and accepted\n→ Bank collects orders until the FX cut-off\n→ FX trade happens after the cut-off\n→ Tenor counts from the next day, not today\n→ Value date slips a day, late fee follows\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"The instruction is in. The value date is today.\"\n\nSo the team promised the value date from the instruction time, and never checked the trade time.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn FX tenor such as Today, Tomorrow or Spot counts from the day the deal is traded, provided the trade happens before the FX cut-off time. Traded today before the cut-off, a Today tenor settles today, a Tomorrow tenor settles tomorrow, and Spot settles in two days or more. A trade after the cut-off settles a day later, because once the cut-off passes the bank is already working on the next day.\n\nThe cut-off decides the value date, not the instruction.\n\nThe instruction was on time. The trade was not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrade before the cut-off or re-promise the value date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction time\n→ FX trade time against the cut-off\n→ Tenor counted from the trade day\n→ Value date promised to the customer\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When was the payment instructed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the trade happened before the cut-off?\"\n\nHave you seen a value date slip because the trade missed the cut-off?", "id": "ADD-017", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 546, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The cut-off decides the value date, not the instruction. Trade before the cut-off or re-promise the date.", "title": "FX traded after cut-off, value date slipped a day", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Payment complete, value today promised", "title": "Instruction accepted"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the tenor counts from tomorrow", "title": "Traded before cut-off?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Bank is already working on the next day", "title": "Trade after cut-off"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Today becomes tomorrow", "title": "Value date slips"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Supplier bills for the missed date", "title": "Late fee charged"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch15-charges-fx-and-gpi-tracking.html", "label": "Chapter 15: Charges, FX and gpi Tracking", "chapter": "ch15", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "The MT940 statements arrived every day.\nThe sequence ran 12, 13, 15.\nInside the missing statement 14 sat a credit nobody noticed was gone for days.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT940 statements arrive daily\n→ Field 28C checked on each statement\n→ Sequence shows 12, 13, then 15\n→ Statement 14 is missing, and no one asks for it\n→ A credit inside statement 14 stays unreconciled for days\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Reconcile the transactions. The statement numbers look after themselves.\"\n\nSo the team reconciled what arrived and never noticed what did not arrive.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 28C carries the statement number and the sequence number, pinning down exactly which statement this is and where it sits in the run. A missing statement, or one arriving out of order, shows as a gap in the 28C sequence before any transaction is examined. That makes 28C the first completeness check on every MT940 received.\n\nA missing statement is found in the numbering, not in the money.\n\nThe transactions all reconciled. The books were still incomplete.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck field 28C first. A gap in the sequence is a gap in the books.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Statement number in field 28C\n→ Sequence number in field 28C\n→ Gap chased on the day it appears\n→ Transactions reconciled only on a complete run\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do the transactions reconcile?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every statement in the run arrived?\"\n\nHave you seen a missing statement hide a missing credit?", "id": "ADD-018", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 547, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "A missing statement is found in the numbering, not in the money. Check field 28C first.", "title": "Statement 14 never arrived, the missing credit hid", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "28C: 12, 13, 15. Where is 14?", "parts": [{"label": "Statement 12", "note": "Arrived and reconciled"}, {"label": "Statement 13", "note": "Arrived and reconciled"}, {"label": "Statement 14", "note": "Never arrived, never chased"}, {"label": "Statement 15", "note": "Arrived, hiding the gap before it"}, {"label": "Credit inside 14", "note": "Unreconciled for days, unseen"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch49-standby-letter-of-credit.html", "label": "Chapter 49: Standby Letter of Credit", "chapter": "ch49", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A cardholder disputed a EUR 4,800 transaction.\nThe merchant had delivery proof.\nThe proof arrived a day after the scheme deadline, and the merchant bore the loss.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Cardholder disputes the transaction\n→ Issuer raises the chargeback with the scheme\n→ Merchant gathers delivery evidence\n→ Evidence arrives after the scheme deadline\n→ Loss stands against the merchant\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Good evidence wins. The deadline is flexible.\"\n\nSo the merchant built the perfect case and missed the only date that decided it.\n\nWhy?\n\nA chargeback starts when a cardholder disputes a transaction and the issuer raises it with the scheme, and the acquirer and merchant respond with representment evidence while the scheme applies its timelines and reason codes. The financial impact moves between issuer, acquirer and merchant as the case progresses, so knowing the reason code matters: it decides what evidence is needed and who bears the loss at each stage.\n\nThe reason code is the case, not a label on it.\n\nThe evidence was perfect. It was also late, and late evidence does not exist.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRepresentment evidence has a deadline. Miss it and the evidence does not exist.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason code, read first\n→ Evidence the code demands, gathered fast\n→ Scheme deadline, tracked as the real deadline\n→ Loss owner at each stage, known in advance\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do we have good evidence?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves our case inside the scheme deadline?\"\n\nHave you seen a winnable chargeback lost to a deadline?", "id": "ADD-019", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 548, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Representment evidence has a deadline. Miss it and the evidence does not exist.", "title": "Evidence a day late, EUR 4,800 lost to chargeback", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Cardholder disputes a EUR 4,800 transaction", "title": "Dispute raised"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Issuer raises it with the scheme", "title": "Chargeback opened"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the case is lost whatever it proves", "title": "Evidence inside deadline?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Delivery evidence, one day too late", "title": "Proof arrives late"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Good evidence, wrong date", "title": "Merchant bears loss"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch51-documentary-collections-and-swift-category-4.html", "label": "Chapter 51: Documentary Collections and SWIFT Category 4", "chapter": "ch51", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A corporate submitted a pain.001 file with ten payments.\nThe file timed out with no confirmation.\nThe file was re-sent whole, and three payments were paid twice.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 submitted with ten payments\n→ Seven become pacs.008 to the next bank\n→ Three are on-us, posted internally at once\n→ Timeout, no confirmation received\n→ Whole file re-sent, three on-us payments posted again\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"No confirmation means nothing happened. Re-send the file.\"\n\nSo the retry treated an unknown as a failure, and the payments that never become pacs.008 were paid a second time.\n\nWhy?\n\nA pain.001 file promises a batch of payments, yet not every payment in the file becomes a pacs.008. When a corporate submits ten payments and only seven pacs.008 messages reach the next bank, the other three are on-us payments, where sender and beneficiary hold accounts with the same bank and the payment is handled internally. Counting only pacs.008 messages shows seven payments and suggests three are missing, while the three on-us payments were already paid. A timeout is an unknown, not a failure, and a blind retry can create a duplicate.\n\nA timeout is an unknown, not a failure.\n\nSeven payments could be traced by message. Three could not, and those three were paid twice.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFind the outcome first. Never retry a file blind.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ File total, ten payments promised\n→ pacs.008 count, seven sent onward\n→ On-us count, three handled internally\n→ Outcome known before any retry\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast can we re-send?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which payments already settled before we send anything again?\"\n\nHave you seen a blind retry pay the same payment twice?", "id": "ADD-020", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 549, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A timeout is an unknown, not a failure. Find the outcome first. Never retry a file blind.", "title": "File retried after timeout, three on-us paid twice", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Ten payments promised in one file", "title": "pain.001 sent"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Seven pacs.008 reach the next bank", "title": "Seven go onward"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Same bank, handled internally at once", "title": "Three post on-us"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, a retry is a guess, not a fix", "title": "Outcome known?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Three on-us payments posted twice", "title": "File re-sent whole"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch10-us-fedwire-fednow-ach-chips.html", "label": "Chapter 10: US: Fedwire, FedNow, ACH, CHIPS", "chapter": "ch10", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"explain": "A customer paid USD 40,000 to an overseas supplier. The supplier's bank confirmed it had received funds. The supplier was never credited.\n\nThe money had landed in the beneficiary bank's own account, booked as the bank's own funds.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent to the beneficiary bank with the customer instruction\n→ MT202 COV sent to the funding bank to settle the funds\n→ Beneficiary bank reads the cover as a plain bank-to-bank transfer\n→ Cover booked as proprietary funds, MT103 left unmatched\n→ Supplier unpaid, nobody can see why\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"An MT202 is an MT202. Book it as bank funds.\"\n\nSo the cover was processed without ever looking for its MT103.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT202 COV exists to settle one specific MT103. It carries the reference that binds the two messages together. A plain MT202 is a bank's own transfer, moving proprietary funds with no customer payment behind it. Both move money between banks, but only the COV answers to a customer instruction.\n\nA cover without its MT103 is just money with no owner.\n\nThe beneficiary bank must recognise the cover by its reference and match it to the MT103 before booking anything. Without that link, the cover looks exactly like an ordinary bank-to-bank transfer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA COV is not a plain MT202. The reference is what makes it a cover.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover-to-MT103 matching\n→ Reference check on every MT202 COV\n→ Booking of bank funds versus customer funds\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we book an MT202?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this cover belongs to that MT103?\"\n\nHave you ever seen funds booked to the wrong account because a cover was read as a plain transfer?", "id": "XBR-002", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 551, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "A cover without its MT103 is just money with no owner.", "title": "USD 40,000 cover booked as the bank's own money, supplier never paid", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Customer instruction reaches the beneficiary bank", "title": "MT103 arrives"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Funds leg arrives separately through the correspondent", "title": "MT202 COV arrives"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Does the reference bind this cover to the MT103?", "title": "Match the two legs"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Cover booked as the bank's own funds, MT103 left unmatched", "title": "Booked wrong"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Hold booking until the reference matches the MT103", "title": "Fix the control"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "An MT103 announced EUR 30,000. The MT202 COV that arrived to settle it carried EUR 29,965. The beneficiary bank credited nobody and raised an exception.\n\nEUR 35 was missing, and the whole payment stopped.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent announcing EUR 30,000\n→ MT202 COV arrives for EUR 29,965\n→ Reference matches, amount does not\n→ Sequence B of the cover does not mirror the MT103\n→ Beneficiary bank holds both legs and investigates\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Match on the reference. Small amount differences can be adjusted later.\"\n\nSo a EUR 35 gap was treated as a rounding problem instead of a broken link.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 COV must agree on amount and on the references that link them. A mismatch is not a minor data error. It is a break in the link between the information leg and the funds leg, and the bank must investigate before it credits the beneficiary. Sequence B of the cover must mirror the MT103 exactly wherever a field is provided, or the field must be left empty. Some banks reject a cover whose sequence B does not match the announcement, even when the amounts agree.\n\nTwo legs, one truth. If they disagree, nothing moves.\n\nThe EUR 35 was a charge taken from the cover somewhere in the chain, which is itself forbidden. The mismatch was the symptom, not the cause.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe MT103 and the cover must agree, or the payment stops.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount match between MT103 and cover\n→ Reference match between MT103 and cover\n→ Sequence B mirror check\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the difference small enough to ignore?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the two legs describe the same payment?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment stall over a difference so small nobody wanted to own it?", "id": "XBR-004", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 553, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Two legs, one truth. If they disagree, nothing moves.", "title": "EUR 30,000 announced, EUR 29,965 arrived, nobody got paid", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "MT103 announces EUR 30,000 to the beneficiary bank", "title": "MT103 sent"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Cover arrives EUR 35 short of the announced amount", "title": "Cover arrives short"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Do amount, reference and sequence B agree?", "title": "Compare the two legs"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Mismatch stops settlement, exception raised", "title": "Payment held"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Trace the EUR 35, then re-send a matching cover", "title": "Investigate and fix"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A cover payment of USD 10,000 was announced. The cover that arrived was USD 9,965. The beneficiary bank refused to credit the full amount, and the USD 35 gap turned into a two-week dispute.\n\nAn intermediary had taken its charge straight out of the cover.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 announces USD 10,000\n→ MT202 COV sent for USD 10,000\n→ Intermediary deducts USD 35 from the cover\n→ Cover arrives at USD 9,965\n→ Beneficiary bank holds the payment as an exception\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Intermediaries may deduct their charges from the funds they handle.\"\n\nSo the intermediary treated the cover like a serial payment and helped itself.\n\nWhy?\n\nIt is forbidden to take charges from an MT202 COV. The cover amount must never be lower than the amount announced in the MT103. If an intermediary needs to be paid for its part in the chain, it asks the sending bank to pay through an MT191, or debits the account directly under the agreement between the banks. A cover that arrives short of the announced amount is an exception, not a normal deduction.\n\nNever take charges from the cover. The cover is the full amount, or it is an exception.\n\nThe MT190 and MT191 messages exist precisely for this charges leg between banks, so the payment itself is never touched.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCharges never come out of a cover payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover amount versus announced amount\n→ Charges claimed through MT191\n→ Intermediary agreements on direct debits\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How does the intermediary get paid?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cover arrived in full before we credit the beneficiary?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a shortfall that turned out to be a charge taken from the wrong place?", "id": "XBR-005", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 554, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Never take charges from the cover. The cover is the full amount, or it is an exception.", "title": "USD 10,000 cover arrived USD 35 short, and the payment stopped for two weeks", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "Charges must never come out of the cover", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 announces USD 10,000", "note": "The amount the beneficiary must receive"}, {"label": "Cover sent in full", "note": "MT202 COV for USD 10,000"}, {"label": "USD 35 taken", "note": "Intermediary deducts its charge from the cover"}, {"label": "Cover arrives short", "note": "USD 9,965 breaks the link to the MT103"}, {"label": "MT191 is the route", "note": "Charges are claimed separately, never from the funds"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "A customer sent EUR 8,000 to the wrong beneficiary by mistake. The recall went out the same day. The beneficiary had already spent the money.\n\nThe bank sent the recall, received a polite answer, and the funds never came back.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent, EUR 8,000 credited to the wrong beneficiary\n→ Beneficiary spends the funds the next day\n→ camt.056 recall sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ Beneficiary bank asks its customer whether it agrees\n→ Customer refuses, no pacs.004 returns\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If a payment went to the wrong person, recall it and the money comes back.\"\n\nSo the customer was promised a reversal that no message can deliver.\n\nWhy?\n\nA camt.056 can still be sent to ask the beneficiary bank to return the money, but the beneficiary bank must ask its customer whether it agrees. If the funds are no longer there, the recall cannot be completed by message alone, and the customer must take action against the beneficiary. A recall is a chain of messages, not a single message: the originator bank sends camt.056 to the intermediary, which forwards it to the beneficiary bank, and the funds come back only when every bank in the chain acts.\n\nOnce the money is spent, a recall is a request, not a reversal.\n\nThe camt.029 answer closes the recall question by stating whether the recall was accepted or refused, but even an accepted recall does not move money. Only the pacs.004 return does that.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA recall asks. Only a return pays.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Recall request status\n→ Beneficiary consent and funds availability\n→ Return (pacs.004) status\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we send the recall?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds are still there and the return is on its way?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer that a recall cannot bring spent money back?", "id": "XBR-009", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 558, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Once the money is spent, a recall is a request, not a reversal.", "title": "EUR 8,000 recalled the same day, but the beneficiary had already spent it", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "A recall asks, only a return pays", "parts": [{"label": "pacs.008 credited", "note": "EUR 8,000 lands with the wrong beneficiary"}, {"label": "Funds spent", "note": "Money gone before the recall arrives"}, {"label": "camt.056 sent", "note": "Recall travels the chain to the beneficiary bank"}, {"label": "Consent asked", "note": "Beneficiary bank must ask its customer"}, {"label": "No pacs.004", "note": "No funds, no return, recall cannot complete"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A payment of USD 15,000 was returned because the beneficiary account did not exist. The customer waited a week, then asked where the refund was.\n\nThe return was still travelling. It had to pass through every bank that carried the payment forward.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent for USD 15,000\n→ Beneficiary bank cannot apply it, account does not exist\n→ pacs.004 sent back to the intermediary\n→ Intermediary forwards a new pacs.004 to the originator bank\n→ Originator bank credits the customer, minus charges already deducted\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"When a payment is returned, credit the customer the full amount straight away.\"\n\nSo the customer was promised USD 15,000 while the return was still two banks away.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a beneficiary bank returns a payment with a pacs.004, the intermediary that received it sends a new pacs.004 back to the originator bank, so the return travels the chain in reverse. The intermediary never keeps the return. It forwards it with the reference to the original payment so the originator bank can match it. A return is not a new payment. It is the original payment travelling backwards.\n\nThe charges on the return follow fields 71A and 71F, so the amount that comes back is the amount sent minus the charges already deducted. The customer is not made whole for charges the chain already took.\n\nA return is the same payment travelling backwards, not a new payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA return follows the chain home, step by step.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return reference to the original payment\n→ Forwarding at each bank in the chain\n→ Refund amount after charges already deducted\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we credit the customer?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the return has arrived and been reconciled at each step?\"\n\nHave you ever waited on a refund that was still travelling backwards through the chain?", "id": "XBR-010", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 559, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "A return is the same payment travelling backwards, not a new payment.", "title": "USD 15,000 returned for a bad account, and the refund took the long way home", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Payment cannot be applied, account does not exist", "title": "Return started"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Beneficiary bank sends pacs.004 to the intermediary", "title": "First leg back"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Intermediary forwards a new pacs.004 onward", "title": "Forwarded in reverse"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Originator bank matches the return by its reference", "title": "Matched to original"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Is the refund the sent amount minus charges already deducted?", "title": "Credit the customer"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A payment to Australia was returned five times over six weeks. Each time the note said the same thing: the beneficiary bank's SWIFT code had to appear in field 57A.\n\nThe code was in the message. It was in the wrong field.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent with the beneficiary bank code in field 72\n→ Returned, code must be in field 57A\n→ Resent with the code in field 52A\n→ Returned again, code must be in field 57A\n→ Resent three more times, returned every time\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Put the beneficiary bank code somewhere the receiving bank can see it.\"\n\nSo the team moved the code around the message instead of putting it where the receiving bank reads it.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 57A is the field the receiving bank reads to find the account with institution. A code placed in field 72 or in field 52A can be seen by the receiving bank, but that does not satisfy the validation. The beneficiary bank had also been taken over and its SWIFT code had changed, so the sender had to use the new code in field 57A before the payment could be applied.\n\nThe receiving bank reads field 57A. Nowhere else counts.\n\nA misspelt name in field 50 can return a payment for the same reason. Screening and reconciliation match on the name, and a name that does not match the account can look like a different party. Data quality failures return payments just as surely as funds failures do.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe right value in the wrong field is still the wrong field.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 57A validation before sending\n→ Current BIC check for the beneficiary bank\n→ Return reason read before resending\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the code in the message?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the code is in field 57A and current?\"\n\nHave you ever resent a payment five times before reading what the return actually said?", "id": "XBR-011", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 560, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The receiving bank reads field 57A. Nowhere else counts.", "title": "Returned five times in six weeks, the code was in the message but not in field 57A", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "Right value, wrong field, payment returned", "parts": [{"label": "Code in field 72", "note": "Visible to the receiver, but not where it reads"}, {"label": "Code in field 52A", "note": "Seen, but still not field 57A"}, {"label": "Five returns", "note": "Same reason every time, unread"}, {"label": "BIC had changed", "note": "Beneficiary bank taken over, old code dead"}, {"label": "Field 57A, new code", "note": "The only combination that applies the payment"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A customer sent a payment in a less common currency and was told it would arrive in two to three days. Nine days later it still had not arrived, and the customer assumed the money was lost.\n\nThe money was not lost. The bank had not bought the currency yet.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ Payment instructed in a less common local currency\n→ Bank collects orders until its cut-off time\n→ Bank checks its nostro balances before buying\n→ Currency bought late, payment released days after instruction\n→ Customer told the standard timeline, not the real one\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Tell every customer the payment takes 48 to 72 hours.\"\n\nSo a payment that needed a currency the bank did not hold was promised the timeline of a major currency.\n\nWhy?\n\nA bank does not buy the currency of every payment the moment it is instructed, because it does not know in advance how much foreign currency it will need. It collects orders until a cut-off time and then decides whether to buy, sell or do nothing, based on the balance of orders and the funds already sitting in its nostro accounts. A payment can therefore wait for the bank's currency position to be settled, even when the instruction itself is complete.\n\nA transfer in a major currency such as the dollar or the euro usually arrives in 48 to 72 hours, while a transfer in a less common local currency takes longer because the currency is harder to buy. A large amount can also slow a payment down, because it may need extra checks before it is released. If the money has not arrived after two weeks, there is definitely a problem, and the sending bank should be asked to trace it, since it knows every party in the chain.\n\nThe instruction can be complete while the money waits for the bank's currency position.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe timeline follows the currency, the cut-off and the nostro balance, not the instruction date.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cut-off time for the currency\n→ Nostro balance before buying\n→ Trace trigger when the payment passes two weeks\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How long do payments usually take?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this currency, amount and cut-off can settle on the date we promised?\"\n\nHave you ever promised a standard timeline for a payment that needed a currency nobody held?", "id": "XBR-014", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 563, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The instruction can be complete while the money waits for the bank's currency position.", "title": "Nine days for a payment promised in three, the bank had not bought the currency yet", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Payment instructed in a less common currency", "title": "Instruction complete"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Orders collected until the cut-off time", "title": "Wait for cut-off"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Do nostro balances cover it, or must the bank buy?", "title": "Check the position"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Currency bought late, payment released days on", "title": "Currency bought"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Trace it if it has not arrived after two weeks", "title": "Trace trigger"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 3, "stage_name": "Operational exceptions", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-136", "seq": 142, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 10,000 to a non-euro account, euro leg done in seconds, rest waits hours", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 10,000 from Europe to a non-euro account.\nThe euro leg finishes in seconds.\nThe customer expects the whole payment done.\nNothing happens for hours while the other leg is processed.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Euro leg inside SEPA, completes in seconds\n→ Non-euro leg outside SEPA, different rules, different timeline\n→ Customer sees the euro leg finish, expects the whole payment done\n→ Hours pass, nothing happens, other leg still processing\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One payment, one speed, one status.\"\n\nIn an instant cross-border payment from Europe, the euro leg inside SEPA can complete in seconds, but the non-euro leg outside SEPA follows different rules and a different timeline.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe two legs must be explained separately, with their own coverage and responsibilities, because a single status cannot describe two different journeys. A customer who only sees the fast leg believes the payment is done, and the hours that follow look like a failure.\n\nOne status cannot describe two journeys.\n\nWhy does the customer think a half-finished payment is done?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nExplain each leg separately, with its own speed, coverage and owner.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Euro leg, inside SEPA, seconds\n→ Non-euro leg, outside SEPA, its own rules and timeline\n→ Customer status, per leg, never one blended status\n→ Responsibility, who owns each leg\n\nShow both legs on the payment status, each with its own state and time.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the euro leg finish?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the customer saw the state of both legs?\"\n\nHave you watched a customer celebrate a leg as if it were the whole payment?", "takeaway": "The two legs must be explained separately, with their own coverage and responsibilities.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Euro leg inside SEPA", "note": "Euro leg inside SEPA can complete in seconds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Non-euro leg outside SEPA", "note": "Non-euro leg outside SEPA follows different rules and timeline."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer sees euro leg finish", "note": "Customer sees the euro leg finish and expects the whole payment done."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Hours pass, nothing happens", "note": "Nothing happens for hours while the other leg is processed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Both legs explained separately?", "note": "Two legs must be explained separately, with own coverage and duties."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-147", "seq": 148, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 60,000 payment, app says pending, engine says processed, ledger says nothing", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 60,000 abroad.\nThe app says pending.\nThe engine says processed.\nThe ledger has no posting.\nThree systems, three truths, one customer on the phone.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment passes through several payment service providers\n→ App says pending\n→ Engine says processed\n→ Ledger has no posting\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each system can use its own meaning of a status.\"\n\nWhen a cross-border payment passes through several payment service providers, each one can show a different status: the app says pending, the engine says processed, and the ledger has no posting.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe customer and the contact centre cannot tell whether the money has left, arrived, or is lost. The fix is consistent status meanings, a status enquiry path and clear customer communication at every step, because a payment whose state cannot be stated cannot be managed.\n\nThree statuses, no truth.\n\nWhy can no one tell the customer where EUR 60,000 is?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every status one meaning everywhere, and give the customer a way to enquire.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ App status, what the customer sees\n→ Engine status, what processing says\n→ Ledger posting, what the money did\n→ Status enquiry, the path to the true state\n\nMap every local status to one shared meaning, and show the ledger truth beside the app status.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does the app say?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the status the customer sees matches the ledger?\"\n\nHave you told a customer pending when the engine said processed?", "takeaway": "A payment whose state cannot be stated cannot be managed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment passes several PSPs", "note": "Cross-border payment passes through several payment service providers."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "App says pending", "note": "App says pending."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Engine says processed", "note": "Engine says processed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ledger has no posting", "note": "Ledger has no posting."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer cannot tell", "note": "Customer and contact centre cannot tell whether money left, arrived, or is lost."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Statuses consistent?", "note": "Fix is consistent status meanings, a status enquiry path, clear communication."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-170", "seq": 150, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 70,000 to a supplier, screen says pending, supplier says unpaid", "explain": "A company sends EUR 70,000 to a supplier.\nThe screen says pending.\nThe supplier says it has not been paid.\nThe clerk cannot say where the money is, because the status hides every step in the middle.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment travels through a bank, a correspondent and the supplier bank\n→ Customer screen shows only one status, usually Pending\n→ Supplier says it has not been paid\n→ Clerk cannot say where the money is\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One status is enough for the customer.\"\n\nA payment that travels through a bank, a correspondent and the supplier bank shows only one status on the customer screen, usually Pending, while the supplier says it has not been paid.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe clerk cannot say where the money is because the status does not reflect the intermediary steps. Customer-facing statuses must stay accurate while the underlying processing is still pending, so the customer is not left guessing.\n\nPending hides the journey.\n\nWhy can the clerk not say where EUR 70,000 is?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nShow the intermediary steps, and keep the customer status accurate while processing is still pending.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bank leg, where the payment left\n→ Correspondent leg, where it travels between banks\n→ Supplier bank leg, where it lands\n→ Customer status, accurate at every step, never a single flat Pending\n\nShow each leg with its own state, so the clerk can point to the exact step.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does the screen say pending?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the customer can see where the payment is, leg by leg?\"\n\nHave you told a supplier the payment is pending when you could not say where it was?", "takeaway": "Customer-facing statuses must stay accurate while the underlying processing is still pending.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Customer visibility across systems", "parts": [{"label": "Bank, correspondent, supplier", "note": "Payment travels through a bank, a correspondent, and the supplier bank."}, {"label": "One status on screen", "note": "Customer screen shows only one status, usually Pending."}, {"label": "Supplier says not paid", "note": "Supplier says it has not been paid."}, {"label": "Clerk cannot say where it is", "note": "Clerk cannot say where the money is."}, {"label": "Status hides middle steps", "note": "Status does not reflect the intermediary steps."}, {"label": "Accurate statuses needed", "note": "Customer-facing statuses must stay accurate while processing is pending."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "SCN-01", "seq": 155, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "One payment, two amounts: USD 100,000 in, USD 99,400 out", "explain": "A customer instructs a cross-border payment of USD 100,000.\n\nThe beneficiary receives USD 99,400.\n\nNobody stole the difference. A rule took it.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ pain.001 instructs USD 100,000\n→ Payment engine validates and converts to pacs.008\n→ Clearing applies fees and an FX conversion at a shifted value date\n→ Clearing settles USD 99,400\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The instructed amount is the settled amount.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe payment engine and the clearing layer apply different rules. The engine validates the instruction, while clearing settles a different amount after fees, charges or an FX conversion at a shifted value date.\n\nThe amount gap always has a rule behind it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart the investigation by comparing the instructed amount in the pacs.008 against what clearing actually settled, then find which rule changed it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instructed amount in pacs.008\n→ Amount clearing actually settled\n→ Value date and charges applied\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Why is the amount different?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which rule changed the amount?\"\n\nHave you chased an amount gap and found the rule hiding behind it?", "takeaway": "The amount gap always has a rule behind it. Compare pacs.008 against what clearing settled.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Engine validates instruction", "note": "Engine validates the instruction and converts it to ISO 20022"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 carries USD 100,000", "note": "The instructed amount is USD 100,000"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing applies fees and FX", "note": "Fees, charges or an FX conversion at a shifted value date"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing settles USD 99,400", "note": "A different amount is settled"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which rule changed it?", "note": "Compare the instructed amount against what clearing settled"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-02", "seq": 156, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The payment settled, but Treasury funded from a stale screen", "explain": "A payment settles correctly.\n\nTreasury funds from the screen. The screen is stale.\n\nThe real funding need moved from USD 2 million to USD 5 million before cut-off, and nothing alerted Treasury.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Payment settles correctly\n→ Nostro ledger feed fails\n→ Treasury screen still shows USD 2 million\n→ Real funding requirement moves to USD 5 million\n→ Nothing alerts Treasury before cut-off\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Treasury will see the funding need on screen.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe payment settled, but the Nostro ledger feed failed silently. Treasury kept making funding decisions from outdated data, which is a critical operational risk even though no payment failed.\n\nA settled payment can still leave the bank blind.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA feed failure is a funding risk, not just a data issue. Treasury needs a live feed and an alert when the feed stops, not just a screen.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payment settlement\n→ Nostro feed health\n→ Treasury funding view\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payment settle?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funding screen is live, not stale?\"\n\nHave you seen a desk make a funding decision from data that had already gone stale?", "takeaway": "The payment settled, but the bank was making funding decisions from outdated data.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment settles correctly", "note": "The payment itself settles without a problem"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Nostro ledger feed fails", "note": "The feed stops, silently"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Treasury funds from stale screen", "note": "The screen still shows USD 2 million"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funding need moves to USD 5 million", "note": "The real requirement moves before cut-off"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Any alert to Treasury?", "note": "Nothing alerted Treasury"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Decisions from outdated data", "note": "A critical operational risk, even though no payment failed"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B07", "seq": 167, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "One statement line, 400 entries, and one missing", "explain": "A settlement batch holds 400 entries under one statement line worth EUR 850,000.\n\nOne entry is missing.\n\nThe whole group fails, and nobody can tell the customer why.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Settlement happens in a batch\n→ One statement line stands for 400 ledger entries\n→ Entries share one batch reference\n→ One entry is missing\n→ The whole group fails reconciliation\n→ A temporary GL entry is raised while investigating\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Match each entry to the statement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nReconciliation must collect the entries by batch reference and reconcile that group as one against the statement line, rather than matching a single entry. If one entry is missing, the whole group fails.\n\nReconcile the batch, not the entry.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe engine must preserve the batch reference linkage across the items. Grasp the reason the batch exists, not merely how it matches.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Batch reference linkage\n→ Group reconciliation\n→ Missing entry handling\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does this entry match?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the batch reference survives across every entry?\"\n\nHave you seen one missing entry fail a whole batch reconciliation?", "takeaway": "Reconcile the batch, not the entry. One missing entry breaks the whole group.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reconcile the batch, not one entry", "parts": [{"label": "One statement line, 400 entries", "note": "One statement line worth EUR 850,000 represents many ledger entries"}, {"label": "Common batch reference", "note": "The entries share a common batch reference"}, {"label": "Group and reconcile together", "note": "Collect the entries by batch reference and reconcile the group as one"}, {"label": "One missing entry breaks the group", "note": "If one entry is missing, the whole group fails"}, {"label": "Temporary GL entry", "note": "A temporary GL entry may be raised while investigating"}, {"label": "Keep the reference linked", "note": "The engine must preserve the batch reference linkage across the items"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C02", "seq": 171, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The optional field that stopped a EUR 120,000 cover payment", "explain": "In a four-bank cover payment, Bank A sends pacs.008 to Bank B and a pacs.009 cover to intermediary Bank C.\n\nBank C cannot link the cover to the customer payment.\n\nThe EUR 120,000 cover sits unreconciled, because one optional field was left out.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Bank A sends pacs.008 to Bank B\n→ Bank A sends pacs.009 cover to Bank C\n→ Bank C must link the cover using the same UETR and remittance reference\n→ The pacs.009 RltdRmtInf field is left out, because it is optional\n→ Bank C cannot reconcile the cover\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Optional means we can leave it out.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe pacs.009 RltdRmtInf field carries the link, and although it is optional in the message, the intermediary cannot reconcile the cover without it.\n\nOptional in the message, mandatory for the flow.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOptional or mandatory is a property of the message, not of the process. Handle the message flow and the field status as two separate things.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Message field status\n→ Process need for the field\n→ Reconciliation link\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the field optional?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the intermediary can link the cover?\"\n\nHave you seen an optional field left out, and the whole flow stall because of it?", "takeaway": "Optional in the message, mandatory for the flow. A field marked optional can still be required to make the payment work.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Bank A sends pacs.008 to Bank B", "note": "The customer payment goes directly to the beneficiary bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank A sends pacs.009 cover to Bank C", "note": "The cover goes to the intermediary bank"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Link cover with UETR and reference", "note": "Bank C must link the cover using the same UETR and remittance reference"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "RltdRmtInf carries the link", "note": "The pacs.009 RltdRmtInf field carries that link"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Optional in the message?", "note": "The field is optional in the message, yet the intermediary cannot reconcile without it"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Treat flow and field status separately", "note": "Optional or mandatory is a property of the message, not of the process"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-004", "seq": 182, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The settlement that looked fine, and the Rs. 18 lakh that did not reconcile", "explain": "Card payments clear for the day, and customers see their payments succeed.\n\nBehind the scenes, the ledger is Rs. 18 lakh out from the settlement position.\n\nNobody notices, because nobody compared the three records.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Clearing completes\n→ Settlement position is the net amount each bank owes or is owed\n→ Ledger postings are made\n→ Clearing records, settlement positions and ledger postings do not agree\n→ The break is not investigated\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The customer payment succeeded, so settlement is fine.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAfter clearing, the settlement position must match the ledger postings exactly. Reconciliation compares all three records, and any break between them is investigated rather than written off.\n\nA settlement that does not reconcile is a control failure.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA settlement that does not reconcile means money is unaccounted for, even if the customer-facing payment appeared to succeed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Clearing records\n→ Settlement position\n→ Ledger postings\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the payments clear?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves clearing, settlement and ledger agree?\"\n\nHave you seen a settlement break found days after the customers had already been told everything was fine?", "takeaway": "A settlement that does not reconcile means money is unaccounted for, even if the payment appeared to succeed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing completes", "note": "The settlement position is the net amount each bank owes."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare the records", "note": "Clearing records, settlement positions and ledger postings."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Do the records agree", "note": "Any break between them is investigated."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigate the break", "note": "A break is investigated rather than written off."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch21-card-authorization-clearing-and-settlement.html", "label": "Chapter 21: Card Authorization, Clearing and Settlement", "chapter": "ch21", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-034", "seq": 186, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Approved for Rs. 15 lakh, and the money never arrived", "explain": "A loan is approved for Rs. 15 lakh.\n\nThe customer waits. The money never arrives.\n\nThe team checks the approval first, and finds nothing wrong. The break is somewhere else.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Approval is given\n→ Account validation runs\n→ Disbursement instruction is sent\n→ Ledger posting is recorded\n→ Customer communication goes out\n→ One of these stages breaks, and the customer simply sees that the money has not arrived\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The approval was the problem.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA valid approval with a wrong account, a failed instruction or a missing ledger posting all look the same to the customer, who simply sees that the money has not arrived.\n\nCheck each stage in order rather than assuming the approval was the problem.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the path from approval through account validation, the disbursement instruction, the ledger posting and the customer communication, because the break can sit at any of these points.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Approval\n→ Account validation and instruction\n→ Ledger posting and communication\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the loan approved?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which stage broke?\"\n\nHave you traced a failed disbursement and found the break in the last place anyone looked?", "takeaway": "Check each stage in order rather than assuming the approval was the problem.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Approval", "note": "The loan is approved."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Account validation", "note": "The account details are validated."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Disbursement instruction", "note": "The instruction to release funds is sent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ledger posting", "note": "The posting is recorded in the ledger."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which stage broke?", "note": "The break can sit at any stage, so check them all."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer communication", "note": "The customer is told whether the money arrived."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C018", "seq": 240, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 200,000 announced, but the funds had not arrived yet", "explain": "An MT103 announced EUR 200,000 to the beneficiary bank.\nThe beneficiary called, asking where the money was.\nThe funds leg had not arrived yet, and the beneficiary had not been credited.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ → MT103 reaches the beneficiary bank first\n→ → MT202 COV still in transit\n→ → Beneficiary bank holds the instruction\n→ → Beneficiary asks for money that has not settled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The MT103 arrived, so the money is here.\"\nIn a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 COV travel separately.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe beneficiary bank must hold the instruction and wait for the cover, because it cannot credit the beneficiary until the funds have settled. This timing gap is a normal feature of the cover method, not an error.\n\nMT103 first, funds later, is normal. Crediting early is the error.\n\nWhat proves the funds have settled, not just been announced?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nHold the MT103 until the cover arrives. Credit only on settled funds. Use the waiting time to check that the amount and the reference on the cover match the announcement.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instruction received\n→ Cover received\n→ Amount and reference matched\n→ Funds settled\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the MT103 arrived?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds have settled, not just been announced?\"\n\nHave you ever had a beneficiary demand money that was announced but not yet settled?", "takeaway": "MT103 first, funds later, is normal. Crediting early is the error.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 reaches beneficiary bank first", "note": "The information leg can arrive ahead of the funds leg"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank holds the instruction", "note": "It holds the instruction and waits for the cover"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cover arrives and funds settle", "note": "No credit to the beneficiary until the funds have settled"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check amount and reference match", "note": "A mismatch is most likely to surface at this moment"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary credited on settled funds", "note": "Credit only after the cover matches and settles"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C019", "seq": 241, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 200,000 cover arrived, and it looked like an ordinary transfer", "explain": "EUR 200,000 arrived at the beneficiary bank as an MT202 COV.\nNothing on it said which customer payment it was for.\nAn MT103 for the same amount was waiting, unmatched, and the beneficiary was not credited.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT202 COV arrives at the beneficiary bank\n→ No reference links it to the waiting MT103\n→ Cover looks like an ordinary bank-to-bank transfer\n→ Beneficiary bank cannot match it, so it holds the funds\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The amount is the same, so the match is obvious.\"\nAmounts repeat. References are the bridge.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen an MT202 COV reaches the beneficiary bank, the bank must recognise that its purpose is to cover a specific MT103, and the link is the reference and the fields that tie the two messages together. Without that link the cover looks like an ordinary transfer.\n\nThe reference is the bridge between the information leg and the funds leg.\n\nWhat proves this cover belongs to that MT103?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMatch the cover to the MT103 on reference and fields, not on amount alone. Hold the funds until the link is proven, and send the cover back for correction if the reference is missing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reference on the cover\n→ Reference on the MT103\n→ Fields tie the two together\n→ Funds matched before credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the cover arrived?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this cover belongs to that MT103?\"\n\nHave you ever held a cover you could not match to any MT103?", "takeaway": "The reference is the bridge between the information leg and the funds leg.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Recognising a EUR 200,000 MT202 COV", "parts": [{"label": "Purpose is to cover a specific MT103", "note": "The cover must be recognised as settling a named MT103"}, {"label": "Reference links the two messages", "note": "Reference and fields tie the two messages together"}, {"label": "Without the link, just a transfer", "note": "Without the link the cover looks like an ordinary transfer"}, {"label": "Hold the funds until matched", "note": "No credit until the link is proven."}, {"label": "Amount alone is not a match", "note": "Amounts repeat. References are the bridge."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT202", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C025", "seq": 247, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 100,000 sent, two deductions, one credit that did not add up", "explain": "USD 100,000 was sent through two intermediary banks.\nEach took a charge.\nThe beneficiary bank could not prove the final credit was right, because the fields that explain the deductions were missing.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Two intermediaries each deduct a charge\n→ Field 71A states who bears the charges\n→ Field 71F states each charge deducted\n→ Beneficiary bank works out the final credit from those fields\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The credit speaks for itself.\"\nWithout fields 71A and 71F, the credit is a number with no story.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 71A states who bears the charges, and field 71F states the amount of each charge deducted, so the credit can be reconciled against the amount the ordering customer sent. Without those fields the beneficiary cannot reconcile the credit against the amount sent.\n\nWithout fields 71A and 71F, the beneficiary cannot reconcile the credit against the amount sent.\n\nWhat proves this final credit is the amount sent minus the agreed charges?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead the final credit from fields 71A and 71F. Check field 71A for who bears the charges and every field 71F for the amount deducted, and hold the credit until the arithmetic balances.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount instructed\n→ Field 71A\n→ Each field 71F\n→ Final credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the credit the right amount?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this final credit is the amount sent minus the agreed charges?\"\n\nHave you ever had a credit you could not reconcile because the charge fields were missing?", "takeaway": "Without fields 71A and 71F, the beneficiary cannot reconcile the credit against the amount sent.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Two intermediaries each deduct a charge", "note": "Two intermediary banks each take a charge"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Field 71A states who bears charges", "note": "Field 71A states who bears the charges"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Field 71F states each charge deducted", "note": "Field 71F states the amount of each charge deducted"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check the arithmetic balances", "note": "The credit must equal the amount sent minus every deduction"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank works out final credit", "note": "The beneficiary bank derives the final credit from those fields"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C036", "seq": 258, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 130,000 cover, and the two legs disagreed by EUR 500", "explain": "A EUR 130,000 cover payment arrived.\nThe MT103 said EUR 130,000. The MT202 COV said EUR 129,500.\nThe beneficiary bank would not credit a cent until someone explained the EUR 500 gap.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 announces EUR 130,000\n→ MT202 COV arrives for EUR 129,500\n→ Amount and reference do not agree\n→ Bank investigates before crediting\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A EUR 500 difference is a minor data error.\"\nA mismatch is a break in the link between the legs.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 COV must agree on amount and on the references that link them, and a mismatch between the two creates an exception that stops settlement. The discrepancy can be a different amount, a missing reference or details that do not line up.\n\nA mismatch is not a minor data error. It is a break in the link between the information leg and the funds leg.\n\nWhat proves the two legs of this cover payment agree?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCompare amount and references on both legs before crediting. Investigate any difference, however small, and do not credit until the legs agree.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount on the MT103\n→ Amount on the MT202 COV\n→ References match\n→ Settlement only on agreement\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is EUR 500 a big deal?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the two legs of this cover payment agree?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment over a small amount mismatch?", "takeaway": "A mismatch is not a minor data error. It is a break in the link between the information leg and the funds leg.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 and MT202 COV must agree", "note": "They must agree on amount and on the linking references"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Check amount and references match", "note": "A different amount, a missing reference or misaligned details stop settlement"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank investigates before crediting", "note": "The bank must investigate before crediting the beneficiary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Mismatch is a break in the link", "note": "The mismatch breaks the link between the two legs"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Credit only when the legs agree", "note": "No credit until amount and references match"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C062", "seq": 270, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 100,000 cover rejected even though the amounts agreed", "explain": "A EUR 100,000 cover payment arrived with the amounts matching exactly.\nThe beneficiary bank rejected it anyway.\nSequence B of the MT202 COV did not mirror the MT103.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 and MT202 COV amounts agree\n→ Sequence B of the cover differs from the MT103\n→ Beneficiary bank rejects the cover\n→ Funds stop even though the amounts agree\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the amounts agree, the cover will pass.\"\nSome banks reject a cover whose sequence B does not match the announcement.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn sequence B of an MT202 COV, only the ordering and beneficiary customer information in fields 50a and 59a is mandatory, and the other fields are optional. When the bank does provide a field such as field 72 or field 59, it must match the same field in the MT103 exactly, or the field must be left empty.\n\nA mismatch in sequence B stops the funds even when the amounts agree.\n\nWhat proves sequence B of the cover mirrors the MT103?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMirror the MT103 in sequence B: fields 50a and 59a always, and any other field provided must match the MT103 exactly or be left empty. Check the mirror before sending the cover.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Fields 50a and 59a\n→ Other fields match or empty\n→ Amounts agree\n→ Cover accepted\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do the amounts agree?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves sequence B of the cover mirrors the MT103?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a cover rejected when the amounts were right?", "takeaway": "A mismatch in sequence B stops the funds even when the amounts agree.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Sequence B of a EUR 100,000 MT202 COV", "parts": [{"label": "Only fields 50a and 59a are mandatory", "note": "Only the ordering and beneficiary customer information in fields 50a and 59a is mandatory"}, {"label": "Other fields are optional", "note": "The other fields in sequence B are optional"}, {"label": "Provided fields must match the MT103", "note": "A field such as 72 or 59, if given, must match the MT103 exactly"}, {"label": "Or the field must be left empty", "note": "If it cannot match, the field must be left empty"}, {"label": "Some banks reject a mismatched cover", "note": "Some banks reject a cover whose sequence B does not match the announcement"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["MT202", "MT103"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C075", "seq": 279, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 100,000 credit that did not match the equation", "explain": "USD 100,000 was instructed.\nThe beneficiary bank credited an amount that did not fit the charges equation.\nThe operations team could not say whether the credit was right, because no one had checked the arithmetic.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Field 33B states the amount instructed\n→ Field 71G states receiver charges prepaid by the sender\n→ Field 71F states each charge deducted\n→ Field 32A must equal 33B plus 71G minus all 71F\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The system works the credit out, so the arithmetic takes care of itself.\"\nThe relationship always holds, and it must be checked.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 32A is the amount that settles between banks, field 33B is the amount the ordering customer instructed, field 71G is the receiver's charges prepaid by the sender, and field 71F is each sender's charge deducted along the way. The relationship always holds: 32A equals 33B plus 71G minus all the 71F charges.\n\n32A equals 33B plus 71G minus all the 71F charges. If the equation does not balance, something is wrong.\n\nWhat proves this credit balances under the charges equation?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck the equation on every payment: 32A equals 33B plus 71G minus all the 71F charges. Remember that field 71A is set by the bank that generates the MT103 and cannot be changed by later banks, which can only add a 71F when they take a charge.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field 32A\n→ Field 33B\n→ Field 71G\n→ Each field 71F\n→ Field 71A set once\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the credit about right?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this credit balances under the charges equation?\"\n\nHave you ever found a credit that did not balance under the charges equation?", "takeaway": "32A equals 33B plus 71G minus all the 71F charges. If the equation does not balance, something is wrong.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The charges equation on USD 100,000", "parts": [{"label": "32A", "note": "Amount that settles between banks"}, {"label": "33B", "note": "Amount the ordering customer instructed"}, {"label": "71G", "note": "Receiver charges prepaid by the sender"}, {"label": "71F", "note": "Each sender charge deducted along the way"}, {"label": "71A", "note": "Set by the MT103 bank, later banks can only add a 71F"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C089", "seq": 287, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 140,000 cover arrived from a bank the announcement never named", "explain": "An MT103 announced EUR 140,000 and named the correspondent that would send the cover.\nThe cover arrived from a different bank entirely.\nThe beneficiary bank could not match the two legs and would not credit the beneficiary.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 announcement states field 53\n→ Beneficiary bank knows where funds will come from\n→ Cover arrives from an unmentioned bank\n→ Beneficiary bank will not credit\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The cover will arrive from somewhere. The source does not matter.\"\nThe announcement must name the cover's source.\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cover payment the MT103 announcement should state in field 53 which correspondent will send the cover, so the beneficiary bank knows where the funds will come from. When the cover arrives from a bank the announcement never mentioned, the beneficiary bank cannot match the two legs and will not credit the beneficiary.\n\nThe fix sits with the sender, whose bank must correct the announcement so the two legs agree.\n\nWhat proves the cover came from the bank the announcement named?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nState the cover's source in field 53 of the MT103 announcement. If the cover arrives from another bank, the sender's bank must correct the announcement so the two legs agree.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover source in field 53\n→ Cover arrives from that bank\n→ Two legs match\n→ Beneficiary credited\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Has the cover arrived?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the cover came from the bank the announcement named?\"\n\nHave you ever held a payment because the cover came from an unmentioned bank?", "takeaway": "The fix sits with the sender, whose bank must correct the announcement so the two legs agree.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 announcement states field 53", "note": "The announcement should state in field 53 which correspondent will send the cover"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank knows where funds come from", "note": "The beneficiary bank knows where the funds will come from"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Cover from the named bank or another", "note": "When the cover comes from an unmentioned bank, the legs cannot be matched"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank will not credit", "note": "The beneficiary bank cannot match the two legs and will not credit the beneficiary"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sender corrects the announcement", "note": "The sender's bank must correct the announcement so the two legs agree"}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN3-002", "seq": 298, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 50,000 pacs.008 rejected, and the pacs.002 was booked as a payment", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 pacs.008 was rejected.\nThe pacs.002 that reported it was booked as a debit.\nThe same payment was counted twice, and the books no longer balanced.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent for EUR 50,000\n→ Payment rejected, pacs.002 reports it\n→ pacs.002 booked as a debit\n→ Same payment counted twice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every message that arrives gets a posting.\"\nA pacs.002 reports an outcome. It moves no money.\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.002 reports the outcome of a pacs.008; it moves no money, so it normally needs no accounting entry of its own. When it carries a negative acknowledgement, the postings made for the original payment are undone exactly, debit and credit reversed, restoring the books to their pre-payment position.\n\nBooking the status report itself as a debit or credit would count the same payment twice.\n\nWhat proves the books were restored, not double-counted?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBook nothing for the pacs.002 itself. On a negative acknowledgement, reverse the original postings exactly, debit and credit, and check the books are back to their pre-payment position.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Original postings\n→ Exact reversal\n→ Books restored\n→ No posting for the report\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Should we book the pacs.002?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the books were restored, not double-counted?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a status report booked as if it were a payment?", "takeaway": "Booking the status report itself as a debit or credit would count the same payment twice.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "pacs.002, a status report only, on EUR 50,000", "parts": [{"label": "No entry of its own", "note": "It reports an outcome; it moves no money, so no posting arises from it."}, {"label": "Negative acknowledgement", "note": "A negative outcome triggers an exact reversal of the earlier postings."}, {"label": "Books restored", "note": "Reversal returns the books to their position before the payment was attempted."}, {"label": "No double count", "note": "Posting the report itself would count the same payment twice."}, {"label": "Reverse exactly, debit and credit", "note": "An exact reversal restores the books."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002"], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-010", "seq": 310, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The nostro showed EUR 1 million. The mirror showed something else.", "explain": "A EUR 1 million credit lands on the nostro.\nThe mirror account still shows the old balance.\nTreasury funds the day on a balance that is already wrong.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 1 million credited to the nostro\n→ Correspondent advises with MT910 or MT950\n→ Mirror updated late, from a daily batch\n→ Treasury works on a stale mirror balance\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The mirror nostro updates once a day, in a batch.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nEvery movement on the real nostro is mirrored one transaction at a time, never in a daily batch. When the nostro is credited, the correspondent advises the holder, for example with an MT910 or MT950, and the mirror is updated from that advice. When the bank instructs its correspondent to debit the nostro, the mirror is updated at once, and the two entries always carry the same amount with opposite signs.\n\nMirror nostro moves transaction by transaction, never in a batch.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUpdate the mirror from each advice as it arrives, and never let a batch stand between the nostro and the mirror.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Real nostro movements\n→ Correspondent advice\n→ Mirror updates\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the mirror matches the real nostro right now?\"\n\nHave you funded a day on a mirror balance that was already out of date?", "takeaway": "Mirror nostro moves transaction by transaction, never in a batch.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Movement hits the real nostro", "note": "Each movement is reflected on the mirror one transaction at a time."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent advises the holder", "note": "A credit triggers advice, for example an MT910 or MT950."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Mirror updated from the advice", "note": "The holder updates the mirror from the advice received."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Was the mirror updated from this advice?", "note": "A missing update leaves the mirror behind the real account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Same amount, opposite signs", "note": "Both entries carry the same amount with opposite signs."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT910", "MT950"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-011", "seq": 311, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A debit on the nostro, a credit on the mirror, and a team that read it backwards.", "explain": "A EUR 300,000 debit hits the nostro.\nThe mirror shows a credit of EUR 300,000.\nThe team reports a gain, and treasury releases funds that were never there.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 300,000 debit on the real nostro\n→ Mirror shows a EUR 300,000 credit\n→ Team reads the mirror as a gain\n→ Funds released against money that left\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A credit on the mirror means money arrived.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA debit on the real nostro appears as a credit on the mirror, and a credit on the real account appears as a debit on the mirror, always for the same amount. The mirror reflects the real account, so the two sides sit opposite by design rather than by coincidence. Reading the mirror balance and its movements therefore tells a bank exactly what the real account should show.\n\nMirror nostro always shows the opposite sign, by design.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRead every mirror entry with its sign flipped, and reconcile the mirror against the real account before acting on it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Real nostro entry\n→ Mirror entry\n→ Sign convention\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this mirror entry means money in, not money out?\"\n\nHave you seen a mirror balance read backwards?", "takeaway": "Mirror nostro always shows the opposite sign, by design.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Mirror nostro: opposite by design", "parts": [{"label": "Real debit, mirror credit", "note": "A debit on the real nostro appears as a credit on the mirror."}, {"label": "Real credit, mirror debit", "note": "A credit on the real account appears as a debit on the mirror."}, {"label": "Same amount", "note": "Both sides always carry the same amount."}, {"label": "Opposite by design", "note": "The mirror reflects the real account, so the signs sit opposite."}, {"label": "Mirror reveals the real account", "note": "The mirror balance shows what the real account should hold."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "SCN-21", "seq": 349, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Small breaks, ignored for months, added up to a USD 2 million surprise before the audit.", "explain": "Small uncleared items pile up for months, each one looking harmless.\nVolumes rise, and a USD 2 million discrepancy appears just before an audit.\nThe team had optimised the manual workaround, never the process that created the breaks.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Small breaks accumulate for months\n→ Two systems derive value dates differently\n→ USD 2 million discrepancy found before the audit\n→ Manual workaround optimised, root cause untouched\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Small breaks are harmless until the audit.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSmall uncleared items accumulated for months, looking harmless, until rising volumes exposed a 2 million dollar discrepancy just before an audit. The cause was timing: two systems derived the transaction value date with different logic. Aligning that logic cleared 80 percent of breaks automatically, which is the lesson to keep: fix the process that creates the break, never optimise the manual workaround, and run reconciliation as an early-warning system.\n\nFix the process that creates the break, not the workaround.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAlign the logic that creates breaks, and run reconciliation as an early warning, not as a month-end chore.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Value date logic\n→ Break aging\n→ Reconciliation alerts\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the process that creates these breaks is fixed?\"\n\nHave you optimised a workaround while the break kept coming back?", "takeaway": "Fix the process that creates the break, not the workaround.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Breaks that compounded unnoticed", "parts": [{"label": "Mismatched value dates", "note": "Each system derived the date its own way."}, {"label": "Small breaks, compounding", "note": "Small daily differences compounded for months."}, {"label": "Align the logic", "note": "Aligned derivation cleared 80 percent of breaks."}, {"label": "Watch break aging", "note": "Spot drift early, not at the audit."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-29", "seq": 354, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "One empty parameter passed QA, staging and a small rollout. At full volume it broke everything.", "explain": "A new flow passes QA, staging and a small rollout.\nAt full volume a parameter everyone assumed was always filled comes back empty.\nPartner API calls fail, reconciliation mismatches, and the fix takes three hours while trust takes far longer to rebuild.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Small rollout goes clean\n→ Full volume exposes an empty parameter\n→ Partner API calls fail\n→ Reconciliation mismatches across the board\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A parameter that is always filled in testing will always be filled.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nOne unchecked assumption can survive QA, staging and a small rollout, then fail at full volume: a parameter assumed to always return a value sometimes came back empty. Partner API calls failed, reconciliation mismatched, and rebuilding partner trust took longer than the three-hour fix. Validate every input, above all the ones assumed always present, and add contract tests with the partner covering empty and null variants of every field.\n\nThe parameter everyone assumed was filled is the one that breaks you.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidate every input, above all the ones assumed always present, and test empty and null variants with the partner.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Assumed inputs\n→ Contract tests\n→ Empty and null variants\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every assumed input was tested empty and null?\"\n\nHave you shipped an assumption that only full volume could expose?", "takeaway": "The parameter everyone assumed was filled is the one that breaks you.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Small rollout goes clean", "note": "Edge case hid until full volume."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Full volume exposes the gap", "note": "Assumed-populated parameter came back empty."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find the empty parameter", "note": "Partner API failures, reconciliation mismatches."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Was the assumption tested, or just believed?", "note": "Repair the code, then the assumption."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Add the contract tests", "note": "Empty and null variants of every field."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "SCN-35", "seq": 357, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The bank moved its cut-off. The fintech kept sending on the old one, and every payment settled late.", "explain": "A bank moves its transaction cut-off time and tells no one.\nThe fintech keeps sending on the old settlement window.\nRequests are accepted, then processed in a later cycle, with green dashboards and no errors anywhere.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank moves its cut-off time\n→ Fintech keeps the old settlement window\n→ Requests accepted, settled a cycle late\n→ Dashboards stay green, no errors raised\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the code is right and the dashboards are green, the payments are fine.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCode can be perfect and dashboards green while transactions fail, because the bank moved its transaction cut-off time and the fintech kept sending on the old settlement window. Requests were accepted, then processed in a later cycle, breaking user expectations without a single error. Most production incidents in finance are coordination failures of this kind, cut-off changes, holiday calendars, or report timings that moved without notice, so keep cut-off and calendar configuration synced with every bank partner.\n\nMost incidents are coordination failures, not bugs.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSync cut-off and calendar configuration with every bank partner, and treat a partner change as a release, not a rumour.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Partner cut-off times\n→ Holiday calendars\n→ Configuration sync\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves your cut-off times match your partners' cut-off times today?\"\n\nHave you debugged code for a failure that was really a moved cut-off?", "takeaway": "Most incidents are coordination failures, not bugs.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Coordination failure, not a bug", "parts": [{"label": "Cut-off moved", "note": "Transaction cut-off moved without notice."}, {"label": "Old window kept", "note": "Fintech kept sending on the old time."}, {"label": "Silent late settlement", "note": "Accepted, settled in a later cycle."}, {"label": "Partner change process", "note": "Sync cut-offs and calendars across every partner."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-151", "seq": 432, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 45,000 payment carried rich remittance data, and the last bank in the chain threw it away", "explain": "A EUR 45,000 payment was sent with structured purpose and remittance data.\n\nThe data was meant to help compliance screening and let the beneficiary reconcile against the right invoice.\n\nThe last bank in the chain dropped it, and the beneficiary could not match the payment.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Payment sent with structured purpose and remittance data\n→ First bank captures it correctly\n→ Second bank maps it inconsistently\n→ Last bank in the chain drops it, its system cannot consume it\n→ Beneficiary cannot reconcile against the right invoice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Rich data helps by itself.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nStructured purpose and remittance data helps compliance screening and lets the beneficiary reconcile the payment against the right invoice. But the data is only useful if it is captured correctly, mapped consistently, and consumed by the downstream systems that need it. Poor data quality in one system becomes a compliance and reconciliation problem in the next.\n\nData that does not survive the chain helps no one.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nData quality has to be owned end to end. Name the owner at each hop: who captures, who maps, who consumes, and what happens when a system cannot consume it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Capture, correctly\n→ Mapping, consistently\n→ Consumption downstream\n→ The owner at each hop\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send rich data?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the data survived every hop to the beneficiary?\"\n\nHave you seen rich payment data dropped by the last bank in the chain?", "takeaway": "Data that does not survive the chain helps no one.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Richer data for AML and reconciliation", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 45,000 with rich data", "note": "Structured purpose and remittance data sent with the payment."}, {"label": "Purpose data", "note": "Structured purpose data helps compliance screening."}, {"label": "Remittance data", "note": "Structured remittance data lets beneficiary reconcile against the right invoice."}, {"label": "Captured correctly", "note": "Data is only useful if it is captured correctly."}, {"label": "Mapped consistently", "note": "Data is only useful if it is mapped consistently."}, {"label": "Consumed downstream", "note": "Data is only useful if consumed by the downstream systems that need it."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "sanctions or fraud block", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A04", "seq": 437, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "SAR 250,000 left the account, the beneficiary was paid, and the nostro showed nothing", "explain": "A SAR 250,000 payment left the customer account and was sent onward.\n\nThe beneficiary was credited.\n\nBut the statement showed no nostro debit, and Operations wanted to send the payment again.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ SAR 250,000 debited from the customer account\n→ Payment sent onward to the correspondent\n→ Beneficiary credited\n→ Corresponding nostro debit never posts\n→ Statement shows no debit, team proposes re-effecting the payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No nostro debit means the payment never went.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThis is a Payment Missing a Nostro Entry. The payment left and the beneficiary may already stand credited, but the nostro debit never posted. Re-effecting would duplicate the payment and break the nostro at reconciliation. The governing discipline is evidence before action.\n\nA missing entry is not a missing payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nProve the payment state first with pacs.002, camt.052 or camt.053, or pacs.028 where no notification arrived. Once proved, the correspondent bank posts the nostro debit. Never re-effect the payment.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The customer debit\n→ The payment state, proved with evidence\n→ The nostro debit, posted by the correspondent\n→ The re-effecting, forbidden\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the nostro debit post?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment's state before we touch the nostro?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment nearly sent twice because the nostro entry was missing?", "takeaway": "A missing entry is not a missing payment.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "SAR 250,000 sent, no Nostro debit", "note": "Customer debited and confirmation sent, yet the statement shows no debit."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Prove the payment state first", "note": "Confirm with pacs.002, camt.052 or camt.053, or pacs.028 if nothing arrived."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "State proved with evidence?", "note": "Evidence must precede any action on the missing Nostro debit."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent posts the debit", "note": "Once proved, the correspondent bank posts the missing Nostro debit."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Never re-effect the payment", "note": "Re-effecting duplicates the payment and breaks the Nostro at reconciliation."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "camt.052", "camt.053", "pacs.028"], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A10", "seq": 439, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A USD 10 million payment was two days late, and the interest bill proved the value date mattered more than the booking", "explain": "A USD 10 million payment was delayed by two days.\n\nIt was booked on the day it was processed.\n\nThe interest bill arrived anyway, because interest runs from the value date, not the booking date.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ USD 10 million payment processed, booked on the processing day\n→ Value date differs from the booking date by two days\n→ Customer leg credited on Monday at a Monday value date\n→ Nostro leg booked later at that same value date\n→ Interest charged on the value date gap, no one had checked it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The booking date decides the interest.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBack valuation is a mismatch between value date and booking date, and it matters because interest runs from the value date, not the booking date. In a single-sided case the mismatch sits on one side, while in a double-sided case it sits on both sides. The working sequence is to confirm the value date first, establish whether the mismatch is single or double-sided, then assess the impact on interest, liquidity and reconciliation.\n\nValue date drives interest, not booking.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment is delayed or rebooked, confirm the value date first, then whether the mismatch is single or double-sided, then the impact on interest, liquidity and reconciliation. The booking date is the record. The value date is the money.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The value date\n→ The booking date\n→ Single-sided or double-sided mismatch\n→ The impact on interest, liquidity and reconciliation\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When was the payment booked?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the value date, and what interest does it carry?\"\n\nHave you seen an interest bill arrive because no one checked the value date?", "takeaway": "Value date drives interest, not booking.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Value date drives interest", "parts": [{"label": "USD 10 million, two days late", "note": "Payment delayed by two days, interest charged on the gap."}, {"label": "Value date vs booking date", "note": "Payment processed with a value date that differs from its booking date."}, {"label": "Single-sided mismatch", "note": "Mismatch sits on one side, for example customer credited Monday, Nostro debited later."}, {"label": "Double-sided mismatch", "note": "Mismatch sits on both sides of the transaction."}, {"label": "Confirm the value date first", "note": "Bank should confirm the value date before anything else."}, {"label": "Impact on interest", "note": "Assess the impact on interest, liquidity and reconciliation."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch37-how-10-large-banks-do-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 37: How 10 Large Banks Do Payments", "chapter": "ch37", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-A15", "seq": 441, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One settlement code was mapped wrong, and a EUR 150,000 payment was debited twice", "explain": "A EUR 150,000 payment arrived with the settlement code INGA.\n\nThe migration mapped it as INDA.\n\nThe sending bank account was debited again, and the double posting was found at day-end as a reconciliation break.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 arrives with INGA, the instructing agent has already settled this leg\n→ Migration maps INGA as INDA\n→ Engine follows INDA logic, the instructed agent settles this leg\n→ Sending bank account debited again, a double posting\n→ Reconciliation break found at day-end\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"MT to MX migration is a one-to-one field replacement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt is not. The settlement method code on a pacs.008 changes the accounting: INDA means the instructed agent settles this leg, while INGA means the instructing agent has already settled it. Information once implied is now explicit and carries business meaning, so an incoming INGA payment wrongly mapped as INDA makes the engine follow the wrong settlement logic.\n\nA code is a business decision, not a field.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMigration must ask who settles each leg, whether settlement has already happened, and what accounting entries should follow. A field mapping shows where data goes. Business analysis shows what it means.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The settlement code as sent\n→ Who settles this leg\n→ Whether settlement already happened\n→ The accounting entries that must follow\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Where does the field map to?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the mapped code means the same thing in the new message?\"\n\nHave you seen a field mapped right and a meaning mapped wrong?", "takeaway": "A code is a business decision, not a field.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "INDA vs INGA settlement code", "parts": [{"label": "INGA: already settled", "note": "The instructing agent has already settled this leg."}, {"label": "INDA: instructed agent settles", "note": "The instructed agent settles this leg of the payment."}, {"label": "Wrong mapping, double posting", "note": "INGA mapped as INDA debits the sending bank again, a double posting."}, {"label": "Reconciliation break at day-end", "note": "The double posting is found as a reconciliation break at day-end."}, {"label": "Who settles each leg?", "note": "Ask who settles each leg and whether settlement has already happened."}, {"label": "Mapping vs meaning", "note": "A field mapping shows where data goes, business analysis shows what it means."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008"], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-006", "seq": 450, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The app said a EUR 850 payment failed, but the account was debited anyway", "explain": "A customer sent EUR 850 through a mobile app.\n\nThe app showed the payment as failed.\n\nYet the account statement showed the EUR 850 debit, and the customer asked for the money back.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer sends EUR 850 through the mobile app\n→ App shows the payment as failed\n→ Account statement shows the EUR 850 debit\n→ Payment is in an uncertain state: the debit may mean it reached clearing and is still processing, or the ledger posted while the payment status update failed\n→ Customer asks to retry the payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Failed on screen means failed everywhere.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt does not. The debit may mean the payment reached the clearing system and is still processing, or it may mean the ledger posted while the payment status update failed. Treat the payment as one transaction with one identity across every system, and use the original instruction ID, end-to-end ID and ledger posting reference to trace it through the channel, the payment engine, the clearing adapter and the core ledger.\n\nOne payment, one identity, every system.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDo not retry until the true status is known, because a blind retry could create a duplicate payment of EUR 850. Trace the single identity through every system first, then act on what the evidence says.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The app status, failed\n→ The ledger posting, debited\n→ The true payment status, traced by identity\n→ The retry, only after the status is known\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the app say it failed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment's true status across every system?\"\n\nHave you seen a failed screen hide a payment that actually went through?", "takeaway": "Do not retry until the true status is known, because a blind retry could create a duplicate payment of EUR 850.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the transaction identity", "note": "One instruction ID, end-to-end ID and ledger reference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the channel", "note": "Follow the payment through the channel."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the payment engine", "note": "Follow it through the payment engine."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the clearing adapter", "note": "Follow it through the clearing adapter."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the core ledger", "note": "Follow it through the core ledger."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Decide before retrying", "note": "Do not retry until the true status is known."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-032", "seq": 451, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Gateway said success, EUR 40,000 never posted", "explain": "A payment gateway returns success for a EUR 40,000 supplier payment.\nThe channel shows the payment as accepted.\nHours later the supplier has not been paid, and the finance team finds no debit in the core banking ledger.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pain.001 instruction submitted through the channel\n→ Gateway returns a success response\n→ Channel shows the payment as accepted\n→ Core banking ledger shows no debit\n→ Payment engine still shows the payment as in progress\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the gateway says success, the payment is done.\"\n\nThe gateway is authoritative for one thing only: acceptance of the instruction. Posting the debit to the ledger is a separate step, and it failed silently. The engine state agreed with neither side, because it was never reconciled against the ledger.\n\nGateway success ≠ Ledger posted.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSeparate the three facts and find the authoritative source for each. The gateway proves acceptance. The ledger proves the debit posted. The engine state must be reconciled against both, and a success response from the gateway never proves the ledger posted.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gateway acceptance\n→ Ledger debit posting\n→ Payment engine state\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the gateway accept it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the debit posted?\"\n\nHave you chased a payment the channel called successful while the ledger said nothing happened?", "takeaway": "Gateway success does not prove the ledger posted. Check the posting, never trust the channel message.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Gateway returns success", "note": "The gateway confirms it accepted the instruction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ledger shows no debit", "note": "The core banking ledger has no posting for the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Engine still shows in progress", "note": "The engine state agrees with neither side."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which source is authoritative?", "note": "Gateway for acceptance, ledger for posting, never one for the other."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-071", "seq": 452, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Screen timed out, EUR 25,000 supplier payment in limbo", "explain": "A corporate customer submits a EUR 25,000 supplier payment through the online channel.\nThe screen spins, then times out.\nNo confirmation appears and no reference is shown. The payment may have been accepted, executed, or never received.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Instruction submitted through the online channel\n→ Screen spins and times out\n→ No confirmation and no reference shown\n→ Payment state is unknown\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the screen times out, the payment failed.\"\n\nA timeout only means the response was lost on the way back. The instruction may already have been accepted and executed downstream, so a retry on the same instruction can create a duplicate payment.\n\nA timeout is not a failure. It is an unknown.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake the timeout path explicit in the product. The channel must show a pending or unknown state, never a success and never a failure, until the outcome is confirmed. The status enquiry uses the original correlation ID and payment reference to ask the payment engine, then the ledger, then the downstream clearing status, in that order, and only then is a retry safe.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channel display state\n→ Payment engine state\n→ Ledger posting\n→ Downstream clearing status\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the screen time out?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment did or did not happen?\"\n\nHave you ever retried a payment after a timeout and found it had already gone through?", "takeaway": "A timeout is not a failure. It is an unknown, and a retry is safe only once the outcome is confirmed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Show pending or unknown", "note": "Never a success and never a failure until confirmed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ask the payment engine", "note": "Use the original correlation ID and payment reference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ask the ledger", "note": "Check the ledger next, in that order."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ask clearing status", "note": "Check downstream clearing status last."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Retry only when confirmed", "note": "A retry is safe only once the outcome is known."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"explain": "A bank processed 20,000 transactions in a day.\nThe morning reconciliation showed 500 transactions unmatched between the ledger and the clearing reports.\nA report called it a INR 40 lakh loss before anyone had investigated a single item.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ 20,000 transactions processed\n→ Ledger compared with clearing reports\n→ 500 transactions unmatched, an unmatched rate of 2.5 percent\n→ Illustrative exposure calculated as 500 multiplied by an average of INR 8,000, giving INR 40 lakh\n→ The illustrative figure reported as a confirmed loss\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Report the unmatched exposure as the loss.\"\n\nSo a framing figure became a headline loss, and the real investigation started on the back foot.\n\nWhy?\n\nUnmatched is not lost. Most unmatched items turn out to be timing differences, late clearing files, or posting lags that resolve on their own. The INR 40 lakh figure frames the scale of the problem, but the real exposure is only known once the unmatched transactions are investigated one by one.\n\nUnmatched is a question, not a loss.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPopulation, unmatched rate and confirmed loss are three different numbers, so keep them separate:\n\n→ 20,000 transactions processed\n→ 500 unmatched, 2.5 percent\n→ Confirmed loss only after item by item investigation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How big is the gap?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which unmatched items are real losses, and which are timing?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an illustrative exposure reported as a confirmed loss?", "id": "INS-033", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 455, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Unmatched is a question, not a loss.", "title": "500 unmatched out of 20,000, and a INR 40 lakh figure that was not a loss", "visual": {"stacked": {"segments": [{"label": "Matched transactions", "note": "19,500 of 20,000 transactions matched, 97.5 percent.", "value": 97.5}, {"label": "Unmatched transactions", "note": "500 of 20,000 transactions unmatched, 2.5 percent, still to be investigated.", "value": 2.5}], "unit": "%"}, "type": "stacked"}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-035", "seq": 456, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "App says EUR 4,120, the bank says EUR 3,850", "explain": "A customer checks the mobile app and sees a balance of EUR 4,120.\nShe calls the contact centre, and the agent's screen shows EUR 3,850.\nThe difference is EUR 270, exactly the amount of a card payment she made that morning.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Card payment of EUR 270 made this morning\n→ Mobile app shows EUR 4,120\n→ Contact centre screen shows EUR 3,850\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One balance must be wrong.\"\n\nNeither balance is wrong. The ledger balance is the posted balance in the core banking system and it is authoritative for posted funds, while the app balance may include pending or unposted items. The EUR 270 difference is the pending card payment, so the two balances are both accurate for what they represent, and the fix is to explain the difference rather than to change either balance.\n\nTwo balances can both be right. They measure different things.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStart by defining what each balance means before touching either one. The ledger balance is posted funds. The app balance may include pending items. When the difference matches a pending payment exactly, the answer is an explanation, not a correction.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Ledger balance, posted funds\n→ Available balance\n→ Pending and unposted items\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which balance is wrong?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what each balance represents?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain to a customer why two screens showed two different balances, and both were right?", "takeaway": "The EUR 270 difference is the pending card payment, so the two balances are both accurate for what they represent.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define each balance", "note": "Ledger balance is posted; the app may include pending items."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Identify the EUR 270 difference", "note": "It is the pending card payment from this morning."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Correct a balance or explain it?", "note": "Both are accurate for what they represent, so explain the difference."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Explain the difference", "note": "The customer sees why the two screens disagree."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch34-recurring-payments-and-3ri.html", "label": "Chapter 34: Recurring Payments and 3RI", "chapter": "ch34", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-057", "seq": 457, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One payment, three different amounts", "explain": "A cross-border payment complaint shows one amount in the online banking screen, a different amount transmitted by the API, and a third value held in the database.\nThe beneficiary address looks complete on screen, but arrives truncated in the payment message.\nEUR 8,000 was sent. Nobody can agree what the payment actually says.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer enters the payment on screen\n→ Screen displays one amount and a full address\n→ API transmits a different amount\n→ Database holds a third value\n→ Payment message arrives with a truncated address\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the screen looks right, the payment is right.\"\n\nThe value changed somewhere between the screen and the message. Fix the record first: pick one affected payment and its stable identifier, such as the end-to-end identification, and use that single identifier at every layer so the comparison is about the same record. Then capture the value at each point, what the customer entered and what the screen displayed, the API request payload as received, the transformed canonical model, the validation and defaulting applied, and the persisted record, to find where the value changed.\n\nThe screen is not the payment. The message is.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace one payment through every layer with one identifier. The screen, the API payload, the canonical model and the persisted record are four different facts, and the defect sits at the layer where the value first changes.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Value entered and displayed\n→ API request payload as received\n→ Transformed canonical model\n→ Validation and defaulting applied\n→ Persisted record\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What did the customer type?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the value changed between the screen and the message?\"\n\nHave you ever traced one payment through five layers and found the value changed in the middle?", "takeaway": "Capture the value at each point to find where it changed, using one stable identifier at every layer.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the record", "note": "Pick one payment and its end-to-end identification."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Capture screen values", "note": "What the customer entered and what the screen displayed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the API payload", "note": "The API request payload as received."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the canonical model", "note": "The transformed model, validation and defaulting."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the persisted record", "note": "The record as stored in the database."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Where did the value first change?", "note": "Compare the layers to find where it changed."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-036", "seq": 458, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Migration rehearsal: thousands of mismatches", "explain": "A bank migrates from a legacy core system to a new platform.\nThe first rehearsal shows thousands of mismatches: names that do not match, addresses that cannot be placed into the new structured format, dates in different formats, and payment records whose beneficiary details are incomplete.\nThousands of customers, and the new platform cannot tell who they are.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Legacy data extracted\n→ Field mapping applied\n→ Data transformed and loaded\n→ Control totals fail at every stage\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Move the data, then check it.\"\n\nA migration without proof at each stage is a hope, not a plan. Define the migration as a controlled data movement with proof at every stage: start with a field-level mapping from the legacy system to the new platform, with every source field, its target field, its transformation rule, its validation rule and its owner, then prove the movement with control totals and exception handling at each stage.\n\nA migration without proof at each stage is a hope, not a plan.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite the field-level mapping before moving a single record, and prove the movement with control totals and exception handling at every stage. A name, an address, a date and a beneficiary record each need a named owner and a validation rule.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Source field and target field\n→ Transformation rule\n→ Validation rule\n→ Control totals per stage\n→ Exception handling\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the data move?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every field arrived intact at each stage?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a rehearsal fail on thousands of records because nobody had mapped the fields first?", "takeaway": "A migration without proof at each stage is a hope, not a plan.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define the field mapping", "note": "Source field, target, transformation, validation, owner."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Prove movement with control totals", "note": "Control totals and exception handling at each stage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Handle exceptions", "note": "Exception handling proves the movement at each stage."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Go live or fix the mapping?", "note": "No proof at a stage means no go-live."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-064", "seq": 463, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Rs 18,500 debited, the order never arrived", "explain": "A customer pays Rs 18,500 for an order through a retailer's checkout.\nThe bank account is debited, the payment gateway reports success, and the order remains pending.\nNo confirmation, no dispatch, no delivery date. The money is gone and the goods never move.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer pays Rs 18,500 at checkout\n→ Bank account debited\n→ Gateway reports success\n→ Order stays pending, no confirmation\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Gateway success means the order exists.\"\n\nThe payment outcome was lost at one of four boundaries, and the fulfilment path and the refund path depend on which one failed. Start by fixing the reference: take the payment reference from the customer's bank statement and the gateway transaction ID, and confirm they belong to the same payment. Then trace the four boundaries in order, the gateway, the bank, the merchant callback and the order creation, to find where the payment outcome was lost.\n\nA debit without an order is a broken boundary, not a lost payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFix the reference first, then trace the four boundaries in order. The gateway, the bank, the merchant callback and the order creation each get checked, because the fix depends on which boundary lost the outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gateway outcome\n→ Bank outcome\n→ Merchant callback\n→ Order creation\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the customer charged?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which boundary lost the payment outcome?\"\n\nHave you ever paid for an order, been debited, and watched the order sit pending for days?", "takeaway": "Trace the four boundaries in order, the gateway, the bank, the merchant callback and the order creation, to find where the payment outcome was lost.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the reference", "note": "Payment reference and gateway transaction ID, same payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the gateway boundary", "note": "Find where the outcome was lost at the gateway."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the bank boundary", "note": "Find where the outcome was lost at the bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the merchant callback", "note": "Find where the outcome was lost at the callback."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which boundary failed?", "note": "Fulfilment or refund depends on which boundary failed."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-062", "seq": 464, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Paid and confirmed, but the shop never saw the order", "explain": "A retailer runs a checkout where the payment gateway returns a success response.\nThe customer sees a confirmation screen, and then nothing happens.\nThe order system never receives the callback, so no order is created, no goods are picked, and the customer is charged for a purchase the retailer cannot see.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Gateway returns success\n→ Customer sees a confirmation screen\n→ Order system never receives the callback\n→ No order created, customer charged\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Show the customer success as soon as the gateway answers.\"\n\nThe outcome was lost between the gateway and the order system. Contain the impact by identifying every payment with no matching order, stop telling customers a payment succeeded until its outcome is known, and give support a holding message that is honest about the uncertainty. Then trace one affected payment end to end using its correlation ID, from the gateway request and response through the payment service state, the event published to the bus, and the callback delivery, to find where the outcome was lost.\n\nSuccess is the order, not the gateway response.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStop confirming success until the outcome is known. Find every payment with no matching order, give support an honest holding message, and trace one payment end to end by correlation ID to find where the outcome was lost.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Gateway request and response\n→ Payment service state\n→ Event published to the bus\n→ Callback delivery\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the gateway answer?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the order system received the outcome?\"\n\nHave you ever been charged for an order the shop could not see?", "takeaway": "Trace one affected payment end to end using its correlation ID, from the gateway request and response through the payment service state, the event published to the bus, and the callback delivery.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Identify payments with no order", "note": "Find every payment with no matching order."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Stop confirming success", "note": "Stop telling customers a payment succeeded until known."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Give support a holding message", "note": "An honest message about the uncertainty."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace one payment end to end", "note": "Gateway, payment service, event bus and callback."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Where was the outcome lost?", "note": "The fix depends on which hop dropped it."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "name mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C047", "seq": 484, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Sent EUR 5,000, the employee received EUR 4,940", "explain": "An ordering customer pays an employee EUR 5,000 with an MT103.\nAn intermediary bank deducts its charges on the way.\nThe employee receives EUR 4,940, and asks why EUR 60 is missing.\nThe ordering customer has no answer, because nobody explained the charge rule before the payment was sent.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Ordering customer pays employee EUR 5,000 by MT103\n→ Intermediary deducts EUR 60 in charges\n→ Beneficiary bank credits EUR 4,940\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The amount sent is the amount received.\"\n\nThe beneficiary bank credits the amount that arrives after the deductions, and the ordering customer must explain the difference to the employee. The situation is a normal consequence of the serial method, and it is why the charge-bearer rule matters before the payment is sent.\n\nIn a serial payment, every hand takes its charge.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAgree the charge-bearer rule before sending, and tell the beneficiary what will arrive. Under the serial method each intermediary deducts its charge, so the amount received is the amount sent minus every hand along the way.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Amount sent\n→ Intermediary charges deducted\n→ Amount credited to the beneficiary\n→ Charge-bearer rule agreed up front\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How much did we send?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the beneficiary knows what will actually arrive?\"\n\nHave you ever had to explain to an employee why EUR 60 went missing between sending and receiving?", "takeaway": "The situation is a normal consequence of the serial method, and it is why the charge-bearer rule matters before the payment is sent.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Ordering customer pays employee by MT103", "note": "An ordering customer pays an employee with an MT103."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Intermediary deducts its charge", "note": "An intermediary bank deducts its charges from the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank credits the reduced amount", "note": "The beneficiary bank credits the amount that arrives after deductions."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Was the charge rule agreed up front?", "note": "The charge-bearer rule matters before the payment is sent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Ordering customer explains the shortfall", "note": "The ordering customer must explain the difference to the employee."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "charge deduction", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C076", "seq": 494, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Two legs, one payment, one thread: the UETR", "explain": "A EUR 110,000 payment passes through a correspondent bank on its way abroad.\nThe correspondent sees two payments: the incoming payment from the sender, and the outgoing payment to the next bank.\nTwo legs, two messages, and the reconciliation team cannot prove they are the same payment.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Correspondent receives the incoming payment\n→ Correspondent sends the outgoing payment onward\n→ UETR travels unchanged in both legs\n→ UETR carried in field 121 of block 3\n→ Bank reconciles incoming and outgoing legs\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each leg is a separate payment.\"\n\nThe two legs are linked by the unique end-to-end transaction reference, the UETR, which travels unchanged from one message to the next and is carried in field 121 of block 3. No bank in the chain may modify field 121, because changing it would break the link that lets every bank reconcile the payment it received with the payment it sent.\n\nThe UETR is the thread. Break it and the payment cannot be reconciled.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCarry the UETR unchanged through every leg, in field 121 of block 3. No bank in the chain modifies it, because it is the only link between the payment received and the payment sent.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Incoming leg\n→ Outgoing leg\n→ UETR in field 121 of block 3\n→ Reconciliation of both legs\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did both legs settle?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the incoming and outgoing legs are the same payment?\"\n\nHave you ever tried to reconcile two legs of one payment and found the thread between them had been changed?", "takeaway": "No bank in the chain may modify field 121, because changing it would break the link that lets every bank reconcile the payment it received with the payment it sent.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent sees two payments", "note": "The bank sees the incoming payment and the outgoing payment to the next bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "UETR travels unchanged in both legs", "note": "The unique end-to-end transaction reference travels unchanged between messages."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "UETR carried in field 121 of block 3", "note": "The UETR is carried in field 121 of block 3."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "No bank may modify field 121", "note": "Changing it would break the link between the payment received and the payment sent."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Do both legs carry the same UETR?", "note": "The UETR lets every bank reconcile the payment received with the payment sent."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C099", "seq": 496, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The MT101 debited the customer. Where did the money sit?", "explain": "A corporate customer sends an MT101 to pay its suppliers.\nThe debtor's account is debited at once.\nBut the suppliers are not paid yet, and the EUR 95,000 sits somewhere inside the bank, on an account nobody on the customer side can see.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT101 received from the customer\n→ Debtor account debited to an intermediary account\n→ MT103 generated to move the money onward\n→ Funds debited from the intermediary account\n→ Funds forwarded to the destination\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The MT101 moves the money.\"\n\nAccounting is done on the MT101 itself, in part: the debtor's account is debited and the funds are placed on an intermediary account inside the bank. When the MT103 is generated to move the money onward, the funds are debited from that intermediary account and forwarded to the destination. The MT101 therefore does the customer-side accounting, and the MT103 does the interbank settlement.\n\nThe MT101 does the customer-side accounting. The MT103 does the interbank settlement.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFollow the money through the intermediary account. The MT101 debits the debtor and parks the funds inside the bank, and only the MT103 moves them onward to the destination.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Debtor account debit\n→ Intermediary account inside the bank\n→ MT103 generation\n→ Interbank settlement\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the customer debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the funds moved from the intermediary account to the destination?\"\n\nHave you ever debited a customer on the MT101 and had to explain where the money sat before the MT103 went out?", "takeaway": "The MT101 therefore does the customer-side accounting, and the MT103 does the interbank settlement.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Debtor account debited to intermediary account", "note": "The debtor's account is debited and the funds go to an intermediary account in the bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 generated to move money onward", "note": "When the MT103 is generated, the funds are debited from that intermediary account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds forwarded to the destination", "note": "The funds are forwarded to the destination from the intermediary account."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "MT101 or MT103, which moved the money?", "note": "The MT101 does the customer-side accounting, the MT103 does the interbank settlement."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT101", "MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch50-mt101-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 50: MT101 in Depth", "chapter": "ch50", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-103", "seq": 501, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The MT103 has no town name. pacs.008 demands one.", "explain": "An MT103 arrives for a EUR 45,000 payment, and it must be translated into pacs.008.\nThe MT103 carries no structured address.\npacs.008 requires a town name and a country code, and the town cannot be invented.\nGuessing writes false data onto the payment. A blank fails validation. The payment is stuck between two message standards.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 taken in exactly as sent\n→ Tested against every field pacs.008 requires\n→ Town name missing, no source in the message\n→ Enrichment needed from an authoritative source\n→ Unresolvable gaps go to exception handling\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Translate the message and fill the gaps.\"\n\nMT to MX coexistence creates a data gap: an MT103 may carry no structured address, yet pacs.008 requires town name and country code. The missing town cannot be invented, guessing writes false data onto the payment and a blank fails validation. Handle the gap with explicit translation rules, enrichment from a trusted source, and exception governance for every field the old message cannot supply.\n\nA missing town cannot be invented. Guessing writes false data onto a payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite the translation rules before translating. Every field the old message cannot supply gets an enrichment rule from a trusted source, or a defined exception path, and guessing is never one of the options.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT103 as sent\n→ pacs.008 requirements\n→ Missing fields flagged\n→ Enrichment from an authoritative source\n→ Exception governance\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we translate it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every required field came from a trusted source, not a guess?\"\n\nHave you ever had a payment stuck because the old message could not supply what the new one demands?", "takeaway": "Handle the gap with explicit translation rules, enrichment from a trusted source, and exception governance for every field the old message cannot supply.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Take in the MT103", "note": "Take the old-format instruction exactly as sent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Test against MX requirements", "note": "Test it against every field pacs.008 requires."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Flag the missing fields", "note": "Flag each field the MT message cannot supply."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Enrich from an authoritative source", "note": "Fill gaps only from an authoritative source, never by guessing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Gap resolved or exception?", "note": "Route unresolvable gaps through a defined exception path."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103", "pacs.008"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "SCN-07", "seq": 507, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two legs settled, two returns needed", "explain": "A EUR 60,000 cover payment settles twice over: the pacs.008 instruction and the pacs.009 cover that moved funds between correspondents.\nThe customer payment is returned.\nThe team closes the case, and the nostro ledger still disagrees by EUR 60,000.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 instruction settles\n→ pacs.009 cover settles between correspondents\n→ Customer payment returned\n→ Cover leg still open\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Returning the customer payment unwinds the whole payment.\"\n\nReturning the customer payment unwinds only the first leg; the cover needs its own pacs.004, matched to the original cover by UETR and settlement references. Both legs post to the Nostro ledger, so reconciliation must pair all four messages, instruction, cover, return of instruction, and return of cover.\n\nOne return unwinds one leg. A cover payment needs its own return.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReturn both legs, and match each return to its original by UETR and settlement references. Reconciliation pairs all four messages, and the case closes only when the nostro ledger agrees.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Return of the instruction\n→ Return of the cover\n→ UETR matching\n→ Nostro ledger pairing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the customer refunded?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both legs were returned and the nostro ledger pairs all four messages?\"\n\nHave you ever returned the customer payment and forgotten the cover leg was still open?", "takeaway": "Both legs post to the Nostro ledger, so reconciliation must pair all four messages, instruction, cover, return of instruction, and return of cover.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Return the instruction leg", "note": "Customer payment returns through its own return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return the cover leg", "note": "Cover is a separate settlement with its own return."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Match on UETR", "note": "Match on UETR and settlement references."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Do all four messages pair?", "note": "Pair instruction, cover, and both returns."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "SCN-10", "seq": 508, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 5,000 debited, failed, and the books disagree", "explain": "A 5,000 euro SEPA credit transfer debited the account.\nIt failed between the engine and clearing.\nThe customer is EUR 5,000 down, the books say the payment went, and reality says it never arrived.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Account debited EUR 5,000\n→ Payment failed between engine and clearing\n→ Books and reality disagree\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Fix the customer first, reconcile later.\"\n\nBefore touching anything, establish the true state across core banking, payment engine, ISO 20022 messages, clearing, settlement and accounting, and rule out duplicates. Then reverse the debit or re-release the payment, post correcting entries with a clear audit trail, and bring every system to the same outcome.\n\nNever touch the money until the true state is known.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEstablish the true state first, across core banking, engine, messages, clearing, settlement and accounting, and rule out duplicates. Then reverse the debit or re-release the payment, post correcting entries with a clear audit trail, and bring every system to the same outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Core banking state\n→ Payment engine state\n→ Clearing and settlement state\n→ Accounting state\n→ Duplicate check\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How do we put it right?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what actually happened to the money?\"\n\nHave you ever had the books say a payment went, while the customer could prove it never arrived?", "takeaway": "Establish the true state across core banking, payment engine, ISO 20022 messages, clearing, settlement and accounting, and rule out duplicates before touching anything.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Establish the true state", "note": "Sent, rejected, duplicated, or never released."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Rule the duplicates out", "note": "Confirm no automatic retry made a second payment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Reverse or re-release?", "note": "Follow the customer instruction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Post the correcting entries", "note": "Tie the correction to the original debit."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the customer outcome", "note": "The customer statement is the final proof."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "SCN-27", "seq": 510, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Acknowledged, then lost: the broker died before replication", "explain": "A payment is charged once.\nThe retry charges it a second time.\nNothing alerted, because each component behaved exactly as designed, and the failure lived in the interaction between them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Leader acknowledges with acks=1\n→ Broker dies before replication\n→ Message lost, payment already charged\n→ Retry charges a second time\n→ No alert fires\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An acknowledgement means the message is safe.\"\n\nWith acks=1 only the leader acknowledges, so a broker dying before replication loses the message, yet the payment had already been charged and the retry charged a second time. Nothing alerted because each component behaved as designed; the failure lived in the interaction. Fix the guarantees, acks=all with idempotent producers and dedup keys on every payment, add the transactional outbox pattern so the business record and the event cannot diverge, and keep reconciliation as the backstop.\n\nEach component behaved as designed. The failure lived in the interaction.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStrengthen the guarantees: acks=all, idempotent producers, dedup keys on every payment, and the transactional outbox pattern so the business record and the event cannot diverge. Reconciliation stays as the backstop, because it catches what the real-time flow misses.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Acknowledgement guarantee\n→ Idempotent producers and dedup keys\n→ Transactional outbox\n→ Reconciliation as backstop\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did each component work?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the message survived, and the payment was charged once?\"\n\nHave you ever had every component pass its checks while the payment was still charged twice?", "takeaway": "Fix the guarantees, acks=all with idempotent producers and dedup keys on every payment, add the transactional outbox pattern, and keep reconciliation as the backstop.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Broker dies before replication ends", "note": "Leader acknowledged before replication finished."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Retry charges a second time", "note": "Believed failed, so the retry charged again."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Strengthen the messaging guarantees", "note": "acks=all, idempotent producers and dedup keys."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Add the transactional outbox", "note": "Record and event written atomically."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Reconcile as the backstop?", "note": "Double debits caught even when messaging fails."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-34", "seq": 512, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Processed, timed out, then paid twice", "explain": "A core banking API processes a EUR 35,000 transaction.\nThen it times out before responding.\nThe caller, believing it failed, re-submits a completed transaction, and the customer is debited twice.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ API processes the transaction\n→ Timeout before the response arrives\n→ Caller retry re-submits the completed transaction\n→ Customer debited twice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A timeout means it did not happen.\"\n\nThe missing controls were idempotency keys, a smart timeout strategy, and a reconciliation layer. The fix is idempotency keys on every mutating call, a pending-state transaction model that checks state before acting, and a standing rule that on any timeout the true state is queried first, asking what actually happened to the money.\n\nOn any timeout, ask what actually happened to the money.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut idempotency keys on every mutating call, keep a pending-state model that checks state before acting, and make it a standing rule: on any timeout, query the true state first, never blindly re-submit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Idempotency keys on every call\n→ Pending-state transaction model\n→ Smart timeout strategy\n→ Reconciliation layer\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the call time out?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves whether the transaction completed before the timeout?\"\n\nHave you ever re-submitted a transaction after a timeout and paid it twice?", "takeaway": "The fix is idempotency keys on every mutating call, a pending-state transaction model that checks state before acting, and a standing rule that on any timeout the true state is queried first.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "API times out once processed", "note": "Work done, response never arrived."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Retry submits it again", "note": "Completed transaction submitted again."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Put idempotency keys on every call", "note": "A repeat request is recognised, not re-executed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Add a pending-state model", "note": "Check state before acting, never blind re-submit."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Query the true state first?", "note": "Ask first what happened to the money."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-36", "seq": 513, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "UPI debit landed. The credit never did.", "explain": "A UPI payment is made for Rs 45,000.\nThe sender account is debited.\nThe receiver is never credited, and the credit acknowledgement timed out somewhere between the two banks.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Local debit commits\n→ Credit acknowledgement never arrives\n→ Retry meets duplicate suppression\n→ Message parks in an internal queue\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A debit means the credit followed.\"\n\nA UPI debit committed locally while the credit acknowledgement timed out, a partial commit across two systems: the sender account debited, the receiver never credited. A retry hit duplicate-suppression logic and the message parked in a queue, leaving one system committed and the other uncertain. The mitigations are globally unique transaction references, idempotent processing at each bank, compensating transactions such as auto-reversal, and settlement-level balancing at the end of the cycle.\n\nOne system committed, the other uncertain. That is a partial commit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for the partial commit. Globally unique references, idempotent processing at each bank, auto-reversal as the compensating transaction, and settlement-level balancing at the end of the cycle to catch what the real-time flow missed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Globally unique transaction reference\n→ Idempotent processing at each bank\n→ Auto-reversal\n→ Settlement-level balancing\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the sender debited?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the credit landed, or the debit was reversed?\"\n\nHave you ever been debited on UPI while the person you paid received nothing?", "takeaway": "The mitigations are globally unique transaction references, idempotent processing at each bank, compensating transactions such as auto-reversal, and settlement-level balancing at the end of the cycle.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "The local debit commits", "note": "Debit committed, that part certain."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Credit acknowledgement never arrives", "note": "Second leg left unconfirmed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Retry meets duplicate suppression", "note": "Message parks in an internal queue."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Auto-reversal compensates", "note": "Auto-reversal returns the debited funds."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Balance at settlement?", "note": "Catch what the real-time flow missed."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E06", "seq": 520, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "AC06 became MS03, and nobody could say why", "explain": "A return code mapping changes: AC06 for a blocked account becomes MS03, reason not specified.\nIt looks like a one-line change.\nThen the receiving bank asks why, the compliance team asks who approved it, and the payment engine cannot explain which blocked accounts produce AC06 at all.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Core banking knows the account is blocked\n→ Business interpretation decides the code\n→ Country rule applied\n→ Engine sends the agreed code\n→ Nobody can say why AC06 became MS03\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Mapping a return code is a one-line change.\"\n\nMapping AC06 for a blocked account to MS03, reason not specified, looks like a one-line change until the BA asks how core banking communicates the condition, what the payment engine actually understands, and which conditions should produce AC06 at all. Core banking may know the account is blocked for a KYC restriction, a debit block, dormancy, or another restriction, while the engine only understands that the account is blocked, and national data-protection requirements may forbid passing the specific reason downstream. The real gap is found by comparing what the ecosystem understands today with what the requirement expects, then tracing the chain from core banking through business interpretation, country rule, and mapping to the return code, and deciding which system changes and who agrees the mapping.\n\nThe gap usually sits between systems, not inside one.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the chain from core banking through business interpretation, country rule and mapping to the return code. Compare what the ecosystem understands today with what the requirement expects, then decide which system changes and who agrees the mapping.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Core banking condition\n→ Business interpretation\n→ Country rule and data protection\n→ Engine mapping\n→ Agreed owner of the mapping\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What code do we send?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each system understands the condition, and who agreed the mapping?\"\n\nHave you ever changed a return code and found nobody could explain which accounts it applied to?", "takeaway": "The real gap is found by comparing what the ecosystem understands today with what the requirement expects, then tracing the chain and deciding which system changes and who agrees the mapping.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Core banking knows the condition", "note": "KYC restriction, debit block, dormancy, or another account restriction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Business interpretation", "note": "Decide which conditions should produce AC06 and when MS03 applies instead."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Country rule and mapping", "note": "Apply the country-specific rule and map the condition to a return code."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment engine sends the code", "note": "The engine returns the agreed code to the receiving party."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Agree who changes what?", "note": "Gap analysis decides which system changes and who owns the mapping."}]}}, "stage": 4, "stage_name": "Cross-system consequences", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-127", "seq": 17, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 3,000 collection late because one of three calendars was closed", "explain": "A EUR 3,000 direct debit is due on a Monday.\nThe creditor's bank is open, the debtor's bank is open.\nThe collection still does not complete that day.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Creditor PSP open on the due date\n→ Debtor PSP open on the due date\n→ Clearing and settlement mechanism closed for a holiday\n→ The collection waits until the mechanism reopens\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If our bank is open, the collection will complete.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA direct debit collection depends on three calendars at once: the creditor PSP, the debtor PSP, and the clearing and settlement mechanism. If any one of them is closed on the due date, the collection cannot complete that day, even when the other two are open.\n\nTwo open calendars ≠ Three.\n\nPlanning on a single calendar, such as only the creditor's own business calendar, is what produces late debits.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlan every collection against all three calendars.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Creditor PSP calendar\n→ Debtor PSP calendar\n→ Clearing and settlement mechanism calendar\n→ Due date actually achievable\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is our bank open on the due date?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves all three calendars were open on the due date?\"\n\nHave you seen a collection go late because a third calendar nobody checked was closed?", "takeaway": "Planning on a single calendar, such as only the creditor's own business calendar, is what produces late debits.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Three calendars for a collection", "parts": [{"label": "Creditor PSP open", "note": "The creditor's bank is open on the due date"}, {"label": "Debtor PSP open", "note": "The debtor's bank is open on the due date"}, {"label": "Clearing mechanism closed", "note": "The clearing system is shut for a holiday"}, {"label": "Collection cannot complete", "note": "One closed calendar blocks the whole collection"}, {"label": "Check all three", "note": "A single calendar is never enough"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A customer sent USD 25,000 overseas.\nA domestic instant payment the same morning arrived in seconds.\nThe cross-border payment took two days.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 raised for USD 25,000\n→ Intermediary bank receives it after its cut-off\n→ Currency conversion queued for the next window\n→ Compliance and sanctions screening holds it\n→ Settlement waits for the next system operating hours\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Each leg is fast, so the whole payment will be fast.\"\n\nSo the customer was promised same-day delivery, and the promise broke at the first slow hop.\n\nWhy?\n\nA domestic instant payment moves inside one system under one set of rules. A cross-border payment passes through several payment service providers, currency conversions, compliance checks and settlement systems. Each hop adds time, and the hops depend on each other's operating hours. A fast leg cannot rescue a chain that waits overnight.\n\nA chain is only as fast as its slowest hop.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEach hop has its own clock, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Intermediary cut-off and operating hours\n→ Currency conversion window\n→ Compliance and sanctions screening\n→ Final settlement system hours\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast is each leg?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the full chain can complete inside the promised time?\"\n\nHave you ever promised a fast cross-border payment that a single slow hop delayed by a day?", "id": "INS-135", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 18, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A chain is only as fast as its slowest hop.", "title": "USD 25,000 cross-border in two days, domestic instant in seconds", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "USD 25,000 cross-border payment starts", "title": "pacs.008 raised"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Arrives after cut-off, waits for the next window", "title": "Intermediary receives it"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Currency conversion and compliance checks each add time", "title": "Conversion and screening clear?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Next system operating hours decide the final leg", "title": "Settlement waits"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "The chain waited at every hop", "title": "Two days, not seconds"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-002", "seq": 71, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 10,000 in a French bank account, sitting in New York", "explain": "A customer opens a USD 10,000 account with a bank in France.\nThe customer checks the balance in Paris and sees USD 10,000.\nThe actual dollars sit with the bank's correspondent in the USA.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer opens a USD account with the French bank\n→ The French bank records the account in its own books\n→ The actual USD rests with its correspondent in the USA\n→ The customer is credited only when the correspondent advises with an MT103\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"My dollars are in the bank where I opened the account.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a customer opens a USD account with a bank in France, the bank records the account in its own books while the actual USD rests with its correspondent in the USA. Funding comes from the customer buying USD or receiving it from someone, and the sending bank locates the French bank's US correspondent and routes the funds there. The correspondent then advises the French bank with an MT103, and only on that advice is the customer's account credited.\n\nA record in Paris ≠ Dollars in Paris.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCredit the customer only on the correspondent's advice, never on the local record alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer account recorded in the French bank's books\n→ Actual USD held by the US correspondent\n→ Funding: customer buys USD or receives it\n→ MT103 advice from the correspondent, triggering the credit\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we record the account?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the correspondent holds the funds before we credit the customer?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer told their foreign currency was in the branch, when it sat overseas?", "takeaway": "The correspondent then advises the French bank with an MT103, and only on that advice is the customer's account credited.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer opens a USD account", "note": "The French bank records the account in its own books"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer funds the account", "note": "The customer buys USD or receives it from someone"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sending bank finds the US correspondent", "note": "It locates the French bank's correspondent in the USA"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent advises with MT103", "note": "The correspondent advises the French bank that funds are for the customer"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Credit now, or wait for the advice?", "note": "Only on that advice is the customer's account credited"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-003", "seq": 72, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 1 million nostro opened, and the first payment bounced on zero", "explain": "A bank opens a new USD nostro and promises a USD 1 million payment from it the same day.\nThe payment bounces.\nThe account opened at zero, and nobody had funded it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank asks a US bank to open the nostro account\n→ The account opens empty, at zero\n→ The team promises a USD 1 million payment from it\n→ The FX deal to fund it was never placed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A new nostro opens with money in it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCurrency never leaves its home country, so a bank funds a new nostro by buying the foreign currency on the FX market and naming the nostro as the account to receive it. Where no banking relationship exists yet, the bank first asks a bank in that currency's country to open the account, which starts at zero, and only then does the FX deal. Where the bank already holds the currency in an account elsewhere, it simply instructs that bank to move the funds across, with no FX deal needed.\n\nAn open account ≠ A funded account.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFund the nostro before promising any payment from it, by FX deal or by moving funds already held.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Account opened, starting at zero\n→ FX deal buying the currency, naming the nostro\n→ Funds already held elsewhere, moved across\n→ Payment promised only after funding lands\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we open the nostro?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the nostro was funded before the first payment was promised?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment bounce from an account that had only just been opened?", "takeaway": "Where the bank already holds the currency in an account elsewhere, it simply instructs that bank to move the funds across, with no FX deal needed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "New nostro requested", "note": "The bank asks a US bank to open the account"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Account opens at zero", "note": "The nostro starts empty, with no funds"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Relationship already in place?", "note": "Without one, the account must be opened before any FX deal"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "FX deal funds it", "note": "The bank buys USD and names the nostro to receive it"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Or move funds from elsewhere", "note": "Currency already held elsewhere moves across with no FX deal"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D07", "seq": 92, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 5,000 to another country, no link to send it on", "explain": "A bank wants to send EUR 5,000 instantly to a country its system has never paid.\nIt has no link to that country's instant system.\nBuilding one link for every country would take years.\nSo the payment waits.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Domestic instant system pays in seconds at home\n→ No one-to-one link to the other country's system\n→ Every pair of countries needs its own connection\n→ Cross-border instant payment cannot start\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each country must connect to every other country.\"\n\nProject Nexus takes the other path: link domestic instant payment systems across countries so a system joins Nexus a single time and through that link can pay any other country on the network.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach national system stays independent, keeping its own rules and running its own business, while Nexus serves as the central hub and global connector. Connection is not interaction: one connection to the hub replaces a web of bilateral links.\n\nConnect once, reach many countries.\n\nWhy does a payment that is instant at home wait days abroad?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge a cross-border design by connections, not by countries, and keep each system independent behind the hub.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic system, its own rules, its own business\n→ Nexus connection, one link to the hub\n→ Reach, every other country on the network through that link\n\nShow the hub connection and the countries it reaches on one map, not a list of bilateral links.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How many countries are we linked to?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves one connection reaches them all?\"\n\nHave you waited on a cross-border payment that was instant at home?", "takeaway": "Connect once, reach many countries. A central platform replaces a web of bilateral links.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "One connection, many countries", "parts": [{"label": "One connection", "note": "Each system joins the hub once instead of building one-to-one links."}, {"label": "Many countries", "note": "Through the hub, a system can pay any other country on the network."}, {"label": "Systems stay independent", "note": "Each country keeps its own rules and runs its own business."}, {"label": "Central hub", "note": "The hub links domestic instant payment systems across countries."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D13", "seq": 94, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 1,000 sent abroad, USD 60 gone in cost, three days gone in time", "explain": "A customer sends USD 1,000 to family abroad.\nUSD 60 disappears in cost.\nThree days pass before it arrives.\nThe same amount moves in a second at home.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Domestic payment, one second, low cost\n→ Cross-border payment, three days, about 6 percent cost\n→ Outdated cross-border system carries most of the blame\n→ Instant systems already run in many countries, but the global gap stays\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The money itself is slow and expensive to move.\"\n\nIt is the system, not the money. A payment that takes a second domestically can take three days across borders, and the outdated cross-border system carries most of the blame.\n\nWhy?\n\nInstant payment systems have already changed this in many countries, including India with UPI, Europe with SEPA Instant, Singapore, Brazil, the United States, the United Kingdom and Thailand, yet a global money transfer is still said to cost about 6 percent of the transaction value against a target of 3 percent. Reaching people in remote areas who do not use smartphones needs processing systems, not just mobile apps.\n\nSlow and costly is a system problem, not a money problem.\n\nWhy does USD 1,000 cost USD 60 and three days to cross a border?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFix the system behind the payment, and design for people without smartphones as well as people with them.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Speed, seconds at home, days abroad\n→ Cost, about 6 percent against a 3 percent target\n→ Reach, processing systems for remote areas, not just apps\n\nShow the cost and the arrival time before the customer confirms, and name the system behind each leg.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast is the payment?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cost and the arrival time the customer was promised?\"\n\nHave you paid 6 percent to send money that moves in a second at home?", "takeaway": "Reaching people in remote areas needs processing systems, not just mobile apps.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Slow, expensive, and changing", "parts": [{"label": "3 days across borders", "note": "A payment that takes a second domestically can take days cross-border."}, {"label": "Outdated system", "note": "The old cross-border payment system is the main culprit."}, {"label": "Instant in many countries", "note": "UPI, SEPA Instant and others already work in seconds domestically."}, {"label": "Cost: 6 percent vs 3 percent target", "note": "Global transfer costs about 6 percent, the target is 3 percent."}, {"label": "Remote areas need systems", "note": "Offline modes and processing systems, not just mobile apps."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D15", "seq": 96, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 20,000 instant in Europe, stuck for hours outside it", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 20,000 instantly inside Europe.\nIt arrives in seconds.\nThe same customer sends EUR 20,000 just outside Europe.\nIt takes hours, and no one can say why.\nThe rail was instant. The payment was not.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ SCT Inst delivers instant payments across Europe\n→ OCT Inst extends instant beyond Europe\n→ Switches and interoperability link the systems\n→ Liquidity, FX pricing, compliance and settlement still lag behind\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Link the instant systems and the payment becomes instant.\"\n\nSwitches and interoperability do not solve everything. Liquidity, visible FX pricing and the correspondent bank role all still count, compliance has to keep up, UETR traceability helps resolve cases on time, and clearing and settlement still occur between systems.\n\nWhy?\n\nCross-border payments become truly instant only when rules are clear, systems are aligned, and technology works with people, so the experience of the payment matters as much as the speed. One fast leg cannot carry a chain whose other pieces run on slower clocks.\n\nOne instant leg does not make an instant payment.\n\nWhy does the payment stall for hours after the instant leg finishes in seconds?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMake every piece instant, or say plainly which piece is not.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Instant rails, SCT Inst and OCT Inst\n→ Liquidity and FX transparency, priced and funded upfront\n→ Compliance at instant speed, with UETR traceability\n→ Clearing and settlement between systems\n\nShow the speed of every step, not just the fastest one, and flag the slowest step to the customer.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the rail instant?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every step of this payment is fast?\"\n\nHave you watched an instant payment stall on its slowest leg?", "takeaway": "Cross-border payments become truly instant only when rules are clear, systems are aligned, and technology works with people.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What makes cross-border instant", "parts": [{"label": "Instant systems, interlinked", "note": "Domestic instant systems linked together make payments global."}, {"label": "SCT Inst and OCT Inst", "note": "Instant payments across Europe and beyond."}, {"label": "Liquidity and FX transparency", "note": "Liquidity, FX transparency and the correspondent bank still matter."}, {"label": "Compliance keeps up", "note": "Compliance must work at instant-payment speed."}, {"label": "UETR traceability", "note": "Traceability helps resolve cases on time."}, {"label": "Clearing and settlement", "note": "Settlement still occurs between systems."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-137", "seq": 143, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 25,000 instant payment, three steps fast, one step slow, promise broken", "explain": "A bank promises EUR 25,000 instant payments.\nThe channel is fast. The engine is fast. Clearing is fast.\nSettlement is slow.\nThe customer promise breaks, even though three steps are instant.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer channel, fast\n→ Payment engine, fast\n→ Clearing, fast\n→ Settlement, slow, the bottleneck\n→ Customer waits, promise broken\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The rail is instant, so the payment is instant.\"\n\nFor an instant payment to feel instant end to end, every step must be fast: the customer channel, the payment engine, clearing and settlement.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf any one step is slow, the customer promise breaks even when the other steps are instant. The work is to find which step is the bottleneck and optimise it, rather than assuming the rail alone makes the payment instant.\n\nOne slow step breaks an instant promise.\n\nWhy does an instant rail still deliver a slow payment?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFind the bottleneck step and fix it, and never sell the rail speed as the payment speed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer channel, must be fast\n→ Payment engine, must be fast\n→ Clearing, must be fast\n→ Settlement, must be fast\n→ Bottleneck, the slowest step, named and fixed\n\nMeasure the time of every step, and report the slowest as the payment speed.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the rail instant?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every step is fast, including the slowest one?\"\n\nHave you promised instant and delivered it everywhere except one step?", "takeaway": "The work is to find which step is the bottleneck and optimise it, rather than assuming the rail alone makes the payment instant.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer channel", "note": "Customer channel must be fast."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment engine", "note": "Payment engine must be fast."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Clearing", "note": "Clearing must be fast."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement", "note": "Settlement must be fast."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which step is the bottleneck?", "note": "Find which step is the bottleneck and optimise it."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "INS-143", "seq": 146, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 80,000 instant payment, FX quote expires while screening runs", "explain": "A bank sends EUR 80,000 instantly to a non-euro account.\nThe FX quote is locked at 09:00.\nScreening runs long.\nAt 09:04 the quote has expired, and the payment cannot settle at the promised rate.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank relies on an FX provider, no liquidity in every currency\n→ Provider sources quotes, locks rates, executes conversion\n→ Screening runs while the quote clock ticks\n→ Quote expires, or the provider has no currency when needed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The FX provider will be there when the payment needs it.\"\n\nA bank that does not hold liquidity in every currency relies on an FX provider to source quotes, lock rates, execute the conversion and supply the settlement currency.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe arrangement works on quiet days, but a quote can expire while screening runs, or the provider may not have the currency when it is needed. The FX provider is a dependency in the payment chain, and its failure modes must be designed for, because a chain is only as reliable as its weakest link.\n\nThe provider is a link in the chain, not a guarantee.\n\nWhy does an instant payment fail on a quote that was good minutes ago?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign for the quote expiring and the currency running out, and hold the payment until the FX leg is proved.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Quote, sourced and locked, with its expiry shown\n→ Screening, timed against the quote clock\n→ Conversion, executed only inside the quote validity\n→ Settlement currency, supplied and confirmed\n\nShow the quote expiry beside the screening time, and re-quote before executing if the clock ran out.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was a quote locked?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the quote was still valid when the conversion executed?\"\n\nHave you watched a quote expire mid-payment?", "takeaway": "The FX provider is a dependency in the payment chain, and its failure modes must be designed for.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Bank relies on FX provider", "note": "Bank does not hold liquidity in every currency."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Source quotes, lock rates", "note": "FX provider sources quotes and locks rates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Execute the conversion", "note": "FX provider executes the conversion."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Supply settlement currency", "note": "FX provider supplies the settlement currency."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Quote still valid?", "note": "Quote can expire while screening runs."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Currency available?", "note": "Provider may not have the currency when it is needed."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-144", "seq": 147, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "USD 100,000 instant dollars promised, no dollars to settle with on Monday morning", "explain": "A bank promises instant dollar payments.\nOn Monday morning a customer sends USD 100,000.\nThe rate is competitive. The system is ready.\nThere are not enough dollars to settle with.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank offers instant dollar payments at a good rate\n→ Customer sends USD 100,000 on Monday morning\n→ Settlement currency not held in enough volume\n→ Payment fails, not on price, on liquidity\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A good rate means the payment can settle.\"\n\nHaving a competitive exchange rate is not the same as having the settlement currency available at the moment it is needed.\n\nWhy?\n\nA bank can offer instant dollar payments and still fail on some mornings because it does not hold enough dollars to settle. The constraint is liquidity, not conversion, and it must be managed by treasury, not just by the payment engine.\n\nRate is not liquidity.\n\nWhy does a payment with a great rate fail on a Monday morning?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nManage settlement liquidity with treasury, and never promise instant settlement the nostro cannot fund.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Competitive rate, what the customer is offered\n→ Currency availability, what treasury holds for settlement\n→ Instant promise, only where liquidity is proved\n→ Treasury management, funding the nostro before the morning peak\n\nShow the nostro balance beside the instant promise, and pause the promise when liquidity is short.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the rate competitive?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the settlement currency is held for this payment?\"\n\nHave you seen a good rate fail for want of the currency itself?", "takeaway": "The constraint is liquidity, not conversion, and it must be managed by treasury, not just by the payment engine.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Currency liquidity constraint", "parts": [{"label": "Competitive rate", "note": "Bank can offer a competitive exchange rate."}, {"label": "Currency must be available", "note": "Settlement currency must be available at the moment it is needed."}, {"label": "Instant dollar payments", "note": "Bank can offer instant dollar payments and still fail on some mornings."}, {"label": "Not enough dollars", "note": "Bank may not hold enough dollars to settle."}, {"label": "Liquidity, not conversion", "note": "Constraint is liquidity, not conversion."}, {"label": "Managed by treasury", "note": "Must be managed by treasury, not just by the payment engine."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "INS-150", "seq": 149, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 45,000 across two instant systems, timeout, customer taps send again", "explain": "A customer sends EUR 45,000 across two fast payment systems.\nThe screen times out.\nThe customer taps send again.\nNow the bank has two instructions and one debit, and nobody knows if the money moved.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment crosses two fast payment systems joined by an orchestration layer\n→ Timeout occurs, outcome uncertain\n→ Money may have moved, or it may not have\n→ Customer taps send again, two instructions, one debit\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A timeout means it failed, so sending again is safe.\"\n\nIn a corridor where a payment crosses two fast payment systems joined by an orchestration layer, a timeout leaves the outcome uncertain: the money may have moved, or it may not have.\n\nWhy?\n\nIf the customer taps send again, the bank can end up with two instructions and one debit. The safe handling is to establish the true state, never retry blindly, and use an inter-PSP investigation to resolve the uncertainty.\n\nTimeout is not failure. It is unknown.\n\nWhy does a helpful retry create a second instruction?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever retry blind. Establish the true state first, then act on it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Timeout, outcome unknown, not failed\n→ True state, established before any retry\n→ Retry, only after the state is known\n→ Inter-PSP investigation, resolving the uncertainty\n\nBlock the send button after a timeout until the true state is shown, and open the investigation at once.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the customer retry?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the true state was known before any retry?\"\n\nHave you seen a retry double an instruction after a timeout?", "takeaway": "The safe handling is to establish the true state, never retry blindly, and use an inter-PSP investigation to resolve the uncertainty.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Two fast systems joined", "note": "Payment crosses two fast payment systems joined by an orchestration layer."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Timeout occurs", "note": "Timeout leaves the outcome uncertain."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Money moved or not?", "note": "Money may have moved, or it may not have."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer taps send again", "note": "Customer taps send again."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Two instructions, one debit", "note": "Bank can end up with two instructions and one debit."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish the true state", "note": "Safe handling is to establish the true state, never retry blindly."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Inter-PSP investigation", "note": "Use an inter-PSP investigation to resolve the uncertainty."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-03", "seq": 157, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "One extra hour at the branch, and the RTGS payment bounced", "explain": "A branch cut-off for RTGS is extended by one hour.\n\nIt sounds small. It is not.\n\nA Rs. 50 lakh payment accepted inside the new hour is rejected downstream, because the clearing window never moved.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Branch cut-off extended by one hour\n→ Payment accepted inside the new hour\n→ Settlement windows stay fixed\n→ Core banking hours, batch jobs, standing instructions and reconciliation still read the old clock\n→ Payment accepted past the clearing window is rejected downstream\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Moving the cut-off is a parameter change.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA cut-off is a contract between the bank, the clearing system and every downstream batch. Moving it means re-validating everything that depends on it.\n\nA cut-off is a contract, not a clock setting.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTreat a cut-off change as a full regression exercise, not a parameter tweak. Every dependent system must be re-validated before the new time goes live.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Branch cut-off\n→ Clearing window\n→ Downstream batches and reconciliation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What time is the new cut-off?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every dependent system reads the new cut-off?\"\n\nHave you seen a small timing change break a payment downstream?", "takeaway": "A cut-off is a contract, not a clock setting. Moving it means re-validating everything that depends on it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cut-off extended one hour", "note": "Branch cut-off for RTGS extended by one hour"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment accepted in new hour", "note": "A Rs. 50 lakh payment is accepted"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settlement windows are fixed", "note": "The clearing window never moved"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Dependent systems read old clock", "note": "Core banking, batches, standing instructions and reconciliation"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "All dependencies re-validated?", "note": "Moving the cut-off means re-validating everything that depends on it"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment rejected downstream", "note": "A payment past the clearing window is rejected downstream"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B05", "seq": 166, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The German Direct Debit flow met the Dutch customer, and failed", "explain": "A Direct Debit process defined for Germany works well there.\n\nThe bank reuses it for the Netherlands.\n\nA customer moves to a new Dutch bank, the debit for the closed account is rejected, and a EUR 5,000 monthly payment bounces. It should have been redirected.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ German Direct Debit process is reused for the Netherlands\n→ Customer moves to a new Dutch bank\n→ Debit arrives for the closed account\n→ Local Overstapservice rule requires redirecting, not rejecting\n→ Debit is rejected, and the payment fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Reuse the process that worked in Germany.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe Netherlands has its own local specification, the Overstapservice, a bank-switching service that lets Direct Debits continue after a customer moves to a new Dutch bank. Reuse looks efficient, yet efficiency is not the test.\n\nReuse is a delivery choice, not a business decision.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore agreeing to reuse, check whether the requirement is a common scheme rule or a local specification, and whether the new country adds requirements the existing logic never had to meet.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Common scheme rule\n→ Local specification\n→ New country requirements\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we reuse this?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the reused logic meets the local rule?\"\n\nHave you seen a reused process fail because the new country had a rule the old one never needed?", "takeaway": "Reuse is a delivery choice, not a business decision. Check the local rule first.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reuse is not a business decision", "parts": [{"label": "German process worked well", "note": "The existing process was defined for Germany and worked well there"}, {"label": "Customer moves Dutch bank", "note": "A customer moves to a new Dutch bank, and a EUR 5,000 debit follows"}, {"label": "Dutch Overstapservice", "note": "A bank-switching service that lets Direct Debits continue after a move"}, {"label": "Redirect, not reject", "note": "An incoming Direct Debit for a closed account may need redirecting, not rejecting"}, {"label": "Common rule or local spec?", "note": "Check whether the requirement is a common scheme rule or a local specification"}, {"label": "New country requirements", "note": "Check whether the new country adds requirements the existing logic never met"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "invalid beneficiary data", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "PAI-C038", "seq": 260, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 50,000 to a bank that is not on SWIFT", "explain": "USD 50,000 had to reach a bank that is not a SWIFT member.\nThe MT103 could not be sent to it directly.\nThe payment sat for a day while the team argued whether it could be sent at all.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Beneficiary bank is not a SWIFT member\n→ MT103 cannot be sent to it directly\n→ Member bank forwards the instruction as intermediary\n→ Payment follows the usual serial or cover logic\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No SWIFT membership means no payment.\"\nNon-membership does not stop the payment. It adds a step to the chain.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe payment must reach the beneficiary bank through a bank that is a member and can forward the instruction. The forwarding bank becomes an intermediary in the chain, and the payment follows the same serial or cover logic as any other payment with an intermediary.\n\nNon-membership does not stop the payment. It adds a step to the chain.\n\nWhat proves the payment can reach a bank outside SWIFT?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute the payment through a member bank that can forward it. Name the forwarding bank as the intermediary, and apply the same serial or cover logic as any other payment with an intermediary.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Beneficiary bank\n→ Forwarding member bank\n→ Route chosen\n→ Payment delivered\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we pay a bank that is not on SWIFT?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment can reach a bank outside SWIFT?\"\n\nHave you ever had to route a payment through a forwarding bank?", "takeaway": "Non-membership does not stop the payment. It adds a step to the chain.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A beneficiary bank outside SWIFT", "parts": [{"label": "MT103 cannot be sent to it directly", "note": "The MT103 cannot go directly to a non-member bank"}, {"label": "Forwarding bank becomes an intermediary", "note": "A member bank forwards the instruction onward"}, {"label": "Same serial or cover logic applies", "note": "The payment follows the usual serial or cover logic"}, {"label": "Non-membership adds a step", "note": "Non-membership adds a step, it does not stop the payment"}, {"label": "Tell the customer the route", "note": "One extra step, not a blocked payment."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-008", "seq": 308, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A small bank sent EUR 500,000 abroad. It owned no correspondent anywhere.", "explain": "A small bank needs to send EUR 500,000 to a supplier overseas.\nIt holds no correspondent account in that currency.\nThe payment still goes through, and the customer never asks how.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Small bank takes a EUR 500,000 payment\n→ No correspondent in the payment currency\n→ Large domestic bank intermediates the payment\n→ Funds settle with the correspondent in the currency country\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A bank must hold its own correspondent to send money abroad.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBuilding and maintaining a correspondent network is costly and slow, so a small bank often opens a foreign currency account with a large bank in its own country that already holds a correspondent in the currency's country. That large bank then acts as intermediary for the small bank's cross-border payments, while the real funds still end up held in the currency's country.\n\nNo correspondent of your own does not mean no route abroad.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap the real chain on every corridor, and know which bank in the chain actually holds the funds.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Small bank account\n→ Intermediary bank\n→ Correspondent in the currency country\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which bank in the chain holds the funds?\"\n\nHave you traced a payment through a bank that was only ever an intermediary?", "takeaway": "No correspondent of your own does not mean no route abroad.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Small bank opens a local account", "note": "It opens a foreign currency account with a large bank at home."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Large bank brings its correspondent", "note": "That bank already holds a correspondent in the currency's country."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the large bank serve this currency?", "note": "If not, the route fails before the payment starts."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Large bank acts as intermediary", "note": "It intermediates the small bank's cross-border payments."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds stay in the currency country", "note": "The real funds still end up held where the currency belongs."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-013", "seq": 313, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Treasury bought EUR 5 million of FX. The nostro was still empty.", "explain": "Treasury buys EUR 5 million to fund the day's payments.\nThe mirror account shows the credit.\nThe payments still fail, because the real nostro was never checked.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Treasury buys FX to fund the day\n→ Funds land in the correspondent account\n→ Mirror reflects the credit\n→ Real nostro balance never verified\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"If the mirror shows the funds, the nostro has them.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA treasury team moves no money itself. It watches balances on the correspondent accounts and buys or sells foreign exchange to hold enough funds for the day's payments. When it buys currency in the FX market, the funds land in the correspondent bank account in the foreign country, and the mirror account simply reflects that credit. The mirror does nothing on its own. It only records what happened on the real account.\n\nTreasury funds the nostro. The mirror only records it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFund against the real nostro balance, confirmed by advice, never against the mirror alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Treasury FX trades\n→ Real nostro balance\n→ Mirror reflection\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds actually landed in the correspondent account?\"\n\nHave you trusted a mirror balance that the real account did not back?", "takeaway": "Treasury funds the nostro. The mirror only records it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Treasury watches the balances", "note": "The team monitors funds on the correspondent accounts."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Treasury buys or sells FX", "note": "It trades FX to hold enough funds for the day's payments."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Did the funds land in the real account?", "note": "The mirror alone cannot prove it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Funds land abroad", "note": "Bought currency is credited to the correspondent account overseas."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Mirror only reflects it", "note": "The mirror records the credit and does nothing on its own."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-014", "seq": 314, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "EUR 400,000 crossed two countries. The money never left the country it started in.", "explain": "A EUR 400,000 payment crosses borders between two banks.\nThe SWIFT message travels internationally.\nThe money itself settles locally, and the customer is charged the international price anyway.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Cover leg between two correspondents\n→ Both correspondents sit in the same country\n→ Local clearing settles the leg\n→ Customer charged as if funds crossed borders\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Cover funds always travel over SWIFT.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn a cross-border payment the cover leg moves funds between the sender's correspondent and the receiver's correspondent, and where both correspondents sit in the same country a local clearing system can settle that leg. The payment message still travels over SWIFT, but the money itself moves locally. The local route costs less than moving the funds over the SWIFT network.\n\nSame country, same clearing. The message travels, the money stays local.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck where both correspondents sit before choosing the cover route, and price the payment on the route the money actually takes.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover leg\n→ Correspondent locations\n→ Settlement route\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where the cover funds actually settled?\"\n\nHave you paid international prices for money that settled locally?", "takeaway": "Same country, same clearing. The message travels, the money stays local.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Cover moves between correspondents", "note": "The cover leg moves funds from sender's to receiver's correspondent."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Both correspondents in one country?", "note": "If both sit in one country, local clearing can settle the leg."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Local clearing settles it", "note": "The money moves locally through the clearing system."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Message still goes by SWIFT", "note": "The payment message still travels over SWIFT."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Local route costs less", "note": "Local settlement is cheaper than moving funds over SWIFT."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-015", "seq": 315, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "A EUR 150,000 payment to Germany went by MT202 COV. SEPA would have cost less.", "explain": "A bank outside Europe instructs its French correspondent to pay EUR 150,000 to a German customer.\nThe payment goes out as an MT202 COV.\nIt arrives late and expensive, when SEPA would have done it faster and cheaper.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Bank outside Europe instructs its French correspondent\n→ EUR 150,000 to a German bank customer\n→ MT202 COV sent over SWIFT\n→ SEPA route never considered\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A euro payment to Europe always needs an MT202 COV.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a bank outside Europe instructs its correspondent in France to pay a customer of a German bank in euros, it need not use an MT202 COV. The correspondent can pay through SEPA or another local system instead, and in practice that local route is the one used most often. It also costs less than sending the funds over the SWIFT network.\n\nA euro leg in Europe belongs on SEPA, not on an MT202 COV.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute the euro leg through the local system first, and use the SWIFT cover only when the local route cannot serve it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Euro leg\n→ SEPA route\n→ MT202 COV route\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the local route could not serve this payment?\"\n\nHave you sent a European euro payment the expensive way out of habit?", "takeaway": "A euro leg in Europe belongs on SEPA, not on an MT202 COV.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Bank outside Europe instructs", "note": "It asks its French correspondent to pay a German bank customer in euros."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Can SEPA serve this euro leg?", "note": "If yes, the MT202 COV is the expensive detour."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent uses SEPA", "note": "SEPA or another local system replaces the MT202 COV."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Local route is the norm", "note": "That local route is the usual choice in practice."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Local route costs less", "note": "It costs less than sending funds over the SWIFT network."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT202"], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-016", "seq": 316, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 20,000 in cash, handed over in Paris. The dollars were in New York.", "explain": "A customer with a USD account in France asks for USD 20,000 in cash.\nThe real dollars sit with the bank's correspondent in the USA.\nThe branch hands over the cash anyway, and the account is debited the same amount.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer asks for USD 20,000 cash\n→ Real dollars sit with the US correspondent\n→ Branch pays from its cash stock\n→ Same amount debited from the USD account\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Foreign cash can only be paid where the currency sits.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA customer holding a USD account with a bank in France can ask for the funds in physical USD cash, even though the real dollars sit with the bank's correspondent in the USA. The bank keeps an estimated stock of USD cash on hand, sized on customer demand, and sources it from customers selling USD for euros or from another bank. It hands over the cash and debits the same amount from the customer's USD account.\n\nCash on hand is not the nostro. The debit is what balances it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep the cash stock and the nostro apart in every control, and reconcile the cash paid against the account debited the same day.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cash stock on hand\n→ USD account debit\n→ Correspondent nostro\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cash paid matches the account debited?\"\n\nHave you ever assumed the cash in the branch was the money in the account?", "takeaway": "Cash on hand is not the nostro. The debit is what balances it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer asks for USD cash", "note": "A USD account holder in France requests physical USD cash."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Real dollars sit in the USA", "note": "The actual dollars rest with the bank's US correspondent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank keeps cash on hand", "note": "It holds an estimated stock of USD cash, sized on demand."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the cash stock enough for this withdrawal?", "note": "If not, source it before promising the cash."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cash paid, account debited", "note": "The cash is handed over and the same amount is debited."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F13", "seq": 364, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 20,000 cross-border payment arrived in seconds. The customer still could not see what it cost.", "explain": "A EUR 20,000 cross-border payment arrives in seconds.\nThe customer cannot see the FX cost, cannot trace the payment, and the remittance data is too thin for the checks.\nSpeed was delivered, and almost nothing else was.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 20,000 instant cross-border payment sent\n→ FX cost hidden from the customer\n→ Payment cannot be traced\n→ Remittance data too thin for AML checks\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Instant cross-border payments are only about speed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nInstant cross-border payments are not only about speed, because several pieces must work together for the whole journey to be efficient. Clearing, processing and settlement must become more efficient, FX conversion must be transparent to the customer, and payment status must be traceable, while richer remittance information supports AML and fraud checks, and common messaging standards and APIs make systems interoperable. When these pieces combine, transaction time and end-user costs fall, which is the real transformation.\n\nInstant is only one piece. Speed without the rest is not a transformation.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the whole journey together: efficient settlement, transparent FX, traceability, rich remittance data and common standards.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ FX transparency\n→ Payment traceability\n→ Remittance data\n→ Common standards\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the customer could see the cost and trace this payment?\"\n\nHave you shipped speed and called the journey done?", "takeaway": "Instant is only one piece. Speed without the rest is not a transformation.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instant is not just speed", "parts": [{"label": "Efficient processing", "note": "Faster clearing, processing and settlement"}, {"label": "FX transparency", "note": "The customer sees the conversion cost"}, {"label": "Status traceability", "note": "The payment journey can be tracked"}, {"label": "Richer remittance data", "note": "More information for AML and fraud checks"}, {"label": "Common standards and APIs", "note": "Interoperability across systems"}, {"label": "Lower end-user costs", "note": "Less time and less cost for the customer"}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-D09", "seq": 370, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two instant rails were connected by API. A EUR 30,000 payment still could not cross.", "explain": "Two payment systems are connected with an API.\nA EUR 30,000 payment is sent across the bridge.\nIt fails, because messaging, rules, clearing, settlement and compliance never lined up.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 30,000 payment sent across the bridge\n→ Systems connected by API\n→ Rules and settlement never aligned\n→ Payment cannot cross\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Connecting two systems makes them interoperable.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nInteroperability goes beyond connecting two systems with an API, because messaging, processing rules, clearing, settlement, compliance and day-to-day operations must also line up. A payment bridge joins two payment ecosystems in business and technical terms, where each side may run its own instant rail, currency and rules, and the bridge works as shared standards, scheme rules and cooperation between systems dictate. ISO 20022 supplies the common language, UETR supplies traceability, OCT Inst supplies rules for the Euro leg, and together they move interoperable instant payments across borders closer.\n\nInteroperability demands more than connectivity.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAlign messaging, rules, clearing, settlement, compliance and operations on every bridge, not just the connection.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Common messaging\n→ Shared rules\n→ Settlement alignment\n→ Compliance alignment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the rules and settlement line up, not just the connection?\"\n\nHave you connected two systems and called them interoperable?", "takeaway": "Interoperability demands more than connectivity.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A bridge, not just a connection", "parts": [{"label": "Technology", "note": "Connecting systems is only the first piece."}, {"label": "Common rules", "note": "Messaging, processing rules, clearing, settlement and compliance must align."}, {"label": "Common messaging", "note": "ISO 20022 gives a common language across systems."}, {"label": "Traceability", "note": "UETR helps trace a payment end to end."}, {"label": "FX transparency", "note": "Currency conversion must be visible across the bridge."}, {"label": "Efficient processing", "note": "Each side may have its own rail, currency and rules."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E05", "seq": 377, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The engine was open, but the USD 100,000 payment had nowhere to go", "explain": "A customer sent a USD 100,000 international payment on a day the USA was open and France was closed.\n\nThe payment engine was open, so the payment was accepted.\n\nBut the French bank holding the receiving account was closed, and the credit never appeared in the customer's intraday reporting.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Payment engine open, payment accepted\n→ Core banking closed at the receiving bank, no account booking\n→ Compliance controls unavailable\n→ Nostro funding feed down\n→ Intraday reporting shows nothing, the customer acts on an incomplete cash position\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Process payments when the engine is open.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe engine is only one link in the chain. A payment also needs core banking, compliance, customer reporting and the nostro funding feed. Asking whether the engine is open is the wrong question.\n\nAn open engine does not mean a ready payment journey.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA holiday calendar is an end-to-end operating decision, not an engine setting. Internal and regulatory reporting and nostro reconciliation may wait under agreed controls, but the BA must not decide that alone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Core banking and account booking\n→ Compliance controls\n→ Customer reporting and nostro funding\n→ Reporting and reconciliation catch-up\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the engine open?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every link in the journey is ready?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment accepted on a day the rest of the journey was closed?", "takeaway": "An open engine does not mean a ready payment journey.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Are we really ready to process?", "parts": [{"label": "Payment engine open", "note": "The USD 100,000 payment was accepted because the engine was open."}, {"label": "Core banking closed", "note": "No account booking at the French receiving bank."}, {"label": "Compliance unavailable", "note": "Compliance controls must run before the payment is processed."}, {"label": "Intraday reporting blank", "note": "The customer acts on an incomplete cash position."}, {"label": "Nostro funding feed down", "note": "The nostro funding feed must be available to fund the payment."}, {"label": "Reporting can wait, with controls", "note": "Internal and regulatory reporting and nostro reconciliation can catch up later, if agreed."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-141", "seq": 424, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two instant payment systems connected by API, and a EUR 10,000 payment still failed at clearing", "explain": "Two banks connected their instant payment systems through an API.\n\nThe connection test passed. A message went out, a response came back.\n\nThen a EUR 10,000 payment was accepted by the channel and rejected by clearing.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Systems connected via API\n→ Message sent, response received, connection declared working\n→ EUR 10,000 payment accepted by the channel\n→ Clearing rejects it, scheme rules do not match\n→ Customer waits, both banks point at the connection that works\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the API connects, the payment works.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nConnecting two instant payment systems through an API proves that a message can be sent and a response received, but it does not prove the payment will work end to end. The systems must also align on message formats, scheme rules, clearing, settlement, compliance and operations. Connectivity is the easiest part of the integration.\n\nConnected is not interoperable.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAn integration is not done when the API responds. It is done when a payment clears, settles, and reconciles under the scheme rules of both sides, with compliance and operations aligned.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ API connectivity\n→ Message formats\n→ Scheme rules\n→ Clearing and settlement\n→ Compliance and operations\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the API respond?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a payment clears end to end?\"\n\nHave you seen an integration declared done on an API test alone?", "takeaway": "Connected is not interoperable.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Connect via API", "note": "API proves a message can be sent and a response received."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Align message formats", "note": "Systems must align on message formats."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Align scheme rules", "note": "Systems must align on scheme rules."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Clearing aligned?", "note": "A payment accepted by the channel can still be rejected by clearing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Align clearing and settlement", "note": "Systems must align on clearing and settlement."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Align compliance and operations", "note": "Systems must align on compliance and operations."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-166", "seq": 425, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 20,000 payment worked on UPI, then failed on FedNow for a reason no engineer could see", "explain": "A bank connected to instant payment systems in several markets: UPI in India, SCT Inst in Europe, and FedNow and RTP in the United States.\n\nA EUR 20,000 payment worked on one system.\n\nThe same payment failed on another, and the engineers found nothing wrong with the connection.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Payment works on UPI in India\n→ Same payment sent on FedNow in the US\n→ Connection healthy, message well formed\n→ Payment fails on scheme rules, not technology\n→ Participants, operating hours and dispute processes differ by market\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Instant payment systems are one system with different names.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUPI, SCT Inst, FedNow and RTP are not one system. The systems differ in scheme rules, participants, operating hours and dispute processes, so a payment that works in one market can fail in another for a non-technical reason. Technical connectivity and scheme alignment must both be designed.\n\nSame payment, different rulebook.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen connecting to a new instant system, design the scheme alignment alongside the technical connection: rules, participants, hours, disputes. A healthy connection proves nothing about the scheme.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Technical connectivity\n→ Scheme rules per market\n→ Participants per market\n→ Operating hours and dispute processes\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the connection up?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment fits this market's scheme?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail on a healthy connection because the scheme differed?", "takeaway": "Same payment, different rulebook.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Instant system interoperability", "parts": [{"label": "UPI in India", "note": "Payment works on UPI."}, {"label": "SCT Inst in Europe", "note": "A different scheme with different rules."}, {"label": "FedNow and RTP in US", "note": "The same payment fails on FedNow."}, {"label": "Scheme rules differ", "note": "Systems differ in scheme rules."}, {"label": "Dispute processes differ", "note": "Systems differ in dispute processes."}, {"label": "Technical plus scheme alignment", "note": "Technical connectivity and scheme alignment must both be designed."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-167", "seq": 426, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One generic field set swallowed a EUR 30,000 payment's remittance data, and no one could reconcile it", "explain": "A EUR 30,000 payment crossed two corridors, and each corridor sent slightly different fields.\n\nThe bank collapsed them into one generic field set.\n\nThe remittance data was lost, and the beneficiary could not reconcile the payment against the right invoice.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Payment arrives with corridor A fields\n→ Payment leaves with corridor B fields\n→ Both sets collapsed into one generic set\n→ Remittance data dropped in the collapse\n→ Beneficiary cannot match the payment to an invoice\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Similar fields are the same fields.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nDifferent channels and corridors send similar but non-identical payment fields, and collapsing them into one generic set of fields loses information. A common data model must define how fields translate, how validation works, and what error semantics apply across different domestic rails. Without that model, small data differences become payment failures that are hard to trace.\n\nSimilar is not identical.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe common data model is the integration, not a document about it. Define the field translation, the validation, and the error meaning for every corridor before the first payment crosses.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Each corridor's fields, as sent\n→ The field translation rules\n→ Validation across rails\n→ Error semantics, agreed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do the fields look the same?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves no data is lost in translation?\"\n\nHave you seen a generic field set lose data a payment needed?", "takeaway": "Similar is not identical.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Common data model across corridors", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 30,000 payment", "note": "The payment whose remittance data was lost."}, {"label": "Similar but non-identical fields", "note": "Different channels and corridors send similar but non-identical fields."}, {"label": "Generic set loses information", "note": "Collapsing them into one generic set of fields loses information."}, {"label": "Field translation", "note": "Common data model must define how fields translate."}, {"label": "Error semantics", "note": "Common data model must define what error semantics apply."}, {"label": "Small differences become failures", "note": "Without that model, small differences become payment failures hard to trace."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-142", "seq": 427, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A father in India sent INR 50,000 to Singapore with just a mobile number, and the transfer failed on scope", "explain": "A father in India sent INR 50,000 to a family member in Singapore using only the recipient mobile number.\n\nThe linkage between the two systems was live.\n\nThe transfer failed, because that account type was not covered by the linkage.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Person in India sends INR 50,000 to Singapore\n→ Sender uses only the recipient mobile number\n→ Proxy identifier resolved to an account\n→ Participating institution model routes the payment\n→ Account type not covered by the linkage, payment rejected\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the linkage is live, every transfer works.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe linkage works through proxy identifiers and a participating institution model, where each side resolves the proxy to the right account. But the scope of the linkage must be validated, because not every account type or transaction type is covered. The first design question is always what the linkage actually covers.\n\nLive does not mean everything is covered.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore offering a linked payment, publish the scope: which account types, which transaction types, which direction. A linkage is defined by its limits as much as by its reach.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The proxy resolution\n→ The participating institution model\n→ The account types covered\n→ The transaction types covered\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the linkage live?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this account and transaction type are in scope?\"\n\nHave you seen a linked payment fail because the scope was never checked?", "takeaway": "Live does not mean everything is covered.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Person in India sends", "note": "Person in India sends INR 50,000 to a family member in Singapore."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Use only mobile number", "note": "Sender uses only the recipient mobile number."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Proxy identifier resolved", "note": "Each side resolves the proxy to the right account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Participating institution model", "note": "Works through proxy identifiers and a participating institution model."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Scope validated?", "note": "Scope must be validated, because not every account or transaction type is covered."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reverse direction works", "note": "Reverse direction works the same way, within the same scope."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-153", "seq": 429, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 60,000 payment from Europe to India took four days, and neither side could say where it was", "explain": "A European buyer paid an Indian exporter EUR 60,000.\n\nThe payment travelled through a chain of correspondents and took four days.\n\nNeither side could say where it was on any of those days.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ European buyer sends EUR 60,000 to an Indian exporter\n→ Payment passes through a chain of correspondents\n→ Each hop adds a day and a fee\n→ No single party can track the payment end to end\n→ Exporter waits, buyer cannot confirm delivery\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A chain of correspondents is a payment corridor.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt is not. A corridor without a trackable path is just a longer chain of correspondents. Building a payment corridor from India to Europe means mapping every dependency: currency, scheme, compliance, data, FX and settlement, and replacing that chain with a defined path that both sides can track.\n\nA chain is not a corridor.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA bridge design must replace the correspondent chain with a defined, trackable path. If neither side can track the payment, the bridge does not exist yet.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Currency and FX\n→ Scheme and compliance\n→ Data and settlement\n→ The trackable path, visible to both sides\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the payment reach India?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both sides can track it at every step?\"\n\nHave you seen a cross-border payment disappear into a correspondent chain for days?", "takeaway": "A chain is not a corridor.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "India-Europe payment bridge", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 60,000, four days", "note": "The payment travelled through a chain of correspondents and took days."}, {"label": "Currency and FX", "note": "Bridge design must map the currency dependency."}, {"label": "Scheme and compliance", "note": "Bridge design must map the scheme and compliance dependencies."}, {"label": "Data and settlement", "note": "Bridge design must map the data and settlement dependencies."}, {"label": "No one could track it", "note": "Neither side had a reliable status at any point."}, {"label": "Defined trackable path", "note": "Bridge must replace that chain with a defined path both sides can track."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch43-payment-security.html", "label": "Chapter 43: Payment Security", "chapter": "ch43", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-066", "seq": 462, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Zero-fee weekend: volume hits 300 percent of peak", "explain": "A bank launches a zero-fee weekend campaign for retail payments.\nWithin the first hour payment volume rises to 300 percent of a normal peak, three times the usual load.\nLatency climbs, channel sessions start timing out, and the clearing connector queue grows faster than it drains.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Campaign launches, zero fees\n→ Volume rises to 300 percent of a normal peak\n→ Latency climbs and sessions time out\n→ Clearing queue grows faster than it drains\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The system handled last year's peak, so it will handle the campaign.\"\n\nAverages hide the risk, and this surge is not an average day. Treat the surge as a capacity problem with three controls: intake, queue and recovery. At intake, apply back-pressure through the API gateway and channel, queue or gracefully defer non-critical traffic, show a clear pending state instead of a silent timeout, and throttle campaign traffic to a rate the downstream chain can sustain.\n\nAverages hide peaks. Peaks break systems.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nControl the surge at intake before it reaches the clearing connector. Back-pressure, graceful deferral and a clear pending state beat a silent timeout, and campaign traffic is throttled to a rate the downstream chain can sustain.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Intake and back-pressure\n→ Queue depth\n→ Recovery and retry\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How much volume did we get?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the peak fits inside measured capacity?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a campaign triple the load in an hour and the queue grow faster than it drained?", "takeaway": "At intake, apply back-pressure, queue or gracefully defer non-critical traffic, show a clear pending state instead of a silent timeout, and throttle campaign traffic to a rate the downstream chain can sustain.", "visual": {"type": "stacked", "stacked": {"segments": [{"label": "Normal peak volume", "note": "The usual peak load, the baseline for the campaign.", "value": 100}, {"label": "Additional campaign volume", "note": "Volume rises to 300 percent of a normal peak.", "value": 300}], "unit": "%"}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C044", "seq": 481, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Two banks, no account between them, EUR 40,000 still arrives", "explain": "A customer wants to transfer EUR 40,000 to a bank that holds no account with the sending bank.\nThere is no direct relationship, no nostro, no vostro.\nThe payment still arrives.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sending bank has no account with the beneficiary bank\n→ Sending bank passes the instruction to its correspondent\n→ Correspondent passes it onward\n→ Payment reaches the beneficiary bank\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No direct account means no payment.\"\n\nA customer can transfer funds to a bank that has no account with the sending bank, because the payment travels through correspondent and intermediary banks that do hold accounts with each other. The sending bank passes the instruction to its correspondent, which passes it onward until it reaches a bank that holds an account for the beneficiary bank. The chain exists precisely to connect banks that have no direct relationship.\n\nThe chain exists precisely to connect banks that have no direct relationship.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFollow the chain, not the direct link. Each correspondent passes the instruction to the next bank that holds an account, until the chain reaches a bank that holds an account for the beneficiary bank.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sending bank\n→ Correspondent bank\n→ Next correspondent in the chain\n→ Beneficiary bank\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Do we hold an account with them?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the chain reaches a bank that holds their account?\"\n\nHave you ever traced a payment through three correspondents to reach a bank your bank had never dealt with?", "takeaway": "The chain exists precisely to connect banks that have no direct relationship.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Sending bank has no account with beneficiary", "note": "The beneficiary bank holds no account with the sending bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Sending bank passes to its correspondent", "note": "The sending bank passes the instruction to its correspondent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent passes it onward", "note": "Each correspondent passes it on until an account holder is reached."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the next bank hold their account?", "note": "The chain ends when a bank holds an account for the beneficiary bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment reaches the beneficiary bank", "note": "The chain connects banks with no direct relationship."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C049", "seq": 486, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The sterling leg went through CHAPS. The payment still had to leave the UK.", "explain": "A cross-border payment needs sterling settlement in the UK, so the team routes the domestic leg through CHAPS.\nThe CHAPS leg settles cleanly in central bank money.\nThen the payment stops, because nobody arranged the leg out of the UK, and the GBP 120,000 sits in London.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Cross-border payment needs sterling settlement\n→ CHAPS settles the domestic sterling leg\n→ No correspondent or scheme arranged for the leg out of the UK\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"CHAPS can carry a payment abroad.\"\n\nCHAPS settles sterling payments between UK banks in central bank money, and a cross-border payment that needs sterling settlement in the UK can use it as the domestic leg. The cross-border leg itself still needs a correspondent or a scheme to carry the payment out of the UK, so CHAPS is one leg of the journey, not the whole journey.\n\nCHAPS settles sterling at home. The cross-border leg still needs a correspondent.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlan both legs. CHAPS is the domestic sterling leg, settled in central bank money, and the leg out of the UK needs a correspondent or a scheme of its own.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic sterling leg, CHAPS\n→ Cross-border leg, correspondent or scheme\n→ Settlement in central bank money\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the CHAPS leg settle?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the leg out of the UK is arranged?\"\n\nHave you ever settled the domestic leg perfectly and forgotten the payment still had to leave the country?", "takeaway": "The cross-border leg itself still needs a correspondent or a scheme to carry the payment out of the UK, so CHAPS is one leg of the journey, not the whole journey.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "CHAPS for cross-border payments", "parts": [{"label": "UK high-value payment system", "note": "CHAPS is the UK high-value payment system."}, {"label": "Settles sterling in central bank money", "note": "CHAPS settles sterling between UK banks in central bank money."}, {"label": "Usable as the domestic sterling leg", "note": "A cross-border payment can use CHAPS as its domestic leg."}, {"label": "Cross-border leg still needs a scheme", "note": "The cross-border leg still needs a correspondent or a scheme."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C051", "seq": 488, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "GBP 10,000 crossed borders with no SWIFT message at all", "explain": "A customer sends GBP 10,000 to a beneficiary abroad.\nNo SWIFT message is sent for either leg.\nThe beneficiary is paid, on time, and the sending bank never touched the correspondent chain.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Customer funds a local account in the sending country\n→ Provider pays the beneficiary from its local account abroad\n→ Each leg settles domestically\n→ Flows netted across customers\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every cross-border payment needs SWIFT.\"\n\nThe provider holds local accounts in the countries it serves and settles each leg domestically. A customer paying from one country to another funds a local account in the sending country, and the provider pays the beneficiary from its local account in the receiving country, so no cross-border SWIFT message is needed for the legs. The model works because the provider nets the flows across its customers, and it is a different design from the correspondent banking chain.\n\nTwo domestic legs can replace one cross-border chain.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecognise the design for what it is. Local funding in, local payout out, flows netted across customers, and no SWIFT message on either leg. It is not correspondent banking done badly. It is a different model.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Local funding leg\n→ Local payout leg\n→ Netting across customers\n→ No SWIFT leg\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Where is the SWIFT message?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each leg settled, and who holds the funds in between?\"\n\nHave you ever looked for a SWIFT message that was never sent, because the payment never crossed a border as a message?", "takeaway": "The model works because the provider nets the flows across its customers, and it is a different design from the correspondent banking chain.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer funds a local account", "note": "A customer funds a local account in the sending country."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Provider pays from its local account abroad", "note": "The provider pays the beneficiary from its local account in the receiving country."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Each leg settles domestically", "note": "No cross-border SWIFT message is needed for the legs."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who holds the funds in between?", "note": "The provider nets the flows across its customers."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C067", "seq": 491, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The beneficiary bank has a BIC that cannot receive messages", "explain": "A EUR 65,000 payment is addressed to a bank whose BIC has the digit 1 in position 8.\nThat marks it as non-connected: a SWIFT participant that cannot receive messages directly.\nThe MT103 bounces, and the payment has nowhere to go.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Beneficiary bank holds a non-connected BIC\n→ MT103 and MT202 COV sent to a live BIC\n→ Live BIC forwards the funds onward\n→ Onward leg uses a normal serial MT103\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Every BIC can receive a message.\"\n\nSome banks hold a BIC with the digit 1 in position 8, which marks it as non-connected: it is a SWIFT participant but cannot receive messages directly. The MT103 announcement and the MT202 COV are then sent to a live BIC, which forwards the funds to the non-connected bank over an internal network, usually with a normal serial MT103. The same pattern appears in bank groups where SWIFT communication is centralised at the head office and the head office forwards payments to subsidiaries and branches.\n\nA BIC with 1 in position 8 is a participant that cannot be reached directly.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRoute to the live BIC and let it forward the funds. The announcement and the cover go to the live BIC, and the onward leg travels over the internal network, usually as a normal serial MT103.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Non-connected BIC, digit 1 in position 8\n→ Live BIC that receives the messages\n→ Forwarding over the internal network\n→ Serial MT103 for the onward leg\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the BIC valid?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which live BIC forwards to this bank?\"\n\nHave you ever had a payment bounce because the beneficiary bank's BIC could not receive messages?", "takeaway": "The same pattern appears in bank groups where SWIFT communication is centralised at the head office and the head office forwards payments to subsidiaries and branches.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Beneficiary bank holds a non-connected BIC", "note": "The BIC has the digit 1 in position 8, so the bank cannot receive messages directly."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "MT103 and MT202 COV sent to a live BIC", "note": "The announcement and the cover are sent to a live BIC."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Live BIC forwards the funds onward", "note": "The live BIC forwards the funds over an internal network."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Forwarding uses a normal serial MT103", "note": "The onward leg usually uses a normal serial MT103."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Head office or direct route?", "note": "The same pattern appears where SWIFT is centralised at the head office."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "PAI-C071", "seq": 492, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The instruction was complete. The payment waited for the currency.", "explain": "A customer instructs a EUR 80,000 payment in a foreign currency, with every field complete and every check passed.\nThe payment does not move.\nHours pass, and the instruction sits untouched, waiting for something the customer never sees.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Bank receives payment instructions\n→ Orders collected until cut-off time\n→ Bank checks its nostro balances\n→ Buy, sell or do nothing, the decision is made\n→ Payment waits for the currency position\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A complete instruction is executed at once.\"\n\nA bank does not buy the currency of every payment the moment it is instructed, because it does not know in advance how much foreign currency it will need. It collects orders until a cut-off time and then decides whether to buy, sell or do nothing, based on the balance of orders and the funds already sitting in its nostro accounts. A payment can therefore wait for the bank's currency position to be settled, even when the instruction itself is complete.\n\nA payment can wait for the bank's currency position, even when the instruction is complete.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nExplain the wait to the customer in terms of the currency position, not the instruction. Orders are collected to cut-off, weighed against nostro balances, and the bank buys, sells or does nothing based on the net position.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Orders collected to cut-off\n→ Nostro balances checked\n→ Buy, sell or do nothing decision\n→ Instruction completeness\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the instruction complete?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the currency position is settled and the payment can move?\"\n\nHave you ever had to tell a customer the payment was perfect, and still not moving?", "takeaway": "A payment can therefore wait for the bank's currency position to be settled, even when the instruction itself is complete.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Bank receives payment instructions", "note": "The bank is instructed on payments but does not buy the currency at once."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Orders collected until cut-off time", "note": "The bank collects orders until a cut-off time."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank checks its nostro balances", "note": "It weighs the balance of orders against funds in its nostro accounts."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Buy, sell or do nothing", "note": "It decides whether to buy, sell or do nothing based on the balance of orders."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Payment waits for the currency position", "note": "A payment can wait for the bank's currency position to be settled."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-017", "seq": 497, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "French bank, German bank, dollars settled in New York", "explain": "A French bank sends USD 500,000 to a German bank.\nTwo European banks, an American currency, and a payment that never leaves the United States to settle.\nThe treasurer expects a cross-border journey. Settlement takes one domestic step.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ French bank instructs its US correspondent\n→ Nostro debited in the USA\n→ German bank's US correspondent credited\n→ Settlement completes in a single domestic step\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A payment between two European banks settles in Europe.\"\n\nWhen a French bank sends USD to a German bank, it tells its US correspondent to debit the nostro and credit the German bank's account with that bank's own US correspondent. Settlement completes in a single domestic step, since both accounts sit in the USA. The sender finds the German bank's US correspondent in its Standing Settlement Instructions and states it in the MT103.\n\nWhen both accounts sit in the USA, settlement is one domestic step.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFind the beneficiary's US correspondent in the Standing Settlement Instructions and state it in the MT103. The nostro is debited, the German bank's account with its own US correspondent is credited, and settlement is done in one domestic step.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Sender's US correspondent\n→ Nostro debit\n→ Beneficiary's US correspondent\n→ Standing Settlement Instructions\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which country are the banks in?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the settlement accounts sit?\"\n\nHave you ever settled a payment between two European banks without the money ever leaving the USA?", "takeaway": "The sender finds the German bank's US correspondent in its Standing Settlement Instructions and states it in the MT103.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "French bank instructs its correspondent", "note": "It tells its US correspondent to debit the nostro."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "German bank credited in the USA", "note": "The German bank's account with its own US correspondent is credited."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settles in a single step", "note": "Both accounts sit in the USA, so settlement is one domestic step."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Correspondent found in instructions", "note": "The sender locates the German bank's US correspondent in the SSI."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "MT103 names the correspondent?", "note": "The sender states that correspondent in the MT103."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103"], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN4-019", "seq": 498, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The letter of credit is issued. How does the money move?", "explain": "A letter of credit for USD 250,000 is issued with an MT700.\nThe documents are accepted.\nNow the beneficiary asks a simple question: how does the money move?\nThe MT700 does not say.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Credit issued with MT700\n→ Documents accepted\n→ Parties agree the settlement instrument\n→ Funds move by serial MT103, or by MT103 plus MT202 COV\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The MT700 settles the credit.\"\n\nA letter of credit is issued with an MT700, but the standard does not fix how it must be settled, so the parties agree the payment instrument between themselves. In general the settlement runs through a serial MT103, or through an MT103 together with an MT202 COV. Either route moves the funds under the credit once the documents have been accepted.\n\nThe MT700 issues the credit. The MT103 moves the money.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAgree the settlement instrument between the parties before the documents are accepted. Either a serial MT103 or an MT103 with an MT202 COV moves the funds, and the agreement is made up front, not after acceptance.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT700, the credit itself\n→ Document acceptance\n→ Serial MT103 route\n→ MT103 plus MT202 COV route\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the credit issued?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the parties agreed how the funds will move?\"\n\nHave you ever had documents accepted under a credit with no agreed settlement route?", "takeaway": "Either route moves the funds under the credit once the documents have been accepted.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Credit issued with MT700", "note": "The letter of credit itself is issued with an MT700."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Parties agree the instrument", "note": "The standard leaves settlement to agreement between the parties."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settle by serial MT103", "note": "One route is settlement through a serial MT103."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Or MT103 plus MT202 COV", "note": "The other route pairs an MT103 with an MT202 COV."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which route was agreed?", "note": "Either route moves the funds once documents are accepted."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": ["MT103", "MT202", "MT700"], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch53-mt103-optional-fields-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 53: MT103 Optional Fields Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch53", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-164", "seq": 505, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 250,000 from Europe to India, and nobody owned the journey", "explain": "A European buyer pays an Indian exporter EUR 250,000.\nThe payment crosses four legs: a domestic euro rail, a cross-border bridge, an FX conversion and a domestic Indian rail.\nEach participant reports only its own leg.\nThe payment is late, and the chain of calls begins: exporter to buyer, buyer to bank, bank to bridge operator, and nobody can say where the money is.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Buyer pays on the domestic euro rail\n→ Payment crosses the cross-border bridge\n→ Euros convert to rupees\n→ Exporter paid on the Indian rail\n→ Nobody can say where the money is\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each party owns its own leg, so the journey is covered.\"\n\nWith no owner of the end-to-end state, a late payment turns into a chain of calls, exporter to buyer, buyer to bank, bank to bridge operator, and nobody can say where the money is. Give the corridor a named owner for the whole journey, with every leg mapped to its rail, FX step, settlement and compliance check.\n\nFour legs, four owners, no owner of the journey.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName one owner for the whole corridor. Every leg is mapped to its rail, FX step, settlement and compliance check, and the owner can say where the money is at any point.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domestic euro leg\n→ Cross-border bridge\n→ FX conversion\n→ Domestic Indian leg\n→ End-to-end owner\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did each leg complete?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves one owner can see the whole journey?\"\n\nHave you ever chased a late cross-border payment through four parties, each of whom could only see their own leg?", "takeaway": "Give the corridor a named owner for the whole journey, with every leg mapped to its rail, FX step, settlement and compliance check.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Euro leg, domestic", "note": "Buyer pays on the domestic euro rail."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "The cross-border bridge", "note": "Payment crosses to the Indian side."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Convert the currency", "note": "Euros convert to rupees."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Indian leg, domestic", "note": "Exporter is paid on the Indian rail."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who owns the whole journey?", "note": "One owner follows the whole journey."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F04", "seq": 518, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The payment is visible end to end. Why not the complaint?", "explain": "A EUR 55,000 cross-border payment is debited, and the customer can track it end to end across linked payment systems.\nThen the payment goes wrong, and the customer raises a complaint.\nThe complaint cannot travel the same way, and the hardest cross-border problem is back: nobody knows where the payment is.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment debited and tracked end to end\n→ Payment goes wrong\n→ Complaint raised, but it cannot travel on the linked rails\n→ Nobody knows where the payment is\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Interlinking is only a connectivity project.\"\n\nInterlinking connects payment systems across countries so a payment can be tracked end to end once it is debited, and the open question is whether the customer-side acceptance of payment instructions can travel the same way, and then complaints and investigations too. If instructions, status and complaints all travel on the interlinked rails, the hardest cross-border problem, not knowing where a payment is, starts to close. That is why interlinking is a way of thinking about payments, not only a connectivity project.\n\nInterlinking is a way of thinking about payments, not only a connectivity project.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDesign interlinking for instructions, status and complaints together. If the complaint travels on the same links as the payment, the question of where the money is finally has an answer.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payment instructions\n→ End-to-end tracking\n→ Status visibility\n→ Complaints and investigations\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are the systems connected?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a complaint can follow the same path as the payment?\"\n\nHave you ever tracked a payment perfectly, then lost it the moment a complaint was raised?", "takeaway": "That is why interlinking is a way of thinking about payments, not only a connectivity project.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Interlinked payment systems", "parts": [{"label": "Payment instructions", "note": "Customer instructions accepted across linked systems."}, {"label": "End-to-end tracking", "note": "Once debited, the whole journey is visible."}, {"label": "Status visibility", "note": "No more not knowing where a payment is."}, {"label": "Complaints and investigations", "note": "Can complaints travel on the same links."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-014", "seq": 523, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "The indirect participant the central bank never sees", "explain": "An indirect participant sends EUR 20,000 through its direct participant.\nThe payment moves, the direct participant's account is debited, and the central bank watches it all happen.\nThe central bank sees the direct participant, and nobody else.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Indirect participant instructs the payment\n→ Direct participant sends the debit\n→ Central bank sees only the direct participant\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The central bank watches every participant.\"\n\nFrom the central bank standpoint the risk is liquidity: whether the indirect participant can meet its financial obligations. Ensuring that funding is normally the direct participant business, since the direct participant must confirm the indirect participant holds enough before sending debit payments. The central bank sees only the direct participant, even when the payment belongs to the indirect participant, and oversees the direct participant as a whole rather than dealing with the indirect participant.\n\nThe central bank sees only the direct participant, even when the payment belongs to the indirect participant.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPut the funding check on the direct participant. It must confirm the indirect participant holds enough before sending debit payments, because the central bank oversees the direct participant as a whole and never deals with the indirect participant directly.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Liquidity risk\n→ Direct participant confirms funding\n→ Central bank sees the direct participant\n→ Oversight covers the direct participant whole\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the indirect participant pay?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the direct participant confirmed the funding before sending?\"\n\nHave you ever assumed the central bank was watching a participant it never sees?", "takeaway": "The central bank sees only the direct participant, even when the payment belongs to the indirect participant, and oversees the direct participant as a whole rather than dealing with the indirect participant.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The central bank view of indirect participation", "parts": [{"label": "The risk is liquidity", "note": "The question is whether the indirect participant can meet its obligations."}, {"label": "The DP must ensure funds", "note": "The direct participant checks the IP has enough before sending debits."}, {"label": "The central bank sees the DP", "note": "It sees the direct participant even when the payment is for the IP."}, {"label": "Oversight covers the DP whole", "note": "The central bank oversees the DP as a whole, not the IP directly."}]}}, "stage": 5, "stage_name": "Complex banking scenarios", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "failure_category": "liquidity shortfall", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-120", "seq": 22, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 20,000 returned because the requirement quoted the rulebook", "explain": "A EUR 20,000 payment is returned by the beneficiary bank.\nThe developers say they built exactly what the requirement said.\nThe requirement quoted the scheme rulebook, word for word.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Scheme rule copied into the requirement as written\n→ Developers build the rule literally\n→ The payment is rejected at the beneficiary bank\n→ Nobody can say what the system was supposed to do instead\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Copying the rule into the requirement is the same as specifying it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPayment scheme rules are written in technical language, but developers and business users need them as a clear trigger, decision, rule, timeline and system behaviour. Translating a rule into what happens, when, and what the system must do is what makes it buildable.\n\nA quoted rule ≠ A buildable requirement.\n\nJargon left in a requirement produces a system that does the wrong thing confidently, so plain, precise wording is a quality control, not a courtesy.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTranslate every scheme rule into trigger, decision, rule, timeline and system behaviour.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Trigger that starts the rule\n→ Decision the system must make\n→ Rule it must apply\n→ Timeline it must meet\n→ Behaviour the user must see\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we quote the rule?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a developer could build the right behaviour from this wording alone?\"\n\nHave you seen a system do the wrong thing confidently because the requirement was quoted, not translated?", "takeaway": "Jargon left in a requirement produces a system that does the wrong thing confidently, so plain, precise wording is a quality control, not a courtesy.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Payment rules without jargon", "parts": [{"label": "Rule copied verbatim", "note": "The scheme rule goes into the requirement as written"}, {"label": "Built literally", "note": "Developers build exactly what the words say"}, {"label": "EUR 20,000 returned", "note": "The beneficiary bank rejects the payment"}, {"label": "Nobody knows the intent", "note": "The requirement never said what should happen"}, {"label": "Translate, do not quote", "note": "Trigger, decision, rule, timeline, behaviour"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C07", "seq": 29, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 1 million project that stalled on one unasked question", "explain": "A EUR 1 million payments project stalls in its third month.\nCompliance says the requirement was never theirs.\nDevelopers say the requirement was never buildable.\nThe BA says everyone agreed it in the workshop.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Stakeholders from compliance, business, developers and vendors meet\n→ The BA presents the payment flow and everyone nods\n→ Nobody is asked whether they understand the concepts or the sequence\n→ Three months later, each team is building a different payment\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A nod in the workshop means the stakeholder understood.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payments BA works with many stakeholders at once, across several projects and dependent systems. The work starts by asking whether each stakeholder understands the payment concepts, the sequence of payments, the business and its rules, and the payment journey, and whether the BA understands the system, the processing and where they fit in the flow. When a stakeholder does not know an answer, the BA helps them understand the question and reach it.\n\nA nod ≠ Understanding.\n\nAsking the right questions is the core of stakeholder management, not a soft skill on the side.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTest understanding with questions, never with nods.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Concepts the stakeholder understands\n→ Sequence and journey they can describe\n→ Rules they can apply\n→ The BA's own understanding of the system\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did everyone agree in the workshop?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each stakeholder could explain the payment flow back to you?\"\n\nHave you seen a project stall because a nod was mistaken for understanding?", "takeaway": "When a stakeholder does not know an answer, the BA helps them understand the question and reach it, because that translation is what makes the requirements workable, and asking the right questions is the core of stakeholder management, not a soft skill on the side.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The BA as stakeholder translator", "parts": [{"label": "Many stakeholders at once", "note": "Compliance, business, developers and vendors in one room"}, {"label": "Everyone nods", "note": "The flow is presented and nobody objects"}, {"label": "Nobody is tested", "note": "No one is asked if they understand the sequence"}, {"label": "Three different payments built", "note": "Each team builds what it thought was agreed"}, {"label": "Ask the right questions", "note": "Translation is the core of stakeholder management"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch29-capstone-project.html", "label": "Chapter 29: Capstone Project", "chapter": "ch29", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-082", "seq": 33, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 500,000 release nobody validated in production", "explain": "A EUR 500,000 payments change goes live on a Friday.\nBy Monday, adoption is near zero and two defects are open.\nNobody can say whether the change delivered what was promised.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Discovery finds the business problem and the real users\n→ Analysis turns it into testable requirements\n→ Build and test keep the increment honest against scope\n→ Release ships, and the analyst moves to the next project\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Release is the end of the analyst's job.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe analyst owns the thread from discovery to live running. After release the work is not over: the analyst validates the outcome in production and reports what remains open. A lifecycle without that closing loop leaves value unproven.\n\nShipped ≠ Delivered.\n\nA lifecycle without the closing loop leaves value unproven.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOwn the thread from discovery to validated live outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Discovery of the real problem and users\n→ Requirements that can be tested\n→ Build kept honest against scope\n→ Post-release validation in production\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we release on Friday?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the change works in production and the open items are reported?\"\n\nHave you seen a release declared done while nobody checked whether it worked live?", "takeaway": "A lifecycle without that closing loop leaves value unproven.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Analyst ownership end to end", "parts": [{"label": "Discovery", "note": "Finds the business problem and the real users"}, {"label": "Analysis and design", "note": "Turns understanding into testable requirements"}, {"label": "Build and test", "note": "Keeps the increment honest against agreed scope"}, {"label": "Release ships", "note": "The change goes live on a Friday"}, {"label": "Validation missing", "note": "No one checks the outcome in production"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-013", "seq": 34, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 200,000 sprint that failed at review", "explain": "A EUR 200,000 payments sprint reaches review.\nThe increment fails its acceptance criteria.\nThe analyst says the criteria were never clear. The team says the analyst was never there.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Refinement skips the analyst, stories stay vague\n→ Planning sets scope the team cannot deliver\n→ Delivery runs with questions unanswered\n→ Review tests the increment against criteria nobody agreed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The analyst's job starts at review.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn Scrum the analyst works the sprint, not around it: refinement turns vague stories into clear acceptance criteria, planning grounds scope and priority in what the team can actually deliver, and delivery is a running conversation as detail emerges. Review and acceptance close the loop by testing the built increment against the criteria agreed.\n\nAn analyst who only appears at review is too late to shape anything.\n\nPresent at review ≠ Present in the sprint.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWork the sprint, not around it, from refinement to acceptance.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Refinement into clear acceptance criteria\n→ Planning grounded in what can be delivered\n→ Delivery as a running conversation\n→ Review against the agreed criteria\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we hold a review?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the criteria were agreed in refinement and tested at review?\"\n\nHave you seen a sprint fail at review because the analyst only arrived at the end?", "takeaway": "An analyst who only appears at review is too late to shape anything.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Analyst contribution across the sprint", "parts": [{"label": "Refinement skipped", "note": "Vague stories never become clear criteria"}, {"label": "Planning overpromises", "note": "Scope is set beyond what can be delivered"}, {"label": "Delivery runs blind", "note": "Questions go unanswered as detail emerges"}, {"label": "Review fails", "note": "The increment is tested against criteria nobody agreed"}, {"label": "Work the sprint", "note": "Refinement to acceptance, not review only"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-080", "seq": 35, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 50,000 payment that was correct, and three seconds too slow", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 payment is validated correctly and posted to the right ledger.\nThe customer abandons it anyway.\nThe payment took three seconds longer than the channel allows, and the session timed out.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Functional requirement: validate the payment, post the ledger entry\n→ The system does both, correctly\n→ No non-functional requirement was ever written for response time\n→ The customer session times out before the confirmation arrives\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the payment is correct, it works.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nFunctional requirements state what the system must do, such as validating a payment or posting a ledger entry, while non-functional requirements state how well it must do it: performance, security, availability, usability and supportability. Both belong in the specification as explicit, testable statements.\n\nCorrect ≠ Usable.\n\nA payment that is correct yet too slow or unavailable still fails the customer. Function without fitness for use is not a working payment.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite both kinds of requirement as explicit, testable statements.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What the system must do\n→ How fast it must do it\n→ How available and secure it must be\n→ How usable it must be under load\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the payment validate?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment is correct, fast enough and available when the customer needs it?\"\n\nHave you seen a correct payment fail because nobody specified how well it had to perform?", "takeaway": "Function without fitness for use is not a working payment.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What the system does, and how well", "parts": [{"label": "Functional requirement met", "note": "Payment validated, ledger entry posted correctly"}, {"label": "No performance requirement", "note": "Response time was never specified"}, {"label": "Session times out", "note": "The customer waits three seconds too long"}, {"label": "Correct payment abandoned", "note": "The customer gives up on a correct payment"}, {"label": "Both kinds matter", "note": "Function without fitness for use is not working"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "reconciliation break", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-016", "seq": 36, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 100,000 refund story that hid the exception path", "explain": "A team writes a user story for a EUR 100,000 refund.\nIt says what the user wants and why.\nIn testing, the refund fails on a duplicate payment, and nobody wrote down what should happen.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ User story written: outcome led, what the user wants and why\n→ The story is used for prioritisation and conversation\n→ The exception path is never described\n→ Testing finds the duplicate case, and the team guesses\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One form fits every need.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA user story is outcome led: it states what a user wants to achieve and why, which makes it the right tool for prioritisation and conversation. A use case is interaction led: it pins down steps, actors and outcomes for one interaction, including decisions and exception paths. Choose by the need in front of you, not by team habit.\n\nThe wrong form hides the detail that matters.\n\nA story for outcomes ≠ A use case for interaction.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nChoose the form by the need in front of you, not by team habit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Outcome the user wants, and why\n→ Steps, actors and outcomes of the interaction\n→ Decisions along the way\n→ Exception paths when things go wrong\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we write a story?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the form we chose captures the detail this need actually has?\"\n\nHave you seen a defect hide in a detail the chosen form never asked for?", "takeaway": "The wrong form hides the detail that matters.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Story for outcomes, use case for interaction", "parts": [{"label": "User story written", "note": "Outcome led: what the user wants and why"}, {"label": "Prioritisation done", "note": "The story works for conversation and ordering"}, {"label": "Exception path missing", "note": "The duplicate refund case is never described"}, {"label": "Testing finds the gap", "note": "The team guesses what should happen"}, {"label": "Choose by need", "note": "Fit the form to the need, not to habit"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-014", "seq": 37, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 300,000 BRD the developers could not build from", "explain": "A EUR 300,000 payments change has a thick BRD.\nThe developers say it tells them nothing they can build.\nThe business says it already approved the requirements.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Business requirements document states what the business needs and why\n→ The document goes to developers as the build instruction\n→ Developers ask for the detail: fields, rules, behaviour\n→ The business points at the BRD and says it is already approved\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One document serves every reader.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe business requirements document states what the business needs and why, the functional requirements document states in detail what the system must do, and a use case describes one specific interaction between actors and the system. Selection turns on audience, complexity, traceability and delivery context, so each reader gets the detail they need and nothing they do not.\n\nApproved ≠ Buildable.\n\nThe right artefact is the one its reader can act on.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMatch the artefact to its reader, every time.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Business need and why (BRD)\n→ What the system must do in detail (FRD)\n→ One interaction between actors and system (use case)\n→ The reader who must act on it\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we write a requirements document?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the reader of this document can act on it without asking for another one?\"\n\nHave you seen a project stall because the right document went to the wrong reader?", "takeaway": "The right artefact is the one its reader can act on.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Matching the artefact to its reader", "parts": [{"label": "BRD states the need", "note": "What the business needs and why"}, {"label": "Sent to developers", "note": "The BRD is handed over as the build instruction"}, {"label": "Detail missing", "note": "Fields, rules and behaviour are nowhere in it"}, {"label": "Business says approved", "note": "Approval is mistaken for buildability"}, {"label": "Match reader to artefact", "note": "Each reader gets the detail they can act on"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-026", "seq": 38, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 1 million release report that hid the open risks", "explain": "A EUR 1 million payments release goes live.\nThe first stakeholder report shows green across the board.\nA week later, adoption is 12 percent and three critical defects are open.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Report covers adoption, incidents, defects, service levels, benefits and risks\n→ The report writer leaves out the open risks to keep it tidy\n→ Stakeholders read green and move on\n→ The defects surface in production, and nobody is prepared\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A tidy report is a good report.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAfter release, stakeholders need one factual, regular report covering adoption, incidents, defects, service levels, benefits realised and open risks. Adoption shows whether the change is used, incidents and defects show what is still broken, service levels show whether it performs, and benefits and open risks show whether the intended outcome is on track. A report built this way lets stakeholders decide what needs attention next.\n\nGreen everywhere ≠ Everything fine.\n\nReporting that hides the open items is worse than no report at all.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReport the open items first, not last, and never leave them out.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Adoption, whether the change is used\n→ Incidents and defects, what is still broken\n→ Service levels, whether it performs\n→ Benefits realised and open risks\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send a report?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the report showed every open risk, not just the green ones?\"\n\nHave you seen a green report hide a release that was failing?", "takeaway": "Reporting that hides the open items is worse than no report at all.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Post-release reporting stakeholders can act on", "parts": [{"label": "Report shows green", "note": "Every section reads green across the board"}, {"label": "Open risks left out", "note": "Three critical defects are missing from the report"}, {"label": "Stakeholders move on", "note": "Nobody prepares for what is actually broken"}, {"label": "Adoption at 12 percent", "note": "The change is barely used a week later"}, {"label": "Report the open items", "note": "Hiding them is worse than no report at all"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-076", "seq": 39, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 400,000 upgrade deferred because it was sold as features", "explain": "A team asks for EUR 400,000 to upgrade the payments platform.\nThe business case lists features: new messages, new screens, new APIs.\nThe steering committee defers it. Again.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Case presented in technical detail: features, messages, screens\n→ Stakeholders ask what it delivers for customers and revenue\n→ The case has no answer in their terms\n→ The decision is deferred to the next quarter\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Good features sell themselves.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nStakeholders decide on outcomes, not on technical detail, so present the change in their terms: the outcome it delivers, the customer impact, the revenue or cost effect, the compliance position, the timing and the decisions needed. Keep the language plain and tie every point to something the stakeholder is accountable for.\n\nA case argued on features gets deferred.\n\nA case argued on value gets decided.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nArgue every change on value, in the stakeholder's own terms.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Outcome the change delivers\n→ Customer impact\n→ Revenue or cost effect\n→ Compliance position, timing and decisions needed\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we list the features?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the stakeholder could see their own outcome in this case?\"\n\nHave you seen a good change deferred because it was sold as features, not value?", "takeaway": "A case argued on value gets decided; a case argued on features gets deferred.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Selling change on value, not features", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 400,000 asked", "note": "An upgrade case built on features and screens"}, {"label": "Stakeholders ask for outcomes", "note": "What does it deliver for customers and revenue?"}, {"label": "No answer in their terms", "note": "The case speaks technical detail only"}, {"label": "Decision deferred", "note": "The committee puts it off to next quarter"}, {"label": "Argue on value", "note": "Outcomes get decided, features get deferred"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-079", "seq": 40, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 50,000 payments outage customers heard about from Twitter", "explain": "A payments outage blocks EUR 50,000 of customer payments.\nSupport has no status to give.\nCustomers learn about the outage from social media before the bank tells them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Outage starts, payments stop\n→ Support is asked for a status and has nothing repeatable\n→ No update is promised, so no update is given\n→ Customers assume the worst and call in a flood\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"No news is fine until we have a fix.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nDuring an incident, support needs a status it can repeat to customers without guessing: the symptom in plain language, the users affected, a workaround if one exists, the owner, the severity and the time of the next update. Update at the promised time even if nothing has changed, because silence makes customers assume the worst.\n\nSilence ≠ Reassurance.\n\nReliable updates are part of the fix, not an extra.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive support a repeatable status, and update at the promised time even with no change.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Symptom in plain language\n→ Users affected and workaround\n→ Owner and severity\n→ Time of the next update, kept even with no news\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we fix it fast?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves customers were told, on time, in words support could repeat?\"\n\nHave you seen an incident made worse by silence while the fix was already under way?", "takeaway": "Reliable updates are part of the fix, not an extra.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A status support can repeat with confidence", "parts": [{"label": "Outage blocks payments", "note": "EUR 50,000 of customer payments stop"}, {"label": "Support has no status", "note": "Nothing repeatable to tell customers"}, {"label": "Silence fills the gap", "note": "Customers learn of it from social media"}, {"label": "Customers assume the worst", "note": "A flood of calls follows the silence"}, {"label": "Update on time, every time", "note": "Even with no change, the update is the fix"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch37-how-10-large-banks-do-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 37: How 10 Large Banks Do Payments", "chapter": "ch37", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-095", "seq": 41, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 250,000 scope dispute nobody wrote down", "explain": "A EUR 250,000 payments project ends in a scope dispute.\nThe vendor says the extra work was agreed in a meeting.\nThe business says it never approved it.\nThere is no record either way.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Meeting agrees an extra scope item, verbally\n→ No one writes down the action, the owner or the due date\n→ The decision and its assumptions are never recorded\n→ Approval is inferred from silence, and the dispute begins\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Everyone was in the room, so everyone agreed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWritten communication is the record that protects a delivery: meeting notes name the action, the owner and the due date, decisions are recorded with the assumptions behind them, and approval is obtained explicitly rather than inferred from silence. A sign-off only works if it states exactly what was approved and by whom.\n\nSilence ≠ Approval.\n\nIf it is not written down, it was not agreed.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWrite the action, the owner, the due date, the decision and the approval, every time.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Meeting notes with action, owner and due date\n→ Decisions recorded with their assumptions\n→ Approval obtained explicitly\n→ Sign-off stating exactly what was approved and by whom\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Were we all in the meeting?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this was agreed, by whom, and on what assumptions?\"\n\nHave you seen a scope dispute where the only record was everyone's memory?", "takeaway": "If it is not written down, it was not agreed.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The written record that protects delivery", "parts": [{"label": "Verbal agreement", "note": "Extra scope agreed in a meeting, never written"}, {"label": "No action or owner", "note": "No note names the action, owner or due date"}, {"label": "Approval inferred", "note": "Silence is taken as agreement"}, {"label": "Dispute begins", "note": "EUR 250,000 of scope is contested"}, {"label": "Write it down", "note": "If it is not written, it was not agreed"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-083", "seq": 44, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The operations analyst whose EUR 30,000 salary pitch listed only a job title", "explain": "An operations analyst applies for a business analyst role paying EUR 30,000 more.\nThe CV lists the old job title and the old employer.\nThe recruiter cannot see a single BA skill in it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Years in operations, support and product work\n→ CV lists the job title and hopes the reader connects the dots\n→ Recruiter searches for elicitation, documentation and delivery support\n→ The profile never surfaces for the role\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The job title speaks for itself.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMoving into business analysis from another domain works when transferable skills are mapped to the work an analyst actually does: experience in operations, support or product maps to domain knowledge, elicitation, documentation, delivery support and outcome measurement. State the mapping explicitly, with concrete examples of work done, rather than listing a previous job title and hoping the reader makes the connection.\n\nA mapped skill is a hiring argument; an unmapped job title is just a label.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap every transferable skill to BA work, with a concrete example attached.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Domain knowledge from prior work\n→ Elicitation and documentation shown by example\n→ Delivery support and outcome measurement\n→ The job title, which proves nothing alone\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we list the old job?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves this experience is BA work, in the recruiter's own words?\"\n\nHave you seen a strong candidate invisible because their CV listed titles, not mapped skills?", "takeaway": "A mapped skill is a hiring argument; an unmapped job title is just a label.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Mapping transferable skills to BA work", "parts": [{"label": "Years in operations", "note": "Experience in operations, support and product"}, {"label": "CV lists only a title", "note": "The reader is left to connect the dots"}, {"label": "Recruiter searches for skills", "note": "Elicitation, documentation, delivery support"}, {"label": "Profile never surfaces", "note": "The mapped skills are invisible"}, {"label": "Map it explicitly", "note": "A mapped skill is a hiring argument"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-096", "seq": 45, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "Six months learning tools, and still no interview for a payments BA role", "explain": "A career changer spends six months learning BA tools.\nThe profile lists every tool on the market.\nNo recruiter calls for a payments BA role.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Tools learned first, domain chosen never\n→ Profile shows tool skill, no domain understanding\n→ Recruiter searches for payments domain credibility\n→ The profile reads as a tool user, not a payments analyst\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Tools are what get the interview.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBefore learning tools, choose the domain to work in, weighing transferable experience, demand for the domain, genuine interest and a realistic entry path. An analyst is hired for domain understanding first and tool skill second, so a focused domain choice makes learning faster and a profile credible to a recruiter in that domain.\n\nTools are easy to learn; credibility in a domain is what gets the interview.\n\nTool skill ≠ Domain credibility.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPick the domain first, then learn the tools it actually uses.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Transferable experience into the domain\n→ Demand for the domain\n→ Genuine interest in it\n→ A realistic entry path\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many tools do we know?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we understand the domain a recruiter is hiring for?\"\n\nHave you seen a tool-rich profile lose to a domain-strong one?", "takeaway": "Tools are easy to learn; credibility in a domain is what gets the interview.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Domain first, tools second", "parts": [{"label": "Six months on tools", "note": "Every BA tool learned, no domain chosen"}, {"label": "Profile shows tool skill", "note": "No payments understanding anywhere in it"}, {"label": "Recruiter wants domain", "note": "The search is for payments credibility"}, {"label": "No interview comes", "note": "A tool user is not a payments analyst"}, {"label": "Domain first", "note": "Credibility in a domain gets the interview"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-098", "seq": 46, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 60,000 BA role that never found a perfect candidate", "explain": "A recruiter fills a EUR 60,000 business analyst role.\nThe perfect candidate exists, with the exact domain and skills.\nThe recruiter never finds them, because their profile uses none of the role's words.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Recruiter searches by keywords that match the role\n→ The candidate's profile uses different words for the same work\n→ The search never surfaces the profile\n→ The role goes to a weaker candidate who used the right keywords\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Good work speaks for itself.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRecruiters search by keywords that match the role they are filling, so align profile keywords with the target role, the domain, the skills and the verifiable experience, and a search for that role will surface the profile. Claim only what can be evidenced, because a keyword that cannot be backed up damages credibility in the first conversation.\n\nVisibility gets the call; evidence wins it.\n\nInvisible ≠ Unqualified.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAlign keywords with the role, and back every one with evidence.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Target role keywords\n→ Domain keywords\n→ Skills keywords\n→ Verifiable experience behind each claim\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are we good at the work?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the recruiter can find us, and that every keyword survives the first conversation?\"\n\nHave you seen a perfect candidate lose a role because their profile used the wrong words?", "takeaway": "Visibility gets the call; evidence wins it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Recruiter search visibility", "parts": [{"label": "Recruiter searches keywords", "note": "The search matches the role being filled"}, {"label": "Profile uses other words", "note": "The same work is described differently"}, {"label": "Profile never surfaces", "note": "The perfect candidate stays invisible"}, {"label": "Weaker candidate hired", "note": "The right keywords beat the better record"}, {"label": "Visibility plus evidence", "note": "The call comes from keywords, the win from proof"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-085", "seq": 47, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 80,000 interview lost on one confident wrong answer", "explain": "A candidate interviews for an EUR 80,000 payments BA role.\nOne question is outside their knowledge.\nThey answer confidently, and wrongly. The interview ends there.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Interviewer asks about a payment flow the candidate has not worked on\n→ The candidate guesses rather than saying so\n→ The reasoning sounds confident and falls apart on the follow-up\n→ The panel marks down judgement, not just knowledge\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Never admit you do not know.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIn an interview, not knowing an answer is acceptable if it is handled honestly: state the assumptions being made, explain the reasoning that follows from them, and identify what would need to be validated before committing to an answer. Never bluff. Interviewers test judgement and honesty as much as knowledge, and a confident guess that turns out wrong is worse than a clear statement of what is known and what is not.\n\nA confident guess ≠ A good answer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSay what you assume, show your reasoning, and name what must be validated.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Assumptions being made, stated plainly\n→ Reasoning that follows from them\n→ What must be validated before committing\n→ What is known, and what is not\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we answer every question?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves our judgement is sound even when our knowledge runs out?\"\n\nHave you seen a confident wrong answer cost more than an honest 'I don't know'?", "takeaway": "Interviewers test judgement and honesty as much as knowledge, and a confident guess that turns out wrong is worse than a clear statement of what is known and what is not.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Handling an unknown answer honestly", "parts": [{"label": "Question outside knowledge", "note": "A payment flow the candidate has not worked on"}, {"label": "Confident guess given", "note": "The answer sounds sure and falls apart on follow-up"}, {"label": "Judgement marked down", "note": "The panel doubts honesty, not just knowledge"}, {"label": "Honest route missed", "note": "Assumptions, reasoning and validation were never offered"}, {"label": "Never bluff", "note": "A clear statement beats a confident wrong guess"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-099", "seq": 48, "module": "Foundations", "source": "Public post", "title": "The unfamiliar EUR 45,000 scenario that rewarded method, not memory", "explain": "A candidate faces an unfamiliar EUR 45,000 payments scenario in an interview.\nThey have never seen this flow before.\nThey answer in a fixed order, and the panel hires them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Scenario presented: a payment flow the candidate does not know\n→ The candidate clarifies the assumptions first\n→ The flow is broken into stages, risks named at each one\n→ The candidate states what evidence they would get before deciding\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"You need to know the answer to answer well.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nFace an unfamiliar scenario in a fixed order: clarify the assumptions, break the flow into its stages, identify the risks at each stage, and state what evidence would be obtained before deciding. This structure shows clear thinking even when the domain is new, and it prevents the answer from becoming a guess.\n\nMethod is what carries you when knowledge runs out.\n\nA guess ≠ An answer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun every unfamiliar scenario through the same fixed order.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Assumptions clarified first\n→ Flow broken into stages\n→ Risks identified at each stage\n→ Evidence needed before deciding\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we know this scenario?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we thought clearly even where our knowledge ran out?\"\n\nHave you seen a candidate win on method when memory had nothing to offer?", "takeaway": "Method is what carries you when knowledge runs out.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A fixed order for unfamiliar scenarios", "parts": [{"label": "Unfamiliar scenario", "note": "A EUR 45,000 payment flow never seen before"}, {"label": "Assumptions clarified", "note": "The candidate states what is being assumed"}, {"label": "Flow broken into stages", "note": "Each stage is worked through in order"}, {"label": "Risks and evidence named", "note": "Risks per stage, and proof needed before deciding"}, {"label": "Method carries you", "note": "Clear thinking beats a guess when knowledge runs out"}]}}, "level": "foundation", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/scenario.html", "label": "Scenario Simulator", "slug": "scenario"}}}, {"explain": "A P1 incident struck mobile banking, fund transfers and ATMs.\nTransfers worth EUR 500,000 queued while customers waited.\nThe war room restored service in about 40 minutes.\nThe cause was still unknown.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ P1 declared across mobile banking, fund transfers and ATMs\n→ War room restores service in about 40 minutes\n→ Customers told service is back\n→ A batch process had consumed critical database resources during peak hours\n→ The same batch is still scheduled for tomorrow\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Close the incident once service is restored.\"\n\nSo the incident closed, and the cause that broke it was left running.\n\nWhy?\n\nIncident and problem management are two disciplines with different clocks. Incident restores service fast. Problem finds why it happened so it never recurs. Restoring service without fixing the batch overlap only buys 24 hours until the next peak.\n\nRestored is not resolved.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRestore and resolve are two different jobs, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Incident timeline, restore the service\n→ Problem timeline, find the cause\n→ Permanent fix, reschedule the batch and add guards and alerts\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When was service back?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the cause is fixed and cannot recur at the next peak?\"\n\nHave you ever seen an incident closed as fixed, only for the same cause to strike again the next day?", "id": "SCN-26", "level": "foundation", "module": "Foundations", "seq": 87, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Restored is not resolved.", "title": "P1 hit mobile banking, service back in 40 minutes, cause still unknown", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "Service back in 40 minutes, cause still running", "parts": [{"label": "Incident: restore service", "note": "Service restored in about 40 minutes here"}, {"label": "Problem: find the cause", "note": "Cause found so it never recurs"}, {"label": "Evidence of the cause", "note": "Database metrics, batch schedules and the overlap window"}, {"label": "The permanent fix", "note": "Reschedule the batch and add guards and alerts"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "SCN-33", "seq": 88, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Public post", "title": "Payments fail only at 11 AM, EUR 90,000 queue stuck", "explain": "Every day at 11 AM, payments start timing out.\nA queue of EUR 90,000 in payments stalls.\nBy noon everything works again.\nThe team keeps fixing the code. The code is fine.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Timeouts appear only at peak hours\n→ Slow processing, then lagging database queries\n→ High server load at peak traffic\n→ Servers not sized for peak traffic\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A failure is a code bug, so fix the code.\"\n\nFailures that appear only at peak hours point at capacity, not code.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe 5-Why chain runs from timeouts to slow processing to lagging database queries to high server load to servers not sized for peak traffic. Fixing code leaves the root untouched, so the same timeouts return at the same hour the next day.\n\nPeak-only failures mean capacity.\n\nWhy do the same payments fail at 11 AM and pass at noon?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFix the root, then monitor to confirm the failures actually stopped.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Symptom, when the failures happen\n→ Root cause, capacity or code, proved by the pattern\n→ Fix, indexing, more capacity, load balancing\n→ Proof, monitoring that shows the failures stopped\n\nLog the hour of every failure, and treat a peak-only pattern as a capacity case from the start.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What code failed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the failures stopped at peak, not just moved?\"\n\nHave you chased a code bug that turned out to be capacity?", "takeaway": "Peak-only failures mean capacity. All-day failures mean code or data.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Start from the symptom", "note": "Failures only at peak hours, EUR 90,000 queue stuck."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Walk the 5-Why chain", "note": "Timeouts, slow processing, lagging queries, heavy load, undersized servers."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Peak-only or all-day?", "note": "Peak-only means capacity, all-day means code or data."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the root cause", "note": "Indexing, more capacity, load balancing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Monitor once fixed", "note": "Prove the failures stopped, not just moved."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "RUN7-010", "seq": 116, "module": "Foundations", "level": "foundation", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "USD 2,000,000 trade, no rule says which instrument comes first", "explain": "A buyer and seller agree a USD 2,000,000 trade.\nThe buyer asks which instrument starts it, letter of credit or bank guarantee.\nThere is no rule that answers that.\nThe team waits for a rule that does not exist.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Trade agreed for USD 2,000,000\n→ Buyer asks for the first instrument\n→ Team searches the rulebook for a fixed order\n→ No rule fixes which instrument a trade starts with\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The rules pick the first instrument.\"\n\nNo rule fixes which instrument a trade starts with, whether a letter of credit or a bank guarantee.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe choice depends on many factors, above all how much security both parties want. Greater appetite for security on both sides calls for more sophisticated payment techniques, so the answer is a judgement about the parties, not a lookup in a rulebook.\n\nSecurity appetite picks the instrument, not a rule.\n\nWhy does the team wait for a rule that cannot answer the question?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDecide the instrument from the parties' security needs, and record the judgement, not a rule reference.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Letter of credit, one instrument option\n→ Bank guarantee, another instrument option\n→ Security appetite, what both parties want above all\n→ Other factors, the many facts that shape the choice\n\nWrite the choice down with the security judgement behind it, so the next trade starts from the reasoning.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which rule picks the instrument?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the instrument matches the security both parties want?\"\n\nHave you waited on a rule that never existed?", "takeaway": "Greater appetite for security on both sides calls for more sophisticated payment techniques.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Which instrument starts a trade?", "parts": [{"label": "No rule fixes the starting instrument", "note": "No rule fixes which instrument a trade starts with, letter of credit or bank guarantee."}, {"label": "It depends on many factors", "note": "The choice depends on many factors."}, {"label": "Security both parties want matters most", "note": "Above all it depends on how much security both parties want."}, {"label": "More security means more sophisticated techniques", "note": "The more security both parties want, the more sophisticated the payment techniques will be."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "ABH-18", "seq": 125, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 600,000 GPP payment, the mapping that nobody tested", "explain": "A EUR 600,000 payment goes through GPP.\nThe ISO 20022 mapping was never tested with this message type.\nThe payment fails at mapping, is routed wrong, and posts twice.\nThree failures, one untested stage.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ ISO 20022 mapping, mapping can fail or delay the payment\n→ Routing, wrong route delays or fails the payment\n→ Duplicate handling, duplicates must be caught before posting\n→ Core banking integration, integration gaps stall the payment\n→ Testing and reconciliation, each stage needs its own proof\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Test the flow end to end and every stage is covered.\"\n\nThe GPP end-to-end flow has real implementation challenges: ISO 20022 mapping, routing, duplicates, core banking integration, testing and reconciliation.\n\nWhy?\n\nEach of these is a place where a payment can fail or be delayed, and each needs its own design and test coverage. An end-to-end test that passes on the happy path proves nothing about the mapping of a new message type or the duplicate check under a retry.\n\nA happy path test is not stage coverage.\n\nWhy does a tested flow fail at mapping, routing and posting at once?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive each challenge its own design and its own test coverage, and prove each stage separately.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ ISO 20022 mapping, tested per message type\n→ Routing, tested per route\n→ Duplicates, caught before posting\n→ Core banking integration, tested per posting\n→ Reconciliation, proving the payment completed correctly\n\nShow test coverage per stage, not just one end-to-end pass.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the flow pass testing?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each stage was designed and tested on its own?\"\n\nHave you seen a happy path test hide three stage failures?", "takeaway": "Each of these is a place where a payment can fail or be delayed, and each needs its own design and test coverage.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "ISO 20022 mapping", "note": "Mapping can fail or delay the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Routing", "note": "Wrong route delays or fails the payment."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Duplicate handling clear?", "note": "Duplicates must be caught before posting."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Core banking integration", "note": "Integration gaps stall the payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Testing", "note": "Each stage needs its own test coverage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Reconciliation", "note": "Proves the payment completed correctly."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "ABH-25", "seq": 132, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 200,000 API change, the endpoint looked simple, the blast radius was not", "explain": "A team changes an API endpoint for a EUR 200,000 payment flow.\nThe endpoint looks simple, one field added.\nThree downstream systems break.\nNobody mapped the consumers.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Endpoint changed, one field added\n→ Data, consumers and dependencies never mapped\n→ Downstream systems read the changed payload\n→ Three break, each in a different way\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The endpoint is the API.\"\n\nAnalysing an API means looking beyond the endpoint.\n\nWhy?\n\nA BA must understand the data, the consumers, the dependencies and the impact of a change, because an API that looks simple at the endpoint can have deep downstream effects. The endpoint is only the surface, and the blast radius lives underneath it.\n\nThe endpoint is only the surface.\n\nWhy does one added field break three systems?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap data, consumers, dependencies and impact before any API change ships.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Data, what the API carries and changes\n→ Consumers, who calls the API\n→ Dependencies, what the API relies on downstream\n→ Impact of change, what a change breaks or moves\n→ The endpoint, only the surface, not the whole API\n\nList every consumer and dependency on the change record, and confirm each one before release.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the endpoint change?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every consumer and dependency still works after the change?\"\n\nHave you seen a small endpoint change break systems nobody mapped?", "takeaway": "A BA must understand the data, the consumers, the dependencies and the impact of a change.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "API analysis", "parts": [{"label": "Data", "note": "What the API carries and changes."}, {"label": "Consumers", "note": "Who calls the API."}, {"label": "Dependencies", "note": "What the API relies on downstream."}, {"label": "Impact of change", "note": "What a change breaks or moves."}, {"label": "The endpoint", "note": "Only the surface, not the whole API."}, {"label": "EUR 200,000 flow", "note": "One field added, three downstream systems broken."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch80-api-design-standards-and-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 80: API design standards and integration patterns", "chapter": "ch80", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "ABH-26", "seq": 133, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 350,000 requirement written without API knowledge, misses the payload gap", "explain": "A BA writes a requirement for a EUR 350,000 payment flow.\nThe requirement looks complete.\nDevelopment finds a payload gap on day one.\nThe BA could not see it, because the API was never understood.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Requirement written from the business view only\n→ Endpoints, payloads and data gaps never examined\n→ Impact, security and backward compatibility never assessed\n→ Development stalls on a gap the requirement never named\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Business requirements do not need API knowledge.\"\n\nA BA who understands APIs can work with endpoints, payloads, data gaps, impact analysis, security and backward compatibility.\n\nWhy?\n\nWithout that knowledge, the BA cannot write a complete requirement or assess the impact of a change. The gap is not a development surprise, it is a requirement that was written blind to the API it describes.\n\nA requirement without API knowledge is incomplete by definition.\n\nWhy does a complete-looking requirement stall development on day one?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nLearn the API before writing the requirement, and name the payload, the gaps and the compatibility in it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Endpoints, where the API is called\n→ Payloads, what the API carries\n→ Data gaps, what is missing in the data\n→ Impact analysis, what a change affects\n→ Security and backward compatibility, old callers keep working\n\nAttach the endpoint and payload to every API requirement, with the data gaps listed.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the business need clear?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the requirement covers the API it describes?\"\n\nHave you written a requirement that stalled on a payload gap?", "takeaway": "Without API knowledge, the BA cannot write a complete requirement or assess the impact of a change.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A BA who understands APIs", "parts": [{"label": "Endpoints", "note": "Where the API is called."}, {"label": "Payloads", "note": "What the API carries."}, {"label": "Data gaps", "note": "What is missing in the data."}, {"label": "Impact analysis", "note": "What a change affects."}, {"label": "Security", "note": "How the API is protected."}, {"label": "Backward compatibility", "note": "Old callers keep working after a change."}, {"label": "Complete requirements", "note": "Possible only with API knowledge."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "INS-119", "seq": 154, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The vendor feature that worked everywhere except where it mattered", "explain": "A vendor demos a capability the bank needs.\n\nIt works beautifully on screen. It only works globally.\n\nThe bank needs it per country. The feature is useless.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Vendor demos the capability\n→ The bank signs up\n→ Configuration only works at global level\n→ The country rule cannot be set\n→ The capability cannot be used where it matters\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The vendor says the capability exists.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA capability that only works globally, when the bank needs it per country, by entity, by product or by payment type, is not fit for purpose. A demoed feature and a usable feature are not the same thing.\n\nA demoed feature is not a usable feature.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nVerify the configuration granularity before accepting that a requirement is met. Ask at which level it can be configured, and prove it with a test, not a demo.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The capability itself\n→ The level it can be configured at\n→ The acceptance test that proves it\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Does the capability exist?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the capability works at the level the business needs?\"\n\nHave you accepted a vendor feature on a demo, only to find it could not be configured where it mattered?", "takeaway": "A demoed feature is not a usable feature. Verify the configuration level first.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Vendor capability: check the configuration level", "parts": [{"label": "Vendor demos the capability", "note": "The vendor says the capability exists"}, {"label": "Configuration is global only", "note": "It cannot be set per country, entity, product or payment type"}, {"label": "Bank needs per country", "note": "The business needs the capability at country level"}, {"label": "Global only is not fit", "note": "A global-only capability is not fit for purpose here"}, {"label": "Verify before accepting", "note": "Check the granularity before accepting the requirement as met"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C10", "seq": 177, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 45,000 payment that failed in the place nobody planned for", "explain": "A EUR 45,000 payment is processed with the wrong details, and the verification of payee check fails.\n\nThe team has no alternative ready, and no communication plan.\n\nThe customer finds out from the beneficiary, not from the bank.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Solution goes live\n→ Requirements change, and the change was captured wrongly\n→ Processing is delayed, and a system slows down\n→ Payment is processed with wrong details\n→ No alternative is ready, and no one tells the customer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The solution will work.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment solution can fail in three places: functionally, when requirements change or were captured wrongly; technically, when processing is delayed, a system is down or performance drops; and in business terms, when a payment is processed with wrong details or a check like verification of payee fails.\n\nDesign for failure, or failure designs the day.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPlan for each kind of failure, know the alternative when a solution fails, and keep a communication plan with the business and stakeholders. One payment change rarely touches only one application.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Functional failure plan\n→ Technical failure plan\n→ Business failure plan\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Will the solution work?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we know the alternative when this solution fails?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail in the one place nobody planned for?", "takeaway": "Design for failure, or failure designs the day. Plan for all three places a solution can fail.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Design for failure: three places", "parts": [{"label": "Functional failure", "note": "Requirements change or were captured wrongly"}, {"label": "Technical failure", "note": "Processing is delayed, a system is down or performance drops"}, {"label": "Business failure", "note": "A payment is processed with wrong details or a check like verification of payee fails"}, {"label": "Plan for each kind of failure", "note": "The BA should plan for each kind of failure and know the alternative"}, {"label": "Keep a communication plan", "note": "Keep a communication plan with the business and stakeholders"}, {"label": "Cover all failure scenarios", "note": "Ask whether all possible failure scenarios have been covered before implementing"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-C14", "seq": 178, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Schedule it for next week: the five rules hidden in one sentence", "explain": "A customer asks to schedule a EUR 25,000 payment for next week.\n\nIt sounds simple. It hides five rules.\n\nThe system accepts a past date, cannot reschedule it, and nobody knows what happens when the date passes unprocessed.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Customer asks to schedule a payment\n→ Requirement is written down as one sentence\n→ No rule stops a past date\n→ No rule covers rescheduling with a new effective date\n→ No rule cancels it when it is not processed on the requested date\n→ In a request to pay such as pain.013, the execution date shows even when the data is empty\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Document what the customer asked.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA high-level need has to be decoded into business rules: a payment can be set for a future date, recreated or rescheduled with a new effective date, never scheduled for a date in the past, scheduled for several payment types, and cancelled when it is not processed on the requested date.\n\nA requirement that cannot be written as a rule is not a simple requirement.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFind the business rule behind the requirement rather than mapping one field to another.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Future date rule\n→ Reschedule rule\n→ Cancel rule\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What did the customer ask for?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the rule behind the requirement is written down?\"\n\nHave you seen a one-sentence requirement hide five rules?", "takeaway": "A requirement that cannot be written as a rule is not a simple requirement. Decode it, do not just document it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Decode the requirement, do not document it", "parts": [{"label": "Schedule for a future date", "note": "A payment can be set for a future date"}, {"label": "Recreate or reschedule", "note": "A payment can be recreated or rescheduled with a new effective date"}, {"label": "Never schedule in the past", "note": "A payment is never scheduled for a date in the past"}, {"label": "Several payment types", "note": "A payment can be scheduled for several payment types"}, {"label": "Cancel if not processed on the date", "note": "Cancel the payment when it is not processed on the requested date"}, {"label": "pain.013 execution date", "note": "In a request to pay, do not show the execution date when the data is empty"}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": ["pain.013"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-023", "seq": 191, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The customers failing five times a day that nobody noticed", "explain": "Customers are failing payments five times a day, every day.\n\nNobody notices, because the rule to find them was never defined.\n\nBy the time the complaints arrive, the customers have already left.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ No time window is defined\n→ No failure condition is defined\n→ No threshold is set for what counts as repeated\n→ Test transactions and duplicates are counted as real failures\n→ The wrong customers are targeted, and the real ones are missed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Count the failures.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nFinding customers with repeated failed transactions means translating the business rule into a time-windowed aggregation: define the window, the failure condition and the threshold, then aggregate and validate the data quality before acting. A count that includes test transactions, duplicates or a wrong window will target the wrong customers.\n\nA precise rule plus clean data is the whole method.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine the window, the condition and the threshold first, then check the data quality before acting on the result.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Time window\n→ Failure condition and threshold\n→ Data quality check\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How many failures?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the count excludes tests and duplicates?\"\n\nHave you seen a team act on a failure count that included test transactions?", "takeaway": "A precise rule plus clean data is the whole method.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define the window", "note": "Set the time window for the aggregation."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define the failure condition", "note": "Set what counts as a failed transaction."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Set the threshold", "note": "Set how many failures count as repeated."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Aggregate the data", "note": "Aggregate transactions over the window."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the data clean?", "note": "Exclude test transactions and duplicates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Act on the result", "note": "Target the right customers."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-007", "seq": 193, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 75,000 payment rule nobody could test", "explain": "A business asks for a rule on EUR 75,000 payments.\n\nThe requirement is written down as one vague sentence.\n\nIt is built, and the problem is found in testing, when fixing it costs ten times more.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Requirement arrives vague and ambiguous\n→ It is written down without discovery questions\n→ No concrete examples pin down the behaviour\n→ No one observes how the work is really done\n→ No playback confirms the understanding\n→ Testing finds the misunderstanding\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Write down what the business said.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAmbiguity is normal at the start of a requirement. Discovery questions find what is actually meant, concrete examples pin down the behaviour, process observation shows how the work is really done, and playback confirms the understanding with stakeholders.\n\nAmbiguity handled early is cheap; ambiguity found in testing is expensive.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe output must be a requirement that is testable: an observable outcome, a boundary condition and an acceptance criterion, rather than a vague wish no one can verify.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Discovery questions\n→ Concrete examples and observation\n→ Playback with stakeholders\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What did the business say?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the requirement states an observable outcome?\"\n\nHave you seen a vague requirement found in testing, when it was already expensive to fix?", "takeaway": "Ambiguity handled early is cheap; ambiguity found in testing is expensive.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Turning ambiguity into a testable requirement", "parts": [{"label": "Discovery questions", "note": "Find what is actually meant."}, {"label": "Concrete examples", "note": "Pin down the behaviour."}, {"label": "Process observation", "note": "See how the work is really done."}, {"label": "Playback", "note": "Confirm understanding with stakeholders."}, {"label": "Testable output", "note": "States an observable outcome, a boundary and a criterion."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-008", "seq": 194, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The requirement that passed review and failed every test", "explain": "A requirement for a EUR 60,000 payment flow passes review.\n\nIt fails every test, because no test can prove it.\n\nThe team argues about whether it was delivered, and there is no way to resolve the dispute.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Observable outcome is stated\n→ Boundary conditions are missing\n→ Negative cases are missing\n→ Acceptance criteria are missing\n→ No one can verify the requirement\n→ Delivery is disputed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The requirement is written, so it is testable.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA requirement is testable when it defines an observable outcome, the boundary conditions, the negative cases and the acceptance criteria. Without these four, a requirement cannot be verified.\n\nWithout these four, a requirement cannot be verified.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck every requirement for all four parts before it leaves analysis. A missing part is a dispute waiting to happen.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Observable outcome\n→ Boundary conditions\n→ Negative cases\n→ Acceptance criteria\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is it written down?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this requirement can pass or fail?\"\n\nHave you seen a requirement disputed because nobody could prove whether it was delivered?", "takeaway": "Without these four, a requirement cannot be verified and a dispute about whether it was delivered cannot be resolved.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The four parts of a testable requirement", "parts": [{"label": "Observable outcome", "note": "States what can be seen or measured."}, {"label": "Boundary conditions", "note": "State where the rule starts and stops."}, {"label": "Negative cases", "note": "State what must not happen."}, {"label": "Acceptance criteria", "note": "State the condition that proves it is met."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-009", "seq": 195, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two stakeholders, one EUR 200,000 decision, no record of why", "explain": "Two stakeholders disagree over a EUR 200,000 payment flow decision.\n\nA compromise is reached in the meeting.\n\nNext month the same stakeholders meet, and the same argument reopens, because nobody wrote down the decision.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Competing requirements clash\n→ Value, risk, urgency, customer impact and dependencies are debated\n→ A decision is made\n→ The decision, who made it and why, is not documented\n→ The compromise is reopened at the next meeting\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"We agreed in the meeting.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe comparison must be evidence based, so each stakeholder can see why one requirement was chosen over another. An undocumented compromise will be reopened the next time the same stakeholders meet.\n\nDocument the decision, who made it and why.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFacilitate a documented decision on evidence, and record who made it and why.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Evidence based comparison\n→ The decision\n→ The record of who decided and why\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we agree?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the decision and its reason survive the meeting?\"\n\nHave you seen the same argument reopen because the decision was never written down?", "takeaway": "Document the decision, who made it and why, because an undocumented compromise will be reopened the next time the same stakeholders meet.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Deciding between conflicting requirements", "parts": [{"label": "Value", "note": "Compare the competing requirements on value."}, {"label": "Risk", "note": "Compare them on risk."}, {"label": "Urgency", "note": "Compare them on urgency."}, {"label": "Customer impact", "note": "Compare them on customer impact."}, {"label": "Dependencies", "note": "Compare them on dependencies."}, {"label": "Documented decision", "note": "Record the decision, who made it and why."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-015", "seq": 196, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The business asked in plain words, and the system was built in system words", "explain": "A non-technical stakeholder describes a EUR 40,000 payment need in business language.\n\nThe team builds it in system language.\n\nPlayback is skipped, the misunderstanding is found after the build, and the rework costs a sprint.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Interviews hear the need in business language\n→ Workshops build shared understanding\n→ Observation shows how the work is actually done\n→ Examples pin down the detail\n→ Structured playback is skipped\n→ The build reflects the misunderstanding\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"We heard the requirement.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nNon-technical stakeholders describe what they need in business language, not in system language. Playback matters most, because it catches misunderstandings while they are still cheap to fix.\n\nConfirm before you write, not after you build.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun structured playback before the requirement is written down, and let the stakeholder recognise their own need in it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Interviews and workshops\n→ Observation and examples\n→ Structured playback\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we hear them?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the stakeholder recognised their own need in the playback?\"\n\nHave you seen a build go wrong because playback was skipped?", "takeaway": "Confirm before you write, not after you build.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Eliciting from non-technical stakeholders", "parts": [{"label": "Interviews", "note": "Hear the need in business language."}, {"label": "Workshops", "note": "Build shared understanding."}, {"label": "Observation", "note": "See how the work is actually done."}, {"label": "Examples", "note": "Pin down the detail."}, {"label": "Structured playback", "note": "Catches misunderstandings while they are cheap to fix."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-017", "seq": 197, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The missing rule found in UAT, and the EUR 60,000 fix that followed", "explain": "A missing rule is found in UAT.\n\nThe fix costs EUR 60,000, because the gap was lost in discovery and a whole stage must be reworked.\n\nThe team fixes the symptom. The same gap recurs next release.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ A piece of the need is missed\n→ The gap is found late, in UAT\n→ The fix is applied to the symptom\n→ The origin is never traced\n→ The same gap recurs\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Fix the symptom.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nTracing the gap to its origin matters because it decides whether the fix is a small correction or a rework of a whole stage, and it prevents the same gap from recurring. The gap could have been lost in discovery, analysis, design, implementation or testing.\n\nFix the origin, not just the symptom.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the gap back to where it was lost, and agree corrective action with the owner of that stage.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Discovery and analysis\n→ Design and implementation\n→ Testing\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is the fix?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where the gap was lost?\"\n\nHave you seen the same gap recur because the origin was never traced?", "takeaway": "Fix the origin, not just the symptom.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Find the gap", "note": "A piece of the need was missed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace it to its origin", "note": "Discovery, analysis, design, implementation or testing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Small correction or whole stage rework?", "note": "The origin decides the size of the fix."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Agree corrective action", "note": "Agreed with the owner of the stage where it was lost."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Prevent recurrence", "note": "Tracing the origin stops the same gap recurring."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-018", "seq": 198, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The process diagram that misled everyone who used it", "explain": "A process diagram for a EUR 100,000 payment flow is drawn from assumptions.\n\nIt looks right. It is wrong.\n\nThe team follows it, and every hand-off goes to the wrong place.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Diagram is drawn\n→ Actors, triggers, decisions, hand-offs and data stores are assumed\n→ Nothing is checked against reality\n→ Diagram is never validated with the people who do the work\n→ Everyone who relies on it is misled\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The diagram looks right.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA process diagram must show the actors, the triggers, the decisions, the hand-offs and the data stores, and each of these must be checked against reality rather than assumed.\n\nA diagram is only as good as its validation.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nValidate the diagram with the people who do the work. Choose the diagram that fits the problem, then prove it matches reality.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Actors and triggers\n→ Decisions and hand-offs\n→ Data stores and validation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the diagram drawn?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the diagram matches the real process?\"\n\nHave you seen a team follow a diagram that did not match the real process?", "takeaway": "A diagram is only as good as its validation.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Process and data-flow diagrams", "parts": [{"label": "Actors", "note": "Shown and checked against reality."}, {"label": "Triggers", "note": "Shown and checked against reality."}, {"label": "Decisions", "note": "Shown and checked against reality."}, {"label": "Hand-offs", "note": "Shown and checked against reality."}, {"label": "Data stores", "note": "Shown and checked against reality."}, {"label": "Validation", "note": "Validated with the people who do the work."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-019", "seq": 199, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The requirements signed off in a day, and rebuilt over six weeks", "explain": "Requirements for a EUR 80,000 payment change are signed off in a day.\n\nValidation is skipped to save time.\n\nThe rebuild takes six weeks, because the misunderstandings were found in development and testing instead.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Walkthroughs are skipped\n→ Consistency checks are skipped\n→ Testability checks are skipped\n→ Sign-off is given\n→ Misunderstandings surface in development and testing\n→ Fixes cost far more than validation would have\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Sign-off means the requirements are ready.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA walkthrough catches misunderstandings, a consistency check catches contradictions, a testability check catches requirements that cannot be verified, and sign-off records agreement. Skipping validation does not save time; it moves the cost into development and testing.\n\nSkipping validation does not save time; it moves the cost.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRun walkthroughs, consistency checks, testability checks and formal sign-off before development starts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Walkthroughs\n→ Consistency checks\n→ Testability checks\n→ Formal sign-off\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are they signed off?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the requirement set was validated before development?\"\n\nHave you seen validation skipped to save a day, and the rebuild take six weeks?", "takeaway": "Skipping validation does not save time; it moves the cost into development and testing, where fixes are far more expensive.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Walkthroughs", "note": "Walkthroughs with stakeholders catch misunderstandings."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Consistency checks", "note": "Checks across the set catch contradictions."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Testability checks", "note": "Checks on each requirement catch what cannot be verified."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "All checks passed?", "note": "Skipping validation moves the cost into development and testing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Formal sign-off", "note": "Sign-off records agreement before development starts."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-081", "seq": 200, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The change nobody could trace, and the release it broke", "explain": "A change is made to a EUR 120,000 payment flow.\n\nNobody can trace it forward to its impact.\n\nThe release breaks, and nobody can trace the defect back to its origin.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Business objective is set\n→ Requirement is written\n→ Story and design follow\n→ Tests are run\n→ A defect appears\n→ No chain links the defect back to its origin\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"We know what changed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA traceability matrix connects business objectives to requirements, stories, design, tests, defects and release evidence, with each row showing the chain from the objective through the requirement to the test that proves it. Any change can then be traced forward to its impact and any defect traced back to its origin.\n\nWithout traceability, a team cannot prove what was built is what was asked for.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep the chain intact from objective to release evidence, or a change cannot be assessed safely.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Objectives and requirements\n→ Stories and design\n→ Tests, defects and release evidence\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What changed?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this change traces forward to its impact?\"\n\nHave you seen a release break because nobody could trace a change to its impact?", "takeaway": "Without traceability, a team cannot prove that what was built is what was asked for, and cannot assess the impact of a change safely.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Tracing objectives through to release evidence", "parts": [{"label": "Business objectives", "note": "The start of each traceability chain."}, {"label": "Requirements", "note": "Linked from the objective."}, {"label": "Stories", "note": "Linked from the requirement."}, {"label": "Design", "note": "Linked from the story."}, {"label": "Tests", "note": "Prove the requirement is met."}, {"label": "Defects and release evidence", "note": "Traced back to their origin."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-038", "seq": 201, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The scope that shrank one change at a time", "explain": "Requirements for a EUR 150,000 payment project change every week.\n\nEach change is small. The delivery date slips, and nobody can say why.\n\nThe scope eroded one uncosted change at a time.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Change is requested\n→ No change intake records what is asked and why\n→ No impact analysis states the cost in scope, effort and risk\n→ No prioritisation ranks it against other work\n→ No decision governance records who approved it\n→ Weekly changes silently erode the scope and the delivery date\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"It is only a small change.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nChange intake, impact analysis, prioritisation and decision governance are the discipline that keeps weekly change visible. Without them, the erosion is silent.\n\nWithout this discipline, weekly changes silently erode the scope and the delivery date.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRecord every change, cost it, rank it and record who approved it, or the date will move without anyone deciding it should.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Change intake\n→ Impact analysis\n→ Prioritisation\n→ Decision governance\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the change small?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves each change was costed before it was accepted?\"\n\nHave you seen a delivery date slip one small change at a time?", "takeaway": "Without this discipline, weekly changes silently erode the scope and the delivery date.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Change intake", "note": "Records what is asked and why."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Impact analysis", "note": "States the cost in scope, effort and risk."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Prioritisation", "note": "Ranks the change against other work."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Approved with the cost visible?", "note": "Decision governance records who approved it and why."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Decision governance", "note": "Records who approved it and why."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-039", "seq": 202, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two weeks wanted, six estimated, and the EUR 150,000 decision in between", "explain": "The business wants a EUR 150,000 payment feature in two weeks.\n\nDevelopment estimates six.\n\nThe meeting argues over the number, and nobody decides what to deliver.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Business wants two weeks, development estimates six\n→ The minimum outcome that would still be valuable is never clarified\n→ Scope is never sliced to the smallest useful increment\n→ Dependencies that drive the estimate are never identified\n→ Trade-offs are argued, not evidenced\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Argue the estimate.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nClarify the minimum outcome, slice the scope, identify the dependencies, and present evidence based trade-offs. The decision is then about what to deliver and what to defer, not about whose estimate is right.\n\nThe decision is about what to deliver and what to defer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTurn an estimate argument into a scope decision, with evidence in front of the people who must choose.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Minimum valuable outcome\n→ Sliced scope\n→ Dependencies\n→ Evidence based trade-offs\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Whose estimate is right?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the smallest useful increment is agreed?\"\n\nHave you seen a two-week versus six-week argument settled by slicing the scope instead?", "takeaway": "The decision is then about what to deliver and what to defer, not about whose estimate is right.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two weeks wanted, six estimated", "parts": [{"label": "Minimum valuable outcome", "note": "Clarify the smallest outcome still worth having."}, {"label": "Slice the scope", "note": "Cut scope to the smallest useful increment."}, {"label": "Dependencies", "note": "Identify what drives the estimate."}, {"label": "Evidence based trade-offs", "note": "Present trade-offs rather than arguing over the number."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-044", "seq": 203, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Build us the solution, said the board, and nobody asked what problem it solved", "explain": "Senior management asks for a solution for a EUR 200,000 payment problem.\n\nNo requirements are given. The team builds the solution anyway.\n\nIt does not solve the real problem, because nobody asked what the problem was.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Solution is requested without requirements\n→ No one asks what outcome is needed\n→ No one asks what constraints apply\n→ No one asks what options exist\n→ No one asks what criteria will decide between them\n→ The wrong solution is built on time\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Build the solution they asked for.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nReframing the request around outcomes, constraints, options and decision criteria turns a request for a specific solution into a decision that can be made on evidence, and avoids building something that does not solve the real problem.\n\nReframe the ask before you build the answer.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAsk what outcome is needed, what constraints apply, what options exist and what criteria will decide, before any build starts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Outcomes\n→ Constraints\n→ Options and decision criteria\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What solution was asked for?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we know the outcome before we choose the solution?\"\n\nHave you built a solution that answered the ask and missed the problem?", "takeaway": "Reframe the ask before you build the answer.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Solution requested without requirements", "parts": [{"label": "Outcomes", "note": "Ask what outcome is needed."}, {"label": "Constraints", "note": "Ask what constraints apply."}, {"label": "Options", "note": "Ask what options exist."}, {"label": "Decision criteria", "note": "Ask what criteria will decide between options."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-045", "seq": 204, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Complete, said the business. Incomplete, said the developers.", "explain": "The business believes the requirements for a EUR 90,000 payment change are complete.\n\nThe developers disagree.\n\nBoth sides read the same words and understand different things, and the rebuild starts before anyone notices.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Requirements are declared complete\n→ Developers read them differently\n→ Concrete examples are never used to show how each side reads them\n→ No walkthrough surfaces the differences\n→ Traceability never shows what is covered and what is not\n→ Shared definitions are never agreed\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The requirements are complete.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe gap is usually in interpretation rather than in content. Close the interpretation gap before development, because it is far cheaper to agree on meaning now than to rebuild later.\n\nClose the interpretation gap before development.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse concrete examples, walkthroughs, traceability and shared definitions to prove both sides read the requirement the same way.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Concrete examples\n→ Walkthroughs\n→ Traceability and shared definitions\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are the requirements complete?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves both sides read the requirement the same way?\"\n\nHave you seen a build start while two sides read the same requirement differently?", "takeaway": "Close the interpretation gap before development, because it is far cheaper to agree on meaning now than to rebuild later.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Business says complete, developers disagree", "parts": [{"label": "Concrete examples", "note": "Show how each side reads the requirement."}, {"label": "Walkthroughs", "note": "Surface the differences in interpretation."}, {"label": "Traceability", "note": "Show what is covered and what is not."}, {"label": "Shared definitions", "note": "Agree definitions for terms read differently."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-046", "seq": 205, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The new payment screen nobody used", "explain": "A new digital payment screen is launched.\n\nNobody uses it.\n\nThe team calls the users stubborn. The resistance has a cause nobody bothered to find.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Digital change is launched\n→ Impacts on each user group are never mapped\n→ Stakeholder concerns are never identified\n→ Training needs are never defined\n→ No champions support the change\n→ Feedback is never collected, and adoption is never measured\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Users are just stubborn.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUser resistance to a digital change usually has a cause that can be identified. Resistance that is understood can be addressed; resistance that is dismissed as stubbornness will grow and will damage the change.\n\nDiagnose resistance before you try to overcome it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap the impacts, identify the concerns, define the training, find champions, collect feedback and measure adoption.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Impacts and concerns\n→ Training needs\n→ Champions, feedback and adoption\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How do we push users to adopt it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves we understand why users resist?\"\n\nHave you seen a launch fail because resistance was dismissed instead of diagnosed?", "takeaway": "Diagnose resistance before you try to overcome it.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "User resistance to digital change", "parts": [{"label": "Impacts on each user group", "note": "Map how the change affects each group."}, {"label": "Stakeholder groups and concerns", "note": "Identify who is concerned and about what."}, {"label": "Training needs", "note": "Define what training each group needs."}, {"label": "Champions", "note": "Find people who will support the change."}, {"label": "Feedback and adoption", "note": "Collect feedback and measure adoption."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-047", "seq": 206, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The BA who could not prove the EUR 300,000 saving", "explain": "A process change claims a EUR 300,000 saving.\n\nStakeholders ask for the proof.\n\nThere is none. Nothing was measured before, and nothing was measured after.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Process change is delivered\n→ Cost is never measured before and after\n→ Cycle time is never measured\n→ Error rates are never measured\n→ Customer experience is never measured\n→ Benefits realisation is never done\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The improvement is obvious.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn analyst demonstrates contribution by connecting process changes to measurable outcomes: link the change to cost, cycle time, error rates, customer experience and benefits realisation, and measure before and after so the improvement can be shown rather than claimed.\n\nMeasured value is the analyst's evidence, not a claim.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMeasure before and after, and finish with benefits realisation, because it proves the change delivered the value that justified it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cost and cycle time\n→ Error rates and customer experience\n→ Benefits realisation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did we deliver the change?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the change delivered the value that justified it?\"\n\nHave you seen a claimed saving dismissed because nobody measured it?", "takeaway": "Measured value is the analyst's evidence, not a claim.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Proving BA contribution with measures", "parts": [{"label": "Cost", "note": "Link the change to cost, measure before and after."}, {"label": "Cycle time", "note": "Link the change to cycle time, measure before and after."}, {"label": "Error rates", "note": "Link the change to error rates, measure before and after."}, {"label": "Customer experience", "note": "Link the change to customer experience."}, {"label": "Benefits realisation", "note": "The final step, proves the value delivered."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-010", "seq": 207, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The mid-sprint change that broke the sprint goal", "explain": "A change is requested mid-sprint on a EUR 100,000 payment feature.\n\nIt is accepted on the spot, without assessment.\n\nThe sprint goal breaks, and the team is held accountable for a target it never agreed to.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Change is requested mid-sprint\n→ Scope is never assessed\n→ Impact on the sprint goal is never assessed\n→ Effort is never assessed\n→ Dependencies are never assessed\n→ Change is accepted, and the goal silently breaks\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Just fit it in.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA change requested mid-sprint must be assessed before it is agreed: scope, impact on the sprint goal, effort required and dependencies introduced. Only then decide whether to accept it, defer it or swap it for something else.\n\nAccepting a change without assessment silently breaks the sprint goal.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAssess first, decide second. Accept, defer or swap only with the evidence in front of the team.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scope of the change\n→ Impact on the sprint goal\n→ Effort and dependencies\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can we fit it in?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the sprint goal survives this change?\"\n\nHave you seen a sprint goal break because a mid-sprint change was never assessed?", "takeaway": "Accepting a change without this assessment silently breaks the sprint goal and leaves the team accountable for a target it never agreed to.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Assess the scope", "note": "Assess the scope of the change."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess the sprint goal impact", "note": "Assess the impact on the sprint goal."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess the effort", "note": "Assess the effort the change requires."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess dependencies", "note": "Assess the dependencies it introduces."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Decide accept, defer or swap", "note": "Decide only after the assessment."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-052", "seq": 208, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The change that arrived when 70 percent of the sprint was done", "explain": "A change is requested when 70 percent of the sprint is done.\n\nThe team absorbs it quietly to be helpful.\n\nThe sprint goal is compromised, and nobody decided that it should be.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Change arrives late in the sprint\n→ Reason for the change is never established\n→ Effort is never assessed\n→ Dependencies are never assessed\n→ Options are never laid out for the product owner\n→ Team absorbs the change and the goal slips\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The team can absorb it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA late change needs a careful assessment: establish the reason, the effort, the dependencies and the options the product owner has, and do not silently compromise the sprint goal to absorb it. The product owner decides, with the evidence in front of them, whether the change is worth the disruption or whether it waits for the next sprint.\n\nA late change is a decision, not an instruction.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPresent the options with the evidence, and let the product owner decide. Never let a late change silently void the sprint goal.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reason for the change\n→ Effort and dependencies\n→ Options for the product owner\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can the team absorb it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the product owner chose with the full cost in front of them?\"\n\nHave you seen a late change absorbed quietly, and the sprint goal pay for it?", "takeaway": "A late change is a decision, not an instruction.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Establish the reason", "note": "Establish the reason for the change."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess the effort", "note": "Assess the effort it requires."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess dependencies", "note": "Assess the dependencies it affects."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Present the options", "note": "Lay out the options the product owner has."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Product owner decides", "note": "Worth the disruption, or it waits for next sprint."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-053", "seq": 209, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two senior stakeholders, opposite outcomes, one undocumented call", "explain": "Two senior stakeholders want opposite outcomes on a EUR 250,000 payment decision.\n\nA call is made in the room.\n\nIt is never documented, and the debate reopens at the next meeting.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Two senior stakeholders want opposite outcomes\n→ Value, risk, cost and customer impact are debated\n→ Evidence drives the ranking\n→ A decision is made\n→ Who made it and why is never recorded\n→ The debate reopens\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The senior person decides.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nFacilitate an evidence based prioritisation and document the accountable decision. A documented decision ends the debate; an undocumented one will be reopened every time the stakeholders meet.\n\nA documented decision ends the debate.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nLay out the value, the risk, the cost and the customer impact of each requirement, let the evidence drive the ranking, and record who made the decision and why.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Evidence for each option\n→ The decision\n→ The record of who decided and why\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Who is senior?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves who decided, and why?\"\n\nHave you seen the same debate reopen because the decision was never written down?", "takeaway": "A documented decision ends the debate; an undocumented one will be reopened every time the stakeholders meet.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Two senior stakeholders, opposite outcomes", "parts": [{"label": "Value", "note": "Lay out the value of each requirement."}, {"label": "Risk", "note": "Lay out the risk of each requirement."}, {"label": "Cost", "note": "Lay out the cost of each requirement."}, {"label": "Customer impact", "note": "Lay out the customer impact of each requirement."}, {"label": "Accountable decision", "note": "Record who made the decision and why."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-037", "seq": 210, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The process change that arrived the day before UAT", "explain": "A process change arrives the day before user acceptance testing on a EUR 180,000 payment release.\n\nIt is approved without assessment.\n\nThe testing already done is invalidated, and the release date is at risk.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Process change is requested just before UAT\n→ Requirement impact is never assessed\n→ Test scope is never assessed\n→ Release risk is never assessed\n→ Decision rights are never confirmed\n→ Change is approved, and completed testing is silently voided\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"It is a small process tweak.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA change approved without this assessment can invalidate the testing that has already been done and put the release date at risk.\n\nNever let a late change silently void completed testing.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAssess requirement impact, test scope, release risk and decision rights before approving any change this close to UAT.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Requirement impact\n→ Test scope\n→ Release risk\n→ Decision rights\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Is the change small?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves completed testing still stands after this change?\"\n\nHave you seen a late change void testing that was already complete?", "takeaway": "Never let a late change silently void completed testing.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Assess requirement impact", "note": "Determine which requirements change."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess test scope", "note": "Determine which tests must be added or rerun."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Assess release risk", "note": "Determine what risk the change adds to the release."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who can approve this?", "note": "Confirm who has the authority to approve it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm decision rights", "note": "Confirm who has the authority to approve it."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-041", "seq": 211, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Built exactly to sign-off, and the user still said no", "explain": "A feature is built exactly to signed-off requirements.\n\nThe user rejects it.\n\nThe team argues about whether it is a defect, and the argument lasts longer than the fix would have.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Feature is built to signed-off requirements\n→ User rejects it\n→ No one classifies the cause\n→ Defect, ambiguity, missed need and scope change are all treated the same\n→ The wrong owner works on the wrong fix\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"It matches the sign-off, so it must be right.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA defect means the build does not match the requirement, an ambiguity means the requirement was read differently, a missed need means the requirement was incomplete, and a scope change means the need has moved. Each cause has a different owner and a different fix.\n\nEach cause has a different owner and a different fix.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClassify the rejection first: defect, ambiguity, missed need or scope change. The classification decides the owner and the fix.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Defect\n→ Ambiguity\n→ Missed need\n→ Scope change\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was it built to sign-off?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which of the four causes this rejection is?\"\n\nHave you seen a rejected feature argued as a defect when it was really a missed need?", "takeaway": "Each cause has a different owner and a different fix, so classifying it correctly is the first step.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "User rejects a signed-off feature", "parts": [{"label": "Defect", "note": "The build does not match the requirement."}, {"label": "Ambiguity", "note": "The requirement was read differently."}, {"label": "Missed need", "note": "The requirement was incomplete."}, {"label": "Scope change", "note": "The need has moved since sign-off."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-020", "seq": 212, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The UAT that tested the system and missed the business", "explain": "User acceptance testing is planned for a EUR 110,000 payment release.\n\nThe test cases cover the system mechanics.\n\nThe business journeys are never tested, and acceptance fails on the first real journey.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Test cases are built from system mechanics\n→ Business journeys are never mapped\n→ Acceptance criteria are never defined per journey\n→ Roles that must perform each journey are never identified\n→ Exception paths are never covered\n→ Tests pass, and the business says no\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Test the system.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUAT coverage is derived from the business journeys, the acceptance criteria, the roles involved and the exception paths. Test cases built from this coverage test the business outcome rather than the system mechanics, which is what acceptance testing is for.\n\nTest the business outcome, not the system mechanics.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap each business journey, define the criteria that prove it works, name the roles that must perform it, and cover the exception paths.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Business journeys\n→ Acceptance criteria\n→ Roles and exception paths\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do the tests pass?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every business journey has a test?\"\n\nHave you seen UAT fail on a journey no test ever covered?", "takeaway": "Test cases built from this coverage test the business outcome rather than the system mechanics, which is what acceptance testing is for.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Map business journeys", "note": "Coverage starts from the business journeys."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define acceptance criteria", "note": "Criteria that prove each journey works."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Identify the roles", "note": "The roles that must be able to perform each journey."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Exception paths covered?", "note": "The exception paths that must be handled."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Build the test cases", "note": "Cases test the business outcome, not system mechanics."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-060", "seq": 213, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The defect debate that ran on opinion, not evidence", "explain": "A system integration testing defect is raised on a EUR 70,000 payment flow.\n\nThe discussion runs on opinion for an hour.\n\nNobody has checked whether the defect can even be reproduced.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Defect is raised in SIT\n→ Reproducibility is never assessed\n→ Traceability to the requirement is never checked\n→ Business impact is never assessed\n→ Fix validation and regression are never planned\n→ The debate runs on opinion\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The defect is obvious.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nReproducibility decides whether the defect is real, traceability shows which requirement it breaks, business impact decides its priority, fix validation confirms the fix works, and regression confirms nothing else broke.\n\nEvidence, not opinion, decides a defect.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAssess reproducibility, traceability, business impact, fix validation and regression for every defect, in that order.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Reproducibility\n→ Traceability and business impact\n→ Fix validation and regression\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Whose opinion is loudest?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this defect is real, and what it breaks?\"\n\nHave you sat in a defect debate that ran on opinion because nobody had checked the evidence?", "takeaway": "This assessment keeps defect discussions focused on evidence rather than on opinion.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Assess reproducibility", "note": "Reproducibility decides whether the defect is real."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check traceability", "note": "Traceability shows which requirement it breaks."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Business impact clear?", "note": "Business impact decides the priority."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate the fix", "note": "Fix validation confirms the fix works."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check regression", "note": "Regression confirms nothing else broke."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-061", "seq": 214, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Hypercare ended on a date, and the EUR 40,000 exceptions were still open", "explain": "A payment release goes live, and hypercare runs for two weeks.\n\nOn day fourteen, hypercare ends, because the calendar says so.\n\nEUR 40,000 of exceptions are still unresolved, and nobody is watching them.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Deployment goes live\n→ Critical journeys are monitored\n→ Exception rates are monitored\n→ Unresolved transactions are monitored\n→ Stakeholder updates go out\n→ Hypercare ends after a fixed number of days, while the measures are still unstable\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Hypercare runs for two weeks.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCritical journeys show whether customers can complete their payments, exception rates show whether the system is handling failures, and unresolved transactions show what still needs attention. Hypercare ends when these measures are stable, not when a fixed number of days has passed.\n\nHypercare ends when the measures are stable, not when the calendar runs out.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEnd hypercare on stability, not on a date. Keep monitoring the critical journeys, the exception rates and the unresolved transactions until they hold steady.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Critical journeys\n→ Exception rates\n→ Unresolved transactions\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How many days of hypercare?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the measures are stable enough to end hypercare?\"\n\nHave you seen hypercare end on a date while exceptions were still open?", "takeaway": "Hypercare ends when these measures are stable, not when a fixed number of days has passed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Monitor critical journeys", "note": "Show whether customers can complete payments."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Monitor exception rates", "note": "Show whether the system handles failures."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Monitor unresolved transactions", "note": "Show what still needs attention."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Send stakeholder updates", "note": "Keep everyone informed through hypercare."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Are the measures stable?", "note": "Hypercare ends when measures are stable, not after fixed days."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-051", "seq": 215, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The behaviour changed in production, and nobody knew which layer broke it", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 payment behaves differently in production than intended.\n\nThe team fixes the code.\n\nThe fault was in the requirement, so the fix changes nothing, and the same miss recurs.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Intended behaviour diverges from production behaviour\n→ Requirement layer is never checked\n→ Development layer is blamed by default\n→ Testing layer is never checked\n→ Environment layer is never checked\n→ Fix lands at the wrong layer\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Fix the code.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCheck the requirement first, then the development, then the testing, then the environment, and find the layer where the divergence was introduced. Locating the layer matters because it decides whether the fix is a requirement correction, a code change, a test improvement or an environment fix.\n\nFix at the layer that introduced the fault.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nUse a layered failure model to locate where it went wrong, and fix at that layer.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Requirement layer\n→ Development layer\n→ Testing and environment layers\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What is broken?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which layer introduced the divergence?\"\n\nHave you seen a fix land at the wrong layer because nobody traced the fault?", "takeaway": "Fix at the layer that introduced the fault.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Check the requirement layer", "note": "Find whether the divergence started in requirements."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Fault in development?", "note": "Find whether it started in development."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check the testing layer", "note": "Find whether it started in testing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check the environment layer", "note": "Find whether it started in the environment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fix at the layer found", "note": "The layer decides the fix and prevents recurrence."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-021", "seq": 216, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The dashboard that said healthy while customers failed", "explain": "A digital banking dashboard shows adoption climbing.\n\nThe dashboard says healthy.\n\nCustomers are failing to complete their payments, and the KPI set never measures it.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Adoption is measured, and it climbs\n→ Task completion is never measured\n→ Failure is never measured\n→ Service performance is never measured\n→ Risk is never measured\n→ Customer outcome is never measured\n→ The dashboard reports health while customers fail\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Adoption is the KPI.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nKey performance indicators should cover adoption, task completion, failure, service, risk and customer outcome measures. Adoption shows whether customers use the feature, task completion shows whether they can finish what they start, failure shows where they are blocked, service shows whether the system performs, risk shows whether controls are holding, and customer outcome shows whether the feature delivered value.\n\nA KPI set that covers only one of these gives a misleading picture of health.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCover all six measures, or the dashboard will report health while customers fail.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Adoption and task completion\n→ Failure and service\n→ Risk and customer outcome\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are customers using it?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the KPI set shows the whole picture?\"\n\nHave you seen a dashboard report healthy while customers were failing?", "takeaway": "A KPI set that covers only one of these gives a misleading picture of health.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "KPIs that show true digital banking health", "parts": [{"label": "Adoption", "note": "Whether customers use the feature."}, {"label": "Task completion", "note": "Whether customers finish what they start."}, {"label": "Failure", "note": "Where customers are blocked."}, {"label": "Service", "note": "Whether the system performs."}, {"label": "Risk", "note": "Whether controls are holding."}, {"label": "Customer outcome", "note": "Whether the feature delivered value."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-072", "seq": 217, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The payment page customers kept abandoning", "explain": "A merchant watches customers abandon the payment page every day.\n\nThe team treats it as one problem with one cause.\n\nIt is five problems wearing one label, and each needs a different fix.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Customers reach the payment page\n→ The funnel shows where they drop off\n→ Validation errors block them\n→ Latency makes them wait\n→ Payment options do not match what they want\n→ The experience they report is poor\n→ Each cause points to a different fix\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Abandonment is one problem.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCustomers abandon a payment page for reasons that can be found in the evidence: the funnel, the validation errors, the latency, the payment options and the user experience. The analysis must separate them rather than treating abandonment as a single problem with a single cause.\n\nAbandonment is several problems wearing one label.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSeparate the five causes before choosing a fix, or the fix will treat the wrong problem.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Funnel drop-off\n→ Validation errors\n→ Latency\n→ Payment options and experience\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Why do customers abandon the page?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which of the five causes is driving the drop-off?\"\n\nHave you seen abandonment treated as one problem when the evidence showed five?", "takeaway": "Abandonment is several problems wearing one label.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Diagnosing payment page abandonment", "parts": [{"label": "Funnel drop-off", "note": "Where customers drop off."}, {"label": "Validation errors", "note": "The errors customers encounter."}, {"label": "Latency", "note": "The latency customers experience."}, {"label": "Payment options", "note": "The payment options available."}, {"label": "User experience", "note": "What customers report."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-073", "seq": 218, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The loan approval that took two days, and nobody knew where the time went", "explain": "Loan approval time rises from hours to days.\n\nEveryone has an opinion about where the delay is.\n\nNobody has measured it.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Approval time rises from hours to days\n→ The process is never mapped\n→ Queues where applications wait are never found\n→ Manual checks that add delay are never found\n→ Dependencies on other teams are never found\n→ Rework where applications are sent back is never found\n→ Opinions fill the gap where measurement should be\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Ask people where the delay is.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMap the process to find the bottleneck: look for queues, manual checks, dependencies and rework. The bottleneck is usually a queue or a manual step rather than the decision itself, so measuring where time is actually spent is more useful than asking people where they think the delay is.\n\nMeasure the clock, not the opinion.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMap the process, measure where time is actually spent, and fix the bottleneck the clock shows.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Queues\n→ Manual checks\n→ Dependencies and rework\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Where do people think the delay is?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where the time is actually spent?\"\n\nHave you seen a delay blamed on the decision, when the clock showed a queue?", "takeaway": "Measure the clock, not the opinion.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Map the process", "note": "Map the approval process to find the bottleneck."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find the queues", "note": "Look for queues where applications wait."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find manual checks", "note": "Look for manual checks that add delay."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find dependencies", "note": "Look for dependencies on other teams or systems."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is rework the bottleneck?", "note": "Look for applications sent back for correction."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-074", "seq": 219, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Every test passed, and UAT still failed", "explain": "All test cases pass.\n\nUAT still fails.\n\nThe suite is green, and the business need is unmet, because the tests never represented the real business journeys.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Test cases all pass\n→ Test design has gaps\n→ Test data does not represent real business use\n→ Expected outcomes are wrong\n→ The unmet business need was never covered by a test at all\n→ Acceptance fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"All tests pass, so we are ready.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPassing test cases do not prove the business need is met. Check the test design for gaps, the representativeness of the test data, the expected outcomes for correctness, and whether the unmet business need was ever covered by a test at all.\n\nPassing tests are not proof. Representation is.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCheck design, data, expected outcomes and coverage of the real business need before calling a suite ready.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Test design\n→ Test data\n→ Expected outcomes and coverage\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Do the tests pass?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the tests represent the real business journeys?\"\n\nHave you seen a green test suite fail acceptance because the tests never matched real use?", "takeaway": "A test suite that passes but does not represent the real business journeys gives false confidence and a failed acceptance.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Passing tests, failed acceptance", "parts": [{"label": "Test design gaps", "note": "Check the design for gaps."}, {"label": "Representativeness of test data", "note": "Check the data matches real business use."}, {"label": "Expected outcomes", "note": "Check the expected outcomes are correct."}, {"label": "Unmet business need", "note": "Check whether the need was ever covered by a test."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-075", "seq": 220, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The same change, requested every sprint", "explain": "The same change is requested every sprint.\n\nIt is implemented every sprint.\n\nIt keeps coming back, because the root cause was never fixed.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Same change is requested\n→ It is implemented again\n→ The unresolved root need is never found\n→ Weak discovery missed the real requirement\n→ Policy uncertainty leaves the team guessing\n→ No one owns the decision, so no one can say no\n→ The request returns next sprint\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Add it to the backlog again.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe root cause is usually unresolved: an unresolved root need that the change does not actually satisfy, weak discovery that missed the real requirement, policy uncertainty, or a lack of decision ownership. Fixing the root cause stops the repeated request; implementing the same change again will not.\n\nRepeat requests are a symptom, not a backlog.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStop re-implementing and find the root cause: the need, the discovery, the policy or the ownership.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Root need\n→ Discovery\n→ Policy and decision ownership\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What change is requested?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the root need behind the repeated request?\"\n\nHave you seen the same change requested sprint after sprint, because the root cause was never fixed?", "takeaway": "Repeat requests are a symptom, not a backlog.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Same change requested every sprint", "parts": [{"label": "Unresolved root need", "note": "The change does not actually satisfy the need."}, {"label": "Weak discovery", "note": "The real requirement was missed."}, {"label": "Policy uncertainty", "note": "Uncertainty leaves the team guessing."}, {"label": "Lack of decision ownership", "note": "No one can say no to the request."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-077", "seq": 221, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The rule the developers guessed, and the EUR 25,000 payment that proved them wrong", "explain": "A rule for a EUR 25,000 payment is stated as a business sentence.\n\nThe developer guesses the boundary.\n\nThe payment proves the guess wrong, and the rule has to be rewritten after the build.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Rule is stated as a business sentence\n→ Triggers that start the rule are never stated\n→ Conditions that apply are never stated\n→ Data involved is never stated\n→ Expected behaviour is never stated\n→ Failure outcomes are never stated\n→ Developer guesses, and the guess is wrong\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The business sentence is enough.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA rule stated as a business sentence leaves the developer guessing about the boundary, while a rule stated with triggers, conditions and failure outcomes can be built and tested without further questions.\n\nPrecision at the boundary is what makes a rule buildable.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nState triggers, conditions, data, expected behaviour and failure outcomes for every rule, so it can be built and tested without further questions.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Triggers and conditions\n→ Data involved\n→ Expected behaviour and failure outcomes\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does the rule say?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the developer can build this rule without asking again?\"\n\nHave you seen a rule guessed at the boundary, and the payment prove the guess wrong?", "takeaway": "Precision at the boundary is what makes a rule buildable.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Rules and edge cases developers can build from", "parts": [{"label": "Triggers", "note": "State what starts a rule."}, {"label": "Conditions", "note": "State the conditions that apply."}, {"label": "Data involved", "note": "State the data the rule uses."}, {"label": "Expected behaviour", "note": "State what should happen."}, {"label": "Failure outcomes", "note": "State what happens when the rule fails."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-078", "seq": 222, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The test that could not pass or fail", "explain": "A test is written for a EUR 30,000 payment scenario.\n\nIt has steps, but no expected result.\n\nIt runs, and nobody can say whether it passed or failed.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Business scenario is translated into a test\n→ Positive scenario is written\n→ Negative scenario is written\n→ Boundary scenario is written\n→ Regression scenario is written\n→ No expected result is stated for any of them\n→ The test cannot pass or fail\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Write the test steps.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPositive scenarios prove the happy path works, negative scenarios prove invalid input is handled, boundary scenarios prove the limits behave correctly, and regression scenarios prove existing behaviour still works. Each scenario needs an expected result, because a test without an expected result cannot pass or fail.\n\nA test without an expected result cannot pass or fail.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nGive every scenario an expected result, across positive, negative, boundary and regression coverage.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Positive and negative scenarios\n→ Boundary and regression scenarios\n→ Expected results\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Are the test steps written?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every scenario states what should happen?\"\n\nHave you seen a test run that nobody could call a pass or a fail?", "takeaway": "Each scenario needs an expected result, because a test without an expected result cannot pass or fail.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Business scenarios into QA coverage", "parts": [{"label": "Positive scenarios", "note": "Prove the happy path works."}, {"label": "Negative scenarios", "note": "Prove invalid input is handled."}, {"label": "Boundary scenarios", "note": "Prove the limits behave correctly."}, {"label": "Regression scenarios", "note": "Prove existing behaviour still works."}, {"label": "Expected results", "note": "Every scenario needs one, or it cannot pass or fail."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-091", "seq": 223, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Feature complete, and the business problem still standing", "explain": "A feature for a EUR 130,000 payment flow is delivered complete, on time.\n\nThe business problem is still there.\n\nThe team celebrates delivery, while the users keep working around the problem.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Feature is built as specified\n→ Hypothesis behind the feature is never revisited\n→ Actual user behaviour is never checked\n→ Process adoption is never checked\n→ Operational constraints are never checked\n→ Outcome measures are never checked\n→ Delivery is celebrated, and the problem remains\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Delivery is success.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe gap is usually that the feature was built as specified but the hypothesis was wrong, or that users did not adopt it, or that an operational constraint was missed. Measuring the outcome rather than the delivery reveals the gap.\n\nMeasuring the outcome rather than the delivery reveals the gap.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRevisit the hypothesis, the user behaviour, the adoption, the constraints and the outcome measures before calling a feature done.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Hypothesis\n→ User behaviour and adoption\n→ Constraints and outcome measures\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was the feature delivered?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the feature solved the problem it was built for?\"\n\nHave you delivered a feature complete, and watched the business problem stay exactly where it was?", "takeaway": "Measuring the outcome rather than the delivery reveals the gap.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Feature complete, problem remains", "parts": [{"label": "Hypothesis behind the feature", "note": "Revisit whether the hypothesis was right."}, {"label": "Actual user behaviour", "note": "Check what users really do."}, {"label": "Process adoption", "note": "Check whether users adopted the change."}, {"label": "Operational constraints", "note": "Check for a constraint that was missed."}, {"label": "Outcome measures", "note": "Measure the outcome rather than the delivery."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-092", "seq": 224, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Loan approval: two hours became two days", "explain": "Loan approval time grows from two hours to two days.\n\nThe team wants to change something, anything.\n\nNobody knows which of the five causes is actually responsible.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Approval time grows from two hours to two days\n→ Queue time is never analysed\n→ Manual reviews are never analysed\n→ Document quality is never analysed\n→ Decision rules are never analysed\n→ Hand-offs are never analysed\n→ A change is made blind\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Change something, anything.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nQueue time shows where applications wait, manual reviews show where human effort adds delay, document quality shows where rework is created, decision rules show where the logic is slow, and hand-offs show where work is lost between teams. The fix depends on which of these is actually causing the delay.\n\nMeasure before changing anything.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAnalyse queue time, manual reviews, document quality, decision rules and hand-offs, then fix the cause the measurement shows.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Queue time and manual reviews\n→ Document quality\n→ Decision rules and hand-offs\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What should we change?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which of the five is causing the delay?\"\n\nHave you seen a fix applied blind, when measurement would have shown a different cause?", "takeaway": "The fix depends on which of these is actually causing the delay, so measure before changing anything.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Analyse queue time", "note": "Queue time shows where applications wait."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Analyse manual reviews", "note": "Manual reviews show where human effort adds delay."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Analyse document quality", "note": "Document quality shows where rework is created."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Analyse decision rules", "note": "Decision rules show where the logic is slow."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which of the five is the cause?", "note": "The fix depends on which of these is actually causing the delay."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Analyse hand-offs", "note": "Hand-offs show where work is lost between teams."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-093", "seq": 225, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The change request that came back, again and again", "explain": "Stakeholders repeat the same change request, release after release.\n\nIt is implemented, again and again.\n\nThe cycle repeats, because the cause is never fixed.\n\nHere is how it happens:\n\n→ Same change request is repeated\n→ It is implemented again\n→ Root need was never satisfied\n→ Decision took so long the requester assumed it was ignored\n→ Discovery missed the real requirement\n→ Feedback loop never closed with the requester\n→ The request comes back\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Implement it again.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe root need may never have been satisfied, the decision may have taken so long that the requester assumed it was ignored, the discovery may have missed the real requirement, or the feedback loop may never have closed the loop with the requester. Each cause has a different fix.\n\nImplementing the change again without fixing the cause will only repeat the cycle.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nInvestigate the root need, the decision latency, the incomplete discovery and the feedback loops, and fix the cause, not the request.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Root need\n→ Decision latency\n→ Discovery and feedback loops\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What change is requested?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the loop with the requester actually closed?\"\n\nHave you seen the same request come back because the loop with the requester never closed?", "takeaway": "Each cause has a different fix, and implementing the change again without fixing the cause will only repeat the cycle.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Stakeholders repeat the same request", "parts": [{"label": "Root need", "note": "It may never have been satisfied."}, {"label": "Decision latency", "note": "A slow decision can look like being ignored."}, {"label": "Incomplete discovery", "note": "The real requirement may have been missed."}, {"label": "Feedback loops", "note": "The loop with the requester may never have closed."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-094", "seq": 226, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 50,000 supplier payment stalled, and no one owned the fix", "explain": "A EUR 50,000 supplier payment sat unreleased for three days.\nEvery team said it was someone else's job.\nThe BA owned the outcome, but no team reported to the BA.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ pacs.008 held for a missing approval\n→ Operations waited for Compliance\n→ Compliance waited for the business\n→ No one chased the decision, so nothing moved\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"The BA owns delivery.\"\nBut ownership came with no authority over any team, no decision record and no date.\n\nWhy?\n\nOwnership without authority fails when the ask is vague. Each team assumed another team was acting, and with no recorded decision there was nothing to follow up on and no one to escalate to.\n\nAuthority is borrowed. Clarity is owned.\n\nWhat proves a blocked payment is actually moving again?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nState what is needed and by when, in writing. Record every decision so accountability is visible. Follow up on actions instead of assuming they will happen. Escalate transparently the moment a decision or a delivery is blocked.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What is needed\n→ Who decides it\n→ By when\n→ What happens if it stalls\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"When will this be fixed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment is moving again?\"\n\nHave you ever owned a payment outcome that no team reported to you on?", "takeaway": "Authority is borrowed. Clarity is owned.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Owning a EUR 50,000 outcome without authority", "parts": [{"label": "State what is needed and by when", "note": "Drive clarity on the ask and the date."}, {"label": "Decision records", "note": "Keep accountability visible."}, {"label": "Follow up on actions", "note": "Do not assume actions will happen."}, {"label": "Escalate transparently", "note": "Escalate when a decision or delivery is blocked."}, {"label": "Evidence the payment moved", "note": "A status change, not a promise, proves progress."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-056", "seq": 227, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The client said our new process broke their EUR 120,000 payroll run", "explain": "A client's EUR 120,000 payroll run failed on the first day of the new process.\nFour hundred salaries were held.\nThe client said the new process had damaged their operations.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ New release went live on Monday\n→ Client payroll file failed validation\n→ EUR 120,000 in salaries held\n→ Support blamed the client file before checking the change\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The client must be doing something wrong.\"\nNobody checked what the release had changed first.\n\nWhy?\n\nA changed validation rule rejected a field the old process had accepted. The file was fine. The process had changed underneath it, and the team defended the release instead of reading the evidence.\n\nEmpathy first, evidence second, blame never.\n\nWhat proves the new process, and not the client file, caused the failure?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAcknowledge the impact first. That does not admit fault, it shows the client is taken seriously. Provide a safe workaround so the client can keep operating. Agree the recovery communications. Then investigate what actually changed.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Impact acknowledged\n→ Safe workaround live\n→ Recovery message agreed\n→ Root cause found\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Whose fault is it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what actually changed?\"\n\nHave you ever had a client prove the new process was the problem?", "takeaway": "Empathy first, evidence second, blame never.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Client says the process damaged operations", "parts": [{"label": "Acknowledge the impact first", "note": "Shows the client is taken seriously."}, {"label": "Safe workaround", "note": "Lets the client keep operating."}, {"label": "Recovery communications", "note": "Agreed with the client."}, {"label": "Investigate what changed", "note": "Find the real cause without becoming adversarial."}, {"label": "EUR 120,000 payroll held", "note": "The scale of the impact sets the urgency."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-068", "seq": 228, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "EUR 2 million in payments, and no one could prove where they were", "explain": "Volumes tripled in an hour during a payment surge.\nEUR 2 million of payments were in flight.\nTimeouts rose, and no one could prove which payments had settled and which had not.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Volumes tripled in one hour\n→ Timeout rates climbed across the gateway\n→ Gateway responses mixed success and failure\n→ Reconciliation showed gaps nobody could explain\n\nThe instruction was:\n\"Just restart it and see.\"\nRestarting without evidence would have risked paying twice or losing payments entirely.\n\nWhy?\n\nWithout transaction IDs, gateway responses, API logs, timestamps and reconciliation status, the team was guessing. Each piece of evidence answers a different question, and only together do they show where the money is.\n\nWithout evidence, incident response is guesswork.\n\nWhat proves a payment in the surge settled, failed, or is still stuck?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCollect transaction IDs to link evidence to individual payments. Read gateway responses and API logs to see what each system did. Watch timeout rates to see where the system is struggling. Use timestamps to fix when it happened. Check reconciliation status to see whether the money is accounted for.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Transaction IDs\n→ Gateway responses and API logs\n→ Timeout rates and timestamps\n→ Reconciliation status\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the system back up?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every payment in the surge is accounted for?\"\n\nHave you ever had to prove where the money was in the middle of a surge?", "takeaway": "Without evidence, incident response is guesswork.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Evidence to collect during a EUR 2 million payment surge", "parts": [{"label": "Transaction IDs", "note": "Link evidence to individual payments."}, {"label": "Gateway responses", "note": "Show what the gateway did."}, {"label": "API logs", "note": "Show what each system did."}, {"label": "Timeout rates", "note": "Show where the system is struggling."}, {"label": "Timestamps", "note": "Show when it happened."}, {"label": "Reconciliation status", "note": "Shows whether money is accounted for."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-063", "seq": 229, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Five teams, one EUR 500,000 outage, five different stories", "explain": "A EUR 500,000 payment run stopped mid-flight.\nBusiness, development, QA, gateway and support each opened their own ticket.\nEach team worked from a different picture of the same outage.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment run stopped mid-flight\n→ Five teams opened five separate tickets\n→ Two teams chased the same gateway error\n→ No team owned the customer message\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Each team will sort its own part.\"\nNobody owned the whole incident, so work overlapped and the customer heard nothing.\n\nWhy?\n\nWithout one incident view, teams duplicate effort and the incident lasts longer than it should. Clear owners stop work falling between teams, and a regular update cadence keeps everyone aligned.\n\nWithout one incident view, teams duplicate effort and the incident lasts longer than it should.\n\nWhat proves the incident is under control, not just busy?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild one incident view first. Name a clear owner for each workstream. Ask for the evidence each team needs to collect. Set an update cadence and keep to it until the money is accounted for.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ One incident view\n→ One owner per workstream\n→ Evidence requests\n→ Update cadence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is every team working on it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the incident is under control?\"\n\nHave you ever watched five teams chase the same outage from five different pictures?", "takeaway": "Without one incident view, teams duplicate effort and the incident lasts longer than it should.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Coordinating five teams during a EUR 500,000 outage", "parts": [{"label": "One incident view", "note": "Prevents teams working from different pictures."}, {"label": "Clear owners", "note": "Prevents work falling between teams."}, {"label": "Evidence requests", "note": "Ensure the right data is collected."}, {"label": "Update cadence", "note": "Keeps everyone aligned through the incident."}, {"label": "One customer message", "note": "The customer hears one story, not five."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "duplicate payment", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-097", "seq": 230, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Five certificates, but no proof of one real payment project", "explain": "A BA showed five certificates for a EUR 250,000 payments migration.\nThe panel asked for one artefact from the work.\nThere was nothing to show.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Certificates listed on the CV\n→ Panel asked for the problem statement\n→ No process map, no user story, no API contract\n→ No UAT evidence that anything was delivered\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Theory proves capability.\"\nTheory shows what was studied. It does not show what was actually done.\n\nWhy?\n\nA portfolio built only from courses has no chain from problem to delivered outcome. A reviewer cannot see the scope, the decisions or the result, so the certificates carry no weight.\n\nShow the chain, not the certificate.\n\nWhat proves this BA delivered the work, not just studied it?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild the portfolio from real work: a problem statement, process maps, user stories, an API contract and UAT evidence. Each piece shows one link in the chain from problem to delivered outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Problem statement\n→ Process maps\n→ User stories\n→ API contract\n→ UAT evidence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What did you study?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves you delivered the outcome?\"\n\nHave you ever had to prove a project with artefacts instead of certificates?", "takeaway": "Show the chain, not the certificate.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A practical BA portfolio, not only theory", "parts": [{"label": "Problem statement", "note": "States the problem the work solved."}, {"label": "Process maps", "note": "Show the process as it was mapped."}, {"label": "User stories", "note": "Show the needs as stories."}, {"label": "API contract", "note": "Shows the integration as defined."}, {"label": "UAT evidence", "note": "Shows the outcome was tested and accepted."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch29-capstone-project.html", "label": "Chapter 29: Capstone Project", "chapter": "ch29", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-084", "seq": 231, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The interview answer listed tasks, but the EUR 1 million result was missing", "explain": "A BA walked through a EUR 1 million payments project for ten minutes.\nThe panel heard tasks, tools and meetings.\nThey never heard what the project delivered.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Project described as a list of tasks\n→ Role and decisions never stated\n→ Systems named, risks never mentioned\n→ No measurable result at the end\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Listing what I did shows my value.\"\nA list of tasks shows time occupied. It does not prove value delivered.\n\nWhy?\n\nMeasurable results matter most, because they prove the work delivered value rather than simply occupying time. Without the result, the panel cannot tell whether the project succeeded.\n\nMeasurable results matter most, because they prove the work delivered value rather than simply occupying time.\n\nWhat proves this project delivered value?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nStructure the walkthrough around the problem, the role played, the decisions made, the systems involved, the risks managed and the measurable result. That structure shows scope and contribution, not just activity.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Problem\n→ Role played\n→ Decisions made\n→ Systems involved\n→ Risks managed\n→ Measurable result\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What did you do on the project?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the project delivered value?\"\n\nHave you ever finished an interview answer and realised you never gave the result?", "takeaway": "Measurable results matter most, because they prove the work delivered value rather than simply occupying time.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Presenting a EUR 1 million project with evidence", "parts": [{"label": "Problem", "note": "Structure the walkthrough around the problem."}, {"label": "Role played", "note": "Show the part played in the work."}, {"label": "Decisions made", "note": "Show the judgement exercised."}, {"label": "Systems involved", "note": "Show the technical landscape."}, {"label": "Risks managed", "note": "Show the risks handled."}, {"label": "Measurable result", "note": "Proves the work delivered value."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-100", "seq": 232, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "Seven questions in, the EUR 750,000 project walkthrough still had no shape", "explain": "A BA started walking through a EUR 750,000 live payments project.\nSeven questions in, the panel still did not know the scope, the stakeholders or the outcome.\nThe walkthrough had become a list of tasks.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Context never set\n→ Scope never stated\n→ Stakeholders never named\n→ Deliverables and outcomes never reached\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Start at the beginning and the story will shape itself.\"\nWithout a structure, a walkthrough drifts, and the listener fills the gaps with guesses.\n\nWhy?\n\nContext sets the scene, scope shows the size of the work, stakeholders show who was involved, systems show the technical landscape, deliverables show what was produced, decisions show judgement, and outcomes show the result. Miss any link and the story has a hole.\n\nA walkthrough without structure becomes a list of tasks.\n\nWhat proves the listener understood the project, not just heard about it?\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWalk through context, scope, stakeholders, systems, deliverables, decisions and outcomes, in that order. The structure keeps the walkthrough focused and complete.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Context\n→ Scope\n→ Stakeholders\n→ Systems\n→ Deliverables\n→ Decisions\n→ Outcomes\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What have you been working on lately?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the project was understood?\"\n\nHave you ever lost a panel halfway through a project walkthrough?", "takeaway": "A walkthrough without structure becomes a list of tasks.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Walking through a EUR 750,000 live project", "parts": [{"label": "Context", "note": "Sets the scene."}, {"label": "Scope", "note": "Shows the size of the work."}, {"label": "Stakeholders", "note": "Shows who was involved."}, {"label": "Systems", "note": "Shows the technical landscape."}, {"label": "Deliverables", "note": "Shows what was produced."}, {"label": "Decisions and outcomes", "note": "Decisions show judgement. Outcomes show the result."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch29-capstone-project.html", "label": "Chapter 29: Capstone Project", "chapter": "ch29", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A payment of EUR 30,000 timed out.\nThe team blamed the network and the receiving bank.\nThe fault was made at the very first step, when the message was created.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pain.001 created with a small deviation from the payment message standard\n→ pacs.008 sent downstream\n→ Each hop passes the deviation on\n→ Receiving system cannot process it, pacs.002 reports a timeout\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Validate the message at entry, then trust it downstream.\"\n\nSo the message passed entry checks, and the deviation compounded at every hop until the payment timed out.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe most visible error is rarely the root cause. A payment timeout is often a downstream effect of something earlier. A small deviation from the standard compounds hop by hop, and by the time the timeout appears, the trail back to the creation step has gone cold unless timestamps and message copies were kept.\n\nThe symptom is downstream. The cause is upstream.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCreation, each hop and the final timeout are separate evidence points, so keep them separate:\n\n→ Message creation and standards validation\n→ Hop by hop trace with timestamps and message copies\n→ Timeout symptom, traced back to its origin\n\nValidate messages against the standard at every boundary, not just at entry.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Where did it time out?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the message was valid at every boundary it crossed?\"\n\nHave you ever traced a timeout back to a tiny error made when the message was created?", "id": "SCN-28", "level": "core", "module": "Core", "seq": 353, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "The symptom is downstream. The cause is upstream.", "title": "EUR 30,000 payment timed out, the fault was made at the very first step", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Small deviation from the payment message standard", "title": "Message created with a deviation"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Timestamps and message copies, hop by hop", "title": "Trace hop by hop"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Check every boundary, not just entry", "title": "Valid at every boundary?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Deviation compounds until the payment times out", "title": "Timeout downstream"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Correct the creation step so it cannot recur", "title": "Fix at the source"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D04 Messaging and standards", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch5-swift-mt-messages.html", "label": "Chapter 5: SWIFT MT Messages", "chapter": "ch5", "fallback": true}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F08", "seq": 361, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 1,500 collection was rejected. The team resubmitted it, and it was rejected again.", "explain": "A EUR 1,500 collection is rejected.\nThe team resubmits it at once, with no timing rule.\nIt is rejected again, because the resubmission was never tied to the original timing.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 1,500 collection rejected\n→ Team resubmits with no timing rule\n→ Resubmission rejected again\n→ No one can say when resubmission should happen\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A rejected collection can be resubmitted whenever the team is ready.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a collection is rejected, the originator can resubmit it only once the resubmission timing rule is clear, so before asking for the rule, the BA should ask what the rule must answer: how it aligns the original timing so the resubmission improves on the original outcome. The method compares the clean-state timeline of a complete collection with the rejection reason and the proposed resubmission timeline, and tests if the earliest resubmission point is tied to the original collection date. Once the rule is clear it goes into the backlog so the feature can be built quickly, and the BA should first see how the business operation works in practice, face to face.\n\nDefine the resubmission timing rule first, then resubmit.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCompare the clean-state timeline with the rejection reason, tie the earliest resubmission point to the original date, and write the rule down before building.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Rejection reason\n→ Original collection date\n→ Resubmission timing rule\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this resubmission improves on the original outcome?\"\n\nHave you resubmitted a collection that was doomed to fail again?", "takeaway": "Define the resubmission timing rule first, then resubmit.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Collection rejected", "note": "The originator wants to resubmit to the debtor"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "What must the timing rule answer?", "note": "Ask first what the rule needs to tell the business"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare timelines and the rejection reason", "note": "Clean-state timeline against the proposed resubmission timeline"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the earliest point depend on the original date?", "note": "Test whether resubmission timing is tied to the original date"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Put the clear rule in the backlog", "note": "A clear timing rule lets the feature be built quickly"}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-F06", "seq": 362, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 45,000 payment came back. The pacs.004 said returned, but nobody knew why.", "explain": "A EUR 45,000 payment is returned by pacs.004.\nThe team knows it was returned, but not why it failed.\nThe investigation starts from the return alone, and steers the technical team to the wrong process.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 45,000 payment returned by pacs.004\n→ Return reason unknown\n→ Investigation starts from the return alone\n→ Technical team sent to the wrong process\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A pacs.004 tells you why a payment failed.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA pacs.004 return tells a bank that a payment was returned, but on its own it does not say why it failed, while the pacs.002 carries the return reason code, and only when the two are reconciled does the bank know whether to flag, retry, investigate, replace or reject the payment. Gap analysis compares the instruction against its structure, the investigation against its evidence, the business rules against their procedure, and the return reason against its expected outcome. It measures the delta between the process the bank has and the process it needs, because a faulty gap analysis steers the technical team toward the wrong process.\n\nRead the reason code, then investigate. Never the other way round.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nReconcile the pacs.004 with the pacs.002 reason code before deciding what to do, and measure the gap between the process you have and the one you need.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ pacs.004 return\n→ pacs.002 reason code\n→ Gap analysis\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves why this payment failed, not just that it returned?\"\n\nHave you investigated a return without ever reading its reason code?", "takeaway": "Read the reason code, then investigate. Never the other way round.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reason code plus investigation", "parts": [{"label": "Instruction vs structure", "note": "Compare the payment instruction with its structure"}, {"label": "Investigation vs evidence", "note": "Compare the investigation process with its evidence"}, {"label": "Rules vs procedure", "note": "Compare business rules with their procedure"}, {"label": "Reason vs outcome", "note": "Compare the return reason with the expected outcome"}, {"label": "pacs.002 plus pacs.004", "note": "Reconcile both messages to learn why the payment failed"}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["pacs.004", "pacs.002"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F01", "seq": 363, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 25,000 payment was rejected for a format fault. Reject or repair? The team guessed.", "explain": "A EUR 25,000 payment is rejected for a format fault.\nThe team rejects it again without checking where the fault sits.\nThe fault was in the bank's own pacs.008, and a mapping fix would have saved the payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 25,000 payment rejected for a format fault\n→ Fault location never checked\n→ Payment rejected outright\n→ Fault was in the bank's own pacs.008\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"A format fault always means rejection.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payment rejected for a format fault is not always a rejection, because the right response depends on where the fault sits, whether the original instruction is intact, who owns the defect, and whether the agreement permits a correction. When the bank's own pacs.008 is wrong, fix the mapping and repair and resubmit after status and duplicate checks, and when the customer pain.001 is wrong and the agreement allows correction, repair within the agreement and keep the audit trail, while a customer pain.001 fault with no agreement gets a rejection with a clear reason. A format fault is a diagnosis to make first, not a verdict, and the business context decides whether the payment is repaired or rejected.\n\nA format fault is a diagnosis, not a verdict.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nLocate the fault, check the instruction is intact, check the agreement, then repair or reject with a reason.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Fault location\n→ Instruction intact\n→ Agreement permission\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves where this fault sits, and whether correction is allowed?\"\n\nHave you rejected a payment your own mapping had broken?", "takeaway": "A format fault is a diagnosis, not a verdict.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Payment rejected for format fault", "note": "A format fault does not decide reject or repair on its own"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Fault in customer pain.001 or own pacs.008?", "note": "Locate the fault and check the instruction is intact"}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the agreement permit correction?", "note": "Repair only where the agreement allows it"}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Repair and resubmit, or reject clearly", "note": "Fix own mapping defects, repair within the agreement, reject with a reason when correction is not allowed"}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pain.001", "pacs.008"], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch60-pain-001-and-pain-002-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 60: pain.001 and pain.002 in Depth", "chapter": "ch60", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "JEB-E01", "seq": 374, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The EUR 100K limit was removed. A EUR 200,000 payment went to TARGET2 anyway.", "explain": "The EU Instant Payments Regulation removes the EUR 100K scheme maximum for SCT Inst.\nA customer with a EUR 300K bank limit sends EUR 200,000 instantly.\nThe old routing rule still sends anything above EUR 100K to TARGET2, and the payment takes the slow road.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ EUR 100K scheme limit removed\n→ Customer given a EUR 300K instant limit\n→ EUR 200,000 sent instantly\n→ Old routing rule sends it to TARGET2\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Removing a scheme limit automatically updates the routing rules.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen the EU Instant Payments Regulation removed the EUR 100K scheme-level maximum for SCT Inst, the old routing rule that sent payments above EUR 100K to TARGET2 lost the scheme limit it was built on. A bank can now set its own customer limits, so Customer A with a EUR 300K limit can send EUR 200K instantly while the unchanged routing rule still sends anything above EUR 100K to TARGET2, which means two live rules produce different outcomes for the same payment. The BA must surface the conflict, trace why each rule exists, and take the choice to the Product Owner or Business Owner, because only the business can decide which outcome it wants once the scheme constraint is gone.\n\nTwo live rules, one payment, two different outcomes.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSurface every rule conflict the moment a scheme limit changes, trace why each rule exists, and let the business choose the outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme limit\n→ Customer limit\n→ Routing rule\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves which rule should win for this payment?\"\n\nHave you found two live rules fighting over the same payment?", "takeaway": "Two live rules, one payment, two different outcomes.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Scheme limit removed", "note": "EU Instant Payments Regulation removes the EUR 100K SCT Inst maximum."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Customer limit set", "note": "Customer A is given a EUR 300K instant payment limit by the bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Which rule wins for EUR 200K?", "note": "Customer limit allows SCT Instant, routing rule sends it to TARGET2."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "BA surfaces the conflict", "note": "Trace why each rule exists and what changed since it was written."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Decision-maker decides", "note": "Product Owner or Business Owner chooses the intended business behaviour."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch91-uk-payment-systems-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 91: UK payment systems in depth", "chapter": "ch91", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-E03", "seq": 375, "module": "Core", "level": "core", "source": "Public post", "title": "The platform handled today easily. Then five new clients arrived, and it fell over at 9am.", "explain": "Payment traffic peaks between 7am and 10am, and today the platform copes.\nFive clients in the pipeline could add 10 million transactions a month.\nNobody asked when they onboard, what their peaks look like, or whether the workload itself changes.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Monthly volume recorded\n→ Peak between 7am and 10am never tested\n→ Five clients could add 10 million transactions a month\n→ Platform fails at the morning peak\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Monthly volume defines what a platform must support.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nMonthly volume describes scale, but peak behaviour describes performance demand, so a BA who only records today's volumes has not defined what the platform must support. With payment traffic peaking between 7 AM and 10 AM and five clients in the pipeline who could add 10 million transactions a month, the BA must establish when they onboard, how certain the forecast is, what their peaks look like, whether they create new bursts, and whether the workload itself changes with more validations, API calls, real-time processing, and concurrency. The answer is a six-part view of current demand, peak behaviour, future demand, planning horizon, workload characteristics, and measurable requirements that cover response time, processing conditions, volume, and peak TPS, because a platform that performs today can still fail tomorrow if growth was never considered.\n\nPeak behaviour defines performance demand, not monthly volume.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine performance from current demand, peak behaviour, future demand, planning horizon, workload and measurable requirements, and test it at the peak.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Current demand\n→ Peak behaviour\n→ Future demand\n→ Measurable requirements\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we act?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this platform holds at the morning peak with the new clients on board?\"\n\nHave you sized a platform on monthly volume and watched it fail at 9am?", "takeaway": "Peak behaviour defines performance demand, not monthly volume.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Performance is a business discussion", "parts": [{"label": "Current demand", "note": "How many transactions today, and when does traffic peak."}, {"label": "Peak behaviour", "note": "Peak concentration defines the performance demand, not monthly volume."}, {"label": "Future demand", "note": "Five clients in the pipeline could add 10 million transactions a month."}, {"label": "Workload change", "note": "More validations, API calls, real-time processing, and concurrency."}, {"label": "Measurable requirements", "note": "Volume, peak TPS, response time, and processing conditions, tested."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-E07", "seq": 378, "module": "Core", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 250,000 payment went to the wrong intermediary, and the engine looked guilty", "explain": "A EUR 250,000 payment reached the wrong intermediary bank.\n\nEveryone blamed the payment engine.\n\nThe engine was innocent. The fault sat in the data it was given.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Instruction received for EUR 250,000\n→ Routing decision made by the engine\n→ Settlement directory checked, but it was incorrectly updated\n→ SWIFT reference data for the correspondent bank was never updated\n→ Payment routed to the wrong intermediary\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If the routing is wrong, the engine is at fault.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA correct routing rule still produces the wrong intermediary when it reads bad data. The settlement directory and the reference data decide the outcome just as much as the rule does.\n\nCorrect logic on wrong data still routes wrong.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTrace the chain before blaming the engine: instruction, routing decision, settlement directory, reference data, intermediary selection, routing. Establish what was expected, what actually happened, where they diverged, which component decided, and what data it used.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Routing logic\n→ Settlement directory data\n→ Reference data\n→ The component that made the decision\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which team is at fault?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the decision went wrong?\"\n\nHave you seen a team blamed for a fault that lived in the data?", "takeaway": "Correct logic on wrong data still routes wrong.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Symptom: wrong intermediary", "note": "A EUR 250,000 payment reaches the wrong intermediary bank."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Trace the routing chain", "note": "Instruction, routing decision, settlement directory, reference data, selection, routing."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Logic wrong or data wrong?", "note": "Check whether the rule, the directory, or the reference data is at fault."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Fix the root cause", "note": "Correct the data or the logic, then apply the corrective action."}]}}, "level": "core", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "wrong routing", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "ABH-31", "seq": 404, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 500,000 payment routed wrong after the person who knew the nostro was replaced", "explain": "A bank replaced its most experienced payments analyst with an AI execution tool.\n\nThe tool processed payments faster than the analyst ever had.\n\nThen a EUR 500,000 payment routed to the wrong nostro, because no one left knew why that nostro arrangement existed.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Experienced analyst leaves, AI tool takes over execution\n→ Tool scales execution, payments move faster\n→ A EUR 500,000 payment needs the old nostro arrangement\n→ No one left knows the business, the customer, or the history behind it\n→ Payment routes wrong, and the context to fix it is gone\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"AI scales execution, so it can replace the person.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAI scales execution, but human expertise gives it context. Replacing a person removes the context they carry about the business, the customer and the history, and that context is not easily rebuilt.\n\nYou can replace a person, not their context.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore replacing a role, capture the context it holds: why each nostro exists, which customers depend on it, and what history shaped it. Execution speed cannot replace that knowledge.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Execution, which AI scales\n→ Context, which a person carries\n→ The nostro arrangements and why they exist\n→ The handover, before the person leaves\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the tool do the work faster?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the context survived the handover?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail because the person who knew the history had left?", "takeaway": "You can replace a person, not their context.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "AI and human expertise", "parts": [{"label": "AI execution", "note": "Scales how fast work gets done."}, {"label": "Human context", "note": "Business, customer and history knowledge."}, {"label": "EUR 500,000 routed wrong", "note": "The nostro arrangement behind the payment was never captured."}, {"label": "Replacing a person", "note": "Removes the context they carry."}, {"label": "Rebuilding context", "note": "Not easily rebuilt once removed."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "ABH-32", "seq": 405, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A payments team was cut before a migration, and a EUR 300,000 payment stalled with no one left who knew the old flow", "explain": "A bank cut its payments operations team ahead of a major migration, to save headcount.\n\nThe migration started on schedule.\n\nThen a EUR 300,000 payment stalled in the old settlement flow, and no one left in the team knew how that flow worked.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Headcount cut in payments operations\n→ Migration starts, old flow still live for some payments\n→ EUR 300,000 payment stalls in the legacy settlement flow\n→ No one left knows the old flow well enough to fix it\n→ Payment waits while the team relearns its own system\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The AI shift is a headcount problem.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nThe AI shift is a skills problem as much as a headcount problem. Cutting the people who know the legacy flow does not remove the legacy flow. It removes the only people who can keep it running while the new one is built.\n\nCutting the team did not cut the work.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore any cut, map the skills the migration still needs, including the legacy flow, and reskill before removing. A migration needs the old knowledge until the old flow is gone.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The headcount decision\n→ The skills the migration still needs\n→ The legacy flow, still live\n→ The reskilling plan, before the cut\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many roles can we remove?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the skills to run the old flow survive the change?\"\n\nHave you seen a team cut before the system it ran was retired?", "takeaway": "Cutting the team did not cut the work.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Reskill for the AI shift", "parts": [{"label": "Headcount cut", "note": "Payments operations team cut before the migration."}, {"label": "EUR 300,000 stalled", "note": "A payment stalled in the legacy settlement flow."}, {"label": "No one left who knows the old flow", "note": "The knowledge left with the team."}, {"label": "Skills problem", "note": "The AI shift is as much skills as headcount."}, {"label": "Reskill first", "note": "Reskill before removing, not after."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D15 Advanced and emerging payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-33", "seq": 406, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A visa programme was suspended overnight, and a payments project lost its team", "explain": "A payments migration project depended on a team of specialists working abroad on a sponsored visa route.\n\nOne morning the sponsoring programme was suspended for the major employers.\n\nThe project did not stop, but its team plan collapsed overnight.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Project staffed with specialists on a sponsored work route\n→ Programme suspended for major tech and IT employers\n→ Specialists face an uncertain future, some must leave\n→ Project milestones slip while replacements are found\n→ Career plans of the whole team are reshaped in a day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Immigration policy is someone else's problem.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn immigration policy change can reshape career plans overnight, and a project that depends on sponsored staff carries that policy as a project risk. The team plan assumed the route would stay open. It did not.\n\nA policy change is a project risk.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a project depends on staff whose right to work rests on one programme, name that dependency in the risk log, with a backup staffing plan. A single programme is a single point of failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The project plan\n→ The staffing route it depends on\n→ The policy risk on that route\n→ The backup staffing plan\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is the team staffed today?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the team can stay staffed if the route closes?\"\n\nHave you seen a project lose its team to a policy change overnight?", "takeaway": "A policy change is a project risk.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Programme suspension", "parts": [{"label": "Major tech and IT employers", "note": "Suspended from the sponsored programme."}, {"label": "Specialists affected", "note": "Career plans reshaped overnight."}, {"label": "Project milestones slip", "note": "The migration loses its team while replacements are found."}, {"label": "Immigration policy change", "note": "Can reshape career plans overnight."}, {"label": "Single point of failure", "note": "One programme, one staffing route, no backup plan."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-34", "seq": 407, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A programme was suspended, and the whole team assumed their visas were cancelled too", "explain": "A sponsored visa programme was suspended for major employers.\n\nWithin hours, the payments team assumed every visa tied to it was cancelled.\n\nPeople started resigning, and the project lost staff it never needed to lose.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Programme suspended for major employers\n→ Team hears the news and assumes all visas are cancelled\n→ Panic spreads, people plan to leave\n→ The two programmes are separate, a suspension in one does not end the other\n→ Staff who could have stayed are already gone\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One programme suspended means every visa ends.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA programme suspension does not automatically cancel the visas issued under a separate programme. The advice is not to panic but to plan proactively, because the two programmes are separate and a suspension in one does not end the other. The damage came from the assumption, not the suspension.\n\nPanic cost more staff than the policy did.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a policy change hits, check which programme it actually touches before acting. Name the affected programme, the unaffected one, and the evidence for each, then plan from the facts.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The suspended programme\n→ The separate visa programme\n→ What each one actually covers\n→ The plan, built on facts not rumour\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What did the headline say?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which programme this change touches?\"\n\nHave you seen a team lose people to a rumour about a policy change?", "takeaway": "Panic cost more staff than the policy did.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Programme suspension and visas", "parts": [{"label": "Programme suspension", "note": "Does not automatically cancel visas under a separate programme."}, {"label": "Visas under a separate programme", "note": "A separate programme from the suspended one."}, {"label": "Panic spread", "note": "People planned to leave before the facts were checked."}, {"label": "No panic", "note": "Plan proactively instead."}, {"label": "Two separate programmes", "note": "A suspension in one does not end the other."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"id": "ABH-35", "seq": 408, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A new BA owned onboarding, fees, limits and payments, and a EUR 120,000 payment had no owner", "explain": "A new corporate banking BA joined a team covering onboarding, entitlements, fees, limits and payments.\n\nSix weeks in, a EUR 120,000 corporate payment failed.\n\nEveryone assumed the BA owned it. No one had said so.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ BA joins, scope listed as onboarding, entitlements, fees, limits and payments\n→ No single owner named for the payments leg\n→ EUR 120,000 corporate payment fails\n→ Each workstream points at the next one\n→ Payment waits while ownership is argued\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Listing a scope means someone owns it.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA scope list is not an ownership map. Onboarding, entitlements, fees, limits and payments are five workstreams, and a payment can fall between all five when no one is named as its owner end to end.\n\nA scope list is not an owner.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a BA takes a scope, name the owner of each leg, including the payments leg, in writing. If no owner is named, the BA should raise it on day one, not after the first failure.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Onboarding and entitlements\n→ Fees and limits\n→ The payments leg, with a named owner\n→ The escalation route when a payment fails\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What is the BA's scope?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a named owner exists for every leg?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment fail because no one owned its leg?", "takeaway": "A scope list is not an owner.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Corporate banking BA scope", "parts": [{"label": "Six or more years experience", "note": "The experience the role asks for."}, {"label": "EUR 120,000 payment failed", "note": "The payment that had no named owner."}, {"label": "Onboarding", "note": "In the BA scope end to end."}, {"label": "Entitlements", "note": "In the BA scope end to end."}, {"label": "Fees and limits", "note": "In the BA scope end to end."}, {"label": "Payments", "note": "In the BA scope end to end, but with no owner named."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-36", "seq": 409, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A hiring manager received a CV full of regions and roles, and no evidence of a single payment delivered", "explain": "A payments professional applied for product and senior BA roles across the UK, Australia, New Zealand, Asia, Europe and APAC.\n\nThe CV listed regions, roles and employers.\n\nIt showed no evidence of a single payment delivered end to end, and the hiring manager set it aside.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Application lists Product Manager, Senior Project Manager, Product Owner and Senior BA roles\n→ Regions listed: UK, Australia, New Zealand, Asia, Europe, APAC\n→ Background listed: three large employers, payments and product experience\n→ No payment journey described, no outcome named\n→ Hiring manager cannot tell what the candidate actually delivered\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Breadth of regions and roles proves capability.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nBreadth proves reach, not delivery. A hiring manager needs evidence: a payment flow delivered, a problem solved, an outcome measured. A list of regions and roles is a map with no journey on it.\n\nA map with no journey proves nothing.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen presenting payments experience, lead with one payment delivered end to end: the flow, the problem, the fix, the outcome. Regions and roles support the story, they are not the story.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The roles held\n→ The regions covered\n→ The payment delivered, with evidence\n→ The outcome, measured\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many regions have you covered?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves you delivered a payment end to end?\"\n\nHave you seen a strong candidate set aside because the CV showed breadth and no delivery?", "takeaway": "A map with no journey proves nothing.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Open to work, product and senior BA", "parts": [{"label": "Product Manager", "note": "Role sought."}, {"label": "Senior Project Manager", "note": "Role sought."}, {"label": "Product Owner", "note": "Role sought."}, {"label": "Senior BA", "note": "Role sought."}, {"label": "UK, Australia, New Zealand, Asia, Europe, APAC", "note": "Regions sought."}, {"label": "No delivery evidence", "note": "No payment journey described, no outcome named."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-37", "seq": 410, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A CV listed SEPA, ISO 20022 and SWIFT, and the interviewer still asked: show me one payment", "explain": "A payments BA applied for Product Owner and Senior BA roles in banking and payments.\n\nThe CV listed SEPA, cross-border, ISO 20022, SWIFT MT and MX, and APIs.\n\nIn the interview, the first question was: show me one payment you delivered, end to end.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ CV lists SEPA, cross-border, ISO 20022, SWIFT MT and MX, APIs\n→ Interviewer asks for one payment delivered end to end\n→ Candidate describes the skills, not a payment\n→ Interviewer asks again, for the flow, the problem, the outcome\n→ The skills list cannot answer a delivery question\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Listing the skills is the same as proving the work.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA skill list is a claim. A payment journey is evidence. The interviewer needs to hear the flow, the message names, the problem that arose, and what the candidate did about it. Skills name the tools. A journey shows the hands that used them.\n\nSkills name the tools. A journey shows the hands.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nPrepare one payment story for every skill on the CV: the flow, the messages, the failure, the fix, the outcome. When asked about a skill, answer with the story.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The skill claimed\n→ The payment that proves it\n→ The flow and the messages\n→ The outcome, measured\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which skills do you list?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves you used them on a real payment?\"\n\nHave you seen a skill list fail the first delivery question?", "takeaway": "Skills name the tools. A journey shows the hands.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Payments BA open to work", "parts": [{"label": "Product Owner", "note": "Role sought in banking and payments."}, {"label": "Senior Business Analyst", "note": "Role sought in banking and payments."}, {"label": "SEPA", "note": "Skill the BA brings."}, {"label": "ISO 20022", "note": "Skill the BA brings."}, {"label": "SWIFT MT and MX", "note": "Skill the BA brings."}, {"label": "Show me one payment", "note": "The interview question the skill list could not answer."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "ABH-38", "seq": 411, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A bank bought payments consulting, and the EUR 200,000 flow still had no BRD", "explain": "A bank hired payments consulting to cover functional advice, all payment types, BRD and FRD documents, AI use cases and product thinking.\n\nThe engagement ran for weeks.\n\nAt the end, the EUR 200,000 payment flow still had no BRD, because no one had agreed what the consulting was actually delivering.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Consulting engaged across functional advice, payment types, BRD and FRD, AI use cases, product thinking\n→ Weeks of discussion, no deliverable list agreed\n→ EUR 200,000 payment flow still undocumented\n→ Build team waits for requirements that never arrive\n→ Engagement ends, the flow still has no BRD\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Hiring expertise is the same as receiving requirements.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nExpertise is not a deliverable. A consulting engagement needs a named deliverable, a date, and an owner on the bank side to accept it. Without those, the knowledge stays in the conversation and the payment flow stays undocumented.\n\nAdvice is not a document.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen buying consulting, agree the deliverable first: which document, for which flow, by when, accepted by whom. Then the expertise has somewhere to land.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The advice given\n→ The document to be delivered\n→ The flow it covers\n→ The acceptance, by a named owner\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we hire an expert?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the BRD exists and was accepted?\"\n\nHave you seen a consulting engagement end with advice and no document?", "takeaway": "Advice is not a document.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Payments and banking consulting", "parts": [{"label": "Functional consulting", "note": "Consulting on how payments work."}, {"label": "All payment types", "note": "Covered by the consulting."}, {"label": "BRD and FRD", "note": "Documents the consulting was meant to produce."}, {"label": "AI use cases", "note": "Covered by the consulting."}, {"label": "Product thinking", "note": "Covered by the consulting."}, {"label": "EUR 200,000 flow, no BRD", "note": "The flow that stayed undocumented."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch37-how-10-large-banks-do-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 37: How 10 Large Banks Do Payments", "chapter": "ch37", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-39", "seq": 412, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 50,000 payment query could not reach support, because the booking channel was blocked by location", "explain": "A customer needed help with a EUR 50,000 payment query.\n\nThe support booking link was blocked because of the customer's living location.\n\nThe customer had no way to reach support, and the query sat unanswered.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer opens the booking link for a support session\n→ Link blocked by current living location\n→ No alternative channel offered on the page\n→ Customer cannot book, cannot reach support\n→ EUR 50,000 payment query waits, unanswered\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One booking channel is enough.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA single channel is a single point of failure. When a platform blocks a customer by location, the customer needs another route, or the bank hears nothing until the complaint arrives. Staying reachable means having a second channel ready before the first one fails.\n\nOne channel is no channel when it is blocked.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment support journey is designed, name the fallback channel for every blocked customer, and test it from the blocked location. A channel that works for most customers is not a channel for all of them.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The primary booking channel\n→ The fallback channel\n→ The customers blocked by location\n→ The query, tracked until answered\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the booking link work?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a blocked customer can still reach support?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer stranded because the only channel was blocked?", "takeaway": "One channel is no channel when it is blocked.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Staying reachable when blocked", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 50,000 payment query", "note": "The query that could not reach support."}, {"label": "Booking link blocked", "note": "Blocked by current living location."}, {"label": "No alternative offered", "note": "No second channel on the page."}, {"label": "Query unanswered", "note": "The customer cannot book, cannot reach support."}, {"label": "Fallback needed", "note": "A second channel ready before the first one fails."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "resolution": "reject", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-40", "seq": 413, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A bank expanded to six Asian markets on one payment assumption, and EUR 150,000 in payments stalled", "explain": "A bank expanded its payments business across six Asian markets: Singapore, Malaysia, China, Vietnam, the Philippines and more.\n\nIt assumed one payment rail and one set of rules would cover them all.\n\nEUR 150,000 in payments stalled in the first month, because each market runs its own rules.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Expansion planned across six markets\n→ One rail and one rule set assumed for all\n→ Payments sent into markets with different scheme rules\n→ Payments rejected or held for local requirements\n→ EUR 150,000 stalled while each market is mapped separately\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Asia is one payments market.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payments career in Asia is built on relationships across many markets, not one, and the same is true of the rails. Each market has its own scheme rules, participants, operating hours and dispute processes. A payment that works in one market can fail in another for a non-technical reason.\n\nSix markets, six rulebooks.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen expanding into a new market, map its scheme rules, participants, hours and disputes before sending the first payment. One market's evidence proves nothing about the next.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Each market's scheme rules\n→ Each market's participants\n→ Each market's operating hours\n→ Each market's dispute process\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we send payments to Asia?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves we mapped this specific market?\"\n\nHave you seen one market's rules assumed for a whole region?", "takeaway": "Six markets, six rulebooks.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Professional network across Asia", "parts": [{"label": "Singapore", "note": "A market in the network, with its own rules."}, {"label": "Malaysia", "note": "A market in the network, with its own rules."}, {"label": "China", "note": "A market in the network, with its own rules."}, {"label": "Vietnam", "note": "A market in the network, with its own rules."}, {"label": "Philippines", "note": "A market in the network, with its own rules."}, {"label": "EUR 150,000 stalled", "note": "Payments stalled because each market runs its own rules."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-41", "seq": 414, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An award-winning AI pilot paid EUR 60,000 with no control owner, and no one noticed", "explain": "A team won an award for exploring AI and turning ideas into impact.\n\nThe pilot it built paid EUR 60,000 in live payments.\n\nNo one had named a control owner for it, because the award had become the proof that it worked.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Team explores AI and builds a payments pilot\n→ Pilot pays EUR 60,000 in live payments\n→ Award recognises the team for turning ideas into impact\n→ No control owner named for the live payments\n→ A payment goes wrong and no one owns the fix\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An award proves the flow is safe.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRecognition celebrates exploration and impact. It does not test controls. A pilot that moves live money needs an owner for authorisation, limits, reconciliation and exceptions, whether or not it wins anything.\n\nAn award is not a control.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore any pilot touches live money, name the control owner and the evidence they will see: authorisations, limits, reconciliation, exceptions. Recognition can wait. Controls cannot.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The award and the recognition\n→ The live payments the pilot moves\n→ The control owner, named\n→ The evidence the owner sees\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the pilot win an award?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves someone owns the controls on live money?\"\n\nHave you seen recognition treated as proof that a flow was safe?", "takeaway": "An award is not a control.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The award and the live pilot", "parts": [{"label": "The award", "note": "Recognises exploring AI and turning ideas into impact."}, {"label": "EUR 60,000 in live payments", "note": "The pilot moved real money."}, {"label": "No control owner", "note": "No one owned authorisation, limits or reconciliation."}, {"label": "A payment goes wrong", "note": "No one owns the fix."}, {"label": "Recognition is not proof", "note": "An award does not test controls."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-42", "seq": 415, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An award was announced, and the EUR 40,000 payment flow behind it had never been tested", "explain": "A team announced an award for exploring AI and turning ideas into impact.\n\nThe announcement went out the same week the flow went live.\n\nThe EUR 40,000 payment flow behind it had never been tested end to end.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Team builds a payments flow with AI\n→ Flow goes live moving EUR 40,000\n→ Award announced for the work\n→ No end to end test ever run on the live flow\n→ First failure arrives with no test evidence to trace it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"The announcement is the milestone.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nAn announcement marks a moment. It does not prove the flow works. A flow that moves live money needs an end to end test before it goes live, and the test evidence must exist before the announcement, not after the first failure.\n\nAn announcement is not a test.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nNever announce a live payments flow before the end to end test evidence exists. The test is the milestone. The announcement is just the news.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The build\n→ The end to end test\n→ The test evidence, kept\n→ The announcement, after the test\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we announce the award?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the flow was tested end to end before go-live?\"\n\nHave you seen a flow announced before it was ever tested?", "takeaway": "An announcement is not a test.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The award and the untested flow", "parts": [{"label": "Exploring AI", "note": "The work the award recognises."}, {"label": "Turning ideas into impact", "note": "The work the award recognises."}, {"label": "EUR 40,000 live flow", "note": "The payment flow that went live untested."}, {"label": "No end to end test", "note": "No test evidence existed when the flow went live."}, {"label": "The announcement", "note": "Marks receiving the award, not a tested flow."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-43", "seq": 416, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "An award was called a milestone, but the EUR 25,000 lesson behind it was never written down", "explain": "A team received an award for exploring AI and turning ideas into impact.\n\nIt was framed as a milestone in a longer journey of sharing practical knowledge.\n\nThen a EUR 25,000 payment failed in the same flow, and the lesson from the failure was never written down.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Award received, framed as a milestone\n→ Journey of sharing practical knowledge continues\n→ EUR 25,000 payment fails in the flow\n→ Root cause found, but never documented\n→ Next team makes the same mistake, because the lesson lived only in memory\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A milestone means the learning is captured.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA milestone marks a point on a journey. It does not capture what the journey taught. Practical knowledge only survives if the failure, the cause and the fix are written down where the next team will find them.\n\nA milestone is not a lesson learned.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nEvery payment failure needs a written lesson: what failed, why, what changed, and where the next team will find it. Memory is not a knowledge base.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The award and the milestone\n→ The failure and its root cause\n→ The written lesson\n→ The next team, who must find it\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we mark the milestone?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the lesson was written down and found?\"\n\nHave you seen the same payment fail twice because the first lesson was never written?", "takeaway": "A milestone is not a lesson learned.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "A milestone in sharing knowledge", "parts": [{"label": "The award", "note": "Marks receiving the award."}, {"label": "Exploring AI", "note": "The journey the award marks."}, {"label": "Turning ideas into impact", "note": "The journey the award marks."}, {"label": "EUR 25,000 failure", "note": "A payment failed in the same flow."}, {"label": "Lesson never written", "note": "The next team made the same mistake."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"id": "ABH-44", "seq": 417, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A payments desk posting said only one group could apply, and a EUR 90,000 queue waited for staff who never came", "explain": "A payments operations desk posted a vacancy that said only one group of candidates could apply.\n\nQualified candidates who could have done the job did not apply.\n\nThe desk stayed short-staffed, and a EUR 90,000 payment queue waited.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Vacancy posted with restrictive wording\n→ Qualified candidates excluded by the wording\n→ Desk stays short-staffed\n→ EUR 90,000 payment queue backs up\n→ Customers wait while the posting is debated\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Posting language is a detail.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPosting language decides who applies. Restrictive wording that excludes qualified people is not a detail, it is a staffing decision, and a short-staffed payments desk is an operational risk. A fairness question about hiring language is a question a professional should be able to ask openly.\n\nWording is a staffing decision.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payments role is posted, check the wording the same way as a requirement: who does it include, who does it exclude, and what happens to the queue if the role stays empty.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The posting wording\n→ Who it includes and excludes\n→ The staffing gap it creates\n→ The payment queue that waits\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we post the vacancy?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the wording lets every qualified candidate apply?\"\n\nHave you seen a payments queue wait because a posting kept the right people out?", "takeaway": "Wording is a staffing decision.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fairness in hiring language", "parts": [{"label": "The question", "note": "Why do some postings say only one group can apply."}, {"label": "Qualified candidates excluded", "note": "People who could do the job did not apply."}, {"label": "Desk short-staffed", "note": "The payments desk stayed short-staffed."}, {"label": "EUR 90,000 queue waits", "note": "The payment queue backed up while the role stayed empty."}, {"label": "Fairness", "note": "A question a professional should ask openly."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch37-how-10-large-banks-do-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 37: How 10 Large Banks Do Payments", "chapter": "ch37", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "ABH-45", "seq": 418, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A bank demanded joining in 30 days, and a EUR 400,000 migration waited six months for a BA", "explain": "A bank needed a payments BA for a migration moving EUR 400,000 a day.\n\nIt demanded the new hire join within 30 days.\n\nEvery qualified candidate had a 60-day notice period, so the role stayed empty for six months.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Bank demands joining within 30 days\n→ Candidates have 60-day notice periods\n→ No candidate can join in 30 days\n→ Role stays empty, migration milestone slips\n→ Six months later the role is still open\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Candidates can leave as fast as we want them to join.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nNotice periods run both ways, and a hiring process that ignores that creates a mismatch. An employee cannot leave in 30 days when the contract says 60, and a bank that demands the impossible gets no one, not a faster hire.\n\nA 30-day demand with a 60-day notice gets no one.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a role is planned, check the market notice period before setting the joining date. The joining date follows the notice period, not the other way round.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The joining date demanded\n→ The notice period candidates actually have\n→ The migration milestone at risk\n→ The plan, if the role stays empty\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can the candidate join in 30 days?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the joining date fits the market notice period?\"\n\nHave you seen a critical role stay empty because the joining demand ignored notice periods?", "takeaway": "A 30-day demand with a 60-day notice gets no one.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Joining in thirty days", "parts": [{"label": "Immediate joining", "note": "What companies demand."}, {"label": "Joining within thirty days", "note": "What companies demand."}, {"label": "Notice periods", "note": "Run both ways, for employee and employer."}, {"label": "EUR 400,000 migration waits", "note": "The role stayed empty for six months and the milestone slipped."}, {"label": "The mismatch", "note": "A process that ignores notice creates it."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "message mismatch", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-46", "seq": 419, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Branches opened on Sundays, while EUR 80,000 in weekend payments failed in the app", "explain": "A bank opened its branches on Sundays.\n\nThat same Sunday, EUR 80,000 in customer payments failed in the bank's app.\n\nCustomers queued at the branches for payments the app should have handled.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Bank opens branches on Sundays\n→ Customers arrive with payment problems\n→ EUR 80,000 in app payments fail over the weekend\n→ Branch staff cannot fix an app failure\n→ Customers queue, staff work Sundays, the app still fails\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Longer branch hours fix customer payments.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nCustomers want better digital banking, not longer branch hours. A payment that fails in the app is a digital failure, and a branch counter cannot process it. Bank employees deserve a Sunday with family, and customers deserve an app that works on a Sunday.\n\nBranch hours cannot fix an app.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen customers queue for payments, check the digital channel first. If the app is the failure, the fix is the app, not the branch rota.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Branch opening hours\n→ The digital payment channel\n→ The weekend failure rate\n→ The staff rota, and who it serves\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are the branches open on Sunday?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the app works on a Sunday?\"\n\nHave you seen branch hours extended to cover a digital failure?", "takeaway": "Branch hours cannot fix an app.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Do banks need Sunday branches", "parts": [{"label": "Sunday branches", "note": "What banks are opening."}, {"label": "EUR 80,000 failed in the app", "note": "Weekend payments failed in the digital channel."}, {"label": "Better digital banking", "note": "What customers actually want."}, {"label": "Employees deserve Sunday", "note": "A Sunday with family."}, {"label": "Digital capability", "note": "What customers need, not branch hours."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D01 Foundations of money and payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch69-what-money-is.html", "label": "Chapter 69: What money is", "chapter": "ch69", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-47", "seq": 420, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 70,000 payment queue waited on a festival night, and the only analyst on shift was far from home", "explain": "On a festival night, a payments operations analyst worked the night shift far from home.\n\nA EUR 70,000 payment queue needed coverage.\n\nThe analyst was the only person on shift, and there was no backup.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Festival night, payment queue needs coverage\n→ One analyst on shift, far from family\n→ EUR 70,000 in payments waiting\n→ Analyst falls ill mid-shift, no backup named\n→ Queue waits until morning, customers wait with it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Someone will always cover the shift.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA payments career often means working far from home, and the salary reaches home even when the person cannot. But a rota that depends on one person, on a festival night, with no backup, is not a rota. It is a hope.\n\nOne person on shift is not cover.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payment queue needs night or festival cover, name two people: the analyst on shift and the backup. A queue that cannot wait needs a rota that cannot fail on one person.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The shift rota\n→ The backup, named\n→ The payment queue that cannot wait\n→ The human side, the analyst far from home\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Is someone on shift?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a backup exists if that person cannot work?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment queue wait because the only analyst on shift was unavailable?", "takeaway": "One person on shift is not cover.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Earning far from home", "parts": [{"label": "Working away from family", "note": "The reality of the payments career."}, {"label": "Salary reaches home", "note": "The money gets home."}, {"label": "EUR 70,000 queue waits", "note": "The payment queue waited until morning."}, {"label": "No backup named", "note": "One analyst on shift, no one to step in."}, {"label": "The human side", "note": "It matters as much as the work."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-48", "seq": 421, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A status update said all green while EUR 110,000 in payments sat unreconciled", "explain": "A payments team lead posted a polished update: the team was doing well, the work was on track.\n\nThe same day, EUR 110,000 in payments sat unreconciled.\n\nThe update said all green. The ledger said otherwise.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Team lead posts a positive update\n→ Update shows the human side, the team, the momentum\n→ EUR 110,000 in payments unreconciled in the ledger\n→ No one raises it, because the update says all is well\n→ Reconciliation break found days later at day-end\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A good update means good delivery.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nA personal update shows the human side, which is part of how a professional builds trust. But it is not a status report. A payments queue is measured by reconciliation, not by tone, and a green update cannot reconcile EUR 110,000.\n\nA green update does not reconcile a ledger.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nKeep the human update and the delivery status separate. One can be warm and personal. The other must be evidence: reconciled, pending, or broken, with numbers.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The human update\n→ The delivery status\n→ The reconciliation position\n→ The evidence, with numbers\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the update sound good?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the ledger reconciles?\"\n\nHave you seen a polished update hide a broken reconciliation?", "takeaway": "A green update does not reconcile a ledger.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "The human side of a professional", "parts": [{"label": "Polished update", "note": "All green, team doing well, work on track."}, {"label": "EUR 110,000 unreconciled", "note": "Payments sat unreconciled in the ledger."}, {"label": "Human side", "note": "Shows the person behind the professional."}, {"label": "Break found at day-end", "note": "The reconciliation break surfaced days later."}, {"label": "Two different reports", "note": "The update and the ledger are not the same report."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/state-machine.html", "label": "Payment State Machine Simulator", "slug": "state-machine"}}}, {"id": "ABH-49", "seq": 422, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One payment switch kept working while everything around it failed, and EUR 200,000 kept moving", "explain": "A disaster hit a region and payment systems failed across it.\n\nOne payment switch kept processing.\n\nEUR 200,000 in payments moved through it that day, because it had been built to a higher standard.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Disaster strikes, systems fail across the region\n→ Payment switch keeps processing\n→ EUR 200,000 in payments move through it\n→ Customers who needed money most could still get it\n→ Other systems stay down for days\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Good engineering is a nice-to-have.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nGood engineering is not abstract: it protects real people in real disasters. The switch that stood was not lucky. It was built with redundancy, tested failover, and a standard the other systems had skipped.\n\nResilience is built, not lucky.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a payments system is designed, name the disaster it must survive and the evidence it will keep working: redundancy, tested failover, and the standard it was built to. Then test it before the disaster does.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The engineering standard\n→ The redundancy, built in\n→ The failover, tested\n→ The payments that must keep moving\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Does the system work on a normal day?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves it keeps working when everything around it fails?\"\n\nHave you seen one system keep working while everything around it failed?", "takeaway": "Resilience is built, not lucky.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "When good engineering saves lives", "parts": [{"label": "Disaster strikes", "note": "Payment systems fail across the region."}, {"label": "One switch keeps working", "note": "EUR 200,000 in payments move through it that day."}, {"label": "Good engineering", "note": "Not abstract, it protects real people."}, {"label": "Built to a higher standard", "note": "Redundancy and tested failover, not luck."}, {"label": "Real disasters", "note": "Where engineering is tested."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "ABH-50", "seq": 423, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A payments post reached thousands, got the settlement rule wrong, and EUR 500,000 followed the wrong advice", "explain": "A payments post with a large following explained a settlement rule.\n\nThousands of readers shared it.\n\nThe rule was wrong, and EUR 500,000 in payments followed the wrong advice before anyone corrected it.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Post explains a settlement rule to a large audience\n→ Thousands share it the same day\n→ A bank follows the post's rule on EUR 500,000 of payments\n→ Settlement breaks, the rule was wrong\n→ Correction arrives days later, after the money moved\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Reach proves accuracy.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nFifteen years in payments, product management, APIs and ISO 20022 earns a following, but a following is not a validation. Practical insights only help if they are right, and a wrong rule shared widely does more damage than a wrong rule shared with no one.\n\nReach is not accuracy.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore acting on payments advice, however widely shared, check it against the scheme rulebook and the message definition. The source of truth is the rulebook, not the share count.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The following and the shares\n→ The rule as stated\n→ The scheme rulebook\n→ The payments that followed the advice\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many people shared this?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the rule matches the rulebook?\"\n\nHave you seen widely shared advice turn out to be wrong?", "takeaway": "Reach is not accuracy.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Fifteen years in payments", "parts": [{"label": "Large following", "note": "The post reached thousands in a day."}, {"label": "Settlement rule stated", "note": "The rule in the post was wrong."}, {"label": "EUR 500,000 followed it", "note": "Payments followed the wrong advice before the correction."}, {"label": "Correction came late", "note": "Days later, after the money had moved."}, {"label": "Check the rulebook", "note": "The scheme rulebook is the source of truth, not the share count."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-148", "seq": 430, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 15,000 instant payment failed after the debit, and both banks pointed at different contracts", "explain": "Two banks connected their instant payment systems.\n\nA EUR 15,000 payment was debited from the sender, then failed.\n\nBoth banks pointed at different contracts, and the customer waited for a refund no one owned.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Banks connect their instant payment systems\n→ EUR 15,000 debited from the sender\n→ Payment fails after the debit\n→ Bank A says Bank B must return it, Bank B says Bank A must investigate\n→ Customer waits, no one owns the refund\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Connecting the systems is the integration.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen two banks connect their instant payment systems, the technical link is only the start. They must agree who investigates a failed payment, who returns the funds, how long the customer waits, and how disputes are resolved. Without that governance, a payment that fails after the sender is debited leaves both banks pointing at different contracts.\n\nA connection is not an agreement.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nInteroperability is a business and operating agreement, not just a connection. Agree the investigator, the refund owner, the waiting time and the dispute route before the first payment flows.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The technical link\n→ Who investigates a failure\n→ Who returns the funds\n→ How long the customer waits\n→ How disputes are resolved\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are the systems connected?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves both banks agreed who owns a failed payment?\"\n\nHave you seen two banks point at different contracts while a customer waited?", "takeaway": "A connection is not an agreement.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Interoperability operating model", "parts": [{"label": "EUR 15,000 failed after debit", "note": "Payment debited from the sender, then failed."}, {"label": "Technical link only the start", "note": "Technical link is only the start."}, {"label": "Who investigates a failure", "note": "Banks must agree who investigates a failed payment."}, {"label": "Who returns the funds", "note": "Banks must agree who returns the funds."}, {"label": "How long the customer waits", "note": "Banks must agree how long the customer waits."}, {"label": "How disputes are resolved", "note": "Banks must agree how disputes are resolved."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-A01", "seq": 435, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A EUR 250 payment went out wrong, and the team sent the wrong message to fix it", "explain": "A legitimate EUR 250 payment was executed in error.\n\nThe team sent a cancellation request.\n\nThe payment had already settled, so the cancellation could not work, and the money stayed where it was.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ EUR 250 payment executed with the wrong details\n→ Team sends camt.055 cancellation request\n→ Bank B has already settled the payment\n→ Cancellation refused, settlement has cleared\n→ Refund now needs camt.056 or pacs.004, not camt.055\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Cancellation fixes any wrong payment.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nSettlement status decides the instrument. Cancellation suits a payment still unsettled, while a settled payment needs a return. A camt.055 works only before Bank B settles, and once settlement has cleared, the bank seeks the funds back through a camt.056 or a pacs.004. The refund route follows the standing agreement, and an unanswered camt.055 leaves pacs.004 as the fallback request.\n\nSettled means return, not cancel.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBefore choosing the instrument, check the settlement status. Then follow the standing agreement: FOCR returns the full amount under its own procedure, and the cover route sends the money back to the original account.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Settlement status\n→ Cancellation, before settlement\n→ Return, after settlement\n→ The standing agreement that decides the route\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did we send a cancellation?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the payment was still unsettled when we cancelled it?\"\n\nHave you seen a cancellation sent for a payment that had already settled?", "takeaway": "Settled means return, not cancel.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "EUR 250 executed in error", "note": "A legitimate EUR 250 payment went through with the wrong execution."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has settlement cleared?", "note": "Settlement status decides whether cancellation or return applies."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Cancel early with camt.055", "note": "Request cancellation before settlement; Bank B refuses it after settling."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Seek return after settlement", "note": "Use camt.056 or pacs.004 to recover funds once the payment has settled."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "FOCR or cover route?", "note": "FOCR returns funds in full; cover returns them to the original account."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.004 when camt.055 is silent", "note": "An unanswered camt.055 leaves pacs.004 as the return request."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.055", "camt.056", "pacs.004"], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B03", "seq": 442, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One leg of a USD 250,000 payment failed, and the team nearly recalled the leg that was fine", "explain": "A USD 250,000 cover-method payment went from India to Europe.\n\nThe pacs.008 was rejected by the bank system before sending.\n\nThe team reached for the recall button, even though the pacs.009 COV had already been processed and was sound.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 rejected before sending\n→ pacs.009 COV already processed\n→ Team proposes an instant recall\n→ Three questions decide the path: has the cover been released, has it settled, is the beneficiary bank USD cut-off still open\n→ If the cover has not been released, halt it, resolve the fault and rebuild both legs\n→ If it has settled and the cut-off is still open, fixing the issue and sending the pacs.008 may complete the payment without a recall\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A failed leg means recall everything.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nRecalling straight away can make things worse, because unwinding a sound cover and processing it again can push the credit later and shift the value date. A failed leg must not trigger an instant recall before the cover is checked.\n\nCheck the cover before recalling it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen one leg fails, ask the three questions first: cover released, cover settled, cut-off still open. Then choose: halt and rebuild, or fix and send. Recall is the last resort, not the first reflex.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The failed pacs.008 leg\n→ The cover, released or not\n→ The cover, settled or not\n→ The beneficiary bank cut-off\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did a leg fail?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the cover's state before we decide to recall?\"\n\nHave you seen a sound cover unwound because one leg failed?", "takeaway": "Check the cover before recalling it.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 rejected before sending", "note": "Bank system rejects the pacs.008 while the pacs.009 COV is already processed."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Has the cover been released?", "note": "First ask whether the cover has been released."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Settled, and cut-off still open?", "note": "Released but unsettled calls for investigation before any cancellation decision."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Not released: stop and regenerate", "note": "Halt the cover, resolve the fault and rebuild both legs."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Settled and cut-off open: fix and send", "note": "Fix the issue and send the pacs.008, which may complete the payment without a recall."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Do not recall straight away", "note": "Unwinding a sound cover can push the credit later and shift the value date."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.008", "pacs.009"], "failure_category": "cut-off missed", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch7-pacs-008-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 7: pacs.008 Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch7", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B06", "seq": 443, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A pacs.002 RJCT arrived for USD 180,000, and the bank nearly refunded money it did not have", "explain": "A USD 180,000 cover-method payment was sent.\n\nA pacs.002 RJCT arrived on the pacs.008.\n\nThe team moved to credit the remitter, even though the cover was still sitting at the correspondent.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ pacs.009 COV sent, funds move through the correspondent\n→ pacs.002 RJCT arrives on the pacs.008\n→ Instinct is to credit the remitter at once\n→ Cover still at the correspondent, not yet returned\n→ Crediting now would refund the customer while the cover is still out, a loss the bank absorbs\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A rejection means the money is back.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nUnder the cover method the pacs.008 goes directly to the beneficiary bank while the pacs.009 COV moves the funds through the correspondent, so a pacs.002 RJCT on the pacs.008 does not by itself justify crediting the remitter. The first question is the status of the cover: not yet processed, on hold, or already processed and settled. The bank must establish where the funds are before acting.\n\nA rejection is not a refund.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMastering pacs.002, pacs.004 and camt.056 one by one is not enough. Senior practice means understanding how the two legs interact and what to check before taking action. Find the funds first, then credit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ The pacs.008 and its RJCT\n→ The cover, and its status\n→ The funds, located before any credit\n→ The credit to the remitter, after the funds are found\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the payment reject?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the funds are before we credit anyone?\"\n\nHave you seen a customer credited before the funds were found?", "takeaway": "A rejection is not a refund.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.008 and pacs.009 COV sent", "note": "pacs.008 goes to the beneficiary bank while the COV moves funds through the correspondent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "pacs.002 RJCT arrives", "note": "A rejection arrives for the pacs.008, and the instinct is to credit the remitter."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "What is the status of the cover?", "note": "Not yet processed, on hold, or already processed and settled."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish where the funds are", "note": "Find where the funds are before acting."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Do not credit too early", "note": "Crediting too early can refund the customer while the cover is still at the correspondent."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["pacs.002", "pacs.008", "pacs.009", "pacs.004", "camt.056"], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch41-payment-exceptions-and-investigations.html", "label": "Chapter 41: Payment Exceptions and Investigations", "chapter": "ch41", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-B10", "seq": 444, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A regulatory deadline and a EUR 1 million client project collided, and the team was told to pick one", "explain": "A team had two pieces of work: CBPR+ structured-address work for a regulatory date, and a strategic client Cash Pooling project worth EUR 1 million.\n\nLeadership said the regulatory work came first and the client work waited.\n\nThe team challenged the assumptions instead of the authority, and found both could be phased.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Regulatory date set for CBPR+ structured-address work\n→ Strategic client Cash Pooling deprioritised against it\n→ Team asks what is truly mandatory, what can be phased, and what the capacity impact is\n→ Cash Pooling side might need only domestic pooling first\n→ Regulatory side might sequence incoming support before outgoing\n→ Plan agreed: incoming structured-address support first, domestic Cash Pooling on track, outgoing support plus international and cross-currency pooling in the next phase\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A regulatory date means the client work must wait.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nPrioritising one over the other looked reasonable, yet the clash was worth challenging on the assumptions, not the authority. Breaking both pieces down turned an either-or choice into a plan. The regulatory date later moved, yet the sequencing approach still held.\n\nPhase both, not either-or.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen two priorities collide, challenge the thinking, not the person. Ask what is truly mandatory, what can be phased, and what the capacity impact is, before locking the priority in.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ What is truly mandatory\n→ What can be phased\n→ The capacity impact\n→ The sequence, agreed by both sides\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which piece wins?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each piece can be phased without breaking the other?\"\n\nHave you seen an either-or choice turn into a phased plan?", "takeaway": "Phase both, not either-or.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Phase both, not either-or", "parts": [{"label": "Regulatory date November 2026", "note": "CBPR+ structured-address work was prioritised for the regulatory date."}, {"label": "Strategic client Cash Pooling", "note": "The client work was deprioritised against the regulatory work."}, {"label": "Challenge the assumptions", "note": "Challenge the thinking, not the person, and question what is truly mandatory."}, {"label": "What can be phased?", "note": "Check what can be phased and what the capacity impact is."}, {"label": "Incoming first, domestic on track", "note": "Incoming structured-address support first, with domestic Cash Pooling on track."}, {"label": "Next phase: outgoing and more", "note": "Outgoing support plus international and cross-currency pooling in the next phase."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-C09", "seq": 446, "module": "Advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A candidate could name every recall message, and still could not explain a EUR 20,000 recall", "explain": "A candidate was asked to explain a EUR 20,000 recall.\n\nThe candidate named camt.055, camt.056, pacs.004 and camt.029, one by one, perfectly.\n\nThen the interviewer asked what happened to the money, and the candidate had no answer.\n\nHere's how it happened:\n\n→ Customer asks the debtor bank to recall a EUR 20,000 payment\n→ Bank starts an investigation and sends a camt.056 to the creditor agent\n→ Creditor bank checks whether the payment was credited and whether the beneficiary agrees\n→ Financial outcome follows the creditor decision on the return of funds\n→ Investigation response closes the journey\n→ Candidate lists the messages but cannot connect them into this journey\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Naming the messages proves payments knowledge.\"\n\nWhy?\n\nIt does not. Many candidates can describe the messages one by one but cannot connect them into the business journey. The interview answer should follow the chain of customer intention, investigation, interbank request, creditor decision, financial outcome and response, because that shows payments knowledge, not just message knowledge.\n\nA list of messages is not a journey.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen learning any ISO 20022 message, ask who sends it, why, what triggered it, and what happens before and after it. Then practise the journey, not the list.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer intention\n→ Investigation and interbank request\n→ Creditor decision\n→ Financial outcome\n→ Investigation response\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can you name the messages?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves you can follow the money through them?\"\n\nHave you seen a candidate list every message and still miss the journey?", "takeaway": "A list of messages is not a journey.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Customer asks to recall the payment", "note": "The journey starts with the customer asking the debtor bank to recall a EUR 20,000 payment."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Bank starts an investigation", "note": "The bank starts an investigation and sends a camt.056 to the creditor agent."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Creditor bank checks the payment", "note": "The creditor bank checks whether the payment was credited and the beneficiary agrees."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Financial outcome decided", "note": "The outcome follows the creditor decision on the return of funds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Investigation response sent", "note": "Finally the outcome and the investigation response close the journey."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Answer as a chain, not a list", "note": "Follow customer intention, investigation, request, decision, outcome and response."}]}}, "level": "advanced", "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": ["camt.055", "camt.056", "pacs.004", "camt.029"], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch52-swift-reporting-messages-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 52: SWIFT Reporting Messages Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch52", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "INS-011", "seq": 459, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Failed instant payments spike just after 09:00", "explain": "Just after 09:00 the monitoring dashboard shows a sharp rise in failed instant payments.\nCustomers report payments stuck in pending.\nThe contact centre queue is growing, and nobody has declared an incident yet.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Failed instant payments spike on the dashboard\n→ Customers report payments stuck in pending\n→ Contact centre queue grows\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Start fixing before declaring, so the incident looks smaller.\"\n\nThe first responder must act in a fixed order and not improvise. First, confirm the incident is real using monitoring, error rates and customer reports, and declare it through the agreed channel with a severity based on customer impact, not on the suspected cause. Then establish the impact, the affected population, the containment, the ownership, the evidence and the update cadence, so that the response is organised from the first minutes rather than assembled under pressure.\n\nThe first minutes decide the incident. Declare first, fix second.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nConfirm, declare, then organise. Severity follows customer impact, never the suspected cause, and the update cadence is set in the first minutes, not when someone asks for news.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Incident confirmation\n→ Impact and affected population\n→ Containment\n→ Ownership\n→ Evidence and update cadence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What caused it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the incident is real, who is affected, and who owns it?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a team fix for an hour before anyone declared the incident?", "takeaway": "Establish the impact, the affected population, the containment, the ownership, the evidence and the update cadence, so the response is organised from the first minutes.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the incident is real", "note": "Use monitoring, error rates and customer reports."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Declare the incident", "note": "Agreed channel, severity by customer impact."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish impact and population", "note": "Establish the affected population."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish containment", "note": "Establish the containment for the incident."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Who owns it, and how often do we update?", "note": "Ownership, evidence and update cadence."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D13 Payments product and business", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch82-product-strategy-and-business-models.html", "label": "Chapter 82: Product strategy and business models", "chapter": "ch82", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"id": "INS-065", "seq": 460, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "300 complaints in 20 minutes, one shape", "explain": "On a weekday morning between 09:12 and 09:32, a contact centre receives more than 300 complaints with the same shape.\nCustomers report a debit that does not match the payment status shown in the app, or a credit they expected that has not appeared.\nThree hundred voices, one pattern, and no incident bridge yet.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Complaints arrive with the same shape\n→ Debits do not match the app status\n→ Expected credits have not appeared\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Find the root cause first, then protect the customers.\"\n\nStart with an incident bridge that separates three workstreams: customer and financial containment, technical root cause analysis, and communications. Containment owns the affected-transaction register, the decision on the next batch and any remediation, while the other workstreams proceed in parallel, so that customers are protected even before the root cause is known.\n\nProtect customers before you understand the cause.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOpen the bridge and split the work. Containment owns the register and the next-batch decision. Root cause and communications run in parallel. Customers are protected while the cause is still unknown.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Customer and financial containment\n→ Technical root cause analysis\n→ Communications\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What caused it?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves which customers are affected, and what protects them right now?\"\n\nHave you ever seen 300 identical complaints land in 20 minutes and known it was one incident, not 300?", "takeaway": "Containment owns the affected-transaction register, the decision on the next batch and any remediation, while the other workstreams proceed in parallel.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Open an incident bridge", "note": "Start with an incident bridge for the complaints."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Separate three workstreams", "note": "Containment, root cause analysis and communications."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Containment owns the register", "note": "Affected-transaction register and remediation decisions."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Hold the next batch or let it run?", "note": "Containment decides the next batch while root cause runs in parallel."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D06 Cross-border payments", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch94-cross-border-payments-in-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 94: Cross-border payments in depth", "chapter": "ch94", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"id": "INS-067", "seq": 461, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "UPI, cards, net banking and wallets all failing on the same day", "explain": "On the same day, UPI payments fail with timeouts, card payments fail at the gateway, net banking sessions drop before confirmation, and wallet payments fail after the wallet is debited.\nFour rails, four failure shapes, one very bad day.\nIs it one cause, or several?\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ UPI payments time out\n→ Card payments fail at the gateway\n→ Net banking sessions drop before confirmation\n→ Wallet payments fail after the wallet is debited\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"One bad day means one cause.\"\n\nBuild one incident table segmented by rail and channel, gateway, bank, response code, time window and transaction state, and join each row to its ledger and clearing evidence by correlation ID. For each segment, establish whether the payment left the customer account, whether it reached clearing or the scheme, and what its authoritative state is now. Segmenting the incident this way shows whether there is one cause or several, which decides whether one fix or several are needed.\n\nSegment first. One cause or several, the evidence decides.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nOne table, segmented by rail, and every row joined to its ledger and clearing evidence by correlation ID. For each segment ask the same three questions: did it leave the account, did it reach clearing, and what is its authoritative state now.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Rail and channel\n→ Gateway and bank\n→ Response code and time window\n→ Transaction state\n→ Ledger and clearing evidence\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What failed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves, per segment, where each payment stopped?\"\n\nHave you ever had four rails fail at once and had to prove it was not one single cause?", "takeaway": "Segmenting the incident this way shows whether there is one cause or several, which decides whether one fix or several are needed.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Segment the incident", "note": "One table by rail, channel, gateway, bank and code."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Join each row to ledger evidence", "note": "Join by correlation ID to ledger and clearing evidence."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Establish each segment state", "note": "Did it leave the account, reach clearing, what is its state."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "One cause or several?", "note": "One cause means one fix, several means several fixes."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Apply the fix", "note": "One fix or several, as the evidence decides."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D08 Cards and alternative payments", "messages": [], "failure_category": "timeout", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch32-digital-wallets-and-pos-systems.html", "label": "Chapter 32: Digital Wallets and POS Systems", "chapter": "ch32", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-030", "seq": 465, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The retailer, the bank API and the missing outcome", "explain": "A retailer wants its checkout connected directly to a bank's payment API, so orders can be paid without leaving the retailer's site.\nOn a EUR 60,000 day of orders, the question is not how to call the API.\nIt is how the retailer learns whether each payment actually completed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Retailer submits payment instructions to the bank API\n→ Bank returns a response\n→ Retailer must learn the final outcome\n→ Order is created only when the outcome is known\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"An API is just endpoints.\"\n\nWalk through the integration in layers and make each layer testable, starting with the contract, the endpoints, the request and response schemas, mandatory and optional fields, validation rules and error codes, then the authentication, the asynchronous status path and the recovery procedures. An acceptance response is never completion, and the retailer needs a status enquiry path for every payment whose outcome is unclear.\n\nA payment API is a promise about outcomes, not a list of fields.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine the contract first: what each response promises, what the caller does next, how the final outcome is learned, how failures are handled and how the integration is monitored. Every layer is testable on its own.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Contract and schemas\n→ Authentication\n→ Asynchronous status path\n→ Failure handling and recovery\n→ Monitoring\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we call the API?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves each payment completed, and what do we do when the outcome is unclear?\"\n\nHave you ever integrated an API that answered every call, yet never told you whether the payment completed?", "takeaway": "Walk through the integration in layers and make each layer testable, starting with the contract, then the authentication, the asynchronous status path and the recovery procedures.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define the contract", "note": "Endpoints, schemas, fields, validation and error codes."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Explain authentication", "note": "How the client authenticates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Explain request and response", "note": "What the request and response mean."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Explain the status path", "note": "How the retailer learns the final outcome."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Explain failure handling", "note": "How failures are handled and recovered."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the outcome known?", "note": "Create the order only when the outcome is known."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch80-api-design-standards-and-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 80: API design standards and integration patterns", "chapter": "ch80", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "INS-025", "seq": 466, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Fast, secure, easy: the gateway brief with no requirements", "explain": "A retailer sells online and in store and wants a third-party payment gateway for card and instant payments at checkout.\nThe requirements are three words: fast, secure and easy to integrate.\nThree words, and no one can build from them.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Retailer wants card and instant payments at checkout\n→ Brief says fast, secure and easy\n→ No methods, currencies, channels or journeys defined\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Fast, secure and easy is a requirement.\"\n\nStart from the customer and finance outcomes, not from the provider brochure: list the payment methods, currencies, channels and customer journeys in scope, and for each one define the expected flow, authorisation, capture, refund, partial refund, void, chargeback handling and settlement timing. Then define the API capabilities, the request and response contracts, the error handling, the security, the retries and the reconciliation, so the provider can be compared on evidence rather than on marketing.\n\nA wish is not a requirement. An outcome is.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine every journey before talking to a provider: flow, authorisation, capture, refund, void, chargeback handling and settlement timing, then the API capabilities, error handling, security, retries and reconciliation. Providers are compared on evidence, not on marketing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Payment methods and currencies\n→ Channels and customer journeys\n→ Authorisation, capture, refund, void, chargeback\n→ Settlement timing\n→ API capabilities and reconciliation\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which gateway is best?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a provider meets each journey's outcome?\"\n\nHave you ever been handed a brief that was three adjectives and told to write requirements from it?", "takeaway": "Define the API capabilities, the request and response contracts, the error handling, the security, the retries and the reconciliation, so the provider can be compared on evidence rather than on marketing.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "List what is in scope", "note": "Methods, currencies, channels and customer journeys."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define the flow per journey", "note": "Authorisation, capture, refund, void, chargeback, settlement."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define API capabilities", "note": "Contracts, error handling, security, retries, reconciliation."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Compare providers on evidence", "note": "Compare on evidence, not on marketing."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-048", "seq": 467, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "SIT passed clean. Production failed on day one.", "explain": "A merchant payment API integration passes system integration testing cleanly, every test case returning the expected response.\nIt fails in production on day one.\nThe same calls, the same payloads, and a failure rate climbing by the hour.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ SIT passes every test case\n→ Production fails from the first submissions\n→ Failure rate is rising\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"If it passed SIT, production is the same.\"\n\nContain the impact first by stopping new submissions if the failure rate is rising, telling the partner what is known and what is not, and confirming which payments have a financial effect and which do not. Then compare the environments side by side in a fixed order: endpoint and configuration, credentials and scopes, certificates and expiry dates, network routes and firewall rules, feature flags, downstream dependency versions and production data. The difference between test and production is usually in one of these, and comparing them in a fixed order finds it faster than guessing.\n\nSIT and production are two different systems that happen to share a name.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nContain first, then compare the environments in a fixed order. Endpoint and configuration, credentials and certificates, network and firewall, flags and dependencies, then production data. Guessing is slower than comparing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Endpoint and configuration\n→ Credentials, scopes and certificates\n→ Network routes and firewall rules\n→ Feature flags and dependency versions\n→ Production data\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did SIT pass?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the two environments match, and which payments have a financial effect?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a clean SIT build fail in production and had to prove the environments were never the same?", "takeaway": "The difference between test and production is usually in one of these, and comparing them in a fixed order finds it faster than guessing.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Contain the impact", "note": "Stop new submissions, tell the partner, confirm effect."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare endpoint and configuration", "note": "Check endpoint and configuration first."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare credentials and certificates", "note": "Credentials, scopes, certificates and expiry dates."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare network and firewall", "note": "Network routes and firewall rules."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare flags and dependencies", "note": "Feature flags and downstream dependency versions."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Found the difference?", "note": "Production data is the last comparison."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-089", "seq": 468, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "HTTP 200, order unpaid, account debited", "explain": "A fintech partner integrates with a bank's payment API to submit customer credit transfers for its merchants.\nIn testing, the API returns a successful HTTP response, but the merchant's downstream order record is not created.\nIn one case the payment is debited while the merchant dashboard still shows the order as unpaid.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ API returns HTTP 200\n→ Merchant order record is not created\n→ Payment debited, dashboard shows unpaid\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"HTTP 200 means the payment completed.\"\n\nStart from the contract, not from the code: the API definition must state, for each endpoint, what a success response promises, receipt, validation, acceptance or completion, and what the caller must do next in each case. Then review authentication, schema, mandatory fields, business validation, response codes, idempotency and observability, because a success response that promises completion when it only means receipt will mislead every caller.\n\nA 200 can mean receipt. It never means completion unless the contract says so.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nState what success promises per endpoint, and what the caller must do next in each case. Then review authentication, schema, mandatory fields, business validation, response codes, idempotency and observability, because a misleading success will mislead every caller.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Receipt\n→ Validation\n→ Acceptance\n→ Completion\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Did the API return 200?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what that 200 promised, and what must the caller do next?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a 200 response debited an account while the order stayed unpaid?", "takeaway": "A success response that promises completion when it only means receipt will mislead every caller.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "State what success promises", "note": "Receipt, validation, acceptance or completion per endpoint."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "State what the caller does next", "note": "What the caller must do next in each case."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Review authentication", "note": "Review authentication for the API."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Review schema and fields", "note": "Review schema, mandatory fields and business validation."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Review response codes", "note": "Review response codes and what each means."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is idempotency in place?", "note": "Review idempotency and observability."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "failure_category": "system or connectivity failure", "resolution": "return", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch47-banking-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 47: Banking Integration Patterns", "chapter": "ch47", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/exception.html", "label": "Payment Exception Investigation Simulator", "slug": "exception"}}}, {"id": "INS-059", "seq": 469, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The API contract that forgot to say what success means", "explain": "A bank is publishing a Payment API for corporate customers and retail partners.\nThe first draft of the contract is a list of fields with no rules.\nIt does not say which fields are required, what happens when a field is missing, which error code means what, whether a success response means the payment completed, or how a caller should safely retry.\nA list of fields, and EUR 100,000 a day will flow through it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Contract drafted as a list of fields\n→ No required or optional rules\n→ No error code meanings\n→ No statement of what success means\n→ No safe retry rule\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A list of fields is a contract.\"\n\nDefine every field with its name, type, cardinality, validation rule and error code, and state explicitly which fields are required, which are optional, and which are conditionally mandatory by rail, destination or product. Where a field is optional under the message schema but the bank's reporting or product needs it, say so in the contract and state the source the bank will use and the derivation rule.\n\nIf success is undefined, every caller will define it differently.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA contract states what success promises, what each error means, and how to retry safely. Every field carries its name, type, cardinality, validation rule and error code, and conditionally mandatory fields are named by rail, destination and product.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Field definition\n→ Required and optional fields\n→ Conditionally mandatory fields\n→ Bank-needed optional fields\n→ Success response meaning\n→ Safe retry rule\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What fields does the API take?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves a caller can use this API without guessing?\"\n\nHave you ever read an API contract that listed every field and answered none of the real questions?", "takeaway": "Where a field is optional under the message schema but the bank's reporting or product needs it, say so in the contract and state the source the bank will use and the derivation rule.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "What does success promise?", "parts": [{"label": "Field definition", "note": "Name, type, cardinality, validation rule and error code."}, {"label": "Required fields", "note": "Stated explicitly in the contract."}, {"label": "Optional fields", "note": "Stated explicitly in the contract."}, {"label": "Conditionally mandatory fields", "note": "Mandatory by rail, destination or product."}, {"label": "Bank-needed optional fields", "note": "State the source and the derivation rule."}, {"label": "Success response meaning", "note": "Say whether success means receipt or completion."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D10 APIs and integration", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch80-api-design-standards-and-integration-patterns.html", "label": "Chapter 80: API design standards and integration patterns", "chapter": "ch80", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "INS-058", "seq": 470, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Two weeks to build a Payment API integration", "explain": "A retailer needs a Payment API integration delivered in a two-week sprint.\nThe checkout must submit payment instructions to the bank, receive a clear acceptance or rejection, and create the order only when the payment outcome is known.\nTwo weeks, and the contract is still unwritten.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sprint starts, two weeks on the clock\n→ Checkout must submit instructions to the bank\n→ Order created only when the outcome is known\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Start building, the contract can follow.\"\n\nStart the sprint with discovery compressed into the first two days: confirm the business outcome, the checkout journey, the payment types in scope, the expected volumes and the response contract. The contract definition matters most, so agree in writing what each response means, so that an acceptance response is never mistaken for completion, and define the status enquiry path the retailer uses when a payment outcome is unclear.\n\nThe contract is the build. Everything else is typing.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDiscovery takes two days, and the contract is agreed in writing before building starts. Acceptance is never mistaken for completion, and the status enquiry path is defined for every unclear outcome.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Business outcome and journey\n→ Payment types and volumes\n→ Response contract in writing\n→ Status enquiry path\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we build it in two weeks?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the contract is agreed before a line of code is written?\"\n\nHave you ever started a two-week build with the contract still unwritten?", "takeaway": "The contract definition matters most, so agree in writing what each response means, so that an acceptance response is never mistaken for completion.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Discovery in two days", "note": "Outcome, journey, payment types, volumes, contract."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Agree the contract in writing", "note": "Acceptance is never mistaken for completion."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Define the status enquiry path", "note": "The path used when an outcome is unclear."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Submit and get a clear outcome", "note": "Clear acceptance or rejection from the bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the outcome known?", "note": "Order is created only when the outcome is known."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch45-payment-product-management.html", "label": "Chapter 45: Payment Product Management", "chapter": "ch45", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/api-playground.html", "label": "API Playground", "slug": "api-playground"}}}, {"id": "INS-022", "seq": 471, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Customers start onboarding, then vanish at document upload", "explain": "A bank wants more new retail customers to complete digital onboarding, but only a small share of started applications reach an active account.\nCustomers abandon the journey at document upload and at identity verification.\nMany who do finish are later held for manual review because documents are unclear or data does not match.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Application started\n→ Identity details entered\n→ Document upload, customers abandon here\n→ Identity verification, more abandon here\n→ Manual review holds the rest\n→ Account opened, first transaction rare\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Customers abandon because they are not interested.\"\n\nStart with evidence, not opinions, and measure the funnel stage by stage: started, identity details entered, document uploaded, identity verified, screening complete, account opened and first transaction. Then map the friction, the document collection, the validation, the screening, the exception handling and the conversion measures, so the redesign targets the stages where customers actually drop off.\n\nMeasure the funnel. Opinions do not fix drop-off.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nMeasure every stage before redesigning anything. The funnel shows where customers actually leave, and the redesign targets those stages, not the ones the team guesses about.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Started\n→ Identity details entered\n→ Document uploaded\n→ Identity verified\n→ Screening complete\n→ Account opened and first transaction\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Why do customers leave?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves at which stage they leave, and what blocks them there?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a funnel and found the drop-off was at a stage nobody had blamed?", "takeaway": "Map the friction, the document collection, the validation, the screening, the exception handling and the conversion measures, so the redesign targets the stages where customers actually drop off.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Started to identity details", "note": "Measure the funnel stage by stage."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Identity details to document upload", "note": "Customers abandon at document upload."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Document upload to verified", "note": "Many are held for manual review after finishing."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Verified to screening complete", "note": "Screening must complete before the account opens."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Screening to account opened", "note": "The account opens after screening."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Redesign at the drop-off stages", "note": "Target the stages where customers actually drop off."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-040", "seq": 472, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "SCT Inst has no maximum, 10 seconds, payee check mandatory", "explain": "A bank must change its instant euro payments to match the current scheme rules.\nThere is no longer a scheme-level maximum amount for SCT Inst, execution must complete within 10 seconds, and verification of payee is mandatory for instant euro payments.\nThe change touches the mobile app, the payment engine, the sanctions and fraud services, the clearing adapter, the ledger and the customer notifications.\nSix systems, one scheme change.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Scheme rules change: no maximum, 10 seconds, payee verification\n→ Mobile app, engine, sanctions, fraud, clearing, ledger and notifications all affected\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"It is a small scheme change.\"\n\nBuild the impact analysis as a map, not as a list of opinions: start from the change and walk upstream to find which channels, products and customer journeys can submit an instant euro payment, which limits and validations apply today, and which of those must change, then walk downstream to find the systems, data, interfaces, controls, tests, operations and release dependencies that are affected.\n\nA scheme change is a map, not a ticket.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWalk upstream from the change to every channel, product and journey that can submit an instant euro payment, then walk downstream to every system, interface, control, test and release dependency. The map is the impact analysis.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Channels and products upstream\n→ Limits and validations today\n→ Systems and interfaces downstream\n→ Controls, tests and release dependencies\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What changed in the scheme?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves every affected channel, system and control has been found?\"\n\nHave you ever scoped a scheme change as small, then found it touched six systems?", "takeaway": "Build the impact analysis as a map, not as a list of opinions: start from the change and walk upstream, then walk downstream.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Start from the change", "note": "No maximum, 10-second execution, payee verification."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Walk upstream", "note": "Find channels, products and journeys that submit instant payments."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Find limits to change", "note": "Find which limits and validations must change."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Walk downstream", "note": "Find systems, data, interfaces, controls, tests and releases."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Is the impact map complete?", "note": "Every channel, system and control found before building starts."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-049", "seq": 473, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One day to go-live, compliance finds a missing requirement", "explain": "A bank is one day from deploying an upgrade to its instant euro payment service.\nUAT is signed off, regression evidence is filed, the deployment window is booked and the customer communications are ready.\nLate in the afternoon the compliance team raises a requirement nobody recorded: verification of payee is mandatory for instant euro payments.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ UAT signed off, deployment booked\n→ Compliance raises an unrecorded requirement\n→ One day to go-live\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"UAT sign-off means the requirements are complete.\"\n\nFirst classify the item precisely as a defect against an agreed requirement, a safety or compliance obligation, or genuinely new scope, and confirm the obligation at its source, for example verification of payee is mandatory for instant euro payments from 9 October 2025 under Regulation (EU) 2024/886, and SCT Inst execution has a 10-second maximum with no scheme-level maximum since the 2025 rulebook change. Then triage defect versus new scope, quantify the risk and obtain a formal go or no-go decision, because a mandatory compliance obligation cannot be deferred the way new scope can.\n\nA mandatory obligation cannot be deferred like new scope.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClassify first, then triage. A defect against an agreed requirement, a safety or compliance obligation, or genuinely new scope, each takes a different path, and a mandatory obligation ends in a formal go or no-go decision, never a quiet deferral.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Defect against an agreed requirement\n→ Safety or compliance obligation\n→ Genuinely new scope\n→ Risk quantification\n→ Formal go or no-go decision\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we still deploy tomorrow?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves what this item is, and who formally decided?\"\n\nHave you ever had a mandatory requirement surface the day before go-live?", "takeaway": "Triage defect versus new scope, quantify the risk and obtain a formal go or no-go decision, because a mandatory compliance obligation cannot be deferred the way new scope can.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Classify the item", "note": "Defect, safety or compliance obligation, or new scope."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Confirm the obligation", "note": "Payee verification is mandatory from 9 October 2025."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Triage defect versus scope", "note": "Triage defect versus genuinely new scope."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Quantify the risk", "note": "Quantify the risk of the late requirement."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Go or no-go?", "note": "A mandatory obligation cannot be deferred like scope."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-054", "seq": 474, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "UAT morning one: most collections rejected", "explain": "A bank is starting user acceptance testing for a new SEPA direct debit collection service.\nOn the first morning the failure rate is critical: most test collections are rejected, mandate references cannot be found, and the test schedule shows collections submitted on the wrong day.\nThe testers are ready. The environment is not.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ UAT starts for SEPA direct debit collections\n→ Most test collections rejected\n→ Mandate references cannot be found\n→ Collections submitted on the wrong day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Testers should push through and log the failures.\"\n\nBegin with a triage that separates the failure causes into four buckets, environment, test data, build and requirements, and check the environment first: whether the UAT build is the intended version, whether the endpoints, credentials and scheme adapters point at the right test systems, and whether the feature flags are set as the release notes state. Only then decide whether to continue, fix and restart, or stop, because continuing a test on a broken environment wastes the testers' time and produces evidence that cannot be trusted.\n\nEvidence from a broken environment is not evidence.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nTriage into four buckets and check the environment first: build version, endpoints, credentials, adapters and flags. Only then decide whether to continue, fix and restart, or stop.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Environment\n→ Test data\n→ Build\n→ Requirements\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many tests failed?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the environment was fit to test in the first place?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a UAT day burn on an environment that was never the right build?", "takeaway": "Only then decide whether to continue, fix and restart, or stop, because continuing a test on a broken environment wastes the testers' time and produces evidence that cannot be trusted.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Triage into four buckets", "note": "Environment, test data, build and requirements."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Check the environment first", "note": "Build version, endpoints, credentials, adapters, flags."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Continue, fix or stop?", "note": "Continuing on a broken environment wastes tester time."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Decide with evidence", "note": "The decision follows the triage, not the schedule."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "failure_category": "mandate issue", "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-055", "seq": 475, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "A few customers, one wrong EMI", "explain": "A bank receives complaints from a small group of loan customers that their monthly EMI is wrong.\nMost customers are unaffected, which makes the problem harder, not easier.\nA handful of complaints, and a calculation that is wrong for some loans and right for the rest.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Small group complains the EMI is wrong\n→ Most customers unaffected\n→ Complaints share no obvious pattern\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Only the complainers are affected.\"\n\nStart by defining the affected population precisely: list every complaining account and search for others with the same characteristics, so the fix covers silent cases as well as complaints. For each affected loan, capture the full calculation inputs: principal, sanctioned rate, rate change history with effective dates, tenure, schedule generation rules, rounding policy, part-payment history and customer data. The pattern only emerges when the cases are compared, because the affected loans share a characteristic, such as a particular rate change date, that the unaffected loans do not.\n\nThe pattern only emerges when the cases are compared.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine the population first, including the silent cases, then capture every calculation input per loan and compare. The affected loans share a characteristic the unaffected ones do not, and the comparison is what finds it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Complaining accounts\n→ Silent cases with the same characteristics\n→ Calculation inputs per loan\n→ Rate change history and effective dates\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Which customers complained?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves who else is affected, and what the affected loans share?\"\n\nHave you ever found a defect that only hit a few customers, and the silent cases were the real problem?", "takeaway": "The pattern only emerges when the cases are compared, because the affected loans share a characteristic, such as a particular rate change date, that the unaffected loans do not.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Define the affected population", "note": "List complaining accounts and search for silent cases."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Capture the calculation inputs", "note": "Principal, rate, history, tenure, rules, rounding, payments."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Compare the cases", "note": "The affected loans share a characteristic the others lack."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Fix the complainers or the population?", "note": "The fix covers silent cases as well as complaints."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D12 Risk, compliance and regulation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch86-regulatory-compliance-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 86: Regulatory compliance in payments", "chapter": "ch86", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "INS-042", "seq": 476, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Make payments faster: the brief with no user, no baseline, no target", "explain": "A bank wants to launch an instant payment product for small business customers.\nThe sponsor has asked the team to make payments faster.\nThe request has no named user group, no baseline, no target speed and no statement of the problem customers actually face today.\nFaster than what, for whom, and by when? Nobody can say.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Sponsor asks for faster payments\n→ No user group named\n→ No baseline and no target speed\n→ No statement of the customer problem\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Faster is a requirement.\"\n\nStart by naming the target users precisely, for example small businesses that pay domestic suppliers on fixed weekly cycles, and exclude segments the product will not serve in the first release, then write a problem statement in the form of who suffers, what it costs them and how often, and gather evidence such as payment volumes, current settlement times, supplier discount losses and support contacts. Then define the hypotheses, the constraints, the minimum viable product and the success metrics, so discovery ends with a decision rather than with a longer list of ideas.\n\nDiscovery ends with a decision, not a longer list.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nName the users, exclude the rest, write the problem as who suffers, what it costs and how often, gather the evidence, then define hypotheses, constraints, the minimum viable product and the success metrics.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Target users\n→ Excluded segments\n→ Problem statement\n→ Evidence\n→ Hypotheses and constraints\n→ MVP and success metrics\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How do we make it faster?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves who suffers, what it costs them, and what success looks like?\"\n\nHave you ever been asked to make payments faster with no baseline and no target?", "takeaway": "Define the hypotheses, the constraints, the minimum viable product and the success metrics, so discovery ends with a decision rather than with a longer list of ideas.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Discovery that ends in a decision", "parts": [{"label": "Target users", "note": "Small businesses paying domestic suppliers weekly."}, {"label": "Excluded segments", "note": "Segments not served in the first release."}, {"label": "Problem statement", "note": "Who suffers, what it costs them and how often."}, {"label": "Evidence", "note": "Volumes, settlement times, discount losses, support contacts."}, {"label": "Hypotheses and constraints", "note": "Defined so discovery ends with a decision."}, {"label": "MVP and success metrics", "note": "Define the minimum product and how success is judged."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D02 Accounts, ledger and accounting", "messages": [], "resolution": "repair", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch70-accounts-and-balances.html", "label": "Chapter 70: Accounts and balances", "chapter": "ch70", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-043", "seq": 477, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The supplier file that passes through three screens and a phone call", "explain": "A bank runs a legacy supplier payment process that has grown by accretion over a decade.\nA corporate customer sends a payment file, operations staff print exception lists, re-key corrected details into three different screens, phone the customer for missing information, and then release the file late in the day.\nOne file, EUR 45,000 of supplier payments, and a whole afternoon of re-keying.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Payment file arrives from the customer\n→ Exception lists printed\n→ Corrected details re-keyed into three screens\n→ Customer phoned for missing information\n→ File released late in the day\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Document the process, then improve it.\"\n\nBegin by observing the work as performed, not as documented: follow real files from intake to release, time each step, and record every workaround, hand-off and re-key, then quantify the pain with measures the business can compare, such as straight-through processing rate, repair rate by defect type, average and worst-case cycle time per step, rework hours, error rate after release and customer contacts per file. Then design the target process, validate the controls and the operational impacts, and only then change the process.\n\nObserve the work as performed, not as documented.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nFollow real files, time each step, record every workaround and re-key, then quantify the pain before designing anything. The target process is designed from evidence, its controls are validated, and only then does the process change.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Work as performed\n→ Straight-through processing rate\n→ Repair rate by defect type\n→ Cycle time and rework hours\n→ Error rate after release\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"What does the procedure say?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves where the time, the rework and the errors actually sit?\"\n\nHave you ever followed a file through a process and found the documented steps bore no relation to the real ones?", "takeaway": "Design the target process, validate the controls and the operational impacts, and only then change the process.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Observe the work as performed", "note": "Follow real files, time each step, record workarounds."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Quantify the pain", "note": "Straight-through rate, repair rate, cycle time, rework, errors."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Design the target process", "note": "Design the target process from the evidence."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Validate controls and impacts", "note": "Validate the controls and operational impacts."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Change the process now?", "note": "Only then change the process."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": [], "resolution": "investigate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch40-payment-reconciliation.html", "label": "Chapter 40: Payment Reconciliation", "chapter": "ch40", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/accounting.html", "label": "Payment Accounting Simulator", "slug": "accounting"}}}, {"id": "INS-114", "seq": 503, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "Ten million a month, and the peak is the only number that matters", "explain": "Five new clients bring ten million transactions a month, roughly 330,000 a day on average.\nThe average looks comfortable.\nThen payroll and supplier files arrive in concentrated morning windows, and the busiest hour is already at about 70 percent of measured capacity.\nThe average fits. The peak does not.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Ten million a month added\n→ 330,000 a day on average\n→ Morning file windows concentrate the load\n→ Busiest hour already at 70 percent of capacity\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Plan for the average.\"\n\nWith the busiest hour already at about 70 percent of measured capacity, the only question that matters is whether the peak fits. Convert the growth into throughput, concurrency, queue depth, storage, monitoring and resilience requirements before the clients go live.\n\nAverages hide peaks. Peaks break systems.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nSize for the peak, not the average. Convert the growth into throughput, concurrency, queue depth, storage, monitoring and resilience requirements, and prove the peak fits before the clients go live.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Measured peak today\n→ Added volume at peak\n→ Throughput and concurrency\n→ Queue depth and storage\n→ Monitoring and resilience\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"How many transactions a month?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the busiest hour fits inside measured capacity?\"\n\nHave you ever signed off capacity on an average, then met the morning peak?", "takeaway": "Convert the growth into throughput, concurrency, queue depth, storage, monitoring and resilience requirements before the clients go live.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Measure today's peak", "note": "Find the busiest hour, not the daily average."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Model the added volume", "note": "Layer the ten million a month onto its morning peaks."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Size for peak throughput", "note": "Test the peak against measured capacity."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Size queues and storage for bursts", "note": "Allow for concentrated file windows."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the peak fit?", "note": "Watch the peak and absorb a surge."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D09 Payment architecture and technology", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch25-payment-hub-architecture.html", "label": "Chapter 25: Payment Hub Architecture", "chapter": "ch25", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/hub.html", "label": "Payment Hub Architecture Simulator", "slug": "hub"}}}, {"id": "INS-115", "seq": 504, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "One extra hour, and every deadline moves", "explain": "The business asks for the customer cut-off to move one hour later.\nOne hour sounds small.\nBut a cut-off is not a single time, it is the end of a deadline chain: channel cut-offs, scheme windows, liquidity checks and operational staffing.\nMove the hour, and every deadline in the chain moves with it.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Cut-off extended by one hour\n→ Scheme windows tested\n→ Liquidity tested for the extra hour\n→ Staffing tested for the later window\n→ Exception path for payments that still miss it\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A cut-off is a single time.\"\n\nA cut-off is not a single time but the end of a deadline chain, channel cut-offs, scheme windows, liquidity checks and operational staffing. Adding an hour moves the whole chain, and the customer promise has to survive at the new time. Judge the extension on four things, downstream scheme windows, liquidity for the extra hour, staffing to run it, and exception handling for payments that still miss the new cut-off.\n\nA cut-off is the end of a deadline chain.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nJudge the extension on four things, and prove each one: downstream scheme windows accept the later time, liquidity covers the extra hour, staffing can run the later window, and payments that still miss the new cut-off have a defined exception path.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Downstream scheme windows\n→ Liquidity for the extra hour\n→ Staffing for the later window\n→ Exception handling\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we move the cut-off?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the whole deadline chain still holds at the new time?\"\n\nHave you ever moved a cut-off by an hour and found every downstream deadline had moved too?", "takeaway": "Judge the extension on four things, downstream scheme windows, liquidity for the extra hour, staffing to run it, and exception handling for payments that still miss the new cut-off.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Map every deadline in the chain", "note": "List every deadline the cut-off closes."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Test the scheme windows", "note": "Test whether the schemes accept the later time."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Test liquidity for the extra hour", "note": "Test whether funding covers the extra hour."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Test staffing for the later window", "note": "Test whether operations can run the later window."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Does the promise survive?", "note": "Define the path when the new cut-off is missed."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D07 Clearing, settlement and liquidity", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch88-clearing-and-settlement-mechanisms.html", "label": "Chapter 88: Clearing and settlement mechanisms", "chapter": "ch88", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-F09", "seq": 514, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The rejected direct debit that landed in a third-party account", "explain": "A direct debit collection of EUR 9,500 is rejected by the debtor bank.\nNormally it returns to the creditor bank, authorising it to debit the originator's account.\nThis time the collection is booked to a designated third-party account instead, and the operations team is not sure the booking was ever allowed.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Collection rejected by the debtor bank\n→ Normal path returns it to the creditor bank\n→ Third-party account proposed instead\n→ Preconditions checked, or skipped?\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"A reject always goes back the way it came.\"\n\nA debtor bank normally returns a rejected collection to the creditor bank, authorising it to debit the originator's account, but a collection can instead be booked to a designated third-party account. The third-party account is funded to meet regulatory obligations and to follow rejected payments through the general ledger, and it can be used only if the originator's instructions allow it, the account was opened and funded first, and it is opened and maintained by the creditor bank. Building it as a system requirement is a sensitive decision that needs product owner and business owner agreement, with checks for the returning customer, fraud signs and valid repayment flows.\n\nA third-party account for rejects is a sensitive decision, not a routing option.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nChallenge the requirement before building it. The account can be used only if the originator's instructions allow it, the account was opened and funded first, and the creditor bank opens and maintains it, with product owner and business owner agreement, and checks for the returning customer, fraud signs and valid repayment flows.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Originator instruction allows it\n→ Account opened and funded first\n→ Creditor bank opens and maintains it\n→ Product and business owner agreement\n→ Fraud and repayment checks\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we book it there?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the originator allowed it, the account was funded first, and the owners agreed?\"\n\nHave you ever been asked to build a routing option that turned out to be a sensitive decision in disguise?", "takeaway": "A BA cannot necessarily refuse a requirement, yet challenging it remains the BA responsibility.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "Collection rejected by the debtor bank", "note": "AC01 wrong account number or AC06 blocked account are typical returns."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Book to a third-party account?", "note": "The normal path returns the collection to the creditor bank."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Are the preconditions met?", "note": "Originator instruction, account opened and funded first, creditor bank maintains it."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Book the reject to the designated account", "note": "The funded account tracks the rejected payment in the ledger."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Run fraud, clearing and accounting checks", "note": "Identify the customer, check fraud signs and repayment flows."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D05 Domestic payment systems", "messages": [], "failure_category": "payment rejected or returned", "resolution": "escalate", "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch62-sepa-direct-debit-deep-dive.html", "label": "Chapter 62: SEPA Direct Debit Deep Dive", "chapter": "ch62", "fallback": false}}}, {"id": "JEB-F05", "seq": 515, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "The consent arrived late. The 500 payments were still moving.", "explain": "A set of 500 payments is being cancelled.\nA negative camt.029 arrives while the payment engine is still processing the set.\nThen a beneficiary consent arrives, after the expected timeframe, for payments that may already be on their way back.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ camt.056 cancels the payment set\n→ Negative camt.029 arrives mid-processing\n→ Late beneficiary consent arrives\n→ pacs.004 return follows, per payment or grouped\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Late consent is just late.\"\n\nA negative camt.029 can arrive while a payment engine is still processing a set of 500 payments, and a beneficiary consent can then arrive after the expected timeframe, while the pacs.004 return that follows can be made per payment or grouped by customer, reason, value or compliance cause. The remaining payments have no universal answer: return them, complete them, or reject the late consent by accepting or overriding the rule, so each bank must define when a late consent can be accepted and when it cannot, and record the message, the consent and the retention rules that apply. Treating a late consent as merely late misses the point, since it can still reflect sound business behaviour.\n\nTreating a late consent as merely late misses the point.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nDefine the bank rule for late consent in writing: when it can be accepted, when it cannot, and what is recorded, the message, the consent and the retention rules. A late consent can still reflect sound business behaviour, and the remaining payments are returned, completed or rejected by that rule, not by habit.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Bank rule for late consent\n→ Message recorded\n→ Consent recorded\n→ Retention rules applied\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Was the consent late?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the bank rule allows this consent, and what was recorded?\"\n\nHave you ever received a consent after the deadline and had to decide whether it still counted?", "takeaway": "Treating a late consent as merely late misses the point, since it can still reflect sound business behaviour.", "visual": {"type": "flow", "flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "title": "camt.056 cancels the payment set", "note": "The cancellation request reaches the payment engine."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Negative camt.029 arrives mid-processing", "note": "Some of the 500 payments are still unprocessed."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Late beneficiary consent arrives", "note": "Consent from the same beneficiary, after the timeframe."}, {"kind": "decision", "title": "Can late consent be accepted?", "note": "Apply the bank rule for accepting or rejecting late consent."}, {"kind": "step", "title": "Return funds and record everything", "note": "pacs.004 returns the funds, record the message, consent and retention."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D11 Operations, exceptions and reconciliation", "messages": ["camt.056", "camt.029", "pacs.004"], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch58-reject-and-return-in-cbpr.html", "label": "Chapter 58: Reject and Return in CBPR+", "chapter": "ch58", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "JEB-F03", "seq": 517, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Public post", "title": "ISO 20022 ready? Eight gaps say otherwise", "explain": "A bank is migrating to ISO 20022, and the project plan says the business is ready.\nThe messages are mapped, the build is on track, and the go-live date is set.\nThen the first customer payment journey breaks, because nobody aligned the processes, the hand-offs, the training, the data quality or the control teams.\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Messages mapped, build on track\n→ Processes not aligned to customer journeys\n→ Hand-offs unplanned, teams untrained\n→ Systems cannot hold high data quality\n→ Compliance, fraud and AML not aligned\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Migration is a message format change.\"\n\nISO 20022 migration is a transformation of the payments business model, not a message format change, and readiness fails where processes are not aligned to customer payment journeys, hand-offs are unplanned, business teams are untrained, systems cannot hold high data quality, and compliance, fraud and AML teams are not aligned. Limited resources, missing governance and weak coordination across stakeholders leave the gap between business and technology in place even after execution starts.\n\nISO 20022 migration is a transformation of the payments business model, not a message format change.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nClose the eight gaps before claiming readiness: journey alignment, planned hand-offs, trained business teams, high data quality, aligned control teams, and governance, resources and coordination across stakeholders.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Journey alignment\n→ Planned hand-offs\n→ Trained business teams\n→ High data quality\n→ Aligned control teams\n→ Governance and resources\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Are the messages mapped?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the business, not just the build, is ready?\"\n\nHave you ever watched a migration go live with the messages ready and the business unready?", "takeaway": "A migration plan must close these eight gaps before it can claim the business is ready.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "ISO 20022 migration readiness", "parts": [{"label": "Journey alignment", "note": "Processes must follow the customer payment journey."}, {"label": "Planned hand-offs", "note": "Every hand-off between teams is defined in advance."}, {"label": "Trained business teams", "note": "Business teams understand ISO 20022 principles."}, {"label": "High data quality", "note": "Systems must hold rich, accurate payment data."}, {"label": "Aligned control teams", "note": "Compliance, fraud and AML work to the same plan."}, {"label": "Governance and resources", "note": "Oversight, skills and coordination across stakeholders."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D14 Business analysis and professional practice", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch84-ba-practice-depth.html", "label": "Chapter 84: BA practice depth", "chapter": "ch84", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/message-explorer.html", "label": "ISO 20022 Message Explorer", "slug": "message-explorer"}}}, {"id": "RUN6-005", "seq": 521, "module": "Advanced", "level": "advanced", "source": "Article and reader comments", "title": "Blockchain for payments: four questions nobody has closed", "explain": "A EUR 40,000 payment is proposed on a shared ledger, and the room is full of confidence.\nThen four questions land, and nobody in the room can answer any of them.\nWho sees the confidential fields? Who maintains the ledger? What format does it use? And can one network really span domestic and cross-border payments?\n\nHere's how that happens:\n\n→ Shared ledger proposed for payments\n→ Confidential fields visible to every node\n→ Formats vary by network\n→ One network proposed for domestic and cross-border\n\nThe assumption was:\n\"Blockchain answers everything at once.\"\n\nTransparency means every node sees transaction detail, and control over confidential fields is still being built. A business relationship links each node with every other node, which simplifies maintenance of the shared ledger. Formats will vary with the algorithm each network uses, so one universal message and ledger format is ambitious yet not impossible. A single network spanning domestic and cross-border payments is achievable if the industry aligns on common standards.\n\nA single network spanning domestic and cross-border payments is achievable if the industry aligns on common standards.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nAnswer the four questions before proposing the ledger: confidentiality of fields, the business relationships between nodes, the message and ledger formats, and the common standards a single network would need.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Confidentiality\n→ Business relationships\n→ Formats\n→ Reach\n\nThe real requirement isn't \"Can we use blockchain?\"\n\nIt's \"What evidence proves the four open questions have answers?\"\n\nHave you ever sat in a room where a new technology was proposed and the basic questions had no answers?", "takeaway": "A single network spanning domestic and cross-border payments is achievable if the industry aligns on common standards.", "visual": {"type": "diagram", "diagram": {"centre": "Four open questions on blockchain payments", "parts": [{"label": "Confidentiality", "note": "Every node sees transaction detail; control of confidential fields is in progress."}, {"label": "Business relationships", "note": "Each node relates to every other node in the network."}, {"label": "Formats", "note": "Message and ledger formats vary by algorithm; one format is ambitious."}, {"label": "Reach", "note": "One network for domestic and cross-border payments needs common standards."}]}}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "domain": "D15 Advanced and emerging payments", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch27-cbdcs-stablecoins-and-ai-in-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 27: CBDCs, Stablecoins and AI in Payments", "chapter": "ch27", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "GBP 40,000 left London for a supplier overseas.\nThe customer was told the payment was sent.\nThe supplier was never paid.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 sent to the beneficiary bank\n→ MT103 passes sanctions screening\n→ MT202 COV held at an intermediary bank\n→ Beneficiary bank waits for the cover\n→ No credit is made\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Screen the MT103. The cover follows the same path.\"\n\nSo only the information leg was screened, and the funds leg was left to travel on trust.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 and its MT202 COV are screened separately, and a hit on either leg stops the payment. The intermediary held the cover for sanctions screening while the MT103 had already arrived, so the beneficiary bank had the instruction and no money to pay with.\n\nA clear MT103 is not a paid beneficiary.\n\nScreening the information leg does not clear the funds leg.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe MT103 is information. The cover is the money.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT103 screening status\n→ MT202 COV screening status\n→ Funds received and reconciled\n\nNever tell a customer a payment is sent until both legs are clear.\n\nThe real requirement is not \"When do we send the MT103?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves both legs are clear and the money can move?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment marked sent while the cover was still held?", "id": "ADD-001", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 530, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The MT103 is information. The cover is the money. Screening one does not clear the other.", "title": "MT103 cleared, the cover held, GBP 40,000 stuck", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Information leg leaves for the beneficiary bank", "title": "MT103 sent"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "The information leg passes its checks", "title": "MT103 screened clear"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the funds stop at the intermediary", "title": "Cover screened clear?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Sanctions screening holds the funds leg", "title": "MT202 COV held"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Beneficiary bank has the instruction, no money", "title": "No credit made"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch14-serial-vs-cover-payments.html", "label": "Chapter 14: Serial vs Cover Payments", "chapter": "ch14", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "A correspondent cut its intraday credit limit without warning.\nThe payment engine kept feeding the same corridor.\nBy the afternoon, payments were queuing, then rejecting, and nobody could explain why.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Correspondent cuts the intraday limit\n→ Engine keeps sending at the old volume\n→ Queue builds on the corridor\n→ Payments reject with no clear reason\n→ Operations cannot reconstruct which payments were affected\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Routing rules are written for the quiet day.\"\n\nSo the rules had no eligibility check, no fallback route and no audit trail, and the bad day arrived exactly as it always does, without warning.\n\nWhy?\n\nA correspondent can cut an intraday credit limit without warning, and an engine that keeps feeding the same corridor will queue payments and generate rejects it cannot explain. Routing decisions must carry eligibility checks, fallback routes, a defined decision order and an audit trail naming the affected payments and the reason.\n\nA routing decision nobody can reconstruct is a control failure.\n\nThe queue was not the failure. The silence about the queue was.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nBuild routing rules for the bad day, not the quiet one.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Eligibility check before routing\n→ Fallback route when a limit is cut\n→ Defined decision order\n→ Audit trail naming every affected payment\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Which route is fastest?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves this route is still open, and what happens if it closes?\"\n\nHave you seen a corridor fail while the engine kept feeding it?", "id": "ADD-006", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 535, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Build routing rules for the bad day, not the quiet one. A routing decision nobody can reconstruct is a control failure.", "title": "Limit cut without warning, the queue filled fast", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "Corridor limit cut without warning", "parts": [{"label": "Limit cut", "note": "Correspondent acts, nobody is told"}, {"label": "Engine keeps feeding", "note": "Same corridor, same volume"}, {"label": "Queue builds", "note": "Payments wait, then reject"}, {"label": "No audit trail", "note": "Nobody can list the affected payments"}, {"label": "Fallback missing", "note": "No second route was ever defined"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A cover payment settled cleanly months ago.\nThen an MT191 arrived, claiming the intermediary charges.\nThe nostro was debited, reconciliation broke, and nobody remembered the payment.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 and MT202 COV settle in full\n→ Intermediary is owed charges for its part in the chain\n→ Intermediary claims them separately by MT191\n→ Nostro debited months after the payment\n→ Reconciliation breaks on a payment everyone had closed\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Charges are taken from the payment. There is nothing left to claim.\"\n\nSo the team closed the books on the payment and never expected the charges leg to arrive on its own timetable.\n\nWhy?\n\nCharges must never be deducted from a cover payment, so an intermediary that is owed money asks for it separately instead of taking it from the funds. The claim travels in an MT191, a request for payment of charges, and the advice travels in an MT190. A reader who sees an MT191 in a chain is looking at the charges leg, not the payment itself.\n\nThe cover is not the charges leg.\n\nThe payment settled. The bill for moving it arrived later.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA cover that arrives short is an exception. The charges travel separately.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Cover amount, never reduced for charges\n→ Charges claimed by MT191\n→ Charges advised by MT190\n→ Nostro reconciliation that expects late claims\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the cover arrive in full?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves every charge in the chain was claimed, advised and reconciled?\"\n\nHave you seen an MT191 arrive long after the payment was closed?", "id": "ADD-008", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 537, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The cover is not the charges leg. Charges travel separately, by MT191, and can arrive months later.", "title": "Charges claimed by MT191, nostro debited months later", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "MT191: the charges leg arrives late", "parts": [{"label": "Cover settles in full", "note": "MT202 COV arrives, nothing deducted"}, {"label": "Intermediary owed", "note": "Charges for its part in the chain"}, {"label": "MT191 claim", "note": "Charges requested separately, months later"}, {"label": "Nostro debited", "note": "A debit on a payment already closed"}, {"label": "Reconciliation breaks", "note": "Nobody remembers the original payment"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch13-correspondent-banking-and-nostro-vostro.html", "label": "Chapter 13: Correspondent Banking and Nostro Vostro", "chapter": "ch13", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/cross-border.html", "label": "Cross-Border Payment Configurator", "slug": "cross-border"}}}, {"explain": "A cross-border payment needed sterling settlement in the UK.\nThe sterling leg went by CHAPS, for a house purchase completion that day.\nThe completion missed its day.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ MT103 arrives for the cross-border payment\n→ Sterling leg sent to CHAPS\n→ Payment queued for insufficient liquidity\n→ Customer cut-off passes with the payment still queued\n→ Settlement moves to the next working day\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"CHAPS settles the same day. Send it and the completion is safe.\"\n\nSo the team promised the day without checking the balance behind it, and without remembering that the cross-border leg still needed its own route out of the UK.\n\nWhy?\n\nCHAPS settles sterling payments between UK banks in central bank money, and a cross-border payment that needs sterling settlement in the UK can use it as the domestic leg. But CHAPS settles the same day only during business hours and only with liquidity behind the payment. A payment queued for insufficient funds waits, and the customer cut-off passes while it waits.\n\nSame day is a promise with a cut-off and a balance behind it.\n\nCHAPS was one leg of the journey, not the whole journey.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nA CHAPS leg needs liquidity confirmed before the customer is promised the day.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Liquidity available for the CHAPS leg\n→ Customer cut-off, checked before promising the day\n→ Cross-border leg, routed separately out of the UK\n→ Queued payment, watched until it settles\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Can CHAPS settle today?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the funds, the cut-off and the onward route all line up today?\"\n\nHave you seen a same-day promise fail on a queued CHAPS payment?", "id": "ADD-009", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 538, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "Same day is a promise with a cut-off and a balance behind it. Confirm liquidity before promising the day.", "title": "CHAPS leg queued, the house completion missed its day", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Cross-border payment needs a sterling leg", "title": "MT103 arrives"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Same-day settlement in central bank money", "title": "Sterling leg to CHAPS"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the payment queues and waits", "title": "Liquidity available?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Queued payment misses the day", "title": "Cut-off passes"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Completion moves, contracts strain", "title": "Settles next day"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch12-uk-faster-payments-chaps-bacs.html", "label": "Chapter 12: UK: Faster Payments, CHAPS, BACS", "chapter": "ch12", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A EUR 5 million payment was accepted on Monday for Friday execution.\nThe customer was promised Friday.\nOn Thursday, the beneficiary was added to a sanctions list.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Payment accepted Monday for Friday execution\n→ Payment warehoused until Friday\n→ Sanctions list changes on Thursday\n→ Friday revalidation checks sanctions status\n→ Execution blocked, customer promise broken\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Valid on Monday means valid on Friday.\"\n\nSo the team treated warehousing as storage, and forgot that four days is enough for the world to change.\n\nWhy?\n\nWarehousing turns a future-dated payment into a commitment to execute, not merely a stored instruction. Balance, account details, sanctions status and cut-off times can all move between submission and execution, so validity on Monday proves nothing about Friday. The payment must be re-validated on balance, account status, sanctions and cut-offs at execution, then executed or rejected.\n\nWarehousing stores the promise, not the permission.\n\nMonday's checks were perfect. Friday's world was different.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nRe-validate at execution. Monday's checks prove nothing about Friday.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Validity at submission\n→ Validity at execution, checked again\n→ Sanctions status on the execution day\n→ Balance and cut-off, checked again\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Was it valid when accepted?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves it is still valid on the day it moves?\"\n\nHave you seen a warehoused payment stopped by a change nobody expected?", "id": "ADD-014", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 543, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Warehousing stores the promise, not the permission. Re-validate at execution.", "title": "Warehoused Monday, listed Thursday, stopped Friday", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "EUR 5 million promised for Friday", "title": "Accepted Monday"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Payment waits four days to execute", "title": "Warehoused"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Sanctions list changes mid-week", "title": "Listed Thursday"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, execution must stop", "title": "Still clear on Friday?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Promise broken, but the payment is safe", "title": "Execution blocked"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A customer with a EUR 300,000 limit sent EUR 200,000 instantly.\nIt did not arrive instantly.\nIt travelled to TARGET2 instead, slower and costlier.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ Customer sends EUR 200,000 by SCT Inst\n→ Routing rule sees an amount above EUR 100,000\n→ Payment routed to TARGET2\n→ Arrives later, at TARGET2 cost\n→ Customer asks why the instant payment was not instant\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"The routing rule follows the scheme limit.\"\n\nSo the rule kept enforcing a limit the scheme had already removed, and two live rules produced different outcomes for the same payment.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen the EU Instant Payments Regulation removed the EUR 100,000 scheme-level maximum for SCT Inst, the old routing rule that sent payments above EUR 100,000 to TARGET2 lost the scheme limit it was built on. A bank can now set its own customer limits, so a customer with a EUR 300,000 limit can send EUR 200,000 instantly while the unchanged routing rule still sends anything above EUR 100,000 to TARGET2.\n\nA rule built on a dead limit is a live defect.\n\nThe scheme moved on. The routing rule did not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nWhen a scheme limit dies, every rule built on it must be re-checked.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Scheme limit, as it stands today\n→ Customer limit, set by the bank\n→ Routing rule, traced to the limit it was built on\n→ Conflict check when any limit changes\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does the routing rule say?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the routing rule still matches the scheme and the customer limit?\"\n\nHave you seen a payment take the slow route because a rule outlived its limit?", "id": "ADD-015", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 544, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "A rule built on a dead limit is a live defect. When a scheme limit dies, re-check every rule built on it.", "title": "EUR 200,000 instant, routed slow to TARGET2", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "EUR 200,000: instant or TARGET2?", "parts": [{"label": "Scheme limit removed", "note": "No more EUR 100,000 maximum for SCT Inst"}, {"label": "Customer limit EUR 300,000", "note": "Bank allows this customer to send instantly"}, {"label": "Routing rule unchanged", "note": "Still sends above EUR 100,000 to TARGET2"}, {"label": "Payment goes slow", "note": "TARGET2 instead of instant, costlier too"}, {"label": "Two rules, one payment", "note": "Each rule is right alone, wrong together"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch59-tips-in-depth-instant-payments-and-liquidity-transfers.html", "label": "Chapter 59: TIPS in Depth: Instant Payments and Liquidity Transfers", "chapter": "ch59", "fallback": false}, "simulator": {"url": "simulators/t2-tips.html", "label": "T2 and TIPS Simulator", "slug": "t2-tips"}}}, {"explain": "A cross-border payment was promised in 60 seconds.\nThe euro leg cleared on time.\nThe beneficiary was paid late, and short of the expected amount.\n\nHere is how that happens:\n\n→ OCT Inst payment starts in Europe\n→ Euro leg clears and settles in seconds\n→ Non-euro leg conversion runs outside the euro area\n→ Conversion is not complete before the beneficiary is due\n→ Beneficiary paid late, for less than expected\n\nThe assumption said:\n\"Sixty seconds covers the whole payment.\"\n\nSo the team sold the headline time and forgot that the second leg lives under different rules, different standards and different bank policies.\n\nWhy?\n\nOCT Inst processes cross-border payments in 60 seconds, handling both the payment and the settlement, with both legs settled through the euro system. But the non-euro leg runs wholly outside the euro area, governed by non-euro rules, standards and bank policies, with conversion completed before the beneficiary is paid, so the beneficiary receives exactly the amount expected. Speed is not the test here, reliability and rule-following are.\n\nSpeed is not the test. Reliability and rule-following are.\n\nThe euro leg was instant. The payment was not.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe 60-second promise ends where the non-euro leg begins. Prove the conversion first.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Euro leg, cleared in seconds\n→ Non-euro leg, governed by non-euro rules\n→ Conversion complete before the beneficiary is paid\n→ Exact amount the beneficiary will receive, confirmed\n\nThe real requirement is not \"How fast is the euro leg?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the conversion is done and the beneficiary gets the exact amount?\"\n\nHave you seen an instant payment go slow on its second leg?", "id": "ADD-016", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 545, "source": "Public post", "takeaway": "Speed is not the test. Reliability and rule-following are. Prove the conversion before promising 60 seconds.", "title": "OCT Inst in 60 seconds, the conversion took longer", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "Euro leg plus a leg outside the euro area", "title": "OCT Inst starts"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Settled in seconds through the euro system", "title": "Euro leg clears"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "If no, the beneficiary cannot be paid exactly", "title": "Conversion complete?"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Different rules, standards and policies apply", "title": "Non-euro leg waits"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Beneficiary gets less than expected", "title": "Paid late and short"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}, {"explain": "A sender instructed a payment of GBP 20,000 with field 71A set to BEN, so the beneficiary would bear every charge. The beneficiary was credited almost in full, and the sender was billed for charges it never agreed to pay.\n\nSomewhere in the chain, a bank copy showed SHA where the sender had instructed BEN.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 generated with 71A BEN\n→ A downstream bank copy shows 71A SHA\n→ Charges split between sender and beneficiary instead of falling on the beneficiary\n→ Sender billed for charges it never agreed to bear\n→ Reconciliation breaks, nobody can agree who owes what\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Copy the charge code from the previous bank's message.\"\n\nSo the code was retyped at every step instead of carried forward.\n\nWhy?\n\nField 71A is set by the bank that generates the MT103 and cannot be changed by later banks. Later banks can only add a field 71F when they take a charge. A bank copy that shows SHA where the sender instructed BEN is a discrepancy worth querying, because the two codes give the beneficiary a different amount.\n\nThe charges equation always holds: field 32A equals field 33B plus field 71G minus all the 71F charges. Field 32A is the amount that settles between banks, field 33B is the amount the ordering customer instructed, field 71G is the receiver's charges prepaid by the sender, and field 71F is each sender's charge deducted along the way. If the equation does not balance, the code or a deduction is wrong.\n\n71A is set once, by the bank that generates the MT103. Nobody downstream may change it.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe charge code is set once and carried, never retyped.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ 71A as generated, never changed downstream\n→ Each 71F deduction as it happens\n→ The 32A reconciliation equation\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What does our copy of the code say?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the charge code matches the original MT103?\"\n\nHave you ever found a charge code that changed hands mid-chain?", "id": "XBR-007", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 556, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "71A is set once, by the bank that generates the MT103. Nobody downstream may change it.", "title": "71A said BEN, the copy said SHA, and the sender paid charges it never agreed to", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "The charges equation must always balance", "parts": [{"label": "Field 33B", "note": "Amount the ordering customer instructed"}, {"label": "Field 71G", "note": "Receiver charges prepaid by the sender"}, {"label": "Field 71F", "note": "Each charge deducted along the way"}, {"label": "Field 32A", "note": "Amount that settles between banks"}, {"label": "Field 71A", "note": "Set once by the generating bank, never changed"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch15-charges-fx-and-gpi-tracking.html", "label": "Chapter 15: Charges, FX and gpi Tracking", "chapter": "ch15", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A payment of USD 75,000 to an overseas supplier passed sanctions screening and was released. The supplier was never paid. The funds were sitting in a hold at an intermediary bank.\n\nOnly one leg of the payment had been screened.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 screened, no hit, released\n→ MT202 COV sent through the correspondent chain\n→ Cover leg hits a sanctions list at an intermediary\n→ Cover held for screening, MT103 left waiting\n→ Originator believes the payment cleared screening\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Screen the MT103 before release. The cover follows the same instruction.\"\n\nSo the funds leg travelled unscreened until an intermediary stopped it.\n\nWhy?\n\nAn MT103 and its MT202 COV are both subject to sanction screening, and the screening must happen before the payment is credited to the beneficiary. The information leg and the funds leg are screened separately, and a hit on either leg stops the payment. A bank that screens only the MT103 and not the cover, or the reverse, leaves a gap that a regulator will find.\n\nOne clean leg does not clear a payment. Both legs must pass.\n\nThe two legs can also name different ordering parties, because the MT103 names the customer who instructed the payment while the MT202 COV names the bank moving the funds. Screening has to cover the parties on each leg as that leg names them.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nScreen both legs, or the payment is not screened.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ MT103 screening\n→ MT202 COV screening\n→ Hold handling and customer communication\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did the MT103 pass screening?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves both legs passed screening before we credited the beneficiary?\"\n\nHave you ever released a payment that was still held on a leg nobody checked?", "id": "XBR-008", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 557, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "One clean leg does not clear a payment. Both legs must pass.", "title": "USD 75,000 passed screening, but only on the leg that was checked", "visual": {"flow": {"stepSeconds": 3, "steps": [{"kind": "step", "note": "MT103 screened, no hit, released", "title": "MT103 screened"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Funds leg sent through the correspondent chain", "title": "Cover sent"}, {"kind": "decision", "note": "Was the MT202 COV screened on its own parties?", "title": "Screen the cover"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Sanctions hit on the cover leg, funds held", "title": "Cover held"}, {"kind": "step", "note": "Hold both legs until screening clears on each", "title": "Fix the control"}]}, "type": "flow"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch23-sanctions-screening.html", "label": "Chapter 23: Sanctions Screening", "chapter": "ch23", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A payment of USD 60,000 passed through a correspondent bank on its way to the beneficiary. The beneficiary was paid. The originator bank could not prove it.\n\nIts reconciliation broke, because the thread linking the two legs had been cut.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ pacs.008 sent with a UETR in field 121\n→ Correspondent bank receives the incoming leg\n→ Correspondent sends the outgoing leg onward\n→ Field 121 changed on the outgoing leg\n→ Originator cannot match the payment it sent with the payment that arrived\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Each bank may set its own reference on the leg it sends.\"\n\nSo the correspondent replaced the reference instead of carrying it forward.\n\nWhy?\n\nWhen a payment passes through a correspondent bank, that bank sees two payments: the incoming payment from the sender and the outgoing payment to the next bank. The two legs are linked by the unique end-to-end transaction reference, the UETR, which travels unchanged from one message to the next and is carried in field 121 of block 3. No bank in the chain may modify field 121, because changing it would break the link that lets every bank reconcile the payment it received with the payment it sent.\n\nThe UETR is the thread. Break it and the payment cannot be traced.\n\nThe same discipline applies in the MT world and the MX world. A pacs.008 is the ISO 20022 customer credit transfer, the equivalent of an MT103, and a pacs.009 is the financial institution transfer, the equivalent of an MT202. The names change, the legs stay the same.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nThe reference belongs to the payment, not to the bank handling it.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ UETR carried unchanged through every leg\n→ Field 121 integrity check at each handover\n→ Reconciliation by UETR, not by local reference\n\nThe real requirement is not \"Did each bank send its leg?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the outgoing leg is the same payment as the incoming leg?\"\n\nHave you ever lost a payment in reconciliation because a reference changed hands?", "id": "XBR-013", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 562, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "The UETR is the thread. Break it and the payment cannot be traced.", "title": "USD 60,000 paid in full, but the bank could not prove it was the same payment", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "One UETR, unchanged, through every leg", "parts": [{"label": "Incoming leg", "note": "pacs.008 arrives with its UETR in field 121"}, {"label": "Correspondent sees two", "note": "One payment in, one payment out"}, {"label": "Field 121 changed", "note": "Outgoing leg carries a different reference"}, {"label": "Link broken", "note": "No bank can match what it sent with what it received"}, {"label": "Carry it forward", "note": "UETR travels unchanged from end to end"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {"lesson": {"url": "chapters/ch1-what-is-a-payment.html", "label": "Chapter 1: What is a Payment", "chapter": "ch1", "fallback": false}}}, {"explain": "A customer sent USD 30,000 to a supplier's US dollar account. The supplier received USD 29,700 and sent the invoice back unpaid.\n\nUSD 300 had vanished in two currency conversions that should never have happened.\n\nHere is what happened:\n\n→ MT103 sent in USD to a USD account\n→ Intermediary converts USD to EUR\n→ Next bank converts EUR back to USD\n→ Beneficiary credited USD 29,700\n→ Supplier treats the invoice as unpaid\n\nThe requirement said:\n\"Each bank may settle in whatever currency suits its own books.\"\n\nSo intermediaries converted the payment twice and charged for both conversions.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe sender decides the currency of a transaction, and no intermediary may change it on the way. If the beneficiary's account is in a different currency, the conversion happens as a separate foreign exchange step, and the beneficiary is credited in the currency of the account. A payment sent in dollars to a dollar account that arrives short because an intermediary converted it twice has gone wrong, and the banks in the chain must be challenged on it.\n\nNo intermediary may change the currency. The sender decides it.\n\nThe same rule protects the cover leg. In a cover payment the MT103 and the MT202 can carry different currencies, and fields 52, 53 and 54 describe the chain of banks that will fund the payment, but the currency of each leg follows the correspondent account it settles in. It is never changed mid-chain by an intermediary.\n\nBA Takeaway\n\nCurrency is set by the sender and carried, never converted in passing.\n\nKeep these controls separate:\n\n→ Currency as instructed\n→ Foreign exchange as a separate, agreed step\n→ Shortfall challenge against the chain\n\nThe real requirement is not \"What currency did each bank use?\"\n\nIt is \"What evidence proves the currency the sender chose reached the beneficiary unchanged?\"\n\nHave you ever seen a payment arrive short because somebody converted it on the way?", "id": "XBR-015", "level": "advanced", "module": "Advanced", "seq": 564, "source": "Article and reader comments", "takeaway": "No intermediary may change the currency. The sender decides it.", "title": "USD 30,000 to a dollar account arrived USD 300 short after two conversions", "visual": {"diagram": {"centre": "The sender sets the currency, nobody changes it", "parts": [{"label": "Sent in USD", "note": "MT103 in dollars to a dollar account"}, {"label": "First conversion", "note": "Intermediary converts USD to EUR"}, {"label": "Second conversion", "note": "Next bank converts EUR back to USD"}, {"label": "USD 300 lost", "note": "Two conversions, two spreads, short credit"}, {"label": "Challenge the chain", "note": "Conversion mid-chain is a failure, not a fee"}]}, "type": "diagram"}, "stage": 6, "stage_name": "Senior SME judgement", "messages": [], "related": {}}];
